\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for June 10, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Pete Williams, Sahil Kapur, Andrew Weissmann, Andrea  Mitchell, Kavita Patel \par \uc2 9,473 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 June 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Thursday, President Biden is sent to speak from the U.K. at any moment about the pandemic and his administration`s global vaccine strategy as vaccinations lag here at home and new variants spread abroad.\par \par \uc2 Plus the FBI Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  testifies on Capitol Hill for the first time since the failed vote to former January 6 Commission, as well as those ransomware attacks against our gas and food supplies.\par \par \uc2 And lawmakers are now trying to salvage any hopes of a bipartisan deal on infrastructure as the President`s aspirational agenda is caught between a divide over pragmatism and progressives. Senator Jon Tester knows a thing or two about that debate. He joins me ahead as well.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Thursday. It is MEET THE PRESS DAILY and I`m Chuck Todd. We`ve got a busy one for you today. The President is scheduled to speak from the U.K. this hour about new efforts to send vaccines to the international community to try to combat this pandemic and see if we can actually stop the global pandemic. We`ll be keeping a close eye on the President`s movement overseas and bring you those remarks when they begin.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with the FBI Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 . He`s been testifying before Congress for the first time today since a bipartisan Senate report faulted the Justice Department for a series of intelligence failures ahead of the January 6 siege on the Capitol. We`ve seen members of the House Judiciary Committee ranging from Democratic Chairman Jerry Nadler to conservative firebrand Louie Gohmert knock re on the issue of the FBI`s intelligence leading up to the attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JERRY NADLER (D-NY): It`s hard to tell whether \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters merely missed the evidence which had been flagged by your field offices and was available online for all the world to see or whether the bureau saw the intelligence, underestimated the threat, and simply failed to act. Neither is acceptable.\par \par \uc2 The FBI`s inaction in the weeks leading up to the January 6 is simply baffling.\par \par \uc2 REP. LOUIS GOHMERT (R-TX): Did the \uc2 FBI\uc2  have information about the violent threat that occurred on January 6, on January 5?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, FBI: Well, the answer to that is complicated, unfortunately. So, we have that -- we`ve already talked about it a little bit here this morning.\par \par \uc2 GOHMERT: It shouldn`t be complicated. You either had information or you didn`t. That was my question.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE COHEN (D-TN): -- the Capitol Police on Sunday who said yes, they had information that said it was going to be violent. You and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  did not make the case. You should have warned and he had a duty to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Urgency. That`s clearly what members of Congress thought was missing. Wray defended his agency`s work saying that while the intelligence the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had about potential violence was "raw," it was disseminated, he said, in multiple ways to the Capitol Police. And as far as the investigation into the insurrection, the FBI Director noted the hundreds of cases spawned from the siege. But on the question of whether the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating all the way to the top, here`s what he had to say about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. SHEILA JACKSON LEE (D-TX): Had any of these words been reviewed to determine whether or not President Trump`s words and deeds should be referred to the Department of Justice as contributing to the violence of the insurrectionists on January 6?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: I`m not sure there`s a whole lot I can add on that subject. But if there`s something I can provide in follow up, I`m happy to --\par \par \uc2 LEE: I asked if you -- if you refer these actions or deeds of the president, you`re the investigatory agency, to the Department of Justice, Donald Trump`s actions, words, deeds on that day?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: I`m not aware of any investigation that specifically goes to that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`ve got a lot to dissect from Wray`s testimony as he`s being been grilled on everything from January 6 to the cyberattacks, border security, domestic violent extremism, and of course ransomware.\par \par \uc2 So, to get us started, I`m joined in Capitol Hill by our own national political reporter Sahil Kapur. Also with me is our own justice correspondent Pete Williams, Andrew Weissmann, former FBI General Counsel, former senior member of the Mueller probe, and now an NBC News Contributor, and Suzanne Spaulding, a Senior Advisor for Homeland Security at the Center for Strategic and International Studies, and a former Undersecretary for Cyber and Infrastructure Protection at the Department of Homeland Security. That`s a lot of titles there that we got everybody.\par \par \uc2 But I want to start with Pete Williams. This is your beat. \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , you know sort of how he works. They knew this was going to be one of those, I guess, you could say a very thorough committee hearing, a lot on the agenda. What have you learned the most, Pete, from Christopher Wray`s testimony?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT, MSNBC: Well, I learned that there -- that he overtly said they`re not investigating the president for any role in the riot. That`s the guidance we had sort of gotten from the Justice Department. No one`s ever said it publicly, though. And so, it wasn`t surprising, but it was something we learned. He says that they`re close to 500 cases. That squares with our own count of the various charges. And he says they`re going to be many more\par \par \uc2 And on this specific question of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is warning. I`m not sure that this committee made a lot of progress in getting the answer. I mean, the fact is, there was this single piece that the Norfolk FBI office passed along about an anonymous posting on a Web site that suggested violent attacks on the Capitol. And that`s what Wray said was passed along.\par \par \uc2 Now, there were a couple of things about that. One is, you heard Ted Deutch there briefly, saying, well, you know, what did you do to follow up on it? And he said, well, we pass it along. And Deutch said, well, I`m not sure that`s enough. Now, I think the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has previously said that they did try to figure out where that posting came from, and we`re not able to verify the source of it.\par \par \uc2 The other thing he said is that of the -- of the thousands of counterterrorism investigations that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is doing, domestic terrorism investigations, virtually none of those people that were under investigation were among those who came to the Capitol on January 6. And one other thing I would say, Chuck. This didn`t come up in the hearing today. But if you look at all these court documents, this 500-plus -- 500 or so court documents that have been filed that in which agents can now look at things that weren`t available before the sixth, actual communications among the people that were coming.\par \par \uc2 There`s lots of talk about coming to Washington to raise hell, lots of talk about beating up Antifa in the street, precious little about actually entering the Capitol. And I think it remains a big question in this investigation of exactly whose idea was it to enter the Capitol? Was it something that just sort of organically happened on the sixth or was there somebody that had that plan that communicated it? And I think we still don`t know the answer to that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s a fair point. Hey, Sahil, I want to play one of Christopher Wray`s responses on the intelligence that they found on January 5, because it was a sort of bipartisan set of questions that went after him on this. Let me let him say it in his own words. Here he is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WRAY: The Norfolk report that you referenced, was a specific piece of raw, unverified intelligence that emerged on January 5, the day before, from a source online unvetted. And despite the raw nature of it, it was quickly passed not one, not two, but three different ways to the Capitol Police. One, an email to their representatives on our Joint Terrorism Task Force, two, in a verbal briefing in our command posts that included members of the Capitol Police, MPD, etcetera, and third, in our law enforcement portal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Sahil, I mean, he clearly knew that there was going to be a lot of questions about this. It came after that report from the Senate Rules Committee, one of the few bipartisan reports on the intelligence failings at the beginning. But I guess the question is, there seems to be a lot of - - is the Capitol Police supposed to be an intelligence agency with the capabilities of an \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Yes, Chuck, one of the though-lines of this hearing and the recent Senate report was how much information both the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Capitol Police had. Jerry Nadler, the Chairman of the Judiciary Committee said it was baffling that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  didn`t do more given that a lot of this was planned right out in the open, including on social media and on pro-Trump forums. There are people who talked about bringing guns, there are maps of the Capitol floating around. There were those who talked about storming it.\par \par \uc2 And one of the -- one of the questions here was did the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Capitol Police have a sense that this was possible but simply not believed that right-wing extremists in this country were capable of storming the Capitol. That`s one of the major forces behind that new kind of Democratic focus on investigating domestic extremism.\par \par \uc2 And Chuck, we also saw some semblance of the politics around the issue at the hearing when you saw Republicans like Tom McClintock, for instance, tried to change the topic to potential criminals crossing the southern border, Louie Gohmert tried to change the topics of Black Lives Matter. We see how this issue has become polarized in the way the two parties talk about it. And it`s a remarkable thing given that they were all together here in this building while it was being stormed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I want to play -- you brought up the McClintock because later, folks, he is --he is totally misinterpreting the legal jeopardy and the criminal potential jeopardy that Rudy Giuliani is in. So, I want to preface that before you hear his remarks. But wait till you hear this question from Tom McClintock. Let me play it.\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM MCCLINTOCK (R-CA): How many persons with criminal records or criminal warrants have been encountered this year crossing our southern border? Again, I don`t -- I don`t have these specific figures, but I know that our field offices down there both -- of all of which I visited, work very closely with this issue. And I agree with you that it`s a significant security concern.\par \par \uc2 MCCLINTOCK: Well, would you think it`s a more dangerous threat to our nation`s security than say, whether Rudy Giuliani filed the right paperwork for his lobbying firm?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: I really can`t discuss any specific of the individual`s investigation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m guessing that little chuckles, Sahil, by \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  didn`t -- probably didn`t play well with some Democrats.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: That`s correct, Chuck. I think that`s one of the frustrations here on the Democratic side is that they want to keep the focus on this. And a lot of the Republican colleagues kind of want to steer the focus off of this. There has been an effort on Capitol Hill, at least on the Republican side, to not use the word insurrection as much even as figures like Mitch McConnell, the Senate Republican leader in the early days following the attack on the Capitol did absolutely use that word. The needle has moved quite significantly on that one side of it.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing, Chuck, I`ll point out is that one thing \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  said in not confirming or denying the invest -- the existence of an investigation into the former President`s rhetoric is something that Democrats will say, undercuts the Republican argument against an independent commission. The fact that they believe that needs to be investigated, the former President`s actions, and potentially those people around him need to be looked at.\par \par \uc2 The inability to confirm an existing investigation into that undercuts the idea that a commission would be duplicative.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, let me bring in -- thank you, Sahil. Thank you, Pete. Let me bring in Andrew Weissmann and Suzanne Spaulding. And, frankly, I`m going to ask you to sort of both do two different things here. First to you, Andrew Weissmann, let me build on what Sahil was just talking about that. We`ve heard from Mitch McConnell, to Liz Cheney, to plenty of other Republican senators who came out against the commission saying, hey, what the President did and his action should be fully investigated.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know how you interpreted Christopher Wray`s remarks. I it sounded to me as if the President is not yet under investigation.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Chris Wray`s remarks were not a model of clarity. When he was actually asked whether there was an \uc2 FBI\uc2  referral to the Department on that issue, and that is not how things typically go until an investigation sort of pretty long, far along to ask for a prosecutor. So, I`m not sure he crystallized that.\par \par \uc2 But I think that his overall testimony left so much substance out that you don`t know whether this was an intelligence failure or this was a law enforcement failure and not acting on the intelligence or both. And just focusing on the Norfolk report and passing that on doesn`t answer any of the questions that the American public has a right to understand and know.\par \par \uc2 And I actually found this hearing so far to be quite dispiriting because the FBI Director works for the public and needs to be a lot more forthcoming about the intelligence and what they knew and what they did with that intelligence.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Andrew, it does seem to follow a pattern where it does seem as if only until recently, and I think Christopher Wray`s stance on domestic extremism has shifted with the threat. But it does seem as if law enforcement has not taken this threat from right-wing domestic extremism as seriously really until now, until post-January 6. And that seems to come through in this testimony.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Well, there was something quite telling in that which is \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  seem to not understand the actual statutes involved, because he said, we don`t look at the motive, we just look at the violence. But the statute actually is about motive. It is -- there are special penalties if you are motivated by white supremacy, if you`re doing something based on race. Those -- that is actually part of what you`re supposed to look at.\par \par \uc2 And so, you really did not get the sense that he understood and was taking what happened here seriously when it`s such a dramatic failure, not just by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , to be fair, but this really is something where we`re owed a much more thorough debriefing than what we`re getting so far.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, to me, it only screams why we need a more thorough commission. Suzanne Spaulding, let me move and shift gears to the other issue that he was questioned on quite a bit, and that is on the cyber -- on the ransomware. Let me play his response to the issue of whether or not companies should pay ransoms. Here`s what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WRAY: It is our policy, it is our guidance from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  that companies should not pay the ransom for a number of reasons. And the most important thing is whether the company pays or not, what we really need is to make sure that the companies or other organizations who are victimized, reach out and coordinate with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and with our partners as quickly and promptly as possible. And it`s when they do that, enables us to take all sorts of creative action that we can`t always do, but that certain cases we can and speed matters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, Suzanne, I think he got -- the question he might have been tougher had they not had that success earlier this week clawing back some of the ransom, which shows that maybe we`re making progress on cyber terrorism. But you know, how do you think he handled this? And do you feel like we got everything we should be knowing?\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE SPAULDING, SENIOR ADVISOR FOR HOMELAND SECURITY, CENTER FOR STRATEGIC AND INTERNATIONAL STUDIES: Well, I don`t think we ever get everything that should be known when it comes to cybersecurity. You know, the director in a different context, and I think he was questioned about it at today`s hearing, made a reference to 9/11 in the context of some comparison with the ransomware.\par \par \uc2 He clarified today that he wasn`t obviously talking about the scale and scope of the loss of life on that day, but rather about the way in which it mobilized government and society to recognize the need to respond, to come together to respond on an urgent basis. And one of the other things that 9/11 did was to change the culture and the way we think about classification, from -- moving from does this person have a need to know this information to a presumption that we need to share it? And that needs to happen in cyber. There`s too much secrecy associated to the government`s activities in cyber.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. You know, he was stressing begging companies to contact with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  after this has happened. What -- at this point, what can we do before? I mean, what should companies be contacting whom to sort of see how well protected they are? That seems to be something that`s missing here.\par \par \uc2 SPAULDING: Companies definitely need to particularly those that are involved in critical functions and enabling critical functions for our nation. They really showed us the government has recently asked them to do, have a senior official who is the point of contact for working with the government starting now, not when you have an attack.\par \par \uc2 You know, but the other thing that we need to recognize is that if you, even after you`ve been victimized, if you share that information with the government, they can use that information to protect future victims. And we have seen the bureau increasingly have success with this with taking down other hackers for hire, criminal network site, Emotet, which brought about international coordination to take down a massive criminal network that would have had many, many more victims.\par \par \uc2 They have the information up and shared with the government. The government shared that with its international partners, and come together to take that down. They`ve also used their ability to track this cryptocurrency to identify individuals and arrest those individuals. Those are the things that need to happen to prevent this activity to deter it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Suzanne Spaulding, Andrew Weissmann, I appreciate both of you, Andrew, before I let you go though, I just wanted to get your reaction to the McGahn testimony. I assume the fact that he affirmed everything that was in your report only affirms your investigative work, but I`m just curious what your take was.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Well, our team`s investigative work, absolutely. And it belies the attack that this was a witch hunt and that these were just a bunch of Democrats who were the skewed report because you had Don McGahn under oath saying that everything about him was accurate.\par \par \uc2 I do think that -- I thought that his reference to being concerned about lying to the press and going back on his testimony to the special counsel was interesting, because it shows that the so-called process crimes of charging people with making false statements to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had the deterrent effect that it was intended to have, which is like Don McGahn looked at that and said, I`m not going to lie.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and that`s the thing. Are there going to continue to be more Don McGahns that serve the next time there`s a President Trump or not. Anyway, Andrew Weissman, Suzanne Spaulding, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we are expecting President Biden to speak any minute now on the coronavirus pandemic and his administration`s plan to try to help vaccinate some of the poorest countries in this world.\par \par \uc2 We`ll bring those remarks to you live as soon as that happens. But first, we`ve got an update from Capitol Hill with the latest round of infrastructure talks are making some progress. A Republican senator says they`ve reached a general agreement on a package. Senator Jon Tester as part of the new round of talks. He`ll join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We may have a little breaking news. A bipartisan group of senators have now taken the baton in the search for an infrastructure deal. And according to my colleague, Garrett Haake on Capitol Hill, Republican Susan Collins and Senator Mitt Romney are telling folks that the group has reached something of what they`re calling a general agreement on the parameters of the package.\par \par \uc2 Well, I have a member of this bipartisan group with me right now. It`s Senator Jon Tester. Senator Tester, well, what -- we`ve heard from Collins, we`ve heard from Romney to our reporters, what can you tell us? How are you as close as they are in saying you are?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JON TESTER (D-MT): Look, Chuck, I think we`re very, very close. And I think general agreement is the right way to put it. I think there are still some outliers out there that have been agreed upon totally. But we`re very, very close. And I`m hopeful that we can get something nailed down hopefully by the end of the day, and certainly by the first part of next week.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, you had confirmed earlier that you had took taxes, some form of taxes off the table. Can you give us a hint as to what ideas are in it that is going to pay for this thing or are we looking at deficit spending?\par \par \uc2 TESTER: Well, I guess -- I do not want to deficit spending, so I want to have it paid for. I think all 10 members feel the same way I do. I will tell you that part of what I`m concerned about is that maybe what we`re projecting on the numbers on the pay fors because they`re not taxes, they`re fees, they`re funds, they`re different facets of money out there or pots of money out there that we can draw out of.\par \par \uc2 And Chuck, I would love to tell you each and every one of them, but I don`t know, it`s kind of Washington stuff quite frankly pay for it. But in the end, I don`t want this to be smoking mirrors. I want this to be real dollars, because this is going to be a real spending on infrastructure for this country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And we look at new money. I mean, you know, one of the big disagreements between Biden`s ask and what the Republican leadership had responded with, I mean, you know, $800 billion wasn`t $800 billion, it was more like maybe two to $300 million in new spending to $1 billion in new spending. And I know we sit here talk about these numbers as if they`re their pennies and dimes.\par \par \uc2 Are we looking at something that`s a real trillion dollars or pretty close?\par \par \uc2 TESTER: It depends on how you want to measure it, but is real money above the baseline. And it depends on how long -- how many dollars of baseline money you want to put into it for how many years? But you`re in the ballpark. I mean, I think that we`re looking at a very significant infrastructure package over five or eight years, however you want to pencil it out. Of course, the dollars will change whether it`s five or eight.\par \par \uc2 But in the end, I would just say this, Chuck, really quick. And you know this. You know this very, very well. China is a basic threat to this country. They have spent a lot of money on infrastructure over the last 20 years. We have not. We put (INAUDIBLE), we haven`t. If we`re going to -- if we`re going to compete with China, we need a significant infrastructure bill passed, maybe more than one.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s some Democrat -- Senate Democrats, not in this working group with you that are starting to get concerned that climate spending is not going to be a part of this deal, that it is getting short shrift here. How would you respond to that criticism?\par \par \uc2 TESTER: Well, I would say, I`ll let them make their assessment after the agreement is put out. But the beauty will be in the eyes of the beholder. And I can tell you there are things in this package that I would like to see different. And I`m sure there are things in this package that Sheldon Whitehouse, for example, probably the greatest fighter for climate change, would say, you know what, I`d like to see different.\par \par \uc2 In the end, it`s a compromise package if we come out with it, which I think we will. And then, folks will have to make up their own mind whether it`s the right thing to do or not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting, there seems to be a debate inside the Democratic Party, which says, hey, you know, why talk with the other side, it`s only going to get a watered-down version, you can`t get it -- you can`t get the big stuff that they want to do. But do you have the votes for that? I mean, I guess that`s the question, right? I mean, you`re only as strong as your 50th Senator, right?\par \par \uc2 TESTER: That`s exactly right, Chuck. And that is exactly right. You`ve got to have the votes, even for reconciliation. Even if you do away with the filibuster, you still have to have the votes to get these pieces of legislation across the line. I have always said, and I do believe this, that bipartisan legislation stands the test of time better.\par \par \uc2 But I also tell you that I didn`t come here to do nothing. We have things to do. Infrastructure is one of those things. I know your last segment was on the commission, in part. And I think the commission is important too. So, we need to get some things done around here for the American people. The people that are saying, you know, why negotiate, they make a point. But in the end, I think you have to negotiate in good faith and try to get to a point where you can get both sides together. If you can`t, then we`ll have to do something to it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you feel good about these 10 Republicans that they`re going to keep their word, they`re going to vote, that if disagreement is what comes to, that they`re going to be there through thick and thin because the pressure they`re going to be under? You know, this is such a polarizing environment, that we know who those 10 republicans are, and some of them know how to withstand pressure. I don`t -- some we`ll find out. But do you feel pretty good about this?\par \par \uc2 TESTER: Chuck, I think we need more than 10 Republicans. I think the Republicans will tell you that we need more than 10 Republicans. And I think that the group that I`m working with, and it`s only five Republicans, are solid people, and I think they`ll be with us in the end. It`s up to those guys to get the rest. It`s up to folks like me to try and keep the Democratic caucus together. It`s going to be a tough challenge on both sides of the aisle because this is not a perfect legislation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. Senator Jon Tester, I got to move to President Biden. I hope room rater is kinder to you on this one, because to me, this is who you are, plainspoken, no fanciness. So, thank you for coming on, sir.\par \par \uc2 TESTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I know there are a lot of people feeling his absence today. In addition, I`d like to point out that the greet from the \uc2 British government\uc2  has been exemplary. We`ve had a good first full day here in the U.K. Prime Minister Johnson and I had a very productive meeting.\par \par \uc2 We discharged and discussed a broad range of issues on which the United Kingdom and the United States are working in very close cooperation. We affirmed the special relationship, this is not said lightly, the special relationship between our people, and renewed our commitment to defending the enduring democratic values that both our nations share, and the strong -- excuse me -- the strong foundation of our partnership.\par \par \uc2 80 years ago, Prime Minister Winston Churchill and President Franklin Roosevelt signed an agreement known as the Atlantic Charter. It was a statement of first principles, a promise that the United Kingdom and the United States would meet the challenges of their age and they would meet it together. Today, we build on that commitment with a revitalized Atlantic Charter updated to reaffirm that promise while speaking directly to the key challenges of this century. Cyber security, emerging technologies, global health and climate change.\par \par \uc2 We discussed our common goals for driving ambitious global action to address the climate crisis. And the climate leaders` summit that I hosted in April was in part about helping drive forward the momentum toward the critical COP 26 that the U.K. will host in Glasgow later this year. We talked about the shared sacrifices our service members have made, bravely serving side by side in Afghanistan for close to 20 years.\par \par \uc2 The U.K. was with us from the start, as they always are, equally committed to rooting out the terrorist threat. And now we`re coordinating our withdrawal together. And of course we talked about how our two nations can, together, lead the global fight against COVID-19. That`s been a major focus of the G7 under British leadership, particularly and focusing and coordinating our resources that helped vaccinate the world.\par \par \uc2 And tonight I`m making an historic announcement regarding America`s leadership in the fight against COVID-19. America knows firsthand the tragedies of this pandemic where we`ve had more people die in the United States than anywhere in the world. Nearly 600,000 of our fellow Americans. Moms, dads, sons, daughters, brothers, sisters, grandparents. More deaths from COVID-19 in the United States than from World War I, World War II, the Vietnam War and 9/11 combined. Combined.\par \par \uc2 We know the tragedy. We also know the path to recovery. The United States has now vaccinated 64 percent of our adults with at least one shot. Just 4 1/2 months ago, we were at only 5 percent with one shot. It took a herculean effort on the part of our government to manage one of the biggest and I would say most complicated logistical challenges in our history. Took the ingenuity of sciences, building on decades of research to develop a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 It took the full capacity of American companies, manufacturing and delivering the vaccines around the clock. And as a result, we have the lowest number of daily deaths since the first day of this pandemic. Our economy is rebounding. Our vaccination program has already saved tens of thousands of lives. With that count growing each day has allowed millions, millions of Americans to get back to living their lives.\par \par \uc2 And from the beginning of my presidency, we`ve been clear-eyed that we need to attack this virus globally as well. This is about our responsibility, our humanitarian obligation to save as many lives as we can. And our responsibility to our values. We value dignity of all people. In times of trouble, Americans reach out to offer help and offer a helping hand. That`s who we are. And when we see people hurting and suffering anywhere around the world, we seek to help as best we can.\par \par \uc2 That`s why under both Republican and Democratic presidents, the United States has made transformative commitments to bolster global health. Commitments under President Bush like PEPFAR which changed the global fight against HIV-AIDS. And in this moment our values are calling us to do everything we can to vaccinate the world against COVID-19. It`s also in America`s self-interest.\par \par \uc2 As long as the virus rages elsewhere, there`s a risk of new mutations that could threaten our people. We know that raging COVID-19 in other countries holds back global growth. Raises instability and weakens governments. And as we`ve seen in the United States, with evidence clear day by day, the key to reopening and growing economies is to vaccinate your people. Our vaccination program has helped the American economy begin to recover from the worst economic crisis in a century.\par \par \uc2 Over two million new jobs created just in the last four months since I`ve become president. A historic decline in long-term unemployment. Businesses reopening and a projected economic growth of -- excuse me, 6.9 percent. The fastest in nearly four decades in America. Just as the American economy is recovering, it is in all of our interest to have the global economy begin to recover as well. And that won`t happen unless we can get this pandemic under control worldwide.\par \par \uc2 That`s why, as I said in my address to the Joint Session of Congress in April, America will be the arsenal of vaccines in our fight against COVID- 19 just as America was the arsenal of democracy during World War II. Over the past four months, we`ve taken a number of steps toward this historic effort. We have contributed more than any nation to COVAX, a global collective effort that is delivering COVID-19 vaccines across the world.\par \par \uc2 We`ve supported manufacturing efforts abroad through our partnerships with Japan, India and Australia known as the Quad. We`ve shared doses with our neighbors, Canada and Mexico, and in addition, three weeks ago with America`s vaccine supply secured and with confidence we have enough vaccines to cover every American who wants one, we announced that we would donate 80 million doses of our own vaccine in house now to supply the world by the end of June.\par \par \uc2 Many of these doses are shipping to countries around the world as we speak. And today we`re taking a major step that will super charge the global fight against this pandemic. In my direction, the United States will purchase an additional half billion doses from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 . \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine. We`ll donate nearly 100 low and lower middle-income countries. They will be the beneficiaries.\par \par \uc2 Let me say it again. The United States will purchase a half a billion doses of Pfizer`s COVID-19 vaccine to donate to nearly 100 nations that are in dire need in the fight against this pandemic. That`s a historic step. The largest single purchase and donation of COVID-19 vaccines by any single country ever. Importantly, this is the MRNA vaccine which has proven to be extremely effective against COVID-19 and every known variant of that virus thus far.\par \par \uc2 These half a billion vaccines will start to be shipped in August as quickly as they roll off the manufacturing line. 200 million of these doses will be delivered this year, in 2021, and 300 million more will be delivered in the first half of 2022.\par \par \uc2 Let me be clear, just as with the 80 million doses we previously announced, the United States is providing these half billion doses with no strings attached. Let me say it again. With no strings attached. Our vaccine donations don`t include pressure for favors or potential concessions. We`re doing this to save lives, to end this pandemic. That`s it. Period.\par \par \uc2 I also want to thank \uc2 Albert Bourla\uc2  of Pfizer CEO and chairman for joining me today. We`ve gotten to know each over the last few months. He and I, and his entire team have really -- he`s really stepped up in this critical stage in our fight against the pandemic. And the plan is for half a billion doses that we`ll be sending around the world to be produced in the United States, including in Pfizer`s manufacturing plant in Kalamazoo, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Eighty years ago, not too far from that plant in Kalamazoo, in the Detroit area, American workers built tanks and planes and vehicles that helped defeat the global threat of fascism in World War II. They built what became known as the arsenal of democracy. Now a new generation of American men and women, working with today`s latest technology is going to build a new arsenal to defeat the current enemy of world peace, health instability, COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 Albert was gracious enough to welcome me to the Kalamazoo plant back in February. It`s incredible. The ingenuity, the care, the safety that goes into every single dose as I toured the entire plant. Most of all, when you`re there, you feel the pride of every worker there feels, how the pride they feel in what they`re doing. I have been to a lot of plants. I`ve worked, I`m a big union guy. I`ve been doing it my whole career.\par \par \uc2 But you can see the looks on their faces. They were proud. I mean this sincerely. They were proud of what they were doing. They knew what they were doing. American workers will now produce vaccines to save lives of people in Africa, Asia, Latin America and the Caribbean. People they will never meet and have never met. Places they`ve never visited and probably won`t have an opportunity to. But lives saved all the same, thanks to American leadership, American workers. Hard work and values.\par \par \uc2 Let me close with this. This is a monumental commitment by the American people. As I said, we`re a nation full of people who step up in times of need to help our fellow human beings, both at home and abroad. We`re not perfect, but we step up. We`re not alone in this endeavor. That`s the point I want to make. We`re going to help lead the world out of this pandemic, working alongside our global partners.\par \par \uc2 Under the U.K. chairmanship of the G7, democracies of the world are posed to deliver as well. This U.S. contribution is the foundation for additional coordinated efforts to help vaccinate the world. The \uc2 British government\uc2 , the prime minister, has led a strong campaign to get people vaccinated across the U.K. And I`m grateful they`re making their own generous donation. Tomorrow, the G7 nations will be announcing the full scope of our commitment, our meeting, the G7.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank all of my G7 partners for stepping up to recognize our responsibility to meet the moment. I`m looking forward to working with my counterparts on these efforts in the coming days and much more. One final point I want to make clear, this is not the end of our efforts to fight COVID-19 and vaccinate the world. We have to turn manufacturing doses into shots and arms to protect people and communities.\par \par \uc2 That`s why the United States has already provided hundreds of millions in funding to support last-minute vaccination efforts including new funding from Congress as part of the American Rescue Plan, and working with programs in Latin America, Asia and Africa. We`re going to keep manufacturing doses, donating doses, getting jabs, as they say here in the U.K., in arms, until the world has beaten this virus.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank you all. Now I`d like to turn it over to my friend, the CEO and chairman of Pfizer, \uc2 Albert Bourla\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Albert, it`s all yours. And again, personally, thank you for stepping up.\par \par \uc2 AL BOURLA, PFIZER CEO: Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thank you,\par \par \uc2 BOURLA: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Mr. President. And it is, of course, a great honor to be with you today for this historic announcement. As the G7 countries come together for this critical summit, the eyes of the world are on the leaders of this powerful nations to help solve the ongoing COVID-19 crisis. While great progress has been made in many developed nations, the world is now asking the G7 leaders to shoulder the responsibility to help vaccinate people in all countries.\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, I know from our conversations that we agree that every man, woman and child on the planet regardless of financial condition, race, religion or geography deserve access to life-saving COVID-19 vaccines, and once again, the United States has answered the call and we are grateful to you and your administration for your leadership in this front.\par \par \uc2 Today we are providing 500 million doses of the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 -BioNTech vaccine to the world`s poorest nations. These will significantly enhance our ability to meet our goal of providing two billion doses of the vaccine to low and middle-income countries over the next 18 months. Thanks to the ingenuity of so many scientists and the dedication of so many manufacturing workers today we can see clearly the light at the end of the tunnel, but we still have work to do.\par \par \uc2 And I can assure you, Mr. President, that we will be relentless in pursuing more solutions to end the pandemic. Just this week, we began those in participants aged 5 to 11 years old in a global phase two, three study. And as we speak, we continue our studies in pregnant woman. We are also closely monitoring and addressing the emerging variants. We are testing our vaccine`s response to newly arising variants and we are coordinating with public health authorities around the world on surveillance efforts.\par \par \uc2 So far, data saw that none of the existing variant strains has escaped the protection provided by our vaccine. I repeat, none, not one. Still, we have built a process to develop within 100 days a new vaccine if needed, God forbid. Our scientists are also pursuing an oral treatment against COVID- 19. Initial indications are promising and if things go well we could apply for approval before the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 But I wanted to finish by coming back to the importance of your announcement today, Mr. President. In a pandemic, everyone is only as protected as their neighbors. Their neighbors down the street as well as their global neighbors around the world. Today`s announcement with the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  gets us closer to our goal and significantly enhances our ability to save even more lives across the globe.\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, I want to thank you for your leadership, vision and partnership. We look forward to continuing to work with your administration to ensure that science wins the battle against COVID-19. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Mr. President, do you believe --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Mr. President --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Mr. President, do you believe your meeting with Putin can change his behavior in a way sanctions have not yet?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Mr. President, did you warn Prime Minister Johnson that a trade deal (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I was giving him a little bit of a chance there. As we know, the president does seem to get lured into answering a shouted question every now and then.\par \par \uc2 But let me bring in NBC News chief foreign affairs correspondent who is in Cornwall following the president, Andrea Mitchell. Also with me is president of \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2  Richard Haass and an NBC News medical contributor and physician, \uc2 Dr. Kavita Patel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Andrea, I want to start with you because what`s interesting here is this has been -- the demand for the United States to do more globally has been there for about 60 days I`d argue. And they have been very reticent. I mean I had Tony Blinken on this show early on in March, and he`s like, you know, they clearly were concerned about not having enough vaccine here. There`s still -- you could tell there still was some concern that, you know, we were a little bit late with this big move, which, of course, obviously better late than never.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Absolutely. And as you point out, they have been cautious. They have been concerned. They`ve been under criticism that they have not done more. They did the 80 million doses. But this is big. And they`re doing it before the G7, on the eve of the G7 which is going to do more and do it bigger collectively.\par \par \uc2 And for this president to come here to the G7 in Cornwall and say that we are the leader of the world, they did need to do more. There`s even criticism today, I was talking to Dr. Vin Gupta, who you know so well, and he was saying that the problem is, from his perspective, that we need to do a billion doses not 500 million.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And that the world needs to do more, and that COVAX, the agency that is going to distribute this, to which the U.S. is contributing it, distributes so that every country or the countries that are needy get 20 percent of their people vaccinated rather than to the countries where COVID is surging. So there`s going to be some argument about that. The administration says that this will be well done. As you heard the president say no politics involved, no favor.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: That is a shot to China.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Some said Russia, that is involved in vaccine diplomacy. So that`s what he is talking about here today. And doing it as the summit is about to convene tomorrow. This is obviously a big calling card.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No doubt. You know, \uc2 Richard Haass\uc2 , I want to pick up on something Andrea just brought up and it was the no strings attach shot that was a clear shot. We know that both China and Russia, China trying to do -- I think strong arm some countries on their Taiwan policy even in exchange for a vaccine that isn`t actually that good.\par \par \uc2 This is an important moment for the United States and for Biden. I mean, COVID is an opportunity if we are able to vaccinate the world.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAASS\uc2 , COUNCIL ON FOREIGN RELATIONS PRESIDENT: Exactly right, Chuck. Look, what he`s done with COVID domestically in many ways is the great accomplishment of the first few months of his presidency. Both the public health science and it`s allowed the economy to get going. This is an important international step. It`s a great demonstration of what you might call soft power, and in a sense, the less we try to get credit for it, the more credit we actually get.\par \par \uc2 This is on a mass scale like coming in after a hurricane or after an earthquake. It really is a -- it`s a good thing. That said, Andrea also raised the point, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is a two-dose vaccine. There`s eight billion people in the world. So 500 million doses is sizeable. It covers 200 million people. It just gives you a sense of the enormity of the task. And we still need a lot of other nations to step up and we still need greater production if we`re going to break the back of this around the world. But yes, it`s a good day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Richard, I want to bring up, the G7 countries are all not well- vaccinated. Like this is an awkward moment here, isn`t it, in this meeting where you have the U.K. and the United States sort of the outliers, and everybody else still -- I mean, look, Canada is asking us for vaccine. We know Japan has had some issues. You know, this hasn`t been a great moment for the G7 countries collectively when it comes to vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 HAASS: No, it hasn`t. The E.U. clearly dropped the ball and then further hurt their standing with European publics. And what the Asian countries learned the hard way, they thought they had broken the back of COVID. What they found out is all the social distancing, the masking, the quarantining, the contact tracing only took you so far. And then because of travel and the like, still the virus got in and they had real effect.\par \par \uc2 The vaccines are essential. That is the lesson of the last couple of weeks. And yes, you know, another subject that you talk about so much, we could say immigration. If we are going to allow other countries to get their economies going, they have to be vaccinated, otherwise people are going to head toward the United States.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re coming here.\par \par \uc2 HAASS: Because that`s where the jobs are. So the scale of this that still remains is wide.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. Patel, the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine is not the most efficient vaccine, right, the two-dose? I have to ask, I mean, in a perfect world, had the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine not had such a, you know, had so many bad PR moments, I mean, wouldn`t that have been the better global vaccine? I mean I think that was what the world was counting on. You know, we sort of have now forgotten about it, but this two-dose vaccine, it`s just -- it`s harder to get circulated around the world, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 DR. KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , NBC NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, absolutely, Chuck. The good news is that even one of the two shots confers an incredible amount of effectiveness. And I`ll just say, I don`t think this is the last we`re going to see from the Biden administration kind of doubling down on being part of a global solution. COVAX, which is going to help kind of lead a lot of these efforts with the G7 countries, Chuck, they`ve only given out 100 million doses to date.\par \par \uc2 So 500 million takes them from, you know, pauper to riches. And that involves a lot of logistics. So you`re right, a one-shot would have been preferable. But, you know, countries are going to have to wrestle with allocation because, as Richard and Andrea alluded to, you`re not going to vaccinate the whole world with even 500 million doses if you`re splitting that in half. But I bet Pfizer`s manufacturing capacity -- this speaks, by the way, to the confidence we have in Pfizer`s capacity which I`m frankly stunned by and pleased to see and hope other countries can benefit from it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. But what does this say about AstraZeneca`s vaccine and \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 . I mean, in some ways have we just decided, sorry, this is just -- we know this is -- is the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine just known as a -- \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna are just the better vaccines and there was no way you were going to sell the rest of the world on the other two vaccines?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: No, I do think that, you know, while the attention today is on \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , and again, stunning kind of manufacturing ability, the other manufacturers and even the ones we don`t talk about, \uc2 Novavax\uc2  and others, they do play a very important role, \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 , \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  and all the others. To your point, though, Chuck, the MRNA vaccines are holding up against the variants. They`re providing that almost unbelievable effectiveness.\par \par \uc2 So the other vaccines do the same but not to the same degree. Having said that, Chuck, the faster -- you know, it`s what we talked about when we didn`t have enough supply.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Whatever shot you can get, you get. That holds for the world. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  will just be a part of that solution, but a big one.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andrea, let`s wrap things up with the rest of the -- with the start of the G7. Again, I want to go back to something I brought up to Richard, which is the awkward nature of the fact that some G7 countries are going to be asking Biden and Johnson for some vaccine.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Exactly. And, you know, COVID is a big issue here. They`ve been in virtual lockdown again with recent resurgence and with the concern about the Indian variant. So that`s one reason why this is so hard to cover. This is not like a G7 you`ve ever covered. We haven`t been here within two years because of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, you know, pool movements, movements by White House reporters, very, very limited access to the principals. There`s always a concern of an outbreak. And the E.U. has not done very well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And the U.K. has done better but has still had these upswings.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Andrea, I got 30 seconds. How is the special relationship? Is there going to be one between Boris and Biden?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I think so, yes. It`s in both of their interests except over Northern Ireland. That is a big issue. The Brexit negotiations on the Northern Ireland.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: That`s not going so well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look, you`re not missing anything here other than horrible D.C. humidity and a lot of cicadas, so I`m jealous of you being in Cornwall.\par \par \uc2 Andrea Mitchell, \uc2 Dr. Kavita Patel\uc2  and \uc2 Richard Haass\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: We`ve got -- we got seagulls.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, it looks a lot -- it looks a lot cooler than it is right now. So thank you all.\par \par \uc2 And as we look ahead to the president`s meeting with Vladimir Putin amid the growing hack attacks from Russia, we have a quick across-the-network programming note.\par \par \uc2 From risk to regulation to ransomwares. CNBC`s "Power Lunch" is going to dedicate its entire show to the crypto craze, speaking to insiders about the state of the industry. That`s coming up at 2:00 p.m. Eastern today over at CNBC. And we know if you stream and DVR and all the stuff, you don`t miss anything. You get to see it all. So see it on your time. But don`t miss that crypto. We`re all trying to learn more.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210611eh6a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 28, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Jake Sherman \par \uc2 8,423 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to Friday, it is MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And I`m Chuck Todd. And we begin with the breaking news from Capitol Hill where Senate Republicans have just blocked the bipartisan agreement to establish a commission, and a political one to investigate the January 6thth siege on the Capitol. Needed 60 votes to overcome the filibuster. It got just 54 with just six Republicans breaking ranks with Mitch McConnell, and by extension Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 Eleven senators also missed the vote, entirely nine Republicans including two who voted to convict the president and two Democrats. In a few moments, we are expecting Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer to hold a press conference to discuss what has just happened on the floor and where Democrats go from here. Moments ago Senator Schumer reacted to the failed vote while speaking on the Senate floor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER (D-NY): This vote has made it official. Donald Trump`s big lie has now fully enveloped the Republican Party. Trump`s big lie is now the defining principle of was once the party of Lincoln. Republicans chose to defend the big lie because they believe anything that might upset Donald Trump could hurt them politically.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Notable we have not heard much reaction from Senate Republicans after the vote with the exception of one of their members who back the commission.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Cast the American people, are they more likely to trust an independent commission not composed of members of Congress? Are they more likely to trust one handpicked by Speaker Pelosi? Pretty clear who they would most trust? And I think this is as much as anything about building trust with the American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kind of give Senator Cassidy credit. He tried to -- he tried to make an argument that he thought fellow Republicans might be persuaded by. We got a lot happening right now in Washington. So let`s dive in. Turn to a couple of our Capitol Hill reporters. Joining me now is NBC News colleague Leigh Ann Caldwell, Jake Sherman, founder of Punchbowl News and an MSNBC contributor is here as well.\par \par \uc2 And I know we`re got a lot of moving parts here. Leigh Anne, let me start with this. On one hand, this was not unexpected. On the other hand, why not -- is this -- what is the next move by Senate Democrats? because what it does sound like is they`re not going to bring this up for a vote again. There`s a part of me wondering if your Senator Schumer, why not bring this up for a vote once a week if they`re not going to vote for it?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HOUSE REPORTER: Well, Chuck just right before we came on air, Senator Schumer sent a letter to his Democratic colleagues saying that he reserves the right to bring this commission up for a vote again, and so we could see it. So even though Republicans and Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell said that he does not want this commission to happen, he has publicly cited the politics of it.\par \par \uc2 And privately, I`m told by many sources that politics is really driving his decision here. The politics of this is not going away. Democrats do not plan to shelve this and never talk about it again. So we could see Schumer force Republicans to vote on it again. Also, in that same letter Chuck, he said that an investigation will happen. And so that perhaps is an open door to perhaps a select committee or whatever form that Democrats want to take to investigate what happened on January 6thth.\par \par \uc2 And the point that Senator Cassidy made that I`ve heard from some other Republicans as well, including Senator Lisa Murkowski is that the most bipartisan way to investigate is with this outside independent commission. And now there`s some concern among Republicans that they have just -- that the party has put this in the hand of Democrats to completely control the narrative, not only this year, but next year is the midterms approach, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to play what Mitch McConnell said yesterday because, Jake Sherman, what was astounding to me is, is that he didn`t hide the reason. He basically use politics as the rationale. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): If we set up this commission, I think the basic goal of our Democratic friends, is to keep re litigating in public what happened back on January 6thth, rather than getting to a quick solution through arrest of those who did it and security adjustments to make sure it never happens again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jake, did he potentially hurt himself there by committing Kevin McCarthy when McCarthy admitted the Benghazi committee was about hurting Hillary Clinton`s political standing? Because he said Democrats wanted to re litigate. We haven`t litigated yet. I don`t know what we`re relitigating.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SHERMAN, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: You know, Chuck, he`s been saying this since Wednesday. He`s been saying this on repeat this week that this would be a political loser for Republicans because Democrats want to keep relitigating January -- the attack on January 6thth. So, I don`t know if it`s definitely not a gaffe. He means to say this. I would add to that -- I mean, the irony of all of this is there`s going to be an investigation into January 6thth.\par \par \uc2 There`s no question about it, either. Nancy Pelosi is going to do it through her standing committees. Through one of the investigatory committees she already has stood up or they`re going to create a select committee. There`s no way out of this Congress, Chuck, without an investigation, but the difference is those investigations are not going to have any Republican involvement.\par \par \uc2 So Republicans have ceded their involvement, and they`re betting that they can make the case publicly that the investigations that will happen are just partisan. One party Nancy Pelosi, as your Bill Cassidy say also. Just Nancy Pelosi selecting these people to investigate. So, you know, and I agree with Leigh Ann. I do think that Schumer is going to bring this back up. I don`t think as you suggested, Chuck, he`s going to bring it back up every week.\par \par \uc2 That would be a time suck for him. But I do believe he is going to bring this back up again. Although I don`t anticipate there will be any difference.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that`s what I wondered, Leigh Ann. If -- OK. You look at the 11 missing senators, there`s two convectors. Pat Toomey has put out a statement saying had he been there, he would have voted for the commission. You -- two Democrats missing, you get it up to 58 votes. You`re still too short. Roy Blunt is on that list. He`s retiring. So, you know, is there -- is there an opening here if Republicans see just how poorly this plays with normal Americans?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Well, Senator Toomey me was not at the vote. But he put out a statement saying that he had to leave for a family reason. But if he were here, he would vote for the commission. So there`s one vote there. But as you point out, that`s still short of the 10 votes necessary. And it`s -- the -- they`re not going to get the 10 Republicans unless McConnell comes out and says it gives them the leeway to do that.\par \par \uc2 And McConnell has made his decision. Sources near him say he`s willing to sacrifice the short term pain of opposing this commission for perhaps what is the long-term political gain of it? I, you know, I just had a conversation with Senator Manchin before the vote took place, and he said that McConnell is making this extremely difficult. And I know Mitch -- Manchin has been in the Senate for a while, and he was governor before that.\par \par \uc2 So he`s not new to politics, but he seemed still mystified for some reason that what he said McConnell is just playing to the raw politics of the situation. So, unless McConnell sees a political reason to change his mind, what he`s seeing in the polls, what he`s seeing what will gain him back as majority next year. I don`t see the 10 votes in the senate coming happening.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, McConnell is the ultimate transactional politician. But boy, Jake Sherman, this is the ultimate of putting party over country, party over institution. I mean, it is -- I know he can -- I know his skin is as thick as anybody`s on there. People like to compare him to a tortoise. He`s got a hard shell. Nothing seems to penetrate him. But, you know, he`s a long way away from the Mitch McConnell that essentially gave his speech to convict Donald Trump without voting to convict.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Well, he, you know, I wouldn`t say that the institution and McConnell have probably both changed. I would -- I would concede that to you, Chuck. And I think that -- listen, I mean, we`re in an environment where everybody is trying to win the next majority. And I that`s the reality of it. I agree with Leigh Ann though. I don`t think the political calculation for McConnell will change at all. I think that this this ship has sailed and there`s not going to be a new -- in my estimation, based on my reporting, just talking to all these Republicans.\par \par \uc2 There was some misnomer I think that there were Republic -- a large group of Republicans in the last week or so that were open to voting for this. I just don`t think that was the case. I think everyone out there mind made up and I don`t think that McConnell had to do much there. I think people saw it as a partisan exercise and they weren`t going to be involved. And I`m not sure - I don`t I really don`t think that`s going to change.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, part one of this story has to do with getting to the bottom of January 6thth. If we can`t get to the bottom of January 6thth, does this become a story about the beginning of the end of the filibuster? With that, I want to turn to Colorado Democratic Senator Michael Bennett, who is on his way to the airport himself, but he at least waited to cast his vote on like 11 of his colleagues.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET (D-CO): So anyway, Chuck, I`m not by myself because this is a carpool. So john Hickenlooper is back there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Some -- there is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So we have a caucus. All right. We have a caucus of two.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: A caucus of two.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator Hickenlooper. Well, are there two votes to get rid of the filibuster in that carpool right now?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: I can`t speak for John. But I can say for myself that the country can endure another period of obstruction like the one that Mitch McConnell rod when Barack Obama was president of the United States. There is too much for us to do with respect to building an economy that works for everybody, not just people at the very top to make sure that this democracy can compete with the Chinese government`s -- all those policies goes on and on and on.\par \par \uc2 The need for us to get our act together. So, I don`t know what the reform of the filibuster, what form it will finally take and I don`t know, we need all 50. But I think we should not perpetuate McConnell`s bastardization of the Senate filibuster.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me ask you this with the January 6thth commission vote. As a citizen, I`d like to have heard the 35 Republicans make their argument as to why they were voting against this rather than just cast their vote and run to the airport. So how could you reform the filibuster, to at least force them to have to publicly defend what they`re doing?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Look, we forced them to stay today and get some combination of forces made (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Must be headed to Dallas. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, Senator Bennett, I think -- I think our connection here is not going very well. So look, I appreciate you making the -- making the effort there. And I`m going to turn to the press conference for Chuck Schumer right now. Let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Democracy. And unless people in his own party stand up to him, it`s bad for the party. It`s bad for those individuals. But most of all, it was bad for the country. So I hope that will change. OK. Now, just reviewing our last work period, we`ve had a really productive May session. Our committees have advanced critical issues and the President`s bill back better agenda. We confirm many of the President`s nominees.\par \par \uc2 And we came together on a bipartisan basis to advance historic and wide reaching piece of legislation, the U.S. Innovation and Competition Act. The most significant investment in science technology and American manufacturing in an entire generation. This is one of the most significant pieces of legislation that`s come before the Senate in a very long time. And it`s going to have huge effects down the road.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t get the attention it should. I`ve said for three reasons. Number one, because it`s bipartisan. So there`s no fighting, you know, I know you guys like fighting. Number two, because it is going to take effect not immediately, although the chips part will. But in general, it won`t take effect, but it`ll have dramatic effects four, five, 10 years from now. And I forgot the third reason. I had three reasons. I said him in my speech, you remember, what is it? What?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Positive.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Oh, yes, it`s positive. Not negative. OK. It`s a positive piece of legislation. OK. Anyway, we`re very proud of it. All right. Exercising our authority over the CRA, the Congressional Review Act, we reversed several egregious Trump era rules that hurt our environment and gave big corporations more power over American workers circumvented critical protections for consumers.\par \par \uc2 We also wit witnessed President Biden sign the COVID-19 Hate Crimes Act. It`s a piece of legislation that originated in the Senate. And it`s critical to combating the wave of attacks against Asian-Americans. So those were all good things done on a bipartisan basis. At the same time, we have seen the limits of bipartisanship and the resurgence of Republican obstructionism.\par \par \uc2 Despite three months of regular orders on (INAUDIBLE) the input of no fewer than six committees, dozens of Republican amendments accepted to the bill. 21 roll call and voice votes on amendments, a vote out of the two major committees, 21 to one and 24 to four. Senate Republicans sought to delay the inevitable passage of U.C. by two weeks, even as American manufacturers are experiencing devastating semiconductor shortages and pleading with us for help.\par \par \uc2 It was only when this morning when the five or six Republicans who were holding up the bill saw all 50 Democrats on the floor that they realized they couldn`t roll us and then they accepted our agreement. But it ensures that U.C. seek a will pass as soon as we resumed legislative session. Maybe even worse, certainly worse. Senate Republicans at the personal request of Leader McConnell can continue their brazen attempts to whitewash the attack of January 6th by filibustering the House passed bipartisan January 6thth commission.\par \par \uc2 Even though as I said, Speaker Pelosi and I agreed to change is proposed by Senator Collins, she`s still -- they were still only able to get six votes. Senators should rest assured that the events of January 6th will be investigated, and that as Majority Leader, I reserve the right to force the Senate to vote on the bill again, at the appropriate time. Senate Democrats are doing everything we can to move bipartisan legislation when we can where the opportunity exists.\par \par \uc2 And -- but we will not wait for months and months to pass meaningful legislation that delivers real results for the American people. So looking ahead, when the senate reconvenes on June 7th, we`re forced to vote on H.R 7. That`s called the Paycheck Fairness Act. It`s equal pay for women. It`s a long overdue piece of legislation. It`s supported by all 50 Democrats. It previously was filibustered by Senate Republicans during President Obama`s second term.\par \par \uc2 It`s common sense a legislation. I have promised it will receive a vote in the Senate. And in a couple of weeks, it will. We`ll also begin to confirm President Biden`s judicial nominees, including very famous and illustrious Judge Ketanji Brown Jackson for the very important Court of Appeals of the D.C. circuit. The Senate may also -- I may also ask the Senate to consider gun legislation and LGBTQ equality legislation during the June work period.\par \par \uc2 And finally, in the last week of the June work period, the week of June 21st. The Senate will vote on S1, the For the People Act. Legislation vital, vital to defending our democracy, reducing the influence of dark money that pernicious influence of powerful special interests and stopping the wave of Republican voter suppression happening in states across the country in service to the President Trump`s big lie.\par \par \uc2 So I repeat, the Senate will vote on S1, the For the People Act the week of June 21st. In addition, the Senate Democratic majority will continue to make progress on President Biden`s bill back better economic agenda. We had two things going at once which shows you will work bipartisan where we can. And Senator Cooper and Senator Capito reported unanimously out of EPW, the ground transportation, the Road Transportation Bill.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, the Senate Finance Committee reported historic clean energy legislation that will begin to fight climate change. And that passed for it to keep -- was voted in committee 14 to 14. That was not obviously bipartisan. So, as the President continues to discuss infrastructure legislation with Senate Republicans, our committees are going to hold hearings and continue their work on build back better with or without the report -- the support of Republican senators.\par \par \uc2 We must pass comprehensive jobs and infrastructure legislation this summer. The American people gave us a Democratic Senate to produce big and bold change on the major issues confronting us and that is what we are doing. Ready for your questions. We`ll start here and work all. You change your view. Usually you`re over here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think --\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Oh.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Leader Schumer, how much more time should you give bipartisan negotiations on infrastructure? You sound (INAUDIBLE) letter to your colleagues, a little frustrated with working with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Well, we`re -- we were frustrated when we did the most bipartisan bill. I think he`ll check it. There were more amendments on this bill than in several years of McConnell`s entire tenure as majority leader, you know, certain years. So, it is frustrating that when you go totally bipartisan, we take a lot of tough votes on Republican amendments that they still -- not just that they still try to block it a group of them but McConnell is nowhere on the floor, nowhere around trying to prevent that from happening.\par \par \uc2 And so, it is frustrating, but that`s not going to stop us where we can from working in a bipartisan way. That`s the preferred way to go. It`s just not possible in many different areas with this Republican Senate, and it won`t stop us from acting. Yes, nope, nope.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE) negotiate with Republicans?\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Look, I have faith -- the president and my beliefs are the same. We`ll try to go bipartisan when we can. But it shouldn`t stop us from the kind of big bold action we need. And I have faith in the President and we talk all the time. And he`s going to try to pursue bipartisan where we can but not let it stop them. Yes, nope, nope, nope. No, Manu, you`re -- we`re working our way around. You could wear those jeans and have a cicada but got to wait your turn.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What are your views on Congress (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Well, look, it should be investigated, it would be much better to do it in a bipartisan way. And that`s what this bill did. But better to investigate with a select committee than not investigate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Senator Schumer, you had exchange with Senator Collins in the senate floor just ahead of the January 6th Commission bill. I just want to see if you could talk --\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: I`ll just be brief about it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Senator C