licans where we can but we need big bold action. And reconciliation is certainly a serious consideration to get that big, bold action if we can`t get it with Republicans. Next.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The filibuster of the January 6th bill today and any of the actions that surrounded this China competitiveness bill, make you feel any more willing to or open to -- or open to the idea of filibuster reform? I know this is going to be a big call considering --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: look, I think the events of the last few days, probably made every member of our caucus realize that a lot of our Republican colleagues are not willing to work with us on a whole lot of issues, even issues where we try to be bipartisan. Nope, it was somebody was -- I`m trying to get everybody here. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, Senator. You`ve mentioned in your talk frequently to President Biden. Do you sense any impatience on his part or on his (INAUDIBLE) intransigence?\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: He`s a -- he`s a very patient fellow. But he`s a persistent fellow. And as I said, he`ll keep talking to Republicans when he thinks there`s an opportunity to get something done, but he`s not going to let it get in the way of getting the kind of big bold action he and I believe America needs and requires and wants. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Would you change the deadline for the January 6th commission if you`re going to have votes on --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: I`m not going to debate, I`m not going to -- it`s -- right now, Leader McConnell, we all know twisted a lot of arms not to get it, not even move to proceed. If we were to get a vote to move proceed, we could begin to discuss different aspects. Manu.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. Than you. You said that you`re open to a sled committee in the House. would you encourage your Chairman in the Senate to investigate this? Number one. And number two, two members of your caucus skipped today`s vote. Is that OK with you given how important of a vote this was?\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: OK. I`m not going to get into individual members of our caucus. We always made sure we`d have enough votes on all of the issues. On the first issue, I have a lot of faith already. Senator Klobuchar and Senator Peters are looking extensively at what went on January 6th, and I have a lot of faith in what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of it, they`re working together. And occasionally they reach out to other committees like intelligence that where they need some expertise there, too. Yes. Nope, nope, nope. You got your two, you got two. That`s more than one. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The House does end up moving towards the select committee, Democrats end up pushing this forward. How quickly do you think they should move on that? And are you worried that any findings would lose credibility with the American public if it`s not --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Look, we prefer -- we prefer to do it bipartisan. We -- every Democrat voted for bipartisan but facts must come out. Go ahead. Next.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Senator Schumer, yesterday, Republican senators stood where you`re standing now and proposed using leftover COVID money that`s been appropriated for years now to pay for infrastructure. Is that a red line for you or would you consider doing that?\par \par \uc2 SCHUME: Look, we need big bold -- I`m not going to get into the specifics. We need big bold action. And that means we need very significant infrastructure money along the lines of what President Biden is proposed. Go ahead, Bresnahan. He just went like this. I hardly saw his hand. What?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You`re having budget resolution on your colleagues. Bring you -- it`s going to bring the floor --\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: I said -- I think I said previously that we would look to do that in July. This is about June. Next.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Crapo-Wyden got 91 votes yesterday (INAUDIBLE) why did you want that in the substitute amendment? And did Chairman Wyden fully apprise you of the feeling was working --\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Yes. President -- Chairman Wyden sometimes has the power of a president over certain matters. We were in touch all the time. There were some objections to that provision. From the administration. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You set on the floor last hour, you hope that this wasn`t indicative of Republican obstruction on other bills. But why would we not think what we saw the past couple of days your vote on the 1/6 bill. Why would you not think this is the beginning of the end of the filibuster?\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Look, about it. I`ve said it over and over again. We have to have big bold action. We have to we cannot fail on key things to our democracy like S1 and everything`s on the table. And we`re going to continue to discuss it as we move forward.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It sounds like you`re (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: That`s all I`m going to -- everything`s on the table. We hope we can get bipartisan support. So far, we have not seen any glimmers on S1. And if we don`t, everything`s going to be on the table.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Leader.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Last one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Senator Scott.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The patient DeBonis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The senator counselor Booker have a deal on policing next month. Will you guarantee that you will bring that to the floor? And next month is LGBT Pride Month, you mentioned the LGBT equality building. You want your team vote on it. Why not?\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Well, it`s one of the things we`re considering on LGBTQ, we believe in it strongly. You know, it`s a very busy June and so it`s -- we`re looking at it. What was the first thing?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Will you guarantee your vote on policing bill --\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Look, Senator Booker briefed me yesterday. They`re making good progress. I`m not going to set any artificial deadlines, but I would hope that they could come to a good bipartisan agreement. Thank you, everybody. Nope, have a nice break. And I will not use the words for the DeBonis for you, Manu. Patience.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, as a -- as a journalist watching this, I appreciate the speed dating version of answering questions there at that presser by Senate Majority Leader Schumer. Look, the big takeaway is he plans to bring up the January 6th commission bill again for another vote. He doesn`t sound very optimistic that there will be. Numbers as he said that -- and then after that he expects a select committee propped up by House Democrats who end up being the vehicle that investigates January 6th.\par \par \uc2 I think the questions on the filibuster and his answers there are going to be eye of the beholder answers. Many of the answers he gave are answers he`s given all the time. Too soon to tell. We shall see more senators keep seeing who wants to work with us, who don`t et cetera, et cetera. But at the end of the day, it does sound as if you combine the words of Senator Bennett, it`s chipping away but they`re not there yet.\par \par \uc2 They don`t have 50 votes to reform the filibuster or they`d start reforming the filibuster. Keep that in mind. Up next, the potentially game-changing week in the fight against climate change. The Green Movement is gaining some momentum. His company shareholders in the courts are now calling on big oil to make some big changes.\par \par \uc2 Plus, another Russian hack attack. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  reveals what may be on an ongoing cyberattack. Apparently targeting government agencies again, we`ll dig into what we know, who`s at risk and whether there`s anything that can be done to stop it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Yes, there`s been a lot of news this week. But don`t miss what happened in the fight against climate change. Some consequential actions. Big oil is facing its own climate reckoning. There`s some clear calls to change the business model entirely. Just this week alone, two of the world`s largest oil companies, \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  and \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , faced successful rebukes from their shareholders for stalling on climate initiatives.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Exxon shareholders elected two members of climate focused investor group to serve on the company`s board. It was a major blow to Exxon management and a clear directive about clean energy`s role in that company`s future. On the same day, over 60 percent of \uc2 Chevron\uc2  shareholders voted in favor of a petition by climate activists making \uc2 Chevron\uc2  responsible not just for their own emissions but the emissions of the products they sell to customers as well.\par \par \uc2 Also this week in a landmark case overseas, a Dutch court decided that Dutch Royal Shell -- yes, Shell -- is individually liable for their role in climate change. The Dutch court ruled that the company will have to drastically speed up the rate at which they cut carbon emissions.\par \par \uc2 So to discuss the impact of this big week, I`m joined now by Professor Leah Stokes, assistant professor of Climate Policy at the University of California Santa Barbara, UCSB, and Robinson Meyer, the climate reporter for the "Atlantic."\par \par \uc2 And, Robinson, I want to start with you. Am I overhyping this? Or was this as consequential of a week as it seems when it comes to engaging the private sector and the oil industry in becoming part of the solution instead of part of the problem?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON MEYER, THE ATLANTIC CLIMATE REPORTER: No. You`re absolutely right, it`s a huge, huge week. And I think there`s really two big reasons for that. You know, the first is that investor groups, groups like Vanguard and BlackRock have been making noise for a long time about how they`re concerned about climate change and they want to see companies take more of a stand on it.\par \par \uc2 But mostly, that has remained in this kind of, you know, proxy petition mode, has remained in -- they`re asking companies to do things in a kind of non-binding way or they`re expressing a lot of concern. And in some ways it`s not that far from the Senate. And this is the first time with this Exxon vote where they really changed the makeup of a company`s, you know, board of directors, the most powerful people at the company, because the company was not acting fast enough on climate change.\par \par \uc2 And what we know, too, is that this -- with this Exxon vote specifically, you know, we know that two directors on the Exxon board were elected to these activist directors, a third may still make it on the board. There`s still kind of a Damocles sword over the whole company, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MEYER: So they won at least two directors this week. Next time, you know, these shareholders have already acted, next time they could replace the full board. That is the kind of laden power that we all saw this week.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Professor Stokes, practically, usually, when you see the private sector pressured on something, they end up acquiescing, realizing there are some market forces here and there are certainly -- you know, I would argue, there is a market to be on the side of fighting climate change that can be economically -- it bears some economic fruit for an industry. What do you expect this can do? What are some of the practical impacts of the sort of corporate punishments that we saw this week?\par \par \uc2 PROF. LEAH STOKES, UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA SANTA BARBARA ASSISTANT PROFESSOR: Well, I think in terms of the practical impacts, we have to look at the Shell case because that was even more important in some ways. What the Dutch court said was that Shell has to be aligned with the Paris Agreement and with what scientists say is necessary. So the company has to cut its emissions by 45 percent by 2030 and also do that when it comes to the emissions from the product that it sells.\par \par \uc2 That`s a very transformative change for an oil company. Right? They are selling fossil fuels that fundamentally create pollution. So I think that that kind of a ruling could really start to shift the business model of a really big oil company.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Robinson, though, could Exxon fight back here? Let me go back to the Exxon or even Shell. I mean, how much will they acquiesce and how much do you expect them to fight?\par \par \uc2 MEYER: I think -- so at this point, the climate activist directors do not control a majority of the board. And you could imagine, you know, a kind of ongoing skirmish. I think it will be harder for them to fight back. This is a real rebuke for the CEO \uc2 Darren Woods\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s also just -- the fact that BlackRock and Vanguard and the kind of these large index investors have taken the step to start evicting members of the board of directors, you know, this is -- they`ve already rung the bell. Right? Like they will now keep doing this until they start to see results that they like.\par \par \uc2 One thing I`d add is that the market in some ways has already started to punish some of these companies. You know, as Exxon and other big oil companies.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEYER: Were already -- were minting money at the moment, right? If they were the biggest, most productive, most exciting part of the economy, I don`t think we`d be seeing this. You know, but what has happened over the past 10 years is that energy, the oil sector, is actually the smallest component part of the S&P 500. (INAUDIBLE) of the largest. So the market has kind of already punished these oil companies. And in some ways what we`re seeing now is, you know, these big institutional investors starting to react to that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Professor Stokes, will -- should we expect to see other country court systems -- you know, obviously, \uc2 Royal Dutch Shell\uc2  was based in the Netherlands. So there was -- that`s why that lawsuit was there. Do you expect copycat lawsuits around sort of in the rest of Europe and should we see -- could you imagine success in the courts against the oil companies in this country?\par \par \uc2 STOKES: Well, it`s not really just Shell that`s come under regulation in the EU. It`s also BP. They have had not a court decision, but policy saying that that company also needs to be cutting emissions. So we are seeing oil companies facing this kind of pressure across Europe. In the United States, there have been efforts to hold fossil fuels accountable -- sorry, fossil fuel companies accountable through a variety of ways.\par \par \uc2 Some young people activists have been basically suing the federal government or trying to say that the federal government isn`t doing enough on climate change. There have also been attorney generals taking up specific cases. One in New York, another one in Massachusetts, trying to hold companies like Exxon accountable for things like saying, we`re really going to transition to new clean fuels when they are spending more advertising that policy than actually investing in those clean fuels in their company.\par \par \uc2 So there is a whole court strategy around this. I don`t know if we`ll see as landmark decisions in the United States as we just saw on Wednesday with Shell in the Netherlands. But this is a strategy that people are pursuing. And I think it`s a really important strategy that`s part of the climate fight.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Robinson, would you compare it to sort of the tobacco company lawsuits? Is that where we`re headed? Or will the oil companies sort of try to stay ahead of American lawsuits and try to make some changes before the court case -- before being taken to court?\par \par \uc2 MEYER: Yes, it`s a great question. I mean, definitely in the U.S., these cases have been modelled off the tobacco company lawsuits. And one thing we`ve seen -- a key difference between the U.S., you know, court system and other court systems around the world is that the makeup of the Supreme Court is just what it is right now. One thing we`ve seen from lawsuits trying to compensate from that is to try to tailor the lawsuit very specifically so that it stays within state courts, which might be more --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEYER: You know, less right-leaning and more friendly to climate claims. I think we could eventually see a kind of broader federal lawsuit against oil companies. Certainly there`s been a lot of documentation that indicates the oil -- in fact some from Professor Stokes that indicate that the oil companies, like the tobacco companies, were aware of climate change as they were selling their product, that they were not disclosing it to customers.\par \par \uc2 It`s very hard to imagine that kind of legal case, you know, being successful in the United States without a friendlier Supreme Court. But maybe down the road, if there are a number of international rulings like this, it could start to set a kind of expectation that this is something the U.S. might move on. And if the makeup of the Supreme Court were to change, we could see it happen here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. But I could, you know, the tobacco cases, you know, were in many cases state-based. So you`re right, I could see --\par \par \uc2 MEYER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Keeping it in the states might end up being the more effective tool.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Professor Leah Stokes, Robinson Meyer, thank you for making some time. Glad we got to explain the relevance of what feels like a big week in the fight against climate change. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, as the White House gears up for a meeting with Putin, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  admits there is an active ongoing act from the same Russian group that was responsible for the massive cyberattack on government agencies that has been taking place over the last year.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. In an overnight alert, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  announced what appears to be another hack attack by the same Russian-based group that executed that massive \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  hack. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  this morning said the hackers are targeting 150 organizations, including several government agencies. According to \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , this is a spear fishing campaign and it`s been ongoing since at least January. The company is describing the attack as a, quote, "active incident."\par \par \uc2 Remember the Biden administration imposed more sanctions on Russia last month in response to the \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  hack. Meanwhile, House Intelligence Committee chair Adam Schiff just released a statement saying in part that if appears Russia is undeterred by the earlier sanctions. It now appears necessary to tighten the economic screws further. And Senate Intelligence Committee chair Mark Warner also released this statement insisting that we have to step up our cyber defenses.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Kiersten Todt, she`s the managing director of the Cyber Readiness Institute. She also served on President Obama`s National Cybersecurity Commission.\par \par \uc2 So, Kiersten, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  made this announcement late last night. Forgive us for wondering, what aren`t they telling us? This coming right before Memorial Day weekend. It felt like one of those, to us political journalists, like a late news dump of something that perhaps we should have known about a lot sooner. What can you tell us about this?\par \par \uc2 KIERSTEN TODT, CYBER READINESS INSTITUTE MANAGING DIRECTOR: So, Chuck, thank you. What we know so far is that the same criminal actor group that was responsible for \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  and other attacks was responsible for this attack where it took advantage -- essentially it gained access to a trusted technology partner called Constant Contact, which is part of a legitimate mass mailing services infrastructure. It took advantage of that company`s vulnerabilities and then it infected its customers.\par \par \uc2 And now as we`ve seen continuously, it`s continuous undermining our trust in the technology ecosystem. And we`re still -- obviously it`s ongoing. We`re figuring it out. Three thousand individual accounts were compromised, as you said, about 150 organizations. We`re seeing about 24 countries exposed through this breach right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So they said it was a phishing attack. And so which is sort of just - - they`re sending out a whole bunch of e-mails, it looks like, in the name of the agency. And you can end up giving. Describe the level of that kind of, quote, "hack" versus what we saw with \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2 . These are sort of two different attacks, are they not?\par \par \uc2 TODT: Very much so. So \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2 , we saw it as an incredibly sophisticated attack by Russia, our nation state adversary, with a criminal actor group. Very precise, going after infrastructure. They were in our networks for nine months before they were detected. This was much more pedestrian, almost for entertainment. We can speculate as to the reasons why we think this group went after this type of attack in this way.\par \par \uc2 I can speculate, it`s a little bit of the you can, you know, do the sanctions, I can still get you`re your open networks, and you`re still very vulnerable based on these very simple tactics. I think it certainly calls into question again human behavior, the lack of training and education across the board on some of the basics in cybersecurity. But in addition to education and training, I think we really need to be calling upon our technology partners to help prevent these types of very basic attacks that we`re seeing across networks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can the Russian government shut this down if they wanted to, in your opinion?\par \par \uc2 TODT: I think they could. But I think you bring into a very important question, which is that we absolutely have to be working with our like- minded economic partners, with our allies, to look at what are the clear rules for conduct in cyberspace and what are the consequences? As long as we go back and forth with action and retaliation, we`re never going to compel nation state adversaries or criminal actor groups to deter their behavior.\par \par \uc2 There`s no reason right now for them to deter their behavior because there are no consequences for their action. And this has to change. I think we`ve seen language from the Biden administration. We see some leadership in the executive order, the cyber executive order that was released a couple weeks ago, and it is now incumbent upon the United States in collaboration with international partners to really create action and ensure that there are consequences, significant consequences, for this type of malicious activity.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. What does that look like? Abd I sit there and I take a deep breath because sanctions. We hear about sanctions. And it seems like there is always sanctions. There`s always somebody that`s sanctioned. Putin`s chef gets sanctioned, you have this, and it doesn`t seem to deter anything. What do you think deterrence is going to have to look like if economic sanctions aren`t effective?\par \par \uc2 TODT: Well, Chuck, I think we`ve got to be looking at more significant consequences. I was talking to a colleague who was part of the Obama administration just yesterday and we`re talking about the election interference, and this was before we knew about this hack. And when we respond with a proportionate response, what we`re learning from Russia is that there will just be a tit-for-tat and we`re not making any progress in again deterring this behavior.\par \par \uc2 So we can assume that more significant consequences, and again, not just by the United States alone, but a global code of conduct for what rules of the road are with outlines consequences for what that looks like, and I do think it absolutely has to go beyond sanctions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kiersten Todt, an adviser, cybersecurity adviser back during the Obama administration and a cybersecurity expert that we like to turn to quite a bit, thank you for coming on and providing that perspective.\par \par \uc2 TODT: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, the country prepares to mark 100 years since the worst incident of racial violence in American history.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Right now hundreds of people are marching through Tulsa to commemorate one of the worst race riots in our nation`s history. This march through the Greenwood District, once one of the most thriving black communities in the country, is one of more than a dozen events planned for this weekend as we approach the 100th anniversary for what is known as the Tulsa massacre.\par \par \uc2 One hundred years ago next week, a white mob destroyed the district that was known as Black Wall Street, killing an estimated 300 people and obliterating more than 1200 homes as well as dozens of churches, school, and a hospital.\par \par \uc2 In a new documentary, NBC`s Trymaine Lee explores what happened in 1921 and the impact it is still having today. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TORI TYSON, GREENWOOD BUSINESS OWNER: Houses destroyed. Businesses destroyed. But, you know, yes, we`re resilient, we`ll rebuild. I can just imagine how they felt. Imagine where we would be had it not happened. Our family would be destroyed.\par \par \uc2 ELLOUISE COCHRANE-PRICE, DICK ROWLAND`S SECOND COUSIN: Repair me. Repair me as a person, repair me as a citizen, whatever that takes, whatever that looks like. We`re talking about going on four generations of tragedy.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can watch the documentary "BLOOD ON BLACK WALL STREET" this Sunday night at 10:00 p.m. Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. It`s also available on demand on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us this hour. We`ll be back next week with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY and you can of course catch "MEET THE PRESS" this Sunday on your local NBC station. MSNBC`s coverage, though, continues with Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC HOST: It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210529eh5s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 14, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd Ellison Barber, Richard Engel, Savannah Sellers \par \uc2 7,830 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: It`s Friday, businesses, states public health officials and everyday Americans are looking at what comes next. After the CDC major announcement that vaccinated people can ditch the mask in most cases. We`ll speak with the director of the National Institutes of Health, Dr. Francis Collins in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a major bipartisan agreement is announced on Capitol Hill to form a commission to investigate the January 6th siege at the Capitol. But some Republicans are still trying to push back. We`ll speak with the top Democratic Party negotiator on the deal, the chairman of the House Homeland Security Committee, Bennie Thompson.\par \par \uc2 And the fighting only intensifies as thousands of Israeli troops gather on the edge of Gaza while Jewish and Arab civilians clash on the streets. This one seems a lot worse than 2014 with no clear end in sight.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Friday, it is MEET THE PRESS DAILY and what a Friday it is. I`m Chuck Todd, the shockwaves of the CDC`s announcement are being felt across America after the agency lifted nearly all mask recommendations for fully vaccinated Americans. From a public health standpoint, it`s great news. Marking a turning point in our battle against the pandemic. It`s also an important assurance and incentive for many that the vaccines really do work and they`re worth it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: For now, after a year of hard work and so much sacrifice, the rule is very simple. Get vaccinated or wear a mask until you do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: From a political standpoint, the CDC announcement could not have come at a better time for a White House that 24 hours earlier was mired in a massive headlines about gas shortages and panic at the pump. The CDC`s announcement actually caught some public health officials and state officials by surprise. Remember, it was just two weeks ago that the CDC put out guidance reiterating many of its mask recommendations for vaccinated people.\par \par \uc2 Not anymore. And today, the front page news is everywhere. Boston to St. Louis, South Bend, Omaha, Louisville, Flagstaff, Silicon Valley, Sin City, everybody lifting on the lifting of the mass guidance, the gas shortage story, it`s not even below the full. The House physician has told members who are vaccinated that they can resume pre pandemic activities. The Department of Defense has loosened its mask requirements for vaccinated personnel.\par \par \uc2 A number of states have already begun lifting their mask mandates after the CDC`s announcement. And a number of companies are reviewing their guidance. I know we got an email ourselves this morning at this company saying that guidance is going to get reviewed. We are following a whole bunch of reaction to this news. And still the big questions that it is raising. So we want to dive in. In a moment I`m going to speak with Dr. Francis Collins, Director of the National Institutes of Health.\par \par \uc2 But first for some reaction on the ground, NBC News colleague Ellison Barbers in Biloxi, Mississippi, a state that I believe is still under 50 percent when it comes to vaccination rate. Ellison, what do you find?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Actually 30 percent, under 30 percent fully vaccinated, that`s not just the lowest or one of the lowest states, that is the lowest According to The New York Times when I checked this morning of all 50 states. They`re 50 out of 50. But despite that, they have previously lifted almost all of their COVID-19 related restrictions. The only one that remained since the end of the month was wearing masks in schools.\par \par \uc2 But I want to show you where I`m at right now because we are at a restaurant, Half Shell Oyster House in Biloxi, Mississippi, and they`re now going looking at this new CDC guidance and trying to decide how they handle what they do with their employees moving ahead as it relates to mask. So, if you look behind me, you can kind of see they`re working on shucking oysters over here. Back in this area, staff don`t technically need to be wearing face masks according to the owner`s rules here.\par \par \uc2 But you can see the one person he still is anyways what they say right now for the time being. They are still asking servers and having their servers wear face masks when they come to say get the oysters and take them to the tables. And as we see servers come in and out of here. They`re typically wearing face masks before they go back to the table. He says that they`re making that decision right now because they want to give staff time to hear about the new changes, the new guidelines.\par \par \uc2 And also customers and then they want to talk to them and see how staff feels about it as well as customers. The owner that we`ve spoken to. He says that he feels really confident. His staff is going to be eager to stop wearing face masks when they`re dealing with customers and going to serve them at tables. Customers though the reaction could be a bit different and from speaking with people in this area, just out and about, Chuck, we`ve heard everything from this is great.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been waiting for this to -- we haven`t been wearing masks for weeks because the mandates were already lifted here too. Hey, have you looked at the vaccination rate? I don`t feel comfortable not wearing a mask in a state that only has about 26 percent of their residents fully vaccinated. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ellison, you just did a good job laying out what I think is the new conundrum here for the American public and trying to figure out OK, when do they wear a mask? Anyway, Ellison on the ground force in Biloxi. Thank you. Which brings us to Dr. Francis Collins. Dr. Collins, it is -- first of all, it`s an exciting 24 hours to know that we -- it feels like the closest we`ve come to being able to declare some sort of end here.\par \par \uc2 But I got to ask, in the last couple of weeks, under -- whether it was questioning on Capitol Hill, questioning by the media, the CDC with -- and a lot of the public health officials seem to be pretty sure that we were going to be in this some form of a mask requirement much longer. What changed and how did it change so quickly?\par \par \uc2 DR. FRANCIS COLLINS, NIH DIRECTOR: I think -- well, first of all, you started off calling this a shockwave. Isn`t it nice we have a shock waves that`s good? Let`s just take a moment here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Away for that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, right, right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: OK. So, how did -- how did things change? Well, some of it is new data that`s arriving, the efficacy of the vaccines which we knew was really high, 94, 95 percent and carefully controlled clinical trials, you always wonder, well, what happens when you get out in the real world? And guess what, it`s 94 to 95 percent. These vaccines are incredibly effective. So people who are fully vaccinated like me, really are now in a position where you can be in various circumstances, and not worry nearly as much as you did before.\par \par \uc2 I think also, there`s more data that people who are vaccinated are less likely to be carrying the virus without knowing it themselves, and therefore, potentially posing a risk to others. That risk seems to have gone down. So putting that all together, looking at what`s happened in Israel, which is maybe a couple of months ahead of us in terms of their vaccination rate, and seeing that they have relaxed all of these stipulations, and seem to be doing fine.\par \par \uc2 Although there`s other horrible things happening in Israel. It does give you confidence that the time is now to ease up on this. You know, CDC has a tough time with this, let`s give them a break. If they make a mistake, and I`m sure some people think maybe they just did, of getting a little too relaxed. And then we have an outbreak and there has to be like going back to mass again. There are going to be a lot of mad people.\par \par \uc2 But come on, I think it`s time to take that chance as they have now done, and to see if all of us can get a little closer back to normal life. I got to tell you a funny story. I have a trainer that I work out with twice a week and today for the first time, he took off his mask as I did because we`re immunized. And I realized the guy had braces put on 12 months ago, I never saw his face. I did know that. There`s going to be a lot of discoveries happening here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, there is. But you just actually anticipated and I`d like to follow up a little bit more on a -- on a potential question. Look, you sort of hinted, hey, you never know the CDC may have to rescind this leniency because of a spike and I -- what would you be looking for or what would they be looking for where they would think, oh, you know what, maybe we lifted this -- we lifted this too soon. Is it -- is it a spike? Is it a move in the number of cases in vaccinated places?\par \par \uc2 I mean, is there a specific metric you`d be looking at that would say, oh, maybe we did this too quick?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Well, I don`t think they changed the opportunity now for fully vaccinated people to go around outside without mask, that`s not really going to change. I think that edge of this is what happens indoors with crowded spaces, which already if it`s really crowded, you`re still encouraged to wear your mask but less crowded spaces. If it looks as if in certain communities, the viruses coming back or if there`s a new variant that seems particularly contagious, then we may need to adjust that.\par \par \uc2 Part of CDC`s problem, of course, is they have to make recommendations for the whole country. And the situation in different parts of the country is very different. As you just heard about that report from Alabama, where if you look at Maine, for instance, where the vaccination rates are very high, doesn`t seem quite like that`s the same thing. But they have to come help - - somehow come up with an average.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s be clear, CDC put out their recommendations but they made it very clear that state and local jurisdictions may have different views. And you should listen to those and businesses may have different views about whether it`s safe to be indoors, and certainly hospitals and correctional facilities and nursing homes, and travel on planes and buses and trains. This is not a total moment to take your mask off in all those spaces. So follow those guidelines.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, this guidance almost seems to encourage the private sector to come up with a vaccine mandate, right? Because you have to come up with some if the -- if the -- if the rule is fully vaccinated people can go without masks, well then, the next inevitable question is how do we check to see if somebody is vaccinated? Well, we have these vaccine cards and different companies, we know cruise lines are doing this and others are doing this.\par \par \uc2 Is that what you`re hoping happens here? That essentially the private sector becomes the enforcer?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Well, I don`t know that hoping. I think the private sector is pretty smart. And they`ll figure out in their given situations, what makes sense, I can assure you again, the federal government is not going to be issuing vaccine passports that`s been made very clear. But private concerns have the opportunity to do so universities, of course, many of them have already declared. If you`re going to live on campus, you need to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 There is some legal issue about this because the vaccines have not yet had final approval. They`re under emergency use authorization, we`ll have to see how those legal battles play out. But I think every business owner, every university president is going to have to figure out what makes the most sense to keep their workers safe, get their economy going again, and then make that judgment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There was no guidance given or at least it was an obvious guidance given to parents of unvaccinated. You`re, you know, I`m in a household of two fully vaccinated people, one almost fully vaccinated teenager, and one who`s not yet vaccinated. And I imagine I`m not the only person with children under 18 at home that way. What is the guidance if you have a child under 12? You know, where you don`t even have a -- you don`t have an opportunity to get vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes, I think if you dig down into the deeper pages of CDC they address that. I`m not sure it gives you a precise answer. A lot of it has to do with what is the risk in your community, that that under age child is potentially going to be infected and going to spread it. But if you and the rest of the family are vaccinated, your chance of catching that is now so low that I would say the chances are really low and you should not have to worry about that case.\par \par \uc2 Now on the other hand, that child who`s not vaccinated, may still need to wear a mask when they go to school if there`s still community spread in order to being part -- in order not be part of that next outbreak.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are you going to do when it comes to wearing a mask? How often do you expect to wear one and where would you do it?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: I am looking forward to new opportunities to go to the grocery store and not wear a mask because I`ve been pretty faithful about that. I think I mentioned this morning, being able to do a workout with my wife and with his trainer with all of our masks off. That hasn`t happened before. When it comes to going to work at NIH, we`re still a federal facility, we`re trying to figure out whether the federal mandate for mask wearing has now been abolished by this finding. We don`t quite know that yet.\par \par \uc2 And otherwise, you know, people are a little confused by this. Let me give a recommendation. If you go to \uc2 Google\uc2  and just do CDC choosing safer activities, there is a very helpful sort of visual that looks like this, about when you can wear a mask and when you don`t have to. One side of this, this side is people who have immunized. A lot of green there. This is the side for people who aren`t yet immunized who still need to wear masks and for whom there are dangerous settings.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we need to be really clear about that, Chuck. Everybody`s going, oh, we don`t have to wear a mask anymore. That applies to fully immunized people. If you`re not immunized, you`re still risk. You`re -- to yourself and to others and things still need to be pretty faithful as far as mask wearing. And I hope that came across and maybe it`s even an incentive for people who have been, yes, I`ll get around to it.\par \par \uc2 OK. It`s time to stop getting around to it and actually do so. And if you`re wondering where to get vaccinated, 438829, a text data on your phone, punch in your zip code and you`ll get answers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Look, carrots and sticks, lotteries in Ohio. Vaccine mandates, whatever it takes, let`s get more people vaccinated. Dr. Francis Collins.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Johnson beer in New Jersey, whatever it is. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There you go. I take it if it -- if it entices people to get the vaccine, you`re for it. Anyway, Dr. Collins --\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: And we need it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thanks for bringing your -- bringing your optimism as well to the show. It`s always a pleasure to get that.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Well, it`s a good day for optimism. And we even talk about the fact that adolescents can get now safely immunized as well. And my 14-year- old granddaughter is getting her a shot tomorrow. This is great.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I -- my 14-year-old is ecstatic about next week. So, it`s when he gets it. I hear you. Thank you, sir. Well, let me bring in somebody who has to interpret this new CDC guidance. It`s a governor of the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, it`s Governor Tom Wolf. Governor Wolf, thanks for coming on. So, you just heard Dr. Collins there. You`ve read the CDC requirements, you`ve talked to your public health officials, what conclusions are you coming to?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TOM WOLF (D-PA): Well, it is confusing. The CDC guidelines basically say if you`re fully immunized, that means you`re two weeks after your second or your final dose. You don`t have to wear a mask if you`re going to be outside and about. But they`re saying if you`re in close quarters inside, if you`re going to hospital, if you`re going to be in public transit, if you`re going to be in a long-term care facility, you have to wear a mask.\par \par \uc2 And I think is the doctor said people are going to make different interpretations of this in different places. I know businesses, private organizations are already making decisions. We`ve had companies in Pennsylvania say, you know, if you want to come in, no shirt and shoes, no shirt, no service, no mask, no service. So, I think that there are a lot of different interpretations of this. I think we`re following the CDC guidelines.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, we`re going to take away the mask fully, the mandate, as soon as we get to 70 percent with people having had -- fully -- being fully vaccinated. We think that`ll happen within the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. I`m confused about that because it --\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Excuse me?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you waiting until you hit 70 percent before you left or are you now lifting immediately?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: No, I mean, we`re still looking at this. I mean, we were taken by surprise by the CDC announcement. And as of today, we`re still looking at it. And so what we initially said, which is we get to 70 percent herd immunity, then we`ll lift the mask mandate. We`re going fully open aside from the mask mandate on May 31st. So we`re only a few weeks away from being fully open. And I think that`s what people are looking forward to.\par \par \uc2 And as of the 17th, we`re saying anybody, you know, all outdoor events can be at 75 percent of capacity. People still want to wear masks. The indoor events should be at 50 percent of capacity. People still should wear masks but we`re moving quickly toward being reopened.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What kind of incentive programs are you trying to develop to get folks vaccinated? The report that had half of Philadelphia`s firefighters unvaccinated, and we know first responders were some of the first people offered the vaccine. This is clearly a hesitancy issue perhaps in some form or another. So what tactics are you thinking of here? Are you going the bonus route, going the mandate route? All of the above?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes. I mean, Pennsylvania is doing very well right now. We`re at 53.6 percent. In first doses, over 18 is actually approaching 70 percent. The vaccinated population of those 65 and older who have had at least one doses over 95 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, in Pennsylvania, people seem to be willing to get the vaccine. So, we`re at the top of the charts, were the highest in terms of first doses of any large state. The 10 most populous state. So, I think Pennsylvania is doing well at this point. I`m not sure we need incentives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, you`re not concerned about this firefighter survey in Philadelphia where about half of firefighters --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- reported being vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I`m always -- I`m always -- I`m always concerned when there`s a group that is at 50 percent but that varies across state. I was at a hospital yesterday where the rate of medical professionals was like 80 percent. So, it varies from place to place. And what I think we`ve seen is the hesitancy is not so much a matter of just definitely not going to get the vaccine. It`s a matter of saying, I`m not sure I wanted it.\par \par \uc2 You know, the folks are persuadable. So I`m not sure what was working at the -- with the firefighters in Philadelphia and my hope is that eventually they get vaccine too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you a lot of -- quite a few Republican governors, when it comes to unemployment insurance have decided to not use the additional money, add the extra benefits. Where are you on that? Or do you want to keep the extra benefits going out for as long as the federal government provides?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes. I want to keep the benefits. I think people are getting better benefits and they deserve it. I don`t -- I know the April jobs report was a disappointment. But I don`t think it`s because the benefits were too rich. If you -- if you look back at coming out of the Great Recession, the benefits were lower, the unemployment rate was actually higher. So, I think there are other things that work. I think people want to feel safe going back to work.\par \par \uc2 I think there`s a need for childcare. But I`m not sure that the generosity of benefits had anything to do with it. If you look in the United States total, the biggest job growth was in the hospitality industry which is not known for high wages.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A fair point there. Pennsylvania Governor Tom Wolf, Democrat from the Commonwealth there. Thanks for coming on, sharing your perspective with us. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, Democratic Congressman Bennie Thompson will be here for his first T.V. interview since this morning`s big announcement that he and his Republican counterpart had finally struck a deal to form a bipartisan commission to investigate the January 6th insurrection. But are the House Republicans totally on board? We`ll find out.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. For the first time today there appears to be real movement forward on the creation of a bipartisan commission to investigate the seeds in the Capitol. Something that like the 911 Commission in the Iraq study group. More than four months after the January 6th riots.\par \par \uc2 House Homeland Security Committee Chairman Bennie Thompson and the ranking member on Homeland Security, John Katko who voted to impeach the president, by the way, announced today they reached an agreement on a bill that would allow for the formation of a group of 10 folks to help investigate the events of January 6th and the security failures and the days leading up to it.\par \par \uc2 The announcement comes just days after some House Republicans minimize defended and even denied some of the basic facts of what happened on January 6yh. And also comes in the same day that House Republicans elected Congresswoman Elise Stefanik to replace Liz Cheney as the number three in the House leadership because of Cheney`s criticism of Trump`s election lies that of course led to the January 6th riots.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by the lead negotiator in the steel. The Democrats` Chairman of the House Homeland Security Committee, Mississippi Congressman Bennie Thompson. So, Congressman, first of all walk me through who is it -- it`s a 10-person commission, five appointed by Republicans, five appointed by Democrats, who is eligible to be on this commission and who is not eligible to be on this commission?\par \par \uc2 REP. BENNIE THOMPSON (D-MS): Well, when I`ll make it easy. No sitting member of Congress is eligible to participate on the commission. We want to look at law enforcement, civil rights. other individuals who might have an interest in what went on January 6th but specifically no member of Congress can sit.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And their subpoena power, it is -- it is what happens when there is a deadlock on a subpoena, you know, if it`s either five-five or the chairman and the vice chairman can agree.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Well, you know, it`s a shared responsibility. It`s a bipartisan commission. I`m anticipating very little challenges with respect to getting access to information. I`ve personally supported the foundation of the commission. And I think every other chairperson or agency here in this administration would be amenable to providing whatever information that we deem necessary, the commission might need.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, you guys have come to this agreement. Are you -- this now gets written as legislation and just brought to the floor or will this still need to be haggled with a bit?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Well, we go to rules on Tuesday morning with the bail.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Got it.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: And we hope to get a favorable ruling from rooms and go straight to the floor. And ultimately, by the close of business on Thursday, we will have done our job in the House and then it`s up to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m sure you`ve seen Kevin McCarthy`s initial reaction to the announcement that you and Congressman Katko came to a deal. He said he hasn`t signed off on it yet. And he added that it was very concerning to him. That it had been -- that some parts of the agreement, you know, with the scope what`s in is broad?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Well, we have been negotiating in good faith since mid-January back and forth. People have exchanged concerns to Congressman Katko and myself and to the extent we could -- we met people where they want it to be. I hope that minority leader will come forth and support it. It`s a good document. It`s a good piece of legislation. We have to have the men and women who put their lives on the line on January 6th deserve the best protection.\par \par \uc2 The public, who come to Washington and want to see the citadel of democracy, deserve to feel safe. So this is our opportunity to identify the shortcomings and recommend from the standpoint of a final product, what we can do to ensure that that is in fact the case.\par \par \uc2 TODD: At the issue of scope, what do you believe this commission will not being investigated?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Well, we want to look at the air -- the incident around January 6th. Chuck, we could go and go back two years, three years. But what happened on January 6th is an affront to our democracy. And with some exception in majority people in this country don`t want to ever see that, again. It`s the challenge for this commission to come with recommendations to protect the Capitol, protect the workers, so that it won`t happen.\par \par \uc2 But also, we still have to stand for principles and integrity. And so, that`s why this bipartisan commission, composed of experts in various areas, will get us a document that we hope to codify and make laws, just like we did with the 9/11 commission. They made certain recommendations and those recommendations, I`m proud to say, became law in time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you have an idea of some folks that will recommend a Speaker Pelosi and Majority Leader Schumer and you maybe you have recommendations on the Republican side, too. But, you know, I know it`s experts in specific -- when you look at a chair --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: There is no shortage --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you thinking something like -- yes. Leon Panetta, Bob Gates, people like that?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Well, there are no shortage of people making contact already. Actually nominating themselves to serve so that will not be a problem.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s very Washington.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: I think there will be some -- I think it will be diverse as well as competent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you`ve seen the attempt to change what happened, right, and we have this narrative attempt. I mean, we saw it on Wednesday in the Oversight Committee. I know one in your committee. You know, how much -- to me, is it a coincidence that after that gaslighting session that many House Republicans participated in that suddenly you and Congressman Katko were able to come to an agreement?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Well, I would like to say that that got us to that point. But we were just about at this point where we are now. It`s unfortunate that individuals are actually recreating what occurred on January 6th to basically infer that what people saw with their own eyes did not occur. And that`s unfortunate. But we think that this bipartisan commission will look at it, will come back with sound recommendations that the entire Congress can look at.\par \par \uc2 Again, we take pride in the fact that this is a bipartisan product. It has taken us from mid-January to this point to get to where we are. We now have until December 31 for this commission, subject to approval by the Senate, to do its work.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: We anticipate movement on that. And so I`m -- which we heard yesterday that this was kind of like a tour of the Capitol. Most people know that that`s not the case. So I think you`ll have competent people looking at this, making a good decision on behalf of the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, these commissions can do good work. 9/11 Commission, Iraq Study Group, both accomplished a lot and helped a lot of folks at least figure out some of the things that we didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Bennie Thompson, good luck to you. Hopefully this all goes as we are hoping it goes. So thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the latest on the violence escalating in Israel and Gaza. Can anything or anyone keep this from becoming a full-scale war? We have a live report from Tel Aviv next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We want to turn now to the crisis in the Middle East, a crisis at the U.N. now fears could be on the brink of a deteriorating to the point of an all-out war. The violence has only escalated at the last 24 hours. Overnight, Israel stepped up its assault on Gaza. 160 strikes that were intended they say to destroy a network of tunnels used by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . The Israeli military has also called up 9,000 reservists and positioned additional troops along its border with Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , meanwhile, has continued its attack on Israel launching some 1800 missiles this week. And for the first time in this conflict, rockets were fired at Israel from Lebanon last night, though no one was injured by those rockets which fell off the coast. Violence this week has already killed at least 119 Palestinians and eight Israelis.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now from Tel Aviv is NBC News chief foreign correspondent Richard Engel.\par \par \uc2 And Richard, there are so many reasons why this is an even -- you can`t -- there`s always a bad time for violence in the Middle East, but when you look at the political situations whether it`s among the Palestinians where essentially Abbas and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  don`t speak and obviously the political situation in Israel. Is there any small G governor here that can sort of tap things, tap the brakes, and reel this in?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes, there are a lot of reasons why both the Israelis and the Palestinians don`t want to escalate this and might be looking for an off ramp. First on the Palestinian side, the Palestinian issue has not been on the agenda for the last several years. President Trump was a close supporter of Israel and gave Israel almost all of its demands, moving the U.S. embassy to Jerusalem, recognizing the Golan Heights, making settlements more legitimate.\par \par \uc2 And the Palestinian issue fell off. It fell off to the point that Israel made a peace deal with the United Arab Emirates. So the Palestinians felt that their cause had been forgotten, but they won`t -- are determined, determined right now to put it back on the international agenda. They may have succeeded in doing that. And they don`t want to see Gaza destroyed. And Gaza is being destroyed now but it would be much more destroyed if there was a ground invasion.\par \par \uc2 On the Israeli side, Israel was surprised that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  had been able to stockpile build so many rockets over the last several years. Rockets that it`s been firing in enormous salvos. It has been destroying some of them and according to Israeli officials will continue to target those rockets. But there is a risk for Israel, too, of taking this too far because it has that peace agreement with the UAE, because we`re starting to see divisions within Israeli society, within Arabs and Jews who are all Israeli citizens fighting against each other in militia groups.\par \par \uc2 That is a serious challenge to the fabric of Israeli society. So if the prime minister here and the military decide that they have done enough to set back Hamas` weapons program and its rockets program, you could see both sides feeling that they have achieved their goals with the Palestinians putting the issue back on the agenda, saying they have not been forgotten, will not be forgotten, and Netanyahu walking away claiming victory as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And what have you learned about the rockets that came from Lebanon? If it was \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , would they have already taken credit?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: The Israelis are talking a lot about this situation. I was in Gaza in 2006. The 2006 war started actually in a situation almost exactly like this. There was an escalation between Gaza and Israel. That`s why I was in Gaza. And then a new front opened up in Lebanon. And \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  started firing rockets into northern Israel and the Israelis ended up invading Lebanon, and it became a major, very, very costly war.\par \par \uc2 There are reasons that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  wouldn`t want to do that right now. Beirut has been suffering tremendously after that fertilizer explosion that tore through the city. It`s having a currency crisis. So it might not want to open up a destructive war with Israel right now as the country has been going through such domestic hardship. But it`s possible.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, yes. Yes, more evidence that it`s more likely, probably was not \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and maybe just have been some rogue militants.\par \par \uc2 Richard, on the ground there. Richard, thank you. Be safe.\par \par \uc2 After the break, I`m going to be joined by the former point man on the Middle East peace for both presidents H.W. Bush and Bill Clinton. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Again a little more context. And we`re back. We want to put a little more context around the escalating conflict in the Middle East so joining me now is Ambassador Dennis Ross. He`s of course a former top U.S. adviser on all things in the Middle East. He has served as a special assistant to multiple presidents in both parties, Presidents Obama and Clinton as well as Presidents Bush, I believe both of them in different ways.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador Ross, it`s always good to catch up with you. So this situation - - you heard Richard Engel`s take. Do you accept his -- that this is going to be one of those moments where while there`s fear it could get worse, there are -- there`s too many reasons why both sides actually want to deescalate and deescalate fast? Do you buy that?\par \par \uc2 AMB. DENNIS ROSS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL FORMER SENIOR DIRECTOR: I buy it only in part. I buy it that it`s true that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has in a sense demonstrated its, quote, "the defender of Jerusalem" and it`s seizing the mantle of leadership from Abbas having scored those points better stop things now before they lose even more in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 I buy it that Israel understands that if it actually has to go in on the ground into Gaza, the price would be very high, and it`s not exactly if you decapitate \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , what are you left with, you kind of own Gaza then? Is that really what you want?\par \par \uc2 So I think there`s -- it`s true that on each side, neither sees great benefit in having this go on for a long time. But there`s also a problem at the same point. And the reason I say it is, from an Israeli standpoint, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  basically broke the rules of the game. They shot rockets at Jerusalem. They tried to establish, in a sense, new rules of the game. And Israel has to re-establish its deterrent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROSS: Now to re-establish its deterrent, it has to inflict a high enough price as \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders measure that price for this to be effective. Now this isn`t a science. And the question is, do the Israelis have both a clear definition and is that definition right? And hoes \uc2 Hamas\uc2  decide that this is the point to stop or not? So I think in my mind I think there`s a potential to bring this to an end soon. But I also see a lot of uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do we have the same amount of influence on Israel as we have? And do we have -- and we obviously, since we don`t speak to \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , we only speak to Abbas, we have zero on the Palestinian side. So we`re sending an envoy. What would be -- you`ve been in a similar situation. What would be your message to the Israelis in trying to get -- and convince them to deescalate here?\par \par \uc2 ROSS: My message to them would be, let`s talk specifically about what you think you have to be able to achieve to have deterrents. And it`s very much in your interest not to create such an elastic definition that you can never achieve it because then you`re trapped. And you really don`t want to have to go in on the ground. So let`s have a pretty clear definition in your own mind what this is. I would be working very closely with the Egyptians right now.\par \par \uc2 The only ones who have real leverage on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  right now are the Egyptians because Gaza has two borders, one with Israel and one with Egypt. So Egypt actually has a lot of leverage. They have been the ones who brokered the last four ceasefires when there were major conflicts going back to 2012. And, you know, they`re in a position where they can go to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and they can say, look, we can use our influence with the Israelis as well. But if you`re not responsive to us, you know, don`t expect us to save you from the Israelis.\par \par \uc2 And don`t expect us to open the border. You want us to be more responsive, we can be more responsive. But here`s what we need to see from you. So I would use the Egyptians right now because I think they`re a pivotal player in this. And I think they have an interest in showing the Biden administration the value of the relationship.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. But what about -- I mean, look, I got to ask you about the current political situation in Israel. You`ve got a sort of a lame duck prime minister who could have been on the verge of being deposed literally, you know, two days before this happened. There seemed to be some momentum. Now that`s done. There`s going to be another election. How concerned are you that Bibi Netanyahu sees a lot of political upside in what`s happened this week?\par \par \uc2 ROSS: I think, look, it`s a fact of life that politically his position is better today than it was a couple of days ago. On Sunday, it looked very much like there was going to be an alternative government probably sworn in by the end of the week or at least this coming weekend. That`s not going to be the case now. Having said that, he`s already in a different position because of it. And he also has to be careful not to let this go too far.\par \par \uc2 Indeed if in the end, if the pressure remains on Tel Aviv, Ben Gurion remains shut down, it will be very difficult to resist the impulse to send Israeli forces into Gaza. And then the price will be very high and the gains won`t be so clear. So I think he`s quite mindful that he needs to find a point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 ROSS: Where, as I said, Israel`s deterrents has been reestablished, but also it doesn`t become trapped in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ambassador Dennis Ross, I really appreciate you coming on and getting your perspective and expertise on this. I just think we`re all hoping in de-escalates quicker than so far it appears.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, sir. Much appreciated.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a possible political power shift as both parties are fighting to become the party of millennials.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. If it`s Friday it means we have a new episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." And it`s on demand right now on Peacock. This week, we took a deep dive into the biggest voting bloc in the country now. Not the baby boomers, it`s millennials. There are more millennials of voting age than any other generation.\par \par \uc2 Sorry, boomer. And how they view politics will change the course of culture politics and so many institutions. Now as the oldest millennials turned 40, and they start to make their way into the highest reaches of government, we look at how they will leave their marks, so for this episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS," my colleague Savannah Sellers talked to millennial politicians and voters. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH SELLERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well before the 2020 election, millennial representatives started becoming household names as they harness the power of social media. Now one of the first millennial White House contenders has become the first millennial Cabinet member. And the nation`s first senator born after 1980 has won a seat in Congress.\par \par \uc2 There are even enough millennial reps on Capitol Hill, 31 of them, that they formed their own caucus focused on the future. Representative Sarah Jacobs co-chairs it. Susan Davis finished out her last term at age 76. Congresswoman Jacobs is 32.\par \par \uc2 REP. SARA JACOBS (D-CA): Our generation has a very different perspective on things. We graduated college in the middle of that great recession. We`re now experiencing the second huge economic crisis in our adult life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Savannah, we wanted to do this because millennials are technically the largest voting generation, but they`ve yet to be the largest actual group of voters yet, but we know they`re about to be. Democrats, Republicans or none of the above have the best shot at becoming the party of millennials?\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: You know what, Chuck, actually, one of the most fascinating things that we found out is that they are members of all parties. In fact, there is somebody in our piece who is a GenZeer, the generation below younger. He`s the youngest Iowa state representative, Carter Nordman. He`s a Republican, but the really fascinating thing about millennials here is that he agrees with a lot of what his Democratic counterparts say because millennials have been forged by these shared crises.\par \par \uc2 You just heard Representative Sara Jacobs there mentioned the recession. She brought up 9/11. One of the big ones that they all agree is a problem that needs to be dealt with, for example, is climate change. So these shared crises have kind of made them able to reach across the aisle. They want to find some common ground, and I think that`s going to be one of the most interesting things we see as they come up into these positions more and more and get elected to them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, you know what, the greatest generation had the war veterans who were from both sides of the aisle who commonly got together to get things done in the `60s, `70s and `80s. So you may be onto to something there.\par \par \uc2 Savannah, there`s a lot more to watch here. Great work by you.\par \par \uc2 SELLERS: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Please take a look. This is a terrific episode just like all of our "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." You can watch the full thing streaming on Peacock right now.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us this hour. We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And if it`s Sunday, it`s MEET THE PRESS on your local NBC station. I have a packed show for you on Sunday because it`s been one heck of a week.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage, though, continues after with Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210514eh5e0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 12, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Mark Stone, Kasie Hunt, Sahil Kapur, Carlos Curbelo,  Mike Memoli \par \uc2 8,334 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The affordability is more difficult. Those are the things I want to discuss and correct and also the border. All right, last question.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you concern about your future in leadership giving you protected Cheney for so long and miss further calls to (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You`re not concerned at all.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: No. I`m looking forward to being speaker in the next Congress. Thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Leader McCarthy, is there room in the Republican Party for voters who don`t back Trump?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to Wednesday. You`ve just been watching Kevin McCarthy and Mitch McConnell at a pool spray there outside of the White House, outside the West Wing there. It`s obviously been a very busy Wednesday. It`s been an extraordinarily busy Wednesday for Kevin McCarthy in particular.\par \par \uc2 I think probably the most newsworthy non-infrastructure part of the conversation in the remarks is we heard McCarthy claim that his caucus in ousting Liz Cheney is not questioning the legitimacy of the election. We actually heard him say no, in fact, we just met with the President. So, I`d be curious to think what the former President thinks of Kevin McCarthy`s choice of words there. In fact, we will play that clip to get the exact quote for you as soon as we have it recut.\par \par \uc2 We are expecting also to hear from Speaker Pelosi and Chuck Schumer to hear their version of the meeting and what came out of it. Look, all of this comes after House Republicans met this morning quite quickly and ousted Liz Cheney from their leadership. They did it by a voice vote. They seem to do it as if they were just having a coffee break.\par \par \uc2 You didn`t hear many speeches or anything like that. But they ousted him -- ousted her simply due to her criticisms of the party`s embrace of Trump`s lies about the 2020 election. Biden`s first in person meeting with Mitch McConnell and Kevin McCarthy comes as Republicans seem to have surrendered to Trump and his lie that Biden`s election was illegitimate even as Kevin McCarthy tried to claim at the White House that though he did meet with the legitimate president.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are framing the ousting of Cheney today as an internal squabble in leadership. That was it about messaging, that it`s not about something bigger. Well, we think it is about something much bigger. It is alarming. It is an alarming giant flashing red warning siren that a major political party in this country is willing to embrace or ignore a problem that is an existential threat to this country`s democracy. And that they will punish or cancel, to borrow a term that they like to use, someone like Liz Cheney, because she is willing to acknowledge that this is a problem in public.\par \par \uc2 The lie that the election was stolen from Trump didn`t just fuel Cheney`s ouster from leadership, it also fueled a riot which put every member of Congress, Republicans included, in very real danger. In fact, there are more hearings right now taking place on Capitol Hill seeking to understand the full extent of the dangers from that January 6, riot. We have a former defense secretary claiming fear of the media as to why he didn`t act sooner.\par \par \uc2 But in these hearings, some Republicans have tried to absolve Trump for what happened. Some are even trying to paint Trump supporters as the true victims of this incident.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Liz Cheney warned about the direction of her party when she spoke to reporters following the vote to remove her from leadership.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): We must go forward based on truth. We cannot both embrace the big lie and embrace the constitution. And going forward, the nation needs it, the nation needs a strong Republican Party. The nation needs a party that that is based upon fundamental principles of conservatism.\par \par \uc2 And I am committed and dedicated to ensuring that that`s how this party goes forward. And I plan to lead the fight to do that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Congresswoman, how concerned are you that former President Trump might end up back in the Oval Office and what are you prepared to do to prevent it?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I will do everything I can to ensure that the former president never again gets anywhere near the Oval Office. We have seen the danger that he continues to provoke with his language. We have seen his lack of commitment and dedication to the Constitution. And I think it`s very important that we make sure whomever we elect is somebody who will be faithful to the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And after those remarks, Congresswoman Cheney sat down with my NBC News colleague Savannah Guthrie for more. Here`s a quick clip from that sit-down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, MSNBC Are you the leader of the opposition in exile right now in the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I intend to be the leader -- one of the leaders in a fight to help to restore our party, and a fight to bring our party back to substance and principles, and in a fight to make clear that we won`t participate in a really dangerous effort that`s underway.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: A lot of people frame this as a battle for the soul of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: This is the, I think, opening salvo in that battle. And it`s a battle we have to win because it`s not just about the Republican Party, it`s about the country.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The Trump political team is actively looking to coalesce around a primary challenger to you. What is your message to them?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: You know, bring it on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can see a lot more on Savannah`s interview with Congresswoman Liz Cheney tomorrow morning on Today. So, where do things stand? Joining us now with the latest in Capitol Hill are my NBC News colleagues, Kasie Hunt and Sahil Kapur. Also with us, former Republican member of Congress Carlos Curbelo, he`s an MSNBC Political Analyst, and Michael Steele, a former adviser to Jeb Bush and John Boehner.\par \par \uc2 And again, we now have -- I want to play Kevin McCarthy`s quote from the White House stakeout cameras, because it may come as a surprise to somebody down at Mar-a-Lago. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I don`t think anybody is questioning the legitimacy of the presidential election. I think that is all over with. We`re sitting here with the president today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Kasie Hunt, here we go again. Every time Kevin McCarthy has tried to straddle the fence of the wackadoodle wing of the party that`s trying to claim something crazy happen and the rational world of reality, he usually gets shut down. How is a comment like that going to play in the current makeup of the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 KASIE HUNT, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think it`s a good question, Chuck, because I think what you saw from Kevin McCarthy today was all about him wanting to be Speaker of the House in 2022. And there are going to be two prerequisites for that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He said it.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: One, they obviously have to win the seats. I know. He said it at the end. They`re going to have to win the seats they need. But he`s also going to need former President Trump to be on his side because no matter what happens, when we`ve seen the right-wing of this parties stay loyal to Donald Trump through thick and thin for years now, you can`t tell me that if Trump decides that he doesn`t think that Kevin McCarthy should be the right Speaker of the House in a Republican majority -- if he says that, I don`t see how Kevin McCarthy then has the votes to become Speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 And he also created some problems for himself today with people who, originally, when McCarthy first came to the House, were naturally allied with him. That would include a Congressman Adam Kinzinger who tweeted about the speaker -- potential speaker vote today and said, you know, he`s not -- I`m not going to vote for Kevin McCarthy for speaker or at least Kevin McCarthy shouldn`t assume that I will do that if we win. So, that`s a pretty remarkable dynamic.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, McCarthy obviously has watched John Boehner go down in the face of Tea Party activism and hardliners. He watched Paul Ryan bow out after trying to deal with Trump. And he thought that he had successfully co-opted the right-wing of his party, get them on board. That has always been sort of his main worry. He didn`t have to worry so much more about these moderates who have to win in tough districts.\par \par \uc2 And so, that calculus, I think, is now scrambled because of the way Donald Trump is wielding power in this party. I mean, it`s strictly on the basis of fear, right? It`s not as though Donald Trump has more levers than that. All he has are threats to deploy himself against people, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Sahil, it was so anticlimactic today. And they did it with such speed, no recorded vote. It`s sort of -- it`s almost as if they wanted to hurry up and get this behind them as if they`re embarrassed for what they`re doing. They`re either embarrassed that they`re doing it or they`re embarrassed that they have to do it or for whatever reason, but nobody seems to be wanting to celebrate this vote, which I think tells you they know deep down what they`re doing here is probably not a good thing.\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: They didn`t want to go on the record, Chuck, and that surprised some members of the House Republican Conference, including Adam Kinzinger, who said, Kevin got up, he said a few words, and then there was the vote, and it happened within seconds.\par \par \uc2 It also shows that some of the republicans who privately, secretly support Liz Cheney, probably voted for her a few months ago to keep her in leadership do not feel that way anymore. And they are more content going in the other direction, in large part because they are caught at home having to answer questions between the truth of what happened in the presidential election, which is that Donald Trump lost and many Republican voters, passionate supporters of the former president who believe his falsehoods and lies about a stolen 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 That`s where the, you know, the tide really turned within the House Republican Conference. The median lawmaker was willing to keep Cheney in office, but now it`s made life complicated for them that she keeps going after the president. She keeps debunking his lies. She`s refusing to look the other way on those and that`s why she had to go.\par \par \uc2 And it`s a very momentous development in American history, Chuck, to see one major political party ousting a leader simply for accepting the legitimacy of an election that her party lost. That is not something that`s going to be easily forgotten in the tide of history.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Carlos Curbelo, I got to read you this quote from a freshman from Florida, Byron Donalds. This is what he put to reporters. "Let me posit to you this way. If you had a member of the Democratic leadership who said they don`t believe in climate change anymore, would they stay in leadership? I don`t think so."\par \par \uc2 I have to tell you. Of all things to pick, right, climate change, which of course climate change denialism is something that is -- that is arguably an issue on the -- on the right, and he uses that. I mean, denialism about Trump`s loss does to me -- that is a pretty good comparison to those that don`t think climate change exists. But of all metaphors to use, basically because there`s groupthink you can`t have Liz Cheney around.\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Chuck, the problem with that statement or criticism is that obviously, policy doesn`t matter in the Republican Party right now, at least not on the House Republican conference. This is not about policy. This is about loyalty to one person and to the lies that he is telling.\par \par \uc2 If this were about policy and conservative litmus tests, Liz Cheney would be in great shape. She is an extremely conservative member of the House. A lot of groups out there like Heritage and Club for Growth and other conservative groups are not happy that she might be replaced with someone who has a more moderate record.\par \par \uc2 So, the Republican Party in the House, at least, is sending a couple of dangerous messages today. Number one is that any Republican or Republican- leaning independent who isn`t loyal to Donald Trump, who doesn`t believe everything he says, is not welcome in the party, not a good message ahead of 2022 when you want to grow the tent and invite people in and help you win back doors in the House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 And then the other message is that policy really doesn`t matter. It doesn`t matter what you think about taxes or abortion or energy. What matters is if you tolerate Donald Trump and his lies or not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Michael Steele, I got to read you this one. This is Glenn Grothman. He said he voted to outs Cheney. He said this. "I felt she was in part responsible for the high spending in the first Trump budget." Are we supposed to accept that rationale with a straight face?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEEL, FORMER SENIOR ADVISER TO JOHN BOEHNER: That`s not an argument I would have made. I mean, I think House Republicans, not just Kevin McCarthy, want to win the majority next year. They are 100 percent focused on that.\par \par \uc2 They hope that this effort today turns the page, clears the decks, allows them to focus on the stuff that you just heard Leader McCarthy talking about, inflation, the crisis of the border, the Middle East, infrastructure, all the things that they feel like they haven`t been able to talk about, haven`t been able to communicate about effectively because they keep re-litigating January 6 and President Trump`s role.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Michael Steel is somebody who was with Boehner trying to wrangle the Trump candidacy with Jeb Bush trying to duel with the Trump candidacy. You`ve seen what happens. When you appease Donald Trump, he always asked for more. Why do House Republicans somehow think that Donald Trump is going to go silent now, that he`s satisfied, he`s been appeased, he`s not going to come asking for more?\par \par \uc2 STEEL: Well, I think it`s less about the former president and more about his supporters. If I felt we lived in a universe where tens of millions of voters were going to wake up tomorrow morning, like the old VA dads dumped themselves on the head and say, what was I thinking, Trump was wrong all along, that would be great. But that universe doesn`t exist. That`s not the universe that we exist in.\par \par \uc2 And so, a durable majority, a solid majority for House Republicans is going to require both strong supporters of President from and people who are repelled by his actions in office. You got to put that coalition together. I think that`s exactly what Kevin McCarthy is trying to do right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Kasie Hunt, you talked about how, in many ways, this is Kevin McCarthy, we`ll find out. Is he learning all the lessons for why he didn`t become speaker the first time? Is he learning the wrong lessons, right? We`re going to -- we`re going to have a definitive answer and that in `22.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you this. Donald Trump decided to make it a crusade to get rid of Liz Cheney. The second Republican he trashes the most effortless Cheney is Mitch McConnell. I go back to a version of the question I just asked Michael Steel. Whenever Trump`s been appeased, whenever the Republican Party has done one of these, you know, ritualistic sacrifices, you know, OK, you want us to trash the Bushes, we`ll do that. You need us to trash the McCains, you needed to trash the Romneys, well do that. Trump always asked for more. Is McConnell safe?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Well, Chuck, I think McConnell is very good and very practiced at keeping his head down. I think he has a different set of interests and ambitions and a different type of profile than Liz Cheney has. His approval rating has been on the uptick. You`re right to point out that Donald Trump criticized him heavily. But in the -- in the meantime, since he has not been out front on attacking the former president, those numbers have ticked up slowly.\par \par \uc2 He also -- you know, he just won another reelection term and six years is a real lifetime in politics. It`s possibly even McConnell`s last term here. So, I think the dynamics are completely different. And I know that we focused a lot on the fist bump between President Biden and Liz Cheney at the non-State of the Union, Joint address to Congress.\par \par \uc2 But I actually thought one thing that I saw that was really interesting that I don`t think was caught on all the main feeds was Mitch McConnell walking down that aisle, Liz Cheney was standing on the side. He pointed at her and then he reached out and he shook her hand.\par \par \uc2 And it was very clear that the two of them were sharing a moment of connection at a time when Cheney and McCarthy who were seated in the same row and spent a long time on that -- on that house floor together while I was sitting in the gallery to watch, didn`t speak to each other at all.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that we know where McConnell stands privately. I think he made it pretty clear in his speech after the impeachment trial. I think Donald Trump may continue to go after him. I`m not sure necessarily that Mitch McConnell`s team cares very much. We`ll see if it starts to affect their finances. I think that`s one place where perhaps you may see some cracks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. You know, Sahil, given what the House Republicans did, we know that a lot of congressional Democrats don`t have a lot of interest in working with many of these Republicans right now. But Joe Biden does. He hosted Kevin McCarthy and Mitch McConnell today. Some of it may be symbolic, but there obviously is some interest in working together.\par \par \uc2 I am curious, is there going to be tension between the Congressional Democratic leadership and the Biden White House over how much to work with these -- with this version of the GOP?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Yes, there is, Chuck. And that is with or without the Liz Cheney situation. It`s not as though House Republicans would be, you know, chomping at the bit to cut a deal with the Biden White House if Liz Cheney was still on leadership. I mean, she has a more ideologically conservative record than Elise Stefanik, the woman who`s being tapped to replace her.\par \par \uc2 But to your point, yes, there is going to be tension. And there already is tension within the Democratic Party and between them in the White House, you know, with progressives on one hand, same cut the rope and let`s move to reconciliation because Republicans are not going to be serious about pursuing any kind of an infrastructure package of the size that they believe is necessary.\par \par \uc2 But the White House is doing this. It`s holding these bipartisan meetings. It`s pursuing this approach, because there are moderate Democrats who insist on trying, and some progressive activists are -- let President Biden, in the words of one, get caught trying to work with Republicans before he goes it alone, kind of like he did on COVID, but they need to give more rope this time.\par \par \uc2 The White House believes to the idea of bipartisanship because infrastructure is not the same kind of emergency as the COVID-19 pandemic. People aren`t dying every day, so they want to do this. But they also have a deadline in mind. Not a specific deadline, but they don`t want to let this run out, you know, for months and months, kind of like they did with the ACA in 2009. Those lessons are very fresh in the minds of Democrats, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Carlos, I`m going to pivot to, you know, there was a letter out today from a lot of longtime Republicans, most of whom are not in office anymore, talking about starting a third party or basically trying to -- trying to compete for the for the soul of sort of the conservative movement, if you will. Where do you think that stands? And is Liz Cheney the right leader of this movement?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Well, Chuck, I think it`d be very difficult to establish a third party in this country. I mean, people have tried it before. It`s an uphill battle for sure. I think that it probably makes more sense to try to have this fight within the Republican Party and just save the party from lies and dishonesty and everything we`ve seen over the last few weeks.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know if Liz Cheney is the perfect figurehead for this kind of party. I think you would really need a new, fresh person who doesn`t have all the baggage from the past of the party, and that includes the Iraq war, and other issues that really turn off younger generations of voters.\par \par \uc2 So, I think if the -- if people really did want to split from the Republican Party and have a chance at succeeding, it would need to be a more libertarian party that appealed to younger voters. And that could also win over some of those college-educated types, suburban types who have been turned off by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But without a doubt, Chuck, today, House Republican leadership certainly fed that kind of movement by sending the message that if you`re not willing to go along with Trump, there`s no place for you here. That`s the message that`s going to get through to most Americans and that`s not a good message as you`re trying to recover from what was a difficult election cycle.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Michael Steel, speak to the issue that I think a lot of Republicans I talked to have, which is they want to move past Trump. They want to -- they want to sort of get back to the old version, but they don`t want to -- they don`t want to get there by having to confront the Trump stuff. I don`t know if that`s possible. But speak to that tension.\par \par \uc2 I think you deal with a lot of Republicans who I think are in this sort of mushy middle when it comes to Trump. They`re going ahead and appeasing for now, but they wish they couldn`t.\par \par \uc2 STEEL: I mean, I think there`s a strong desire for a return to normalcy among all Americans, including Republicans. I think that we`ve found over and over again, that there will be no defining moment, no great -- one great clash where Trumpism is defeated, where the old Republican Party springs back to life.\par \par \uc2 Instead, it`s a steady, incremental effort to build on the base that Trump has so solidly cemented, and add other voters who are repelled by the former president until we get a coalition that can actually win national elections, win the majority in the House in the Senate. And that`s -- we`re not going back, right? We`re building a new party based on what we`ve seen over the past five years and also the knowledge that the coalition that Trump put together is insufficient to win elections going forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But you`re comfortable starting the foundation of the party on something that isn`t true. I mean, that -- it is right now the energy of the party is about something that is -- that is a lie. Like, is that a way to build a party? Michael?\par \par \uc2 STEEL: No one should ever argue with the fact that President Trump lost the Presidential Election. He pursued a campaign of misinformation that in part led to the horrible events of January 6th. At the same time, we need his supporters in order to build an enduring majority. And so, I was -- I loved hearing Kevin McCarthy just say that Joe Biden is the legitimate President of the United States. I think it needs to be said over and over and over again. And I think that that`s the foundation that we can build on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kasie Hunt, Sahil Kapur, Carlos Curbelo, Michael Steel, four of the smartest Capitol Hill folks I can think of having right now. A terrific way to open this up. Thank you guys. I much appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`ve got more on President Biden`s first meeting with the so- called Big Four of Congress. It`s McConnell, and McCarthy, Pelosi, and Schumer, all in the Oval Office together. In fact, we expect to hear from Pelosi and Schumer at the stakeout cameras at the White House in a few minutes. When we do, we`ll get you there.\par \par \uc2 But we`ll get the latest in the meeting as well and what he`s heard from Democratic House Majority Leader, the number two man in the -- in the House, Steny Hoyer. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 And later, new fears of an all-out war as violence escalates between Israeli forces and Palestinian militant. We`ll get a live report from Jerusalem just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to talk a lot about infrastructure today to see if there`s any way we can reach a compromise that gets to people`s work done and is within the bounds of everyone agreeing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How do you expect to do that, sir. How do you expect --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Easy. With just the snap of my fingers, it will happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That`s what we heard ahead of the first Oval Office meeting between President Biden and the Big Four congressional leaders this morning, happening after the first 100 days. That meeting wrapped up about an hour -- an hour and a half ago.\par \par \uc2 Just moments ago, we heard Republican leaders McConnell McCarthy speak after the meeting. And we`ve just gotten some comments from the Democratic leaders Pelosi and Schumer. They all seem to agree there is bipartisan desire to get something done and infrastructure, though the two sides differ on what an infrastructure package should contain and what the definition of infrastructure even is. McConnell also said the Republicans are unwilling to revisit any part of the 2017 tax reform package.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now with more of the meeting is NBC`s Mike Memoli. So, Mike, obviously the awkward timing, right? McCarthy goes from one meeting to that meeting. And certainly, it`s almost like he`s walking with a cloud, as he pretend, the only thing we talked about was infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 But I want to get at something that I talked about before, which is, I know I`m sensing, in my own reporting indicating some tension between where the White House is on negotiations with Republicans, and where congressional Democrats are.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I sense very little desire of Congressional Democratic leadership to go down this road except they don`t have Joe Manchin`s vote yet, so they kind of have to pretend to entertain it. But the Biden folks seem a little more serious about bipartisanship, is that fair?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Chuck, they absolutely are. And this is the quandary for House Democrats, which is there`s nothing that`s going to pass unless you have Joe Manchin`s vote, Kyrsten Sinema`s vote, and that`s why the President among many meetings this week, also met with those two Democratic senators.\par \par \uc2 And I think the democrats are also in the House anticipating the traditional midterm counter to the President`s party, and looking at everything a little bit nervously, while the White House assures them that it has a strategy here that would lift all tides.\par \par \uc2 And you talked about the awkwardness of the timing for Kevin McCarthy. I think there`s no better illustration, in fact, of what complicates President Biden`s interest in bipartisanship. He taught -- having covered him for as long as I`ve had, he`s always described his negotiating strategy as to sort of put himself in the other person`s shoes, understand their political internal limitations, and try to get them to move as far as they can without asking them to go further than they`re able to.\par \par \uc2 There is no clear understanding, and the President has made several comments in recent weeks to say this publicly of whether the Republicans know, their internal limitations are. And so, I think that`s why as interested in the -- as the White House is in reaching some compromise, it`s really difficult to do that when Republicans are still taking their cues from potentially former President Trump, but really, there is no leadership clearly in that party.\par \par \uc2 But that being said, it was interesting as Biden talks about politics as the art of the possible, you did hear Mitch McConnell, again, as you say, reiterate his red line which is revisiting as he puts it, the 2017 tax law. But he also talked about something I`ve heard behind the scenes as well, which is all the money that was in the COVID relief law for state and local governments, which as it turns out, some of these states we saw with California yesterday, may night need as badly as president thought that they might.\par \par \uc2 And so McConnell hinted at potentially reprogramming some of that money to be used for infrastructure. Maybe that`s the way forward for a more narrow package. But a lot of Democrats don`t want a smaller narrow package. They want to go big and they hope that they can get Manchin and Sinema to go along with that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say though, the current economic environment, I think, certainly pushes back against the progressive wing of the party, right? You`ve got inflation concerns, and this is very likely more supply chain issues than an overheated economy, though, I think, no doubt I know everybody is looking for their spin for their point of view here. But you have a lot of Democrats nervous about voting for tax increases. Other Democrats nervous about what we`re seeing, you know, is too much money being thrown at here?\par \par \uc2 I mean, is the current economic situation actually playing into the advantage of the Biden White House having more flexibility and negotiations?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Well, it`s difficult to see how this plays out because in part what the timeline the White House has offered is, I mean, if the White House, when we see the inflationary numbers today is urging us to look at the broader view of the same, that they said Friday with the jobs report talking about monthly averages rather than one specific report.\par \par \uc2 But the White House has been pretty clear in terms of their preferred timeline which is Memorial Day for progress, quote-unquote, whatever progress means, and legislation passed by July. This is a White House that knows what happens if you extend that timeline further down the road. You ultimately may end up with nothing, and so they may have to make a decision very soon. Memorial Day being just two and a half weeks from now already.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I`ll tell you, they need to get their arms around the supply chain issue because that is having the bigger impact on the perception of inflation right now more than anything else.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Mike Memoli from the White House for us. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`re going to look at the big push for President Biden`s ambitious agenda with a view from inside the congressional Democratic leadership. It is House majority leader Steny Hoyer who will join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Amid the developments on Capitol Hill today, Speaker Nancy Pelosi as well as other House Democrats and mayors are pressing for the need for Congress to pass an infrastructure package.\par \par \uc2 This comes of course just hours after President Biden touted his infrastructure plan to congressional leaders. But it also comes after the number two House Republican Steve Scalise e-mailed his team urging them to, quote, "remain focused on stopping Speaker Pelosi and President Biden`s socialist agenda."\par \par \uc2 So with me now is House majority leader Steny Hoyer. He`s a Democrat from Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Hoyer, it`s good to see you. Let me start with getting at a question that I`ve been asking our congressional reporters. And that is, this is what it looks like to many of us observers and sort of reporting seems to bear this out, that the congressional Democratic leadership has a little less patience for working with Republicans than the Biden White House. Would you say that`s a fair critique?\par \par \uc2 REP. STENY HOYER (D-MD): I think we`ve frankly long experience of finding it very, very difficult to get to a bipartisan resolution with our Republican colleagues when you have Steve Scalise putting out what notice that he did with Senator McConnell saying he`s 100 percent focused on defeating the Biden agenda. You have to be pretty cynical in talking about bipartisanship because the Republicans do not believe it`s in their political interest in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 But it is in the country`s political interest and that`s what President Biden said today. We ought to look at ourselves not as competition with one another but in competition with the rest of the world, China in particular but others and I think if we do that, we`d have much more likely bipartisanship. But we want bipartisanship. Obviously it`s in our best interest to pass legislation. Have legislation passed that has broad support.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has said and I agree with him that what he is proposing has broad bipartisan support in the country but not in the Congress and that`s unfortunate. So I think we`re going to continue to work towards bipartisanship. Both parties have said they`re for infrastructure, both parties know that we need to make substantial investments in infrastructure. So there is a basis for bipartisan support, Chuck, and I`m hopeful that we can get there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious, you know, there was an incident earlier this week between member of the Democratic Conference Madeleine Dean and a member of the Republican side, Buddy Carter. There was a piece of legislation that they both at one time used to be -- were both co-sponsors of, and she didn`t want him to be a co-sponsor due to him not voting to certify the election. And the thing is, I`ve heard this from many members on the Democratic side, who were like, I cannot bring myselves to work with a Republican who tried to undermine the Constitution and what happened on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 How do you do a bipartisan deal then? And I say this because the Republican, the House Republicans certainly have made a decision and Senate Republicans have made a decision. And yet there`s a desire for a bipartisan outcome. Can it be done if there is this fundamental lack of -- you know, if -- and I get the feeling if one side can`t accept working with another side because they think they`re seditionists.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Look, that was a particular sentiment that a lot of our members had. And it started to spill over to what we call the suspension process. You`re talking about Madeleine Dean`s suspension bill which was about orphan drug which is overwhelmingly I supported and Carter supported it. The last congressman a principal sponsor. Madeleine Dean said to him you`re welcome to be a co-sponsor of the legislation. It wasn`t that she wanted him to be cut out of sponsorship. She just didn`t want him to be the principal sponsor. It`s her bill.\par \par \uc2 And I think they blew that way out of proportion very frankly with sentiment that we blow up in effect that one bipartisan process that we have, and that suspension bills which can pass with two-thirds vote because both sides are for them. I`ve worked very hard to try to maintain that bipartisanship on suspension bills. I hope I can do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: We`ve had some success. Last night we didn`t have success on that particular bill but hopefully will overcome that. And the answer to your question, though --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about -- yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Chuck, the answer to your question is two minutes ago, you know, if one side wants to have bipartisanship and the other doesn`t, and the other -- my view is the problem we have is I believe that Republicans and sometimes the Democrats have thought that, as well, if they believe that the success of the other side is to their detriment as opposed to being to the country`s credit, then it`s a tough time to get to bipartisanship and, you know, McConnell has said again with the Biden administration the same thing he said in the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 He was asked and Obama`s first term what`s your principal objective? And his principal objective was not to make the country better, get more jobs, and make sure people have access to health care and clean up the environment. It was to make sure that Obama didn`t have a second term. If that`s your objective, bipartisanship really is not a part of that plan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious, you`ve been around this town a long time. Mitch McConnell did something we rarely see him do after he made that comment, though. He walked it back and the next day said I`m just working to have a more moderate Biden presidency. What do you make of the walk back? I understand the comment you made and I understand the political hay you want to make out of it, but unlike what happened 10 years ago, he walked this one back. Why do you think he did?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Well, perhaps he did it because it got bad reaction. Not among his base. Their base is anywhere from 35 percent to 40 percent, but they need much more than that to win either in the House or in the Senate. and I think that may be political reasons he walked it back because it didn`t play well with the average American that they needed to get and that we need to get to win elections. And I think it was right. I`m glad that he walked it back. I hope he not only walked it back rhetorically, I hope walked it back in his heart and soul and really felt that that`s what he wants to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Final question here between the debate that`s going to happen inside the party between sort of the centrists and the progressives. Is the progressive wing of House Democrats prepared to accept the fact that the package is big as Kyrsten Sinema and Joe Manchin are willing to accept? Do progressives get that?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: I think all of us would like to see first of all the filibuster eliminated. We ought to be able to pass things by a majority vote but your point is, if you don`t have Joe Manchin or Kyrsten Sinema, you`re not going to have a majority and that is correct. I think what you`ve seen, Chuck, is a very unified Democratic Party over the last quarter and the last Congress. And we pass things in 30 major votes. We lost 29 Democrats.\par \par \uc2 In all of those 30, 15 of which were on one bill. That was interesting, that was the waiver for a Secretary Austin because he hadn`t been out of service, active service for five years. We lost 15 Democrats on that. Otherwise we lost 14 Democrats on 29 bills. A half a Democrat a bill. So we`ve been very unified. The progressives, the moderates, the more conservatives, the problem solvers. We`ve been very unified and I expect us to continue to be very unified.\par \par \uc2 And we want to get results and we want get investment infrastructure. Will it be everything that Joe Biden wants, everything that we want? Perhaps not but we want to get results for the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: That`s what they want us to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: House majority leader Steny Hoyer, the number two Democrat from suburban Maryland, here, thank you as always, sir. Appreciate your coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we have a dire situation that`s unfolding in the Middle East as dozens are killed in the latest conflict between Gaza and Israel. We`ve got a live report on the ground.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. This afternoon the CDC`s Vaccine Advisory Committee is expected to give the official green light to use Pfizer`s vaccine in adolescents. The meeting is underway now and the vote should be happening shortly.\par \par \uc2 Some states like Georgia, Arkansas, and Delaware have already begun immunizing adolescents. A spokesman for Georgia`s Health Department said the move is to help get as many people vaccinated as possible. One would think you would wait until the CDC approval there especially in the state where the CDC is head quartered.\par \par \uc2 Be sure to tune into MSNBC special "VACCINATING AMERICA" town hall as MSNBC`s Lawrence O`Donnell sits down with President Biden to discuss how to get every American vaccinated. Dr. Anthony Fauci, Surgeon General Vivek Murthy, and HHS Secretary Javier Becerra will also participate. It`s airing at 10:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC and streaming on Telemundo.com. And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, SECRETARY OF STATE: The United States remains committed to a two-state solution. This violence takes us further away from that goal. We`ll continue to engage with Israelis, Palestinians and other regional partners to urge de-escalation and to bring calm.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was Secretary of State Antony Blinken this morning addressing the escalating conflict between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and officially announcing that the U.S. is sending its own envoy to the region.\par \par \uc2 This outbreak of violence in Israeli and the Palestinian territories is the most significant and violent conflict in over seven years. A little more than an hour ago Israeli airstrikes leveled a 14-story building in Gaza. The Israel Air Force says the building house \uc2 Hamas\uc2  military intelligence offices and communications infrastructure. Israeli airstrikes which overnight took down two other high-rise buildings have been continuing today.\par \par \uc2 Palestinian militants have fired more than a thousand rockets toward Israel, though the majority have been intercepted by Israel`s Iron Dome defense system. At least six people in Israel have been killed and more than 50 are dead in Gaza including more than a dozen children.\par \par \uc2 "Sky News" foreign correspondent Mark Stone, as you know, "Sky News" is part of the larger NBC family, he is in Jerusalem for us.\par \par \uc2 And Mark, look, we`ve got this escalating violence. What is the end game here for the Israelis and what role -- what constructive role could an envoy from the United States do here?\par \par \uc2 MARK STONE, SKY NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So much to talk about, and actually, another thing which I`ll talk about in a second is the tension on the streets. We just had an incident where real tension between an Israeli and a Palestinian. It`s quite extraordinary, really, and I`ll talk about that in a moment, in cities across the country. It`s a sideline issue but I think it`s potentially an even more dangerous one.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the central issue at the moment, the onslaught of Gaza, the rockets from Gaza that continue to come into Israel and tonight they are still falling on cities like Ashkelon in the south of Israel, and in retaliation the Israelis are continuing to strike in Gaza. You talked about Antony Blinken and his envoy who he has sent here or is in the process of sending here. It`s a great question, what can he do?\par \par \uc2 Yes, he can certainly talk to the Israelis. I mean, the Biden administration doesn`t have quite as good a relationship as the Trump administration had with Benjamin Netanyahu and his team. But yes, they can talk with the Israelis and he`s going to talk to the Palestinians. But it`s the Palestinians in the West Bank that he`ll talk to him. \uc2 Mahmoud Abbas\uc2 , the president of the Palestinian Authority.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 STONE: He`s not going to speaking to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  because they don`t talk. So it`s -- and clearly, there is a massive gap between the \uc2 Palestinian Authority\uc2  on the West Bank and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  down in Gaza. They don`t really talk either. So it`s hard to see what he can actually achieve. I think the key will be the Qataris, the Egyptians, as they have been before, they`re the ones who are going to be able to broke some sort of a cease fire on this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that`s what I`m asking you, is sort of -- this could end up being the Egyptians having to broker, which you just said. Let me ask you, I`d like you to talk about that incident. You said, look, this latest fighting is actually over land that Israelis believe belonged to the Jews before it ever belonged to the Palestinians in East Jerusalem, and these folks have lived there for a couple of generations, but there is obviously that dispute. How much of that is now creating more tension on the streets of Israel? It sounds like you`re finding more.\par \par \uc2 STONE: Unquestionably, I mean, there has always been tension in the West Bank where Israeli settlers have -- on unprecedented levels right now over the course of the past year have been moving into the West Bank, taking over settlements because they believe firmly, an ideological belief, the land, the whole of greater Israel is theirs. On the other side, the other extreme, you do have Palestinians who believe, as they say, from the river to the sea, from the Jordan River to the Mediterranean, the whole land is theirs.\par \par \uc2 And in the middle, you`ve got the moderates who are willing to share, who are willing to go for that so-called two-state solution, that seems very distant at the moment. But what we have seen tonight and today and last night in cities across Israel, not the West Bank but Israel where Palestinian citizens of Israel, as they`re known, these are Palestinians who weren`t pushed out, who managed to stay in their homes in Israel after 1948, have lived alongside Jewish Israelis in these places, places like Lod and (INAUDIBLE) up in the north.\par \par \uc2 We`re now seeing clashes between these two groups of people. The Palestinian citizens of Israel said to be attacking the Israeli Jews. The Israeli Jews attacking the Palestinians as well. So you have kind of interracial conflict now in cities. Now that potentially is an existential threat for Benjamin Netanyahu. If he is seeing a breakdown of society within his own country, that is something very different from the bloodshed that we`re seeing down in Gaza, horrific as that is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mark Stone with "Sky News," our partner in Europe. Mark, thank you for this report. Stay safe as much as you can.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210513eh5c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 11, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Mark Murray, Molly Hunter \par \uc2 8,034 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: McCarthy pens a new letter about the future of the party which begins with ousting Liz Cheney from leadership for criticizing Trump`s lies about the election.\par \par \uc2 And later, new national security concerns following that massive cyber attack that shut down a major U.S. pipeline. It has been a giant wake-up call for security officials. We`ll speak with the last administration`s point person for protecting our nation`s infrastructure from cyber attacks. It`s Christopher Krebs. That`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Tuesday. It is MEET THE PRESS DAILY and I`m Chuck Todd. We`ve got a busy hour ahead, I promise you that. Any moment now President Biden is expected to begin a meeting with a bipartisan group of governors to discuss COVID vaccinations. That comes as the FDA is now authorized Pfizer`s vaccine for 12 to 15-year-olds. We`ll have a lot more on that development and what it means coming up.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin the hour with the long shadow cast over our politics emanating for now from Mar-a-Lago. House Republican leader Kevin McCarthy has formally notified his caucus and a letter that they will indeed vote tomorrow to dump Liz Cheney from leadership. Cheney has refused to toe the line on Trump`s lie that he won the 2020 election. As we`ve noted before the lie has consumed the GOP.\par \par \uc2 Just look at what happened with Republicans in Virginia where an exceedingly bizarre and Trump infused convention ended up having to choose a gubernatorial nominee in Glenn Youngkin who found himself campaigning on the issue of election integrity. And he`s one of the Republicans that refuses to say whether or not Biden won the election legitimately.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin got Trump`s complete and total endorsement today, which comes as the former president`s barrage of public statements have gone further down the rabbit hole of election conspiracy theories almost more so by the hour some days, which is what makes McCarthy`s letter to the caucus about ousting Cheney that much more remarkable.\par \par \uc2 Because in it McCarthy said this about Cheney, "Each day spent re- litigating the past is one day less we have to seize the future." It`s a clear indication from Republicans that the future of the party revolves around ignoring the damage inflicted by Trump`s false election claims and the Capitol riot they fueled. So, what now for the GOP?\par \par \uc2 Well, elections across this country this year, like the governor`s race in Virginia could end up being major barometers for the future of the Republican Party and the future of elections under the shadow of Trump. Because Democrats have already said in the Virginia race, they are going to play up January 6th, and play up Trump`s attacks on democracy in their campaigns.\par \par \uc2 The Virginians takes this personally. This race could show us how much of a motivated that issue is especially with suburban voters and voters with college degrees which Democrats seek key to their coalition nationwide.\par \par \uc2 There are other races to keep an eye on this odd-numbered year for important signals about the future of the GOP, even in typically blue strongholds like New York City mayor`s race or the New Jersey governor`s race. But Virginia is a big one. Every vote will be revealing for Republicans starting with tomorrow`s when they moved to oust Liz Cheney from leadership.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now on Capitol Hill is NBC`s Leigh Ann Caldwell. Also with us is Mark Murray from the political unit. He`s been following the gubernatorial race there in Virginia, and we have Republican Strategist Matt Gorman.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann, I want to start with you. And I want to start with -- we`re all speaking almost in the past tense about Liz Cheney and leadership. What will be the -- how many votes will she get in support of her staying in leadership tomorrow? Is it 10, is it 20, is it 50? What is that line and how nervous is Kevin McCarthy?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: No one really seems to know. I`ve been asking this question for about a week and I was wondering if she was only going to get 10 votes to keep her. But let`s remember this is a secret ballot and this is not a direct choice between Liz Cheney and the heir apparent Elise Stefanik.\par \par \uc2 So first, they have to -- people could vote to keep Liz Cheney. She could still lose that vote. And then they could also vote then later that day or later that week, whenever the vote happens, for Elise Stefanik. But there`s been some people that I`ve been talking to who actually think that she is going to get a significant number of votes because it is a secret ballot but it`s really hard to tell what that breakdown is going to be.\par \par \uc2 The fact though that Leader McCarthy encouraged his members to vote against her, to recall her from that position is pretty remarkable that he wants to remove someone from his leadership team. So, that gave the big flashing green light to his members to do that, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leigh Ann, I want to play a soundbite from Joni Ernst, a Republican Senator from Iowa. She invoked a favorite phrase of the right-wing these days. Take a listen to it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JONI ERNST (R-IA): You know canceled culture is canceled culture no matter how you look at it. And unfortunately, I think they`re there are those that are trying to silence others in the party. I support President Trump and his policies, so I have a slightly different view on that. But I still think we shouldn`t be trying to cancel voices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Leigh Ann, you know, playbook seemed to hint that Kevin McCarthy may succeed today -- tomorrow in getting rid of Liz Cheney, but he`s done damage to his own -- his own perception of what kind of leader he is by how he`s handled this. I mean, even in his own letter to the caucus yesterday, he actually wrote the phrase, unlike the left, we embrace free thought and debate.\par \par \uc2 I`m not going to read the rest of the quote because I don`t know how you get rid of Liz Cheney for her free thought, and then claim you`re for free thought. Is this handling the way he`s handled this, rubbing some of the wrong way?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Well, that`s always been the question with Leader McCarthy doing this. It`s always been thought that he is playing the short game. That`s what his critics say. And that`s even what some of his supporters say. His immediate goal is to keep Trump on his good side so that Trump can help them win back the majority in 2022, and also, so Trump can help McCarthy put him in the speakership role should that happen.\par \par \uc2 Leader Cheney thinks that she is playing the long game. She plans to remain in Congress. She plans to run for reelection. Those are the near-future plans. But she also was going to continue to be a very loud voice on this. She thinks that she is on the right side of the history and she is moving forward in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a lot of risk for what McCarthy is doing, first of all, because being loyal to the president today doesn`t mean that that loyalty is going to reward you in the future. There are some conservatives, Trump supporters in Congress who don`t like the coronation of Elise Stefanik, who has a moderate voting record, so that could come back to bite McCarthy as well.\par \par \uc2 So, this is actually a very risky Gambit, especially since what McCarthy pretty much single handedly did. Trump was on -- his popularity was waning. We weren`t talking about him as much, Republicans were talking about him as much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It sure was.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: But McCarthy whipped him right back into the center of this political storm, and he -- and Trump likely feels emboldened. And it`s going to be very hard to get rid of him now, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Boy, Leigh Ann, you make a really good point. Kevin McCarthy went out of his way to bring Trump back into the mainstream of the Republican Party. Now, he may say he had to do it because his constituents in the House Republican Conference would have dumped him if he didn`t, but he did do this on his own. There`s no doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 And straddling the world of trying to have it both ways on Trump, Mark Murray, brings me to Virginia governor in that Republican primary. Because what`s interesting about the new Republican nominee is that he is somebody who I think is trying not to be very Trumpy, but he had to -- he ended up embracing the two -- you know, one or two issues that Trump cares about the most or it`s like they had to do the minimum to get his endorsement, which already may be too much to win in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 MARK MURRAY, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL EDITOR: Yes, Chuck. It`s going to be quite a tight rope for Glenn Youngkin. A little background on Glenn Youngkin. He`s the former co-CEO of the \uc2 Carlyle Group\uc2 , a private equity firm. He has lots of money. He`s very wealthy. He has already used some of that wealth to be able to win the Republican Convention and he will be able to use it in the fall election.\par \par \uc2 He`s also a political outsider. He`s never run like this before, which sometimes brings good qualities because you get to be portrayed as the outsider. But sometimes, having never done this before, there are certain things you have to learn while on the fly.\par \par \uc2 But as you mentioned, Chuck, in the -- lead up to the convention, he embraced the former president airing a TV ad with the former president praising Glenn Youngkin. He also ended up questioning and campaigning on election integrity. And he has declined to say that Joe Biden won the 2020 election, fair and square.\par \par \uc2 Chuck, you and I`ve actually watched this Virginia governor`s race over the past two decades. It`s in our backyard. And historically, it has actually benefited the party that`s out of power in Washington. A party that controls the White House usually has a very difficult time of winning this election the very next year. And Republicans have a good shot at being able to win it.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen people like Larry Hogan win multiple terms in Maryland. We`ve seen Charlie Baker win multiple terms in Massachusetts. The difference here is that Youngkin has ties to Trump that Hogan and Charlie Baker really never had. And that`s going to be put to the test over the next several months.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, in fact, that leads me to Matt Gorman who`s with us, a Republican strategist. And Matt, let me play a little mash here of Youngkin`s -- of a little bit of the ads. And look, he was no doubt it was a very small primary electorate he was appealing to, but take a look at this mash.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Handfull of California mega corporations are deciding who can say what, promoting positions that they agree with and canceling conservative opinions and religious beliefs.\par \par \uc2 Major league baseball and some corporations forget that Americans and Virginians still value common sense. As governor, I`ll Institute reforms like bringing back voter ID for all our elections.\par \par \uc2 Anything that teaches division is not of him. And therefore, we will not teach critical race theory in schools, we will not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Matt, when you look at the recipe of when Republicans have won the governorship in Virginia, it`s never been by running on those issues. It was the car tax, get rid of it. It was Bob McDonald sort of running more about roads and bridges, then he was about jobs than he was about social issues.\par \par \uc2 Youngkin has a background that could make him a very viable candidate. But he`s choosing to run on some base issues here. Break it down for us.\par \par \uc2 MATT GORMAN, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: You`re right. And if you remember, Bob McDonald, his yard signs, all they said were Bob`s for jobs. He totally avoided a lot of those social issues. But in fairness, he really didn`t have a serious, serious primary unlike Younkin.\par \par \uc2 And I think two, when you see in that ad, whether it`s canceled culture, the donors and billionaires from California, it was just enough, to your point before, to strike the right conservative tone without totally getting out on a limb and going where Amanda Chase was kind of that really far right candidate was going.\par \par \uc2 I do also think Virginia is changed. I don`t think it`s really applicable as much anymore to say, it`s really the balancing act between the President`s party going back into an election year. I think it`s much more of a blue state now, so I think that`s the real key.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Matt, let`s go to the national issue of Kevin McCarthy. He`s doing what he has to do he thinks to become speaker. But you know, how much risk is he -- is he acquiring here in the way he`s gone about dumping Cheney, in the language he`s used? I mean, again, to sit here and say we respect free and open debate and then dump her, you know, and how he`s gone about this, he does seem as if he`s a leader, so worried about factions, that he only caters to the factions, he doesn`t worry about the big picture.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Well, I`ll say this, I think this is going to be -- if he takes back the House and become speaker, it`s going to be a very, very different calculus than in 2017. when Trump took office. Whether he likes it or not, Donald Trump will have a lot more control over the House if they take it back even though he`s not president anymore.\par \par \uc2 I think it`d be talked to him and the people around Trump. They felt he was much too deferential to Paul Ryan in 2017. That`s why they went with Obamacare replacement and not with Trump preferred an infrastructure or tax reform. So, even though he`s not president, and especially if McCarthy being the ally there, he is going to exert much more control whether Republicans like it or not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The role of January 6, Mark Murray, you said very quickly, we heard from the Democratic side of the aisle, they said, that is something that they think particularly in Virginia, that they can make a closing argument out of -- out of here. What is the Republican counter on that in the Virginia race?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Yes, I don`t have an answer to that, Chuck, but you`re exactly right. Democrats say that they want to use the imagery of January 6 to be able to seize upon Youngkin`s doubts about the legitimacy of the 2020 elections, his comments about some of the voting machines that are there.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats think that the polling is on their side, that you show the images of January six you link people to Trump. But as we pointed out in our first read newsletter this morning, Chuck, that there is a possibility that it doesn`t work. And if it doesn`t work in Northern Virginia, then an entire, you know, Democratic playbook thing that the party is very focused on making January 6 front and center for the 2022 midterms could be in serious doubt.\par \par \uc2 So, I think this is a fascinating trial run for us to watch to see how it plays in Virginia, and maybe across the country later on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Well, the question is going to be, are we going to have new information about what happened on January 6. And with that, let me close with you, Leigh Ann. There appears to be, I don`t know, sometimes motion does equal movement and sometimes motion is just motion.\par \par \uc2 But that Nancy Pelosi has deputized Bennie Thompson of the Homeland Security Committee, the ranking there is John Katko, an impeacher, one of the Republicans that impeached. Are they going to be able to come up with a January 6th compromise where we might actually get an investigation and what happened?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: That`s the question. Democrats are pretty confident. They`re feeling good about it, even going out to say that they hope that there`s a deal as early as this week, perhaps a vote on the floor next week. I want to caution that I`ve also heard this before on the January 6th Commission.\par \par \uc2 But Pelosi has made concessions on a couple issues. She says that now there could be an equal amount of Democrats and Republicans. She said they both can have subpoena power instead of just the Democrats. But the big question remains on the scope. Republican Leader McCarthy and Senate Minority Leader McConnell both want to expand the scope beyond January 6 to the racial justice strife of last summer.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll see if Bennie Thompson and Katko, as you said, as someone who voted to impeach the former president in the second impeachment, can come to some sort of agreement on that scope, because that is the final sticking point, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, Matt Gorman, what is -- how do Republicans -- how do you expect Republicans to talk about January 6? Is it going to depend on what kind of campaign they`re in, a statewide Republican in a swing state will say one thing, versus somebody in a -- in a more red area?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Look, I think they`re going to try and look forward as much as possible unless there`s new information on it. I think that would be the key. And I think whether it`s the election in 2020, or the insurrection, unless there`s new information, I think they`re going to look forward, I think you`re going to see Youngkin do the exact same thing. He`s going to pivot away from, you know, who won the election, hopefully, the insurgency, and look forward and talk about school reopening and the like.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, Mark Murray, there is a Democratic primary. Terry McAuliffe is the -- is the boldface name. It`s got some national name ID so people know who he is. But it`s a crowded primary. Does Youngkin`s nomination at all help or hurt McAuliffe here?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Yes, Chuck, it`s unclear. I have seen that Terry McAuliffe has been the one Democrat who`s been most aggressive trying to fundraise and tie Trump`s endorsement that he gave today to Youngkin and really elevate that. But as you mentioned, there are five total Democrats who are running, two women who are running to be the first black female governors of Virginia in the entire South, the state`s lieutenant governor, a state delegate. And that primary, and it`s not a convention, the primary is a month from now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Mark Murray, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Matt Gorman, getting us all started here. A little -- a little odd year politics to mix in with the national story that we`ve got going on that`ll all culminate tomorrow. Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, President Biden meets with a bipartisan group of governors as they discuss the vaccination strategy to deal with the vaccine skeptics as \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  gets FDA approval for their shots in younger teens.\par \par \uc2 Plus, new national security concerns over that cyber attack on a major U.S. pipeline. And what the government plans to do about the latest threat of more attacks. The former head of CISA, the \uc2 Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\uc2 , Christopher Krebs, will be here so stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Right now, President Biden is meeting with a bipartisan group of the nation`s governors on the Coronavirus response and vaccine best practices as everybody figures out how to get to the skeptics. We`ll monitor the event for any newsworthy updates.\par \par \uc2 But here are the facts you need to know today on all things COVID. The \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccines, as we told you before, have now been authorized for adolescence by the FDA. The two \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine doses given three weeks apart are exactly the same as what`s going to be given -- of what has been given to adults.\par \par \uc2 CDC Director Dr. Rochelle Walensky still needs to sign off on the FDA decision first, which could make the shots available for kids ages 12 to 15 as soon as this Thursday. So, if you haven`t registered your child, do so. In many states, you can preregister now if they`re in that age group for what it`s worth.\par \par \uc2 Top public health officials, including Dr. Walensky, testified in front of the Senate Help Committee this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROCHELLE WALENSKY, DIRECTOR, CDC: To add their -- some parents want to sort of see how it goes first, but I`m encouraging all parents to get their children vaccinated. Some parents don`t want to be first, but I`m also encouraging children to ask for the vaccine. I have a 16-year-old myself and I can tell you, he wants to get the vaccine. He wants his life back. These kids want to go back to school. They want to go back to the things they love.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  has labeled a Coronavirus variant from India as one of concern, indicating that it could be a global health threat. The variant appears more transmissible and one of the driving factors behind the crisis in India, which has overwhelmed the healthcare system there. It isn`t known for sure whether this variant will evade some of our best vaccines. Hopefully, we`ll find that out soon.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re going to have more on the FDA`s moved to clear Pfizer`s vaccine for use in adolescence and what that means. I`ll speak to a top medical expert who helps advise the FDA on vaccines. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we mentioned a few minutes ago, the FDA has authorized Pfizer`s vaccine for kids aged 12 to 15. The first shots could be available as soon as Thursday. So, joining me now is Dr. Paul Offit. He is a member of the FDA Advisory Committee on Vaccines. He`s also a practicing physician and the director of the Vaccine Education Center at the Children`s Hospital of Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 So Dr. Offit, let me start with a question I`m going to ask just as a parent of an adolescent 12 to 15. I have an older kid who`s already gotten it, both of us have gotten it, and we`ve all had uncomfortable experiences with shot two. So, 12 to 15-year-olds, if they`re getting the same shot, should we expect them to have an even rougher time with the second shot?\par \par \uc2 PAUL OFFIT, MEMBER, FDA ADVISORY COMMITTEE ON VACCINES: It`s possible. I mean, young children make -- generally make a better immune response than say, older adults do. And we`re seeing that already now with the second shot of the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna vaccine where you can get fever, fatigue, headache, muscle pain, joint pain, generally tends to happen people less than 65, not over 65.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not a leap to believe that a younger child has a vigorous immune system so they could develop those symptoms. Those are just products of your immune system. So, when you have those symptoms, it`s because your immune system is revving up to protect you against the fight should you ever encounter SARS COVID.\par \par \uc2 It`s actually the first vaccine, I think, in the history of man where people have complained when they haven`t had side effects, wondering whether the vaccine has been effective for them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say in this perverse, the more side effects, the better your immune response is?\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: That`s possible. It hasn`t been formally studied, but it is -- it is we certainly know that say for the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna trials, those vaccines were roughly 95 percent effective, and about 50 percent of people had side effects. So, you don`t have to have those side effects in order to have had an effective vaccine.\par \par \uc2 But I think the immune system is finally getting the recognition it deserves. I mean, when you have those symptoms, it`s because your immune system has been activated, so that`s good.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, so 12 to 15 is now -- how -- you know, at this point, do you expect sort of a systemic addition of kids under the age of 12 in the next three months?\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: That`ll be surprising. I know that certainly there are studies that are ongoing now down to six years, and even as young as six months of age. My prediction would be that we probably won`t see those -- the results of those studies until early next year. But the good news is that the FDA has approved, and I think on Wednesday, the CDC will also recommend that these vaccines be given down to 12 years of age, which means now that the 12 to 15-year-old won`t also have to suffer this.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people think wrongly that children don`t suffer this disease. They should come around in our hospital on our COVID ward. You know, more than three and a half million children have been infected. I`m sure that`s a low number.\par \par \uc2 There are 100 -- at least 200 who`ve died of this virus. And there`s also this multi-system inflammatory diseases that is a primary disease of the 6 to 14-year-old that is hard to watch. So if you can protect children safely against this virus, you should do that and that`s what this data show, that you can do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And this is a technical question on Emergency Use Authorization and then full authorization. Assuming \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  gets the approval, gets full approval for its vaccine, will that be for all age groups or will there be a staggered approval process for that to the way it has as we`ve brought age groups online?\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: Right. So there`s a difference between authorization for Emergency Use Authorization then licensure, which you`re referring to I think as approval. It sounds like at least by no later than the end of the summer that at least the MRNA vaccines would likely be licensed products. But you know, people, when they hear the word emergency use, you know, that sounds like things are being rushed or, worse, that safety guidelines are being ignored.\par \par \uc2 That`s really not true. The size of these trials have been the size of any typical adult or pediatric vaccine trial, and the safety follow-up is the same. The only real difference is length to follow up for efficacy. When we approved those vaccines, you know, in December and February, we could say that they were effective for a few months and the licensure is going to be based on efficacy for at least six months. So I think we probably will see licensure by the end of the summer.\par \par \uc2 TODD: As schools, parents and teachers and all of that debate the issue of a vaccine mandate for kids in schools, where do you come down on it, and considering we saw youth sports being there? If it becomes too politically tricky to do it in the schools, is mandating it for youth sports probably the best way to get the impact from the vaccine that we need?\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: You would like to think in a better world we wouldn`t need mandates, that people would look at this and like the data on the 12-year-old to 15- year-olds.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Of course.\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: Hundred-person trial, half the children got the vaccine, half the children got the placebo. There were 18 cases of disease, all in the placebo group. I`m sure the parents who happen to, because of a coin flip, end up in the vaccine were happy about that, whereas those who ended up in the placebo group weren`t. Well, now you can take the coin flip out of it. You can dramatically reduce or virtually eliminate your child`s chance of getting the disease by choosing the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 But yet still some people choose not to do that. And so then it becomes what`s the problem? If it`s access, that`s solvable. If it`s education, that`s solvable. If people just need a nudge, then incentives can solve that. Otherwise, if it`s sort of vaccine denialism, that people -- they aren`t skeptical of the data, they`re really cynical about the data. They think that the data don`t apply to them or that somebody is lying to them, and then it comes down to mandates.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about the variant that has been identified in India. I want to say it`s b-1617, not to get it confused with the one out in the U.K., that came from the U.K. b-117, which is our dominant strain. How soon will we be able to know if this variant out of India can be stopped by our vaccine?\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: Well, at least my understanding of the preliminary data is that it looks like it`s falling into that category for the South African variant or the Brazilian variant, where there may not be as good a protection against, say, mild or moderate disease but it`s still -- you still would get protection against severe critical disease, meaning the kind of disease that would cause you to be hospitalized or go to the ICU or to die so, that`s my understanding of the preliminary data.\par \par \uc2 We will learn more. You know, generally these viruses -- it`s a bad virus, It`s a bad coronavirus that came out of China that is adapting itself to growth in people. So it will definitely find ways to be more contagious. But at least to date it hasn`t escaped completely the immunity that`s been inducted by natural infection or immunization.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it going to be fairly easy with these new MRNA vaccines to sort of tweak them to handle new variants, or are we going to be starting from not quite square one but further back in the process to deal with them?\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: That`s the advantages of MRNA vaccines is they`re very easy to construct. I mean, that`s why they were the first vaccines that were out there in mid-December in this country. And so they`re easy to construct. Nothing is easy to mass produce.\par \par \uc2 You know, the lipid nanoparticles, the so-called fatty droplets in which the MRNA resides, is not easily scaled up. So that`s always the -- as you saw with this, it`s not easy to mass produce a vaccine, it`s not easy to mass administer a vaccine. But the construction of the vaccine itself is not that hard.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. Paul Offit, always good, like I said, you`re one of the advisers, outside advisers to the FDA on these issues, particularly vaccines, particularly specializing in kids. Always good to get your expertise on. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 OFFIT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And be sure to tune in to our special, "VACCINATING AMERICA." It`s a town hall. It`ll be hosted by my colleague Lawrence O`Donnell. It`s tomorrow night at 10:00 p.m. Eastern on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, new national security concerns after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  blames a Russian criminal group, not the nation state, for that cyberattack on a major U.S. pipeline. One of the government`s top former officials on cybersecurity, Christopher Krebs, joins me now.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. For a second straight day, Biden administration officials will brief reporters about the cyberattack that has shut down one of the nation`s largest pipelines. Energy Secretary Jennifer Granholm and the Deputy Secretary David Turk will answer questions at the White House daily briefing later today.\par \par \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 , which carries about 45 percent of the East Coast`s fuel supply, said it hopes to have its outage resolved by the end of the week, but, yes, it is still unresolved. And while the focus may then shift to preventing attacks at the federal government, they first need to focus on how cyberattacks are reported in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Wait until you here this. This morning the acting director of CISA, which is the federal agency in charge of cybersecurity for critical structure in this country, told the Senate Homeland Security Committee that his agency only learned of the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2  attack from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and that it still has not been given technical details of the attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROB PORTMAN (R-OH): So they did not contact CISA. Did CISA contact Colonial?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We were brought in by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  after they were notified.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: If the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had not brought you in, would Colonial, do you think, have contacted you to ask for your assistance?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: Do you think that`s a problem?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think that there is benefit when CISA`s brought in quickly because the information that we glean, we work to share it in a broader fashion to protect other critical infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, the diplomatic answer of somebody in government at the moment. OK. With me now is Chris Krebs, who was the first director of CISA. He`s now a partner of the Krebs Stamos Group.\par \par \uc2 And Chris, I feel like we all know each other really well on these topics because we`ve been dealing with it quite a bit over the last few months. And I have to say, that was the most startling fact that we discovered today, was finding out that CISA was not looped into this. And I think this has been sort of your -- I think you`ve been evangelizing about this, if you will, that CISA isn`t looped in and yet it`s supposed to be. How did this happen?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS KREBS, FORMER CISA DIRECTOR: Well, first off, Chuck, thanks for having me on. But look at Brandon Wales, the acting director, look at his response. And you ask yourself the question, why is it like this? Why do we not know more? Why do we not have more information? Why aren`t breaches or incidents at critical national infrastructure like Colonial, you know, why isn`t that more automatic?\par \par \uc2 And there are a couple different conversations happening on the Hill right now, and it was a recommendation out of last year`s Cyberspace Solarium Commission that critical national infrastructure particularly the systematically important infrastructure needs to have certain requirements in place where they have a bad day, that they get that information to the partners quickly.\par \par \uc2 Now the last piece here that I would note is that my sense of things is that it would be helpful that if Colonial could share what the initial vector of attack, how the bad guys got in, and if they can share that quickly to make sure that there`s not another ransomware attack that happens in the next week or so, that would be a net policy and operational positive for the entire country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you were at CISA, is there any regular contact between CISA and private companies like Colonial when there`s not -- when there`s not a major attack going on? Like what is the regular dialogue that takes place between private companies that oversee critical infrastructure and CISA?\par \par \uc2 KREBS: Well, I think you start with the fact that there are hundreds of thousands of companies in the United States that are owning and operating our nation`s critical infrastructure. And when you step back and think about critical infrastructure, it`s everything from banks to bridges, from schools to sewers. And everything in between. And so it is a massive operational scope.\par \par \uc2 There are a number of mechanisms that we and the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and other agencies have established over the years to coordinate with owners and operators of infrastructure, but ultimately particularly in the nonregulatory space where CISA operated, it`s a voluntary public- private partnership so that the organization has to come to the government.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s not to give the government a free pass. The government has to have something of value to attract the private sector organization to come work with us. And I think that`s the biggest struggle right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there a way to model this off of how the Pentagon does it? You know, look, I`m sure you have friends that have done this. I`ve got -- I`ve known people who work for the government and their job is to make sure the defense contractors are correctly handling classified material and things like that, making sure their security systems are up to snuff so that stuff isn`t pilfered from them. Is there a way to create a similar relationship?\par \par \uc2 KREBS: So I think the difference there, Chuck, is that those private sector organizations are choosing to contract with the federal government. And the federal government has the power of the purse to set certain standards. And I think what you`re going to see some time in the pretty near future is a cybersecurity executive order out of the White House that will raise the bar on software standards at least for those procured by the federal government.\par \par \uc2 There will be trickle down effects, though, into the private sector. The separate piece here, though, is I think we need to have a pretty real conversation with our businesses that have enjoyed a significant amount of success in the economy. If you`re in a position where you own, you know, delivery for 50 percent of the refined product in a major region in the United States, if not the largest pipeline in the United States, you`ve got a certain obligation and you have some corporate citizenship responsibilities that you need to live up to.\par \par \uc2 And again, the investigation is still in the early stages here with Colonial, but we all know that everybody needs to do a little bit more on cybersecurity.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let`s talk about the perpetrators of this. It`s a Russian crime gang, it appears, a cyber gang. But I want to play something that the chairman of the House Intelligence Committee said earlier today, that maybe Russia the government does have more responsibility here. Take a listen to what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): This is a known actor operating out of Russia, and, you know, from my own perspective, Russia has the ability to shut people like this down if they choose to. So I think they have responsibility here, even if they`re not engaged in the conduct themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: From what you know about this group, is Congressman Schiff right, that they have some deep ties to the GRU and the Russian intelligence folks?\par \par \uc2 KREBS: So broadly speaking, I think Chairman Schiff is spot on. I`m not certain that this crew, the DarkSide crew, is tied to intelligence services nor, you know, their predecessor organizations, (INAUDIBLE) and the GandCrab crew.\par \par \uc2 But it`s blatantly obvious to anyone that`s been paying attention in the last four, five years that ransomware is a business and it`s a successful business that operates out of Russia and some of the former Soviet states. And it is almost a permitted business by the intelligence services.\par \par \uc2 Look at their techniques. They actually deploy their ransomware and as a part of the deployment, they check for Russian language packages on the infected computers, and if they find it, they exit out. That tells me that, you know, as I said earlier today, they know where their bread is buttered and they`re not going to mess, they`re not going to, you know, tee off against their hosts. So I think President Biden is absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 He`s got an opportunity to have a conversation with President Putin saying, knock it off. This ends, this ends now. This is an attack on critical infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Chris, is dealing with ransomware a whack-a-mole that is going to be impossible to deal with, or will at some point we have the technological tools to at least cause some pain for these ransom cyberattackers?\par \par \uc2 KREBS: Oh, I absolutely think that there is an approach that we can adopt that will change the game. I think, first, we`ve got to have better cyber defenses here in the United States. I think President Biden has got a great opportunity this summer to probably pull together a couple of different cybersecurity summits. Bring in CEOs of large businesses, medium businesses, meet with state and local executives and ask them what more they need.\par \par \uc2 You know, the government can do a lot to help here but ultimately, there are a lot of decisions that need to be made in the boardroom and in the C- suite to improve cybersecurity investments. I think the second thing we need to do is break the business model.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. How interesting. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KREBS: Break the business model. A lot of this is enabled by cryptocurrency.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Oh, well, talk about opening up a can of worms there and debating cryptocurrency, that we will leave for another time and perhaps another pair of folks.\par \par \uc2 Former assistant director Christopher Krebs, really appreciate you coming on and sharing your experiences and expertise with us. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KREBS: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And coming up, new pleas for peace as deadly clashes escalate between Israeli and Palestinian forces. The very latest on a very volatile situation. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Ongoing violence in Israel turned deadly after clashes between Israeli police and Palestinians at the Al-Aqsa mosque and in East Jerusalem escalated into an exchange of rockets between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , which of course is a militant group that controls Gaza.\par \par \uc2 The death toll from the strikes has continued to rise this morning, according to the Gaza Health Ministry, 28 Palestinians including 10 children were killed and over 150 more were injured as a result of the Israel strikes on, quote, "terror targets." And Israel reports that two Israeli women were killed in the strikes from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  after the group expanded their rocket attacks from Jerusalem to cities in southern Israel.\par \par \uc2 NBC foreign affairs correspondent Molly Hunter has been following the latest and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 You know, Molly, I have to say it was startling to me to read today that this had been the first time in seven years that rockets had emanated from Gaza. On one hand it reminded me that time flies, and it`s actually been a remarkably -- a remarkable peace over the last seven years, and now this. It feels like we`re in a bad spot at a time when the Palestinians are divided politically and the Israelis are divided politically.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY HUNTER, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chuck, that`s exactly right. It has kind of been an extraordinary peace for that place, but this is absolutely the best escalation we have seen since the end of the 2014 war. And actually, Chuck, I just want to say we were just getting breaking lines in right now. Obviously I`m in London, I`m not in Jerusalem, we have a great team in Jerusalem, in Tel Aviv, right now.\par \par \uc2 And we`re getting breaking lines from our team in Tel Aviv. So I want to show you this video that we just saw in Gaza. The IDF, the Israeli military, has flattened a residential building. And airstrikes, as you mentioned, are unrelenting, are going into Israel. And Prime Minister Netanyahu has vowed to step up the intensity and the rate of that. So that residential building which was reportedly evacuated had 80 apartments in it, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 And it`s just been flattened immediately after \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , the militant group, who`s in control of Gaza, threatened to start striking Tel Aviv, and almost minutes later our producer on the ground in Tel Aviv said he heard sirens in Tel Aviv and a large explosion above his Tel Aviv house. Now the IDF`s initial report says sirens have sounded in Tel Aviv. We`re certainly going to keep watching that.\par \par \uc2 But this has all kind of been bubbling as you said for the last seven years really. But really for the last week we have seen a stepped-up intensity, and that started in Jerusalem and it started, look, the fundamental reasons for this violence are not new, Chuck, but this cycle started with a renewed Jerusalem push by Israeli settlers to push out Palestinian families living in a neighborhood called Sheikh Jarrah in occupied East Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 Now our team on the ground spoke with one of those families. This was her message to President Joe Biden. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAMRA DAJANI, SHEIKH JARRAH RESIDENT: Put yourself in my place and think about your future, and think about your right -- oh, Joe Biden, think, think, think about the humanity. Please, we are human beings. We are owners of this house. My parents, my brothers, my children, my grandchildren, we are growing here.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: And let`s be very clear, the push to put Jewish settlers in East Jerusalem houses is not new, but a lot of what we are seeing right now, as you said, Chuck, we haven`t seen in seven years, and this rhetoric by Prime Minister Netanyahu down at the southern border combined with the fact that tomorrow is the last day of Ramadan, of course the holiest month for Muslims, it`s a potent combination, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, it is. Never mind you have again the political split between Abbas and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and of course the unsettled situation in Israel, a complete and total chaos that you have this situation in, could get uglier.\par \par \uc2 Our Molly Hunter on the story for us. Molly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`re going to be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues, though, with Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210511eh5b0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 10, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Hallie Jackson, Ken Dilanian, Mike Memoli, Sahil Kapur, Cal Perry,  Nahid Bhadelia \par \uc2 9,580 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, MSNBC SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: If it`s Monday, a presidential response to a crippling cyber-attack with President Biden`s set to speak just a couple minutes from now and expected to touch on that Russian criminal attack that shut down a big us pipeline with big national security implications.\par \par \uc2 He`ll also hit on the economy with the White House facing lingering questions about the most recent disappointing jobs report in the midst of its push for trillions of dollars in new federal programs.\par \par \uc2 And the Houses top Republican formerly throwing his support behind Liz Cheney`s challenger for a job that`s technically not even open yet. As Republicans gear up for a vote to give the boot to Liz Cheney from leadership, what it all means for the party for President Biden`s agenda and for our democracy.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Monday. It`s MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Hallie Jackson in for Chuck Dodd. And we`re coming on the air with some new developments in the White House`s response to that cyber attack on a big U.S. fuel pipeline which is still shut down. You`ve got the \uc2 FBI\uc2  just releasing a statement attributing that attack on \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2  to a Russian criminal group confirming NBC`s reporting that group DarkSide might be responsible.\par \par \uc2 The pipeline carries about 45 percent of the East Coast fuel supply. That`s your gas. That`s your diesel. We expect to hear from President Biden for the first time on camera about this when he speaks from the White House in just about 15 minutes or so. We`re going to take you there live. You`ve also got White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki and two top national security officials who`ve just wrapped up briefing reporters on the response to this attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNE NEUBERGER DEPUTY NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR FOR CYBER AND EMERGING TECHNOLOGY: In this case, the ransomware that was used is a known variant. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has investigated many cases of this in the past, as I noted, beginning in October. So, the first and most important thing is to ensure that systems are patched and that cybersecurity is maintained at the level needed in a given network. We want to see ransomware not be successful. And that begins with greater resilience, particularly in critical infrastructure networks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: One of the concerns, of course, is what does this means for gas prices. Those haven`t really been affected yet, but that might change the longer this pipeline stays shut down. And you`ve got Commerce Secretary Gina Raimondo warning, we should be ready for more attacks like this in the future.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO, SECRETARY OF COMMERCE: Unfortunately, these sorts of attacks are becoming more frequent. They`re here to stay and we have to work in partnership with businesses to secure networks to defend ourselves against these attacks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So, the White House is grappling with this cyberattack as it looks ahead to a very busy week here in Washington, a week in which Republicans are poised to purge Congresswoman Liz Cheney from her leadership position, and a week in which President Biden is set to reach out to Republicans to try to generate some bipartisan support for his multitrillion-dollar agenda.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s get to it. I`m joined now by Ken Dilanian who covers intelligence and national security for us, leading our coverage on this pipeline attack. I`m also joined by NBC News White House Correspondent Mike Memoli, and by Kiersten Todt, Managing Director of the Cyber Readiness Institute. She also served on President Obama`s National Cyber Security Commission.\par \par \uc2 Ken, let me start with you on this. I know you`ve been on the forefront of our reporting here. We just heard from the White House during that briefing, no response to the question of whether this company, whether Colonial actually paid the ransom that was demanded from it, although they did talk about the need for companies -- private companies to shore up and make sure that they have the right cyber defenses in place.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: That`s right, Hallie. Yes, that person speaking was Anne Neuberger, who`s a former \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2  official who`s now heading up the White House cyber response. And she was alluding to what is the crux of the problem, in this case, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 This was not a sophisticated attack by a foreign intelligence service of China or Russia. This is a criminal gang using basic ransomware from everything we can tell, the kind that unfortunately, has become all too common. But in this case, it`s really capturing the imagination because this was such critical infrastructure, and it may lead to a spike in gas prices, and it may lead to service interruptions.\par \par \uc2 And it`s a wake-up call that, you know what, 85 percent of American infrastructure is in private hands, and the government has very little visibility on whether these private corporations have good cybersecurity or bad cybersecurity. In this case, whatever cybersecurity they had was not enough to keep out these attackers.\par \par \uc2 And just so people understand, ransomware is when the hackers get into the network and encrypt data and demand payment to free that data. And in this case, they also see some data and threatened to leak it on the internet. So, it`s an extortion scheme, one that most companies should be able to defend against, this one was not able to.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why the button administration is contemplating some regulations on cybersecurity, which have never been successful to make companies have basic cyber hygiene, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Ken, that`s an important point that you`re making here, right, because you`re talking about how this is -- this appears to be a Russian criminal enterprise, right, this group DarkSide. The White House, what you heard from Neuberger from the podium there at the White House was that they are looking into the potential.\par \par \uc2 It sounded like to me, Ken, and tell me if you`re reading this differently, about what kind of connections might there be to the Russian government. Is that something that would be typical in a situation like this?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Yes, in a sense that many of these Russian criminal groups or Eastern European criminal groups freelance for Russian intelligence services. There`s a symbiotic relationship there. So, that`s one thing to investigate. But even if there`s no Kremlin hand on this at all, it remains the case that Russia grants sanctuary, grants impunity to these hacker groups, as long as they don`t attack Russia, as long as they attack the English speaking West, which is what these this group says that they do.\par \par \uc2 And that`s a problem. I mean, that`s essentially this group attacking American interest and then retreating back to Russia, where beyond the reach of the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2 , that`s a national security threat that only the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  can deal with, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Ken Dilanian, thank you for that and for your great reporting. I appreciate that. Mike Memoli, let me go to you because I know you`ve been monitoring this news, this press briefing. I know we`re going to hear from President Biden, if he`s not running behind schedule, in just about 10 minutes. We`re gonna bring that to you live when it happens.\par \par \uc2 And the expectation is he will talk about this because, Mike, this is not nothing, right? This is a pipeline that carries, as we said, nearly half of the fuel supply to the East Coast. It is 5500 miles long. It winds through all the states that you see on the screen. This is significant.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Oh, absolutely, Hallie. When you think about what the conversation at the White House has been over these last few weeks, it`s been about the nation`s infrastructure and the need for critical improvements of really after years in which the President`s argument has been, we`ve neglected it while other countries have been fortifying, shoring up, expanding their infrastructure capacity.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the light -- this is what we would more traditionally call critical infrastructure, this area of cyber warfare that as former President Obama once put it is becoming really the Wild West in terms of an emerging battlefield for not just nation-states, but criminal actors, potentially given safe harbor by nation-states, as Ken was just outlining with Russia.\par \par \uc2 Now, as far as what we just heard from the White House, you heard from Neuberger as well as Liz Sherwood-Randall, who is the White House national -- Homeland Security Adviser, excuse me. They are -- they said that the White House has been in touch with Colonial throughout the past few days. The President has been briefed regularly.\par \par \uc2 And it`ll be interesting, I think, the President`s response is designed to piggyback on what we just heard which is that the White House is on top of this. This has been so much of the approach from this White House since replacing President Trump in January.\par \par \uc2 And think back to what President-Elect Biden at the time was saying in December when we learned first about the \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  hack. He directly blamed President Trump for, as he put it, taking his eye off the ball, for allowing the nation`s guard to be let down in the face of this cyber hacking that we`ve been seeing increasingly over time.\par \par \uc2 And that`s one reason why the administration has been in naming, for instance, Chris Inglis as the first-ever National Cyber Director. Neuberger, who we`ve not mentioned several times, is actually a newly created position on the National Security Council focused exclusively on cyber. And so, they want to make the point, reassure Americans that they`ve been on top of this, while also taking steps in the meantime to deal with issues like gas prices, the Department of Transportation, relaxing some of those freight guidelines to allow more fuel to be transported on the roads, if not through pipelines.\par \par \uc2 But certainly, expect the President to try to reassure Americans that this administration, after four years in which they feel their predecessor did not necessarily keep their eye on the ball here, is firmly in control of this.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So, Kiersten, let me pick up on this point here. What is more alarming to you, the fact that this specific attack happened, or the potential vulnerabilities in the nation cyber infrastructure that it`s now exposing?\par \par \uc2 KIERSTEN TODT, MANAGING DIRECTOR, CYBER READINESS INSTITUTE: So, I`ll answer by saying both, because the issue is that, as you said repeatedly, you know, this is a critical infrastructure company that carries 45 percent of fuel to the east coast. It is an owner and operator, but it`s privately held. And so, what it calls into question is the ability for the government to weigh cybersecurity standards on these companies.\par \par \uc2 We had movement in Congress in 2012 to look at cybersecurity standards and Congress deemed that it was too onerous on these companies to have these basic cybersecurity standards. I think we`ve far outgrown that perception and we`ve absolutely got to be -- we have to be working with these owners and operators to know what their baseline level of cybersecurity is.\par \par \uc2 The other piece to this is this type of company should not have been this vulnerable to this kind of attack. Ken mentioned earlier, this is not a sophisticated nation-state attack of the precision that we saw with \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2 . This is a ransomware attack. We`re seeing this across the nation.\par \par \uc2 The good news is that the Biden administration has been on ransomware from day one. The Department of Justice released an 81-page report that it developed in collaboration with the private sector a few weeks ago, and it highlighted this issue around countries, nation-states like Russia that are providing safe havens to these criminal actor groups and the need to penalize and punish them.\par \par \uc2 It also talks about making sure that cryptocurrency is liable under money laundering regulations. We saw the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  identify ransomware as the first of its 60-day sprints. And Secretary Mayorkas was just talking this past week on the threat of ransomware and what needs to be done.\par \par \uc2 So, the good news is that this is not a surprise, we know that we`re on top of it, but how this government needs to work with owners and operators of critical infrastructure is certainly the next step in securing and as in Neuberger said, making sure our networks are resilient to these types of attacks so we`re not having a discussion on whether or not to pay ransom.\par \par \uc2 We should not be in a position where in order to keep our critical infrastructure operating, we have to pay a ransom.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You heard this thread come up, Kiersten, in the briefing room right about what is the onus on the White House now? What kind of guidance should the administration for example be giving to these private companies? And then the other question, of course, is what happens down Pennsylvania Avenue as it relates to Congress and what they should be doing as you`ve alluded to.\par \par \uc2 What ultimately -- you talked about the next steps -- what should that next step be? Like, what does the administration need to do today, tomorrow, this week, in order to, A, give guidance to these private companies, and B, make sure that something like this can`t happen again?\par \par \uc2 TODT: Well, again, the good news is that the Biden administration has been working proactively with industry on looking at these pieces and how to work together to be more resilient. And I think this is one of the key pieces right now is you can`t expect government to do this by itself. We can`t ask the private companies to do this by themselves.\par \par \uc2 We have to come together to identify what is that reasonable, practical, secure set of standards that we need to hold these owners and operators to have secure standards by, and then working with Congress to potentially enact legislation. But I think let`s start with that collaboration between industry and government.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen industry step forward to say, hey, we want to work with government on this in the energy sector. We need real-time information sharing. So, there is absolutely an opportunity for collaboration. And we should be encouraged by the actions by this White House to have already been on top of this to be able to create, I think, solid recommendations, but more importantly, the execution of those recommendations to secure our networks.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Mike, Senator Cassidy was on MEET THE PRESS and talk with Chuck about this exact issue. I want to play free what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Congress has attempted to fix that. There`s been problems in the past with sharing classified information with private entities, and Congress has passed the law to fix that. But it`s going to take an ongoing relationship. By the way, a bipartisan relationship in which we better equip small businesses and large businesses to withstand cyber attacks.\par \par \uc2 Chuck, the implications for this for national security cannot be overstated. And I promise you this is something that Republicans and Democrats can work together on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Mike, do you see that as realistic? Could this be an area where there is, in your view, bipartisan cooperation?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Well, it certainly has to be. The area on Capitol Hill which has tended to be least partisan, although there have been some very real challenges to that over these last few years, of course, has been in the intelligence committees both in the House and the Senate side.\par \par \uc2 And typically, I think with Mark Warner as the chairman, you would see Marco Rubio, the ranking member on the Republican side, this has been something that they`ve been very focused on. Mark Warner of Virginia, for instance, with a background in the tech industry would certainly like to advance this. And this is something that the White House is going to be pushing as well.\par \par \uc2 But as I think I referred to when President Obama call this sort of the Wild West, this is an area that the White House is also dealing with potentially some new guidance, as we`ve been discussing, that needs to go out to the communities themselves. A reporter pressed Neuberger, for instance, about this idea of is the FBI`s guidance to private companies to still not pay ransom when these attacks are made on their infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 And they said they recognize that increasingly, because of the nature of this data, especially if they don`t have backups, if they don`t have the resilience in place when they have these kinds of intrusions, that they really have no choice in the matter. And so, the White House is looking at other things that they could potentially be doing especially so much now.\par \par \uc2 We saw over the weekend with Elon Musk on Saturday Night Live \uc2 Dogecoin\uc2 , Bitcoin as the funding mechanism by which some of these transactions tend to take place. Treasury -- the Treasury Department is looking at ways in which they could better police this on the cyber front as well.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Kiersten, we had the \uc2 FBI\uc2  coming out and putting the blame on this group DarkSide. Was it unusual to you that in an instance like this, in a major cyber attack like this, that happened relatively quickly in the process?\par \par \uc2 TODT: I think it`s very reassuring because one of the failures that we`ve had is that right now, and we`ve talked about this earlier, individuals who impose ransomware attacks, who have ransomware attacks, operate with impunity. We have no ability right now to indict, to prosecute and to incarcerate these individuals. We have to change that.\par \par \uc2 So, I commend the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for coming out to say, here`s a criminal actor group, we know that Russia harbors this group. Then we have to come together as the Department of Justice report outlined with an international coalition to work against these nation-states and to ensure that they penalize those nation-states that harbor these criminals.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the challenge with cybersecurity is that you can act and commit these offenses and you have no consequences. If an individual had placed a bomb on that pipeline and it exploded, we would have no question right now, how are we responding.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve got to be acting in the cyber world the consequences the same way we would in the physical world. There`s not always a one for one, but in this case there is. And so we have to make sure that these individuals don`t walk away with that anonymity and that belief that they can get away with this.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Kiersten Todt, thank you very much for your expert perspective on this. Mike Memoli, great reporting. Thank you. We are still waiting for President Biden to speak on this issue, as well as the economy. It was supposed to start about 24 seconds ago or so. He`s running a little bit behind, but we will bring that to you live when it happens.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, coming up, we`ve got this deep dive into frankly, a bit of an uncertain future for the GOP as they go all-in on their loyalty to President Trump. The plan to abandon Liz Cheney is just about 48 hours from now.\par \par \uc2 And later, as the U.S. appears to be inching closer to a new normal, some very harsh realities of the pandemic are still on the table. We`re talking about some new concerns over what the actual COVID death count really is.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Welcome back. As we wait to see President Biden live from the White House, you can see the White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki is still briefing reporters as we speak. We`re also going to talk a bit about what is happening on the other end of Pennsylvania Avenue, and that is this push to oust Congresswoman Liz Cheney from her leadership role as the number three House Republican.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got the top Republican leader Kevin McCarthy publicly endorsing now the person who is expected to replace Cheney. Here he is explaining his rationale.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Any member can take whatever position they believe in. That`s what -- that`s what the voters vote on the individuals and they make that decision. What we`re talking about, it`s a position in leadership. We are in one of our biggest battles ever for this nation in the direction whether this next century will be ours. As conference chair, you have one of the most critical jobs as the messenger of going forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So, McCarthy`s ultimate takeaway was that a member in leadership cannot have a position that`s at odds with where most of the rest of the conference is. And that position with regards to Cheney is that she is telling the truth in the face of former President Trump`s lies about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 So, as for timing, the way the calendar shakes out, Republicans could make their moves to expel Cheney from leadership as early as Wednesday, but New York Republican Congresswoman Elise Stefanik expected to replace her.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn now to a couple of my NBC News colleagues Sahil Kapur is on Capitol Hill, Cal Perry is out in Casper, Wyoming where he`s been talking with some of Liz Cheney`s constituents. Sahil, let me start with you here. When you look ahead to the way these next 48 hours ago, it seems like the writing is on the wall in Sharpie. Could anything change the trajectory right now?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: It`s highly unlikely Hallie, I think it`s all been a foregone conclusion that Elise Stefanik, the Congresswoman from New York, will replace Liz Cheney and that Liz Cheney will be ousted. It will happen on Wednesday in a conference meeting where basically one Republican member has to make a motion of no confidence against Cheney, then if leadership considers it of immediate importance so that they can call a vote, a simple majority can vote her out.\par \par \uc2 This has -- the writing has been on the wall for quite a while. And the message that Republicans are essentially sending is that you can`t be in party leadership if you`re going to actively contradict President Trump. He is the leader of the party. And Liz Cheney, for actively debunking his falsehoods about the 2020 election was not appreciated by her party. And that`s the reason it appears she has to go.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So, it`s the act of debunking, but it`s also what you kept hearing, Sahil, again and again and what you continue to hear from those Republicans who want to see her gone is that it`s a messaging issue, right. She`s not on message with where the rest of the GOP is. Here, for example, is Congressman Banks on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM BANKS (R-IN): Any leader who`s not focused on that, on pushing back against the by the radical dangerous Biden agenda at this point needs to be replaced. But the point here is that we are almost entirely unified on that mission except for Liz Cheney.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let me ask you --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: But the person who`s not talking about President Biden`s policy agenda is Donald Trump, right? The former president releasing more than 20 statements over the last six weeks continuing to push his lies about the 2020 election, five in just the last week alone, Sahil. Why is it then that Republicans are blaming Cheney and not the former president?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Yes, there`s a bit of a contradiction in that Hallie. They`re saying Cheney needs to go because she keeps talking about the past, whereas she`s really only responding to former President Trump who has put out a steady stream of statements talking about the past, talking about the 2020 election and insisting falsely that it was illegitimate.\par \par \uc2 The real irony here about the message that they`re -- that they`re discussing is that Liz Cheney has a more ideologically conservative voting record than Elise Stefanik. One of the signature achievements, if not, the signature achievement of the Trump era was the 2017 Tax Law. Liz Cheney voted for it. Elise Stefanik voted against it and yet Stefanik is seen as the right messenger to carry forward the party`s message.\par \par \uc2 That tells you about all you need to know about what the message is. It`s Donald Trump. It`s not conservative ideology. It`s not about taxes. It`s not about spending. Hallie?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And Sahil, I know that you and our colleagues on Capitol Hill have been reporting on what exactly are Liz Cheney`s next steps. And it`s our understanding, your understanding from a source familiar, that she is obviously planning to stay in Congress, to run again.\par \par \uc2 Cal, you`re in her district talking with people who she serves. Tell us more about what you`re hearing.\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. And before we play this sound, people are going to say, well, you went to Trump country. Well, this was Liz Cheney country. Look, she won her election by a landslide. Here in Casper, she won by an even bigger margin. But as we`re hearing around the country, what seems to be the issue here for voters is speaking out against the former president seems to be the ultimate betrayal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think Liz Cheney needs to go. She does not represent the people of Wyoming. 73 percent of the people here in Wyoming voted for Trump. And she basically stabbed us in the back with she felt it was the right thing to do. Well, I didn`t vote for her -- for her feelings. I voted for her to represent us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think she`s a RINO. I think she votes on the liberal side, and we voted her in as a Republican. She doesn`t have our best interests at heart.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She`s a traitor.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Why? What did she do?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, she`s a Republican, and then she votes against the Republican president. She`s a traitor. She gets what she deserves to get run out of office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Keeping in mind that Liz Cheney voted with the president more than 93 percent of her time while the President was in office. I think one of the most fascinating things about this, and you were just talking about it, is Liz Cheney is going to be running for reelection here in 18 months.\par \par \uc2 And when you talk to liberals, when you talk to Democrats who live in the state, they`re now basically living in the upside-down, a world in which they may support Liz Cheney because she`s the more liberal have two options on the ballot in 18 months, Hallie?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: That is something to think about it, Cal. Two questions to follow up with you on this. Number one, did you find anybody who was willing to speak to you publicly about their support or at least, you know, getting where she`s coming from for Liz Cheney?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: I was reminded of one person who spoke to us yesterday in Casper who was willing to talk on camera. But most liberals that we bumped into said they didn`t want to speak on camera. They felt like the minority. It`s a little different three hours south of here in Cheyenne, but no, people just didn`t want to talk on camera about their support for Liz Cheney.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You also played an interview -- part of your interview with a woman, I believe, who said something like I didn`t vote for her -- for her feelings, right, to tell me how she feels. But on the policy issue, what did some of these folks tell you when you pointed out or to the idea that, as you say, Liz Cheney actually votes in line more with the policies that Donald Trump represented than Elise Stefanik did, as Sahil points out, who, for example, opposed the former president`s biggest policy push in 2017, which were his tax cuts?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Not a great answer for that, except I will say it feels like it`s not Liz Cheney versus Donald Trump as much as it`s Donald Trump versus America again. And I keep hearing this answer as I talk to people, and it`s true on vaccine hesitancy -- excuse me -- as well. I`ll ask people, what is this really about? Is this really about the vaccine, the same thing on Liz Cheney? Is this really about Liz Cheney?\par \par \uc2 And the answer inevitably comes back, no, this is about former President Donald Trump and what happened during the election and what happened on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Sahil, let me ask you, because Congress is dealing -- you know, the House Republicans have this on their plate. This is obviously expected to happen as early as Wednesday. But there`s a whole lot of other stuff on the to do list for lawmakers as they come back into town now and back into session starting with infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 And you had Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell who`s shifting a little bit where he is on this. He initially said, OK, I would support something like a $600 billion plan, now it`s like six to $800 billion. Do you think that is a sign that there could actually be some compromise or is this just more two parties talking over each other at this point?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR Hallie, it really depends on who you ask. There are a variety of opinions about this among Democrats. Some figures in the White House believe it`s still possible that they could get a bipartisan deal with Democrats. There are Democrats here on Capitol Hill who are insisting that they at least try it.\par \par \uc2 There are progressive lawmakers in the House in the Senate who I`ve talked to who say this is a fool`s errand, that Republicans are simply not going to be on board with anything major, anything close to the level of what needs to happen. But progressives are willing to give President Biden a chance to, in the words of one activist, get caught trying to get bipartisan support. And if he can`t, then he can go it alone and say he tried.\par \par \uc2 They believe that he needs a space to do that. But there is a timeline. The clock is ticking. Democrats realize they have a limited window to get something big done. And many of them see Memorial Day as a kind of pivot point. By which point they`ll realize that either they can pursue a bipartisan deal or they need to start, you know, moving toward reconciliation which is the filibuster-proof way to get something done. Hallie?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Thanks to the magic of television, we`re able to show you what one of our colleagues in the White House briefing room asked What House Press Secretary Jen Psaki. Kelly O`Donnell put into her this question, Sahil, that I`d like to hear from you on as well. I want to play what Kelly had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Obviously, the House Republicans have some of their own internal matters going on. Does the President think any discord they are having has an influence or comes into the meetings that he will have with leadership? Does that affect how he views sitting down with them at all?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: No. The President knows that there is some introspection going on in the Republican Party right now and a determination about who they`re going to be, who they want to lead them, and what they want to represent moving forward. He`s not going to focus on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Sahil, what is your reporting tell you about how what`s happening in the House Republican sort of world right now could affect any potential negotiations between Democrats and Republicans in the White House moving forward?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: It probably won`t, Hallie, because very few people expect House Republicans to be on board with a major deal.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: And the truth is --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Do you think it will come up? I`m sorry, Sahil. Do you think it`ll come up on Wednesday when they all meet?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Well, surely it will because President Biden wants to be in the driver`s seat on the issue of infrastructure and his safety plans in a way he`s not quite doing on things like police reform and guns where he`s punting to Congress. He`s happy to be in the backseat on those issues. But the economic agenda is crucial for him. That is going to be the defining feature of at least this year of his presidency in the eyes of the White House.\par \par \uc2 So surely he`s going to ask them. He has nothing to lose by meeting with Kevin McCarthy and trying to, you know, gauge where he is and where he isn`t. The fact where Republican politics are make it very difficult for Kevin McCarthy to join forces with the White House on anything. And the White House also recognizes, President Biden recognizes where the Republican politics are which is why he decided, for instance, to go it alone on COVID relief, the $1.9 trillion bill.\par \par \uc2 His advisers believe that he has to give Republicans a chance and give a little more time for potential bipartisanship because infrastructure is not quite the same level of an emergency as COVID. So you can`t quite justify moving as quickly this time around. But the end game may be the same, Hallie. It might just be conversations that don`t really amount to much of anything. And you saw Senate Republican leader Mitch McConnell moving his rhetoric a little bit, saying the window is not just $600 billion, maybe $600 billion to $800 billion.\par \par \uc2 And that came after he got a lot of criticism for saying his 100 percent focus is on stopping the Biden administration, which emboldened some of the Democrats who say, why even bother with bipartisanship?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Sahil Kapur, Cal Perry, thanks to the both of you for your reporting from Washington to Wyoming. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is set to speak from the White House any minute ostensibly on the economy. But we do expect him to likely address the cyberattack on the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 . We`re going to bring those remarks to you live.\par \par \uc2 And up next, we`re also talking about how with hopes growing of a somewhat normal summer maybe, Dr. Fauci reminds us, we`re not exactly out of the woods just yet. How vaccine hesitancy is still spiking in some states and why.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Welcome back. We will take you to the White House live when President Biden begins his remarks on the economy and that attack on the U.S. gas pipeline. But first, a bit of good news in the fight against COVID. Case numbers are at their lowest since last September.\par \par \uc2 But also going down, the number of daily vaccinations. Now below two million for the first time since March. You`ve got several states turning away vaccine supply since the demand is dropping. According to the AP, Wisconsin, for example, has asked the federal government for just 8 percent of its allocated doses for the coming week. Those extra doses will end up being given to other states that do have more of a demand.\par \par \uc2 Loyal MTP viewers will know that Dr. Fauci told Chuck right here on Sunday that meeting the president`s Fourth of July vaccination goal will help keep the virus at a level that`s more manageable. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, BIDEN`S CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER ON CORONAVIRUS: If we get, which we will, to the goals that the president has established, namely, if we get 70 percent of the people vaccinated by the Fourth of July, namely one single dose, and even more thereafter, you may see blips, but if we handle them well, it is unlikely that you`ll see the kind of surge that we saw in the late fall and the early winter.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Connecticut is now the first state in the country to vaccinate 70 percent of adults with at least one shot, according to Governor Ned Lamont.\par \par \uc2 And overseas, the U.S. is facing pressure to expand the pandemic fight outside of its own borders. European leaders, including French President Emmanuel Macron and German Chancellor Angela Merkel are criticizing the U.S. for not exporting more doses, instead choosing to support waving intellectual property rights for vaccines. The U.S. has promised to send overseas 60 million doses of the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Up next, while case numbers seem to be on the sharp decline, concerns are now growing over the actual number of deaths caused by the pandemic. Why the nation`s top experts are now saying the data we`ve been seeing might be way off.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Welcome back. As we told you just a couple of minutes ago, COVID cases are going down in this country. And so are the number of deaths. Even though the true toll of the pandemic is still unknown. The seven-day moving average is at its lowest since last July, although, nearly 700 coronavirus deaths a day is arguably still too high.\par \par \uc2 According to NBC News, nearly 600,000 people have died from this virus. But a new projection from IHME, a key model cited by the White House early on in the pandemic, shows the full extent of the death toll from this pandemic may be 50 percent more and maybe already over 900,000.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: I think there`s no doubt, Chuck, that we are and have been undercounting. What that tells us is something that we`ve known. You know, we`re living through a historic pandemic, the likes of which we haven`t seen in over 100 years.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY, CDC DIRECTOR: We`ve known that the toll of this pandemic is not necessarily just those who have passed from COVID-19 specifically, but deaths relating to access to care, not presenting to care and many other things.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Joining me now Dr. Nahid Bhadelia, infectious diseases physician and associate professor at the Boston University School of Medicine. She is also an MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bhadelia, thanks for being on.\par \par \uc2 DR. NAHID BHADELIA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Thanks for having me, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: OK, so 500,000, 600,000 is the number we have. The projection closer to 900,000. Have we really been undercounting by that much?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Yes. Actually, I agree with what Dr. Fauci and Dr. Walensky has said, is that we`ve known it. I think that we`ve said potentially at least 40 percent more than what we have seen. And the reason why is, Hallie, what`s happened is we know that there`s a certain number of people who were actually officially diagnosed. But there`s a difference between people who are diagnosed before their death and the total number of access mortality.\par \par \uc2 Just for our viewers, the idea of access mortality is that most places with a semblance of public health system are able to count how many people die in a particular month, in a particular year, and compare it to what`s expected from prior years. And what we`ve seen almost all over the world is that access mortality has gone up. And some of that is secondary to the fact that there are more people who died of COVID before being diagnosed with COVID.\par \par \uc2 There are some people who died because they were not able to get access to care. And then there is the inverse part, too, that fewer people have died from other respiratory viruses or injuries or other things that might come from the world being completely open. So the IHME`s new model is trying to assess that and give a more, sort of fuller picture.\par \par \uc2 And this is important, Hallie, because it shows clearly that, you know, we have been underestimating how badly our impact, our mortality has been.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: But it shows globally the impact and the lack of sort of assessment of how many people might have died. India, for example, per their model, has 2 1/2 times the number of deaths that are officially reported are likely what`s estimated per their model. And that`s important for us to get a better handle of how bad this pandemic is.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I`m wondering what you make, Dr. Bhadelia, of the reporting that we`re now getting in that some states are actually turning away doses of the vaccine because there`s just not enough of a demand for it.\par \par \uc2 Do you foresee where we are right now, we`re like, what, less than two months away from the Fourth of July, which has been kind of President Biden`s goal to get back to a little bit more like normal? Do you think that`s going to make that goal a lot harder? And how do you get past this sort of vaccine hesitancy hurdle that we`re facing right now?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Yes. I do think that we`ll get there kicking and screaming. And the reason why is because the vaccines are now being sort of approved for children 12 and 15, you know, as well. And so they`ll be adding to that population level sort of immunity. JACKSON: Yes. Doctor --\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: But the concern is that, you know -- yes.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Dr. Bhadelia, I`m interrupting you and I`m so sorry. But we are hearing from President Biden now who is set to speak on the economy and that cyberattack on the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 . Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And I have been personally briefed every day. The Department of Energy is working directly with Colonial to get the pipelines back online and operating at a full capacity as quickly and safely as possible. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  also is engaged to assess and address this attack. The agencies across the government have acted quickly to mitigate any impact on our fuel supply.\par \par \uc2 And over the weekend, at my direction, the Department of Transportation issued an emergency order to loosen restrictions on truck drivers in order to allow more fuel to be transported by tanker. We`re prepared to take additional steps depending on how quickly the company is able to bring its pipeline back to full operational capacity.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  has determined Colonial`s network was infected by ransomware and it`s a criminal act, obviously. \uc2 FBI\uc2  released details of the attack so others can take steps to prevent being infected.\par \par \uc2 My administration takes issue -- takes this very seriously. We have efforts underway with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ, Department of Justice, to disrupt and prosecute ransomware criminals. And my administration will be pursuing a global effort of ransomware attacks by transnational criminals who often use global money laundering networks to carry them out.\par \par \uc2 My administration is also committed to safeguarding our critical infrastructure which -- much of which is privately owned and managed like Colonial. Private entities are making their own determination on cybersecurity so to jump start greater private sector investment in cybersecurity, we launched a new public-private initiative in April. It began with a 100-day sprint to improve cybersecurity in the electric sector.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll follow that with similar initiatives in natural gas pipelines, water and other sectors. In addition to companies stepping up, we need to invest to safeguard our critical infrastructure. That`s one of the many things my American jobs plan is designed to do. I also want to update the people on the progress that we`ve made in our recovery and the next steps that we`re going to be taking.\par \par \uc2 As we learned Friday, the economy created 266,000 jobs in April. In fact, all together, since the time we took office, we`ve created more than 1.5 million jobs in the country. It`s the most jobs created in the first 100 days of any president on record. More than three times the solid job creation President Carter saw in his first 100 days and more than six times that President Reagan saw.\par \par \uc2 In the three months before I got here, our country was creating roughly 60,000 jobs a month. In the three months since we`ve gotten here, we`ve been creating 500,000, on average 500,000 jobs per month. In the first quarter, our economy grew at 6.4 percent rate, outpacing the growth we`re seeing from our friends in the euro zone. Because of the American Rescue Plan, forecasters are projecting we`ll see the fastest economic growth in nearly 40 years in the months to come.\par \par \uc2 We`re moving in the right direction. So let`s be clear. Our economic plan is working. I never said and no serious analyst ever suggested that climbing out of the deep, deep hole our economy was in would be simple, easy, immediate or perfectly steady. Remember, 22 million Americans lost their jobs in this pandemic. So some months will exceed expectations, others will fall short. The question is, what is the trend line?\par \par \uc2 Are we headed in the right direction? Are we taking the right steps to keep it going? And the answer clearly is yes. I also want to offer a bit more detail on the month`s jobs numbers in particular. These monthly reports are a snapshot, as you all know. They take one week, a moment in time. Since this snapshot was taken around the week of April the 12th, since then COVID-19 counts are down by more than 40 percent since then.\par \par \uc2 And vaccination rates among working age Americans has roughly doubled since then. This survey was taken before adults -- before every adult was eligible to be vaccinated. So back then, 18 percent of working age adults were fully vaccinated. Today if it were taken, 34 percent are fully vaccinated. No wonder things in America feel better today than it did back when the survey was taken.\par \par \uc2 With that context, here are the steps we`re going to be taking to maintain our progress. First, today we are opening a portal for state and local governments to apply for the first funds from the American Rescue Plan. Because states and local governments have to balance their budgets, a lot of them had to lay off employees when the economy -- state employees and local employees when the economy slowed and tax revenues fell.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about 1.3 million state and local employees out of work. But now the money we`re distributing -- going to be distributing now is going to make it possible for an awful lot of educators, first responders, sanitation workers to go back to work. Second, this is a year-long program.\par \par \uc2 Second, we`re delivering assistance to tens of thousands of restaurants and bars across the country. Today we`re sending out the first relief checks to 16,000 hard-hit restaurants with help for many more to follow. A lot more people are going to be employed.\par \par \uc2 Third, the employers bring back laid-off workers to help them do that. We`re going to remind them that there is some progress they can take advantage -- some programs they can take advantage of. For example, employers can hire back their laid-off workers part time without those workers having to give up all their unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 Here`s another example. In the American Rescue Plan, we expanded a tax incentive called Employee Retention Tax Credit. This can provide a recovering small or medium-sized business a direct credit for each worker they keep on or a job that they rehire.\par \par \uc2 Fourth, we`re going to provide real help for people struggling with the challenge of child care, which is making it hard for many parents who need to work, especially women, to get back to work. During this crisis, thousands of child care providers and centers were forced to close because they couldn`t make ends meet with fewer students and higher costs to keep them safe. As a result, parents lost the support system they depended on for child care.\par \par \uc2 And tens of thousands of child care workers lost their jobs. In fact, there are 150,000 fewer child care jobs now than there were pre-pandemic. The American Rescue Plan has already allocated funds to states to address the immediate challenge to our economy and of too few child care operations. This is the largest investment in child care since World War II.\par \par \uc2 Today my administration is releasing guidance to states to help get the funds they need out the door as quickly as possible to allow dramatic expansion and availability of child care in this country. With these funds, states can help hundreds of thousands of providers reopen and stay open and provide safe and healthy learning environments for more than five million children. These funds will also allow states to provide over 800,000 families with subsidies to pay for child care.\par \par \uc2 Simply put, this can help working parents get back to work. This one-time investment is a real answer to a real problem our economy is facing right now. Look, the fifth thing we`re going to do, we`re going to make it clear that anyone collecting unemployment who is offered a suitable job must take the job or lose their unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 There are a few COVID-19-related exceptions so that people aren`t forced to choose between their basic safety and a paycheck. But otherwise that`s the law. I know there`s been a lot of discussion since Friday`s report that people are being paid to stay home rather than go to work. Well, we don`t see much evidence of that. That is a major factor. We don`t see -- look, it`s easy to say, the line has been because of the generous unemployment benefits that it`s a major factor in labor shortages.\par \par \uc2 Americans want to work. Americans want to work. As my dad used to say, a job is about a lot more than a paycheck, it`s about your dignity, your place in the community, being able to look your kid in the eye and say everything is going to be OK. I think the people who claim Americans won`t work even if they find a good and fair opportunity, underestimate the American people.\par \par \uc2 So we`ll insist that the law is followed with respect to benefits or we`re not going to turn our backs on our fellow Americans. 22 million people lost their jobs in this pandemic through no fault of their own. They lost their jobs to a virus and to a government that bungled its response to the crisis and failed to protect them.\par \par \uc2 We still have eight million fewer jobs than we did when the pandemic started. And for many of those folks, unemployment benefits are a lifeline. No one should be allowed to game the system, and we`ll insist the law is followed, but let`s not take our eye off the ball. Families, families who are just trying to put food on the table, keep a roof over their head, they aren`t the problem. We need to stay focused on the real problems in front of us, beating this pandemic and creating jobs.\par \par \uc2 Again, the law is clear. If you`re receiving unemployment benefits and you`re offered a suitable job, you can`t refuse that job and just keep getting the unemployment benefits. We`re also working to overcome all the barriers that may be making it harder for people to get back to work. Child care, getting vaccinated, getting schools open. We`re making progress in all these things, and we need others to step up and play a role as well.\par \par \uc2 For example, we need employers to step up in a couple of ways. First, we need them to get help to their people and get them vaccinated. Two weeks ago, I announced that we`ll provide a tax credit to employers if they give workers paid time off to get a shot and recover from the effects -- the side effects if there are any. Our economy can`t achieve its full potential until we get more people vaccinated. Employers can help that.\par \par \uc2 We also need to recognize that people will come back to work if they`re paid a decent wage. The last Congress, before I became president, gave businesses over $1.4 trillion in COVID relief. Congress may have approved that money, but let`s be clear. The money came from the American people, and it went from the American people to American businesses, many of them big businesses, to help them get through this pandemic and keep their doors open.\par \par \uc2 I`m not questioning it. It was the right thing to do. But my expectation is that as our economy comes back, these companies will provide fair wages and safe work environments. And if they do, they`ll find plenty of workers and we`re all going to come out of this together better than before. So we need to stay focused on creating jobs and beating this pandemic today and building back better for tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 The American Rescue Plan is just that, a rescue plan. It`s to get us out of the crisis and back on track, but it`s not nearly enough. That`s why we need the American Jobs Plan, which is an eight-year investment, an eight- year investment strategy to make sure working people of this country get to share in the benefits of a rising economy and to put us in a position to win the competition with China and the rest of the world for the 21st century.\par \par \uc2 That`s the next stage. That`s what we`re doing right now. We`re working to get that passed. I want to thank you all and may God protect our troops. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Mr. President, if you can`t protect criminal infrastructure from a criminal act. How can you possibly protect it from an escape actor?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You can do both, and we will.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Do you think Russia is involved at all?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m going to have -- I`m going to be meeting with President Putin. And so far there is no evidence based on from our intelligence people that Russia is involved, although there is evidence that the actors` ransomware is in Russia. They have some responsibility to deal with this. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: (INAUDIBLE), Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC HOST: Remarks from President Biden in the East Room there. He was addressing the economic recovery from the pandemic but also talking about the news of the day. You heard a question from Peter Doocy, I think, at the end, asking if President Biden believes that Vladimir Putin is responsible and the president didn`t entirely engage on that question.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to see you. I`m Geoff Bennett in for my friend Katy Tur. As we come on the air, there is breaking news on what is potentially the most crippling cyberattack every against the U.S. In a short time ago, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  confirmed Russians are behind it. First, what it means for you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210511eh5a0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 10, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Hallie Jackson, Ken Dilanian, Mike Memoli, Sahil Kapur, Cal Perry,  Nahid Bhadelia \par \uc2 9,580 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, MSNBC SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: If it`s Monday, a presidential response to a crippling cyber-attack with President Biden`s set to speak just a couple minutes from now and expected to touch on that Russian criminal attack that shut down a big us pipeline with big national security implications.\par \par \uc2 He`ll also hit on the economy with the White House facing lingering questions about the most recent disappointing jobs report in the midst of its push for trillions of dollars in new federal programs.\par \par \uc2 And the Houses top Republican formerly throwing his support behind Liz Cheney`s challenger for a job that`s technically not even open yet. As Republicans gear up for a vote to give the boot to Liz Cheney from leadership, what it all means for the party for President Biden`s agenda and for our democracy.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Monday. It`s MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Hallie Jackson in for Chuck Dodd. And we`re coming on the air with some new developments in the White House`s response to that cyber attack on a big U.S. fuel pipeline which is still shut down. You`ve got the \uc2 FBI\uc2  just releasing a statement attributing that attack on \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2  to a Russian criminal group confirming NBC`s reporting that group DarkSide might be responsible.\par \par \uc2 The pipeline carries about 45 percent of the East Coast fuel supply. That`s your gas. That`s your diesel. We expect to hear from President Biden for the first time on camera about this when he speaks from the White House in just about 15 minutes or so. We`re going to take you there live. You`ve also got White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki and two top national security officials who`ve just wrapped up briefing reporters on the response to this attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNE NEUBERGER DEPUTY NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR FOR CYBER AND EMERGING TECHNOLOGY: In this case, the ransomware that was used is a known variant. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has investigated many cases of this in the past, as I noted, beginning in October. So, the first and most important thing is to ensure that systems are patched and that cybersecurity is maintained at the level needed in a given network. We want to see ransomware not be successful. And that begins with greater resilience, particularly in critical infrastructure networks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: One of the concerns, of course, is what does this means for gas prices. Those haven`t really been affected yet, but that might change the longer this pipeline stays shut down. And you`ve got Commerce Secretary Gina Raimondo warning, we should be ready for more attacks like this in the future.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO, SECRETARY OF COMMERCE: Unfortunately, these sorts of attacks are becoming more frequent. They`re here to stay and we have to work in partnership with businesses to secure networks to defend ourselves against these attacks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So, the White House is grappling with this cyberattack as it looks ahead to a very busy week here in Washington, a week in which Republicans are poised to purge Congresswoman Liz Cheney from her leadership position, and a week in which President Biden is set to reach out to Republicans to try to generate some bipartisan support for his multitrillion-dollar agenda.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s get to it. I`m joined now by Ken Dilanian who covers intelligence and national security for us, leading our coverage on this pipeline attack. I`m also joined by NBC News White House Correspondent Mike Memoli, and by Kiersten Todt, Managing Director of the Cyber Readiness Institute. She also served on President Obama`s National Cyber Security Commission.\par \par \uc2 Ken, let me start with you on this. I know you`ve been on the forefront of our reporting here. We just heard from the White House during that briefing, no response to the question of whether this company, whether Colonial actually paid the ransom that was demanded from it, although they did talk about the need for companies -- private companies to shore up and make sure that they have the right cyber defenses in place.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: That`s right, Hallie. Yes, that person speaking was Anne Neuberger, who`s a former \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2  official who`s now heading up the White House cyber response. And she was alluding to what is the crux of the problem, in this case, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 This was not a sophisticated attack by a foreign intelligence service of China or Russia. This is a criminal gang using basic ransomware from everything we can tell, the kind that unfortunately, has become all too common. But in this case, it`s really capturing the imagination because this was such critical infrastructure, and it may lead to a spike in gas prices, and it may lead to service interruptions.\par \par \uc2 And it`s a wake-up call that, you know what, 85 percent of American infrastructure is in private hands, and the government has very little visibility on whether these private corporations have good cybersecurity or bad cybersecurity. In this case, whatever cybersecurity they had was not enough to keep out these attackers.\par \par \uc2 And just so people understand, ransomware is when the hackers get into the network and encrypt data and demand payment to free that data. And in this case, they also see some data and threatened to leak it on the internet. So, it`s an extortion scheme, one that most companies should be able to defend against, this one was not able to.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why the button administration is contemplating some regulations on cybersecurity, which have never been successful to make companies have basic cyber hygiene, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Ken, that`s an important point that you`re making here, right, because you`re talking about how this is -- this appears to be a Russian criminal enterprise, right, this group DarkSide. The White House, what you heard from Neuberger from the podium there at the White House was that they are looking into the potential.\par \par \uc2 It sounded like to me, Ken, and tell me if you`re reading this differently, about what kind of connections might there be to the Russian government. Is that something that would be typical in a situation like this?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Yes, in a sense that many of these Russian criminal groups or Eastern European criminal groups freelance for Russian intelligence services. There`s a symbiotic relationship there. So, that`s one thing to investigate. But even if there`s no Kremlin hand on this at all, it remains the case that Russia grants sanctuary, grants impunity to these hacker groups, as long as they don`t attack Russia, as long as they attack the English speaking West, which is what these this group says that they do.\par \par \uc2 And that`s a problem. I mean, that`s essentially this group attacking American interest and then retreating back to Russia, where beyond the reach of the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2 , that`s a national security threat that only the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  can deal with, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Ken Dilanian, thank you for that and for your great reporting. I appreciate that. Mike Memoli, let me go to you because I know you`ve been monitoring this news, this press briefing. I know we`re going to hear from President Biden, if he`s not running behind schedule, in just about 10 minutes. We`re gonna bring that to you live when it happens.\par \par \uc2 And the expectation is he will talk about this because, Mike, this is not nothing, right? This is a pipeline that carries, as we said, nearly half of the fuel supply to the East Coast. It is 5500 miles long. It winds through all the states that you see on the screen. This is significant.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Oh, absolutely, Hallie. When you think about what the conversation at the White House has been over these last few weeks, it`s been about the nation`s infrastructure and the need for critical improvements of really after years in which the President`s argument has been, we`ve neglected it while other countries have been fortifying, shoring up, expanding their infrastructure capacity.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the light -- this is what we would more traditionally call critical infrastructure, this area of cyber warfare that as former President Obama once put it is becoming really the Wild West in terms of an emerging battlefield for not just nation-states, but criminal actors, potentially given safe harbor by nation-states, as Ken was just outlining with Russia.\par \par \uc2 Now, as far as what we just heard from the White House, you heard from Neuberger as well as Liz Sherwood-Randall, who is the White House national -- Homeland Security Adviser, excuse me. They are -- they said that the White House has been in touch with Colonial throughout the past few days. The President has been briefed regularly.\par \par \uc2 And it`ll be interesting, I think, the President`s response is designed to piggyback on what we just heard which is that the White House is on top of this. This has been so much of the approach from this White House since replacing President Trump in January.\par \par \uc2 And think back to what President-Elect Biden at the time was saying in December when we learned first about the \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  hack. He directly blamed President Trump for, as he put it, taking his eye off the ball, for allowing the nation`s guard to be let down in the face of this cyber hacking that we`ve been seeing increasingly over time.\par \par \uc2 And that`s one reason why the administration has been in naming, for instance, Chris Inglis as the first-ever National Cyber Director. Neuberger, who we`ve not mentioned several times, is actually a newly created position on the National Security Council focused exclusively on cyber. And so, they want to make the point, reassure Americans that they`ve been on top of this, while also taking steps in the meantime to deal with issues like gas prices, the Department of Transportation, relaxing some of those freight guidelines to allow more fuel to be transported on the roads, if not through pipelines.\par \par \uc2 But certainly, expect the President to try to reassure Americans that this administration, after four years in which they feel their predecessor did not necessarily keep their eye on the ball here, is firmly in control of this.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So, Kiersten, let me pick up on this point here. What is more alarming to you, the fact that this specific attack happened, or the potential vulnerabilities in the nation cyber infrastructure that it`s now exposing?\par \par \uc2 KIERSTEN TODT, MANAGING DIRECTOR, CYBER READINESS INSTITUTE: So, I`ll answer by saying both, because the issue is that, as you said repeatedly, you know, this is a critical infrastructure company that carries 45 percent of fuel to the east coast. It is an owner and operator, but it`s privately held. And so, what it calls into question is the ability for the government to weigh cybersecurity standards on these companies.\par \par \uc2 We had movement in Congress in 2012 to look at cybersecurity standards and Congress deemed that it was too onerous on these companies to have these basic cybersecurity standards. I think we`ve far outgrown that perception and we`ve absolutely got to be -- we have to be working with these owners and operators to know what their baseline level of cybersecurity is.\par \par \uc2 The other piece to this is this type of company should not have been this vulnerable to this kind of attack. Ken mentioned earlier, this is not a sophisticated nation-state attack of the precision that we saw with \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2 . This is a ransomware attack. We`re seeing this across the nation.\par \par \uc2 The good news is that the Biden administration has been on ransomware from day one. The Department of Justice released an 81-page report that it developed in collaboration with the private sector a few weeks ago, and it highlighted this issue around countries, nation-states like Russia that are providing safe havens to these criminal actor groups and the need to penalize and punish them.\par \par \uc2 It also talks about making sure that cryptocurrency is liable under money laundering regulations. We saw the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  identify ransomware as the first of its 60-day sprints. And Secretary Mayorkas was just talking this past week on the threat of ransomware and what needs to be done.\par \par \uc2 So, the good news is that this is not a surprise, we know that we`re on top of it, but how this government needs to work with owners and operators of critical infrastructure is certainly the next step in securing and as in Neuberger said, making sure our networks are resilient to these types of attacks so we`re not having a discussion on whether or not to pay ransom.\par \par \uc2 We should not be in a position where in order to keep our critical infrastructure operating, we have to pay a ransom.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You heard this thread come up, Kiersten, in the briefing room right about what is the onus on the White House now? What kind of guidance should the administration for example be giving to these private companies? And then the other question, of course, is what happens down Pennsylvania Avenue as it relates to Congress and what they should be doing as you`ve alluded to.\par \par \uc2 What ultimately -- you talked about the next steps -- what should that next step be? Like, what does the administration need to do today, tomorrow, this week, in order to, A, give guidance to these private companies, and B, make sure that something like this can`t happen again?\par \par \uc2 TODT: Well, again, the good news is that the Biden administration has been working proactively with industry on looking at these pieces and how to work together to be more resilient. And I think this is one of the key pieces right now is you can`t expect government to do this by itself. We can`t ask the private companies to do this by themselves.\par \par \uc2 We have to come together to identify what is that reasonable, practical, secure set of standards that we need to hold these owners and operators to have secure standards by, and then working with Congress to potentially enact legislation. But I think let`s start with that collaboration between industry and government.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen industry step forward to say, hey, we want to work with government on this in the energy sector. We need real-time information sharing. So, there is absolutely an opportunity for collaboration. And we should be encouraged by the actions by this White House to have already been on top of this to be able to create, I think, solid recommendations, but more importantly, the execution of those recommendations to secure our networks.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Mike, Senator Cassidy was on MEET THE PRESS and talk with Chuck about this exact issue. I want to play free what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Congress has attempted to fix that. There`s been problems in the past with sharing classified information with private entities, and Congress has passed the law to fix that. But it`s going to take an ongoing relationship. By the way, a bipartisan relationship in which we better equip small businesses and large businesses to withstand cyber attacks.\par \par \uc2 Chuck, the implications for this for national security cannot be overstated. And I promise you this is something that Republicans and Democrats can work together on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Mike, do you see that as realistic? Could this be an area where there is, in your view, bipartisan cooperation?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Well, it certainly has to be. The area on Capitol Hill which has tended to be least partisan, although there have been some very real challenges to that over these last few years, of course, has been in the intelligence committees both in the House and the Senate side.\par \par \uc2 And typically, I think with Mark Warner as the chairman, you would see Marco Rubio, the ranking member on the Republican side, this has been something that they`ve been very focused on. Mark Warner of Virginia, for instance, with a background in the tech industry would certainly like to advance this. And this is something that the White House is going to be pushing as well.\par \par \uc2 But as I think I referred to when President Obama call this sort of the Wild West, this is an area that the White House is also dealing with potentially some new guidance, as we`ve been discussing, that needs to go out to the communities themselves. A reporter pressed Neuberger, for instance, about this idea of is the FBI`s guidance to private companies to still not pay ransom when these attacks are made on their infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 And they said they recognize that increasingly, because of the nature of this data, especially if they don`t have backups, if they don`t have the resilience in place when they have these kinds of intrusions, that they really have no choice in the matter. And so, the White House is looking at other things that they could potentially be doing especially so much now.\par \par \uc2 We saw over the weekend with Elon Musk on Saturday Night Live \uc2 Dogecoin\uc2 , Bitcoin as the funding mechanism by which some of these transactions tend to take place. Treasury -- the Treasury Department is looking at ways in which they could better police this on the cyber front as well.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Kiersten, we had the \uc2 FBI\uc2  coming out and putting the blame on this group DarkSide. Was it unusual to you that in an instance like this, in a major cyber attack like this, that happened relatively quickly in the process?\par \par \uc2 TODT: I think it`s very reassuring because one of the failures that we`ve had is that right now, and we`ve talked about this earlier, individuals who impose ransomware attacks, who have ransomware attacks, operate with impunity. We have no ability right now to indict, to prosecute and to incarcerate these individuals. We have to change that.\par \par \uc2 So, I commend the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for coming out to say, here`s a criminal actor group, we know that Russia harbors this group. Then we have to come together as the Department of Justice report outlined with an international coalition to work against these nation-states and to ensure that they penalize those nation-states that harbor these criminals.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the challenge with cybersecurity is that you can act and commit these offenses and you have no consequences. If an individual had placed a bomb on that pipeline and it exploded, we would have no question right now, how are we responding.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve got to be acting in the cyber world the consequences the same way we would in the physical world. There`s not always a one for one, but in this case there is. And so we have to make sure that these individuals don`t walk away with that anonymity and that belief that they can get away with this.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Kiersten Todt, thank you very much for your expert perspective on this. Mike Memoli, great reporting. Thank you. We are still waiting for President Biden to speak on this issue, as well as the economy. It was supposed to start about 24 seconds ago or so. He`s running a little bit behind, but we will bring that to you live when it happens.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, coming up, we`ve got this deep dive into frankly, a bit of an uncertain future for the GOP as they go all-in on their loyalty to President Trump. The plan to abandon Liz Cheney is just about 48 hours from now.\par \par \uc2 And later, as the U.S. appears to be inching closer to a new normal, some very harsh realities of the pandemic are still on the table. We`re talking about some new concerns over what the actual COVID death count really is.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Welcome back. As we wait to see President Biden live from the White House, you can see the White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki is still briefing reporters as we speak. We`re also going to talk a bit about what is happening on the other end of Pennsylvania Avenue, and that is this push to oust Congresswoman Liz Cheney from her leadership role as the number three House Republican.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got the top Republican leader Kevin McCarthy publicly endorsing now the person who is expected to replace Cheney. Here he is explaining his rationale.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Any member can take whatever position they believe in. That`s what -- that`s what the voters vote on the individuals and they make that decision. What we`re talking about, it`s a position in leadership. We are in one of our biggest battles ever for this nation in the direction whether this next century will be ours. As conference chair, you have one of the most critical jobs as the messenger of going forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So, McCarthy`s ultimate takeaway was that a member in leadership cannot have a position that`s at odds with where most of the rest of the conference is. And that position with regards to Cheney is that she is telling the truth in the face of former President Trump`s lies about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 So, as for timing, the way the calendar shakes out, Republicans could make their moves to expel Cheney from leadership as early as Wednesday, but New York Republican Congresswoman Elise Stefanik expected to replace her.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn now to a couple of my NBC News colleagues Sahil Kapur is on Capitol Hill, Cal Perry is out in Casper, Wyoming where he`s been talking with some of Liz Cheney`s constituents. Sahil, let me start with you here. When you look ahead to the way these next 48 hours ago, it seems like the writing is on the wall in Sharpie. Could anything change the trajectory right now?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: It`s highly unlikely Hallie, I think it`s all been a foregone conclusion that Elise Stefanik, the Congresswoman from New York, will replace Liz Cheney and that Liz Cheney will be ousted. It will happen on Wednesday in a conference meeting where basically one Republican member has to make a motion of no confidence against Cheney, then if leadership considers it of immediate importance so that they can call a vote, a simple majority can vote her out.\par \par \uc2 This has -- the writing has been on the wall for quite a while. And the message that Republicans are essentially sending is that you can`t be in party leadership if you`re going to actively contradict President Trump. He is the leader of the party. And Liz Cheney, for actively debunking his falsehoods about the 2020 election was not appreciated by her party. And that`s the reason it appears she has to go.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So, it`s the act of debunking, but it`s also what you kept hearing, Sahil, again and again and what you continue to hear from those Republicans who want to see her gone is that it`s a messaging issue, right. She`s not on message with where the rest of the GOP is. Here, for example, is Congressman Banks on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM BANKS (R-IN): Any leader who`s not focused on that, on pushing back against the by the radical dangerous Biden agenda at this point needs to be replaced. But the point here is that we are almost entirely unified on that mission except for Liz Cheney.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let me ask you --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: But the person who`s not talking about President Biden`s policy agenda is Donald Trump, right? The former president releasing more than 20 statements over the last six weeks continuing to push his lies about the 2020 election, five in just the last week alone, Sahil. Why is it then that Republicans are blaming Cheney and not the former president?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Yes, there`s a bit of a contradiction in that Hallie. They`re saying Cheney needs to go because she keeps talking about the past, whereas she`s really only responding to former President Trump who has put out a steady stream of statements talking about the past, talking about the 2020 election and insisting falsely that it was illegitimate.\par \par \uc2 The real irony here about the message that they`re -- that they`re discussing is that Liz Cheney has a more ideologically conservative voting record than Elise Stefanik. One of the signature achievements, if not, the signature achievement of the Trump era was the 2017 Tax Law. Liz Cheney voted for it. Elise Stefanik voted against it and yet Stefanik is seen as the right messenger to carry forward the party`s message.\par \par \uc2 That tells you about all you need to know about what the message is. It`s Donald Trump. It`s not conservative ideology. It`s not about taxes. It`s not about spending. Hallie?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And Sahil, I know that you and our colleagues on Capitol Hill have been reporting on what exactly are Liz Cheney`s next steps. And it`s our understanding, your understanding from a source familiar, that she is obviously planning to stay in Congress, to run again.\par \par \uc2 Cal, you`re in her district talking with people who she serves. Tell us more about what you`re hearing.\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. And before we play this sound, people are going to say, well, you went to Trump country. Well, this was Liz Cheney country. Look, she won her election by a landslide. Here in Casper, she won by an even bigger margin. But as we`re hearing around the country, what seems to be the issue here for voters is speaking out against the former president seems to be the ultimate betrayal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think Liz Cheney needs to go. She does not represent the people of Wyoming. 73 percent of the people here in Wyoming voted for Trump. And she basically stabbed us in the back with she felt it was the right thing to do. Well, I didn`t vote for her -- for her feelings. I voted for her to represent us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think she`s a RINO. I think she votes on the liberal side, and we voted her in as a Republican. She doesn`t have our best interests at heart.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She`s a traitor.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Why? What did she do?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, she`s a Republican, and then she votes against the Republican president. She`s a traitor. She gets what she deserves to get run out of office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Keeping in mind that Liz Cheney voted with the president more than 93 percent of her time while the President was in office. I think one of the most fascinating things about this, and you were just talking about it, is Liz Cheney is going to be running for reelection here in 18 months.\par \par \uc2 And when you talk to liberals, when you talk to Democrats who live in the state, they`re now basically living in the upside-down, a world in which they may support Liz Cheney because she`s the more liberal have two options on the ballot in 18 months, Hallie?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: That is something to think about it, Cal. Two questions to follow up with you on this. Number one, did you find anybody who was willing to speak to you publicly about their support or at least, you know, getting where she`s coming from for Liz Cheney?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: I was reminded of one person who spoke to us yesterday in Casper who was willing to talk on camera. But most liberals that we bumped into said they didn`t want to speak on camera. They felt like the minority. It`s a little different three hours south of here in Cheyenne, but no, people just didn`t want to talk on camera about their support for Liz Cheney.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You also played an interview -- part of your interview with a woman, I believe, who said something like I didn`t vote for her -- for her feelings, right, to tell me how she feels. But on the policy issue, what did some of these folks tell you when you pointed out or to the idea that, as you say, Liz Cheney actually votes in line more with the policies that Donald Trump represented than Elise Stefanik did, as Sahil points out, who, for example, opposed the former president`s biggest policy push in 2017, which were his tax cuts?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Not a great answer for that, except I will say it feels like it`s not Liz Cheney versus Donald Trump as much as it`s Donald Trump versus America again. And I keep hearing this answer as I talk to people, and it`s true on vaccine hesitancy -- excuse me -- as well. I`ll ask people, what is this really about? Is this really about the vaccine, the same thing on Liz Cheney? Is this really about Liz Cheney?\par \par \uc2 And the answer inevitably comes back, no, this is about former President Donald Trump and what happened during the election and what happened on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Sahil, let me ask you, because Congress is dealing -- you know, the House Republicans have this on their plate. This is obviously expected to happen as early as Wednesday. But there`s a whole lot of other stuff on the to do list for lawmakers as they come back into town now and back into session starting with infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 And you had Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell who`s shifting a little bit where he is on this. He initially said, OK, I would support something like a $600 billion plan, now it`s like six to $800 billion. Do you think that is a sign that there could actually be some compromise or is this just more two parties talking over each other at this point?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR Hallie, it really depends on who you ask. There are a variety of opinions about this among Democrats. Some figures in the White House believe it`s still possible that they could get a bipartisan deal with Democrats. There are Democrats here on Capitol Hill who are insisting that they at least try it.\par \par \uc2 There are progressive lawmakers in the House in the Senate who I`ve talked to who say this is a fool`s errand, that Republicans are simply not going to be on board with anything major, anything close to the level of what needs to happen. But progressives are willing to give President Biden a chance to, in the words of one activist, get caught trying to get bipartisan support. And if he can`t, then he can go it alone and say he tried.\par \par \uc2 They believe that he needs a space to do that. But there is a timeline. The clock is ticking. Democrats realize they have a limited window to get something big done. And many of them see Memorial Day as a kind of pivot point. By which point they`ll realize that either they can pursue a bipartisan deal or they need to start, you know, moving toward reconciliation which is the filibuster-proof way to get something done. Hallie?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Thanks to the magic of television, we`re able to show you what one of our colleagues in the White House briefing room asked What House Press Secretary Jen Psaki. Kelly O`Donnell put into her this question, Sahil, that I`d like to hear from you on as well. I want to play what Kelly had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Obviously, the House Republicans have some of their own internal matters going on. Does the President think any discord they are having has an influence or comes into the meetings that he will have with leadership? Does that affect how he views sitting down with them at all?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: No. The President knows that there is some introspection going on in the Republican Party right now and a determination about who they`re going to be, who they want to lead them, and what they want to represent moving forward. He`s not going to focus on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Sahil, what is your reporting tell you about how what`s happening in the House Republican sort of world right now could affect any potential negotiations between Democrats and Republicans in the White House moving forward?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: It probably won`t, Hallie, because very few people expect House Republicans to be on board with a major deal.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: And the truth is --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Do you think it will come up? I`m sorry, Sahil. Do you think it`ll come up on Wednesday when they all meet?\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Well, surely it will because President Biden wants to be in the driver`s seat on the issue of infrastructure and his safety plans in a way he`s not quite doing on things like police reform and guns where he`s punting to Congress. He`s happy to be in the backseat on those issues. But the economic agenda is crucial for him. That is going to be the defining feature of at least this year of his presidency in the eyes of the White House.\par \par \uc2 So surely he`s going to ask them. He has nothing to lose by meeting with Kevin McCarthy and trying to, you know, gauge where he is and where he isn`t. The fact where Republican politics are make it very difficult for Kevin McCarthy to join forces with the White House on anything. And the White House also recognizes, President Biden recognizes where the Republican politics are which is why he decided, for instance, to go it alone on COVID relief, the $1.9 trillion bill.\par \par \uc2 His advisers believe that he has to give Republicans a chance and give a little more time for potential bipartisanship because infrastructure is not quite the same level of an emergency as COVID. So you can`t quite justify moving as quickly this time around. But the end game may be the same, Hallie. It might just be conversations that don`t really amount to much of anything. And you saw Senate Republican leader Mitch McConnell moving his rhetoric a little bit, saying the window is not just $600 billion, maybe $600 billion to $800 billion.\par \par \uc2 And that came after he got a lot of criticism for saying his 100 percent focus is on stopping the Biden administration, which emboldened some of the Democrats who say, why even bother with bipartisanship?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Sahil Kapur, Cal Perry, thanks to the both of you for your reporting from Washington to Wyoming. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is set to speak from the White House any minute ostensibly on the economy. But we do expect him to likely address the cyberattack on the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 . We`re going to bring those remarks to you live.\par \par \uc2 And up next, we`re also talking about how with hopes growing of a somewhat normal summer maybe, Dr. Fauci reminds us, we`re not exactly out of the woods just yet. How vaccine hesitancy is still spiking in some states and why.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Welcome back. We will take you to the White House live when President Biden begins his remarks on the economy and that attack on the U.S. gas pipeline. But first, a bit of good news in the fight against COVID. Case numbers are at their lowest since last September.\par \par \uc2 But also going down, the number of daily vaccinations. Now below two million for the first time since March. You`ve got several states turning away vaccine supply since the demand is dropping. According to the AP, Wisconsin, for example, has asked the federal government for just 8 percent of its allocated doses for the coming week. Those extra doses will end up being given to other states that do have more of a demand.\par \par \uc2 Loyal MTP viewers will know that Dr. Fauci told Chuck right here on Sunday that meeting the president`s Fourth of July vaccination goal will help keep the virus at a level that`s more manageable. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, BIDEN`S CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER ON CORONAVIRUS: If we get, which we will, to the goals that the president has established, namely, if we get 70 percent of the people vaccinated by the Fourth of July, namely one single dose, and even more thereafter, you may see blips, but if we handle them well, it is unlikely that you`ll see the kind of surge that we saw in the late fall and the early winter.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Connecticut is now the first state in the country to vaccinate 70 percent of adults with at least one shot, according to Governor Ned Lamont.\par \par \uc2 And overseas, the U.S. is facing pressure to expand the pandemic fight outside of its own borders. European leaders, including French President Emmanuel Macron and German Chancellor Angela Merkel are criticizing the U.S. for not exporting more doses, instead choosing to support waving intellectual property rights for vaccines. The U.S. has promised to send overseas 60 million doses of the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Up next, while case numbers seem to be on the sharp decline, concerns are now growing over the actual number of deaths caused by the pandemic. Why the nation`s top experts are now saying the data we`ve been seeing might be way off.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Welcome back. As we told you just a couple of minutes ago, COVID cases are going down in this country. And so are the number of deaths. Even though the true toll of the pandemic is still unknown. The seven-day moving average is at its lowest since last July, although, nearly 700 coronavirus deaths a day is arguably still too high.\par \par \uc2 According to NBC News, nearly 600,000 people have died from this virus. But a new projection from IHME, a key model cited by the White House early on in the pandemic, shows the full extent of the death toll from this pandemic may be 50 percent more and maybe already over 900,000.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: I think there`s no doubt, Chuck, that we are and have been undercounting. What that tells us is something that we`ve known. You know, we`re living through a historic pandemic, the likes of which we haven`t seen in over 100 years.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY, CDC DIRECTOR: We`ve known that the toll of this pandemic is not necessarily just those who have passed from COVID-19 specifically, but deaths relating to access to care, not presenting to care and many other things.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Joining me now Dr. Nahid Bhadelia, infectious diseases physician and associate professor at the Boston University School of Medicine. She is also an MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bhadelia, thanks for being on.\par \par \uc2 DR. NAHID BHADELIA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Thanks for having me, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: OK, so 500,000, 600,000 is the number we have. The projection closer to 900,000. Have we really been undercounting by that much?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Yes. Actually, I agree with what Dr. Fauci and Dr. Walensky has said, is that we`ve known it. I think that we`ve said potentially at least 40 percent more than what we have seen. And the reason why is, Hallie, what`s happened is we know that there`s a certain number of people who were actually officially diagnosed. But there`s a difference between people who are diagnosed before their death and the total number of access mortality.\par \par \uc2 Just for our viewers, the idea of access mortality is that most places with a semblance of public health system are able to count how many people die in a particular month, in a particular year, and compare it to what`s expected from prior years. And what we`ve seen almost all over the world is that access mortality has gone up. And some of that is secondary to the fact that there are more people who died of COVID before being diagnosed with COVID.\par \par \uc2 There are some people who died because they were not able to get access to care. And then there is the inverse part, too, that fewer people have died from other respiratory viruses or injuries or other things that might come from the world being completely open. So the IHME`s new model is trying to assess that and give a more, sort of fuller picture.\par \par \uc2 And this is important, Hallie, because it shows clearly that, you know, we have been underestimating how badly our impact, our mortality has been.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: But it shows globally the impact and the lack of sort of assessment of how many people might have died. India, for example, per their model, has 2 1/2 times the number of deaths that are officially reported are likely what`s estimated per their model. And that`s important for us to get a better handle of how bad this pandemic is.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I`m wondering what you make, Dr. Bhadelia, of the reporting that we`re now getting in that some states are actually turning away doses of the vaccine because there`s just not enough of a demand for it.\par \par \uc2 Do you foresee where we are right now, we`re like, what, less than two months away from the Fourth of July, which has been kind of President Biden`s goal to get back to a little bit more like normal? Do you think that`s going to make that goal a lot harder? And how do you get past this sort of vaccine hesitancy hurdle that we`re facing right now?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Yes. I do think that we`ll get there kicking and screaming. And the reason why is because the vaccines are now being sort of approved for children 12 and 15, you know, as well. And so they`ll be adding to that population level sort of immunity.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes. Doctor --\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: But the concern is that, you know -- yes.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Dr. Bhadelia, I`m interrupting you and I`m so sorry. But we are hearing from President Biden now who is set to speak on the economy and that cyberattack on the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 . Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And I have been personally briefed every day. The Department of Energy is working directly with Colonial to get the pipelines back online and operating at a full capacity as quickly and safely as possible. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  also is engaged to assess and address this attack. The agencies across the government have acted quickly to mitigate any impact on our fuel supply.\par \par \uc2 And over the weekend, at my direction, the Department of Transportation issued an emergency order to loosen restrictions on truck drivers in order to allow more fuel to be transported by tanker. We`re prepared to take additional steps depending on how quickly the company is able to bring its pipeline back to full operational capacity.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  has determined Colonial`s network was infected by ransomware and it`s a criminal act, obviously. \uc2 FBI\uc2  released details of the attack so others can take steps to prevent being infected.\par \par \uc2 My administration takes issue -- takes this very seriously. We have efforts underway with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ, Department of Justice, to disrupt and prosecute ransomware criminals. And my administration will be pursuing a global effort of ransomware attacks by transnational criminals who often use global money laundering networks to carry them out.\par \par \uc2 My administration is also committed to safeguarding our critical infrastructure which -- much of which is privately owned and managed like Colonial. Private entities are making their own determination on cybersecurity so to jump start greater private sector investment in cybersecurity, we launched a new public-private initiative in April. It began with a 100-day sprint to improve cybersecurity in the electric sector.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll follow that with similar initiatives in natural gas pipelines, water and other sectors. In addition to companies stepping up, we need to invest to safeguard our critical infrastructure. That`s one of the many things my American jobs plan is designed to do. I also want to update the people on the progress that we`ve made in our recovery and the next steps that we`re going to be taking.\par \par \uc2 As we learned Friday, the economy created 266,000 jobs in April. In fact, all together, since the time we took office, we`ve created more than 1.5 million jobs in the country. It`s the most jobs created in the first 100 days of any president on record. More than three times the solid job creation President Carter saw in his first 100 days and more than six times that President Reagan saw.\par \par \uc2 In the three months before I got here, our country was creating roughly 60,000 jobs a month. In the three months since we`ve gotten here, we`ve been creating 500,000, on average 500,000 jobs per month. In the first quarter, our economy grew at 6.4 percent rate, outpacing the growth we`re seeing from our friends in the euro zone. Because of the American Rescue Plan, forecasters are projecting we`ll see the fastest economic growth in nearly 40 years in the months to come.\par \par \uc2 We`re moving in the right direction. So let`s be clear. Our economic plan is working. I never said and no serious analyst ever suggested that climbing out of the deep, deep hole our economy was in would be simple, easy, immediate or perfectly steady. Remember, 22 million Americans lost their jobs in this pandemic. So some months will exceed expectations, others will fall short. The question is, what is the trend line?\par \par \uc2 Are we headed in the right direction? Are we taking the right steps to keep it going? And the answer clearly is yes. I also want to offer a bit more detail on the month`s jobs numbers in particular. These monthly reports are a snapshot, as you all know. They take one week, a moment in time. Since this snapshot was taken around the week of April the 12th, since then COVID-19 counts are down by more than 40 percent since then.\par \par \uc2 And vaccination rates among working age Americans has roughly doubled since then. This survey was taken before adults -- before every adult was eligible to be vaccinated. So back then, 18 percent of working age adults were fully vaccinated. Today if it were taken, 34 percent are fully vaccinated. No wonder things in America feel better today than it did back when the survey was taken.\par \par \uc2 With that context, here are the steps we`re going to be taking to maintain our progress. First, today we are opening a portal for state and local governments to apply for the first funds from the American Rescue Plan. Because states and local governments have to balance their budgets, a lot of them had to lay off employees when the economy -- state employees and local employees when the economy slowed and tax revenues fell.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking about 1.3 million state and local employees out of work. But now the money we`re distributing -- going to be distributing now is going to make it possible for an awful lot of educators, first responders, sanitation workers to go back to work. Second, this is a year-long program.\par \par \uc2 Second, we`re delivering assistance to tens of thousands of restaurants and bars across the country. Today we`re sending out the first relief checks to 16,000 hard-hit restaurants with help for many more to follow. A lot more people are going to be employed.\par \par \uc2 Third, the employers bring back laid-off workers to help them do that. We`re going to remind them that there is some progress they can take advantage -- some programs they can take advantage of. For example, employers can hire back their laid-off workers part time without those workers having to give up all their unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 Here`s another example. In the American Rescue Plan, we expanded a tax incentive called Employee Retention Tax Credit. This can provide a recovering small or medium-sized business a direct credit for each worker they keep on or a job that they rehire.\par \par \uc2 Fourth, we`re going to provide real help for people struggling with the challenge of child care, which is making it hard for many parents who need to work, especially women, to get back to work. During this crisis, thousands of child care providers and centers were forced to close because they couldn`t make ends meet with fewer students and higher costs to keep them safe. As a result, parents lost the support system they depended on for child care.\par \par \uc2 And tens of thousands of child care workers lost their jobs. In fact, there are 150,000 fewer child care jobs now than there were pre-pandemic. The American Rescue Plan has already allocated funds to states to address the immediate challenge to our economy and of too few child care operations. This is the largest investment in child care since World War II.\par \par \uc2 Today my administration is releasing guidance to states to help get the funds they need out the door as quickly as possible to allow dramatic expansion and availability of child care in this country. With these funds, states can help hundreds of thousands of providers reopen and stay open and provide safe and healthy learning environments for more than five million children. These funds will also allow states to provide over 800,000 families with subsidies to pay for child care.\par \par \uc2 Simply put, this can help working parents get back to work. This one-time investment is a real answer to a real problem our economy is facing right now. Look, the fifth thing we`re going to do, we`re going to make it clear that anyone collecting unemployment who is offered a suitable job must take the job or lose their unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 There are a few COVID-19-related exceptions so that people aren`t forced to choose between their basic safety and a paycheck. But otherwise that`s the law. I know there`s been a lot of discussion since Friday`s report that people are being paid to stay home rather than go to work. Well, we don`t see much evidence of that. That is a major factor. We don`t see -- look, it`s easy to say, the line has been because of the generous unemployment benefits that it`s a major factor in labor shortages.\par \par \uc2 Americans want to work. Americans want to work. As my dad used to say, a job is about a lot more than a paycheck, it`s about your dignity, your place in the community, being able to look your kid in the eye and say everything is going to be OK. I think the people who claim Americans won`t work even if they find a good and fair opportunity, underestimate the American people.\par \par \uc2 So we`ll insist that the law is followed with respect to benefits or we`re not going to turn our backs on our fellow Americans. 22 million people lost their jobs in this pandemic through no fault of their own. They lost their jobs to a virus and to a government that bungled its response to the crisis and failed to protect them.\par \par \uc2 We still have eight million fewer jobs than we did when the pandemic started. And for many of those folks, unemployment benefits are a lifeline. No one should be allowed to game the system, and we`ll insist the law is followed, but let`s not take our eye off the ball. Families, families who are just trying to put food on the table, keep a roof over their head, they aren`t the problem. We need to stay focused on the real problems in front of us, beating this pandemic and creating jobs.\par \par \uc2 Again, the law is clear. If you`re receiving unemployment benefits and you`re offered a suitable job, you can`t refuse that job and just keep getting the unemployment benefits. We`re also working to overcome all the barriers that may be making it harder for people to get back to work. Child care, getting vaccinated, getting schools open. We`re making progress in all these things, and we need others to step up and play a role as well.\par \par \uc2 For example, we need employers to step up in a couple of ways. First, we need them to get help to their people and get them vaccinated. Two weeks ago, I announced that we`ll provide a tax credit to employers if they give workers paid time off to get a shot and recover from the effects -- the side effects if there are any. Our economy can`t achieve its full potential until we get more people vaccinated. Employers can help that.\par \par \uc2 We also need to recognize that people will come back to work if they`re paid a decent wage. The last Congress, before I became president, gave businesses over $1.4 trillion in COVID relief. Congress may have approved that money, but let`s be clear. The money came from the American people, and it went from the American people to American businesses, many of them big businesses, to help them get through this pandemic and keep their doors open.\par \par \uc2 I`m not questioning it. It was the right thing to do. But my expectation is that as our economy comes back, these companies will provide fair wages and safe work environments. And if they do, they`ll find plenty of workers and we`re all going to come out of this together better than before. So we need to stay focused on creating jobs and beating this pandemic today and building back better for tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 The American Rescue Plan is just that, a rescue plan. It`s to get us out of the crisis and back on track, but it`s not nearly enough. That`s why we need the American Jobs Plan, which is an eight-year investment, an eight- year investment strategy to make sure working people of this country get to share in the benefits of a rising economy and to put us in a position to win the competition with China and the rest of the world for the 21st century.\par \par \uc2 That`s the next stage. That`s what we`re doing right now. We`re working to get that passed. I want to thank you all and may God protect our troops. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Mr. President, if you can`t protect criminal infrastructure from a criminal act. How can you possibly protect it from an escape actor?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You can do both, and we will.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Do you think Russia is involved at all?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m going to have -- I`m going to be meeting with President Putin. And so far there is no evidence based on from our intelligence people that Russia is involved, although there is evidence that the actors` ransomware is in Russia. They have some responsibility to deal with this. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: (INAUDIBLE), Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC HOST: Remarks from President Biden in the East Room there. He was addressing the economic recovery from the pandemic but also talking about the news of the day. You heard a question from Peter Doocy, I think, at the end, asking if President Biden believes that Vladimir Putin is responsible and the president didn`t entirely engage on that question.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to see you. I`m Geoff Bennett in for my friend Katy Tur. As we come on the air, there is breaking news on what is potentially the most crippling cyberattack every against the U.S. In a short time ago, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  confirmed Russians are behind it. First, what it means for you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210511eh5a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word for May 7, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Glenn Kirschner, Vin Gupta \par \uc2 8,142 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: I leave you with the sincere hope that the whole Chinese rocket thing is something that we`re all laughing about by Monday. I hope you have a fantastic weekend. I`m planning on not thinking about it at all. Now it`s time for "The Last Word" with Ali Velshi who is in for Lawrence tonight. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: I mean, I don`t if I`m actually allowed to do this, but I would have just preferred you continue that conversation with \uc2 Matt Kaplan\uc2 . That was wild and it was interesting. But I thought it was interesting that you point out that we earthlings can do little about this right now, so I think you`re right. Let`s just hope for the best and we`ll talk about it on Monday.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Exactly, exactly.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Or let`s hope that we`ve forgotten about entirely. That`s what we`re hoping for. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Correct. That`s right. That`ll be success. Rachel, thanks for telling your viewers about Texas Representative Rafael Anchia. He`ll be joining us tonight. Have a great weekend. I`ll see you see you Monday.\par \par \uc2 All right. New tonight, Democrats and Republicans have reached something that`s rare in Washington these days, common ground. Three weeks after Derek Chauvin was found guilty in the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 , "The Wall Street Journal" reports tonight Democratic and Republican staff in Congress are drafting language for a possible police overhaul legislation that would limit the transfer of some military equipment to local departments, ban police use of choke holds except in life-threatening situations, and set federal standards for so-called no-knock warrants.\par \par \uc2 This news is a good sign of bipartisan momentum for something supported by 60% of Americans according to a recent "Washington Post" poll. Still unresolved are the issues of individual liability protection for police officers and whether the standard to federally charge officers should be changed.\par \par \uc2 What is clear is that that threshold to federally charged police officers was met for the four former police officers involved in the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 . A federal grand jury has indicted those four former Minneapolis police officers on civil rights charges today.\par \par \uc2 According to an indictment unsealed this morning, Derek Chauvin, J. Alexander Kueng, Thomas Lane and Tou Thao were acting under control -- under color of law, wilfully deprived \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  of his constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 All four former officers are charged with deliberate indifference to George Floyd`s serious medical needs. Former officers J. Alexander Kueng and Tou Thao are charged with violating George Floyd`s right to "be free from an unreasonable seizure." Derek Chauvin is also charged with violating George Floyd`s right to be free from the use of unreasonable force by a police officer. A\par \par \uc2 According to the Minneapolis "Star Tribune," federal prosecutors have long felt they`ve enough evidence to indict the officers on civil rights violations, but charges were delayed so they wouldn`t disrupt Derek Chauvin`s state trial. J. Alexander Kueng, Thomas Lane, and Tou Thao all made an initial court appearance this morning via Zoom.\par \par \uc2 They`re not currently in custody and they are scheduled to be tried together on state charges in August. Derek Chauvin, by the way, is currently in a Minneapolis prison -- a Minnesota prison awaiting sentencing after a jury found him guilty on all three state charges for his role in the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A second and entirely separate federal indictment was also unsealed today against Derek Chauvin in a different case charging him with violating the civil rights of a 14-year-old during a 2017 arrest involving use of force and a neck restraint.\par \par \uc2 The civil rights charges facing all four former officers are brand-new charges today, meaning all four could face another trial, this time in federal court. Prosecuting police officers for federal civil rights violations is incredibly rare in this country. Before the charges filed today, the "Star Tribune" reports only three cases in Minnesota have been charged in the last decade.\par \par \uc2 Now the question is whether the case involving the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  is an exception or is it the beginning of holding bad cops accountable for their actions.\par \par \uc2 The day after \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  was murdered, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  tweeted, "\uc2 George Floyd\uc2  deserved better and his family deserves justice. His life mattered. I`m grateful for the swift action in Minneapolis to fire the officers involved. They must be held responsible for their egregious actions. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  should conduct a thorough investigation."\par \par \uc2 Here`s White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  today on the new charges.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It`s a reminder, as was the verdict in the Chauvin case just a few weeks ago, that there`s still more that needs to be done. While that was a moment of justice, certainly, that it is just the beginning and it`s a reminder of the need to put police reform in place through our legislative process and put those reforms in place across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: There`s still more that needs to be done. The House passed the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Justice in Policing Act in March and a bipartisan group of senators are currently working on their version of a police reform bill. But the Biden administration is not waiting for Congress to take action.\par \par \uc2 After four years of a corrupt Justice Department under Trump`s attorneys general Jeff Sessions and Bill Barr, the Biden Justice Department is showing us what can be done to protect the rights, civil and otherwise, of all Americans.\par \par \uc2 Under the leadership of Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the Justice Department has launched investigations into both the Minneapolis and Louisville police departments to determine whether they engaged in patterns or practices of unlawful behavior.\par \par \uc2 Last week, separately, the department announced federal hate crime charges against three men accused of murdering Ahmaud Arbery in Georgia. Their state trial will begin in October. In March, amid the rise in hate crimes against Asian-Americans, the Justice Department announced an expedited review to determine how it can better track and prosecute crimes involving race.\par \par \uc2 And as Republicans across the country are doing everything they can to make it as difficult as possible to cast a ballot, the Department of Justice has questioned the GOP-driven private re-count of ballots in Arizona`s most populous county over fears that it could spark voter intimidation in future elections.\par \par \uc2 This is what happens when honest people are in charge. Bad people are held accountable. And those who need protection are offered protection. The Biden Justice Department has taken incredibly important steps to correct the Trump legacy, but there is still more to be done. There will always be more to be done. Here`s Michelle Obama this morning on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER FIRST LADY: We don`t want to be out there marching. I mean, all those Black Lives Matter kids, they`d probably not have to worry about this. They`re taking to the streets because they have to. They`re trying to have people understand that we`re real folks and the fear that many have of so many of us is irrational and it`s based on a history that is just -- it`s sad and it`s dark and it`s time for us to move beyond that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Leading off our discussion tonight are Gloria Browne-Marshall, a civil rights attorney and constitutional law professor at John Jay College of Criminal Justice, and Glenn Kirschner, a former federal prosecutor and an MSNBC legal analyst. Good evening to both of you.\par \par \uc2 Gloria, let me start with you. I want to read to you an article that was published in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," which reads as follows. Federal prosecutors have hesitated to bring the civil rights charge in high-profile police killings because it is harder to meet the law`s willfulness standard, which would mean proving that an officer intended to use excessive force in order to violate someone`s civil rights.\par \par \uc2 The split-second nature many police shootings, including those at the center of several of those cases, make them particularly difficult to prosecute." Your thoughts on the decision to charge the four former police officers in Minneapolis?\par \par \uc2 GLORIA BROWNE-MARSHALL, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Well, I think the operative word here is hesitant to bring these charges. Why don`t we let the courts decide? Let a grand jury determine whether or not there`s enough evidence to indict them even if the standard is high as willfulness under -- of course, you have to prove beyond a reasonable doubt.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s excellent that these federal charges have been brought and that the prosecutions of police officers in state court is so willfully negligent on the part of these prosecutors to ignore their duties that the federal government has been forced to step in. This federal legislation was put in post-slavery.\par \par \uc2 This is civil rights legislation post-slavery that we`re talking about because it was understood then that states were not going to protect the rights of African-Americans. This has been tweaked over years with different presidents coming in and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for one, is going to be a part of this national criminal justice legislation, but there is so much more that needs to be done.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Glenn, how much of this is a shot across the bow or as Cynthia Alksne said, the Department of Justice rising from the ashes and sort of letting states, police, and everybody else in this country know that they`re back on the scene?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, Ali, there are only three counts in this indictment and it only runs about three pages. It`s very short, but boy, does it speak volumes about the fact -- I think it`s fair to say there`s a new civil rights sheriff in town, and his name is \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When you look at the three charges, the first one is, I would say, predictable. It charges Derek Chauvin with using unreasonable force to end the life of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 . And we all enjoy a constitutional protection against the police using unreasonable force.\par \par \uc2 That is a seizure. It`s an unconstitutional seizure. But what I`m even more intrigued by is the second count on this indictment because the second0 count charges officers Thao and Keung with not applying unreasonable force, but with standing by and watching Derek Chauvin apply unreasonable force, ending the life of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 , and not intervening.\par \par \uc2 That is where I think it`s a real shot across the bow to all state and local police departments, not only if you are an officer and you`re applying deadly force, unreasonable force, unconstitutional force, but if you are other officers and you`re watching it happen and you`re not coming to the aid of that citizen, you could be federally charged with a civil rights violation. That is a really important message to send.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Gloria, regardless of what the bar is and proving the willfulness many people say is a high bar, is the law sufficient? In other words, if you are faced with these charges, if you`re one of these police, former police officers or anyone else in the country faced with a civil rights violation charge by the federal government, how serious is that?\par \par \uc2 BROWNW-MARSHALL: It is very serious. My concern is there are more state prosecutors and they are for state police officers outnumber federal. There are 18,000 police jurisdictions in this country. So, we have to do something in the prosecutor`s office. And I`ve said these many times. It doesn`t matter what the law is if a prosecutor fails to prosecute.\par \par \uc2 And so yes, this is symbolic of the federal government stepping in finally. Back in a case, Cruz v. United States in 1945, they said even then that this was rare that federal prosecutors step in. So, I`m concerned. Eric Garner, \uc2 Michael Brown\uc2 , the federal prosecutors did not step in.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it sends a message, but what about all those state prosecutors who are failing to uphold the rights of these people harmed and murdered by police officers? There has to be something done that holds them accountable, forces them to do their jobs.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And Glenn, how much does this force someone to do their job? Is there a level of embarrassment when a state doesn`t succeed in bringing charges that the federal government has moved in and found something to charge people with?\par \par \uc2 KISCHNER: You know, here`s what`s interesting about this choice by Merrick Garland`s Department of Justice to bring these charges. If we think back to Rodney King, that felt like a civil rights prosecution by the Feds to clean up a state system that couldn`t get the job done, that didn`t convict the officers who assaulted Rodney King.\par \par \uc2 This is not that, Ali. This was a successful prosecution. I would argue as a former career prosecutor. This was an expert state prosecution against Derek Chauvin. So, this was not the Feds galloping in to clean something up.\par \par \uc2 This was the Feds saying we are going to protect the civil rights of the citizens not only when the states fail to do it, but whenever it`s the right thing to do regardless of what the states are doing. This is really an important development for the Department of Justice and, I think, for the civil rights of the American people.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, everybody should get their copy of their constitution out and read it tonight because it sounds as a non-lawyer sounds like an important development to me that that is what the federal government is now saying. Thanks to both of you for being here tonight. Gloria Browne-Marshall and Glenn Kirschner. Appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, today`s shocking jobs report was a stark reminder we`re not out of the pandemic when it comes to the economy. What needs to be done, who`s still hurting the most, and will congressional Republicans continue to obstruct in the face of the needs of their constituents? We`re going to talk about all that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: More help is on the way if Republicans don`t stop it. That was President Biden`s message to Americans after today`s shocking jobs report, revealing that the American economy is still very much in a pandemic recovery. Just 266,000 new jobs were added in April falling far short of the 1 million jobs that some economists expected.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate ticked up slightly to 6.1 percent though I always say don`t pay too much attention to that number. Economists caution that the number is misleadingly low given how many people have dropped out of the labor force in the last year.\par \par \uc2 Eight million jobs that were lost when the pandemic forced businesses to close last spring still have not come back. Republicans have claimed we don`t need the Biden proposals because the economy is coming back all on its own. But it`s not. President Biden said today`s jobs report proves more help from the federal government is needed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Some critics said that we didn`t need the American Rescue Plan, that this economy would just heal itself. Today`s report just underscores, in my view, how vital the actions we`re taking are. Checks to people who are hurting, support for small businesses, for childcare and school reopening, support to help families put food on the table. Our efforts are starting to work, but the climb is steep, and we still have a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The Biden administration advocated that the American Jobs and the American Families Acts are intended to tackle some of the issues keeping people from rejoining the workforce. Vice President Harris echoed the need to give families more support, particularly women who left the workforce due to the pandemic, saying in a statement, "Roughly 5 million women still can`t get work or still can`t work due to child care issues. Our American Families Plan will lower the cost of child care, making it possible for more women to work. When we lift up women, we lift up families and communities and our economy is stronger."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, William Spriggs, professor of economics at Howard University. He`s the chief economist at the AFL-CIO and the former assistant secretary of policy at the Department of Labor. Bill, good to see you. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SPRIGGS, PROFESSOR OF ECONOMICS, HOWARD UNIVERSITY: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let`s talk about this. There is a fundamental disagreement in this country that seems to go down ideological lines at the moment about whether we have a need for government intervention to get us out of this problem or whether the economy will fix itself just fine once more people are vaccinated and cities start to opening up. What`s the truth of the matter as far as you`re concerned?\par \par \uc2 SPRIGGS: This report was kind of startling because there were a lot of job losses. There were a lot of permanent job losses and temporary job losses, which I think shocked many of us who have been watching the numbers. We had been anticipating continued growth. We hadn`t really been anticipating the setbacks that we saw in manufacturing and in some of the other areas where we thought that we would still see steady, but modest job growth.\par \par \uc2 And the ability to reconnect workers both on the side of employers who have lost contact with workers because the job networks that supplied them with workers have been so disrupted, but also from the workers who now are separated from those kind of jobs and those kinds of contacts. It`s very complex to get them all back. And it was disturbing to see how hard it is for those who are unemployed to find jobs.\par \par \uc2 We saw a big jump in people who are under five weeks unemployed, that`s 237,000 people. And a large chunk of those were new job entrants, not people getting unemployment checks. These are new job entrants who found it very difficult. Their unemployment jumped.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So this is interesting because there is a real concern out there. And I`ve spoken to small businesses, particularly restaurants, who say we`re having trouble hiring people. But that`s not actually the case for most of the population.\par \par \uc2 "The Washington Post" had a story here which reads that, "Two Republican- led states, Montana and South Carolina, have canceled federal unemployment benefits in the last week, blaming them for hampering the return to work. And the business lobby has echoed those concerns as well with the Chamber of Commerce using the disappointing jobs report to call for the end of the $300 a week unemployment supplement that the federal government is currently providing."\par \par \uc2 Bill, give me a fact check on this. People are not taking jobs because they`re getting a $300 a week temporary unemployment assistance from the government.\par \par \uc2 SPRIGGS: We studied this heavily in the summer because the $600 that have been added to the unemployment check, not the $300. The $600 that have been added last year caused this same argument that people wouldn`t return. But we saw throughout the summer that people, in fact, did return to jobs and re-employment.\par \par \uc2 It wasn`t because of the $600. And it`s kind of common sense from an economist perspective just from the job theory of job search. You have to think about what`s the expected value of continuing to look? We have 4 million Americans who`ve been unemployed over 26 weeks. That means there are a lot of Americans who know they have very little chance of finding a job.\par \par \uc2 So they expect the value of continuing to search is zero. So it doesn`t make sense and the data and the reports that we have. There are articles in the American Economic Review, a wonderful report from JPMorgan Chase Institute all documenting that the extra money, what it did do, was provide stability for the economy, continue consumption by those who were unemployed and kept us from having a much worse recession.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes. I mean, just to be clear, $300 a week is $7.50 an hour, is $15,600 a year if you work full time. If that`s what you`re robbing the system of, you`re not a very successful robber. I want to talk to you about women, the point that the vice president was making. Let`s look at this thing called labor force participation, which is a much better measure of what`s going on in the economy -- 67.6 percent men, 56.1 percent women. How do you interpret that?\par \par \uc2 SPRIGGS: Well, there have always been a gap there. Labor force participation did not recover for women for most of this century, and we know where the problems are. We don`t have paid family leave. We don`t have enough childcare facilities in the United States. What we do have is too expensive, and we haven`t done what other industrialized countries have done to increase women`s labor force participation.\par \par \uc2 That was a problem before this happened, and it`s been exacerbated by this situation. That`s why what`s in that American Family Plan that the president has proposed is so vital because we have to get women`s labor force participation rate back up.\par \par \uc2 We saw the birthrate in the U.S. has collapsed. The only way we`re going to continue to grow is we have to have bigger labor force participation.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And you studied other countries. The United States is uniquely bad at this.\par \par \uc2 SPRIGGS: We are uniquely bad at this. Japan, which has (inaudible) the same aging as Europe, knew they had to get women`s labor force participation rate up. Many people thought it was just cultural or something about the Japanese culture that, you know, you couldn`t get women`s labor force participation up, but it`s exceeded the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, they started off behind us and they`ve gotten ahead of us. And it was through doing the things that the president is proposing in terms of paid leave, help with child care, the child tax credit that he wants to extend beyond this year, is vital for getting women into the labor force, and is getting child care workers paid so that we can keep a supply, a steady supply of well-paid child care workers. It`s vital to women`s jobs, women`s wages and getting women in the labor force.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Bill, thank you for your analysis tonight. We appreciate it. Williams Spriggs is a professor of economics at Howard University and the chief economist at the AFL-CIO. We appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, once again, in a time of crisis, congressional Republicans are voting against helping people then going home and taking credit for Democratic-passed relief in their districts. But if they have struck help going forward, will swing state voters hold them accountable. I`ll ask Pennsylvania Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman about that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have to point out, though, that some of the very same folks in Congress who opposed the Recovery Act and claimed that it hasn`t worked have been all too happy to claim credit for recovery act projects and the jobs those projects have produced.\par \par \uc2 They come to the ribbon cuttings and -- they find a way to have their cake and vote against it too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That was former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  11 years ago. 11 years later, Republicans are still having their cake and voting against it all over again. Not one single Republican voted for President Biden`s $1.9 trillion American Rescue Plan. That hasn`t stop some in the GOP from promoting parts of the plan that help their constituents.\par \par \uc2 Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2  of New York promoted, quote, "bringing federal funding to the district and back into the pockets of taxpayers. She listed the $3.7 million in health care grants from President Biden`s bill under her personal list of achievements on her, quote, "first 100 days report card".\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press writes, quote, "Republicans from New York and Indiana, to Texas and Washington State have promoted elements of the legislation they fought to defeat."\par \par \uc2 It`s even happening in House leadership. House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy touted the rescue plan`s restaurant revitalization fund encouraging constituents to apply for the program.\par \par \uc2 In response, Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said, quote, he called the American Rescue Plan "socialist", claimed it would turn the U.S. into Venezuela, and convinced every member of his caucus to vote against it. No one should be surprised that McCarthy, a man with such strong principles, voted no and took the dough with the American Rescue Plan. The only question is, which parts of the American jobs and families plans will he pick to brag about," end quote.\par \par \uc2 Time will tell.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Lieutenant Governor of Pennsylvania, John Fetterman. He`s running for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in Pennsylvania. Lieutenant Governor, this is -- it would be funny if it weren`t so serious. They literally -- not a single Republican voted in favor of this plan and they are literally taking credit for this.\par \par \uc2 If they were in a college, they would be caught out for academic dishonesty, but I guess there`s no such thing in Congress.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. JOHN FETTERMAN (D-PA): Yes, I mean --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Hang on a second. I thought I wasn`t able to -- there we go. We got you, we got you. Go ahead, sorry, we didn`t hear the beginning of that, Lieutenant Governor.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Sure. I mean when has the Republican Caucus ever let telling the truth get in the way of reality? I mean it`s like, this is the group that`s claimed that there was massive voter fraud, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It was rigged for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . But when they`re on the same ballot, well, the voters love me. I mean there wasn`t any fraud in my race.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they`re estrangement from the truth continues but this is hardly surprising.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So what does success look like on this front? Because if the American Rescue Plan and the American Jobs Plan and the American Family Plan, which are just basically large government interventions into the economy that some of us think is the right thing to do, if they work fundamentally, does \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , do Democrats, do people like you ultimately reap the benefits for it? Or do people who put this on their resume or their first 100 days report card benefit from the fact that they fooled people into thinking that they were responsible for it?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: You know, we`re all going to benefit from things like the American Rescue Plan. I mean that`s obvious. Communities are no stronger than the weakest family, and cities are no stronger than the weakest community within a city. And states are no stronger than the weakest cities in their state.\par \par \uc2 And it just comes down to providing the resources America needs to get through and get past this pandemic. And you know, a question was put to me earlier, what`s the midterms going to look like? It`s like well, if it`s running on policies that the kind that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has already passed and put forward, then I should be so lucky. That`s going to be the climate that we`re going to find ourselves in the midterm.\par \par \uc2 Yet everyone knows that this is the kind of intervention that`s needed. And when the Republicans will campaign and tout the success that they actively voted against and tried to stop, it tells you you`re heading in the right direction and doing the right things.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, there`s the concept of having discussions on policy in the midterms. There`s also the issue of the number of Republicans who have not accepted the outcome of this election and continue to fan this delusion of Donald Trump`s and the stuff that`s going on in a number of state houses and the recount going on in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 There`s a public radio station in Harrisburg, WITF, that has a story that they are telling listeners. I want to just read to our viewers what it says.\par \par \uc2 "As part of the WITF`s commitment to factual reporting, we will use language in our reporting to show how elected officials` actions are connected to the election fraud lie and the insurrection. We are not taking this approach lightly and will apply it to lawmakers who took at least one of these actions: signed on to a Texas lawsuit aimed at invalidating Pennsylvania`s election, signed on to a state House or a state Senate letter urging congressional representatives to object to or delay certification, and voted against certification."\par \par \uc2 Do you think that kind of thing works to bust up the big lie?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Sure. It`s something that we have to push against. I mean the bottom line is this. Everyone knows there was no voter fraud. Everyone knows that in Pennsylvania we`ve had a total of four or five documented cases of voter fraud out of nearly seven million ballots cast.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  owned the dead mother vote in Pennsylvania, and he got 100 percent of it. But the truth of the matter is they`re going to keep telling these kinds of lies and they`re going to keep trying to pass legislation that decreases the access to the poles in Republican-controlled states.\par \par \uc2 They can`t do that in Pennsylvania, so all they can do is amplify the propaganda of the big lie, as I mentioned earlier, claiming that we rigged it for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but we kept our Republican members of our congressional delegation around because they`re just such swell guys.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that`s how they`re going to continue to play it because that`s the only way they can play it at this point.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Does it strike you as odd because in places like rural Pennsylvania, there could in some quarters be an argument for old-fashioned ideological conservatism in some quarters.\par \par \uc2 Today`s Republicans are not making that argument at all. The few that still make it are retiring from politics. But does that strike you as odd that that`s the best argument Republicans have right now?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I mean no because it`s all they`ve got. I mean, like, to your point, I mean they`ve given up on traditional -- the whole William Buckley kind of traditional George Will conservatism. It`s just how many crazy things can we say, how many lies can we tell, and we`ve continued to get away with it. And you look at the numbers of the number of Republicans that believe there was election fraud. I don`t think they actually believe that there`s election fraud. I just think it`s a protest belief.\par \par \uc2 But this idea that if you can`t traffic in reality, you traffic in fantasy. And they have an entire media ecosystem where you can live and not be challenged on any of that and then -- until they`re actually sued do they actually acknowledge that they, in fact, you know, take me seriously. That wasn`t -- how could anyone take me seriously? And they have to end up apologizing to Dominion and these other entities that they`ve smeared.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I`m going to be talking to Texas state Representative Rafael Anchia next. You`ve claimed the reward, right from -- the lieutenant governor of Texas offered money if anybody found voter fraud and you did find four or five cases in Pennsylvania. I think that you didn`t get your money. I`d have heard if you did, right?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: No. We`re still waiting for my handsome reward for discovering voter fraud. But I guess the voter fraud of course that they really wanted was the Democratic voter fraud for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  which we didn`t have in Pennsylvania. But yes, still waiting on my handsome reward. If you can get them to pay up, I mean that would be outstanding. We would thank you.\par \par \uc2 But regardless, I don`t anticipate them ever playing it straight up in the future because the facts are never on their side, from the pandemic to the relief packages that we passed to the actual election integrity.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Lieutenant governor, good to see you. Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman of Pennsylvania. Thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, everybody`s talking about our next guest after he delivered a history lesson in racism and voting to one of the Republican authors of the Texas voter suppression bill in the Texas house today. You`ve probably seen the clip on Rachel`s show. That Texas representative Rafael Anchia joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Texas Republicans are determined to force through voter suppression bills despite outcry from voting rights advocates, some corporations and frankly being utterly embarrassed by Democrats in debate.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Democratic state representative Jessica Gonzalez quoting the Republican secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA GONZALEZ (D-TX): The secretary of state said that the 2020 elections was free, fair, safe and secure. Do you now agree with that -- what the secretary of state said?\par \par \uc2 BRISCO CAIN (R), TEXAS STATE REPRESENTATIVE: I think that -- that that`s their opinion, sure.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Are you saying that you disagree with the secretary of state`s office is what I`m asking you.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: I mean, I think they`re probably right in the adding up of it all from what they saw -- free, fair, and secure election.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: What are we trying to fix here that is not broken?\par \par \uc2 CAIN: We don`t need to wait for bad things to happen in order to try and protect and secure these elections.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: They don`t need to wait for bad things to happen. The Republican who said that, by the way, is Representative \uc2 Briscoe Cain\uc2 . He wrote the House version of the voter suppression bill that has now passed both chambers of the Texas state legislature.\par \par \uc2 And when he wrote the bill, he chose to use a phrase from the state constitution, a phrase that is deeply steeped in racism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL ANCHIA (D-TX), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: You talked about preserving the purity of the ballot box, is that correct?\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Yes. That`s a quotation from the Texas Constitution, Article 6, Section 4.\par \par \uc2 ANCHIA: Right. And are you aware of the history behind that provision of the constitution?\par \par \uc2 CAIN: I`m not.\par \par \uc2 ANCHIA: That provision was drafted specifically to disenfranchise black people, black voters, in fact, following the Civil War. Did you know that?\par \par \uc2 CAIN: No. I`m sorry to hear that.\par \par \uc2 ANCHIA: Did you realize that that purity of the ballot box language in the Texas Constitution gave rise to all-white primaries?\par \par \uc2 CAIN: No, I didn`t.\par \par \uc2 ANCHIA: And did you know that this purity at the ballot box justification was also used during the Jim Crow era to prevent black people from voting?\par \par \uc2 CAIN: No. No, those are troubling things. I didn`t know that --\par \par \uc2 ANCHIA: Did you know that in states across the country that penal disenfranchisement schemes were put in place, including in Texas, as far back at 1845 to effectively lock African-American people out of the political process? Are you aware of this history?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Joining us now is Texas state representative Rafael Anchia. He is a Democrat representing the western corridor of Dallas. He`s the chairman of the Mexican-American Legislative Caucus, which is the largest and oldest Latino legislative caucus in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Representative, thank you for being with us. Did anything come of that remarkable debate? It`s very rare for us to see actual political debates in a legislature. So when you watch something like that and you think you presented your counterpart on the Republican side with a lot of interesting information that should have fed his decision as far as writing this bill goes. What happened after that?\par \par \uc2 ANCHIA: Well, thanks for having me, Ali, and shout out to my mom who I know is always watching and to all moms on this Mother`s Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 I got to tell you, you know -- the last, I don`t know, 48 hours have been really tough. We stayed up until about 3:00 a.m. last night, came back this morning and fought this bill. Regrettably, the bill passed.\par \par \uc2 Democrats were able to chip away at the edges of this thing, but you know, when a bill is sort of based on the big lie -- I was glad to see the lieutenant governor of Pennsylvania talking about that -- you know, it`s irredeemable. The bill sought to have partisan -- these election observers looking over people`s shoulders, intimidating voters.\par \par \uc2 We were able to chip away at some of those things so election judges, independent election judges won`t have somebody looking over my mom`s shoulder when she`s trying to vote. And so we thought that was an important provision to take out of the bill.\par \par \uc2 The history lesson is not only something that, you know, hearkens back to Jim Crow or even that purity of the ballot box language. I mean we`re seeing it real time here in Texas.\par \par \uc2 During the last decade, we had multiple findings of intentional discrimination against the Texas legislature in voting rights matters, not 50 years ago, not in black and white, but real time and digital during the last decade.\par \par \uc2 So it`s something that continues to happen in this state, already the toughest state in which to cast a vote in the union. And Republicans are continuing to pile on.\par \par \uc2 We scored some points last night, but this is still a terrible bill that passed today. That`s going to hurt voting rights in the state.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Representative Cain when confronted with the idea that this was a free and fair election in Texas, we know that your lieutenant governor has offered a bounty for people who have found examples of voter fraud. Doesn`t seem to want to pay that bounty out for examples of Republican voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 But when Representative Cain was confronted with that information and he said well, we don`t want to have to wait for bad things to happen, Texas has enjoyed freer voting rights in the past without bad things happening.\par \par \uc2 What was the precipitating event in Texas or in Pennsylvania or in Georgia or in Iowa or in Arizona that has caused everybody to make this priority number one?\par \par \uc2 ANCHIA: Well, it`s the loss of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the last election. The ex- president has continued to talk about stopping the steal and he`s perpetuated the big lie. His enablers have done it over and over again, and that big lie is that people of color stole this election from him in cities like Dallas, and Houston, and across the nation. The reality is, as you heard Representative Gonzalez, my colleague in the legislature say, you know, the secretary of state, that`s Governor Abbott`s chief elections officer, she said that the election was safe, secure, and successful. There`s no evidence of voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 But that has -- you know, that`s not deterred Republicans in this state either in the last decade or this decade as they continue to trample on voting rights. Might I say not of Democrats, not of Republicans, but of all Texans, Texans of all stripes.\par \par \uc2 I mean in my closing speech today, I said help me preserve the votes of Texas Republicans. Help me preserve the votes of Texas Democrats and everybody else in between because this should not be a partisan issue.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANCHIA: You know, my parents are immigrants to this country, and we think this is the greatest country in the world. And we`re very proud that we still have this precious right to vote. Lot of places around the world people don`t have it. But we sure as hell are going to fight all night like we did last night. We`re going to fight and we`re going to make some good trouble on bills that seek to curtail these rights.\par \par \uc2 And we have to live up for the best ideals of this country we`re not doing it here in Texas.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Representative, good to see you. Thank you for being with us. And Happy Mother`s Day to your mother since I know she is watching. Representative Rafael Anchia from the Texas legislature.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back with some good news on a Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Time for some good news.\par \par \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and German company BioNTech have become the first to apply to the \uc2 FDA\uc2  for full approval of their COVID vaccine for use in people 16 years old -- and older. The vaccine`s currently being administered under an emergency use authorization. Full approval would allow \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  to market the vaccine directly to customers.\par \par \uc2 Moderna plans to apply for full approval for its vaccine later this month. More good news by the way, coronavirus infections in the United States are now at their lowest level in seven month.\par \par \uc2 The vaccines are turning the tide in America`s battle with the virus. Deaths and serious illnesses have dropped significantly. And now cases are falling too, an important piece of protection for the future if we can keep it up.\par \par \uc2 Over half of all American adults have now received at least one shot of a vaccine. 41 percent of adults are fully vaccinated. The U.S. averaged about 48,000 new cases per day over the past week. So it is not gone. That`s a 13 percent improvement however over the week before. And it`s the first time since October that average daily cases have dipped below 50,000.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is a guy who needs no introduction, Dr. Vin Gupta who may have been new to some of you a year and a half ago but unfortunately has become very familiar to you. He`s a pulmonologist, a global health policy expert, an affiliate assistant professor at the University of Washington Medical Center and an MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Vin, it is -- I mean, for a lot of last year and change we have not been able to talk about good news. There is something good happening here. I didn`t know what the full approval of these drugs would mean. It basically allows them to market the drug to people. What difference will that make in the uptake of vaccinations in this country?\par \par \uc2 DR. VIN GUPTA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, good evening, Ali. I think this is huge and cannot be underestimated because what I hear from the younger people especially is, well hey, Doc, isn`t this vaccine still experimental? Are we sure we know the full spate of side effects, the short and long term? This alleviates that, Ali because this takes this notion of emergency use out the window and that scared a lot of people. It caused a lot of people to say let me wait and see.\par \par \uc2 So I do think that this is going to be a positive signal to everybody across the country and we can message more confidently. Moderna`s following suit in the next month. So this is definitely a boon to messaging especially to younger people, Ali.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It also does another thing and that is for companies who is are thinking about, you know, mandating that you have to be vaccinated. It sort of gives them a little bit of a leg up.\par \par \uc2 But what I didn`t know about, that I read in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is that it could allow the military to mandate vaccination and that`s been a bit of an issue because the uptake (ph) in the military has not been as high as people were hoping.\par \par \uc2 DR. GUPTA: Well, I can say as a reservist that that`s absolutely probably going to happen. I mean I`m not making the decision but from what I have heard internally they`re waiting for that. Colleges and universities are waiting for that.\par \par \uc2 So this is the signal here that you`re going to start seeing uptake across the different sectors of society, Ali. Definitely universities, probably the military from what I`m hearing and likely parts of the entertainment industry, Restaurants, hotel chains.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to start see more of a movement there and that`s going to be really important as well because really the critical difficulty for small and medium businesses, Ali, is the capacity issue.\par \par \uc2 But if you can create a safe bubble, you can get to 100 percent capacity and that`s the key piece. You`re seeing Major League Baseball actually leading on this issue.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You got a discount at some games if you can either prove vaccination or get vaccinated on site.\par \par \uc2 The White House wants 70 percent of Americans vaccinated. Polling recently indicates that probably that many Americans are willing to get this vaccine. What should we be thinking about in terms of herd immunity? Does the matter? Is there a number? How do you think about this?\par \par \uc2 DR. GUPTA: I think the metric that matters here, Ali, across zip codes is hospitalizations. What is the stress in local health systems? Here in (INAUDIBLE) county we have 80 plus percent of our ICU bed capability available. That`s reassuring, as Governor Inslee decided to keep us in phase three here. That is a key piece here because people are not going to test as much, Ali, once they get fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Their behaviors are going to change so hospitalization rates are going to be a key metric, key surrogate (ph) of have we or have we not reached herd immunity. So that`s going to be number one.\par \par \uc2 And then two, I think we need to be mindful here if we do not reach 70 percent across certain regions of the country what happens come fall or winter? Ali, as you know, coronavirus likes cold, dry air, not warm humid air.\par \par \uc2 So I worry we`re entering a phase here where people might feel like we`re actually past the worst of it. But come fall or winter I do expect there will be areas of the country that are going to see potentially surges in their hospital systems because they didn`t reach their optimal uptake of the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Vin, thanks for joining us tonight. Thanks for having joined us for so much of this time since the beginning of this pandemic. Vin Gupta is a pulmonologist and global health policy expert.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 You can catch me tomorrow morning at 8:00 a.m. Eastern on my show "VELSHI". We`ve got a lot lined up for you. I`ll be joined by Senator and former vice presidential nominee Tim Kaine, Congresswoman and former impeachment manager \uc2 Stacey Plaskett\uc2 , Congressman \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2 , and Arizona secretary of state \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That`s it for me.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH BRIAN WILLIAMS" begins right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020210508eh5700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 7, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Mark Murray, Cal Perry, Pete  Williams, Vanessa Hauc, Mike Memoli \par \uc2 8,117 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Friday, President Biden reacts to a disappointing April jobs report which showed a surprising slowdown in hiring what`s behind it. What could it mean for the COVID recovery and the Biden agenda, the Commerce Secretary Gina Raimondo will join us this hour.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Justice Department sends a warning to Arizona Republicans about their so called election audit, as Trump inspired chaos spreads within the GOP from its state conventions, of course to the national leadership.\par \par \uc2 And a federal grand jury has now indicted Derek Chauvin and three other former Minneapolis police officers charging them with violating George Floyd`s civil rights during the arrest that caused his death. They also charged Chauvin for a separate incident. Details on that as well.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I am Chuck Todd. We begin today with a number of developments inside the Republican Party as it deals with the consequences of its full embrace of Trump and Trumpism and surrenders to his lie that the election was rigged against him.\par \par \uc2 In the State of Arizona both the state`s democratic Secretary of State Katie Hobbs, and officials at the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  have told Republicans that they have serious concerns about how it`s so called audit of the Maricopa County presidential results is being conducted.\par \par \uc2 This comes as more reports have been surfacing about just how disorderly bizarre, biased, conspiracy theory laden, this audit process has become. And that`s just in the State of Arizona. In Virginia, a state party has had its share of problems of this last decade, Republicans are about to hold one of the strangest and most convoluted conventions and I`ll put that in quotes if I could here, you`ll ever see they`re going to choose their nominee for governor in a Byzantine process.\par \par \uc2 A process that appears to have spun out of control of the former president ongoing lies about voting. One of the Republican candidates in that race, one of the president to declare martial law on the presidential election. And some of this conventions chaos may be the result of Republicans so worried that a certain candidate might get the nomination that they`ve essentially created a bizarre process to avoid that and then calling into question the entire process itself.\par \par \uc2 Then you have the State of Texas where Republican state lawmakers have advanced new voting restrictions. The day after Florida`s Republican governor Ron DeSantis, signed into law, new restrictions there. There was really no actual impetus for this other than Donald Trump`s whining again, both measures appear to be motivated by simply the embrace of the lie. So, all of this is happening on the state and local level.\par \par \uc2 While on the national level, the Republicans in Congress are in the process of ousting Liz Cheney from leadership due to her criticisms of the former president. And as Republican show Cheney the door, they`ve been showing Trump their loyalty.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM (R-SC): I would just say to my Republican colleagues, can we move forward without President Trump? The answer is no. I`ve always liked Liz Cheney, but she`s made a determination that the Republican Party can`t grow with President Trump. I`ve determined we can`t grow without him.\par \par \uc2 HALEY BYRD WILT, ASSOCIATE EDITOR, THE DISPATCH: My vision is to run with support from the President and his coalition of voters which was the highest number of votes ever won by a Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): We are a pro America Populist Party rooted in conservative principle with Donald Trump as our leader. That -- he has brought so many new people to this party. And that is a darn good thing.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN BARRASSO (R-WY): In terms of President Trump. He brings incredible energy to our voters to our party, and to the fight that we`re in against this Biden administration. And I welcome all of that energy and I see that in Wyoming every day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, there is only one leader of the Republican Party, you heard them say it. And it`s a party that also is embracing their form of canceled culture because if you`re not with Donald Trump, they`re canceling you.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to talk to Arizona`s top elections officials about the Republican-led audit in her state in just a moment. But let`s start with Leigh Ann Caldwell. She is at The Villages in Florida. And a lot of you may know what The Villages are. Its following a couple of diehard Trump loyalists revealing -- reveling in the House Republican caucuses effort to oust Cheney. We also have Mark Murray, who is keeping tabs on tomorrow`s strange Republican convention that will pick a nominee for governor in the State of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 But Leigh Ann, we`ve got Marjorie Taylor Greene and Matt Gaetz who is under a cloud of controversy with a Justice Department investigation involving possible sex with another with an under -- with an underage woman. So Leigh Ann, what are you covering today at The Villages?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HOUSE REPORTER: Chuck, I am covering the first stop on Marjorie Taylor Greene and Matt Gaetz`s America first tour. What this essentially is it`s a fundraising tour for their joint political committees for them to raise money. Marjorie Taylor Greene has proven that she`s an excellent fundraiser, although she has spent a lot of money to actually raise those funds.\par \par \uc2 But what they`re also going to do is they`re going to highlight the Trump wing of the party, they are expected to of course show their deep loyalty to the former president. And they`re expected to call out the members of the party who they think are not sufficiently loyal enough. Representative Gaetz wrote an op-ed today in a pro Trump publications calling out Representative Cheney saying that she is a relic of the past.\par \par \uc2 That she is no longer part of the current day Republican Party, and that she should not be a member of leadership and that the people of -- that she shouldn`t even be a member of Congress that the people of Wyoming do not support her. So, the divide within the Republican Party is on a clear display today because while you have Matt Gaetz and Marjorie Taylor Greene, well, they are not centers of power and Washington, they still do have a following.\par \par \uc2 And they are also close with the former President still. And meanwhile, you have what`s happening in Washington with Liz Cheney most likely losing her leadership position, because she is talking about what the former president did. Meanwhile, Republicans are, you know, talking to Republicans who are saying that she keeps talking about the past, that we need to move forward and talk about the future.\par \par \uc2 Well, I spoke with a couple of Republican sources today, or yesterday, excuse me, and I said, Well, the former President continues to talk about the past. He`s not talking about the future of the party. They didn`t really have an answer to that, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, look, and I guess the other thing is, this is now -- I mean, the party apparently only stands for one thing. And the reason I ask it this way is Elise Stefanik, the single most important piece of legislation, according to Senate Republicans that`s been passed in a generation was their tax cut. Elise Stefanik voted against that.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got -- you`re covering an event that the House Republican leadership is certainly not disavowing with Matt Gaetz and Marjorie Taylor Greene two people that frankly, should be politically radioactive in sort of -- in a - - in a decent society.\par \par \uc2 What is happening here? The only common denominator is loyalty to Trump versus disloyalty to Trump.\par \par \uc2 CALDWEL: Yes. So my sources in Washington say these are not apples to apples comparison, Marjorie Taylor Greene and Matt Gaetz are not members of leadership like Representative Cheney is. But it doesn`t really matter to the voters and to the public who see the message that Marjorie Taylor Greene and Matt Gaetz are giving. And that is the message that the leadership and the Republican Party are embracing, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, we`re all looking at it. And it is just something you can`t believe it`s playing out this way. But when you have an information ecosystem that is broken and polluted as the one that is on the right, I guess this is where we`ve gotten. Let me turn to Mark Murray. And so, let`s sort of set lay the predicate here, the Virginia Republicans decided they had a problem that they were -- they had one candidate that was extremely supportive of Donald Trump, that was going to win a primary.\par \par \uc2 With that as their problem, they decided, well, we can`t have that. So we need to design a convention. And then it seemed to get really out of hand. I`ll let you pick it up from there.\par \par \uc2 MARK MURRAY, NBC NEWS POLITICAL EDITOR: Yes, Chuck. You and I have covered a lot of nominating contests in our years covering politics. But I`ve never seen a nominating contest like what we`re going to end up seeing on Saturday in Virginia on the Republican side. And so, it is an unassembled convention due to the pandemic. And so you`re going to see delegates drive to about 40 locations across the State of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 And basically hand in their ballots on who they think should end up being their nominees for governor, lieutenant governor and attorney general. And we`re going to see maybe most about 50,000 delegates they applied and we`re just selected by local Republican units on who can be a delegate, that 50,000 number, it`s far less than the hundreds of thousand that we wouldn`t have seen participated in normal primary like we`ll see on the Democratic side on June 8th.\par \par \uc2 And certainly the 1.9 million Virginians who voted for Trump in 2020. And then here`s where it gets really complicated, Chuck. The ballot that they`re turning in is rank choice. There are seven Republicans who are running, so delegates get to pick one through seven. And then, it is determined by weighting by locality. And so there`s a mathematical formula on the county that you live in. It`s Republican performance and how big the population is there.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, they`re not going to start counting by hand until Sunday, then the process could actually last if earliest through Tuesday, Chuck, or as late as Thursday, and you add it all up. And this certainly sounds a whole lot like the Democratic Iowa caucuses of 2020 with a pandemic and drive by voting mixed in. And so things could go smoothly on Saturday and into next week. But things could also turn incredibly chaotic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. And I just want to emphasize something. So obviously, this is about fear of the Fairfax County moderate Republican. So if you`re a Republican from Fairfax County, your vote does not count as much is if you`re a Republican from a county in southwest Virginia. Correct?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: That`s right, Chuck. That`s right. And so there`s -- it`s a combination, that Virginia Republican Party has it on its spreadsheet on what counties count for what. And so, right. If you end up having a thousand people in Fairfax County versus a thousand people in the Roanoke, and how they kind of match out, a lot of it`s determined by just mathematical formula. It is not one person, one vote.\par \par \uc2 And so there`s going to be a lot of interesting observations and potential complaints by a Republican Party that has been very suspicious about voting procedures.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you try to design a system to avoid a result, you`re usually going to create a terrible system. Congratulations for junior Republicans. Good luck with this this weekend. Leigh Ann Caldwell, Mark Murray, thank you both for getting us started. Joining me now with the yet another bizarre event that we`re covering inspired by a state Republican Party, it`s the Arizona Secretary of State Katie Hobbs.\par \par \uc2 And this comes to this sort of -- I don`t know what to call it. A sideshow. My apologies for calling it a sideshow. I don`t mean to insult the State of Arizona. But that`s what this looks like from the outside here. This audit, you seem to be concerned about it, it`s being paid for in some part by taxpayer money, some with private funds. What is happening with it and why is it still going on?\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS, SECRETARY OF STATE OF ARIZONA: Well, I think you`re spot on for calling it a sideshow. And it certainly is not reflective of the electorate in Arizona. This is a small group of fringe Republicans who are driving this and, you know, essentially trying to re litigate the 2020 election which is over and done. And anyone looking at what`s happening in this process, it is fraught with problems in terms of security, chain of custody of the ballots and the equipment problems.\par \par \uc2 It is fraught with problems. Quite Ironically, the same kind of problems that the conspiracy theorists said, without any basis, in fact, happened during the actual election, which we know is not true. And that`s what we`re seeing carried out in this process right now. There`s no way that what`s going on right now can result in any kind of reliable or valid outcome in terms of a deal audit.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So who`s in charge of keeping these ballots secure? I mean, there is there is a law that indicates they need to be kept secure. Is that your office? Are you able to supervise this in any way to make sure they`re not, you know, messing up ballots, things like that? I mean, is there sort of a third party supervisor?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: No, there`s not. And, you know, honestly, what has been happening here is it -- they`re making it up as they go along. The Senate, issued the subpoena to do this audit and recount without really understanding what they were getting into and ignoring the concerns that we brought up to them. We outlined a number of security measures that are in statute and procedure that should be taken into consideration that was ignored.\par \par \uc2 We offered up election experts to help craft procedures that was ignored. And so the subpoena basically turns the capacity of these ballots and equipment over to the State Senate and they`re responsible for it.\par \par \uc2 They seem to have just brushed off the concerns from the Department of Justice in terms of potential violation of ballot preservation laws for federal elections. So it just seems they`re not taking any of these issues seriously. But we know from the comments and concerns we`re getting in our office that voters across the State of Arizona are very concerned about what`s going on here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So is there any other legal recourse? You obviously -- you wrote a letter to the Justice Department they`ve they -- it looks like this state senate isn`t. Republicans aren`t responding to it. Do you have other legal avenues to go -- to go through that? Do you think this should be stopped?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Well, so there was a lawsuit and part of the lawsuit was to try to stop this audit, it was brought by the Arizona Democratic Party and my office intervened. A settlement was reached in that lawsuit earlier this week. And one of the terms of the settlement is that they -- if we brought concerns to their attention they had 48 hours to respond to those. So we -- as soon as that lawsuit settlement was filed, we issued a six-page letter outlining 13 very broad concerns about security and processes and procedures.\par \par \uc2 And from what we`ve seen so far, it also doesn`t look like they`re taking that seriously. And so this very well could end up back in court.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can you remind viewers how many -- what kind of audits and recounts we`ve had already in Arizona? How many of them have there been?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: So, before each election, every county does a logic and accuracy test, which determines that the equipment is working the way that it`s supposed to. And there`s a lot of things that go into that too. I`m just brushing over it. But the LMA test assures the equipment`s working properly. Post-election, there`s another LMA test, as well as a hand count of a statistically significant number of randomly selected precincts. And all of those came out clean.\par \par \uc2 And then Maricopa County decided because of this subpoena battle, to do an additional logic and accuracy test, which also came out clean. And then they had two independent U.S. election assistance commission certified auditing firms come in and do two separate independent forensic audits of the equipment. That obviously hasn`t satisfied the concerns and so this is where we are today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what does this mean for 2022? And what of this experience has you planning, you know, alternative ways of conducting the vote in 2022?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Well, you know, what we`ve seen across the country since the 2020 election is legislatures with majorities who are not satisfied with the election outcome, trying to change the laws to change how people vote. That is happening in Arizona. In fact, I think we led the charge in terms of number of voter restrictive measures that were introduced into as bills. And so that potentially has the potential to change how voting takes place in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 But in general, what this is doing is just continuing to undermine people`s confidence in the integrity of our elections. And that is dangerous for our democracy. And that I think really is the end game here that they -- that they want to accomplish.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, no, I don`t think that`s the flaw. I think you`re right. I think that`s supposed to be the logical outcome that they want to have and put power of the elections to the state legislatures. Anyway, Katie Hobbs, Secretary of State in Arizona, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, amid new progress in fighting the coronavirus \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is now applied for full FDA authorization of its vaccine. Will it happen? The assumption is yes. How about when will it happen? And will it help convince some hesitant people to get vaccinated? We`re live in one of the least vaccinated places in the country.\par \par \uc2 And later, the Biden administration reacts to those disappointing April jobs numbers. I`ll have the Commerce Secretary Gina Raimondo here to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We learned this morning that \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is applying for full approval from the FDA for its coronavirus vaccine, a move that would allow the company to market it and possibly lead businesses to officially mandate it. The approval would make Pfizer`s vaccine, the first in the US to gain full FDA approval and it`s a process that still could take months. Overall the U.S. has vaccinated 57 percent of adults with at least one shot inching towards President Biden`s goal of 70 percent by the 4th of July.\par \par \uc2 CDC data shows the percentages of adults who have received at least one shot in every state. You can see that rates are lower in the southern region. They just are in the Rocky Mountain area around Idaho and Wyoming. And if you were to look at that map as red and blue, you would see a lot of red states. And in fact, one of those is Wyoming. That`s where Cal Perry is. It`s a state that ranks near the bottom per capita, fully vaccinated adults.\par \par \uc2 So Joining me is former acting CDC Director \uc2 Dr. Richard Besser\uc2 . Cal, let me start with you. You`re down there. And the reason we picked Wyoming is because it`s a state that it`s near the bottom. What have you learned on the ground there as to this? How much of this is just environmental where they`ve -- because of the sparseness of the state, the virus has never been as front and center perhaps there as it has in other places?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Look, I think that`s part of it. I think you`re finding that people who don`t want a vaccine are looking for a reason not to get one. People who wanted it have already gotten one. And so officials are focusing on that middle group. People that could still be persuaded. But as I said, there is an entire group of people here, it`s not just Wyoming, it`s around the country who don`t want the vaccine and they`re giving a variety of reasons for that.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to what Tony told us. We actually visited the feed store here in Cheyenne, which is a requirement, when you come to Wyoming you have to visit the feed store. Here were the reasons that he gave.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So many people we know come in the store getting sick, you know, I can afford, it`s just my wife and I that work here. We can`t afford to be gone. You know, this is our livelihood. I`ve worked here for 43 years. I can`t -- I can`t lose it over. You know, getting the shop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: A county health official told us, Chuck, that there`s an easy way to reach herd immunity and a hard way, the easy way is to vaccinate as many people as you can. The hard way is for people to get sick. The other thing that is happening here, the folks who are looking for an excuse not to get the vaccine are finding it inside mainstream media. Fox News is providing them plenty of reasons not to get a vaccine. It is something that we are hearing out here time and time again. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Cal Perry in Wyoming with what is a sort of familiar story now. And that`s where we bring in Dr. Besser. This is the story you hear. You`ve got -- we`ve got sort of a two-pronged issue, you know, sort of the hesitancy that would be there but there is an information stream that is feeding this hesitancy, that is -- it is actually -- I don`t want to -- well, some people might say is encouraging this hesitancy. So what do we do?\par \par \uc2 DR. RICHARD BESSER\uc2 , FORMER ACTING CDC DIRECTOR: Well, you know, I think one thing is to not look at all people who are hesitant as being in the same bucket. You know, there`s a lot of reasons that people are -- have not gotten vaccinated. Excuse me. The biggest -- the biggest reason is that, for some people, that hasn`t been easy. And, you know, as we`re seeing states make it easier and easier for people to get vaccinated, we`re going to see the numbers moving up.\par \par \uc2 We`re not going to see the three and four million dose days that we saw before, because that was really based on built-up demand that hadn`t been met yet. But we`re going to see slow increase. And, you know, the point you made earlier about a vaccine getting approved, that could change things in a big way. And one is that there are people who`ve been on the fence, wanting more safety information.\par \par \uc2 And to get approved by the FDA, the company will have to present more safety information, more data on effectiveness. And for people who had concerns that the emergency use process wasn`t rigorous enough, that should meet those concerns and bring those people forward. The other way that an approved vaccine may change things a little bit is that there`s some people who may not want to get a vaccine but they`re not opposed to.\par \par \uc2 And if they`re required to get vaccinated to do some of the things they want to do, like go to work, go to certain events, get on planes, they may say, because we`ve seen this in children. They may say, you know, it`s just not worth the effort to push back on getting vaccinated, I`ll just do it so I can do the things I want to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I was just going to say, I mean, is that ultimately, what you -- what we have to do here which is have all -- it`s an all of the above solution. So yes, getting, you know, I can tell you my kids school, since its emergency, youth authorization, they can`t require everybody to have it. But if you want to go to prom, you got to have it. Or if you want to go to the student section for game X, you got to have it.\par \par \uc2 Is that really probably our best way to sort of encourage that kind of -- don`t call it a mandate, mandate, encouragement. What do you think of financial incentives?\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Well, you know, I think it`s a -- it`s a -- it`s a wide range. So when you look at the groups that haven`t gotten vaccinated so far, you know, younger people, a lot of young people have just been granted access to vaccine. But a lot of younger people are like, well, if it`s really easy, I get vaccinated, or I want to wait a little while. When you look at Black Americans there, we`re seeing an increasing number of black Americans who want to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 But we have to make sure the barriers aren`t there. So bring it to communities does a lot. When you look at political conservatives, you know, it`s important to recognize that the vast majority of political conservatives want to get vaccinated. It`s a smaller sliver that say absolutely no way. I wouldn`t spend a lot of time on the people who are absolutely no way, maybe some will be moved by some of the requirements and mandates, but there is a group there that have questions.\par \par \uc2 And hopefully the safety information from approval will move some of -- some of that group. I like to make the case that, you know, we have to remember that there aren`t vaccines for children. Yes, I`m a pediatrician and a parent. And children are only protected by what we do as adults. And if we take those steps, follow public health guidance and get vaccinated we`re protecting our kids. And that`s a really important thing that resonates with people across the political spectrum.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is the process now with the formal application by \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 ? The estimate is months. What is months? Is months, two or three months? Or is months six to 12 months?\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Well, you know, it depends if they fast track this. I`m hoping it`s not six to 12, that it`s much shorter than that, that the FDA does their internal review. They`ll pull together again, the committee that reviewed the vaccines for emergency use. And they`ll look at this and make their consideration. We`ll see the same kind of meeting we saw before. And then we`ll see -- we`ll see the decision by FDA.\par \par \uc2 But I hope it doesn`t go that long. Because there`s a lot of familiarity. The companies -- all the companies have been submitting rolling data. So ongoing data as studies come in. And there`s a lot of interest at all levels of government at all levels of society, for the FDA to do their work as speedily as they can, but also not to cut corners. So that we`re assured that if these vaccines are approved, that they are safe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, it can`t have mandates or the semi requirements until you have full authorization from the FDA. So I imagine they get that. \uc2 Dr. Richard Besser\uc2 , always good to have your expertise on with us. So, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Thanks, Chuck. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve got it. Coming up. A federal grand jury is -- has now indicted Derek Chauvin and his fellow police officers. Former police officer for violating George Floyd civil rights. And also reveals another incident where Chauvin is accused of violating a young teenager`s civil rights. We`ll have more in a minute.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. New developments today in the Department of Justice`s crackdown on civil rights abuses following George Floyd`s death. This morning, the DOJ unsealed a grand jury indictment of the four former police officers that were all involved in Floyd`s arrest and death on charges that they violated Floyd`s civil rights. All four former officers were charged with depriving Floyd of his right to be free of unreasonable force.\par \par \uc2 Derek Chauvin, who was found guilty for Floyd`s death on three criminal counts last month was also charged with violating his right to reasonable seizure, which includes the right to reasonable use of force by a police officer. Two of the three other officers were also charged under the statute for failing to intervene. This morning`s indictments also revealed a disturbing new charge against Chauvin alleging abuse of force separate from Floyd`s death.\par \par \uc2 Chauvin was indicted on two more counts of civil rights violations, this time for his actions against a 14-year-old during an arrest in 2017.\par \par \uc2 So for more on these developments, I`m joined by NBC News justice correspondent Pete Williams.\par \par \uc2 All right, federal charges mean what? And where do we go from here with that legal fight and when would that trial begin?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Well, let me answer the second one first because it`s the easiest one. So as a general matter, whenever the government does this, whenever it comes into a state case that`s already proceeding, it usually stays in the background and waits to see what the outcome is in the state case to see whether or not there should be a second subsequent federal trial or whether the interest that the federal government has been vindicated by the state convictions.\par \par \uc2 So Chauvin obviously has already been convicted and will be sentenced. The other three officers will go on trial soon. And my guess here, and the government hasn`t said yet, but my guess is the Justice Department will wait to see what happens on the state charges. Now it`s a separate matter on the incident involving the 14-year-old in 2017 when the government says that he struck this child with his flashlight and state court documents said Chauvin put his knee on the young man`s back for 17 minutes.\par \par \uc2 So that`s a separate case. And the government could go ahead with that if it wanted to now, although, obviously, it`s a hard thing to get somebody who is already serving time and is being detained to come into federal court to face the charges. Could be done but it`s a complicated thing. So my guess, Chuck, is that they`ll wait to let the state cases play out before they proceed with any federal charges.\par \par \uc2 Now the first thing is why. And I think the reason is that the government wants to show that it`s going to be really tough on police violations of people`s civil rights. Police excessive use of force, this a real interest of the Justice Department under President Biden and Merrick Garland. You`ve already seen two pattern of practice investigations launched and two federal investigations.\par \par \uc2 So my guess is that`s the point they`re trying to make here, that they want to get tough on police misconduct.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So are these safety net charges then? That if the Justice Department feels as if these officers weren`t -- you know, weren`t -- didn`t go through a fair process with the state, however they determine it, then they`ll decide whether to take this to trial?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That`s typically the way it goes. Now there is a federal policy that says the government shouldn`t intervene unless the state cases go off the rails or if the state process is already in play, should not intervene, unless there is a federal interest to be vindicated. And what I gather here is, you know, the essence of the state charge was not a civil rights violation. The essence of the state charge was assault and excessive use of force. So this is a different case, a different theory. And that`s what the government is trying to protect here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In theory, this could add more prison time for all of them, or if they do end up getting charged, you don`t expect them to push forward?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I don`t know whether the government will go ahead if all four are convicted, whether they would still go ahead with a trial on the George Floyd matter.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: They could go ahead with a trial on the other involving the teenager.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Because that was never charged by the state or hasn`t been so far that I know of. The maximum penalty under the federal law -- I mean, here`s the duality here. The maximum penalty under the federal law is life. And so that`s more than what it would be to the state charges. On the other hand, the federal law has a much higher evidentiary standard to meet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Pete Williams with the latest on these indictments of Derek Chauvin and those former police officers.\par \par \uc2 Pete Williams, thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You bet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, our look at how the Republican Party evolved from a party that championed immigration to one calling for building a wall. Plus Commerce Secretary Gina Raimondo will be here in just a few minutes. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As the Biden administration continues to confront the influx of migrants coming over the southern border, we`re taking a deeper dive into the topic on the new episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." It`s actually the second episode of our two-part series focusing on immigration and the border. And this one is a bit more of a political look as we`re looking how the GOP went from Ronald Reagan who famously granted amnesty to nearly three million undocumented immigrants to Donald Trump who campaigned on harsh immigration rhetoric and a promise to build a wall.\par \par \uc2 Telemundo`s Vanessa Hauc dives into the party`s immigration evolution on this week`s episode. Here`s a quick look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VANESSA HAUC, TELEMUNDO REPORTER: Illegal immigration at the border is not new. In fact, apprehensions peaked early in the century then stabilized for a decade before rising again. Still a large influx of immigrants has unnerved many Americans who worry about the country`s change in demographics.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL GARCIA, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, THE LIBRE INITIATIVE: I think there`s been a concern, an angst about the changes in America. And a lot of that has to do obviously with, you know, how culture changes, how the face of America begins to change.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: As you know, we have what they call the caravan.\par \par \uc2 HAUC: Despite his harsh anti-immigrant rhetoric, President Trump gained four points among Latinos in the election. One reason many Latinos hold traditional conservative views.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA LLAMAS, BUSINESS OWNER: I`m a businessowner. And I don`t want the government to tell me what to do or to put many regulations on me. For me that`s very important.\par \par \uc2 DRIENA SIXTO, CONSERVATIVE ACTIVIST: Latinos are naturally conservative because we believe in family. We believe in our right to work and put food on the table for our families, because we believe in free speech, because in our countries where either we or our parent came from, we didn`t have that.\par \par \uc2 HAUC: It`s that kind of conservative leaning attitude that encourages Republicans like Congresswoman Maria Elvira Salazar of Florida. She hopes spirit of bipartisanship will eventually lead to meaningful immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARIA ELVIRA SALAZAR (R-FL): All they want is dignity. They do not want to wait for this path to citizenship that never comes. They want dignity now. And what does that mean? For them to be able to work, raise their American children, pay taxes, not commit a crime and be able to go home for Christmas.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: I`m starting to see members of both the Senate and the House coming together. I think they are starting to realize that the only way we`re going to arrive at immigration reform is by working in the spirit of bipartisanship.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We have a lot more of both Vanessa`s report and more on the show itself, a look at how immigrants have changed American politics throughout the fast few decades. You can see the full episode on demand right now on Peacock. So binge away. And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The April jobs report showed we added just 266,000 new jobs last month. It`s far below what was expected. That used to be a pretty good jobs number but this was not what folks expected. The unemployment rate also ticked up slightly to 6.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Consider this, the economy needs to create another 8.2 million jobs at a minimum just to put us back where we were before COVID hit. And some argue we need 10 million more jobs to sort of stay in the trajectory we were on before COVID hit. President Biden called for patience with our economic recovery a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It`s going to continue to improve. Today`s report makes clear, thank goodness we passed the American Rescue Plan. Help is here. And more help is on the way. And more help is needed. The American Rescue Plan was for the whole year. It plays out over a year. And it`s working. But we can`t let up. This jobs report makes that clear.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m joined first by NBC White House correspondent Mike Memoli,\par \par \uc2 So, Mike, we`ve seen partisans from both sides spin this report in order to make a point about the president`s various spending proposals. Look, the White House, are they disappointed that these numbers are as anemic as many have described?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chuck, if there was any doubt about whether this president consumes cable news coverage just like the last one did, I think it was put to rest when we heard the president come to the cameras today because there was some sensitivity I heard in terms of him explicitly saying in the coverage that he was watching about how dismal this report was, he tried to set the record straight and, in fact, push back on some of the criticisms.\par \par \uc2 For instance saying that for those who worry that the trillions in dollars he`s been proposing would overheat the economy, this jobs report shows that this is an economy that still very much needs that federal assistance.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: We had the plain-spoken president talking about family expressions that job is more than a paycheck. Then we had the Fed speak of Janet Yellen, the Treasury secretary at the podium in the press briefing room talking about the fact that yes, we still have a country that`s dealing with a pandemic, and the caregiving needs is what she attributed a lot of the criticism from Republicans about whether unemployment assistance is too much if it`s deterring people from going back to the work force.\par \par \uc2 She making a push implicitly for the families plan which has a big push for increased daycare, free kindergarten, things like that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: That`s what this data showed to her.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mike Memoli at the White House for us. Mike, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 And as I indicated I`m now joined by the Commerce Secretary Gina Raimondo and for those of you that don`t recall this, she most recently was also the governor of Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Raimondo, it`s good to -- I think it`s my first time interviewing you as a Cabinet secretary, so it`s good to be doing that. Look, there`s a lot of analysis out there that we clearly have an issue on the supply side when it comes to labor. And I guess the question I have for you is, what is the driver here in what you`ve been looking at so far in your opinion? Is the driver the benefits? Is the driver the fact that not enough schools and daycare is open? Is the driver all of it?\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO, COMMERCE SECRETARY: Yes. So good afternoon and it`s good to be with you. I think the driver is the fact that we`re still in a pandemic. People are still afraid to go back to work. Some schools are still closed in many places, people are struggling with child care, people are afraid to go back to work particularly in close in-contact service industries. There is really no evidence to suggest that this has to do with the increased unemployment insurance payments.\par \par \uc2 In fact, more people were looking for work last month than the month before and they`re not finding jobs. And you`ve even seen actually a small dip in manufacturing jobs available. Now this -- we can`t make, you know, too much out of one month.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: But the point of it is we have to -- we better pass this jobs package so we can get people back to work because basically what this nets out to is the American people are still struggling and we have to be there for them to help them get through the pandemic and get back to work.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious on the manufacturing number. I`ve seen some analysis that indicates how much of this is a supply chain issue. For instance, you know, we know there is high demand for cars, but right now there is not enough computer chips to make those cars.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: I do think that that is a factor. Again, there is a lot of noise in the data of just one month. But it`s certainly a factor. You know, obviously auto manufacturers have had to furlough workers and close some of the manufacturing lines because they can`t get their hands on enough chips. We`re working very hard to get the president`s plan passed in Congress to fund the semiconductor fund.\par \par \uc2 The fact of the matter is we need to get back into the business of making more chips in America, and the supply chain vulnerabilities are very real, exactly as you say, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When I go back to the issue of the unemployment benefits, do you expect -- do you think that is particularly is what`s making it harder for lower wage jobs to get filled, particularly in the restaurant industry? Do you accept that maybe that`s a factor there?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: I don`t really. You know, you hear it anecdotally, certainly, but there is no evidence to suggest that that is what is driving this. You know, there is greater evidence to suggest that people are afraid to go back to work, or that they can`t, you know, their kids are still out of school and they can`t get child care. So, you know, you hear it anecdotally, certainly, but I do not think that that`s what`s driving it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`ve heard the president yesterday, going back to the plan, that he is -- we`ve heard two things this week, that he`s ready to negotiate on even the scope but particularly on the tax cuts. But he also said he is not going to deficit spend. So, you know, how much should lawmakers take that to the bank? I mean, should be expect he is not going to support any deal that adds to the deficit if he would really turn down some of his signature proposals if it was deficit spending?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: I think the president is very serious. We should take him at his word. You know, he wants to compromise. He absolutely wants to find bipartisan agreement. As a member of his jobs Cabinet, that`s certainly the direction he`s given us in our conversations with Capitol Hill. Find common ground where we can. But I`ll tell you this, he strongly believes, we all do, that doing nothing is not an option, and so we`re all working as hard as we can to drive towards consensus and, you know, compromise will be part of that, but it`s time for action and it`s time for bold action.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When it comes to this bipartisan deal, perhaps, for infrastructure, we heard Senator Capito saying that they didn`t make their final offer either. How optimistic are you that the lion`s share of this bill gets passed in a bipartisan way?\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: I am optimistic. I am optimistic, and I am working towards that. We`re all working towards that. We have a meeting later this afternoon with the president to talk about that. I think we have to stay optimistic and we have to work to make it a reality.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Secretary Gina Raimondo, I know I`m up at the end of my hour. I appreciate you coming on. Jobs report Friday. I imagine a month from now, you hope it`s a better jobs number that we can all talk about.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Secretary Raimondo, thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 RAIMONDO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And before we go, I want to share some pretty good news from inside our little house here. The MEET THE PRESS DAILY senior producer Diane Sherry Gorman, her husband Ryan and big sisters Laurel and Vivian, they welcomed Joseph Ryan Gorman into the world this morning.\par \par \uc2 He`s named after his grandfather, a decorated veteran of the Vietnam War. So a big congratulations to the whole Gorman family from their MEET THE PRESS family. And how about that? Two older sisters. You will someday be glad about that. My son swears having an older sister is not good, but he`ll come around.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us today. We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And if it`s Sunday, it`s MEET THE PRESS on your local NBC station. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, NBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: It`s good to see you. I`m Geoff Bennett in for my friend Katy Tur. As we come on the air, we are following a lot of breaking news on this Friday.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210508eh5700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word for April 30, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Jonathan Capehart, Glenn Kirschner \par \uc2 8,316 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: This weekend, there`s a big special that`s going to run on MSNBC and on NBC and on Telemundo and on Peacock. That`s something that never really happens, but this is big deal. It`s called "Inspiring America: The 2021 Inspiration List." It`s got people like Becky Hammon who is the first woman to coach an \uc2 NBA\uc2  team. Chef Jose Andres who served 35 million hot meals to people in need this year. Even Lin-Manuel Miranda.\par \par \uc2 It`s a big deal. You can catch it here on MSNBC this Sunday at 10:00 p.m. eastern. Inspiring people doing inspiring things. I`m at the time in my year when I`m ready to be inspired in that way. Again, 10:00 eastern here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us tonight. See you on Monday. Now it`s time for the "Last Word" where Ali Velshi is in for Lawrence tonight. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel. I, too, am at that time in my year and just getting ready for this has been fun because we`ve been going through people who are doing inspiring things in our lives, in the news. That doesn`t bubble to the top as much as it should, so I`m excited about it. Rachel, have yourself a fantastic weekend and we`ll see you on Monday.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thanks, my friend. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, it`s just 101 days into President Biden`s term, six months after his election. The new president has already proven that the Trump Republican claims about a Biden economy were total lies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He`s a headwind on the stock market because if he gets in the market will crash.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Not only is the economy booming under Biden but we`re at transformational moment for the American economy. A moment that could improve the course of the nation for generations to come. In 101 days President Biden has overseen a really strong start to our economic recovery as more Americans get vaccinated and a return to pre-pandemic life is in sight.\par \par \uc2 Household incomes increased in March by 21.1 percent. That`s the largest monthly increase since 1959. Largely due to the stimulus checks in President Biden`s American Rescue Plan. In the first three months of this year the gross domestic product, GDP increased at an annual rate of 6.4 percent. That`s the second largest quarterly increase since 2003.\par \par \uc2 And the stock market is performing the best that it has performed during the first 100 days of a presidency going back to at least the 1950s and the Eisenhower administration. But, of course, lots of people don`t benefit from a rising stock market.\par \par \uc2 Poverty has deepened in America. It`s expanded for many during the pandemic. The inequality gap grew especially for black and brown communities. And women have been disproportionately affected by job loss. So as this recovery takes shape and builds, the important question becomes whether those at the bottom of the economic ladder will be left behind.\par \par \uc2 Will the post-COVID economy benefit those who have been traditionally marginalized? Well, that`s at the core of Biden`s proposals which he detailed during his address to Congress this week. President Biden wants to use the incredible power of the presidency to share the financial prosperity that usually benefits the rich particularly in the wake of a recession.\par \par \uc2 That`s the complete opposite of what Trump and Republicans gave us the last four years. Many voters supported Trump because they thought that he would do wonders for the economy, which was turns out based on reality show gimmicks, phony business credentials and some straight up lies. He didn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 He just rode the successes that President \uc2 Obama\uc2  had laid out for him. Along the way he did help top earners and corporations through some massive tax cuts, which increased inequality for most Americans. The first real test of Trump`s ability to manage economic troubles was the coronavirus pandemic. And that`s when the world saw what many of us already knew. He wasn`t up to the job.\par \par \uc2 And his failure to manage the pandemic meant that the economic impact of COVID would last far longer and hurt many more Americans than it would have. Now, instead of injecting bleach, President Biden and Democrats are injecting cash into the economy to help it grow, to better the lives of average Americans.\par \par \uc2 That`s why he`s proposing to reduce the cost of childcare, to ensure paid family and medical leave, to extend the child tax credit, to expand access to care for older and disabled people, and to offer free universal preschool and two years of free community college.\par \par \uc2 That`s why the president just raised the minimum wage for federal contractors to $15 an hour, which is interesting. You can be a federal employee and earn less than that. He`s creating a road map for tangible long-term economic progress for working people just like FDR did with the new deal and after the Great Depression.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is daring America to understand that government can adapt and respond to the economic needs of a nation that is emerging from a once in a generation recession. Biden`s economic vision is bold, but boldness is what this country needs right now to ensure that all Americans not just the top 1 percent or the top 5 percent or the top 10 percent recover and flourish.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight, Mark Zandi, chief economist for Moody`s Analytics. Mark, it`s good to see you. Thank you for being with us. I want you to give us some sense of this economy that we are in right now and why it`s happening, why the stock market is performing as well as it is? Why GDP growth was as strong as it was? How much of that has to do with Biden? How much of it has to do with the end in sight of coronavirus?\par \par \uc2 MARK ZANDI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, MOODY`S ANALYTICS: Well, it`s good to be with you, Ali. The economy is, I`m happy to report, is booming. Very, very strong growth. We saw GDP, that`s the valuables, the things we produce, that grew 6.4 percent in the first quarter. That`s very robust growth.\par \par \uc2 Lots of jobs, unemployment insurance claims are falling. Unemployment is declining. Consumer sentiment is improving. The housing market is -- man, I can go on and on and on. In fact, you know, I`ve been a professional economist, Ali, for I don`t know 30-odd years, and I don`t think I can remember a time when the prospects for the economy over the next 12, 18 months are as good as they are today.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s all good. We got a big hole to dig out of. There are still a lot of folks that are struggling, but we`re well on our way of digging out of that deep hole we got into. For the reasons why, there`s two key ones. One, clearly the pandemic is winding down. You know, we`re approaching herd immunity.\par \par \uc2 The vaccination process has gone well. Over half, well over half now of adult Americans have been vaccinated. So, that`s all good. And by the summer I think we`ll be open and -- open for business and that`s all-good news. And of course, the stock market is reacting to that.\par \par \uc2 And then of course the other really very important factor is the American Rescue Plan. That is the $1.9 trillion fiscal support package. You mentioned the stimulus checks, unemployment insurance, rental assistance, food assistance, help to the airline industry, I mean, I can go on and on and on.\par \par \uc2 That is really super charging the economy, and then of course you have very low interest rates provided by the Feds. It`s all coming together. We have these very significant tail winds.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ZANDI: The economy is (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Mark, you say you`ve been a professional economist for about 30 years. You and I have talked for a very large portion of that. And one of the things that`s become bigger each year has been this equality gap, the idea that, you know, when we hit recession, if you go into a recession with pretty good credit and capital you come out stronger, and if you went in weak you sometimes come out weaker.\par \par \uc2 We have this problem. We`ve got food insecurity. We`ve got people are still earning $7.25 an hour. We have 8 million fewer employed people than we had a year ago. Biden seems to want to have a recovery that doesn`t leave all of them behind.\par \par \uc2 ZANDI: Yes, totally agree. I think, you know, if you look at the American Rescue Plan where the support goes, it goes to lower income households that are under the most stress, the folks that are unemployed, underemployed who got hit hard by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Then of course the build back better agenda, which we`ve now got a much clearer vision of, that`s the American jobs plan, the infrastructure plan you were talking about and the American families plan. That`s all the support that goes to families through education and childcare and family leave.\par \par \uc2 Those plans are designed to help support lower income, lower middle-income Americans. And it`s paid for by higher taxes on large corporations and the well-to-do, high income households. So, the idea behind this, build back better agenda, is to help improve long-term economic growth but then take the benefits of that growth and make sure that low-income Americans who have been left behind benefit from that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right. I`m going to go back to sort of the early days of your and my career where it was more broadly accepted that the idea of trickle- down economics, that benefits will trickle down if you give them to top earners and corporations. A recent Ipsos/Reuters poll exactly was released yesterday, said that 51 percent agree with the statement that trickle-down economics have never worked with America.\par \par \uc2 Among Republicans, four in 10 agreed that it was failed theory. This is something that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said to Congress on Wednesday. What`s your evaluation of that, the idea of the trickle-down economics don`t work?\par \par \uc2 ZANDI: They don`t. We`ve tried this a few times and each time it`s failed miserably. I mean, it`s, you know, evident in the data. The income and wealth distribution has become increasingly more skewed, you know, going all the way back, you know, to the late `70s and early `80s. So this has been a long running development, not something that just happened overnight.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve tried trickle-down economics a couple three times during that period and so, that has not worked. And I do think it makes a lot of sense to address this problem head on and provide support directly to the groups that need it.\par \par \uc2 And that, you know, that is immediate help to navigate through the pandemic because many lower income groups didn`t have any financial restrictions (ph) coming into the pandemic, so they were left without any support. They needed it.\par \par \uc2 But even more importantly going forward making sure they get the help they need to, you know, get their kids educated, get themselves educated, get the childcare they need so they can go to work, make the economy work for them.\par \par \uc2 And so, these programs are designed to, you know again, lift the entire economy and support stronger and longer (inaudible) economic growth, improve productivity, increase labor force participation, all the things we want to see in a well-functioning economy. But then make sure that, you know, those benefits go to those folks that have really been left behind.\par \par \uc2 And try, you know, it took us 30, 35 years to get here over this income wealth distribution. It`s going to take us a long time to get out. So, it`s going to be --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ZANDI: -- it`s going to require, you know, a very persistent policy effort and we`re really now beginning.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I for one am pleased to be in a world in which we -- you and I can have economic policy discussions again. The news cycle didn`t allow that to happen for a while. Mark, good to see you as always. Mark Zandi is the chief economist for Moody`s Analytics.\par \par \uc2 Well, today, President Biden visited his favorite swing state, Pennsylvania, to promote his economic proposals and of course he delivered his speech at an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  station in Philadelphia. A place very familiar to him from his years of commuting by train.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from Pennsylvania is state representative Malcolm Kenyatta. He is running for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in Pennsylvania. Representative -- Senator Kenyatta, good to see you again. Thank you for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 I want to continue the conversation that Mark and I were having about sort of bottom-up economics that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is talking about, and the philosophical idea that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  shares with many Democrats, that government has a leading role to play at moments like this, that government is a force for good. How do you overcome in a place like Pennsylvania? A whole bunch of conservatives who have for years believed the opposite.\par \par \uc2 REP. MALCOLM KENYATTA (D-PA): Yes, I think -- first of all, it`s good to be with you. I think one of the things you`re seeing is that actually investing in working families is not only good for the economy, it`s good for politics. These proposals that the president has rolled out one after the other are deeply popular with the American people.\par \par \uc2 You know, and you talked about, you know, philosophy, but for a lot of folks here, people in my district, for people like me who grew up in a working poor family, this is not philosophical or hypothetical, it`s real. Whether or not we expand broadband for rural communities that`s real for those family.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not people can afford childcare, that`s real. Whether or not we do something about the care economy -- my mom was a home health care aide - - that is real. And so, what he is doing is laying out proposals that really meet the moment where American people are trying to get out of this pandemic but also trying to confront a lot of the systems that made this pandemic as bad as it was.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: What do you do, though, in a place like Pennsylvania where you`re running outside of your district? Where you`re going to run in a state that has some major cities, some major urban centers and a lot of rural in between where people have been subject to stories, in some cases the big lie which has played out quite effectively in rural Pennsylvania. How do you get that argument out to people out there? Have you tried, and how`s it going?\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: So, you know, my campaign is really about answering a simple question. And if you`ve been an organizer as I have, if you`ve stood on picket lines with working folks demanding better as I have many times, there`s a song, Ali, that you`ll hear a lot of times on the picket line. And it says which side are you on, which side are you on?\par \par \uc2 And the answer to that has to be the people side. That`s why when we look at our campaign, we see so many people rushing to malcolmkenyatta.com to chip just 33 bucks at a time. That`s our average contribution because no matter if you`re in Philadelphia or you`re in Altoona or you`re in Scranton, you`re asking that question.\par \par \uc2 Which side is the government on? Is it on the side of working people or is it going to continue to be on the side of big corporations that have been able to write the rules to their benefit time and time and time again? And they`ve really had a good employee in \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  that has worked for those big corporations and not for the people of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let me play for you a little snippet of a conversation that my colleague Craig Melvin had with President Biden yesterday, and let`s talk on the other side of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don`t have any inordinate faith in government, but there`s certain things only the government can do. Is the private sector going to go out and build billions of dollars` worth of highways, ports, airports, bridges? Are they going to do that? And so these are things that only government can really do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That message, can you get that to resonate with people, the idea there are things that government just does better than individuals or corporations?\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Absolutely. And not only is the president absolutely right, but I think a part of what we need to change is having people in government who understand in their bones what it looks like when government fails, what it looks like when government doesn`t work.\par \par \uc2 You know, I watched my mom all the time ration her insulin, my dad the same thing. Lost both of them before I turned 27. We moved four, five different times. So, whether you`re talking about infrastructure or you`re talking about housing, or you`re talking about confronting the climate crisis by thinking big and investing in good clean energy jobs, these are things that government has to take the lead on.\par \par \uc2 And we see right now what happens when government steps up to the plate. You talked about how the economy is roaring and how so many people are beginning to get their footing again. But we still have to deal with some of the systemic challenges, and that`s what the American Families Plan and the American Jobs Plan are all about.\par \par \uc2 You know, I watched the president`s remarks the other day before a joint session of Congress and watched as he talked about cutting childhood poverty in half, and you have a whole section of the room that didn`t think that that was worthy of applause and didn`t want to support the president in making that happen.\par \par \uc2 And so, we do know there are people in government that for so long have been committed to making government not work and then telling the lie that government doesn`t work without acknowledging the fact that they are the reason that government doesn`t work.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: And so, we have a president now that is doing big, bold things. And that`s what we`re doing in this campaign, and I think Pennsylvania`s ready.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Representative Kenyatta, I always enjoy our conversations, but I think you may have made history tonight. I don`t know anybody has ever sung to me live on T.V. So, I`m grateful for that. It has added a new layer to our relationship. Representative Malcolm Kenyatta of Pennsylvania, thank you for joining me tonight.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, coming up, so if Republicans can no longer baselessly claim Republicans good, Democrats bad on the economy then what`s left? After four years of incompetent and corrupt governance from the Trump administration, the way the Republicans plan to win is to stop voters from voting. \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  and Renee Graham join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: President Biden`s agenda is unabashedly progressive, arguably the most progressive since FDR. And despite having razor thin margins in the House and Senate, Democrats are happily blazing forward with those plans. They can do that because Republican attacks on Biden as socialist aren`t working.\par \par \uc2 A new NBC News poll shows that voters believe Biden is more moderate than \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was at this same stage of his presidency even though Biden`s plans are far more ambitious. NBC notes that Biden`s $1.9 trillion stimulus package was considerably larger than the $787 billion recovery aid enacted by \uc2 Obama\uc2  in the early days of his administration.\par \par \uc2 Add on top of that Biden`s $4 trillion for infrastructure proposal that includes liberal-favored policies like paid family leave, universal pre-K and free community college. What`s more, all of this is popular with majorities of Americans, so are his proposals to pay for these policies by raising taxes on corporations and the rich.\par \par \uc2 It might explain why Republicans are choosing a different strategy to gain the upper hand. The Associated Press reports, "Biden himself simply isn`t proving to be an easy target or animating figure for the GOP base prompting Republicans to turn to the kind of cultural issues the party has used to cast Democrats as elitist and out of touch with average Americans."\par \par \uc2 That`s why you see Republicans and conservative media complaining about cancel culture instead of economic relief. But this GOP strategy can do real world harm to real people. Take a look at how they`re targeting transgender athletes from competing in school sports or how they`re trying to stop people from voting.\par \par \uc2 If they can`t beat Democrats on the issues, they`ll just try to keep voters from voting. In fact, just last night, Republicans in Florida passed the latest voter suppression bill which Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  says that he is going to sign.\par \par \uc2 The bill puts new restrictions on the use of drop boxes, adds new I.D. requirements to vote by mail and makes voters request absentee ballots more frequently.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  who is an opinion writer for "The Washington Post" and the host of "The Sunday Show with \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 " on MSNBC, Renee Graham, opinion columnist and associate editor at the "\uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ." Good evening to both of you. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 Renee, it is kind of interesting because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as I just discussed with Representative Kenyatta, is embracing this idea as government as meaningful, government as influential in people`s lives, big government being okay. And that flies in four plus decades of opposition from conservatives. You don`t see conservatives up there fighting that. They`re fighting other things.\par \par \uc2 RENEE GRAHAM, COLUMNIST AND ASSOCIATE EDITOR, THE \uc2 BOSTON GLOBE\uc2 : Right. I mean, it`s hard to fight against what people actually want. The polls keep proving that they want what Biden is proposing. So, all the conservatives can do is raise all these other issues which have nothing to do with people`s day to day lives.\par \par \uc2 Vilifying trans students isn`t helping America. All you`re doing is vilifying this group of vulnerable people. So, I think it`s sort of absurd that Republicans are spending all this time on these issues and not on what people actually care about.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes, which is interesting because we could have valid discussions, Jonathan, about the things that people care about, right? Everybody wants a higher minimum wage. You`ve got two Republicans. I think \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  and Tom Cotton proposing a $10 minimum wage as opposed to the $15 that Biden is suggesting. Ten is not great, but it`s better than $7.25.\par \par \uc2 Republicans could go to every issue that Biden is on and try and put forward a compromise, but it always ends up trickling out. They always happen to have five or six or maybe 10 Republicans who engage in the discussion and then it all just fizzles out because \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  wants nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Right. They`re not interested in governing, Ali. I mean, basically, that`s what it boils down to. A functioning party, a functioning Republican Party, there would be more Mitt Romneys, more Tom Cottons coming to the table on every issue to say to the American people, yes, you heard President Biden in his joint session speech, but here`s our plan for childcare, our plan for paid family leave, our plan to boost the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Yes, he says $15, here`s our proposal and here`s how we`re going to pay for it. That`s what a functioning, governing or functioning party and a party interested in governing would do, but that`s not what`s happening. What they`re trying to do instead because what the president is doing is popular with a broad swath of the American people is they`ve just decided, well, let`s just try to keep as many people who could vote for the Democrats from voting.\par \par \uc2 Remember, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got to most votes for president of any person ever in American history and Republicans saw that and said not going to do it again.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes. Renee, I was intrigued and maybe a little excited to hear Republican Senator Tim Scott`s rebuttal to Joe Biden`s address on Wednesday night. You know, he has shown some propensity to try and find some common ground on the George Floyd Justice and Policing Act. So, I was surprised to hear this from Tim Scott. Let`s listen and talk on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOT (R-SC): Hear me clearly. America is not a racist country. It`s backwards to fight discrimination with different types of discrimination. And it`s wrong to try to use our painful past to dishonestly shutdown debates in the present. I`m an African American who`s voted in the south my entire life. I take voting rights personally. Republicans support making it easier to vote and harder to cheat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, I`m going to take Tim Scott at his word as an African American from the south he takes voting rights seriously, but personally, I have no reason to doubt that. But that last sentence was a little odd because Republicans around the country are not demonstrating that they want to make it easier to vote.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Not at all. I mean, you know, the thing is that you have to go back about a decade. The GOP was fretting about the changing demographics and it was loosening their hold on power. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  said we`re not generating enough angry white guys to stay in business for the long-term.\par \par \uc2 So now what they have are all these bills and laws to make it more difficult. That`s why \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is around now saying, oh, 2022 is going to be a blowout year for Republicans because they don`t have policies. What they have is voter suppression. That`s what they`re using to win. So this whole idea that somehow it`s all good and these laws are actually going to make things better is complete nonsense.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Thanks to both of you for joining me tonight. Renee, always good to see you. Jonathan, we are going to see each other again on Sunday morning so thanks for giving me a little warm-up on Friday night. \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  is the host of the Sunday show 10:00 a.m. eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, breaking news on the federal investigation into \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . New details about the search warrants and why they were not executed under the Bill Barr Justice Department is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Breaking news on the federal investigation into \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . The Associated Press reports tonight that Justice Department officials in the Trump administration considered serving Giuliani with a search warrant last fall. But an internal dispute over the case never resolved leaving Biden officials to make the decision.\par \par \uc2 The AP previously reported that, quote, "Federal prosecutors in Manhattan pushed last year for a search warrant for records, but officials in the Trump era Justice Department would not sign off on the request. DOJ officials raised concerns about both the scope of the request which they thought would contain communications that could be covered by legal privilege between Giuliani and Trump and the method of obtaining the records."\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we`re learning more about the search warrants that were executed this week on Giuliani`s home and office. According to "The Wall Street Journal" the warrants sought communications with an array of Ukrainian officials including information related to the ouster of \uc2 Marie Yovanovitch\uc2 , the former U.S. ambassador to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Quote, "Federal prosecutors have pursued a theory that hinges on the idea that Mr. Giuliani`s efforts to remove Ms. Yovanovitch were done at the behest of Ukrainian officials in exchange for damaging information about the Bidens. Such an exchange, even if it involved no financial payment could violate federal lobbying laws," end quote.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Glenn Kirschner. He`s a former prosecutor and an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Good evening to you, Glenn. I would love to know not only what you make of this -- the raid, the seizing of documents, and of course, Rudy Giuliani`s responses. I guess you could have expected that he was going to have them, but he`s been all over TV and Web sites responding to it.\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, Ali. I did watch the \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  interview of \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and he just completely rewrote the law on search warrants.\par \par \uc2 So, for example, he told the viewers that the only way prosecutors can get a search warrant is if there`s evidence that the target is going to destroy or, in his words, run away with the evidence.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t know why he is misleading the Fox viewers, but there actually is only one thing that has to be proved in order to get a search warrant. You have to show that there`s probable cause that a crime was committed and that there`s evidence of that crime in the place to be searched.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge concluded there is evidence of crime presently in Rudy Giuliani`s home, in his office and in his electronic devices. So, you know, Rudy Giuliani`s attempt to explain it all away as, in his words, an illegal search warrant really falls flat.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, there`s another bombastic lawyer and confidant of Donald Trump`s who had a search warranted executed on his work and home, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 . He was on with Nicolle Wallace tonight with a little advice for \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . Let`s listen to what he has to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER PERSONAL LAWYER OF \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : This is my speech to Rudy and to, you know, anybody else. They have him. and so, if he`s looking to protect himself so that he doesn`t end up spending the rest of his life in prison, he may want to actually start to cooperate.\par \par \uc2 It`s something that I didn`t do that I probably should have done, hindsight being 20-20.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: What do you make of that advice, Glenn?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes, that`s the voice of experience talking because let`s look at what happened last time. Federal search warrants were executed on an attorney`s home, a presidential attorney and in an attorney`s office.\par \par \uc2 Well, that was \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , he ended up going to prison. So, if past is prologue, I think \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  ought to hunker down with his lawyers and start to discuss, ok, who are the bigger fish that I can give up.\par \par \uc2 So, if it were me as the prosecutor I would be debriefing \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  about what he knows about Bill Barr, about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , about Don, Jr. and others. Because if anybody knows the value of cooperating with a criminal probe, it`s a former U.S. attorney like \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And there`s more to it than the bombast and the things having to do with the election, which were most recent in our memory about \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . This actually has to do with Ukraine and the American presence there and the acting Ambassador \uc2 Marie Yovanovitch\uc2 , who by the way, talked about \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  targeting her, which probably ultimately led to her losing her job in the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Let`s listen to what she had to say about \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIE YOVANOVITCH\uc2 , FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO THE UKRAINE: I do not understand Mr. Giuliani`s motives for attacking me, nor can I offer an opinion on whether he believed the allegations he spread about him. Clearly no one at the State Department did.\par \par \uc2 What I can say is that Mr. Giuliani should have known those claims were suspect coming as they reportedly did from individuals with questionable motives and with reason to believe that their political and financial ambitions would be stymied by our anti-corruption policy in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: \uc2 Marie Yovanovitch\uc2  was somebody that some powerful people in Ukraine would have liked out of the way, and ultimately that worked. I don`t know how that makes it more or less serious as a crime, but it is more -- it`s serious as a matter of national security that \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  might have been up to this kind of activity either at the behest of the Russian -- of the Ukrainians or not.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes. And this is a really interesting piece, Ali, because a president has plenary authority to remove an ambassador for good reason or for no reason at all. So at any moment \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could have picked up the phone and recalled Ambassador \uc2 Marie Yovanovitch\uc2 , but that`s not what was going on.\par \par \uc2 What was going on was \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was working behind the scenes to try to dislodge Ambassador Yovanovitch who by all accounts was a really accomplished corruption fighter in her post as an ambassador.\par \par \uc2 So if the president could have summarily removed her, why did \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  have to work behind the scenes to almost covertly try to push her out? That tells us -- and this is why I think you read that it`s the working theory of the prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 It tells us that \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  wasn`t doing this for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He could have done it himself. What he was probably doing is what the evidence is beginning to look like was he was acting at the behest of powerful Ukrainians who did not like Ambassador Yovanovitch`s anti-corruption work. That is a really bad look for \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Glenn, always good to see you. You make everything substantially clearer for me. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Kirschner is a former federal prosecutor and an MSNBC legal analyst. Thank you for joining us, sir.\par \par \uc2 Well coming up, just weeks before Andrew Brown, Jr. was killed by police in what his family called an execution, Democrats in North Carolina were fighting to change the extremely restrictive body cam video laws that exist in that state. We`re going to talk about that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: In the six-plus years since Michael Brown was shot and killed by police in Ferguson, Missouri 8,000 police departments across the country have outfitted their officers with body-worn cameras. Police body cameras matter because in most cases there isn`t a witness like Darnella Frazier who heroically held up her phone and recorded Derek Chauvin murdering George Floyd by putting his knee on his neck for 9 minutes and 29 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Brown, Jr. was shot and killed by police in North Carolina one day after Derek Chauvin was convicted. But in North Carolina footage from police body cameras can only be released to the public with a court order. And this week a judge ruled against releasing the Brown footage at this time.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks before Brown was killed by police, lawmakers in North Carolina introduced a bill that would require all law enforcement agencies in the state to release any body camera footage as a public record after 48 hour.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is one of the cosponsors of that North Carolina bill, state Senator Don Davis. Senator, good to see you. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 I`m curious as to the response you`re getting against your proposal including from the judge who didn`t come up with a particularly compelling reason for not having the video in this particular case released publicly.\par \par \uc2 DON DAVIS (D-NC), STATE SENATOR: Ali, let me say truly I`m glad to be with you. And first and foremost, my heart goes out to the Brown family and the Pasquotank County community here in North Carolina. To the family and the community, I want it to be loud and clear that we`ve heard the demands for transparency and accountability and that to me is what this is about.\par \par \uc2 That`s why you see this legislation and the need to get this footage released not only to the family but to the public and even to the law enforcement community.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And you got this started before this particular killing that has made the news. One of the things according to the "Raleigh Observer" is that if there was some reason not to release it that was in the public interest it would have to be released within 48 hours unless the agency that is responsible for it, whatever policing agency it is, receives a court order to seal the video for a set amount of time.\par \par \uc2 So you`re default -- you would like the default to be it gets released within 48 hours unless someone can prove to a court that there`s some negative effect to doing so.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Absolutely. And what we believe in this case, what happens if you after 48 hours allow that review process to happen and to therefore make it public, it gets it out to the public much faster. And what we`re seeing right now honestly, delays which I believe erodes public trust.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Senator, tell me what -- what you think comes of this because you`ve got a governor who`s in favor of having these videos released. You`ve got local officials in Elizabeth City calling on it to be released.\par \par \uc2 Do you know why the judge decided in this case not to do so when there`s so much public interest in this?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Well, I`ll tell you we`re still looking at this whole scenario. I`ve been in communication with other lawmakers, and we`re examining this whole process. And I believe we need a process that works for the people. We still have protesters out as we speak right now.\par \par \uc2 There`s loud cries for this to become public. And I`ve heard those cries and we remain committed to taking every step to make that happen for the people of North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Senator, good to talk to you. Thank you for joining us. North Carolina State Senator Don Davis is with the North Carolina general assembly.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the Biden administration announced a big step this week to keep kids fed in school next year. The economy`s improving a lot but there`s still so many people struggling to survive, literally struggling to eat and feed their kids. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: One of the defining images, at least from my perspective, in this crisis has been cars lined up -- cars lined up for miles. And not people just barely ever start those cars -- nice cars lined up for miles. Waiting for a box of food to be put in their trunk.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know about you but I didn`t ever think I`d see that in America. And all of this is through no fault of their own.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: As we said at the top of the show, Biden`s economic recovery is real, but so is the pain that people and families have been feeling after a year of the pandemic. 42 million people including 13 million children may experience food insecurity this year which the government describes as eating less food than you`d like or need or food of a lower quality and having regular eating patterns disrupted because you can`t pay for enough food on a regular basis. It means hunger or living in fear of hunger.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic has had devastating effects on food security and hunger. The U.S. Department of Agricultural announced this week that it would extend universal free lunch to more than 30 million low-income school children through the end of next school year.\par \par \uc2 And on his first full day in office, President Biden issued an executive order asking the \uc2 USDA\uc2  to expand other federal nutrition assistance programs.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now are Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jim McGovern\uc2  of Massachusetts. He`s the head of the House Hunger Caucus. And \uc2 Jean McMurray\uc2 , the CEO of Worcester County Food Bank in Massachusetts. Thanks to both of you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Representative McGovern, you actually would like to have a conference of hunger convened. We had one in 1969 and some remarkable things came out of it including the school lunch program, the food stamp program, the school breakfast program, SNAP for women, infants and children. And you say that it needs to happen now. This crisis is much more serious than people who don`t go hungry believe it to be.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM MCGOVERN\uc2  (D-MA): Well, first of all, I thank you for having me and thank you for all that you have down to call attention to the issue of hunger in America.\par \par \uc2 Look, I really do believe this is time for another White House conference on food nutrition and hunger. We have a hunger problem in this country. And, you know, one of the challenges that we face in solving it is that the jurisdiction to solve it falls under the multiple committees, multiple agencies.\par \par \uc2 So a White House conference could pull everybody together, anybody who`s relevant in solving this problem, bringing together in one room and let`s develop a road map with benchmarks to actually end hunger, not to manage it but to end it.\par \par \uc2 And I want included in that room those with lived experiences, those with lived experiences, those who are going through -- dealing with hunger.\par \par \uc2 So this is the moment. We have 45 million Americans who are hungry. We should be ashamed of that. We are the richest country in the history of the world, we can do better.\par \par \uc2 And so as the chairman of the Rules Committee we began a series of hearings that we`re going to work with all the other committees to bring them together to actually build a -- you know, the record that we can give to the White House and hopefully they will do this White House conference on food, nutrition and hunger.\par \par \uc2 I`ve talked to the Secretary of Agriculture, I`ve talked to the Secretary of Transportation, others in the White House. I think they`re supportive of the idea but I think now is the time to have that conference and let`s end hunger.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Jean, this whole idea of hearing from people in a conference like that who have a lived experience of hunger, you see people and talk to people every day who have that lived experience. And one of the things that has struck you in the last year is what happens when you encounter somebody who`s new to hunger or food insecurity.\par \par \uc2 The remarkable fear that goes through you, it`s paralyzing. It embarrasses you. You don`t know what the resources are that are available to you and sometimes your food bank is their first stop.\par \par \uc2 JEAN MCMURRAY\uc2 , CEO, WORCESTER COUNTY FOOD BANK: Absolutely. And thank you, Ali, for having me on the show. It`s a pleasure to be here.\par \par \uc2 In fact, in April of 2020, the first full month of the pandemic the Worcester County Food Bank and our network of food pantries saw a 205 percent increase in the number of people going to a food pantry for the very first time compared to April 2019.\par \par \uc2 These are people who until recently had worked in nonessential service jobs, in retail and in the hospitality industry. People like Marie when I answered the phone I could detect the panic that was in Marie`s voice as she explained to me that she had three children, she was laid off. She had never had trouble feeding her family before. And she just didn`t know who to turn to or where to go. And she was trying to pay her bills but it was a real scramble while she waited for unemployment to be approved.\par \par \uc2 So the first thing we did was Marie and I e took a deep breath together and I told her she had come to the right place. I gave her assistance on how to apply for the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program. I told her the school where she could go and get takeout meals for her children. And then I looked up the food pantry closest to where she lives and I discovered that the food pantry was closed.\par \par \uc2 So I invited Marie to come to the food bank because I didn`t want her to go without food for one moment or her family to go without food for one moment. The pantry would be open the next day but that was just too long for her to wait.\par \par \uc2 So when she came to the food bank and my co-workers and I brought out boxes of fresh food, frozen food and a range of nonperishable food items, Marie met us with a smile and some tears of relief.\par \par \uc2 But you know, Maria is just one of tens of thousands of people who had never needed to go to a food pantry before the pandemic gave them little choice. And when I met with her, you know, she reminded me of my neighbor.\par \par \uc2 And that`s when I thought, that`s who the food bank and our the food pantry partners help. We`re helping our neighbors whether they live next door, around the corner or across town. And they are kids who are in school with our kids and grand kids and they`re senior citizens who stand next to the market checkout line with us.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You know, some of Congressman McGovern`s colleagues in Congress have said that this expanded aid to people discourages work. I only have about 30 seconds left but have you -- what have you seen as a result of expanded aid to people who do not have an income right now?\par \par \uc2 MCGOVERN: Well, let me just say that those who are criticizing President Biden and thank God that we have a president who recognizes we have a hunger problem in this country. But the bottom line is if government is not there to help solve this problem then, you know, I don`t know what good government is.\par \par \uc2 And the bottom line is the -- one of the statistics I want to tell you is that, you know, people say to me all the time people should just go to work. Well, the majority of people who are able-bodied who can work who are on SNAP actually they do work. The majority of people --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: They want to work.\par \par \uc2 MCGOVERN: Yes, they work. They work, they earn so little, they`re still on SNAP. So the bottom line is, you know, we need this White House conference to look at this issue holistically to be able address the challenges that Jean just outlined and we can do this. We can end hunger in our country.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congressman, if you get that White House conference on hunger I will make sure that we cover it extensively on MSNBC. This is a crucial matter and it doesn`t need to exist.\par \par \uc2 MCGOVERN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congressman \uc2 Jim McGovern\uc2 , thank you. Jean, thanks for putting a real face on this for us so that we understand those who tonight are not sure where tomorrow`s food comes from. \uc2 Jean McMurray\uc2  is the CEO of the Worcester County Food Bank in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 You can catch me tomorrow on my show "VELSHI" at 8:00 a.m. We`re going to learn more about the raid on Trump lawyer Rudy Giuliani`s apartment. I`m going to talk to former Trump lawyer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  who`s lived this moment for himself.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday I`ll be joined by the former -- by the current White House economic adviser \uc2 Heather Boushey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH BRIAN WILLIAMS" begins now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020210501eh4u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for April 29, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Craig Melvin, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Joyce Vance,  Trymaine Lee, Jacob Soboroff \par \uc2 8,083 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 April 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST (voice over): If it`s Thursday, President Biden bets big on big government and Republicans are left with a big question of how to push back against popular proposal.\par \par \uc2 Plus, this our formal talks on police reform are beginning in Congress. We`ll talk to a bipartisan pair of lawmakers who hope to be a part of the small group tackling the issue as President Biden challenges Congress to pass a bill by the anniversary of George Floyd`s death.\par \par \uc2 And later, the investigation into Rudy Giuliani. It takes high level approval from main Justice to even execute a search of someone like Giuliani or even to ask for the search. So, what could that tell us about what the feds already know?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Welcome to Thursday. It is MEET THE PRESS DAILY and I`m Chuck Todd. Today is President Biden`s 100th day in office. I hope you bought the Hallmark cards to denote the holiday. It`s a day though after he proclaimed that the era of big government being over that era is now over.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My fellow Americans, trickledown, trickledown economics has never worked. It`s time to grow the economy from the bottom and the middle out.\par \par \uc2 There`s a broad consensus of economists left right and center and they agree what I`m proposing will help create millions of jobs and generate historic economic growth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, President Biden and the entire Democratic Party is making a big bet that Americans want a more active government and will keep wanting a more active government even as the pandemic fade, because you know what Americans have been telling us that they do want exactly that, a more active government.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at our latest NBC poll. 55 percent of Americans say the government should do more to solve the country`s problems. This is a question, by the way, we asked -- we`ve asked for decades. And it fluctuates back and forth, but right now it`s been consistently in that mid-50s. And that support has been across almost all demographics out there except for one, and it is Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But what is noteworthy about these numbers is that they`ve been unchanged for the last five years, meaning they`ve encompassed the Trump era. Like any good politician, President Biden is following what is popular. And right now, going big, getting government active, it is popular.\par \par \uc2 By the way, you just had a previous president who started talking up the idea of government getting more involved. So it leaves this current Republican Party left wondering, who are they and what do they do? After all, former President Trump encouraged this mindset. He brought out the era of big government and brought in voters to the Republican Party with it.\par \par \uc2 Don`t touch Medicare and Social Security. Remember all that? It makes these popular and populist proposals a bit more difficult for the Trump wing of the GOP these days to fight back against it. Are they really going to play the deficit card? But there are some in the Republican Party who are still grounded or who essentially were raised in the pre-Trump era where increased government spending was a political no-no.\par \par \uc2 In the official Republican response last night, South Carolina re Publicans Tim Scott hit at the president spending proposals as a liberal wish list. It felt like sort of the Romney days of the Republican Party or the Bush days. But at the same time, there are also some of the Republican Party who see what the public wants. And they want to work with the Biden administration on some of these items, like a smaller infrastructure package.\par \par \uc2 So, as we try to figure out how much of Biden`s big agenda can get through Congress, and that`s really the big question all over Washington, OK, how much of it can pass, we are left with the other big question of what is today`s Republican Party for.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now from the White House fresh off of his exclusive sit down with President Biden on day 100 is my colleague Craig Melvin. Also with us, NBC News Capitol Hill Correspondent Leigh And Caldwell.\par \par \uc2 Craig, let`s start with this. I assume you dealt with the entire size of government in this sort of era. Joe Biden spent his entire political career under what I would argue the political influence of two political parties who were starting to move in the direction of less government. And here he is now at the precipice of introducing a new era. How does he deal with that sort of what his life was like before President as a politician and the guy we`re seeing today?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, MSNBC HOST: You know, Chuck, you know, you hit the nail on the head there. And in terms of -- Senator Biden, as you know, is in support of the Balanced Budget Amendment back in the 90s. But not only did we hear something we have not heard from the sitting president in decades last night that the era of big government is back, this morning, through my conversation with the President, he really leaned into it.\par \par \uc2 To your point, he is betting that the American public want a bolder government. And there are two calculations here, Chuck, that I think are interesting. First, the President is banking on the fact and he sort of alluded to it during our conversation, that because of the pandemic and because of the government`s response to the pandemic, that`s one of the reasons that people may want more government in their lives instead of less.\par \par \uc2 And the second, you heard this last night in the speeches, well, he`s sort of framing this as perhaps an existential threat to democracy because of China. This idea that if we don`t spend trillions of dollars on infrastructure, as it was traditionally known, and infrastructure as the president is trying to redefine it, if we don`t spend that money, we`re not going to be able to compete with a growing threat from China. It was interesting to hear him sort of frame the debate through that lens.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, another thing that sort of jumped out I think a lot of folks is how he wanted to -- you know, it almost as if he would listen to one of former President Trump`s chief critiques at former President Obama is he used to say that he thought that President Obama didn`t cheerlead enough the economy, you know, spent too long explaining things.\par \par \uc2 What did Joe Biden do last night? Every proposal he put out there, Craig, he ended it with a riff on one word, and let me play an excerpt that emphasizes that one word. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: 100 days ago, America`s house was on fire. We had to act. And thanks to the extraordinary leadership of Speaker Pelosi, Majority Leader Schumer and the overwhelming support of the American people, Democrats, Independents, and Republicans, we did.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): This administration inherited a tide that had already turned. The Coronavirus is on the run.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: For too long, we`ve failed to use the most important word when it comes to meeting the climate crisis, jobs, jobs, jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Craig made a little bit of a -- we played a couple of extra bytes there. It`s the last bite was the quick point I was trying to make there. He said the word 43 times. He obviously thinks he knows a good way of selling something.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Well, and that`s what he`s going to do. I mean, he`s headed to Georgia today. He`s going to -- we`re told the President, the Vice President, the First Lady and the Second Gentlemen, would spend the better part of the next week crisscrossing this country, not selling a $6 trillion spending bill or spending bills, but instead selling what you just heard that this is a jobs-creation package.\par \par \uc2 I also found it interesting last night, Chuck, when the President talked about not just the number of jobs that would be -- would be created, but the kinds of jobs. The President went out of his way to talk about how the overwhelming majority of these jobs wouldn`t require a college degree. Most wouldn`t even -- many wouldn`t even require a high school degree.\par \par \uc2 It`s obvious who the President is trying to speak to hear with regards to job creation, and specifically the kinds of jobs that he contends these bills would create. But going back to what you alluded to as well a few moments ago, Chuck, the President did point out during my conversation this morning, and we`ve heard it from other Democratic leaders as well. It`s a little difficult to listen to some Republicans talk about soaring deficits and debts when the last four years have been so in deficits and debts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that gets to the larger issue that we`re pointing out today, which is how are the Republicans going to combat President Biden here? Because one of their main targets is something they don`t have a good record on, like the national debt.\par \par \uc2 Craig Melvin, I`m anxious to see the entire interview. We`ve gotten a sneak peek on some of the transcripts, important stuff on immigration, police reform, and I know we`re going to see a lot of it on the "TODAY SHOW," "NIGHTLY NEWS," you name it. Nice work, my friend.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me move over to Leigh Ann Caldwell on Capitol Hill. And Leigh Ann, I will tell you this. In a briefing yesterday, before the speech with Speaker Pelosi and Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer, the only question any of us had was how are you going to get this passed? It means they said it`s the only question they get from their members and they`re not ready to say how this is going to get passed. But it does seem to be the biggest question lurking on Capitol Hill today.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. President Biden laid out his speech. Now, it`s Congress`s job to try to get it passed, especially the Democrats` job. And that is the question, Chuck, that`s trying to ask that question in a million different ways to Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 It was really interesting to hear Craig say that President Biden is betting on big government because of COVID, because the American people have become used to government support. Well, Republicans think that is providing a skewed reality. Because of so much government support and COVID, it`s hard to find an American family who did not get something from the government and then multiple COVID relief bills.\par \par \uc2 They`re saying that there`s a skewed view of what government should do, and they think that is going to backfire because they keep coming back to nothing is free, and so that people are going to have to pay for this through their taxes. And so, they have created the opposition to what the President has laid out.\par \par \uc2 But the big question is, can they come together on a smaller transportation-focused infrastructure bill? I just talked to Senator Shelley Moore Capito of West Virginia who is leading that front. And she sounds optimistic, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you know, I do think you get the sense that a lot of Republicans realize infrastructure money is going out the door and they want to put their name on it. The question is, how much of that are they going to put their name on and how much of it are they going to tell Democrats, go do it yourself on reconciliation? Anyway, Leigh Ann Caldwell on Capitol Hill for us. Leigh Ann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And hopefully, that gives us a clear picture of how they`re going to pass this thing. Let`s talk to Democratic Senator Chris Coons of Delaware. So, Senator Coons, you`ve heard a bit of the conversation we`re having, and I guess, let me start with this. Do you accept the premise here that the era of the big government being over is now over?\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Well, Chuck, I think President Biden certainly gave an encouraging forceful, optimistic speech last night about how we`re going to meet this moment. And he`s already demonstrated -- the Democratic caucus already demonstrating with the American rescue plan, that we need to deliver bowls, big answers to how we`re going to recover from this pandemic, how we`re going to address vaccination, how we`re going to help the American people get back out of the recession we`ve been in.\par \par \uc2 And so, what the President laid out last night, Chuck, was a vision for a generational investment in infrastructure and job creation, tackling our other existential threat, climate change, and then finding ways to come together and invest in American families through things like more money for daycare, for apprenticeships, for community college, as he put it, the investments that will help us win the future.\par \par \uc2 I think we are in a moment when the American people are looking hard, and how we got into and how we`re going to get out of the pandemic and the recession. And I think we`re going to see a broad embrace of a bold government response to the challenges the American people and American families face right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, so square that answer you just gave me with what you said earlier today to a reporter saying there`s real value in passing a smaller infrastructure bill if it`s bipartisan. And I guess, do you view it as an either or proposition or do you view it as do as much stuff you can do in a bipartisan way, and then save whatever you can`t get done, then try reconciliation?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Absolutely the latter, Chuck. Just to be clear, I think there is value to bipartisanship both domestically in the United States. As President Biden said several times last night, we need to show the American people that we can hear each other, we can negotiate, we can deliver answers to the problems facing their families.\par \par \uc2 And there`s also international value to bipartisanship to showing our leading global competitors, China and Russia, who are running around the world saying that America after that horrific riot in the Capitol on January 6th, that our democracy is in decline.\par \par \uc2 But Chuck, there`s also reasons we may need to pursue a bipartisan infrastructure bill first is that there`s members of both caucuses negotiating in good faith, willing to put pen to paper. And over the next few weeks, not an interminable period, a period that I think should last through, you know, maybe a month or two more, we will work with senators like Senator Capito of West Virginia, to do our absolute best to come up with a bold infrastructure package that will make that generational investment.\par \par \uc2 I`ll remind you, Chuck. We all drive on an interstate highway system that was built in the 50s and 60s. We`ve got airport and ports and roads and bridges and tunnels that are failing by any analysis or grade and makes us less competitive globally.\par \par \uc2 I think we can and should find ways to pay for that, to build that, and to do that on a bipartisan basis, and then turn to the parts of the Biden plan and proposal that deal with things like climate change, resiliency, and investment in the caring economy that probably won`t get Republican votes, but are very well worth doing and that I strongly support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, now, let`s talk about how you pay for it, and frankly, whether you pay for all of it. Your colleague, Brian Schatz of Hawaii, a Democrat from Hawaii, he said this to Axios. I`m not a big pay-for guy. I think some investments are worth deficit financing.\par \par \uc2 Because this seems to be, I`m going to guess, the only way a bipartisan bill is going to come together because it doesn`t sound like either party is ever going to agree in a pay-for unless there is no pay-for.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, look, one of the challenges we`ve had -- I`ve only been here 10 years, Chuck. But we talk about infrastructure, we talking about infrastructure, and then there`s a basic divide in how to pay for it. I think the President has done a great job of putting forward proposals, suggestions for how we pay for all of his major plans, the rescue plan, the jobs plan, the family plan, he`s put forward ways to pay for them, and turned to us and said, you`re the Senate, do your job. As he said last night, roll up your sleeves get to work.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to pay for as much of it as we reasonably can. But we shouldn`t leave bold proposals on the table simply because we can`t come to an agreement on pay-fors. Infrastructure is a long-term benefit to the American people. This is a 40 or 50-year benefit if we invest in modernizing our electrical grid, our rail and transit systems, our broadband, and clean water and wastewater systems.\par \par \uc2 And Chuck, for those who question whether we get anything done on a bipartisan basis, we just passed yesterday, a clean air bill, a climate- related bill on a bipartisan basis. And we are going to pass later today, a clean water bill that Senator Carper and Senator Capito are helping lead on the floor.\par \par \uc2 So, I do think it`s possible, but we have to bear down on it and be willing to have some of it be unpaid for.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I was just going to say, it sounds like it also means if you`re going to keep 50 Democrats in the same place, they`re not all going to feel comfortable voting for every one of these tax increases.\par \par \uc2 COONS: That`s likely true. But you know, part of the job of being senators is communicating clearly within our caucus and across the aisle and publicly and at all. Here`s what I stand for it. Here`s what I`m willing to vote for. And there are some priorities the President has laid out that I think will really move our country forward, that are long overdue investments in making sure that folks can get back to work as we recover from this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 We`ve discovered I think, more clearly than ever how we`ve under-invested in things from public health to childcare. And I suspect we may not get everybody on board with the tax increases, but we will get everybody on board with these priorities in my caucus.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator Chris Coons, Democrat from Delaware, anybody that watches this interview goes through this transcript I think is going to have a good idea of where things are headed. I really appreciate the insight and perspective you gave us.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you. Up next, the ambitious goal set by President Biden on police reform. We`re going to talk to a bipartisan pair of lawmakers who are heading into a negotiation to try and get something done.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My fellow Americans, we have to come together to rebuild trust between law enforcement and the people they serve, to root out systemic racism in our criminal justice system. And on that, police reform in George Ford`s name that passed the House already. I know Republicans have their own ideas and are engaged in a very productive discussions with Democrats in the Senate. We need to work together to find a consensus. But let`s get it done next month by the first anniversary of George Floyd`s death.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Formal talks on police are former beginning actually in just a few minutes. Those negotiations will include eight lawmakers, four Democrats and four Republicans. And two of those lawmakers, one from each side of the aisle joins me now. They are the co-chairs of the Problem Solvers Caucus. It`s Congressman Josh Gottheimer, Democrat from New Jersey and Republican Congressman Brian Fitzpatrick, across state lines in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to both of you. And let me just start, Congressman Gottheimer, with the President`s decision to set a deadline. Helpful or can that -- sometimes deadlines can harm things. What say you?\par \par \uc2 REP. JOSH GOTTHEIMER (D-NJ): I mean, I always think deadlines are good to force section. But frankly, on this topic, it`s something that we`ve been working on for months now in a bipartisan way, and a bicameral way, Chuck, and having very productive conversations.\par \par \uc2 The talks we`re having today, in just a few minutes are a continuation of that dialogue. And I`m grateful to people like Brian Fitzpatrick, my good friend here and co-chair of the Problem Solvers Caucus, who was a former FBI agent, for the insight he brings to the table every day. And, you know, I`m optimistic that if we get to work together, we`ll get somewhere.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Congressman Fitzpatrick, in negotiations like this, it`s easy to see the compromise if everybody wants to get there. So, I want to start there. I take that everybody in the room wants to get there. Do you believe if everybody in the room wants to get there, that the politics, maybe the campaign operatives will stay out of it and let it happen?\par \par \uc2 REP. BRIAN FITZPATRICK (D-PA): Well, we have to. You know, that`s our job. And that`s what Josh and I believe in, that`s what our caucus believes in. You got to show courage, political courage, to do the right thing because we`re all going to be former members of Congress at some point. You want to be able to look back at your time here and say you have the courage to do what is right.\par \par \uc2 This is an area that I think, you know, we`ve had great conversations on. We kind of taken that to the next level now, still informal, but to the next level, smaller group, bicameral, to figure out where we agree and move forward on that. And where we disagree, we can put that to the side and pick that up in round two.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Congressman Fitzpatrick, let me follow up with you. As a former FBI agent, I`m curious of what you think of the qualified immunity compromise that Senator Scott has been -- that has is put out there, the idea of not the individual police officer, but the department essentially would not have immunity here. What do you make of that? And do you think that`s fair? Do you think you could go further?\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: Well, we`ll find out in short order here, when we -- when we talk to our bipartisan, bicameral group, what ideas they have. I mean, certainly, that was an issue that came up in the House discussions that Josh and I were part of, about how do we reform immunity in a manner that still protects our police officers and protects their families and protects the institution of policing.\par \par \uc2 We all know, there`s a lot of reforms that need to be made. Just like in all areas of life. This is an area that we really need to modernize. And, and that`s for the good of the police officers in our community simultaneously. So, we`ll see what the proposal is. You know, obviously, we always keep an open mind to try to get to a yes and get to the right policy.\par \par \uc2 But to Josh`s point earlier about deadlines, the deadline here ought to be when we have enough time to get this right. This is really, really important stuff. We want to make sure that we enhance community policing and protect the profession of law enforcement at the same time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Gottheimer, I want to switch gears here a little bit and ask you since you guys are the chair of the Problem Solvers Caucus, what role do you think you guys should have in these infrastructure proposals? What role do you hope the caucus has?\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: Well, we`ve actually, Chuck, been working now for months on the infrastructure side of the larger jobs package in a bipartisan way. A group of us met, actually, in Maryland with a bipartisan group of governors and senators at the end of last week. The problems aren`t put out on the infrastructure package.\par \par \uc2 And I think that the three big pieces we`ve identified here and all of our bipartisan discussions is one, deciding on the scope of what that looks like. Is there a piece that we can really narrow that down to roads, bridges, tunnels, water, infrastructure, energy broadband, we think, the physical infrastructure side so that we can get a bipartisan, bicameral group together on that we`ve been meeting with our counterparts of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Then secondly, what should that actually cost? What do we need behind that? And of course, third, how do you pay for it. That`s been -- we`ve been having those discussions. I`m really optimistic that we can get something done. It`s good that we heard the President say that he`s open to ideas from both sides. I think that`s critical. So, we`re going to continue to play. The Problem Solvers have a very important role here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Fitzpatrick, I`m curious on pay-for ideas. I don`t know how much you heard of Senator Coons, but you`re hearing a handful of Senate Democrats float the idea of, well, maybe you finance some of this with deficit spending. And frankly, the public doesn`t seem to be -- to care about the debt and the deficit the way frankly, it was perceived to be for decades.\par \par \uc2 FITZPATRICK: We all need to care about debts and deficit because it`s going to be our children or grandchildren that would deal with it. That said, the infrastructure, the key is making wise investments. Because if federal infrastructure dollars are spent wisely, they will yield two, three, four dollars in private investment in return.\par \par \uc2 And we talked about that a lot in the summit that Josh just referred to, bipartisan bicameral meeting at the statehouse in Annapolis. Senator Manchin and Governor Hogan left the discussion. Broadband? No. Netflix will benefit from that. Maybe they can be part of a P3. Airports, the airlines like Delta. Look at the airport in Salt Lake City. They finance a lot.\par \par \uc2 So, if we make the right, wise investments, you`re actually encouraging private investment. They need to be segregated out from the dollars that are being spent that (INAUDIBLE) 30 cents in private investment. So the key as Josh said is number one, defining infrastructure, coming up with a consensus definition.\par \par \uc2 Number two, identify the scope, both in aggregate in individual buckets. And then third are the paid-fors. So, as long as we`re dealing with real numbers, we`re not (AUDIO GAP) blue sky, we`ll find a way to get to the pay-for because (AUDIO GAP) the best (AUDIO GAP) and our national security.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Gottheimer, do you accept the definition of human infrastructure that many of your fellow Democrats are putting out there? I mean, obviously, some Republicans don`t. Do you accept it on the Democratic side? Do you think that`s a fair pitch?\par \par \uc2 GOTTHEIMER: Well, I certainly think, in the President`s positive ambitious speech that he gave last night, he brought up a lot of issues that are all about things that are critical to making sure we have a strong economy going forward, and that people have good jobs and that we`ve got a strong - - that our kids and grandkids are able to succeed the way we`ve been able to succeed.\par \par \uc2 And that includes things like child care and education. And so, I definitely believe in those areas. I think the key and getting a bipartisan deal done is we`re going to have to separate out pieces of this as some have referred to, right? We`re going to have to take that physical infrastructure piece and look at it.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, there`s other areas that we`re also going to have to tackle. I think what Brian and I are doing with some senators with the Problem Solvers now is looking at that first piece, the physical infrastructure and we`re obviously going to have, in my opinion, address all these areas over time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. All right, I`ve got to let you guys go because you guys are going to go into that gun reform meeting I think in a minute. So I don`t want to make you late.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Gottheimer and Fitzpatrick, the co-chairs of Problem Solvers Caucus, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Up next, what does it take to get the green light to search the homes of two former high-level Justice Department officials? The latest on the investigation into Rudy Giuliani as the \uc2 FBI\uc2  seizes his private phones and computers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We`re learning more about the execution of a search warrant on Rudy Giuliani`s home and office yesterday by federal agents as part of their ongoing investigation into his dealings inside the country of Ukraine. In a statement Giuliani`s lawyer revealed the warrant involved Giuliani`s alleged failure to register as a foreign agent. Sources tell NBC News the warrant was for electronic devices and that Giuliani turned over one cell phone, one iPad and one laptop.\par \par \uc2 Also, Victoria Toensing is another Trump associate and also a former Justice Department official married to a former U.S. attorney, also had her house searched in connection with this investigation.\par \par \uc2 And again, these weren`t ordinary search warrants. Chuck Rosenberg, our NBC legal analyst and a former DOJ official himself tells us these kinds of searches of public officials or former public officials, like Giuliani and toensing, would need high-level approval from main Justice before they even went to the judge to ask.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is NBC legal analyst Joyce Vance, a former U.S. attorney herself with the Northern District of Alabama.\par \par \uc2 So, Joyce, let me start with this, as a former U.S. attorney, would you expect some deference if you were potentially under investigation from the Justice Department, meaning that you would assume Justice isn`t going to move on you as a former official unless they`ve dotted every I and crossed every T?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, NBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: I think that`s absolutely correct, Chuck. Even in a, you know, not a politically involved case where you`re seeking to search an attorney`s office and there`s an attorney-client relationship, these search warrants are reviewed in main Justice.\par \par \uc2 In a case like this, every effort would be made, particularly with a new administration where there`s not a confirmed assistant attorney general for the criminal division, I wouldn`t at all be surprised if the deputy attorney general had reviewed this application and affidavit to justify the search warrant.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, if you`re in charge of this investigation and you know you`re about to ask for a search warrant from a -- of a former U.S. attorney and not any former U.S. attorney, the former head of your department itself, right, SDNY, how -- when you`re preparing your case for why you have cause for this search warrant, you know, how thorough are you going to have that? I mean, I guess what I`m trying to figure out is how much probable cause did they have?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So first let me say that as a U.S. attorney, I insisted that we maintain high standards for probable cause in all of these cases and I think that that`s typical in the department. But probable cause itself is a threshold of proof that means that it`s very likely that something is the case.\par \par \uc2 In this case that a search will produce evidence or fruits or instrumentalities of criminal conduct. You want to be really sure that it`s there, and it has to be fresh. You can`t say, well, we believe that there is evidence two months ago or a confidential informant was in the house and he observed Mr. Giuliani engaging in an online transaction two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Judges are very strict about this. And when you discuss with the federal judge who has to authorize this kind of a search warrant, the probable cause, they`re really very strict about that evidentiary threshold. That means that people inside of DOJ, from the line prosecutor and agents, all the way on up to the people that authorized this, will look at the probable cause from a couple of different directions to make sure it`s sound.\par \par \uc2 And add to that the requirement that you only obtain a search warrant in this situation when you`ve tried other less invasive methods of obtaining evidence and they have failed. That really brings home the notion that DOJ had a lot of evidence, a lot of probable cause here before they jumped with the search.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. And I want to jump ahead to this question, which is how likely is it that they already have the evidence that they -- that they think is on those electronic devices and they want to confirm that indeed, yes, our source of that information was right because now we`ve got proof, we have said evidence there? I mean, is it more likely they kind of know the evidence they already have and they`re trying to confirm sort of its -- that it physically was in his -- you know, that he communicated or is there a fishing expedition aspect to this?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So I suspect -- let me just say, we can`t know this for certain because we don`t know what was contained in the affidavit but it`s unlikely that it`s a fishing expedition. It`s possible that they`re looking to confirm what Giuliani has in his possession because an important element in any of the range of prosecutions they could be considering here will be his intent or his knowledge, what he hoped to do when he was involved, let`s just say, in transactions with Ukraine officials.\par \par \uc2 Also, it`s possible that there`s an element of obstruction here. They may want to look at these devices to see if there`s been any effort to delete information that could also run afoul of the law so lots of possibilities once they`re able to take a look at the electronic equipment that they seized.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And finally, I`m running a little short on time, we know with this clip we have Craig Melvin ask the former -- excuse me, the current president, Joe Biden, if he had any advance notice on this, he said he didn`t. He had no idea any of this was under way. So let me ask you this, the fact that Merrick Garland was willing to sign off on something that Bill Barr wasn`t, what does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: It`s hard to know for sure. It could be that there was not probable cause to do this search when it was put in front of Bill Barr. We don`t know that. It could be that they`ve got additional probable cause or it could be that there`s something here that bears further scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joyce Vance, a former U.S. attorney, really appreciate you trying to give us all a little bit of insight on how Justice works, not just any justice, the Justice Department in this case. Anyway, as always, thanks for your expertise and perspective.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, how black voters in one of the key counties we`ve been watching throughout 2020 view President Biden`s first 100 days, his efforts on police reform, and more.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As you heard just moments ago from Congressman Gottheimer and Fitzpatrick, Congress is getting down to some actual negotiating today on the issue of police reform after President Biden set a bit more of an ambitious goal of next month to get it done. President Biden`s first 100 days in office have been marked by multiple instances of deadly police encounters with black Americans, forcing him to meet the issues of systemic racism and police reform head on in his first 100 days.\par \par \uc2 And Americans had mixed reviews, frankly, about his response in our latest NBC News poll. 49 percent of Americans approve of President Biden`s handling of race relation while 43 percent disapprove. It`s a slimmer margin than his overall approval rating.\par \par \uc2 And as part of our county-to-county project, NBC`s Trymaine Lee is talking to black voters in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. As you know, Milwaukee was one of our five counties to watch in 2020. And Tremaine joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So, Trymaine, what are you hearing, and are you hearing the same sort of mixed messages, if you will, from some voters?\par \par \uc2 TRYMAINE LEE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chuck, I certainly would say that black voters here are giving Biden in his first 100 days kind of mixed at best. I was asking votes to grade his performance so far and, you know, pound for pound he`s, you know, got a B-minus.\par \par \uc2 They say he`s doing OK with lots of room for improvement. But certainly around issues of police reform and violence lobbed against the black community, other environmental issues, a range of issues, but let`s hear from them exactly how they`re feeling about Biden`s first 100 days. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAANAAN SABIR, MILWAUKEE VOTER: I voted for Joe Biden.\par \par \uc2 LEE: You feel good about the vote?\par \par \uc2 SABIR: That`s my -- that`s my answer.\par \par \uc2 MELODY MCCURTIS, MILWAUKEE VOTER: I haven`t seen anything that really creates sustainable change where we need to dismantle, you know, these racist systems, right, that really harm our people.\par \par \uc2 JOANNE SABIR, MILWAUKEE VOTER: I want a bill that`s passed that specifically speaks to racism and discrimination and white supremacy in policing. And I want that bill to trickle down to police policy and reform. We have a broken system. And so giving word or a voice that is heartfelt isn`t enough.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE: Chuck, it`s that last part, wanting some specificity around legislation or at least executive orders aimed squarely at the black community. Many people I spoke with said, you know, while we can`t get any anti-lynching legislation through Congress, you do have the anti-Asian COVID hate legislation, you do have Violence Against Women Act, you have all these different efforts aimed specifically at certain groups of people but black folks say, you know, the Chauvin trial did little to assuage our concerns about the safety of our community and the well-being of our sons, brothers, sisters and daughters. And so they want real action, not just talk. They want some real police action.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Well, I`m curious, what would you assess as the potential penalty for, frankly, the Biden administration and Democrats if they can`t get police reform done?\par \par \uc2 LEE: You know, it`s the same thing we heard even before, you know, the election. It`s like you need to come to these communities and listen because they are not just purely for your votes but there are voters here who cared deeply about these issues because they affect black people in a very specific kind of way.\par \par \uc2 And so if the administration doesn`t listen, doesn`t push hard to make sure that Joe Biden sticks to what he said he was going to do, have the backs of black voters and black people who have supported him all along, he`s going to risk a tough re-election. Folks simply won`t come out because you said you`re going to do everything you could to make sure that our community is safe and protected, and if he falls short of that, he`s going to leave a lot of votes on the table, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s pretty clear, words may have worked in the past. Just words aren`t going to work this time. There clearly needs some action behind it.\par \par \uc2 Trymaine Lee on the ground for us in Milwaukee. Trymaine, fine work as always, sir, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, a deep dive into the human toll of the political fight over immigration and it happens to be a preview of tonight`s new episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS," That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. If it`s Thursday right now, that means there is a new episode of our streaming show "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS" which premieres tonight on NBC NEWS NOW. This week we are taking a deep dive into the human toll of the political fight over immigration.\par \par \uc2 Last night President Biden urged Congress to pass comprehensive immigration reform, but he also signaled an openness to compromise on the issue, and that prospect of a smaller, more targeted legislation is actually one of the concerns for mixed status families, like the one in Los Angeles that my colleague Jacob Soboroff spoke to for tonight`s new episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Anna`s parents, like most of the 11 million undocumented U.S. residents, entered the country by evading detection. Now most people who arrive at the border attempt to enter differently. They actually want to turn themselves in so they can declare asylum. But often the forces driving them are the same -- economic ruin.\par \par \uc2 For years hunger and poverty caused by climate extremes has been intensifying, driving Central Americans to the U.S. In 2019, the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2  took us to a village in remote Guatemala where they were feeding school kids whose parents could no longer grow their cash crops nor buy supplementary nutrition.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): So every one of these kids who`s raising their hand right now knows somebody who has left to go to the United States because they couldn`t survive here. Who do you know that went to the United States?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Papa.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Papa. What`s he doing there now? Cleaning. He`s cleaning. So her dad is in the U.S. cleaning.\par \par \uc2 Do you want to go there or do you want to stay here? She wants to stay.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over): Now the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2  says conditions are worsening, complicating President Biden`s immigration agenda.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): And now you think the current controversy might distract from helping people who have lived here for years, if not decades, just like you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. As a matter of fact, I think yes.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And we`re going to pick up on that point right now. Jacob Soboroff joins me. He`s been reporting on the border crisis and the family separation issue for years, and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, Jacob, what was interesting there is this concern that Anna and her parents have about this -- all right, we have this new crisis at the border, but, you know, we`ve been dealing with the 11 million folks that are here undocumented, we used to say for a decade, I think we`re on our second decade.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Three decades when it comes to Anna`s father, Chuck, who everybody will meet tonight on "MTP REPORTS." Many documentaries, and I`m grateful for you guys for asking us to do it because the truth of the matter is we spent a lot of time focused on the situation at the border itself, and there is so much more to it. There is what`s happening in Central America, but it`s also the implications of what is happening in Central America and on the border to the politics in Washington, D.C. and who that ultimately affects.\par \par \uc2 And it affects directly the 11 million undocumented folks who have been here, in the case of Anna`s family, for three decades when it comes to her dad, two and a half decades when it comes to her mom, and they`re worried that while President Biden last night said he wants to push for that comprehensive immigration reform that would give all 11 million undocumented residence status that more targeted legislation, you know, for TPS holders, temporary protected status holders and DREAMERS and farm workers is going to come at the expense of people who have been fighting that fight for just as long and with just as much skin in the game.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, and you noted that there`s sort of -- that these mixed status families, they`re actually trapped in this country now for a while. They can`t leave because they`re not here legally, and if they leave, and then they get separated from their family -- I mean, it`s really been a nightmare for folks who, again, we could have the debate about why they came, but, boy, they`ve been here a long time, been paying taxes, their kids are citizens. I mean, this is sort of the real-life impact here.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I`m so glad you brought that up because family separation is something that we associate with President Trump and the Trump administration, and surely the policy that they carried out, zero tolerance, was cruel, it was deliberate, they meant to separate families at the border, but there is an entirely different type of family separation that plays out every day when it comes to immigration enforcement and that is the separation of mixed status families if they get ensnared in the immigration enforcement system.\par \par \uc2 So Anna is a U.S. citizen. Her three younger sisters are U.S. citizens. Their parents obviously are not, and they`re still at risk of separation today, especially if President Biden goes with that more targeted immigration reform proposal.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s why you need more than five minutes to do stories like this and tonight we have a lot more than five minutes.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Soboroff, fine work by you, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 You`ll be able to see much more of Jacob`s reporting, years in the making in some cases, on tonight`s episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." It streams on NBC NEWS NOW at 8:00 p.m. Eastern tonight. And of course will be available on demand in perpetuity on Peacock beginning tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage, though, continues with my friend Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210430eh4t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS for April 23, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Peter Alexander, Kavita Patel,  Deepa Shivaram, Ellison Barber \par \uc2 8,405 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST (voice-over): If it`s Friday, it`s decision day for the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine. Health officials were meeting to determine whether or not the nation`s only single dose vaccine can go back into circulation after the FDA paused its use over concerns about some rare instances of blood clots.\par \par \uc2 Plus, President Biden is getting ready to unveil a massive plan for roughly $1.5 trillion in spending aimed to help American families, as Capitol Hill sees a flurry of movement on potential deals around police reform: China, and infrastructure, and even immigration.\par \par \uc2 And the White House keeps its focus on the climate crisis, with the president and the Cabinet secretaries ramping up their pushes to slash greenhouse gas emissions. And one Biden official declaring that the issue is now at the center of American foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Welcome to Friday. It is MEET THE PRESS daily, and I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 For the first time in what seems like a very, very -- I`ll try another very. Long time in there, we are tracking multiple developments on Capitol Hill where Republicans and Democrats are talking about this weird thing, I think, we call it compromising.\par \par \uc2 It`s obviously, it`s very hard to pronounce that word since we don`t use it very often. On potential deals around police reform: Immigration, infrastructure, China, and some other issues. See, it`s pretty strange, right? Especially in the otherwise toxic political climate.\par \par \uc2 Look, we don`t know we don`t want to get ahead of ourselves. And we know I would call this cautious pessimism, if you will. And that`s still an upbeat view. But this is what we do know. In the wake of the guilty verdict in the Derek Chauvin trial, Democrats Karen Bass, Cory Booker are talking with Republican Senator Tim Scott on a serious way forward on police reform.\par \par \uc2 They`re not just talking, talking, but they`re starting even to exchange a proposal or two. And they are sounding increasingly optimistic about a prospect for a deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KAREN BASS (D-CA): I think we have to resolve a few things, continue our discussions. But hopefully, my fingers crossed. Hopefully, we`re very close.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I think we are on the verge of wrapping this up in the next week or two, depending on how quickly they respond to our suggestions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Whether a deal actually comes together, obviously, we shall see. The question is, does everybody want to get to yes? Because if they do, then, a deal`s in sight.\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Scott, does have the power we think to pull 10 or 12 Republicans on board with him if he can sign off on a compromise with Democrats. He has that kind of following inside the Senate on, at least, on this issue.\par \par \uc2 There are also multiple bipartisan bills in the works right now to address a rising threat of China. In a bipartisan working group on immigration, as the emergency at the border continues to grab headlines. Whether deals can actually come together, again? We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 And on Biden`s big tick on agenda item of infrastructure, Senate Republicans unveiled a framework yesterday for their counteroffer to President Biden $2.3 trillion plan. It`s obviously a fraction of what the White House wants. It`s a bit vague on the pay force.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say it`s just a starting point for negotiations. And the fact that they call them a starting point is something Democrats, in some ways, are taking them at their word, it appears, or at least, the Biden White House is now. But let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. SHELLEY MOORE CAPITO (R-WV): We see this as an offer that is on the table and deserves a response. And I think we will get a response, and we look forward to that, and we`re ready to get to work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And we`re already noticing a difference. The White House did not engage with Senate Republicans when their opening offer on COVID relief was a fraction of their plan. But on this, officials do seem to be signaling that, yes, this time on the issue of infrastructure, things might be different.\par \par \uc2 White House already today said they will invite the Republicans behind that framework for a meeting. Whether a deal actually comes together, let`s say it again together, we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 But folks, at his first press -- presidential press conference, President Biden says he sees successful electoral politics as the art of the possible. And over the next few weeks, we`re going to see what is possible. Starting in few days with the president`s first address to a joint session of Congress.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, I`m going to talk to the number two Democrat in the Senate, Dick Durbin. But I`m going to kick things off here with our own Leigh Ann Caldwell on Capitol Hill, and Peter Alexander, our chief White House correspondent at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann, I want to start with you since it`s -- like I said, we`re covering multiple fronts of potential deals being struck. There small deals on immigration that are -- they are making its way through. They`re talking on infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 But I want to focus on police reform because that seems to have the most momentum. Is that a fair observation?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): I think that`s a very fair observation. Negotiations or discussions are ongoing. I just caught up with Representative Karen Bass in the hallway. She just met with House Speaker Nancy Pelosi to give her an update of where things stand on her talks with Republican Senator Tim Scott and Cory Booker.\par \par \uc2 And she says that they are very close to at least moving into the next stage of negotiations where they are formalized, leadership is completely behind it. But things seem to be different this year than last year, Chuck, when the same exact negotiations were taking place.\par \par \uc2 What`s different this year is now, Democrats control all three -- you know, both branches or both chambers in Congress, and then, the presidency. We`re not extremely close to an election.\par \par \uc2 And also, Senator Tim Scott is indicating some concessions on this idea of qualified immunity. He gave a counterproposal, and that is something that didn`t happened last year. Last year, Republicans did not want any changes on this idea of qualified immunity.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: And now, they`re talking about it. So, not only the political ramification -- or the political circumstances are different, but the conversations and the discussions are actually different, too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On the other ones, obviously, I want to get to infrastructure. I think we`re -- it`s more on the Biden side of things to see the receptivity there. But the small little working groups on immigration, we don`t know -- we know a big deal isn`t possible there. But are there some little bipartisan deals that might actually pass?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: There could be. I mean, it`s very, very preliminary. But these discussions are happening. This bipartisan immigration working group had its second meeting in the Capitol this week behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 The day after that, Senator John Cornyn of Texas, and Kyrsten Sinema of Arizona, Democrat and a Republican. They introduce some border legislation that is bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 So, there is -- you know, Senator Durbin, who you`re going to speak to in a moment, can give you a much better read on this. But there seems to be a little bit more optimism coming from members of Congress here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: The fact that they`re talk despite what`s happening on the border. And I also want to bring up something not really related, but on a bipartisan level, they passed -- the Senate pass the Asian -- the COVID hate crime bill yesterday with just one Republican opposing: Josh Hawley, which that`s a whole other segment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: But, the fact that they changed the legislation, incorporated Republican changes and got nearly unanimous consent, like, that`s a big bill that is eventually going to reach the president`s desk.\par \par \uc2 And Senate Majority Leader Schumer was championing that saying, we can do things in a bipartisan way around here. And I`m going to continue to show you that we can. And so, I think that you`re seeing an exhaustion up here on Capitol Hill of the bitterness, of the partisanship.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sound like it, yes.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Especially at the last four years that people actually might want to get something done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Now, it`s an interesting you do, and you wonder, does this become a habit? Anyway, Leigh Ann Caldwell on the Hill for us. Leigh Ann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me move to Peter Alexander. And Peter, I want to -- I want to get on two topics with you.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But the first one is, the infrastructure deal that Republicans put on the table. They want a response, essentially, saying, hey, this is our opening bid. We`ll talk. And obviously, the White House says, yes, we want to talk. What does that mean? How should folks interpret that? Is that a -- yes, they want to have a meeting because they want to get caught trying? Or maybe there`s something here?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Chuck, you alluded to this in your introduction. I think you`re exactly right. We are seeing significant differences in the way that they handled COVID relief in the first months of the presidency, where he invited Republicans in and then still went it alone on that without any Republican support.\par \par \uc2 Then, we are on this issue of infrastructure. The president earlier this week, hosting bipartisan lawmakers here at the White House. A White House official reminding me just moments ago, we`ve had four such meetings on the topic of infrastructure already that he was "prepared to compromise."\par \par \uc2 That`s clear they are prepared to compromise. What that looks like is not as clear. Certainly, the corporate tax rate right now, the discussion for the White House being they want it to go down to 21 percent from 28 percent.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  does said something in the middle of the White House hasn`t pushed back on that. They said they`re open to these conversations not just with Manchin, obviously, but with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Jen Psaki in her briefing, the press secretary, moments ago saying that they would welcome some of these Republicans on the topic of infrastructure to come back to the White House to discuss it.\par \par \uc2 But this is, of course, part of this Build Back Better Plan. It is a lot of money, trillions of dollars having already been spent on COVID.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 Trillions more on this effort, not just as it relates to jobs and infrastructure, but to families as well that specifically deals with like education and childcare. On that, the pay for comes from the wealthiest of Americans.\par \par \uc2 And while the White House is expected to detail it more actually next week --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well let`s -- yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Chuck, Ron Klain, the chief of staff has already given us pretty good insight into this. The idea that the top tax rate would go back up closer to 40 percent for the wealthiest Americans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting on that, Peter. I`m curious to see that -- to get that through, they would need all Democratic support. And you have a handful of Democrats who have balked at the fact that, that they are -- the salt deduction was eliminated for certain income levels.\par \par \uc2 How do those same Democrats who are fighting essentially to get a tax break back from these wealthier suburban voters?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How are they supposed to sell this next round of tax hikes that the Biden White House is proposing?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, no Chuck, you`re right. Obviously, it`s in some of these states like California and in New York where the wealthiest folks in those -- in those states could be paying upwards of 50 percent in their combined state and federal taxes going forward with the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 The president proposing this capital gains rate would go up above 40 percent for those -- for those wealthiest Americans as well here.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s going to be one of the pressure points. But again, it appears that this is a place where they`re willing to move those numbers and even some of those close to the White House are indicating that when the numbers get that high, it actually, at some point, doesn`t provide the same benefit. So, I think there`s a lot of ground that they believe they can work in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter Alexander at the White House for us. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, joining me now -- I think everybody has previewed this interview in the last few minutes. He`s my -- is the number two Democrat in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , it`s Illinois Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I want to start with how -- with the green shoots of bipartisanship that we saw this week. But specifically, sort of how it came together on Mazie Hirono`s bill to deal with COVID hate crimes.\par \par \uc2 You tell me this, Senator Durbin, do you think the threat that Democrats could be united in eliminating the filibuster motivated Senate Republican leadership to work -- want to work on this bill rather than oppose it?\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN\uc2  (D-IL): Yes, to be put in a word, I think they`ve been coming to the floor regularly saying, don`t kill the filibuster, it`s an important part of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: And we have been saying back to them, well, then, just prove to us that with the filibuster, we can accomplish something and we can really do something. And I think that created an environment that was very positive for the hate crimes bill.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you have been the person that a lot of people have ascribed the optimism on various immigration. Smaller Immigration bills that, you know, that the comprehensive thing we know how that went eight years ago. I think you have the bruises among others to prove it.\par \par \uc2 What is possible do you think in this Congress when it comes to these smaller? I mean, there`s clearly some demands when it comes to improving the processing at the border. That`s one thing we know. Senators Sinema and Cornyn and have a bill on that.\par \par \uc2 We know we have a labor shortage in this country and there`s a lot of work visa things that people want to work out. What is the art of the possible on immigration this calendar year?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Chuck, let`s take a look at what this House has sent us. The DREAM Act to deal with the temporary protected status individuals, the Promise Act. Farm labor bill, that`s a -- it was a very strong bipartisan vote in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 There is a feeling among us that we have to come up with a reasonable approach to improving the situation on our southern border. Those are the four starting points.\par \par \uc2 But when we sit and even in a small group, someone will raise their hand and say what about a J visa in terms of doctors who will serve in critical areas that are brought in from overseas, can we bring that in too?\par \par \uc2 I`m open. I want to bring in all that we can reasonably afford to bring in politically. Still keeping the Democrats together and bringing in at least 10 Republican votes. Our meetings have been positive and they`re scheduled for more in the future. And I just feel good about where we are today. I think we can reach our goal.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me go to police reform, and I want to play a clip from Senator Scott on the issue of finding a middle ground if there -- if one exists on the issue of qualified immunity.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to Senator Scott.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: There is a way to put more of the onus on the burden on the department or on the employer than on the employee. I think that could be a logical step forward and one that, as I`ve spoken with Karen Bass over the last several weeks, it`s something that the Democrats are quite receptive to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator, one of, I think, my favorite phrases on Capitol Hill sometimes is, you know, if you can -- if you want to get to yes, you can get to yes. And I guess that`s the real question here. Does everybody want to get to yes on this? And is there -- is there a middle ground on qualified immunity in your view?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, first, let me tell you that our leader on the Democratic side is Cory Booker. He is chairman of the crime subcommittee. And he, and Senator Scott, and Karen Bass have been negotiating. And I am working with them, but they are the leaders in this process.\par \par \uc2 And let me say, add to this the obvious. What happened in Minnesota this week sets the stage for us to do something. To show that we can be productive, that we can deal with problems in law enforcement in a constructive way.\par \par \uc2 I really want to applaud Tim Scott. I think the words just heard from him were an indication that he`s trying to reach that goal as well.\par \par \uc2 I think we have a chance. There`ll be a follow-up meeting this coming week and I`m going to be sitting down with the principals that I mentioned earlier. I think we`ve got a chance to put something together.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, on infrastructure, there -- at this point, I feel like there`s a lot of -- a lot of ingredients on the table, and perhaps, you`re not quite sure how you`re going to make the meal. And you know, is it go it alone? Is there a bipartisan component? Is it all bipartisan?\par \par \uc2 Is -- it seems to me -- it seems like the most realistic thing is, maybe you can come up with smaller bipartisan deal and you have to do something larger on your own. Is that the most likely outcome when all is said and done with all of these various infrastructure proposals that are on the table?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Chuck, that`s the right question and I don`t think we know the answer at this moment. Shelley Moore Capito put a proposal together which is a bricks and mortar. It`s what you would expect in a classic infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 What Joe Biden is talking about is a bill that gets beyond just infrastructure and looks at jobs. How can we create an environment where we have good-paying jobs in America and make sure that Americans can fill those jobs? And there are elements that are part of it.\par \par \uc2 The traditional bricks and mortar might not include broadband, I believe it should. It`s an element that`s essential for education, for health care, and for business growth. Water projects, they`re going to be considered next week. So, we are bringing in more and more things to this.\par \par \uc2 But we also look at the human element here. Building a beautiful superhighway for a person`s subdivision into a place to work is just great. But can that mother find a safe and affordable place to leave her children during the day, so she can take that superhighway into work, and use that job to build a future for herself and her kids and her family?\par \par \uc2 So, we are looking at the job experience and saying, let`s make sure we make family infrastructure part of this conversation too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you very quickly on the -- there is been some pay force that senator -- that the Biden administration -- excuse me, President Biden and the Biden administration put out for part two of the plan that they`ll be unveiling next week in the speech to Congress?\par \par \uc2 And we`re seeing -- we`re hearing all sorts of various tax increase proposals, capital gains increase here putting up the top tax rate for folks above a certain income threshold with 400,000 as the -- as the line of demarcation of nobody would get a tax hike there.\par \par \uc2 I`m just curious. Is there a -- what -- where is your sensitivity line on, on the tax hikes here? Should there be cleaner messaging on this? I mean, I would understand if the -- if the idea was all income should be taxed the same, but it does look like the way it`s rolled out that there`s a lot of various tax increases.\par \par \uc2 And I`m thinking about suburban Chicago residents. Are you worried you could lose them as Democratic Party supporters due to some of these tax hike proposals?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I think President Biden`s break line of 400,000 is going to keep most of those who live in the suburbs happy with the outcome.\par \par \uc2 I will say this. I was in a meeting two years ago -- it was almost two years ago to the day when President Trump walked into the meeting and made it clear he was all for an infrastructure bill, but he wasn`t for paying for it in any way. They turned around and walked out, end of conversation. So, for four years, we had no infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 Senator Capito has put forward a proposal, and I thank him for doing that. I think it`s constructive. But there`s no pay for included in it. We have to be very real about this. If we`re going to have a program that is successful, whether it`s broad or narrow, it has to be paid for in some respect. And I think that`s what we`re waiting for.\par \par \uc2 I`m looking for the most progressive way that we can pay for this measure as important as it is to make certain that we create good new jobs, but not at the expense of working families. We want to help them get through the troublesome times we`ve had in this country, and we want to give them a chance to have the kind of income that gives their family a future.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2 , I have to say I try to remember the last time I feel like we had an interview where we had so many different moving parts on Capitol Hill to talk about all very substantive -- hey, welcome to the new Congress. Maybe, maybe, maybe we`ll have this -- maybe this becomes a habit.\par \par \uc2 Senator Durbin, thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: That makes me what -- makes me want to go to work, chuck. Thanks. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There you go. All right, makes me want to cover you guys. Very good.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we are waiting word on the announcement of a decision on the future of the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine in this country. The CDC panel is meeting right now following the latest developments.\par \par \uc2 And later, the ripple effect of the pandemic and its economic fallout is still leaving many Americans without food on the table. We have new reporting on the effort to fight food insecurity in this country. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We are watching and waiting as the CDC advisory panel is meeting right now to decide whether to resume the emergency use of the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  one-shot COVID vaccine. A decision is expected sometime today and could come this hour.\par \par \uc2 The use of the vaccine in the U.S. was paused nearly two weeks ago after a small number of people suffered dangerous blood clots. And just moments ago, the CDC panel did reveal that there are now three more deaths to add to that total. And a total of at least 15 possible cases of these blood clots, more than double the number of cases that have been previously reported. Seven of those patients are still hospitalized, four of them in intensive care.\par \par \uc2 The European medicine advisory group has already said that the benefits of this shot outweigh the risk, but they did add a warning. All of this comes as the pace of vaccinations, by the way, is decreasing and health officials across the country are trying to help overcome vaccine hesitancy.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s bring in \uc2 Dr. Kavita Patel\uc2 , practicing primary care physician, former White House director of health policy in the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 You know, Dr. Patel, do you think it`s better or worse for vaccine hesitancy to resume the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine?\par \par \uc2 DR. KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): I actually think it`s better, but I think it`s because right now people are saying, there`s a pause, but when we try to explain that it`s pretty rare, and they say, but there`s still a pause, so, aren`t they hiding something?\par \par \uc2 And I think that resuming this, but being transparent, as you mentioned the deaths and the cases. And the fact that there will be more cases unless we can try to kind of eliminate risk factors for this. That will help hesitancy.\par \par \uc2 I think that overall though kind of we`re hearing public comment right now right before I got unchecked, you`re hearing a lot of the public say you should never have paused this in the first place, even with the deaths and cases. You should have just been working with clinicians to give them warnings, but never to pause.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: So, I do think resuming will help to kind of decrease hesitancy. But some, as I`ve told you before, I think some of the damage has been done already.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, as we deal with this vaccine plateauing that we`re at here, to me, it sounds like that the Biden team is very nervous about this because I don`t think, you know, that the fact that essentially the government announced a financial incentive to try to do this. Is that where we`re headed next? Essentially, we`re kind of saying, hey, you can -- you can give people a paid day off to go get the vaccine. That`s one step away from saying, we`ll pay you to get the vaccine. Just get the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, Chuck, actually that idea has been floated to actually pay people to get vaccinated. It was floated well before we even had vaccines because people were worried that if we had a two-dose shot that we would have a huge drop-off with that second shot. We haven`t seen that, but I think we are getting to the point.\par \par \uc2 This is the first week, Chuck where we`ve got open spots pretty much everywhere that I`ve been kind of working with vaccine distribution and you can walk into many places. But we still have about 30 to 40 percent of the population that we`re trying to get to.\par \par \uc2 From a federal policy level, we do need to shift our allocation strategy. We`re still doing it on a per capita basis. Chuck, we now need to do things like getting more vaccines into areas that actually do not have the uptake, and then pair that with strategies that involve taking down some of these mass vaccination efforts that that`s the route we`re going.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: And doing literally hand to arm combat in the sense that, you know, getting people shots than arms. And that might also involve some incentives that are either cash-based or, to your point, paid time off along with tax credits for small businesses as the Biden administration announced.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When do you -- when did we get to the point where you as a public health official think, you know what, we have to start giving some vaccines overseas because we have to improve the hemisphere. We have to improve these vaccination rates globally, and we`re sitting on too many. Are we almost at that point in your mind?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, we are. We`re here now. But I`d say the tension -- we do need to keep just because of what we`ve seen even before Johnson & Johnson`s pause. Chuck, these issues with their manufacturing facilities they contracted with Emergent Solutions in Baltimore.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: We need to kind of prepare for the -- you know, the worst. That`s why we have a Strategic National Stockpile for everything else, including vaccines. I do think we can walk and chew gum at the same time as a contrary. So, we can prepare to have backups in case our supply drops while we also help other countries.\par \par \uc2 But let me be clear, what`s happening in India? They have -- they are the largest manufacturer for the entire world of everything related to drugs and devices. What we need to do is probably help give them raw materials and raw goods, so they can go ahead and make manufacture their own vaccines as well as other countries that also do need to have some of the assistance. Not just in manufacturing but vaccines themselves.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s why you`re seeing continuing pressure to lift patent restrictions, but it`s never going to be one solution, Chuck, with policy, it`s going to take all of the above.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: We`ll need to give them materials, get global manufacturing on board and some help on the ground with oxygen and basic supplies.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Hey, the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine what -- if we do restart \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 , do you expect that approval then to speed up?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: I don`t, but here`s why. I think that \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  has had issues very different from the clot issues that started before they even submitted data to other countries. Chuck, they have had different issues with the methodology of their trials, there has been a lack of transparency, recall, where they had had a press release about numbers and the -- and the NIH and the data safety monitoring board in a never-before-seen action said no. That`s not consistent with what we know.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it could happen but nowhere near on the time frame, we`ve seen with the other three manufacturers. Where they put in an emergency application, and it`s almost down to the clock several weeks and we see an emergency authorization. That is not going to happen as quickly with \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Very interesting and I`m glad I added that last question in there. That was new to me. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Kavita Patel\uc2 , as always, appreciate having you on and your expertise.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And today at 3:00 p.m. on MSNBC, the man himself, Dr. Anthony Fauci will speak with my colleague Ayman Mohyeldin about the J&J vaccine, COVID variants, and more. By then, we may know the answer on the J&J decision.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me show you something that caught my eye this morning. Despite political division, a pandemic, the ongoing fight for racial equality. New poll out of Harvard shows that young people are more optimistic about their future now than they were four years ago, particularly young people of color.\par \par \uc2 Some astonishing numbers in this poll. 56 percent of Americans under 30 say they are hopeful about the future of the country. That`s compared to 31 percent in the fall of 2017, eight months into Trump`s presidential term. The hopefulness of young black Americans took the largest leap, jumping to 72 percent from a bleak 18 percent four years ago. A 54-point increase in optimism there among young African-Americans.\par \par \uc2 And back in 2017, just under 30 percent of young Hispanics were hopeful about the future of the country. That percentage, it`s now nearly 70 percent. A major change and one worth taking note of.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, a special "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS," we look at the dire warning about our nation`s water systems and the final upgrades needed to keep clean drinking water available for everyone in this country.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Every week on our new streaming show, "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS," where we do a deep dive on a single topic with new in-depth reporting. This week we`re focusing on water security and it`s not just climate change that`s impacting Americans` access to water.\par \par \uc2 Reality is our aging water infrastructure systems are making clean, safe drinking water unreliable in many parts of this country. Too many parts. And the problem is particularly worse in the low-income areas, making this an issue of environmental justice as well.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Deepa Shivaram joined this week`s "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS" with her reporting on one place where the taps went completely dry for weeks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DEEPA SHIVARAM, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In at least 545 municipalities across the country, there are cast iron pipes that are now more than 100 years old. The aging infrastructure has led to a point where a water main breaks somewhere in this country every two minutes.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WILLIAMS JR, PUBLIC WORKS DIRECTOR, JACKSON, MISSISSIPPI: When you look at our communities that are similar like Jackson, yes, we`re all struggling. We`re all trying to find available funding to put toward this infrastructure that continues to age every year. You know, it doesn`t get better. You know, it gets worse.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CHOKWE ANTAR LUMUMBA (D), JACKSON, MISSISSIPPI: We need state and federal resources. Our budgets are not sufficient enough to cover replacing an entire water system.\par \par \uc2 SHIVARAM: Investment in water infrastructure has increasingly fallen to state and local governments over the past few decades as costs to fix the systems continue to rise. But for a city like Jackson where nearly 30 percent of residents live in poverty, people can barely afford to pay their own water bills let alone increase taxes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Deepa Shivaram joins me now. You know, Deepa, you`ve been covering this issue in Jackson. But as you say the reality in the country is that the next Jackson is right around the corner. And I guess the question I have, particularly with the Jackson situation, is I felt that there was a lot of finger pointing. It should be the state and the city, then some say well, maybe it should be the feds. But at the end of the day, it`s like, well, instead of talking about it, why haven`t we fixed it? And it feels like we`re just standing around.\par \par \uc2 SHIVARAM: Right, Chuck. And that`s what a lot of residents we spoke to in Jackson are feeling as well, that this is an issue that`s just been passed over, passed over, passed over by local officials, by the state government and by the federal government as well. They really do need a lot of funding to fix this because in reality, you know, if you keep putting band-aids on a very large problem, it`s only going to get worse over time.\par \par \uc2 And you heard Dr. Williams there, the public works director, say exactly that. And the reality of the situation, Chuck, is that it`s not just a Jackson problem. Our water infrastructure all over the country is literally hitting a breaking point everywhere.\par \par \uc2 The last time there was a major rehaul of water infrastructure, creating these new pipes and systems was about 1945. And most of these pipes, you know, they can`t last more than 75 years, more than 100 years. And so what you`re seeing is that it`s not just happening in Jackson but basically at the same time all of these systems are starting to fail.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is also, you know, we talked about drinking water mostly in this piece, but sewage water as well. Our waste water systems are equally crumbling. And so as we see this all over the country, you know, there`s a town in Illinois, it`s called Centerville, Illinois, and we should be paying a lot more attention to it. Their water systems are more than 100 years old there as well. And people are living with sewage water and sewage in their streets, in their backyards. So this is physically crumbling right before our eyes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, how much is this -- I mean, you look at the situation in Jackson. You have statewide leadership is all Republican. The city of Jackson`s leadership and voting electorate is all Democratic. You can`t help but ask yourself, how much does that play a role?\par \par \uc2 SHIVARAM: I heard that from residents, too, Chuck. They very much know that they -- you know, the population that`s in Jackson, it`s predominantly black, it`s predominantly poor. They are not exactly voting Republican down there. And they`re very aware that despite the fact that they are sitting in the shadow of the state capital building, they are not the state`s priority. They are not their governor`s priority.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s very -- they are very aware. They`re very aware of that and they are basically just crying for help at this point. They have local leaders who they`re pushing and those local leaders are asking state leaders and those state leaders are asking federal leaders. And just like you said, it`s an issue that kind of just keeps getting pushed on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHIVARAM: And at the same time it`s residents who are literally paying the costs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, in an odd way, this is why there has been such -- why the idea of more infrastructure spending has support on all sides of the aisle. Just do it. I think there`s a sneaker company that says that. Sometimes you just want to say, hey, guys, we got water system, fix it. Just do it.\par \par \uc2 Deepa, terrific reporting. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And you can see more of Deepa`s reporting on the latest episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." It`s now on demand on Peacock. It also includes an exclusive interview with Erin Brockovich.\par \par \uc2 Before we take a break, execute the pun, but we`ve got some out of this world pictures to show you. Color me a bit obsessed this morning with all things space. \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  made history again this morning launching its third crew into space in less than a year. Four astronauts hailing from three countries, the U.S., France and Japan are all on board. The first crew in space history to take off in a recycled rocket and capsule.\par \par \uc2 They launched from Florida`s Kennedy Space Center and are expected to reach the International Space Station early tomorrow morning where they`ll spend six months in orbit. But this is not the only exciting news from space this week. NASA`s Perseverance rover marked another first on Tuesday when it successfully generated oxygen. Matt Damon can breathe now.\par \par \uc2 That`s right, folks. We converted the air in Mars into about 10 minutes` worth of breathable oxygen. So we can see Arnold Schwarzenegger movies. No, I`m sorry. The landmark achievement was quickly followed up yesterday with stunning video capturing the Ingenuity helicopter`s second successful flight on the red planet only four days after the helicopter made its first historic flight.\par \par \uc2 So we`re shooting out recycled rocket into space, we`re making Mars an oxygen and we`re flying out in a world beyond the earth. And one week we`ve taken three pretty big leaps for mankind. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Tomorrow marks the 106th anniversary of the systemic killing and deportation of hundreds of thousands of Armenians at the hands of the Ottoman Empire in what is now modern-day Turkey. It`s an atrocity that has never been formally recognized by the United States as a genocide. But officials indicate that President Biden may take that historic step tomorrow, becoming the first American president to categorize it as a genocide.\par \par \uc2 It`s a big symbolic gesture in fulfillment of a promise Biden made to Armenian Americans on the campaign trail. But it`s also a decision that may have an immediate negative impact on our already strained relationship with a NATO member, Turkey.\par \par \uc2 "Washington Post" columnist David Ignatius, an Armenian American himself, explored the impact of this long-awaited acknowledgement in his latest column and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So, David, let`s tackle this from both sides. As an Armenian, what does this mean to you?\par \par \uc2 DAVID IGNATIUS, THE WASHINGTON POST COLUMNIST: Well, this is the day every year, April 24, tomorrow, on which Armenians around the world remember the genocide, the terrible slaughter and suffering that occurred in 1915. So for President Biden to fulfill his campaign promise to affirm the historical reality of that I think will be important for Armenians. But I hope it will also be a day when Armenians and Turks, too, can look forward and not just backward, can begin a process of reconciliation, acceptance.\par \par \uc2 These two peoples, two countries need to find a way to live together. They live side by side but with closed borders. And so I have a hope, maybe naive, that this could mark the beginning of a process of transition towards something more modern, more reasonable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, the symbolic pronouncements by the United States, you know, sometimes when they happen, you look -- you say, well, why is that important? So I guess explain why it is important and explain why it took us so long and how much of this has been just sort of the need to appease Turkey for why we delayed this designation.\par \par \uc2 IGNATIUS: So it`s important, I think, for people everywhere to have the facts of their history, the moments of suffering acknowledged and affirmed. It`s just something that`s universal and human. This took so long principally because Turkey just didn`t want to accept it and exerted enormous pressure on the United States not to affirm it. President Ronald Reagan made a reference to the Armenian genocide in larger proclamations about the holocaust in 1981. So it`s not as if the word has never been said before.\par \par \uc2 Congress last year voted by a very wide majority to affirm the fact of this genocide. Turkey has been deeply unhappy about it, has used every lobbying effort it can to lean against Congress and then lean against the administration. Turkish president Erdogan indicated yesterday that he`s going to continue with that campaign.\par \par \uc2 As somebody would like to see a modern Turkey that`s a real partner for the U.S., we need a strong Turkey in that part of the world. I just hope that Erdogan will be wiser and not cling to his version of the past and get on with it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting, on Erdogan, did the world misjudge him, or did he change? You know, President Obama and I think others thought Erdogan was going to be the guy that brought Turkey into the Western world, I guess is one way to describe it. And obviously, there`s such a -- you know, they are literally, you know, at the border of West and East. But it has gone south with Erdogan. Is Turkey still a democracy in your mind?\par \par \uc2 IGNATIUS: It`s a much more authoritarian state than it was. It represses journalists, it represses human rights in general. It is still a democracy. It has elections. The elections are taken seriously. Turkey was a place that I used to love to visit as a journalist. I covered Turkey like I cover every part of the world, because it was a place where good things were happening. A couple of decades ago it was modernizing quickly. It had good economic management.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan was leading what looked like a moral and democratic country. And then it began to turn the other way. Erdogan became president. He assumed greater, more authoritarian powers. He seemed to join this parade of authoritarians, Vladimir Putin in Russia, Xi Jinping in China. And it was unfortunate. It had back effects on the Turkish economy, which has been in some real difficulty in the last several years.\par \par \uc2 But I think your basic analysis, Chuck, is right. He was going to be the Muslim modernizer. I think Barack Obama saw him as the key to change in that part of the world. And unfortunately, it didn`t end up working that way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: David Ignatius, on this -- it`s an historic, it`s symbolic but it`s still an historic moment. We shall see it formally tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 David Ignatius, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 IGNATIUS: Thanks for asking me on, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it. Up next, we`re going to take you to an unexpected place where they`re setting up a large-scale food bank because the need has become so great.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Food insecurity has been a major issue during this pandemic and for many Americans, despite a recovering economy, the financial struggles that have been caused by the pandemic are still not over. 42 million people, 42 million people, folks, are projected to face food insecurity this year. It`s only a slight decrease from the record highs of 2020. That`s according to a recent analysis by \uc2 Feeding America\uc2 . Before the pandemic hit, food insecurity in this country was at a more than 20-year low.\par \par \uc2 Ellison Barber is in Atlanta for us where the Atlantic Community Food Bank is holding a massive food drive. And they say they`re still seeing a major influx of families needing that food.\par \par \uc2 Ellison, tell us where you are, too, for this.\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chuck, yes, so we are in Henry County, and part of why this mass food distribution site event is taking place is because the Atlanta Community Food Bank said they started to realize that their demand went up, other smaller food banks, their resources were taxed and they were having trouble getting enough food to people who needed it.\par \par \uc2 So they decided to put these big sites that can serve up to 2,000 households in a couple different pockets. And this is in Henry County, not too far outside of Atlanta. But a little further from the metro area because they thought they could reach as many people possible right here. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELE CHIVORE, ATLANTA COMMUNITY FOOD BANK: I think the misconception has always been there, right, that prior to the pandemic is that, you know, it`s specific types of people who need assistance. But a lot of times what we found, especially now with the pandemic is that it`s anybody.\par \par \uc2 It`s somebody who may have had a full-time job and have had hours cut. And so they`re now working half of what they normally would have done. And so suddenly they`ve had an impact to their budget and their ability to serve and kind of provide for their family.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: So you can tell a little with the empty pallets moving some leftover boxes here. They`re wrapping up now. But this morning before this site even opened up, cars were lined up for hours waiting to get inside. Again, they had enough food here to feed 2,000 households. And when we talked to the people who work with Atlanta Community Food Bank, they said that they have seen the demand, the need for food increase just exponentially throughout the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 They say their food distributions are up by 50 percent since the pandemic and that`s not just one month, not just one peak, they say they have consistently seen that need every single month since March of 2020. And in the 29 counties that they focus on working with, food insecurity has increased by over 30 percent among children, by about 48 percent. That is one in five children in these counties that this food bank helps. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ellison Barber, at this Atlanta area food distribution event. Ellison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And be sure, look, when you want to help with food banks, some of them want food -- want you to bring actual food. But if they do, be sure to look at the list that they`re asking for. Don`t just bring any food. (INAUDIBLE). And many of these food banks do prefer financial donations over the actual foods. So just check with your local food bank on that. But please support them. All right. It`s something near and dear to my heart as well.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us this hour. We`ll be back with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY on Monday. And if it`s Sunday, it`s "MEET THE PRESS," don`t miss it this weekend. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210423eh4n0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for April 20, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Shaquille Brewster, Ali Velshi, Ali Vitali, Josh  Lederman \par \uc2 8,271 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 April 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to Tuesday. And we could do the Fritz Mondale`s remembrances all day and it wouldn`t be enough. Anyway, it is MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd. America is now bracing for a verdict. Cities across the country are waiting and watching Minneapolis as jury deliberations are underway in the murder trial of former Officer Derek Chauvin.\par \par \uc2 In Minneapolis, a heavily fortified Hennepin County Courthouse is the backdrop for a city with more than 3,000 National Guard soldiers have flooded the streets. Minnesota`s governor is asking neighboring states for additional assistance. In Philadelphia, businesses are boarding up. For many stores, it is the fourth time this year they had to do something like this.\par \par \uc2 In Chicago, Illinois` governor is activating the state`s National Guard at the request of Chicago Mayor Lori Lightfoot. Over the weekend, thousands demonstrated in response to the shooting -- release of the video of the shooting of a 13-year-old boy Adam Toledo.\par \par \uc2 In New York \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers have been instructed not to take days off until further notice. In Washington, the D.C. National Guard activated approximately 250 troops. Metro P.D. is at full activation posture with leave and vacation canceled for officers this week.\par \par \uc2 And at the White House, President Biden just told reporters that he spoke to the Floyd family and here`s some of what he said about that call and the case.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I can only imagine the pressure and anxiety they`re feeling and so I waited until the jury was sequestered and I called. They`re a good family. And they call for peace and tranquility no matter what the verdict is. I pray the verdict is the right verdict. It`s overwhelming in my view. I wouldn`t say that unless the jury was sequestered. Now, you would hear me say that. So, we just talked and I wanted to know how they were doing just personally. We talked about personal things.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: For nation on edge, local and national officials were preparing for demonstrations for a verdict in the Chauvin trial. The hope, of course, is that all of these protests are peaceful.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEDARIA ARRADONDO, CHIEF, MINNEAPOLIS POLICE DEPARTMENT: Much of the great partnership that I have with Sheriff Hutchinson, it is not my job nor my obligation keep his jails filled. Operation Safety Net is not about arresting people. We want peaceful assembly. We want peaceful protests. We know we have a city that is mourning, that they`re -- that their in grief. The last thing we want to do is turn this into an enforcement situation.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Equally as important is that rage that will be on the street regardless of what happens is channeled into a positive way. And that positive way means change. We have to have that change. We can`t live like this. We cannot continue to live like this.\par \par \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO, MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY, NEW YORK: I won`t prejudge anything that`s going to happen this week. I will only say if folks have feelings they want to express, do it peacefully.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Two of my NBC News colleagues join me from Minneapolis. Shaquille Brewster has been covering the Chauvin trial for us and Ali Velshi has been who`s been following the city`s preparations for the protests after the death of Daunte Wright in nearby Brooklyn Center. Also joining us is Keith Mayes, professor of African-American studies at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Shaq, we`ve been -- I guess this is the first full day of jury deliberations. They did deliberate for a few hours yesterday. Sometimes you find out they ask for certain pieces of evidence or things like that to look at. Anything that we`re hearing from the deliberation room?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: We haven`t heard a peep, Chuck. We-- first thing we heard was this morning at about 8:00 a.m. we got word jury deliberations started for the day. The court had said they will tell us when things start, they will tell us when things end, and they will tell us if there`s a verdict.\par \par \uc2 We know as you mentioned if there`s questions or problems or notes that come from the jury, there are pool reporters ready -- near the courtroom ready to go in if that comes to be. And that has not happened just yet. So, we`re moving at a pace that is determined by the jury. They have a lot of control at this point.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, as that`s happening on the inside as the jury deliberations are happening behind closed doors, you mention that collective sense of waiting not only here in Minneapolis but across the country, but specifically here in Minnesota. 3,000 National Guard members are activated and ready and deployed.\par \par \uc2 You go around, you move around Minneapolis, you see those armed vehicles. We also talked about the boarded-up windows, the boarded-up buildings all across the downtown area in many places that experienced damage last time. And this is affecting school children, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 The Minneapolis public school system announced there will be virtual learning for the rest of the week for many of the students here. No after- school programs, childcare canceled. So, this is having a widespread impact. I will tell you, many people are holding their breath but for different reasons. For some people it`s the outrage and response and reaction to the verdict, but for other people e, it`s the concern of the verdict.\par \par \uc2 Some people are fearful, are skeptical that they will get what they consider the right verdict to be, similar to what you heard from President Biden. Many people just want a conviction. They want a conviction on the murder charges and they`re holding their breath fearing that that`s not what they will receive.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Shaq, the decision by the defense to try to elevate the Maxine Waters comments, which had sort of has taken off in political circles, but then showed up in the courtroom yesterday and it certainly seems to have given the defense no matter what happens, they`re going to end up quoting the judge if they don`t like the verdict.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: That`s exactly right. And you know, we`ve seen outside events influence what happened in the courtroom before. Remember that record $27 million settlement between the city of Minneapolis and Floyd family. The two jurors who were seated were actually dismissed after that. And you heard the judge experience that similar frustration.\par \par \uc2 Let`s play a little bit of that sound of what we heard Judge Cahill say yesterday in the courtroom after Eric Nelson mentioned a congresswoman -- he didn`t even name her at the time. He said a congresswoman came and in his words threatened violence. People defending Maxine Waters say she was just calling for people to become more engaged and continue protesting. Listen to what the judge said in reaction to Eric Nelson`s request for a mistrial.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER CAHILL, JUDGE, HENNEPIN COUNTY COURTHOUSE FOURTH JUDICIAL DISTRICT: Well, I will give you that Congresswoman Maxine Waters may have given you something on appeal that may result in this whole trial being overturned. But what`s the state`s position? This goes back to what I have been saying from the beginning. I wish the elected officials would stop talking about this case, especially in a manner that is disrespectful to the rule of law and judicial branch and our function.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: And just to be clear about what Maxine Waters said. She was in Brooklyn Center, she was on a bull horn. And that`s when she said, I couldn`t sleep. I have been staying up all night because Mr. Wright didn`t deserve to die. And then, afterwards, she was asked about the Chauvin trial. And what she would tell protesters specifically if there were not a guilty verdict, she said "We`ve got to stay on the street, we`ve got get more active, we`ve got to get more confrontational, and we`ve got to make sure they know we mean business."\par \par \uc2 Of course, Speaker Pelosi is defending those comments saying she`s someone who came from the Civil Rights Movement. It was very clear what she was meaning to say. But you have House Republican leaders and Senate Republican leaders saying that she was calling for violence. And that is the tone you also heard Eric Nelson bring up in the courtroom to the judge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right, right. You know, there`s a lot of selective quoting here on this issue right now. Shaquille Brewster getting us started. Shaq, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me go over to Ali Velshi. And Ali, you were -- you were on the ground during the protests right after the death of George Floyd. What are you seeing now? Do you see them preparing in ways that show that they may have learned something from what happened last summer?\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Yes, both police and protesters are doing that. So, I`ll give you a little example, a little taste of it here. There`s National Guard posted on the downtown corner at Fifth Street and Nicollette Mall. And so, this is -- this is usually a busy downtown area. It`s been less busy during coronavirus. But you can see the boarding up of places.\par \par \uc2 So, here`s what we`re seeing. There are no police and EMT people on leave right now. Everybody has been called in. When you see police going down the street, you usually see them in tw0 cars with a National Guard officer. And where you see protesters on the street, you`re not seeing police interaction with them.\par \par \uc2 Where there`s National Guard and police, at the courthouse for instance or in Brooklyn Center which is about 10 miles north of here where Daunte Wright was shot, there are two lines of fencing in front of the police station. So, there`s no interaction between police or National Guard and civilians who are protesting.\par \par \uc2 Let me give you an example right here of what we`re looking at. This is a boarded-up business but you see the door is still available and they can open it. There`s a lock here. A lot of businesses are like this. If you go inside, they will lock the door behind you but trying to stay open.\par \par \uc2 Now, schools here are open today, Thursday -- Wednesday, Thursday and Friday, they said it will be remote learning. No kids are going to go to school. Same thing in Brooklyn Center. So, there`s a lot of -- there`s tension. I will tell you, last night, there was a fairly good size demonstration downtown but it was marshaled. They had people on the sidewalks here.\par \par \uc2 So, the protesters were coming down the street. And if they got on the sidewalk, meaning the approaching property, the marshals who are part of the group of protesters but with vests on, would push them back into the street. If they got too far ahead or too far behind the protest, they`d ushered them.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a high sense of organization even about the protests going right now. And that`s where we stand as we await this verdict.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali Velshi, I really appreciate it, on the ground there for us. Ali, thanks very much. Let me bring in Keith Mayes. Keith, first, I want to -- I`m curious your reaction to President Biden`s comments. Look, this is -- it`s interesting he tried to emphasize, hey, I didn`t say anything until the jury was sequestered. There are -- there are some that think he shouldn`t have said anything until after the verdict itself is in.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure, like the judge said, a lot of politicians, you might wish that they would wait. But what did you make the President`s decision to speak out?\par \par \uc2 KEITH MAYES, PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF MINNESOTA: I mean, I thought -- I thought it was spot on, Chuck. You know, politicians are elected by the people, and they are in many ways responsible to the people. So, they have -- they have every right to speak out. Maxine Waters, what she said wasn`t problematic to me. I think President Biden was trying to be thoughtful and responsible and showing leadership skills to suggest that he`s, you know, the president of everyone, on all sides here.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t -- I don`t -- I didn`t think there was anything wrong with that, Chuck. But what I`m afraid of is that the lead could be buried based on the decision by the jury. So, if there`s an acquittal or hung jury and social unrest begins to take place, I think that will capture the headlines. And we will be talking about law and order and we will be talking about security and not -- and not the injustice that took place.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m concerned. We are holding our collective breaths because based on the decision of the jury, we will begin to tell a different story. Either we will tell the story this was the beginning of a new day when it comes to law enforcement and when it comes -- when it comes to racial politics and racial issues as we move into hopefully a better day, or we will be telling a different story, how people are looting, how people are protesting in the streets.\par \par \uc2 And so, again, the fate of the future is in the jury`s hands, Chuck, right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s funny, I have a friend of mine who said to me, as optimistic as they might be at how this situation has played out, they believe accountability could happen. You know, for the first time, they were -- this is a friend of mine said I was impressed with the police, that the blue wall broke, if you will, with the chief testifying.\par \par \uc2 But this friend of mine just assumes the appeals process is going to go the other way and that`s the ultimate protection that happens. And it`s sort of -- it was -- it was -- in some ways, I understand that sort of dread that my friend has on just because history has proven those predictions true. Where is your head on this?\par \par \uc2 MAYES: Listen, Chuck, I mean, your friend was spot on. Even if we get a conviction, we will still have to live with this, Chuck, for the next three, six, nine, maybe one year afterwards when all of the appeals begin to take place. This is going to get bounced up if he`s convicted through the Court of Appeals and then if he is convicted again or he is found -- if he`s found out guilty, it may be over but, certainly, this could easily end up in the Minnesota Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think for us who are sort of holding our breaths, we may be holding this collective breath for another six months to a year. And so, where does -- where does that leave us, Chuck? It leaves us in the same place, that we don`t receive justice, that any kind of reforms that are now on the table that could take place may be put on hold because the cases have not been resolved.\par \par \uc2 So, I think your friend is absolutely right. Appeals process can work both ways. They can work on the benefit of the defendant or the prosecution. But we will see what happens. But this is not over just because the jury will render its verdict later on this week or even today for that matter.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I didn`t -- it didn`t go -- I notice that the governor right after Daunte Wright was killed, the governor seemed to express some frustration that police reform had been sort of sitting around the Minnesota legislature.\par \par \uc2 And you just sort of made a point that I wanted to ask you in a question but you sort of -- you may have already answered it, which is it does seem as if there`s always -- we got to tackle police reform, but let`s wait. We got to tackle police reform but we got to wait for this investigation or this trial to end. Or we`ve got to wait the -- you know, guess what happens? You never have it picked up. What is going on here, and why wasn`t a police reform -- larger police reform bill able to pass the Minnesota legislature?\par \par \uc2 MAYES: I think because of the electoral politics. I think because of the politics of right and the left and what people see as an appropriate response to the abuses of law enforcement. And what are reasonable calls from the community to overhaul and change.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we get caught up in the strong verbs, abolish, defund. But behind those strong verbs, Chuck, are some really, really serious calls to really transform law enforcement in the state. And I think people pay lip service to it. They really don`t want to do it. There was -- I think it was $12 million that was given after of Castill case. We don`t know how that money is being spent in terms of police training.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that -- and I call this the serve and volley of not only protest politics but electoral politics as well. We just somehow will find a way not to change the status quo. So, Maxine Waters` remarks in Brooklyn Center are apropos. We have to stay out on the streets protesting, advocating until we receive the change that we want to see.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Keith Mayes, professor from the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 , somebody that`s been -- we counted on to give us some perspective from the ground there. I always appreciate your perspective with us. So thank you, Keith.\par \par \uc2 MAYES: Thank you, Chuck. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Listen, we have breaking news involving yet another shooting in America with the whole country on edge. It`s understandable why we`re all so nervous. A manhunt is underway in New York right now as authorities moments ago told reporters they`re searching for a 31-year-old man that they believed ended up opening fire at a Stop and Shop in Long Island, a grocery store, killing an employee and injuring two others.\par \par \uc2 Authorities released his photo moments ago. They know his name. They said he fled the scene and that he`s still armed with a handgun. This is a manhunt. They believe he worked for the Stop and Shop at some point and, in fact, may still be employed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATRICK RYDER, POLICE COMMISSIONER, NASSAU COUNTY: We do have a person of interest. A person of interest, his name is Gabriel Dewitt Wilson. It`s the best picture I can give you now. It`s on my phone. Gabriel Dewitt Wilson, date of birth, 2/17/1990. He`s got Long Beach and Hempstead addresses.\par \par \uc2 He was or may still be an employee of the Stop and Shop. We`re not sure but we`re trying to confirm that now. We have over 150 assets out here right now looking for Mr. Dewitt to question him about the events that occurred here today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: It obviously remains a very fluid situation. Police are asking area residents to remain indoors as they search for the subject -- for the suspect, excuse me. We will be keeping a close eye on the story and we`ll bring you any developments as needed.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Europe`s vaccine regulatory agency makes a recommendation on the J and J vaccine as the company suffers another setback in the United States.\par \par \uc2 And later, inside President Biden`s Oval Office meeting on infrastructure with a member of Congress who was in the room where it happened. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Here are the coronavirus facts you need to know. E.U. regulators say they found a possible link between the \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  vaccine and rare blood clots. And that a warning should be added to the label, but this is where it`s important. Experts there say the vaccine`s benefits outweigh the risks.\par \par \uc2 The shot is still paused here in the United States while the CDC and FDA investigate. A decision here in this country is expected Friday.\par \par \uc2 With COVID-19 surging across the globe, the State Department is announcing its do-not-travel warning will now extend to basically 80 percent of the world, citing an unprecedented risk to travelers. That`s a big, big, do not fly list.\par \par \uc2 And India is now the global epicenter for the virus, accounting for one- third of the world`s daily cases. Hospitals there are facing shortages of ICU beds and oxygen, and vaccines are also in short supply.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, here at home, the CDC said half of all adults in the United States received at least one dose of the coronavirus vaccine. The Biden administration can end up hitting its goal of 200 million shots in the first 100 days as early as tomorrow. So, basically about a week ahead of schedule.\par \par \uc2 That said, in another setback for vaccinations in this country, manufacturing is now paused at a facility in Baltimore that makes ingredients for the already-paused \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  vaccine. The FDA requested the pause while it investigates the \uc2 Emergent BioSolutions\uc2  facility that botched millions of \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Ali Vitali is in Baltimore outside of that plant. And Ali, this is a plant that has been a problem that goes back to last fall. This seems to be a consistent issue with this specific plant. Is it -- at some point, is it worth even still trying to get it up and running?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC REPORTER: I think our friends at \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  would probably argue yes, because remember, Chuck, they`re on the hook for 100 million doses to the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . In the official aftermath, when we heard about these 15 million botched doses, J and J was pretty clear they felt confident they were going to still be able to provide 100 million doses by the end of May.\par \par \uc2 I did notice today though as their CFO was on CNBC and as they did their earnings call, they still say they`re confident they`re going to get to 100 million doses. But gone is the specific rhetoric about the end of May. You will remember too when the Biden administration first announced this deal for 100 million doses of \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2 , it was in addition to millions of shots that were also going to be coming from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna, basically trying to get all of these different vaccines marching in lockstep as more and more Americans were able to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 But now here we stand where every American is eligible and \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  is playing the waiting game on two fronts. You`re right this facility is on a pause while the larger usage of this vaccine is also paused, waiting for the CDC and FDA recommendation on how they can proceed going forward.\par \par \uc2 But for this specific plant at least, listen to how the CFO talked about what this could -- what this could mean in terms of the overall timing and production coming out of this facility.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH WOLK\uc2 , CFO, JOHNSON AND JOHNSON: We are in a very good position assuming the regulatory process goes as we expect. We will certainly do our part to make sure that we have a favorable outcome there. But should that go well, we will be in position to meet our contractual commitments.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VITALI: So, Chuck, that`s the waiting game that they`re saying they`re confident about here on the ground in Baltimore writ large. They`re probably hear recommendations as early as Friday, according to what Dr. Fauci has said.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there a -- there was a time -- is this still a plant that \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  runs or is this part of the partnership the U.S. put \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  together with I believe it was Merck?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Right, exactly. So, back when we first heard about the botched doses, that was at the end of March, a few days later the Biden administration said that this was now going to be a facility that \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  was taking over and they were going to be more in control of the quality control issues here.\par \par \uc2 You`re right that this is a plant that had quality control issues, some training issues, dating back into 2020. Now though, now that we`re at this point in it, that`s why J&J is so involved. It`s something the Biden administration intervened in really quickly. And the FDA stepped in right after that. They did their inspection early last week and they said on Friday that all of the doses need to be quarantined that are already made here and production needs to stop for now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali Vitali on the ground for us in Baltimore. Ali, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Pentagon now warns that Russia has deployed more troops to the Ukraine border than it did in 2014 when the country annexed Crimea, setting off alarm bells across Eastern Europe and beyond.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We`ve got military and diplomatic developments today as tensions escalate with Russia. The Pentagon says the number of Russian troops deployed near Ukraine`s quarter steadily increased over the past two weeks. It`s now larger than the force that was there when Russia annexed Crimea in 2014.\par \par \uc2 Defense officials tell us that the buildup includes tanks, helicopters, fighter aircraft and naval ships in the Black Sea. And according to the "Wall Street Journal`s" satellite images show Russia expanding its military buildup along its border with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The photos were taken between March 27th and last Friday by Maxar Technologies, which provides images to the United States and other Western countries. They appear to show that Russia has moved warplanes to bases near Ukraine. And in the ongoing diplomatic conflict between the U.S. and Russia, the U.S. ambassador is coming home temporarily, four days after the Kremlin suggested that Washington recall him.\par \par \uc2 Joined now by Helene Cooper. She covers the Pentagon for "The New York Times."\par \par \uc2 Helene, and I guess it sort of -- last week, it seemed as if -- and I didn`t know if it was a part of the Biden-Putin phone call, but the Russians seem to send word to say we`re just doing a military exercise. We`ll recede soon. It`s over in a couple of weeks. Don`t send any ships to the Black Sea. And the U.S. apparently took them at their word. Do I have this timeline correct?\par \par \uc2 HELENE COOPER, THE NEW YORK TIMES PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s about correct. The U.S. so far has been taking Russia at its word, but at the same time Pentagon officials talk about these what they call triggers. And they have actually a list of 10 things that sort of signal -- you know, that there could be another invasion of Ukraine coming. And on that list is you move a whole bunch of assets to the region, which Russia has done.\par \par \uc2 You move Russian warships to the region which Russia has done. You move a whole lot of soldiers and tanks and assault teams to the region, which Russia has done. What Russia hasn`t done, though, so far is Moscow has not put any support stuff, you know, all of the logistical stuff that any army needs to survive, the hospitals, the logistics, the stuff that in order to invade a country, you`re going to kind of need in order to sustain a big invasion.\par \par \uc2 Moscow hasn`t done that yet, which is part of the reason why Pentagon and Biden officials so far say that, you know, we don`t believe -- we`re going to take them at their word, that a large invasion isn`t coming but at the same time they`re looking out for it and they do think that chances are higher now that you could see a smaller type of Russian incursion into Ukraine and that is because of a little-known water crisis that is going on right now.\par \par \uc2 When Russia first annexed Crimea back in 2014, they lost -- Ukraine cut off the water flow from the Dnieper River into Crimea. So Crimea for the past seven years since that annexation has had a huge water crisis. Ukraine cut off 90 percent of the volume of water that they were pumping into Ukraine which -- into Crimea, which is normally a pretty arid region. Moscow has been spending billions of rubles so far to try to make up and get to the point because right now the water supply in Crimea is still -- is rationed.\par \par \uc2 And so that is something that the Pentagon is very worried that they`re keeping a -- you know, looking at the area for where you have this Dnieper River coming over the border into Crimea and Russia maybe trying something there in order to seize control of that water source again. So that`s an area of big concern as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll tell you, more proof that water is the next -- is really replacing oil as a commodity that people might be willing to fight wars over but that also sounds like an odd plotline to "Yellowstone," if, for those that watch "Yellowstone," it will be familiar.\par \par \uc2 So, Helene, there`s another angle here with the Russians and that was, what happened with Georgia, which is they`re waiting for an excuse to act. How much of a concern is that?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: That`s always a concern with Moscow. I mean, you`re seeing all this brinksmanship going on between Putin and Biden to begin with. It looks like we`re looking at a relationship where they`re going to pretty much hate each other. Biden, President Biden called Putin, Vladimir Putin a killer a month ago. That didn`t go over very well. You see the rhetoric really escalate.\par \par \uc2 And you have right now a situation where you have a President Biden who looks at Vladimir Putin as somebody who did try to influence the American elections. The sanctions that went into place last week against Russia, that`s going to be a big deal for the relationship between Moscow and Washington. And we`re edging now -- I don`t want to say a new Cold War because I think that`s hyperbole, but things are getting chillier and chillier and chillier.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: And that one -- at some point it feels as though something is going to have to happen. Either the two of them are going to have to get together and sort of like dial things back a little or we`re just going to continue this brinksmanship that we seem to be in the middle of.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Helene, is there any evidence -- I know we decided not to send some military ships into the Black Sea. What about NATO? How have they been responding to this?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: NATO is really worried. Ukraine -- as you know, Ukraine wants to get into NATO. That`s like -- and Russia isn`t having it. You know, right? NATO will say officially that, you know, we`re moving towards Ukraine becoming a member of NATO. They`re not a member right now so you don`t have the collective self-defense issue that you have with other places. And that`s one of the reasons why Russia will do everything it can to stop Ukraine from getting into NATO.\par \par \uc2 I think if Russia does go ahead with something, some sort of smaller invasion or a larger-type incursion over the border, you`re going to have to ask the question of what will the Pentagon in particular do, what will the United States do?\par \par \uc2 So far the United States has given the Ukraine government and the Ukraine military defensive type of equipment. We`ve been very careful not to give them anything offensive because we don`t want to rile Russia anymore. So you`re going to see the question being asked then of, you know, how much further can we go?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Helene Cooper, Pentagon correspondent for "The New York Times," really always appreciate your expertise and perspective here. Helene. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to take a moment to remember a longtime force in American politics and, of course, the Democratic Party. He`s a man known both for his decency and for reinventing the role of the vice presidency. Walter Mondale died last night at his home in Minneapolis. Known to many as Fritz, Mondale was a senator from Minnesota before becoming Jimmy Carter`s running mate in 1976.\par \par \uc2 Mondale was the first vice president to have a West Wing office and weekly scheduled lunches with the president. Then Vice President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  credited Mondale as the role model for how he did the job working for Barack Obama.\par \par \uc2 Mondale talked about transforming the vice presidency on the MEET THE PRESS 30th anniversary special in 1977.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALTER MONDALE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT: I think the role that the president has permitted me to play is unprecedented in American history. I am privy to all of the classified and secret information. I serve on the National Security Council and all of its subcommittees. I serve on all the crucial policy committees, and I have virtually unlimited access to the president. I think that is unprecedented. I think the president has been very kind and gracious to me. I find it a great challenge. And something that I`m most grateful for.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now the Carter-Mondale ticket lost in 1980 and then Mondale became the Democratic presidential nominee against then President Ronald Reagan in 1984. He selected New York Congresswoman Geraldine Ferraro as his running mate, the first woman at the time to be tapped for that role. They lost in a landslide carrying only his native Minnesota and Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 Mondale would later serve as President Clinton`s ambassador to Japan and, of course, was a last-minute candidate for a Minnesota Senate seat in 2002 following the death of Paul Wellstone.\par \par \uc2 And a final note to his former staffers, Mondale wrote this on Monday, just hours before he died. "Before I go, I wanted to let you know how much you mean to me. Never has a public servant had ever a better group of people working at their side." And he was on a fishing pier with his dog.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Mondale was 93.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`re going to talk about our infrastructure package and as I indicated earlier, I am prepared to compromise, prepared to see what we can do and what we can come together on.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was President Biden yesterday again echoing his appetite for compromise on his administration`s infrastructure proposal. The comments were in a bipartisan Oval Office meeting with members of Congress who had previously served either as governors or mayors. Well, some members of both parties are talking about compromise in infrastructure, the differences between the GOP and the Dems on this package right now are pretty vast.\par \par \uc2 On the scope, President Biden`s opening offer on infrastructure is on the $2 trillion range. Texas Republican Senator John Cornyn over the weekend suggested $800 billion for a more narrowly tailored package. That`s $1.2 trillion less than Biden -- than the Biden plan if you`re counting at home. And that may even be too big to get a wide backing among Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to paying for it, President Biden and Democrats back raising the corporate tax rates among other moves, but Republicans including West Virginia Senator Shelley Moore Capito, who`s taken the lead in drafting the Senate Republican counter-offer have deemed that a nonstarter, and they`ve been talking about some sort of user fees.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the lawmakers who was in the Oval Office meeting yesterday with Florida Democratic congressman and a former governor of Florida, Charlie Crist.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Crist, good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHARLIE CRIST (D-FL): Good to see you, Chuck. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let me -- so look, you give us the best -- give us the best observation you can give us inside that room. What is the divide -- how big is the divide between the two sides in your view, and how much of it seems -- how much of it is too big? And is the divide more on scope of the package or on how to pay for it?\par \par \uc2 CRIST: You know, that`s a great question, Chuck. I think that a couple observations if I might. There was a great spirit of bipartisanship in that meeting yesterday. Republicans, Democrats, independents, Senator Angus King, and you know, so I thought that that was very important for the tone.\par \par \uc2 Now you talk about what`s the difference, you know, is it going to be a matter of the numbers in terms of the scope of the bill, $800 billion as you mentioned from Senator Cornyn, and then all the way to $2.1 trillion from President Biden. I think it`s going to be at the higher end. I think that the president is cognizant of the fact that we`re in a deep problem here. A lot of people are still out of work.\par \par \uc2 It`s shifting more to a jobs plan, frankly, instead of a -- you know, infrastructure plan as he mentioned yesterday. You know, and we talked about the Everglades. You`re a Floridian, we talked about the everglades in Florida, how important that would be in terms of infrastructure because eight million of my fellow Floridians get their fresh water from the Everglades.\par \par \uc2 We also touched on solar energy, how important that is not only to the Sunshine State that I`m from but also the country to have renewable energy. You see what a beautiful day it is here today in Washington, to have that kind of solar energy, renewable wind, as you just mentioned water sort of replacing oil as an important material to the future of the world, as was described in the earlier report.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I think there`s a lot of climate change issues that are going to be part of this infrastructure plan, this jobs plan, and I`m encouraged by that. And when I was talking about the Everglades, Carlos Jimenez, the former mayor of Miami-Dade County, chimed in, he said I agree with Charlie, you know, about the Everglades in Florida. This is something that ought to be included in the infrastructure package, a jobs package. But then we differed a little bit on the -- on how to pay for it issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CRIST: Talking about the corporate tax and some other items.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You and I can go off on a tangent. Good luck trying to use solar power if you want to do it in the state of Florida right now. There`s a regulatory bottleneck and if you`re an individual trying to do it, basically it`s being discouraged. But anyway I won`t get off on a tangent on that since --\par \par \uc2 CRIST: You can indeed argue that. And that started utilities in Tallahassee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know, it`s -- yes, it seems to be designed to prevent you from actually having solar panels if you want them. But let me ask you about the group of people that were there. No disrespect, but governors and mayors are the most reasonable -- and I say this with no disrespect.\par \par \uc2 That was a little tongue in cheek. This is the most reasonable group of people you`re going to find in Congress. Why? You all have to deal with either city councils or legislatures. You all had to deal with basically corralling the person farthest away from you but that you still needed their vote. So you understand this ability.\par \par \uc2 I hate to put it this way, isn`t that the wrong group of people? I mean, your group is not big enough to be bipartisan, is it?\par \par \uc2 CRIST: Well, I think it`s good to hear from those voices, though. And your point is well taken. One experience that I have with President Obama, when I was governor, we had the great recession. Not great at all, as a matter of fact. But President Obama, I was then a Republican and he obviously a Democrat, came to me, his office then invited me to go to Ft. Myers to be with him to literally embrace the stimulus package at the time.\par \par \uc2 I did so, reaching across the aisle because I thought it was important, and you`re right. Your more pragmatic as a mayor, the more pragmatic as a governor. You`re the chief executive. You have to find the path forward in order to get the right things done for the people at large, for all the people. You know, as I used to say when I was elected as a Republican, as governor before, I wasn`t elected the governor of the Republicans of Florida, I was elected for the governor of the people of Florida.\par \par \uc2 And that`s how a mayor thinks, that`s how a governor thinks, that`s now President Biden is thinking. And, you know, he`s doing an extraordinary job and he kept a great tone during our meeting yesterday, listening to everyone, looking everyone in the eye, getting their feedback and the stories that they had to share about when they were a mayor at one place or another and had an issue they had to deal.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CRIST: And find compromise to get it done. That was the bottom line. Everybody is willing to do some of that. And that`s important.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I guess what is the plan -- you`ve had some firsthand experience. How do you have that conversation with an elected official who says, you know what, you`re making a good point but my voters aren`t going to believe it so I can`t --\par \par \uc2 CRIST: Well, that`s typical. I mean, you know, but I believe in people, and you know, I believe that most people are pretty rational. There`s extremes on both ends. We both know that. That`s just something that we`re living with. But by and large, most people believe in the same kind of things. They want a good education for their kids, a clean environment for our society, safe streets for our people and an opportunity to excel economically if you had those God-given abilities.\par \par \uc2 So we`re really not that far apart. I think that we can come up with good compromise. One example is talking about the percentage of corporate tax. You know, it`s -- you know, maybe going up to 28 percent according to the president`s proposal. Maybe not want to get that high from some of the Republicans in the Senate. Time will tell, but I think we can get to a good place. I do. We have to. We really do, for the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Republicans are turning their fire on your colleague Maxine Waters for some comments she said about the Derek Chauvin trial. Where do you stand on this? Do you have any issue with what she said, and do you think the Republicans are upset in good faith?\par \par \uc2 CRIST: Well, it didn`t sound to me like she was advocating violence. You know, she said we need to be present, we need to show up, we need to express our views and that`s appropriate. That`s called freedom of speech when I went to law school and the First Amendment. And, you know, that`s important for people to be reminded of, peaceful protest is fine, it`s part of our American heritage.\par \par \uc2 It`s what gives us our voice if we`re dissatisfied with the powers that be at the time. It should never be violent, obviously, what we experienced back here on January 6th, but, my god, you know, to silence people and not have them be able to express their point of view, we don`t want to go there either, so there`s a balance to this to be struck and that needs to be nonviolent, it needs to be productive and it needs to be peaceful.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we have to look for. A big decision in the trial coming up soon, I just pray as the president is for peace and calm.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Charlie Crist, Democrat from the Tampa-St. Pete area, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 CRIST: Thank you, Chuck. My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, electric vehicles could be a major key to tackling the climate crisis, but it won`t be easy getting them up and running. We`ll explain, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. NBC News is taking a special look at the climate crisis this week ahead of Earth Day and ahead of the International Climate Summit later this week as President Biden gears up for that summit. This afternoon he`s going to virtually tour an electric battery plant in South Carolina. The administration promotes electric vehicles as a key part of the solution with the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 Now the infrastructure plan that the White House is pushing proposes $174 billion for electric vehicles. Currently electric vehicles make up less than 2 percent of cars on the road in the United States according to data from the analytics firm IHS Market.\par \par \uc2 Josh Lederman, my colleague at NBC News, has an exclusive inside look at a newly constructed electric battery plant in Lordstown, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Josh, take it away.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, if you think about it, Chuck, if you wanted to go out today and buy an American made, union-built electric vehicle, you can`t do that. Even if you were to go buy a Tesla, there`s a good chance the batteries cells in that care are coming from Asia.\par \par \uc2 Electric vehicles have by far the lowest content of American made products in them. And that`s why auto industry analysts say that while the signals the federal government is sending through its own purchasing power is important, it`s really what`s happening right here, what the auto industry is doing by signaling a different direction that is really going to hasten this shift.\par \par \uc2 Inside Ultium Cells which is going to be about the size of 30 football fields, we talked to the executives who said they`re designing battery cells that can be stacked in pouches either front ways or sideways which will create a range of vehicles that can -- they can be put into so you can have the same battery cells going into an electric Cadillac or an electric Hummer.\par \par \uc2 They hope that creating those new options will make it possible for more consumers to choose electric vehicles over gas guzzling combustion engines that make up about a third of our greenhouse gas emissions, when you look at the transportation sector.\par \par \uc2 And if you think about the politics here, Chuck, it was President Donald Trump who promised jobs will be coming back to Lordstown auto assembly plant that shuttered here two years ago. It is now the big automakers who are betting on an electric future building a battery factory here, one of the largest in the country, because they think that American auto consumer of tomorrow is going to want an electric vehicle, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I certainly think we`ve all learned the need to have the supply chain be as domestic as you can make it, as well as you described there at the beginning.\par \par \uc2 Josh Lederman, in Lordstown, Ohio, for us, with an important aspect of all of this. Josh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210420eh4k0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for April 3, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui, David Jolly \par \uc2 7,291 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: the right side of history. Right now, Georgia continues to be the symbolic center of this brewing war over voting rights, and as the cruelty of the state`s voter suppression law becomes national wreck, Corporate America has found itself contending with whether to keep doing business in the Peach State amid growing calls for a boycott. The fallout is now as American as the Major League Baseball is.\par \par \uc2 Major League Baseball announced Friday that it will be pulling this year`s All-Star game from Atlanta, as a handful of other companies weigh whether to take actions of their own.\par \par \uc2 Of course, G.O.P. lawmakers in the state are weighing now to punish those companies for exercising their freedom to value black and brown consumers.\par \par \uc2 Just a short time ago, Georgia Governor, Brian Kemp, said he will not bow to pressure from Major League Baseball or anyone else, but Georgians of good faith of all races tried to purge Trumpism from their midst.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is counting on proposals to reclaim Republican hearts and minds unveiling his $2 trillion infrastructure package this week amid immediate pushback from Republicans who take issue with this tight tax hikes on corporations to improve conditions for America`s poor and working class.\par \par \uc2 Later in the show, I`ll talk to the point person for the President`s plan, newly appointed and confirmed Housing and Urban Development Secretary Marsha Fudge.\par \par \uc2 But first, Georgia is on the nation`s mind and joining me now is Democratic Georgia State Representative Park Cannon, who was arrested for attempting to observe Georgia`s voting suppression law being signed by the State Governor Brian Kemp, and with her is her attorney, Gerald Griggs, who is also Civil Rights leader, Vice President of the Atlanta Chapter of the NAACP.\par \par \uc2 Now, first State Representative Cannon, and I want to say I`m glad to see you here free, and I know that because of legal reasons, you can`t say much about what happened on March 25th at the Statehouse. And now I want to go into that, but I`m going to be careful and I know Attorney Griggs will say, Rev, now don`t go there, stop. He`ll put me in check.\par \par \uc2 And I know you overlapped, going to school with my daughter at Polly Prep in 2009, so she will be beating me in my chest. So I`m going to be very careful not to go over the line.\par \par \uc2 But what can you share with us about what you want to see done and what you wanted to see done about this bill?\par \par \uc2 PARK CANNON (D), GEORGIA STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Well, thank you for having me, and I would like to make it very clear that it is a responsibility that I hold as a State Representative, as well as the Caucus Secretary to witness bills as they are introduced, discussed, voted on and signed.\par \par \uc2 So not until we witnessed the signing is the process of our legislative responsibilities over. That` what I was doing that day when I lightly knocked on the door.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, when you lightly knocked on the door to observe, which is in your capacity as an elected representative, and you`re there in the State Capitol, so you were not violating anything, you were doing your job is your position and that is what you were elected to do.\par \par \uc2 CANNON: Absolutely. And it`s so important on issues like Senate Bill 202 that we are heard because these have impacts on every single Georgia voter, whether Republican or Democrat.\par \par \uc2 I even went over to the Senate to be heard on the bills when the measures were being debated, but everything with Senate Bill 202 was so irregular.\par \par \uc2 The hearing times would change in the middle of the day, and I would get a Post-It note on my desk that would say this bill is not going to be heard. We`ll hear you in the morning.\par \par \uc2 So when we heard irregularly that the bill was being signed 10 minutes after we adjourned, we definitely want people to understand that that was an important moment for all legislators to be at.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Attorney Griggs, we learned this week that one of the arresting officers in your client`s case said in his incident report that, quote, "The events of January 6, 2021 at the U.S. Capitol were in the back of my mind," end quote. He added that he was concerned that your client`s conduct could embolden other protesters at the State Capitol that day.\par \par \uc2 First of all, I saw the tapes. I didn`t see other protesters anywhere. And I don`t know what conduct of knocking on the door lightly made him think that, but what is your thoughts about that logic, counselor?\par \par \uc2 GERALD GRIGGS, ATTORNEY: I think it`s a false equivalency. I think that he was trying to deflect from the actions that we believe were unlawful that day, and so I`m saddened that he would compare it to that.\par \par \uc2 We all saw what happened on January 6th in this country and we all saw what happened with the arrest of my client. They do not comply or similar in any fashion. So I`m just sad that he will compare that.\par \par \uc2 But what we did see was an African-American female that was trying to defend our democracy and be present in transparency and make sure that she was reporting out to the 4.5 million Georgians that votes will be affected by this new law and she was present with another State Representative and several voters. They were not going to do anything that rise to the level of what happened on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 So again, it`s a false equivalency and I`m sad that he would even compare it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now Representative Cannon, you know, Major League Baseball has pulled out its All-Star game from the state, with the President co-signing on it, and now black faith leaders -- Reverend Bishop Reginald Jackson and others are calling on major corporations to divest by the end of next week, at least some of those businesses they`re having discussion with.\par \par \uc2 But not every Georgian who is against this law is in favor of economic boycott, because it could hurt some working class people, they are saying, perhaps more in this COVID era than before, but where do you stand on this rising call for boycotts?\par \par \uc2 CANNON: I believe in corporate accountability, which is why as the conversations on Senate Bill 202 took place, over 40 non-consecutive legislative days, I spoke with those corporations and let them know, it`s not enough just to decide, you`ll let Election Day be a holiday for a small subsection of your workers who might already have that day off. No. We need more than that.\par \par \uc2 We needed them when the bills were being debated in committee, but instead there was silence, which was very irregular. So I am very pleased to see that the community is being listened to.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll stand with our community because, as I know it, I was speaking with Martin Luther King, III. He walked me into the State Capitol the day after I returned, and what he reminded me was, there will always be the power of the black dollar. There will always be corporate accountability that happens in a city like Atlanta, and a state like Georgia.\par \par \uc2 We can all think of historical boycotts that have happened and I do believe that now is the time for everyone to be in conversation about how we intentionally protect voting rights.\par \par \uc2 This is 2021 not 1921.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, we are having Martin Luther King III in a moment and I know him and his wife, Arndrea who heads the Drum Major Institute has really, really been calling all of us around the country saying, be on standby.\par \par \uc2 Attorney Griggs, I am out of time, but she is facing felony charges. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 GRIGGS: Yes, she is facing felony charges of up to eight years in prison.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For knocking on the door?\par \par \uc2 GRIGGS: On these charges.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For knocking on the door facing eight years in prison. Well, we are going to stay on top of this. Thank you for being with us, Legislator Cannon and thank you, Attorney Griggs and I`ll tell my daughter, Ashley hello for you. I`m sure she is watching and making sure I behaved.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, global civil rights activist Martin Luther King, III. Martin, we`ve talked a lot in the last few years about hundreds of voter restriction bills introduced at the state level since the year began, particularly this year, and according to the Brennan Center for Justice, that is 361 bills in 47 states as of March 24, five of those bills have been signed into law, among them Georgia`s.\par \par \uc2 But the number that sticks out for me is the 108 suppression bills introduced in just the last six weeks. This feels just as much like an insurrection, if you say -- if you want to say that we saw physically in the Capitol in January, only without the beat down pipes and the Confederate flags.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN LUTHER KING, III, CIVIL RIGHTS ACTIVIST: Well, there is no question that this is a rolling back of the rights of people, rights that my father and \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 , so many actually worked to acquire and then some lost their lives, Viola Liuzzo and James Reeb and Schwerner, Goodman and Cheney and Jimmie Lee Jackson, just to name a few who lost their lives in the hope that we would get the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 Last year, of course, we lost \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  and C.T. Vivian, and Joseph Lowery, giants in modern day Civil Rights. It`s beyond tragic that we are at this point. And, you know, I`ve just seen Representative Cannon, I was so honored just to be able to walk with her on her first day back into the Legislature.\par \par \uc2 But it gets -- it shows when you have a bill, the bill says that people can`t drink water -- you can`t bring people water in line. This is the most inhumane thing that we`ve ever seen, and probably, that`s why we`re beginning to see this action from you know, the Major League Baseball, pulling out this All-Star game, and others may do the same.\par \par \uc2 Delta has spoken up as has been stated, and other companies, \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  and we may even see more because this is unfair to people and it is on -- I mean, it`s strange, it`s strange that we`d be dealing with this right now as we approach April 4th, the 53rd anniversary of the tragic of dad`s --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I want to ask you about that. I want to ask you about that. Tomorrow is the 53rd anniversary of the assassination of your father. Today is anniversary of your father`s famed mountaintop speech, the one he gave in Memphis at Mason Temple Church of God and Christ just hours before his assassination.\par \par \uc2 Between the Chauvin trial over the killing of George Floyd, this voter suppression campaign we face, and Trumpism still being a political force, even without Trump, how do you characterize race relations 53 years after your father was taken from us and taken from you and your sister, Reverend Bernice and Brother Dexter?\par \par \uc2 KING: Well, maybe, Rev, it seems like that European cultural supremacy is attempting to take its last breath. These obstacles that unfortunately, we have to go through, and what I mean by that is, as you know in the United States, the black and brown population will overtake the European population very shortly.\par \par \uc2 And every time we`ve made great strides when President \uc2 Obama\uc2  was elected, it took eight years, we`re not all -- really throughout the full eight years he was there, there always is resistance and things that happen, that the adversaries try to take different steps to move us back, so there`s always backlash when there was progress.\par \par \uc2 Remember, right after the march in Washington, the "I Have a Dream" speech, right after that march, just a few days later, was the bombing of a 16th Street Baptist Church and four little girls lost their lives.\par \par \uc2 So we`re not, you know, we`re not surprised. I`m greatly disappointed in 2021 that we`re having to go through this. But we`re going to be vigilant, we always have been. And you know, I`ve been honored to be able to work with you, Rev, on so many occasions to keep pushing this envelope forward.\par \par \uc2 It`s just sad that we are here as we get to April 4th tomorrow. I mean, dad would have been greatly disappointed in our nation, but he`d be very proud of the young people who are coming forward, offering leadership and he`d be so proud of his granddaughter and our daughter Yolanda Renee.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No, I`m sure he would. In fact, I`m expecting you and Arndrea and Yolanda Renee at National Action Network Convention the week after next in New York and all people are asking me, is Yolanda coming? They don`t even want to hear me speak. They might want to hear you, but everybody loves Yolanda, the granddaughter of \uc2 Dr. Martin Luther King\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us by Luther King, III, and I might add, not only did you walk Legislator Cannon, you came to Minneapolis and sat with the family at the funeral of George Floyd and they talked about that this week as I spent time with them for the beginning of the trial.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us tonight ...\par \par \uc2 KING: Thank you, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: ... on an anniversary that I`m sure means so much to you and your siblings.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve seen a week of testimony in the Derek Chauvin murder trial, including contentious exchanges between witnesses for the prosecution and Chauvin`s legal defense team.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors are expected to continue next week, with even more eyewitness testimony, including from Chauvin`s fellow officers providing context to the video that started it all.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Patrisse Cullors, cofounder of Black Lives Matter and activist extraordinaire, and Channa Lloyd, attorney and Managing Partner of The Cochran Firm.\par \par \uc2 Patrisse, you`ve been in the trenches. I`ve watched you and worked with you for years. The head of the Hennepin County Police Department`s homicide unit, Richard Zimmerman, was on the stand yesterday. I want to play some of this testimony for our audience who may have missed it because I want you who has helped to bring this police abuse to the forefront again, it`s happened in our history, but never like now, and you`ve been on the forefront of that for many of us and you helped coined the phrase that we all rally around Black Lives Matter.\par \par \uc2 I want to come back for your reaction after you watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What is your -- you know, your view of that use of force during that time period?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ZIMMERMAN, HENNEPIN COUNTY POLICE DEPARTMENT HOMICIDE UNIT: Totally unnecessary, putting your knee on the neck for that amount of -- that amount of time is just uncalled for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Patrisse, many have noted that several of Chauvin`s fellow officers have not corroborated his story. Now, you have the senior homicide cop in Chauvin`s department, calling his actions unnecessary and deadly force, something we`ve not seen often police do on the witness stand. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 PATRISSE CULLORS, COFOUNDER, BLACK LIVES MATTER: I mean, I just think that`s a powerful moment in history. While we`ll all wait to see the verdict and what happens, what we heard in that video is law enforcement saying it was completely unnecessary to harm and end up killing George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is a powerful moment, and I think we should take stock for the last eight years all the trials that we`ve witnessed, and every single way that people have excused police violence, this time, it`s not happening in the same way.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Attorney Lloyd, American race relations were on trial this week, as they are in all of these instances. And as predicted, the defense tried to have it both ways, painting Mr. Floyd as a dangerous super thug, surrounded by dangerous bystanders and thereby justifying the force used because Officer Chauvin had fear.\par \par \uc2 But in the same token, Floyd was also cast by the defense as being on death`s door due to the fentanyl in his system and not the knee on his neck. So I ask you as an attorney, did it work?\par \par \uc2 CHANNA LLOYD, ATTORNEY: I don`t think that it`s working because unfortunately, they over promised and under delivered. They are talking about this threat, this aggressive crowd that he had to be very concerned with. The problem is, is that the video belies that.\par \par \uc2 The video shows that this crowd was deeply concerned about George Floyd`s health and his condition. They listened to everything the officers have to say. So that undercut their theory that this is an angry and aggressive mob.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me ask you this, I am amount of time, but I want to ask this last question to both of you. I want to know what your general takeaways are from the first week.\par \par \uc2 We got some tense moments as the defense attorneys tried to find daylight in the argument that Mr. Floyd`s death was due to his own habits or that bystander witnesses were biased because they used derisive language to describe Chauvin and his action.\par \par \uc2 Let me start with you, Attorney Lloyd, what are your takeaways?\par \par \uc2 LLOYD: My takeaways with this is that the state is doing a very good job in layering their case. They have put in the bystander testimony. They`ve brought in this video that creates a visceral reaction, and now, they`re tying it to law enforcement that for the first time are testifying against a fellow law enforcement officer. That`s very significant.\par \par \uc2 So I think you see the impact of that on the jury.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You and your two colleagues sat down the night George Zimmerman was acquitted for killing Trayvon Martin, and came with the phrase that has now resounded around the world, Black Lives Matter. You`ve been involved in the trenches. So many things happening that you can`t get to everything and everyone, but you`ve been consistent, Patrisse, now we`re in a court room. We did not get in a court room with Eric Garland and others.\par \par \uc2 We are in a court room. What are your takeaways?\par \par \uc2 CULLORS: That we`ve got to keep fighting, that every single one of us who showed up in the streets last summer need to continue to show up, that Black Lives Matter and all of our members and our leadership, we are determined to get legislation that really looks at challenging law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 I think there`s a long way to go, but right now, we`re in a really powerful and critical moment.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, and we are definitely going to be seeing where this goes and continuing to fight even if there are convictions here that does not change the law. We cannot win around and think the fight is over.\par \par \uc2 Patrisse Cullors and Channa Lloyd, thank you both for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Senate Republicans aren`t just fighting to block voting rights for African-Americans, they even object to our freedom of expression when it comes to our hair.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, good Saturday to you. Some of the stories we`re watching for you this hour. There are renewed discussions of tighter security at the U.S. Capitol after an incident yesterday that left two dead; one, a Capitol Hill police officer. Investigators say, a 25-year- old man from Indiana rammed a car into the northern entrance yesterday, lunged at officers with a knife, then being shot and killed.\par \par \uc2 The officer killed, William Evans, served at the Capitol Police for 18 years.\par \par \uc2 The C.D.C. says fully vaccinated Americans can now travel throughout the country without testing and quarantines. They do recommend though that travelers wear masks while on planes, buses and trains. The T.S.A. says, Friday, it was the busiest day of checkpoint screenings at airports since March 2020. Over one and a half million people were screened across the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And TMZ reports rapper DMX is currently hospitalized from a possible drug overdose. Fifty-year-old DMX suffered a suspected heart attack and was admitted to a critical care unit at a hospital in New York Friday.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s Got-Cha, I want to talk about hair and senate Republicans. You will understand in a moment.\par \par \uc2 Not satisfied with attempts to keep black Americans from exercising our constitutional right to vote, Republicans are determined to even keep every facet of our lives under the heavy weight of discrimination down to the very hair that grows out of our heads. I`m talking about the CROWN Act, which stands for quote, "Creating a Respectful and Open World for Natural Hair."\par \par \uc2 Because, believe it or not, in 2021, schools and workplaces are still allowed to discriminate based on hairstyles, most often targeting those styles primarily worn by black folks like locks, braids and afros.\par \par \uc2 Activists and democratic legislators have been working on ending this kind of discrimination through passage of the CROWN Act since 2019 and the bill even passed the House late September, last September, but it was blocked in the Republican controlled Senate by self-described Grim Reaper, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , never even let the legislation come up for a vote.\par \par \uc2 But a new majority took over in January, thanks in large part to the very black voters hurt most by this kind of discrimination and new versions of the CROWN Act that have been reintroduced in the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Let it sink in a minute, the CROWN Act would ensure that black folks are allowed to wear our hair the way it grows out of our heads, or in a protective style like locks and braids without being forced to alter our hair with heat or chemicals and Senate Republicans are once again, the only thing standing in the way.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me be clear, I`m not against relaxing or perming your hair, I`ve been perming mine for the last 40 years and my surrogate father, surrogate son bond with the Godfather of Soul, James Brown, but I made that choice. It was not something I was forced into under the expulsion, the threat of explosion from school or the firing from my livelihood. It was my choice, which is why I can make this statement.\par \par \uc2 But even as Senate Republicans drag their feet on a Federal solution to this kind of racist discrimination, states and localities are taking up the cause. Eight states have already passed their own version of the CROWN Act along with some of the biggest cities in the country.\par \par \uc2 Republicans claim they want to conserve personal freedom, unless it`s the freedom for black people to wear our hair like we see fit. But guess what, if you keep standing up for discrimination and standing in the way of racial progress, well, just like we do at the barber shop, we`ll just have to cut you off and sweep you away. I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: After four years of neglect under the Trump administration, the \uc2 Department of Housing and Urban Development\uc2  is under new management. With billions already allocated to H.U.D. in the American Rescue Plan and potentially billions more in the upcoming infrastructure package, the Biden administration has some big ideas to get the department working for the American people again.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, I caught up with longtime friend and former chairwoman of the Congressional Black Caucus, the newly confirmed Secretary of H.U.D., Marcia Fudge.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Secretary Fudge, you are a longtime friend of this show, but this is the first time we`ve had you on since you`ve been appointed and confirmed Secretary of H.U.D. Let`s start with that. What are your priorities? What are the things that you really are anxious about getting on to and get done immediately?\par \par \uc2 MARCIA FUDGE, SECRETARY OF HOUSING AND URBAN DEVELOPMENT: Again, thank you so much, Rev, for having me.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to give you my three priorities, maybe not necessarily in any particular order, but certainly at the direction of the President we are doing everything we possibly can to make sure that we can get homeless people off the streets. We know that on any given day in this country, more than 580,000 people go homeless. So that`s one of the priorities.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, to make sure that people stay in their homes or in their apartments. So we want to do the things that are necessary for forbearance and for moratoriums to make sure that people are not evicted.\par \par \uc2 And thirdly, and probably just as importantly as the other two is to level the playing field so that people who are in a position to purchase homes can do it.\par \par \uc2 We know that homeownership in our communities in particular, is the fastest way to create and maintain generational wealth. So we want to ensure that credit is available, that information is available, and that people have a real chance, a fair chance to get into the homeownership business.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now dealing with the evictions or dealing with mortgage problems of people paying their mortgages due to COVID-19 and then dealing with homelessness is going to take a real effort.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that we did know, you were succeeding, the Secretary of H.U.D., Ben Carson, under the Trump administration, who had no background in this, and clearly H.U.D. was not a priority of the Trump administration. How much damage did they do? And how much do you have to really try to repair H.U.D. in order to do the things that you`ve laid out, and the kind of support you`re going to get with this President who seems to have made H.U.D. and housing more of a priority than his predecessor.\par \par \uc2 FUDGE: The first thing, Reverend, that we found is a group of small but mighty employees, consummate professionals, but they are understaffed and overworked. Just over the last four years, H.U.D. has lost 20 percent of all of its employees -- 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 So what we found is people in need of assistance, and through COVID, it has made it even more difficult as people work remotely, but they are truly, truly overworked as well as -- and this is going to really shock you, I think, Rev, that in his budget for the next year, he requested a reduction -- a 50 percent across the board reduction, and a 40 percent reduction just in housing.\par \par \uc2 We know how many people are on --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FUDGE: That`s right, people are on the edge of losing their homes, of losing their apartments and he asked for a reduction of 40 percent. So that`s what we`re dealing with, someone who consistently for four years devalued and disinvested in this agency.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got a lot of work to do, but we have clearly up to the task of getting it done.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow. That is a stunning move, 40 percent reduction given, especially during the period. But that brings me then to the President`s relief plan, the American Rescue Plan around this COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 It pledged billions of dollars literally around rents, around home ownership moratoriums, housing vouchers, assistance to the homeless. How in practical terms, can this be done? What and how will that money be allocated that will mean something to the American people? What -- how do we, on the ground know what that means?\par \par \uc2 FUDGE: Let me just say how excited I am to be a part of this team. The President has just passed a piece of legislation that is generational. It is once in a lifetime.\par \par \uc2 Not only are we talking about the $1,400.00 checks and the additional resources if you have children. Just think about it this way, Reverend, if you are in a family of four that make less than $90,000.00 a year, you`re going to end up getting $5,600.00 over the next year.\par \par \uc2 We have found ways to cut child poverty almost in half in this country through our child tax credits, our earned income tax credits. We are doing things that no one ever believed could be done.\par \par \uc2 But as it relates to housing, Rev, there`s $40 billion -- $40 billion -- just for rental assistance and homeowner assistance. There`s more than $200 billion set aside for us to start looking at how we build more low income and affordable housing. There`s $5 billion to get people off the streets.\par \par \uc2 Now the $200 billion admittedly is in the next plan, but in this plan that has already been passed, there`s $5 billion for homelessness. There`s $5 billion to assist with bringing new and affordable units on board. We believe that over the next couple of years, with just the rescue plan money, we could put more than 30,000 new households in affordable housing. It`s big, Reverend. It`s really, really big.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, talking about the administration coming with probably the biggest social landscape moves I`ve seen probably since I was a kid with Lyndon Johnson. The next big venture the President has announced is the Infrastructure Bill, and a lot of that will be under your agency and you`re one of the leaders in dealing with that Infrastructure Bill and allotment.\par \par \uc2 Explain to us what your role will be, H.U.D.`s role will be in what you hope to do, and convince the Senate and the House where you serve so well, for many years, why this bill becomes so necessary in terms of the infrastructure of the country? And what will it mean to people because it`s got COVID aid in there, it has a lot of different things in the bill, in the whole promise of this huge multitrillion dollar bill?\par \par \uc2 FUDGE: Well, the first thing I would say to you is that just the recognition that housing, this infrastructure is big in and of itself. Other administrations have not really admitted that but housing is one of the most important tenets of infrastructure in this nation.\par \par \uc2 Without decent housing, all of the other things that we`re working on are going to be more and more difficult to accomplish. So certainly, there are resources here to build more than two million new housing units to catch everyone up on their rears, as well as to bring them current on their rents and their mortgages.\par \par \uc2 Not only are we talking about wraparound services, we are talking about things like gun control. We`re talking about education and training.\par \par \uc2 So much of this is going to empower our neighborhoods, Reverend, and give people the opportunity to change their station in life. Let us see people with dignity and respect and with hope.\par \par \uc2 So help really is here in this plan, and as I go to talk to my former colleagues, I know that many of them already believe what we believe, especially when we talk about putting $40 billion just to repair and rehabilitate existing housing.\par \par \uc2 Reverend, you know yourself. You live in New York, where we have the largest public housing footprint in the country. The conditions of some of these buildings are abhorrent.\par \par \uc2 So that $40 billion will just be a downpayment on letting people live without mold on their walls or without lead in their paint, or their children having to be concerned about rodents. It`s going to change their lives.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to make sure we remove all of the lead pipes in this country so the children don`t have to get lead in their water. It is a massive, but necessary piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow. Well, I thank you for being with us. We look forward -- you agreed to speak at National Action Network`s convention week after next, virtually, and 53 years after Dr. King`s assassination, which we mark on Sunday, I think he would be proud, I will take the -- I will step out there and say that he would proud to see a sister like you, S-I-S-T-A sitting over H.U.D. of course, one of his last missions was the poor people`s campaign, part of that was around housing.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us, Secretary of H.U.D., Marcia Fudge.\par \par \uc2 FUDGE: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: My thanks again to H.U.D. Secretary Marcia Fudge and her team.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, another victory lap for the Biden administration after a booming jobs report. We`ll break that down next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. We still have a lot to discuss about other news this week, and I want to turn now to my panel, former Deputy Secretary of Labor under the Obama administration, Chris Lu, and former Florida Member of Congress Republican, David Jolly.\par \par \uc2 Chris, let me go to you first. The latest U.S. jobs report shows a massive gain over 900,000 jobs were added in March, unemployment down to six percent signaling a possible sign of economy recovery from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Labor saying this is the biggest boost since August for the job market. Now, President Biden on Friday calling it a source of hope. What do you think? Will unemployed Americans be able to get back on their feet soon? And we still see black unemployment almost double that to white. How do we do that where we target that black Americans still are not disproportionately unemployed?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LU, FORMER DEPUTY SECRETARY OF DEPARTMENT OF LABOR: Rev, this is a very encouraging jobs report and it`s a testament to the success of the Biden administration both in rapidly accelerating vaccinations, as well as pushing for additional economic stimulus.\par \par \uc2 But we have a long way to go. We are still eight and a half million jobs short of where we were before the pandemic, four million people have left the labor force. Four million people are long term unemployed; that means they`ve been out of work for six months.\par \par \uc2 And you`ve pointed out correctly, the black unemployment rate is almost double what it is for white Americans and we`ve seen that trend whether the economy is good or the economy is bad. And so there`s more work to be done and it`s one of the reasons why the Biden administration is pushing so hard for an infrastructure package, because it will provide educational and training opportunities, which can benefit for people of color.\par \par \uc2 And it also makes important investments in minority communities, things that we`ve been talking about a long time -- transportation, broadband. It will deal with environmental hazards in inner cities.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is an important start, but we have a long way to go. This is not just about bringing the economy back to before it was before the pandemic, but really trying to address some of these systemic inequalities.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I want to also talk to you about -- Chris, about President Biden`s $2 trillion American jobs plan, for which he hopes will fix our country`s infrastructures like bridges and highways, and will also ensure access to clean waters in all parts of the country and high speed broadband internet to all communities.\par \par \uc2 Taking all of this at once, can President Biden get this ambitious plan passed and what will be the economic impact?\par \par \uc2 LU: Well, the economic impact is going to be huge. The analysis is, 18 million jobs created and as importantly, it`s these investments and things that we have been frankly talking about for decades in both Democratic and Republican administrations.\par \par \uc2 It`s roads. Its bridges. It`s modernizing the electrical grid. It`s making investments into clean energy.\par \par \uc2 It`s school construction. It`s veterans hospitals. It`s providing home care to the disabled and the elderly.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s important and each one of these individual components has had bipartisan support in the past. The question is, when you take this big package, and you put -- rollback tax cuts from the Trump administration, whether it could pass, it should pass and ultimately, it will pass if the Democrats go through budget reconciliation in order to get this done.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: David, Republican Congressman from Florida \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  has been making headlines this week for being under investigation by the Justice Department for sex trafficking allegations, including a relationship with a minor.\par \par \uc2 G.O.P. Leader Kevin McCarthy said he wants to wait and see before removing Gaetz from any committee assignments, calling the allegation serious. Are Republicans scared to confront Congressman Gaetz because he is connected to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Certainly, they are, but this is moving very quickly and Kevin McCarthy may face a decision as early as this next week.\par \par \uc2 Rev, Florida Republicans are already looking past \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , assuming that his political career is coming to an end, either through resignation or if the facts prove out to be true through an indictment.\par \par \uc2 And your point of the gravity of these charges. This is significant. Gaetz`s colleague, Joel Greenberg who has already been arrested and indicted, one of the charges against him is causing a minor to engage in a commercial sex act.\par \par \uc2 If that is the investigation to which \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  is subject to, there`s a reason his career should come to an end. There is a child victim in this case, and we see \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  try to play that Trump card, try to hit the media very hard with this. It`s absolutely wrong both on moral levels, but also in terms of press and legal.\par \par \uc2 As I said yesterday, the smartest thing \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  can do is shut up, hire a lawyer and resign from Congress.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: David, veterans of Florida politics will remember a sex scandal involving another Sunshine State Congressman named Mark Foley, contributing to Republicans losing Congress in the control of Congress in 2006. Is the Gaetz`s scandal something voters might keep in mind in the midterms next year?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: They need to and we have to lean into the victim story here. This was a child, right? We`re talking about a young woman, 17 years old. This was a child who under the law is unable to consent to her own sexual behavior.\par \par \uc2 If in fact, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  or his colleague, Joel Greenberg were involved in a sex act with this minor, the Federal law is there for a reason and the reason is to bring to justice the perpetrators who committed this act and who should be considered sexual predators before they`re ever considered a politician or Member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Chris Lu and David Jolly, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Tomorrow is a challenging day. As a Baptist Minister, it is a day that I will celebrate the resurrection of Jesus Christ, which is my religious belief. But also remember his admonition, "If you love me, feed my sheep."\par \par \uc2 And as a Minister engaged in social justice, I will commemorate the 53rd Anniversary tomorrow, April 4th, of the assassination of \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I was 13 years old and already the Youth Director of the New York chapter of his organization, his Operation Breadbasket in New York, under Reverend Dr. William Jones and Reverend Jesse Jackson.\par \par \uc2 And Dr. King challenged us in his last book, "Where do we go from here?" which is why I was in Minneapolis this week, trying to continue to do the work for those that want to continue in his tradition, as his son who was on earlier today.\par \par \uc2 I asked the first day of the trial for the family and friends to bow with me on their knee for eight minutes and 46 seconds, which was the time we thought at that time that George Floyd was under the knee of Officer Derek Chauvin, and was dead. I was there from Sunday leading a prayer vigil.\par \par \uc2 It is not enough to just commemorate Dr. King. It is not enough to just celebrate Christ, we must do the work, which is why, week after next, National Action Network, even in a pandemic, we`ll have our National Convention. It will be virtual this year.\par \par \uc2 We`ve already confirmed 13 members of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- the President`s Cabinet and leading members of government, leading Civil Rights leaders, grassroots activists, youth activists, faith leaders to plan on how we`re coming out of this pandemic and fight for what Dr. King stood for.\par \par \uc2 Martin Luther King, III, Arndrea King, Yolanda King and others will be there because we need to plan for action. We cannot just come out of the pandemic and think we just want to go back to normal. Normal didn`t work for all Americans. We need to imagine the new normal and that`s what we`ll be talking about at the NAN Convention.\par \par \uc2 Go to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.nationalactionnetwork.net"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.nationalactionnetwork.net}}}\uc2  to participate in the 14th through the 17th.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 p.m. Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 My colleague Alicia Menendez picks up our news coverage now.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Thanks so much, Reverend Sharpton. Hello, everyone. I`m Alicia Menendez.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, the world of sports, business and politics collide in a Republican effort to make it harder to vote. The growing fallout comes after Major League Baseball threw a curveball at the Georgia G.O.P.\par \par \uc2 The league pulled its All-Star game out of Atlanta protesting the state`s new restrictive voting law.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020210404eh4300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for April 1, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Shaquille Brewster, Monica Alba \par \uc2 9,202 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 April 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And was there sort of a switch of jobs when the firefighters came in?\par \par \uc2 SETH BRAVINDER, WITNESS: Yes. At some point, the officer stepped out and I kind of moved down to near where he was sitting.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. So, when the firefighters came, did you feel like there were enough people to do what you needed to do for Mr. Floyd in order to get where he needed to go?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, you said the officer stepped off. Did one of the firefighters take over with respect to what you were working on in the airway?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, the airway was established so they`re working on squeezing that bag to breathe for the patient.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And maybe you could just describe how that works. Does someone need to squeeze the bag in order to provide -- how does it work?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, it`s just a bag that you`re going to squeeze roughly every five to six seconds to breathe for the patient.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And so, after you could set it up, and the firefighters arrived, did one of them take over that part?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: From what I remember, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And then, you said you moved down and assisted with the I.V. Can you just describe that?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: I just worked on trying to get I.V. access.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And was there some difficulty with that?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: A little bit. I believe -- I believe we had I.O. access at some point, but I don`t know when that was done.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And what is I.O. access?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: It`s a -- it`s a needle that drills into your bone, in your leg. It`s another way of giving medications.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And why would you need to have that kind of access?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Same reason, to give medications to try to resuscitate them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And if you could describe why you might initially go from one location to moving to I.O. access.\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: I.O. was often done if you`re having difficulty with I.V. access. And it`s common to do in a cardiac arrest.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And I.O. stands for?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Intraosseous.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Intraosseous. And I.V. is?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Intravascular.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. So, at some point, did that get established, an I.O. line?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And was that the way that the medications were administered?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: I would need to look at the documentation to be for sure on that, but I believe that`s how they were administered.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But any event, do you recall that he was administered those medications?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What did you do after that?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: We continue working on our substituting for a period of time. And then I kind of had everything done we wanted to, and I went to the front and started driving to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Maybe you could just explain, you know, was there something that you needed to accomplish before you felt comfortable moving again, getting -- driving the vehicle elsewhere?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes. I think there`s a standard set of -- we call it like ACLS guidelines from \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2 . It`s how we`re trained, and how we do -- provide care in resuscitation like this. We compare all those things in place.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, what are those things? Just generally, what do you -- what are you trying to do?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, just the highlights. I`ll say, chest compressions, breathing form, and doing medications that we need to give them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And is it -- your objective to have those things in place before you transport to the hospital?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, it`s time-sensitive to have them done, so we need to get them done right away.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And maybe you could explain why -- what`s time sensitive about these resuscitative processes?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Just the longer the patient goes without receiving resuscitation, the lower likelihood they will be resuscitated.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And in your training as a paramedic, when you`re talking about time sensitivity, when it comes to resuscitative efforts or things like CPR or anything like that, what are you trained to do in terms of timing?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Can you rephrase the question?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. That was not a good question. Is it important to start resuscitative efforts immediately after someone does not find a pulse?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: As soon as we can, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. And why is that?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: The primary goal, I would say, is circulating blood through chest compressions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And I think you previously testified, if there is a delay, does that lessen you likelihood of success?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Objection (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Rephrase.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What happens if there`s a delay?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Based on my understanding, that`s not good for the outcome.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. So, ultimately after you had the things in place that you just described, did you then transport Mr. Floyd to the hospital?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And were you the driver?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m going to put on the screen just for you an exhibit that has not yet been admitted. That`s Exhibit 67. Can you see that?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And does this image fairly inaccurately show you and your partner and the firefighters unloading Mr. Floyd at the hospital?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Your Honor, I`d offer Exhibit 67.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any objection? 67 is received.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Permission -- thank you. And once you got to the hospital, did you assist in moving Mr. Floyd, transferring him inside the hospital with the others?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We can take that down. Thank you. And what happened at that point in time, once you were bringing into the hospital?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: There`s a room we call the stateroom for critical patients. We bring him into the E.R. We brought him there and moved him over to the hospital bed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And what was his condition? You would talked about asystole at the beginning of your interaction with him. Once you had worked on him and then brought him to the hospital, what was his condition at that point in time?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: I don`t know what his exact cardiac rhythm was at that point, but his overall condition did not appear to change.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And have you noted any changes? Was there a period of time of pulseless electrical activity?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: At some point, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And would that have been at some point after the asystole?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes. I can`t tell you exactly what point it was but --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So just to --so we know we`re -- what we`re talking about here. What is pulseless electrical activity?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: It`s essentially electrodes -- it`s picking up an organized rhythm, so electricity over your heart, but we don`t feel a pulse anywhere. So, it`s -- (INAUDIBLE) purposes, it`s not perfusing so we`d continue chest compressions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK, so you said not perfusing. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, so we`re not -- we don`t feel a pulse, so there`s no blood being pumped through the body to your organs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And if you -- as someone who`s being worked on or compressions are being given, can things change in terms of the heart rhythm in your experience as a paramedic?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But you if you have pulseless electrical activity, does that -- what does that mean in terms of the change?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: It`s -- as far as how we`re going to be resuscitating him, it`s the same as asystole.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, your course of action remain the same?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, they`re -- asystole and PEA is like the same protocol, I guess you`d call it, for how to resuscitate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And even though there was -- when he first arrived, he was asystole, at some point later there was PEA, at any point, did he regenerate a pulse or come to -- was he revived?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And ultimately, the hospital, was there a report given to staff at the hospital about his condition and all of those things that you did?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes. My partner would have given that report to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And were you assisting with the other needs as well as part -- were you part of that process?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And what did you do after the patient was -- Mr. Floyd was handed off to medical staff at the hospital?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: I would have just been bringing equipment back to our truck and putting it back together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. Again, over the course of your interaction with him and treatment and Mr. Floyd, didn`t you notice any positive change, any revival?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Nothing further.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Nelson.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Good afternoon, sir. Yes, we`re afternoon. Sorry, I have to check the time. Thank you for being here. I just have a few follow up questions for you. I want to just understand sort of the very brief -- very, very briefly the progression of your education and kind of the differences. Is CPR training different than being an EMT?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: You`re talking about like a CPR certification?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes. It`s a part of it, but yes, it`s different.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, the basic kind of resuscitative training someone could receive would be a CPR certification?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And then kind of a higher level of education would be an EMT, right?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And then a higher level than an EMT as a paramedic?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And then up through, you know, all the way up to doctors and stuff, right?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Okay. And you are a paramedic, you said, you`ve been about four years now?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: (INAUDIBLE) I was a paramedic elsewhere prior to that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK. How long -- how many years total as a paramedic?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: I started as a paramedic in 2013.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK. And so, in Hennepin -- for Hennepin County, do you frequently service calls in the city of Minneapolis?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you frequently call or service calls where police officers are involved?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you -- have you arrived at a scene where you`ve seen officers on top of a person before?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you been called to other calls where a suspect has struggled with police?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What does it mean to stage as an EMS?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: What does it mean to stage? You`re referring like, staging for safety purposes?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Correct.\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, if a scene is not safe, we will stage in the area and wait for police to give us with a code where we call it that it`s safe to come in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And just for clarification purposes, you were not asked to stage in this event, right, because of the crowd?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: No, we`re not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right. But generally speaking, it`s common for police to arrive at a scene, deal with whatever the circumstances are, and then EMS is called an after police has dealt with the danger of the situation, right?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Can you say that question again?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sure. This kind of -- kind of long-winded. Police will frequently go to a call before EMS to deal with some dangerous situation.\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, it completely depends on the type of call.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right. As the course of being a paramedic, have you responded to other overdose calls or overdose calls?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: I have responded to overdose calls, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And is it Hennepin EMS policy to have police respond to those calls with you?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: They -- yes, they do. I can`t reference a policy but --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: -- in my practice, they are -- in my experience, they do respond with us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And is that because when people are sometimes resuscitated or treated for an overdose, they become aggressive and violent?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Objection, your honor. (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Overruled. (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Sorry, can you say that again?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sure. Is it -- is it the practice to have police respond to those calls because when people are resuscitated or revived from an overdose, they can become violent or aggressive.\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: It can happen sometimes, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you personally seen that happen?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, turning your attention to May 25 of 2020, you were the driver of the ambulance that day, correct -- or for this call, I should say?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: For that call, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And after all of this, you would agree that you have met with law enforcement officers during the course of the investigation of this case?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And you have made statements and you know those statements were recorded?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And do you know that -- have you had an opportunity to review a transcript of your statement prior to today`s testimony?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK. You`ve also met with the prosecution team a couple of times, is that correct?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In preparation, including as late as last night, correct?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And you kind of just talked about what your testimony was going to be today, right?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right. So, on May 25th, when you arrived, you testified that you saw sort of a crowd standing out around the police officers, correct?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And when you arrived, the ambulance actually pulled ahead of where Mr. Floyd and the officers were located, correct?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And that would be for ease of loading him into the ambulance, correct?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, that basically the back of the ambulance would be in line with Mr. Floyd`s -- Mr. Floyd to kind of get them in, get him into the ambulance, right?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right. And so, when you got out of the ambulance and you walked back towards the office, you would have had to walk back towards the officers, correct?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, I can`t -- 