\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And is that a treatment option for you for somebody who is exhibiting those behaviors that you were asked about violence and struggling, that sort of thing if alive and moving, basically, is that right?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, it`s profound agitation, so somebody that`s really violent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You would not give that to somebody who is dead or in cardiac arrest or unresponsive, is that right?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Correct. We would not give it, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You`re asked about responding to overdoses as part of your job. When you respond to overdose deaths or an opioid -- an opioid overdose death, what do you look for in terms of someone`s pupils?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: We look at them to see if they`re small, their pupils are really small.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And when you say it`s small, as that like pinpoint --\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Constricted, pinpoint, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And do you know what Mr. Floyd`s pupils looked like when you and your partner arrived on scene?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: I don`t recall looking at them myself. I know my --like I said, my partner checked him initially.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And did you receive a report about that from him?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, he would have -- he did say something. I`d be kind of guessing. I believe -- I think I know what he said, but --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. All right, we`ll move on from that. You were asked some questions about the crowd and whether that was the reason to move Mr. Floyd to another location. Do remember those questions on cross- examination?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You testified on direct examination, but there are a number of things that you consider when you determine whether to move someone, is that right?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So is having a crown one of those things that you might consider?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: It`s -- yes, one thing to consider. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But you also talked about how you needed a significant amount of equipment that was in your ambulance, is that right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Objection (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Rephrase.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What are the other things that you consider in terms of why to move somebody to another location?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Overruled.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You can answer.\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: So, we -- can you ask that last part just one more time?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What are the things that you take into account when deciding whether to move a patient to another location?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes, so in that case moving into the ambulance the equipment was a consideration of that and being in a good environment to resuscitate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And do you also -- I believe you may have testified about this but does it require focus on your part?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And are all of those things part of your consideration on a scene about what to do?\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If I may have just a moment?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: And we are back. Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. We are in the lunch break of the Derek Chauvin trial. I`m Chuck Todd. You`ve been watching the fourth day of testimony in the murder trial of Derek Chauvin. We`ve had a lot of news happening. You just missed President Biden`s first Cabinet hearing. But in the trial, we just heard what a paramedic who described his efforts to resuscitate George Floyd. He also described what he saw as he arrived on the scene.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRAVINDER: I was standing a little ways away so I can get -- my partner would have a more accurate description of his condition at that point but from what I could see where I was at, I didn`t see any breathing or movement or anything like that.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Earlier we heard emotional testimony from Courtney Ross, Floyd`s longtime girlfriend, about their relationship and their own struggles with addiction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY ROSS, GEORGE FLOYD`S LONGTIME GIRLFRIEND: Floyd and I both suffered with opioid addiction. We both suffered from chronic pain. Mine was in my neck and his was in his back. We got addicted and tried really hard to break that addiction many times.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Prosecutors are not shying away from George Floyd`s past, his opioid addiction, which is likely to be a big part of the Chauvin defense. Jurors once again today saw images of the last moments of George Floyd`s life. That came after yesterday`s video of that moment and what led up to it was shown, some of it made public for the first time.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday jurors were shown surveillance video of George Floyd inside Cup Foods just minutes before his deadly encounter with the police. And surveillance video from outside the store as a crowd watched Floyd`s lifeless body lifted into an ambulance, as well as cell phone video showing police officers confronting Floyd after he left Cup Foods. And then there`s the body camera video from all four officers involved in Floyd`s death from the time they arrived on the scene until Floyd was placed into the ambulance.\par \par \uc2 That includes never-before-seen video from Derek Chauvin`s body camera, including this moment after Floyd was taken to the hospital, attempting to justify what had just happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEREK CHAUVIN, FORMER MINNEAPOLIS POLICE OFFICER: That`s one person`s opinion.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CHAUVIN: We got to control this -- got to control this guy because he`s a sizeable guy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, not --\par \par \uc2 CHAUVIN: Looks like he`s probably on something.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Shaquille Brewster has been covering the trial for us from Minneapolis. I`m also joined by two legal experts today, Glen Kirschner, MSNBC legal analyst and former federal prosecutor, and Channa Lloyd, managing partner of Cochran Firm`s Orlando branch.\par \par \uc2 Shaq, let me start with you once again, perhaps the most interesting strategy to look at this is what the prosecutors, they`re essentially trying to take any -- whatever the defense may use of George Floyd`s past, they want to make sure this jury sees that the prosecution is not afraid of anything in George Floyd`s past?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That`s right. And they used someone very sympathetic to George Floyd, very close to George Floyd to help do that, and it`s Courtney Ross. And, you know, when she started her testimony, it was emotional almost instantly, starting her testimony with how she met George Floyd and the story about how she didn`t know him and how Floyd saw her in distress and offered to pray with her, and that sparked that connection between the two.\par \par \uc2 And she also personalized George Floyd and talked about that opioid addiction that he suffered through, saying that he suffered from chronic back pain and that eventually turned into an opioid addiction and she looked over at the jury and said, a classic American story. That was a moment that when you look at the poll reports, her testimony as she looked over, that`s something that they somewhat responded to and there was the eye contact that you saw there.\par \par \uc2 She also got extremely emotional and the pool report says when I say pool report, I mean, the two reporters, the accounts from two reporters in the courtroom, they say that was a time where you saw one of the jurors possibly look emotional. It`s hard to tell with the masks but one of the jurors, a black man put his hand over his forehead for a second as she was breaking down and becoming so fully emotional talking about George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 The other aspect to it is when we heard her testimony, that also opened the door for the defense to start making some of their points. They harped on the drug use, they talked about specific pills. They asked where she got those pills from and whether or not those pills were still in existence around when George Floyd died. It is very clear that the defense is going to make the drugs in George Floyd`s system a key part of their process of poking holes. When it`s their turn to make their case, it will be a big part of what they`re arguing there.\par \par \uc2 And then we also hear from this paramedic, Chuck. And, you know, the paramedic is another essential bystander for the end of the process, for what happens when the ambulance arrives. The paramedic says that when they first encountered George Floyd, he was pulseless, that he was unresponsive, that he didn`t have a heartbeat and when they finally got him on the ambulance, nothing really changed.\par \par \uc2 Of course, a paramedic could not produce someone dead. That requires usually a doctor or physician. But when you listen to the description of George Floyd`s state by the time that knee was still on the neck, by the time the paramedic arrived and told the officers, all right, it`s time to go, let`s load him on into the ambulance, you got a sense of just how bad George Floyd`s state was bad and that was something the prosecution was trying to make very clear by having this paramedic come up and testify in front of this jury, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Shaq, I think these various witnesses to this scene, whether it was the store clerk yesterday, the younger children that were watching it, the paramedic today or I guess we went through the various versions of what his training was with the lawyer but the EMT today, and all of them paint I think a very emotional picture that benefits the prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Glen, I`m curious of your thoughts today about specifically the prosecution`s decision to -- I guess it`s called fronting? Is that the term of art? I think that`s what one of our legal analysts called it.\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Sometimes it`s fronting the bad evidence or the evidence that is a little bit less appealing to the jury. We typically call it drawing the sting. You want to pull the sting out of evidence that you think is going to be viewed by the jury as perhaps less than favorable of your case or of your witnesses.\par \par \uc2 But I`ll tell you, when I watched Courtney Ross, George Floyd`s girlfriend, testify, it was painful to watch not only the loss she suffered and how she would light up from time to time when she was remembering how, you know, George really loved to play football, he loved to work out. And you could see the sort of the passion that she had for her former boyfriend. But, you know, she was also made to talk about her own illegal drug use, her own opioid addiction, her boyfriend`s opioid addiction.\par \par \uc2 And then, Chuck, even though I was a prosecutor for 30 years, I winced when she was asked, OK, who`s selling you those drugs? When you`re having to talk about the drug dealer, the person from whom you`re getting illegal drugs, that is a really difficult and potentially dangerous area of testimony for a witness. So you know, she felt very much like a victim by extension because her boyfriend was killed and now, she`s trying to relive that while not only 12 jurors watch her but virtually the nation watches her.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Channa, I was struck, Glenn just referred in some ways she was a victim, too. And I have been struck by all of the testimony by various either eyewitnesses or the personal witnesses who knew, character witnesses on behalf of George Floyd, is just how much pain they`ve been in. And it`s been I think emotionally, I would imagine it`s had an impact on the juror.\par \par \uc2 If you`re the defense team, you`ve got to -- you know, how do you deal with -- they`ve chosen and many times not to even bother cross-examining at times. What do you think was going through their minds on this?\par \par \uc2 CHANNA LLOYD, MANAGING PARTNER, COCHRAN FIRM`S ORLANDO BRANCH: At times there is no value in trying to cross-examine a witness that`s bringing something that`s very emotionally charged. Because no matter what you ask, it`s going to be perceived as being aggressive or not being sensitive. So the smarter tact sometimes with those types of witnesses is not to actually engage or ask any questions.\par \par \uc2 When you`re looking at these emotionally charged testimonies, really what you want to do is put it into perspective and focus back on the facts, which is what I think he tried to do. He, you know, offered his sympathies, addressed her addiction and then moved it quickly into facts. He has to get out of the emotional realm for this defense and move into the factual realm.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Channa, do you think that the defense team is going to have to spend some time providing character witnesses on Derek Chauvin? I mean, because - - are they going to have to improve his image after all of this is done?\par \par \uc2 LLOYD: They definitely need to improve his image. Right now it is not a good image. But we also have to remember that the state is still running -- the prosecution is still running their case. These are their witnesses. They`re going to bring forth their strongest witnesses and then he is going to have an opportunity to bring forth what he considered his strongest case, which is going to be more of the science, the medical, you know, that`s the angle -- the drug use, those are the things that he`s going to bring forth.\par \par \uc2 So this is a chess match. It`s not necessarily going to be decided in these first couple of days but he definitely is going to will have to address it. The next biggest question is whether Derek Chauvin takes the stand. He has the right not to.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD: So it will be very intriguing to see if he decides to speak because no one has heard his side of the story.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, that`s for sure. A lot of parlor games there I`m guessing if we see him, a lot of people think that will be because they don`t feel good about their case. We shall see. Anyway, a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 Shaquille, Glenn and Channa, thank you all for getting us started. As you know, they`re at a lunch break. And we, of course, will pick up coverage of the trial after they come back.\par \par \uc2 And during, right just minutes before they went to the lunch break, President Biden convened his first public Cabinet meeting of his administration. And due to social distancing parameters, his first Cabinet meeting was in the East Room because they wanted to keep folks separated a bit, instead of in the actual Cabinet room,\par \par \uc2 President Biden used the occasion to announce five Cabinet point people for selling the sweeping infrastructure plan that he unveiled yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today I`m announcing that I`m asking five Cabinet members to take special responsibility to explain the plan to the American public. Working with my team here in the White House, these Cabinet members will represent me in dealing with Congress, engage to public in selling the plan, and help work out the details as we refine it and move forward. These five members will be Pete Buttigieg, Jennifer Granholm, Marsha Fudge, Marty Walsh and Gina Rimando.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me now from the White House as we await the cabinet meeting is my NBC News colleague Monica Alba.\par \par \uc2 So, Monica, Ron Klain was out there doing interviews today. It`s interesting, they want to -- it seems like the message is, hey, we`re not going it alone yet.\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Exactly, Chuck. They`re trying to build that runway so that then later if there is no Republican support for this infrastructure plan, they can say hey, at least we tried. And you did hear that also from the Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg, who was on MSNBC just a few hours ago, saying that he`s privately been talking to lawmakers from both parties behind the scenes about this.\par \par \uc2 And then you have the president saying he`s going to be inviting those lawmakers into the Oval Office in the coming days. But in the meantime, he did hand out those homework assignments to the five Cabinet secretaries of course representing Transportation, Energy, Commerce, Labor and HUD. And he has essentially asked them to report back before the next Cabinet meeting on their progress in terms of seeing not just how they can sell this on Capitol Hill but also to the American public.\par \par \uc2 That was a signal to me that, again, they`re going to do what they did with the COVID relief bill. They`re going to tout how popular infrastructure is in polling among voters to say this is something that constituents desperately need. But this was just really notable, Chuck. We lived through this the last four years of the Trump administration. Cabinet meetings were a completely different beast.\par \par \uc2 He normally -- the former president would bring in cameras, sometimes allowing them to stay for 60 to 80 minutes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: You have a feature length film of the Cabinet meeting and it was often very fawning, heaping praise on him. This is a completely different approach of course. This was more of a movie trailer version of that. Just a couple of short minutes.\par \par \uc2 The president made very brief remarks. He thanked the press for being there but said I`m not going to take any questions right now, essentially ending the spray then the Cabinet can have this first meeting behind closed doors, do their own business, and then we`ll get readouts later. So again it`s just a very, very different approach and departure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: And of course using a different venue, which is the East Room, given the COVID restrictions. This is probably the largest group of Biden officials we`ve seen together in one room since the beginning of this administration due to the pandemic, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Monica, one of the other I guess I would call it a predictable event that I`m awaiting an announcement on, has to do with when are we going to see the first high-profile group of Republicans to talk infrastructure with President Biden. It`s the type of meeting I would assume is going to happen sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Yes, the president hinted at that in his speech in Pittsburgh yesterday. He said, I`m going to invite them to come to the table. I want to hear their ideas. I want to see where we can find common ground. It`s a very different approach than he had with the American rescue plan, where he said I don`t care how this gets passed, no ifs, ands or buts, we`re going to do it. And ultimately, Democrats did without the Republicans having to support it.\par \par \uc2 With infrastructure he`s saying, before we get to that point, I do want to have these face-to-face negotiations with the Republicans, and he has said they`re going to come and the venue is in the Oval Office in the next couple of weeks while these Cabinet secretaries are also doing a little bit more of the behind-the-scenes work but they are going to make a public display of this.\par \par \uc2 We saw a little bit of that with the COVID relief bill but really we I think are going to see more of that here because the White House has said what they unveiled yesterday is far more of a starting point. They`re really open to making changes. Again, very different from the first major piece of legislation they got across the finish line, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, they have technical challenges. If they want to use reconciliation to fund this infrastructure project, they actually may have to get bipartisan support in order to create some of the vehicles that they want to use in reconciliation. You don`t get to create new spending in reconciliation or create new agencies or create ideas. You have to do that separately in order to use it. So this is more complicated than the way COVID relief worked.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Monica Alba, at the White House for us. Monica, thank you.\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail, candidate Biden called climate change an existential threat of our time and President Biden is taking steps to confront the climate crisis including rolling back some Trump-era climate policies and he rejoined the Paris climate agreement. That was all during the first week in office. And the sweeping plan infrastructure plan the president unveiled yesterday contains investments in renewable energy, electric cars and billions in climate research and domestic development.\par \par \uc2 For the first time this White House has what`s called a national climate adviser and its former Obama EPA director Gina McCarthy leads the National Climate Task Force, and Gina McCarthy joins me now as the first White House national climate adviser.\par \par \uc2 Gina McCarthy, welcome.\par \par \uc2 GINA MCCARTHY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL CLIMATE ADVISER: Hey, Chuck. Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So look, hey look, this is a -- before we get into some details of your job, it`s a brand-new position, I`ve described it as in shorthand as the national security adviser for climate. How would you describe it? Is that -- and I just say that just structurally. How would you describe what your position is?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: That`s not a bad description because climate change is a national security issue, but, you know, I`m just here to make sure that the president is served in terms of his interest in addressing climate change. I bring together the Cabinet. We work together, really, as a whole of government approach, recognizing that climate change impacts everything we do. And what`s so exciting about the package that you see is that it`s not all focused on climate, and yet it is.\par \par \uc2 It`s underpinned with an understanding that we need to have an infrastructure that`s resilient, we need to grow workers in this country, we need to build hope and opportunity. So for me it`s a very exciting moment in time for us to marry the work of the federal government and what we do to actually build a climate-resilient country but also one that grows jobs, union jobs, good-paying.\par \par \uc2 When the president says he hears the word climate, he thinks jobs. And I think that`s great. That`s exactly how we should be talking about this as a big, bold opportunity to invest in America again, especially the communities that have been really systemically disinvested in. It`s time to build hope for everybody and every worker.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious, you know, look, in order to do some of these bigger things, it`s going to take some bipartisan acknowledgment. And when it comes to climate, because for whatever reason, you know, whether it`s the far-right propagandas or whatever, they demonize the word climate, and it`s made Republicans uncomfortable, yet the business community does seem to want to see some resilience built in.\par \par \uc2 What is the best way for you to break through with some Republicans who may be privately want to work on these things but publicly fear the politics?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, I think part of what we wanted to do with this package was show that it is underpinned by the interest of the business community, by the interest of our communities and workers. This is going to be a very popular package because it is simply building the infrastructure that our country needs to succeed. So we`re working with the automakers. We talked to all of the big CEOs yesterday after this was unveiled, and they were excited about it.\par \par \uc2 Because it`s an opportunity to actually build an electric vehicle infrastructure in this country that`s going to allow us to capture and win that market and bring the supply chain back into the United States. We`re talking to the utilities sector. Everybody knows we have to move to clean energy. This allows us to build the transmission grid that`s actually going to serve it and not have us have people stranded and understand that they can`t get the kind of heat they need like what happened in Texas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: So this all should be a people issue, not a planetary concern. It is about winning jobs in America. It is about good jobs and building our middle class, which the union built and the union will build it again. So we`re excited, and I`m excited to not talk wonky but to talk people in what we need today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, let`s talk about a specific person in the state of West Virginia, and I say this because he`s head of the Energy Committee, Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s obviously concerned about the coal industry and what it means to the state of West Virginia. How do you sell this plan to West Virginia voters?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, I think one of the exciting parts about President Biden`s work here in the infrastructure in his head that he brought to this is one of the challenges we have is to look at the job transition issue. And we`ve made it front and center, not hiding it. So we`ve talked to Senator Manchin, and we`ve talked to folks in Wyoming and Montana and other places that are seeing shifts away from coal and towards clean energy.\par \par \uc2 And the task is for us not to bury that but to work to figure out how we grow jobs in those communities. And part of this plan is all about that. You look at sections that talk about the old abandoned oil and gas wells and coal mines that have never been closed that actually we can provide resources to communities like those in West Virginia that will grow jobs, plugging those leaks that will be helpful to reduce methane, which is harmful to climate change, but also allow them to get back to work.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s not a forever, but it is a great opportunity because there`s hundreds of thousands of them. Exact skill sets, same community, get them back to work, let`s keep talking. One of the infrastructure investments is in broadband, which we know that rural communities need to have available if you really want to attract economic development. So we think that we shouldn`t deny that there is a transition afoot, we should embrace it, we should invest in the jobs of the future for every community.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, speaking of transition, there is some to the left who think this is not aggressive enough, this does not do enough -- does not do enough mitigation on climate to have the impact necessary. To me it sounds -- so the future of fossil fuels. Is this -- do most folks have to realize we have to view this as a transition, it`s not something you can wean yourself off immediately?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: No, and this is about growing our economy. This is about doing it at a pace that science demands but also doing it in a way that our economy is going to be strengthened. And so that`s extremely important. And, you know, we have folks on the other side that are saying that we`re doing too much. I guess we win the Goldilocks prize.\par \par \uc2 Maybe we are just right. But I know that the president is interested in working with Congress and he`s going to listen to those who think we should do more and find a good way to pay for that investment, and we`re going to think about those who maybe think we`re being too aggressive, and we can talk to them about how aggressive should we be after coming out of a pandemic with nearly 10 million people out of work. How aggressive should we be in getting people back to work? So we think this is the sort of just right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Gina McCarthy, the national climate adviser for President Biden. Thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us. I appreciate it. Hope to see you again soon.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks, Chuck. Appreciate it, thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And thank you all for being with us -- you got it.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage including the trial which will begin after lunch break continues with my friend Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210402eh4100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for March 22, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Kasie Hunt, Julia Ainsley, Dasha Burns, Kelly O`Donnell, Maura  Barrett, Pete Williams, Kathy Park \par \uc2 8,066 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KASIE HUNT, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): And if it`s Monday, the Biden administration says it`s trying to stem the flow of migrants at the border as record levels of unaccompanied children are now in federal custody amid growing questions about the government`s handling of the emergency situation.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  says clinical trials here in the U.S. show that its vaccine is safe and effective as communities from Michigan to Miami Beach brace for the threat of another possible surge in infections.\par \par \uc2 And nationwide protests cast a spotlight on racism and violence against Asian-Americans as the investigation continues into those deadly Atlanta spot shootings.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): Welcome to Monday. It`s MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Kasie Hunt in for Chuck Todd. The emergency situation at the border is escalating, so is the by the administration effort to address it. President Biden has told reporters he intends to make a trip to the border at some point soon.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 HHS\uc2  is standing up additional facilities to address the surge. ICE securing hotel rooms to house some migrant families. And NBC News has just learned that some border agents in the Rio Grande Valley have been authorized to release migrants without court dates.\par \par \uc2 The number of unaccompanied migrant children is at record levels as well. The Press hasn`t been given access to see the conditions inside these overcrowded border facilities. But today, Texas Congressman Henry Cuellar`s office released five pictures that he says were taken inside of migrant children overflow facility in Donna Texas.\par \par \uc2 According to Cuellar`s aide, each of these eight pods in the facility has a 260 person capacity. But as of Sunday, one pod held more than 400 unaccompanied male minors. The aide says the pictures were taken over the weekend but wouldn`t say by whom. Most of the obscuring of the faces that you can see existed on the photos that NBC News received.\par \par \uc2 And officials at DHS and CBP did not immediately comment on the photos, but a DHS official didn`t dispute their authenticity. The Biden administration, meanwhile, is trying to navigate both the border emergency and the politics that surround it as the White House grapples with questions about whether it should have been better prepared to handle the situation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY, U.S. DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Please remember something that President Trump dismantled the orderly, humane, and efficient way of allowing children to make their claims under United States law in their home countries. He dismantled the Central American Minors Program, so we are rebuilding those orderly and safe processes as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, in the meantime, we will not expel into the Mexican desert, for example, three orphaned children whom I saw over the last two weeks, we just won`t do that. That`s not who we are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: NBC`s Julia Ainsley is covering all the ins and outs of the situation at the border. NBC`s Dasha Burns is on the ground in Mission, Texas, where she`s been talking to migrant families about why they decided to make this journey. And NBC`s Kelly O`Donnell is at the White House with the latest from the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Julia, let me start with you. And I think it`s very important that we be very clear about why this is happening, the mechanisms that are or aren`t allowing us to be here, and the ways in which we can get out of it. So, I think my top line question for you is, can you just explain to everyone what policy changes were made between the Trump and the Biden administration in terms of how unaccompanied minors are dealt with at the border, what that`s led to, and now how -- what our options are or what the administration`s options are to try to address it?\par \par \uc2 JULIA AINSLEY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, I`d be happy to Kasie. I think it`s really important to do this, because a lot of times we`re just putting out facts, but we`re getting blowback on both sides, because a lot of people just don`t understand this situation. It`s very politically fraught, as you know.\par \par \uc2 But basically, what happened is that a year ago, the Trump administration blocked all asylum seekers from coming into the United States, no matter who they were. Then a court order said, you cannot do this to unaccompanied children. That went back and forth until it ultimately came down to a Biden administration decision to let in the unaccompanied children. They said it was because of humanitarian reasons. So, as a policy, unaccompanied children are allowed in while families and single adults are turned back.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, it`s not like they`re making some new exception for unaccompanied children. This is a return to the status quo pre-pandemic. And then when it comes to families, although the policy is to turn back immigrants who arrive as part of a family, Mexico has simply stopped taking back a lot of these families.\par \par \uc2 And so, as of yesterday, there were over 1,800 families apprehended at the border and only about 180 of them were sent back into Mexico which means a lot of them are staying. And that`s overcrowding these facilities.\par \par \uc2 Why does that matter, because even though we aren`t at a record high in terms of overall border crossings, we are at a record high in terms of children in Border Patrol custody. That is not a condition meant for children. The Homeland Security Secretary himself has said that. Lights are kept on all day. They often don`t have a place to sleep or get outside. Again, another reason why we need access to these facilities.\par \par \uc2 But they are supposed to be let out of those facilities within 72 hours and sent to \uc2 HHS\uc2  where they can be better cared for or where they could be matched with a vetted sponsor. But right now, in part because so much of that space was decreased because of COVID precautions, again, the pandemic plays a big part of this, they don`t have the room to send these children to \uc2 HHS\uc2  as quickly as they`d like to.\par \par \uc2 And so, what`s happening now is they`re trying to alleviate the stress on the border patrol and get these children out as quickly as possible and to alleviate that overcrowding. That enters the reporting that Dasha and I are learning today, in which we understand that now the adults will be released, at least in the Rio Grande Valley, in order to alleviate some of this overcrowding even before they get a court date.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Remarkable. Julia, I want to go back to something you said at the beginning. You mentioned that this was a court case that was working its way through under the Trump administration. And they were essentially saying, OK, you can`t do this to unaccompanied children. Is that something that`s still in the system or is this a decision that the Biden team could say, OK, actually, we want to make a different decision and how we`re handling matter? What are their options?\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: I don`t think we`re going to see a different decision when it comes -- from the Biden administration on that piece. Ultimately, this was left in the Biden administration`s hands and they decided, no, we`re going to make this exception for children, even though the Trump administration did not.\par \par \uc2 I think at this point, they`re not going to go back to the border and say, oh, we said we were doing this for humanitarian reasons. Nevermind, let`s expel these children into the desert. I mean, that`s exactly what the Secretary said yesterday he would not do. I think the question is, how do we make things more humanitarian for those children once they cross over. And importantly, how are they going to try to enforce the policy they do have, which is to send families back when Mexico just isn`t taking them.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Julia Ainsley, thank you as always for your -- for your very clear and precise and compassionate reporting on all of this. Dasha Burns, let me go to you because you, as Julie mentioned, have been doing reporting on this in terms of one of the things they`re going to try to do to solve the problem. Of course, not necessarily -- there`s not a ton of good options here, so walk us through what you`ve learned there. And also tell us a little bit about what you`re hearing from people there on the ground about why they`ve decided not just to make this journey, but to do it now.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC REPORTER: Hey, Kasie. Yes, the reporting that you`re hearing from my colleague, Julia Ainsley, we have been watching that unfold here on the ground. The majority of migrant families crossing the border here in the Rio Grande Valley are being allowed to stay in the United States. And we`ve been spending -- we spent the weekend and spent this morning speaking to those migrant families.\par \par \uc2 The majority of them started their journey here to the United States about two to three weeks ago from Central American countries. They came here to the border, surrender to Customs and Border Protection. They were processed, and then released into towns like McAllen which is next door here, many of them taking shelter with local charities like where I am right now.\par \par \uc2 This is Our Lady of Guadalupe, a church here that has been housing migrant families overnight before they go on to their next destination. The families we spoke with, many of them taking a harrowing journey risking their lives to flee dangerous situations back home. We heard stories of devastating hurricanes, extreme poverty, and violence.\par \par \uc2 But as for this question about why now, the majority of folks we spoke with did say that this new administration, that President Biden gave them hope, and that played a role in their decision to take this treacherous journey here. I want you to listen to one of the migrants we spoke with. This is a father of a one-year-old boy. He came here with his son and with his wife who is nine months pregnant. Take a listen to what he told us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: There`s a new administration. There`s a new president in the United States. Did that have anything to do with your decision to come now or did you hear anything about that this might be a good time because of the new, President Joe Biden?\par \par \uc2 SILAS NAHUM, MIGRANT (text): Yes, because the situation was more difficult with the last President. And before I didn`t think it was possible for me to make the trip. But now, seeking the opportunity that the new President is giving to migrants, we decided to travel.\par \par \uc2 And what can I say, first I am thankful to God and to him that it was the opportunity. Because honestly, we`re here to seek out what`s best for our families and also escaping from many -- from many things that can affect us in our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And Kasie, on that new reporting with my colleague, Julia Ainsley, about Border Patrol agents now releasing migrants without a court date, we spoke to some of those migrant families this morning. We talked about a dozen or so people. They all said they did not have a court date. We even took a look at some of those documents. We obtained a copy of one of them. They chose to remain anonymous, but we did see that when it comes to the court date, it says to be determined.\par \par \uc2 We also spoke to some people who didn`t even receive that notice to appear. They just got some identification documents, told us that they did not provide any contact information to authorities. I asked if Customs and Border Protection or immigration agencies had any way of reaching them. They said no, no phone numbers, no addresses. And it`s unclear as we reached out to some of those agencies, how they plan to get in touch with those migrants to get them in front of an immigration judge. Kasie.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Right. Dasha Burns, thank you. Kelly O`Donnell, let me go to you because this, of course, is becoming -- is a crisis that the Biden administration is having to grapple with both humanitarian as Dasha and Julia laid out, but also politically.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, there are -- this has been an incredibly charged, difficult emotional issue for many, many years in our politics. Republicans have been seizing on what we`re seeing at the border. We`ve also had some concern Democrats talking about it as well. What are the options for the Biden administration and what are they thinking about doing about it?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, notable right off the bat, Kasie, is you use the word crisis, and it seems like a simple, appropriate word given all that has just unfolded, and yet that is a word that the administration will not use. I had an exchange with Jen Psaki, the Press Secretary here over that, and she said it is not a crisis having unaccompanied children coming to the U.S. border.\par \par \uc2 In terms of options, some of the things that we were trying to extract from the White House is to understand what will the President do. He talked about making a visit. There are no immediate plans for that. They don`t acknowledge that his words or policies are in any way a magnet, even though the reporting Dasha and others are doing, where you`re talking to real people who among the reasons they chose to come, they cite what they believe is a more welcoming approach under a Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 The White House is saying no. It has consistently said the border is closed, do not come. They did tell us today that they`re through the State Department doing a lot of advertising in Spanish and Portuguese and other appropriate languages through the countries that are affected here to reinforce the message of the border is not open and not to come as another way to get their message out before people begin to make what is a difficult, harrowing, and dangerous journey.\par \par \uc2 The White House is also claiming that they are finalizing opportunities for independent media coverage of these facilities. That is so the American people can see what`s happening inside. As you began the program with photos provided by a member of Congress who is a part of a delegation, there has been no independent coverage of this. They`re saying they`re working on those details.\par \par \uc2 They have possession of photos that are made available to the President and others. They`re not releasing those and they would not commit to how quickly that access to journalists would be made available so the public can see what`s happening. Kasie?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes, I think it`s important to point out Bennie Thompson was just on with our colleague Andrew Mitchell. He`s, of course, the chairman at the Homeland Security Committee in the House and said it`s going to be a problem if we don`t have that access by next week. Kelly O`Donnell, thank you very much as always for your reporting today.\par \par \uc2 And coming up here, new super spreader fears as massive crowds put part of Florida under a state of emergency. Plus, another shot of hope as \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  says its vaccine is 79 percent effective. And later, a Department of Justice official says investigators are getting closer to charging the Capitol rioters with conspiring to overflow -- overthrow the government. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. We`ve got some good news on the vaccine front today. \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  says they`ll apply for emergency use authorization in the U.S. after the latest trial data shows the vaccine is 79 percent effective at preventing Coronavirus infection, and get this, 100 percent effective at preventing severe illness and death.\par \par \uc2 But even as nearly two and a half million people are receiving vaccines every single day, scenes like this have local officials and public health officials concerned. In Miami Beach, Florida officials have declared a state of emergency this weekend and instituted an 8:00 p.m. curfew because of the Spring Break crowds. Come on people.\par \par \uc2 It comes as eight states are reporting a rise in COVID cases including Michigan which has seen cases go up 75 percent over the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now NBC`s Maura Barrett who`s in Detroit this afternoon talking to officials about the resurgence in cases there. And also joining me, infectious disease experts Dr. James Hildreth. He is the president of Meharry Medical College and a member of FDA`s Vaccine Advisory Committee. He is also a member of the Biden administration`s COVID-19 Health Equity Task Force.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here. Maura, let me start with you on your latest reporting. What are you hearing about what`s behind this spike in Michigan?\par \par \uc2 MAURA BARRETT, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Kasie, the concerning thing here in Michigan is the spike in cases but also the spike in hospitalizations despite the fact that nearly 25 percent of the state has gotten at least their first dose of the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Officials here are pointing to the fact that the governor has been rolling back restrictions. Restaurants, gyms, bars allowed at 50 percent capacity, but also the fact that Michigan is the second-highest amount country-wide of the B117 variant. That`s the U.K. variant, and that second only to Florida.\par \par \uc2 The governor is saying that a lot of Michiganders go back and forth to Florida during this season. And so, these are some things that doctors are really concerned about, but they have advice for the general public about how to fight these variants which are more contagious. I want you to hear some of my conversation with Dr. Usamah here at Henry Ford.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 USAMAH MOSSALLAM, DOCTOR, HENRY FORD MEDICAL SYSTEM: Vaccine right now is the only way out of this pandemic and to be able to help curb it. All vaccines have been shown to have activity against two different strains and variants and it really is the only way to end this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Please, whenever you get a chance, please get the vaccine. Please get any vaccine that`s offered to you. They`re all equally effective against significant severe disease and or death. And they all are showing very good activity against these different strains.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Now, what`s also interesting is another doctor here pointed out the case spikes, a lot of what we`re seeing are in the younger range of the population, people aged from 10 up to 29. And so, they`re really again, encouraging those safety precautions that we`ve been talking about for the last year.\par \par \uc2 And again, making sure people are aware of the expanding vaccine eligibility here in Michigan today, those who are 50 and older can get vaccines as well as those 16 and up with disabilities or medical conditions. So, doctors here are feeling optimistic about increased supply and eligibility, increasing availability for people to get the vaccine and to stay safe. Kasie?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Maura, thank you. Dr. Hildreth, let me go to you on this very question, just get you to offer your perspective on what we`re seeing in terms of the case rates in Michigan, why that would be concerning. And how quickly do you think we need to get people vaccinated to try and get ahead of this? Are we on the right pace right now?\par \par \uc2 JAMES HILDRETH, VACCINE AND RELATED PRODUCTS ADVISORY COMMITTEE, FDA: Kasie, I think we`re on a really good pace. You know, we`re exceeding the President`s a goal of 100 million shots in 100 days. We`re way, way better than that already. So, I think we`re on a good track. My concern is, as with the case for the CDC Director, we can`t get ahead of ourselves. Vaccines are getting administered to people, and we`re making great progress, but there`s still work to do.\par \par \uc2 And there could be a resurgence at any time if we let down our guard too soon. We need to keep wearing masks, keep our social distance, and do the other things that that we had been doing to get the virus under control. So, these gatherings that we`re seeing on beaches and crowded restaurants and all this is very worrisome, because we still have work to do.\par \par \uc2 There`s still time left for the virus to surge again. And we don`t want to lose any more people than we have to now that the vaccines are being given all over the country.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: For sure. Doctor, let may ask you about the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine because of because, of course, we`ve been covering some concerns about the vaccine in Europe. They have halted use of it in some countries because of blood clots. SINCE then, of course, the European Health Authority has come out and said no, it`s fine, go ahead. And obviously, we have this new data here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 But what would you say to people based on the information that we have about whether they should feel if in fact, it does receive use authority here in the United States, whether it would be safe to take it?\par \par \uc2 HILDRETH: Well, I think, first, people should know that this virus is -- this vaccine has been given to more than 13 million people, at least in U.K. and Europe. And the most extreme reaction that we`ve seen is an extreme allergic reaction, which is occurring at about one in four million to one and six million cases.\par \par \uc2 So, this vaccine is as safe as all the others. The effectiveness of it is a little bit less in terms of the top line for symptomatic disease. But the most important thing is that this vaccine prevented all hospitalizations, and no one died after receiving it. So, it`s 100 percent effective in doing what we needed to do, which is to keep people out of the hospital and keep them alive.\par \par \uc2 There are no -- there are no safety concerns with this vaccine anymore so than the others that are already being used. And again, millions of people in Europe and the U.K. have already received it, and it`s still being used there today.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Do you have any concerns about contributing to vaccine hesitancy if this candidate becomes -- this vaccine becomes part of the mix here in the U.S.? I mean, do you -- do you think that these reports are confusing enough that they would create additional risks or questions for how many people will ultimately be willing to take the vaccine, or do you think we`re just better off if we have it here in the pipeline?\par \par \uc2 HILDRETH: So, Kasie, I think what we saw in Europe is the way these things should work. The vaccines are given people develop an unexpected reaction, in this case, blood clots. You pause the vaccinations to determine whether or not the temporal relationship between the reaction and the vaccine is a causative one. And once you`ve eliminated that is not you, resumed the vaccinations. That`s exactly what has happened.\par \par \uc2 So, the evidence that we have indicates that these vaccines are not causing the blood clots. These are things that are happening no more so than in the general population. And here in the United States, my advice to people is whatever vaccine becomes available to you first, please take it because the goal is to get as many people vaccinated as quickly as possible. They`re all safe. They`re all effective.\par \par \uc2 And as soon as we get up to an 80 percent or so vaccination rate here, a number in this country, we can get back to a new normal and I think that`s what we all want. But it`s too soon to drop our guard. We still need to wear masks and do the things that we`ve been doing to keep our communities protected.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Certainly, what we all want. Dr. James Hildreth, thank you very much for your time and expertise today. We really appreciate your voice as always.\par \par \uc2 And coming up next here, growing calls across the country to stop the hate against the Asian-American community in the wake of the Georgia spa shooting rampage. We`re live in Atlanta ahead.\par \par \uc2 Plus, new developments and possible serious new charges in connection with the January 6th siege at the U.S. Capitol which has now become one of the largest investigations in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. The Department of Justice likely has the evidence necessary to charge some of the suspects in the January 6th Capitol riot with the most serious possible charge, conspiracy to overthrow the government. That`s according to the federal prosecutor who`d been overseeing the investigation. NBC News Justice Correspondent Pete Williams has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, MSNBC JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voice over): From the beginning, federal prosecutors have said they`d consider charging the Capitol rioters with sedition, a rarely invoked charge of conspiring to overthrow the government.\par \par \uc2 Now, two days after Michael Sherwin stepped down from supervising the investigation into the riots, he said in an interview aired Sunday night on 60 Minutes that prosecutors are getting closer to filing just such a charge.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SHERWIN, FORMER ACTING U.S. ATTORNEY, DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA: I personally believe the evidence is trending towards that and probably meets those elements.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The former interim U.S. Attorney in Washington, Sherwin says he was in the crowd on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 SHERWIN: You could see it was getting more riled up. Where it was initially pro-Trump, it digressed to anti-government, anti-Congress, anti- institutional. When I saw people climbing up the scaffolding, hanging from it, hanging flags, I was like, this is going bad fast.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Police officers were overpowered by a violent mob of Trump supporters who forced their way into the halls of Congress as lawmakers worked to officially count the 2020 presidential vote. Just over 10 weeks later, Sherwin says the Justice Department has filed more than 400 criminal cases.\par \par \uc2 SHERWIN: So the first people we went after, I`m going to call the Internet stars. All right? The low-hanging fruit. The zip tie guy. The rebel flag guy. The Camp Auschwitz guy. We wanted to take out those individuals that essentially were thumbing their noses at the public for what they did.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The most serious charges so far focused on about two dozen members of far-right militias including a group called the Oath Keepers. In one video, Sherwin says their tight single-file formation is evidence of a military-style assault.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY, "60 MINUTES": The infantry calls it a stack.\par \par \uc2 SHERWIN: Correct. A stack or a ranger file. A column. A close quarter combat column going up that staircase.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KASIE HUNT, MSNBC HOST: And Pete Williams joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Pete, can you walk us through what the likelihood is in your view that there are people who end up charged with sedition in this situation?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS (on camera): Well, I think one of the interesting things about this interview is that basically this is what we`ve been hearing from the Justice Department since late January. So in a sense, although you could ask whether it`s appropriate to be talking about it on camera or not, what the former U.S. attorney there said is what we`ve been hearing pretty consistently that they think they will eventually bring seditious conspiracy charges.\par \par \uc2 Now, you know, as I said in the piece, it`s most commonly thought of conspiracy to overthrow the government. But one of the elements of it is anybody who uses force to prevent, hinder or delay the execution of any law of the United States. And of course, what Congress was doing that day was carrying out the Electoral Count Act, formally counting the presidential electoral vote.\par \par \uc2 So there could be a fit here. But, you know, stepping back a little bit, what would the significance of it be? It does carry a 20-year maximum sentence. But so do some of the other charges that have already been filed such as obstructing a government proceeding. So it`s importance maybe is much symbolic as it is in terms of seeking a penalty, harsh penalty.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: No, that -- I mean, that makes sense. And I think the symbolism at hand, and when you look at these images, the symbolism of what happened that day I think brings that to mind and underscores why it`s something that we are focused on.\par \par \uc2 Pete, do you think the president -- the former president Trump could potentially face criminal charges as part of this investigation?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I just don`t know. And, you know, Sherwin seemed to argue both sides here. Again, this is what we`ve been hearing consistently from the Justice Department since day one, since January 7th, literally day one after the riots, which is that they will look at every possible thing, including whether or not the president incited the crowd. And what he said is on the one hand the president`s comments did seem to excite the crowd.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the government`s -- one of the government`s questions here is, when did people decide to storm the Capitol? You know, he said that they`re still trying to answer that question, whether some people made a decision that they were going to storm the Capitol before the rally, before January 6th. And of course, the president also said things during those comments about being peaceful.\par \par \uc2 So what they have said all along is, they`ll look at all things, they`ll look at those people that got tours from some members of Congress inside the Capitol before the riots. All those questions are on the table. You know, is it likely the president would get charged? You know, if I had to guess right now, I would say no, because the threshold for the federal incitement statute is very high in terms of criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, he`s now facing two separate civil lawsuits filed by a former member of --\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: A couple of former members -- a couple members of Congress. But those are -- that`s a different standard of proof and a different law.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, Pete Williams, thank you, as always, for your great reporting on this. We really appreciate your time today.\par \par \uc2 And let`s turn now to a story that`s been developing since late last week after allegations of sexual misconduct were raised against Congressman Tom Reed. The New York Republican announced he`ll not run for any elected office in 2022 after being accused of rubbing a female lobbyist`s back and unhooking her bra without her consent in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Reed apologized to Nicolette Davis and took full responsibility in a statement late Sunday saying the incident occurred, quote, "at a time in my life in which I was struggling," and revealed he entered treatment for alcohol abuse that year.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Reed, who is co-chair of the Problem Solvers Caucus, was one of the lawmakers calling for Governor Andrew Cuomo`s resignation in light of multiple sexual harassment allegations and he was considering running for governor against him.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next here, as calls to stop the hate ring out across the country, memorials and marches nationwide are honoring the victims of the Georgia spa shootings. We`re going to have more on that movement.\par \par \uc2 Plus, I`ll speak with Congresswoman Barbara Lee about efforts on Capitol Hill to combat hate crimes.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. We are seeing an outpouring of grief and calls for change from the Asian American community and beyond after eight people, six of them women of Asian descent, were killed at an Atlanta area spa -- at Atlanta area spas, excuse me, last week. Thousands gathered in cities from San Francisco to New York and, of course, Atlanta at memorials for the victims and at marches to demand justice after a year that saw a massive increase in the number of hateful incidents against Asian Americans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Stop Asian hate.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Love Asian people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Stop Asian hate.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Love Asian people.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And joining me now is NBC`s Kathy Park reporting on the ground for us in Atlanta. Also with me is "Washington Post" White House reporter David Nakamura who`s been reporting on how government and Asian American leaders are confronting anti-Asian bias.\par \par \uc2 Kathy, let me start with you. What are you hearing today on the ground? And what do we know about whether this is going to be charged as a hate crime?\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Kasie, I can tell you that there is still a level of fear and anxiety among Asian Americans in the wake of this tragedy. These rallies that were held all across the country, it offered a safe space for Asian Americans to come together to share their experiences of racism and discrimination that has been going on for quite a long time now. But it really took these tragedies to spur this movement for change.\par \par \uc2 So obviously, there is a lot of pain, a lot of grief as this community continues to heal and mourn the eight victims lost. Meanwhile, you mentioned the investigation. Right now, it`s still in the preliminary stages. And it is being led at the local levels. However, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is also assisting as well. \uc2 Atlanta Police\uc2  just a couple of days ago said nothing is off the table.\par \par \uc2 However, federal officials say that right now there is enough concrete evidence to move forward with a federal hate crime case against the suspect so they are waiting to see how the local investigations play out before they also provide assistance regarding these hate crime charges. Kasie?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And Kathy, can we take a moment to talk about who the victims are? We learned a little bit more about who they are. Could you take us through that?\par \par \uc2 PARK: Sure. These victims were mothers. There was a grandmother who was killed. Businessowners. And it`s difficult when you talk to these victims` families. When you see them, clearly they are in shock. They are in trauma and still trying to process everything that`s happened in the past couple of days.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with Randy Park on Friday. He lost his mother Hyun J. Grant. She used to work here. And he posted this really profound message about his mother and the tragedy that took place here. And it resonated with so many people. And then there was a \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  account that he created as well. And it`s garnered so much attention. In fact donations have been pouring in from all around the world. It`s climbed to more than $2.7 million. And it continues to climb.\par \par \uc2 This is an issue that has not only impacted the Asian American community, but communities all around the country.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PARK: I mean, you might notice here behind me that the vigil continues to grow each and every day. You have people of all backgrounds, of faiths, races, coming here, sometimes breaking down in tears because they are moved by this tragedy as well.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes. All right, David Nakamura, let me go to you. You`ve been reporting on, of course, the community and the feelings and some of the things Kathy was describing, but also what can be done about it from a policy perspective. What do you think is the most important takeaway right now in terms of how we as a society and also how our government try to protect people from this?\par \par \uc2 DAVID NAKAMURA, THE WASHINGTON POST WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: I think there`s two different things going on, Kasie, and they`re not always compatible. One is sort of the immediate outcry of this particular attack and then of course some recent other attacks that drew a lot of attention where you have a crime, this one was so dramatic, you have people dead, people want justice. And what does that look like?\par \par \uc2 And I think you have people in the Asian American community, also Congress members, saying this should be a hate crime based on the fact that it was at an Asian-run spa and had people of Asian descent employed there, and the suspect knew that and there were women there as well. So no matter what the suspect may have said or told police was the motive, there`s a deeper motive there. But not so easy.\par \par \uc2 And I think you have other advocates who actually are saying let`s put the brakes on this. We don`t fully know what`s happening not just in this case but some of other big cases that have come to light recently. In the Bay area, there were some attacks caught on video. And these activists are saying, let`s hold on a second. Let`s remember what the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2  and others have said which we can`t always just go down this sort of harsh rule criminal justice response to add more police, that add hate crimes, that add police units, promoted the hate crimes.\par \par \uc2 Those are important people say but to do so in a larger discussion about how to deal with that. And that`s longer term. Right now you have Merrick Garland at the Justice Department did speak with Asian advocates about a week and a half ago. You had of course Joe Biden go down -- President Biden to speak with advocates just a few days ago. And you had Susan Rice at the White House do the same. Advocates are happy that they`re listening. But how you deal with this and how you stop this is not so easy.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: David, can you talk a little bit about how the community itself is trying to come together to grapple with this? I mean, clearly, the people who have been so hurt by what happened, regardless of what the criminal -- the shooter in this -- alleged shooter in this case has said about his motives or lack thereof, it`s really caused deep pain throughout the community. But also, we don`t want to gloss over the fact that it`s a community made up of people from different countries all over the world, different cultures, different foods, different backgrounds.\par \par \uc2 And to a certain extent, there is a challenge in trying to figure out how to get everyone on the same page because all of a sudden there is this incredible shared pain. How is the community itself coming together, dealing with all of that to try and make some changes here?\par \par \uc2 NAKAMURA: Kasie, I think what you`ve seen over the past year since the pandemic started is advocates saying this is, you know, predicting that some of this could happen. That there is some racially motivated slurs and attacks early on in the days of the pandemic last March. And that for a year now, you`ve had folks trying to organize around the idea that we need to be mindful.\par \par \uc2 You had the former President Trump of course using racist language, racist language, xenophobic language, described the virus to blame China.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NAKAMURA: And advocates have been really -- marshaling support around that. But you do. You have Asian American actors coming in, offering rewards, offering support. You`ve had sort of bigger voices now coming together. But this right now in these last few weeks, we`ve seen some of this sort of more extraordinary crimes happen that advocates are saying, this is what we`ve been talking about, this is what we`ve been warning about.\par \par \uc2 Now you`re having this idea that how can we build a bigger, longer lasting movement around this to make our voices heard? You hear a lot when you talk to advocates, this is a moment where people are finally starting to listen. But even beyond the pandemic, before that, Asian Americans had concerns around this, around immigration, around language access, around the fact that there is no sort of -- you know, just one Asian kind of idea in the community.\par \par \uc2 Some are held up as minorities, some are held up as foreigners, perpetual foreigners, and these are all myths and all stereotypes that can be harm inducing. And I think right now, you`re saying, how do we drive this energy toward a longer lasting movement that can really make changes?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, Kathy Park and David Nakamura, thank you both for your very important reporting. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And coming up next here, what Congress is doing now to combat violence against people of Asian descent across the country. I`m going to be joined by Congresswoman Barbara Lee of California after a quick break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. Right now two pieces of legislation that could make a difference when it comes to combatting violence and racism against the Asian American community are working their way through Congress. Proponents are calling them the COVID-19 Hate Crimes Act which gives the Justice Department more oversight over coronavirus-related hate crimes. And the No Hate Act which would, among other things, improve the reporting of hate crimes.\par \par \uc2 And I`m joined now by Congresswoman Barbara Lee, Democrat from California. She is helping to raise awareness for the upcoming Stop Asian Hate Virtual Day of Action set for March 26th.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, good afternoon to you. Thank you so much for being here. I know you represent many constituents of Asian American descent. Fill us in on what this legislation would do, and what is President Biden`s role in trying to get it passed?\par \par \uc2 REP. BARBARA LEE (D-CA): Well, thank you so much. First let me just say my heartfelt condolences and prayers go out to the families of the victims in Georgia, to the communities. This anti-Asian violence, the murders are horrific, and my district in Oakland, California, the Bay Area, we`ve experienced much of this violence, unfortunately, not only recently but for years. And so it`s a very tough time.\par \par \uc2 And we have to unify around beating back this anti-hate speech which leads to violence. The bills are moving through Congress, and I`m really pleased that President Biden early on established the task force to really inform the legislation that`s taking place, the COVID-19 Hate Crimes Act. Representative Meng and Senator Hirono are putting forward would assign a point person at the Department of Justice to expedite the review of COVID- 19 related crimes.\par \par \uc2 Also, it provides, and we have to have this, support for local law enforcement agencies to respond to these hate crimes and provide resources for community-based organizations to provide the type of education and awareness and coordination with local and federal partners to mitigate against these racially discriminatory acts of violence, and also the language that`s being used to further this pandemic --\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEE: As well as the violence that`s connected to this.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Well, let me ask you on that point about language. Former President Trump used very incendiary language to talk about COVID-19. What role do you think that incendiary language played in what we`re seeing today in terms of violence and crimes against Asian Americans?\par \par \uc2 LEE: It played a very terribly important role in this. When you say -- use language like kung flu, China virus, when you speak hate, we have to remember hate speech leads to hate violence. And listening to this type of hate for the last four years, it didn`t just start recently, has put the Asian Pacific American community at risk. And we`re now seeing this unfortunate backlash and unfortunate embracing of Donald Trump`s overall agenda.\par \par \uc2 Remember, his agenda was based on white supremacy, and the roots of all of this are white supremacy. And so we have to come together like we`re going to do on March 26th and say stop the hate. No more. Communities of color all around the country, every community, is coming together to say enough is enough, we`re going to stop this, and we`re not going to allow this to happen anymore in our country.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Congresswoman, can I ask you about, over the weekend, one thing that our producers pointed out was the fact that there are volunteers in your community who are helping to walk elderly Asian American people to and from places they need to go because they face such violence to the point where, you know, people have been literally being killed, older Asian Americans. What is happening on the ground in your district that has illuminated this problem for you?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Sure. And people are afraid, first of all. Many members of my Asian Pacific American community are really worried about just going shopping, going to the grocery store, going to the banks. A 75-year-old man was attacked and was shoved to the ground.\par \par \uc2 He later died from brain injuries. It`s despicable what`s going on. And so we`re circling the wagons and we have to protect people who are vulnerable and who are frightened from leaving their homes. And we have to do this, all of us.\par \par \uc2 I don`t care what our backgrounds are. We have to come forward and we have to support the Asian Pacific American community in legislative ways where we tighten up all of the issues as it relates to the Department of Justice and hate crimes, but we also have to do this at the grassroots level, at the neighborhood level, and really help our neighbors and help people who really are fearful from leaving their homes because of the possible attacks which we see are just increasing throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 Again, going back to Donald Trump, hate speech leads to hate violence, and that`s what is taking place. So we`re circling the wagons and we`re going to fight and beat this back legislatively, but we`re also going to help our neighbors and our communities to survive through this.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, Congresswoman Barbara Lee, thank you very much for being on with us today. We really appreciate your insights.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to all of you for being with us this hour. I will be back here tomorrow with much more MEET THE PRESS DAILY, and you can also catch me weekday mornings on "WAY TOO EARLY" 5:00 a.m. ET.\par \par \uc2 Don`t go anywhere. MSNBC coverage continues with my friend Katy Tur right after a quick break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210322eh3m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for March 17, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Kristen Welker, Tom Winter, Blayne Alexander, Leigh  Ann Caldwell, Yamiche Alcindor, Nahid Bhadelia \par \uc2 8,432 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to a very busy Wednesday. It is MEET THE PRESS DAILY, and I`m Chuck Todd. In any moment we expect to hear from President Biden. He`s going to be speaking at the White House where we expect him to address the shooting in Atlanta where a lone gunman appears to have gone on a two-county rampage last night at area spas killing eight people, including six Asian women.\par \par \uc2 At a press conference this morning, authority said that the suspect, 21- year-old Robert Aaron Long has taken responsibility for the shootings, and that Long claimed to them that his actions were not racially motivated and they also caution that a determination is not been made about his motive here.\par \par \uc2 The local sheriff told reporters just a short time ago that the suspect says he has an issue with sex addiction. Authorities said long was apprehended last night after a brief manhunt. And it appears he was in route to Florida at the time possibly to carry out additional shootings.\par \par \uc2 When we hear President Biden address this incident he`ll be speaking at a virtual meeting with what is a tradition in this country on St. Patrick`s Day, Ireland`s Prime Minister. They`re going to be commemorating St. Patrick`s Day. Normally this event is held inside the White House. Obviously, for COVID reasons in travel restrictions, it is not being done this way, nor was it done that way last year.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Chief White House Correspondent Kristen Welker is standing by as we await remarks from President Biden. Tom Winter is covering the latest on the investigation. In just a moment, we`ll hear from him. Blayne Alexander is on the ground for us in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 So, Kristin, this is mostly a ceremonial event with the Irish Prime Minister. So, what do we expect to hear from the President?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think that we can expect to hear him address the shooting off of the top of this event. Chuck, I wouldn`t be surprised if that`s the first thing that we hear from President Biden. And he will likely echo what we heard from Vice President Harris earlier today in which she condemned hate crimes against Asian Americans.\par \par \uc2 She stressed the fact that there is still no known motive in this instance, Chuck. But she did make that point because these shootings and the victims of these shootings do include Asian Americans. And she reaffirmed the fact that this is an administration that condemns any behavior. And it comes in the wake of reports that there has been an uptick in anti-Asian sentiment.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that`s the type of tone that you expect -- can expect to hear from President Biden, Chuck, when he addresses this in a short time from now. And then I also think you`re going to hear him reaffirm the United States commitment to Ireland to its partners overseas, particularly coming in the wake of the Trump administration and trying to reset some of those critical relationships, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well -- and well, let`s not forget, President Biden talked about this violence and this xenophobia against Asian Americans in his primetime speech last week. Let me bring in Tom Winter for a little bit more in this investigation, because at the end of the day, while there are a lot of people are condemning the -- what looked to a lot of people like I hate crime, that is not necessarily where this investigation is at, Tom. So, walk us through where we`re at.\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, MSNBC INVESTIGATIONS CORRESPONDENT: Right, Chuck. And of course, it`s important to remember that this investigation is ongoing, and they will develop surely more evidence and speak to more people. But at this point, the investigation is guiding investigators along the lines of this is somebody who one, has admitted to the crime according to sheriffs and police officials in Georgia when they briefed us just several hours ago.\par \par \uc2 Two, this is an investigation that began shortly before 5:00 p.m. yesterday, so it`s not even 24 hours old. And according to statements that the suspect made, apparently, he`d been speaking almost immediately. He admitted to the crimes. And on top of that, he made statements that he had some form of a sex addiction.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, I`ve been trying to follow up and speak to law enforcement officials, OK, fine if he had that, and he wanted to "end his addiction" by committing these crimes, why not go after maybe a strip clubs in Atlanta or go after, you know, different types of stores that might sell pornography? Why attack these particular places, and obviously, predominantly, the victims here are Asian?\par \par \uc2 The idea is that he may have visited some of these or all of these spas in the past. And so, as a result, he may have been trying to "end his addiction by killing the people that might have been behind it in his mind." So, that`s something that they`re following up. That`s something that they`re continuing to track.\par \par \uc2 All of this, of course, occurs in the backdrop of an increase in Asian bias and Asian hate crime incidents that we`ve seen since March of last year. It`s something that we`ve been following closely. Something that law enforcement officials across the country have been paying attention too, particularly in large cities or areas with large concentrations of Asian communities. So, it`s something that is definitely worth following, an investigation that obviously is not yet complete.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, Tom, what do we -- do we know more about who this -- who this person is, the shooter, any of his -- did he -- did he have a job? Did he go to -- did he go to school? We know he`s a young adult. Do we know much about him beyond what authorities have said so far?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: So, you`re asking me what we refer to in law enforcement circles as suspect pedigree. As far as education, we don`t know much. We know he`s Robert Aaron Long. He`s 21 years of age from Woodstock, Georgia. He`s been charged with four counts of murder, one count of aggravated assault just in Cherokee County.\par \par \uc2 Remember, there`s a couple of different scenes as you alluded to chuck at the beginning of the show, so we expect more charges from additional counties to be brought in this case. Those charges, as of within the last hour, when I got off the phone with the clerk`s office in Cherokee County, those charges are still under seal. So, presumably, some more background information is still contained in those filings that we can`t see yet.\par \par \uc2 We do know from the police briefings and separately from conversations I`ve had with law enforcement that his parents appear to have been very much aware of the problem, that they were instrumental in leading to his arrest, providing information that led to his arrest yesterday and helpful in that regard.\par \par \uc2 So, obviously, this idea in his troubled background, this is not the first time that this has come up. Obviously, his parents are well aware of it according to law enforcement. But as far as those particulars, job, background, any prior history of domestic violence against women, I`m curious about that. That`s information that would be great to get. All of those things are still a little bit unknown here less than 24 hours after this started.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tom Winter with what he knows from his law enforcement sources, Tom, thanks very much. Let`s move to Blayne Alexander who`s down in Atlanta. And Blaine, I know we`ve been hearing from some law enforcement and obviously, what -- where the -- where they thought this investigation might be headed. It seems to be moving in a different direction. But what can you tell us from your perspective on the ground?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chuck, I want to start with one of the things that Tom mentioned. The fact that this is -- this was carried out over a number of different crime scenes. So, just to kind of give just a quick lay of the land, there are a lot of moving pieces here in Atlanta and outside of Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Where I`m standing right now, I`m in front of the Gold Spa. This was one of the three spots that was targeted. The other one is just across the street, just literally a few feet away, so those two were in very close proximity. But the shootings actually happened, the first of the three happened about 45 minutes to the north of where I am just outside of Atlanta, a city called Acworth. That`s where the first spot was targeted. That`s where four people were killed, another person was shot and is injured.\par \par \uc2 So, we understand that if you just kind of look at how quickly all of this played out, all of this was over the course of just about an hour or so. The first calls up there came in at about 5:00 p.m. And as officials were working on that, they got a call here in Atlanta saying that, hey, there`s been a shooting here. While they were in one location, they were getting called about shots fired across the street.\par \par \uc2 Now, those are the three locations. He was ultimately apprehended about two hours to the south of where I`m standing right now. So, we`re talking about multiple different jurisdictions all of them kind of working in a coordinated effort to bring this guy into custody relatively quickly.\par \par \uc2 And that`s notable. Chuck, because of course, he did tell law enforcement that he was on his way to Florida. He was driving down 75. He was planning to go to Florida, and he says commit the same type of similar heinous act. So, the fact that they were able to work so quickly to get him into custody, certainly, very likely saved some lives.\par \par \uc2 We`re also learning a lot about though, the people who were killed. We`re talking about eight individuals who died during this kind of spree last night, six of them Asian women. The other two victims, seem a little bit more about these individuals. We also know that there was one stable condition currently in the hospital, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I`m curious, Blayne. I mean, what kind of -- do they -- do they feel like they have all the footage at the crime or are there cameras all over these places? They know exactly this guy`s movement at every one of these places at this point?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Well, that was part of the reason they were able to apprehend him so quickly, Chuck. I mean, I the shooting, we came -- became aware of the shooting yesterday. And it was very quickly after that, that we got that surveillance photo, that we were able to see pictures of the shooter, pictures of his car, pictures of all of these things that ultimately led to his apprehension very quickly.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Atlanta Police\uc2  have said that, yes, they knew that it was connected because they were able to match those images with the surveillance that was taken down here. So, I do think that that kind of speaks to just how quickly they were able to get him into custody.\par \par \uc2 There were a lot of surveillance cameras and photos. And then, of course, as we spoke about before, the fact that his family was cooperative in this. You know, law enforcement officials made that a very big point to talk about that fact when they were talking in news conference earlier today as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. One wonders how much more we`re going to learn -- how much more we`re going to learn about what his family knew about these problems going forward. Anyway, Kristen, Tom, and Blayne, thank you all for getting us started on these tragic stories.\par \par \uc2 We said we expect President Biden will comment on the shooting soon. And when the event with the Prime Minister of Ireland begins, we`ll bring that to you when it happens.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the growing issues of the border are taking center stage in Capitol Hill where President Biden`s Homeland Security chief is facing questions over the administration`s handling of what is turning into a humanitarian emergency. But first, as we go to break, another look at life after lockdown.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Right now, Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas continues to be grilled by House Republicans over the administration`s handling of the humanitarian emergency at the border.\par \par \uc2 Mayorkas has been defiant in this virtual hearing that this administration is not willing to resort to the kind of hardline and what they call inhumane policies of the last administration even as border crossings surge and federal facilities begin to be taxed beyond their capacity.\par \par \uc2 Mayorkas has been repeatedly pressed by Republicans to ramp up the administration`s efforts to deter migrants from making the journey and he has been pressed to call the situation of crisis. But he has made clear to those lawmakers at this hearing that there are moral and ethical lines that this administration is simply unwilling to cross.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Sometimes the tools deterrence defy values and principles for which we all stand. And one of those tools of deterrence that the Trump administration employed was deplorable and absolutely unacceptable. And if we want to speak of language, then let me speak of language.\par \par \uc2 A crisis is when a nation is willing to rip a nine-year-old child out of the hands of his or her parent, and separate that family to deter future migration. That, to me, is a humanitarian crisis. And what the President has committed to, and what I am committed to and execute is to ensure that we have an immigration system that works and that migration to our country is safe, orderly, and humane.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Republicans, of course, say that the administration`s humane approach is what itself is unsustainable and that it is fueling a dangerous surge and crossings amid a global pandemic. They also argue that the President`s approach is playing into the hands of cartels who are using his softer rhetoric to falsely claim that the border is open.\par \par \uc2 In an interview that aired this morning, President Biden was pressed about whether the administration needs to take a more forceful approach.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you have to say quite clearly don`t come?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes, I can say quite clearly don`t come while we`re in a process of getting set up, and it`s not going to take a whole long time, is to be able to apply for asylum in place. So, don`t leave your town or city or community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: NBC`s Leigh Ann Caldwell is on Capitol Hill. Morgan Chesky is outside the Dallas Convention Center which is opening its doors to serve as a temporary FEMA shelter for unaccompanied minors encountered at the southern border. And also with us is Yamiche Alcindor, White House Correspondent for PBS News Hour and MSNBC Contributor.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Morgan, I want to start with you because I`m curious, what are you seeing there now? Is it being prepared or is it already being used?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chuck, great question. It`s our understanding that right now it is still being prepared. I had a chance to speak with some Red Cross volunteers who were very tight-lipped, but they`ve been seen walking in and out of the building behind me, that \uc2 HHS\uc2  says could accept those at least first few 100 of migrant teens on or around today.\par \par \uc2 Now, we don`t know when their exact arrival will be. We do know that they could house several thousand, up to 3,000 migrant teenagers here specifically between the ages of 15 and 17. And when I had a chance to speak to an immigration attorney about what the conditions will be like inside since we`re not allowed in to take a look ourselves, he likened it to what you would expect at a hurricane relief center, pots, socially distance, spread apart.\par \par \uc2 He said that the teams will be provided classwork here, some sort of education. They`ll obviously have cleaner conditions than what he believes they`re subjected to closer to the border because they simply have this space here that those centers do not have further south, closer to the border that have simply been overrun due to this recent uptick\par \par \uc2 Now, we also listening in to Governor Greg Abbott who`s speaking right now just across the street from where I`m standing. And he`s calling on the Biden administration to please provide some more clarification, saying that they haven`t given at least him answers on perhaps how they would treat some of these teenagers should they become infected with COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 He was saying about for the past week now that he`s concerned that the teenagers that are coming across, all migrants for that matter, present the risk of potentially infecting Texas with these variants of COVID-19. I presented that to an immigration attorney who`s closely following this situation and here`s what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Governor Greg Abbott has said publicly, he`s concerned immigrants are bringing COVID-19 to Texas.\par \par \uc2 FRANCISCO DUBOVE, IMMIGRATION ATTORNEY: Governor Abbott just dropped the mask mandate without any scientific or medical basis. And now he wants to try to blame the spread of COVID on these kids. That is gutless and cowardly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Now, as far as how long this center will be utilized, \uc2 HHS\uc2  officials say that they have up to 90 days to use this space here at the Kay Bailey Hutchison Convention Center. At that point, they can either renew that lease, but the immigration attorney you just heard tells me that hopefully within that amount of time, three months, which is still a long time, it can connect these teenagers with foster families or with loved ones.\par \par \uc2 However, that process, the vetting, of course, is not exactly easy. And he said that typically it would take \uc2 HHS\uc2  about 60 days to do so. He thinks that they`re adding an extra 30 days on top of that simply because these numbers just keep going up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Morgan, are they going to test these kids when they bring them in? I mean, I assume they`ll have COVID testing kits. Are they? Are they going to at least do that?\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: We have reached out to the federal officials with \uc2 HHS\uc2  here to ask some specific questions. We`ve only been given a statement saying that, you know, health protocols will be followed, nothing further on that. It is important to note that in our conversations with city officials here, they`ve deferred completely to \uc2 HHS\uc2  saying that they are the sole organization -- agency, rather, responsible for running this and will be the only point of contact from this point forward. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Morgan Chesky outside the Dallas Convention Center, Morgan, thank you. Let me bring in Leigh Ann and Yamiche on this. And Leigh Ann, let me start with you on what Secretary Mayorkas is dealing with.\par \par \uc2 How much -- is there -- is there any constructive criticism coming from the right or is it -- is it all sort of -- just sort of talking points and press releases so they can look like they`re being tough? But is there any sort of constructive like, hey, why don`t we solve the problem like this? Any hint of that?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: So, Chuck, that`s interesting you asked that because when I was listening to the hearing, that`s what I was wondering if there were going to be some new ideas or proposals that we would hear that could perhaps lead to some sort of solution. But we`re not really hearing that not really from either side, actually.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are saying that they did support and they still do support President Trump`s Remain in Mexico policy. They`re saying that that worked. So, they`re pressing Mayorkas to perhaps continue that. But we know that is not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is that this is a political problem for Democrats if you just want to talk about the politics of it. The policy of courses also very problematic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Leigh Ann, I`m going to pause you here. I promise --\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leigh Ann, I am sorry. We got no warning. I got to pause you. The President is speaking. We`re going to go to him right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s good. Good to have you on television. But next year in Washington, next year in Washington. For years, as you know, we celebrated this St. Patrick`s Day. I always put on a breakfast at my home at the Vice President`s residence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, in fairness, it turns out, they gave us no warning, the folks at the White House, we`re going to replay his comments on Atlanta, so give us a few minutes on that. So Leigh Ann and Yamiche, I want to pick up our conversation and focus back on immigration. And again, we will bring you the President`s remarks in a few minutes and what he had to say about the shootings in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 But Leigh Ann, finish your comment there about sort of is there -- you know, I think you were picking up on the political problem this has turned into for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes, it was. Of course, there`s the policy, that`s an issue, but also the politics of it as well. And Democrats know that this is a problem. This is a crisis that the President is having to deal with very early on into his administration. It was immediate. And so, you can see the sense of change that is the optics of what people are thinking across the border regarding immigration.\par \par \uc2 But the House is voting on two immigration bills this week, the DREAM Act and the Ag Worker Bill. None of those deal with what is happening on the border. Those are Democratic priorities, but they don`t address the situation. So, there`s no solutions at this point, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yamiche, I want to play something Joe Biden said to George Stephanopoulos in the interview this morning about this idea that just simply his election, in some ways, has kicked off this mini-surge of sorts. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: First of all, the idea that Joe Biden said come because I heard the other day that they`re coming because they know I`m a nice guy and I won`t do what Trump did.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANOPOULOS: They`re saying this.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes. Well, here`s the deal. They`re not. The adults are being sent back, number one. Number two, what do you do with an unaccompanied child that comes to the border? Do you repeat what Trump did? Take them from their mothers, move them away, hold them in cells, etcetera? We`re not doing that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Yamiche, the question is, what do they do? Because it does seem as if many, many folks have decided, hey, I got a better shot at saving my kid from this horrible life. I`m going to send him on his own because he can least get in. Maybe it only be temporary, maybe he can get to my relative, and maybe this is the way. Is there a point where the administration is going to have to figure out how to deter that as well?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, they`re trying to deter that right now. But the tough spot the Biden administration finds itself in is because President Biden has been very vocal that he`s going to be more humane. He changed this policy so that unaccompanied minors are the only ones who can come to the border without legal status and be let into the United States.\par \par \uc2 And then you have this real narrative that I`ve heard from immigrants myself that I`ve interviewed, who say they see President Biden as more leaning. I had -- I had an immigrant tell me -- an immigrant mother tell me, I see President Biden more as a father than as a president. I had someone else say, I think he has a bigger heart than President Trump. And now is the time to really come.\par \par \uc2 That`s the message that they`re trying to fight back against. And experts tell me and lawyers tell me that even though President Biden is continuously saying, don`t come, don`t come, his actions are speaking louder than his words. And the actions is that he is treating these children in a way that President Trump did not, which is that he`s not throwing them back into Mexico and saying fend for yourself even if you`re nine, or 10, or even an infant.\par \par \uc2 Instead, that he`s saying, welcome, come in, we`re going to put you in these facilities, we`re going to find something to do. And there has to be this -- there`s a real tough line here that they`re trying to do that, especially, of course, President Biden himself saying, this is a spike that happens. But this is this like that`s happening based also on what he himself has said. But they`re going to have to figure out how to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 I`m not seeing a way out for them in some ways when it comes to the numbers of people that are coming because they feel more comfortable with this administration. I will say that there is, of course, this sense that they`re hearing a lot of critics with no solutions. That`s what the White House Press Secretary said to me when I pushed her on this idea that how can we in America even for one day, have children sleeping on the floor and not getting sunlight?\par \par \uc2 She said, we`re getting a lot of critics, not a lot of solutions. Secretary Mayorkas essentially echoed that saying we`re trying to figure this out, trying to wrap our heads around this problem, but it`s a big one.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leigh Ann, what kind of room on the Hill on the left will Biden have? You know, for instance, it seems like one -- they want to -- if the goal is to move the asylum process to in country, then one way to send that message is you send these kids back on a plane to be processed in country.\par \par \uc2 Now, if they do that, what -- is there -- if that ends up being a temporary solution, will the -- will the left go bonkers on this or not?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: That`s a really good question. Democrats are trying to figure out how to move forward themselves on immigration. While this is happening behind the scenes, they`re also debating and discussing among themselves about some comprehensive immigration policy that they cannot get on the same page on.\par \par \uc2 Of course, that deals with a lot of issues, but the asylum issue, the refugee issue are two of those issues as part of that legislation. And that is something that the progressives have positions on, the moderates. And it`s a broad swath of where Democrats stand on so much of this.\par \par \uc2 So, immigration is not just a wedge issue between Republicans and Democrats, it`s also become a very difficult issue for Democrats themselves internally as a family. And what`s happening on the border is only making the conversation more fraught, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Yamiche, I will say this. The Biden administration, while there -- look, there`s been some media restrictions trying to figure out what Border Patrol is doing and with more information on these facilities. On the other hand, the White House isn`t pushing back that this shouldn`t be our focus. So, I do find it -- they do seem to at least accept the premise that this is a problem.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: That`s right. The White House and White House officials both on background and when you talk them off the record, or when they`re publicly speaking, they all do admit that there, of course, is a challenge at the border. The numbers speak for themselves. We`re seeing more and more unaccompanied children come over our borders in dire straits and saying, we need help.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is a challenge that they can`t have -- the challenge that they can`t deny. Secretary Mayorkas was asked specifically today, why can`t you use the word crisis? And he said, I don`t want to focus on the language. I want to focus on our border operations and how to fix the situation.\par \par \uc2 So, whether you call it a crisis, whether you call it a challenge, the problem is, it`s a big problem that they`re going to have to fix. Republicans have a sort of dignity act that`s floating out there. It`s already got a lot of non-starters that Democrats are not going to want to get behind.\par \par \uc2 I think immigration is not only a political wedge issue, I think it`s a political weapon, especially on the Republican side, because they`re wielding this against Democrats saying, we want to make sure we keep a focus on this because this is a problem that Biden can`t simply solve with an executive order.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Leigh Ann, and that brings me -- is there any group of Republicans that could -- that could be part of this solution? Because it certainly looks like at least particularly in the House side, but even I think you have more and more senators, it looks like they would prefer to have the issue since it`s the only thing that unites this party these days.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: -- to be what Republicans would say about Democrats, they don`t really want to solve the immigration problem because Republicans would say they have -- keeping the hope out there enables Latinos to vote for Democrats is what Republicans would say but now the issue seems to be flipped.\par \par \uc2 But there are a few Republicans who say that they`re trying to come up with solutions to the issue. Just this morning, there was a press conference with a freshman Republican from Miami, Representative Salazar, and she says that she wants to be that person to come up with some solutions that do solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 Senator Lindsey Graham was supposed to have joined that press conference. But Senator Graham also just told reporters a couple days ago that he doesn`t think even the Dream Act is something that could come up in the Senate because of what is happening at the border, and that no one that he thinks is willing to even discuss the issue of immigration on the Republican side at this point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I heard that comment. There`s no logic to that comment. I understand the political emotions of the moment, but there`s actually no logic. It makes no sense on any level. You know, I`m not going to pay my bills because I have a crisis. I`m not going to do -- I mean, it`s sort of a bizarre thought process that only seems to reinforce the idea that this is a political decision, not a policy decision.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leigh Ann Caldwell, Yamiche Alcindor, thank you both for the nuance and reporting on these issues.\par \par \uc2 After the break, we`re going to get you those comments from President Biden on Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: As we promised, let`s play some of what President Biden said a few moments ago. Our TV pool that covers the president ended up missing the very top of his remarks. The president got started pretty quick when they did the pool call. But he began by saying that he was just on the phone, being briefed by his attorney general and his FBI director about the shooting in Atlanta, and that the investigation is ongoing, and a motive is still to be determined. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Speaking about the brutality against Asian-Americans for the last couple months, and I think it`s -- it is very, very troublesome. And -- but I`m making no connection at this moment to the motivation of the killer. I`m waiting for an answer from -- as the investigation proceeds, from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and from the Justice Department. So -- and that`s -- so I`ll have more to say when the investigation is complete.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, we`re going to talk more about the political battle over the border with a member of Congress who represents the border, Texas Congressman Henry Cuellar joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, DHS SECRETARY: The bottom line is, and this is something about which we all agree, the immigration system is broken. And it is in need of legislative reform. Hopefully, this year, and I am confident and optimistic that we will actually begin, once and for all, to fix a system that everyone agrees is broken.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas saying what Congress has known to be true for decades, the immigration system is broken and in need of a legislative fix. But while the secretary claims to be optimistic for some sort of grand bargain, recent history shows that is a lot easier said than done.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is a member of Congress, would likely be part of any compromise for a bill about the border, in particular, it`s Texas Democratic Congressman Henry Cuellar.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, it`s good to have you on here. And I -- let me start with a response. Look, Secretary Mayorkas is I guess supposed to sound hopeful. He`s only been in his job a couple of months. But the Congress that I look at here and the way that an immigration bill would have to pass, tell me how there is going to be a bipartisan atmosphere to deal with this issue.\par \par \uc2 REP. HENRY CUELLAR (D-TX): You`re absolutely right. I mean, it`s going to be difficult. I mean, I want to see full, comprehensive immigration reform. If you notice, that`s not on the floor this week. We have a Dream Act and we have a guest worker plan. But we`re hopeful that at least a piecemeal approach will get us where we need to get. Pass it from the House, and then we`ve got to worry about how do we get 60 votes, 10 Republicans in the Senate. So your analysis is on point.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s start with, look, there`s so many -- a comprehensive bill, it used to be, when I first started covering Washington, if you made the bill bigger, you had a better chance of it passing. We know that is not the case here. So if you`re going to slice the immigration bill as it is, what would be the priority pieces that you think are the most achievable now, and the most necessary, in order to at least deal with the situation at the border?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Well, I mean, the Dream Act is one to take care of those 800 plus kids that are here that we need to take care of. Those are people following the law, have not -- you know, working, going to school. Those are the folks that we need here. But I think one of the ways to address the issue at the border is, how do we get people that can come in, work, and they can go back again and send money back over there?\par \par \uc2 That`s where a guest worker plan would be good because then the people that want to come work will follow the law, and then the ones that are coming in for other reasons that Border Patrol can focus. And that actually will help us secure the border if we do a (speaking foreign language) program like we had after World War II.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So I had a good conversation both on this show a couple days ago and on my podcast today with Maria Teresa Kumar and a former colleague of yours, Republican congressman, former Republican Congressman Carlos Curbelo, and they both pointed to the same country as the blueprint of how America can help pull a country out of the depths.\par \par \uc2 And that is Colombia. And the point being is, can we do for Honduras, El Salvador, Guatemala, with investments, with help, can you convince your colleagues that investment in those three countries can do for our relationship what Colombia, which is now a shining example and a really good ally of the United States?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Yes, I agree. I mean, Colombia is a very good example of who happens when they work with the United States to invest. Not only for their military and security, but also the trade agreements and the private interactions that we have, the business, interconnection, that`s important. We can do the same thing.\par \par \uc2 I was involved in 2014, 2015 when Vice President Biden at that time, Kay Granger, myself on the House Appropriations, we added the first $750 million. So we`ve added millions and millions of money down there in South America, in Central America, but we still haven`t seen the results. I know that President Trump cut some of the funding, but we still got to see results. It`s not only the assistance we`re providing, but we need the private sector to create job down there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what you have been a bit critical of the administration a bit in simply saying that -- and I`m curious, is it more of -- was it a failure to imagine what was going to happen? Is it how they`re -- what specifically would you like to see them do differently than they`re doing right now?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: First of all, within the week, one week of the inauguration, I was calling the White House already, my contact, and saying hey, I want you to please see what`s happening on the ground. This was back in January. I was telling the White House folks, you`ve got to understand what I`m seeing here. I`m seeing the numbers because I live at the border. I don`t just go visit the border, I live there. I go there right after my votes in D.C.\par \par \uc2 And so I`ve been warning the administration. There`s a couple of things we need to do. First, we`ve got to understand that there`s three messages. The administration`s message has to be better. You can`t say, don`t come now, come later. That`s a bad message. It`s got to be clear and concise. Don`t come in here illegally. That should be the message. Message number two is what I call the networks of friends and family.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Pedro, I was able to come over. You ought to come over. And they see images like what you`re playing right now. People are coming across. So of course, they`re going to come in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Third message, real quickly, the criminal organizations. That is a powerful message because they charge an average of $6,000. Imagine if we had 100,000 people that crossed in February. Do the math. That`s $600 million that they made. So they got an incentive to recruit aggressively.\par \par \uc2 I just got off the phone with somebody in Mexico, and they went over some things. It`s exactly what I`m telling you. We`ve got to go after the -- cut off the money that are going to the drug cartels.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Otherwise, we feed them and feed them and we feed them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If -- so, look, President Biden has said, if an unaccompanied minor comes in this country, we`re not sending them back, we`re not doing these things. How would you deal with this situation? Because it certainly looks like many -- and I get it, people are desperate, and they think, you know what, I`m better off just sending my young teenage kid on his own. Maybe if there`s a group of them, they can get in.\par \par \uc2 I empathize with the desperation, so I want to preface my question there. But since it`s clear that this is happening, where do you -- what should we do with these kids? Should we place them in the United States, or do you think we have process them in country?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Well, you know, first of all, the ones who are already in the pipeline or are coming over here or are here, we take care of them like they`re our kids. Absolutely. We take care of them. We have to do a better job at moving them out of the border post stations and taking care of them with Health and Human Services. Number one. Number two, if we can go back and try to process them before they come over because when they come over through a dangerous country or countries, we know that the only ones that win are the bad guys. They make money off of them.\par \par \uc2 And you know very well, Chuck, that some of them are abused on the way up here. And the other thing is, what we`ve got to make sure that we understand is we`ve got to make sure that we address the pull factors and the push factors. If you have somebody on your show, you can tell who is a Democrat and who is a Republican. Democrats talk about the push factors only. Republicans talk only about the pull factors. We have to address both of them. Both of the factors.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, it`s true. It`s true. It is -- the only time you get it in stereo is if you have one on each side, and then you hear both parts of this conversation. Yes, border security seems to be two words a lot of Democrats are afraid to talk about.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Congressman Henry Cuellar, Democrat who represents Texas down there at the border, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, a reality check on our virus situation amid growing uncertainty about the trajectory of the pandemic here. Especially because cases are surging across the pond in Europe.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: My concern is that we prematurely pull back and don`t give the vaccines time to continue to protect the country. With regard to the future and your question that you asked, my concern is that we don`t have the corporate memory of lessons learned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was Dr. Fauci cautioning against opening up too soon as he testified today before Congress. Taking a look at where we stand on the numbers, here`s we stand on the virus. More than 27 percent of adults in the United States have received at least one shot, so that`s good news. But nationally our average number of new cases now up 12 percent from last week. And over the past two weeks, some states are reporting significant upticks in cases, including Michigan, which is up over 55 percent.\par \par \uc2 So let`s bring in Dr. Nahid Bhadelia, infectious disease physician, associate professor at Boston University School of Medicine, and of course an MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bhadelia, we`ve been saying it all week. It feels like a deja vu. In fact I had to remind -- I`m reminding viewers yesterday when I reported on the lockdown in Italy, and the surges in Europe, that this was still March of 20221, not March of 2020.\par \par \uc2 Where`s your level of concern right now? Are you -- you have Michael Osterholm who is I think pretty alarmed at what things are. You`ve got others. Dr. Jha who`s been -- who`s concerned but feels like we have it under control. Where are you?\par \par \uc2 DR. NAHID BHADELIA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: I think I`m somewhere in the middle, Chuck. I think, you know, you and I have talked in the past about how this is a race between variants and the vaccines. And what you`re seeing in Europe, and you`ve discussed this earlier this week, is that leaving out the U.K., many of the countries are having to roll back and consider even localized lockdowns because their cases have gone up.\par \par \uc2 And what we have in our advantage and this is why I`m not quite at the level that Dr. Osterholm is that we are distributing more vaccines than most of those countries per 100 people in this country. But we`re also behind, and then also we have in our favor that those countries seem to have much more of the b.1.1.7 variant, the one that seems to be more transmissible, the one that we know has a greater mortality.\par \par \uc2 But we are on our road to giving that variant the chance to keep growing before we get to a point where more of us are protected, so my level of concern is if we don`t get through all the folks who are medically high risk, who are going to end up in the hospital, who can potentially die from this disease before we open up and let our guard down, then we are -- we`re just giving away all the advantages that we have compared to those countries in Europe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it feels like we`re on a pace that, OK, getting all adults by the end of May, let`s say we are able to do that pace, the way this b.1.1.7 variant is moving is more lethal. I mean, do you expect it to be the dominant strain by April?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Yes, I mean, I think you`re already seeing that some states are hitting about, you know, the 50 percent mark of where it potentially may have an impact on the spread. I think it depends on our actions between now and then. I would not be surprised if it becomes the predominant strain. Now what you might see if we get through vaccinating a lot more of us is that there might be a disconnect between cases, hospitalizations and deaths.\par \par \uc2 We`ve always said, you know, getting sick with this disease, even if you don`t get hospitalized, there are other aspects that are, you know, bad versus considering things like (INAUDIBLE) COVID, but if we get everybody vaccinated, at least we have that resilience against getting more people in the hospitals, getting more people who die of this disease. And that`s why I think that even if the number of cases go up, at least if we get through enough of us vaccinated, we may not have more severe impact, at least we`ll make an impact.\par \par \uc2 So what IHME says is if you can hold on to those masks, right, 95 percent mask compliance between now and July 1st, just that simple measure we can save 14,000 more people between now and then. It`s within our power. So get your shot, wear your mask until more of us are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. At today`s hearing, Dr. Bhadelia, Dr. Fauci said high schoolers, he imagined high schoolers may be able to get the vaccine by the fall. Other kids by the first quarter of next year. I`m just curious that the high schooler, that feels late. Is there any reason why high schoolers shouldn`t be able to get vaccinated by the end of June, and at least down to 16?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Well, Moderna currently is available for everybody -- sorry -- \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is actually available for everybody over 16, and so you have that. Moderna is doing their study. It kind of depends on how long it takes for them to get through their enrollment, unless, say, they get through their pediatric involvement and have results by the end of summer, yes, you could potentially see earlier vaccinations of children. So it depends on the age of your high schooler I think particularly if you`re going to be able to get access to a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Currently for those younger than 16, I think you`re going to have to wait until we get results from that Moderna as well as \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. Nahid Bhadelia, \uc2 Boston University\uc2 , always good to have your perspective and expertise on with us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210317eh3h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for March 15, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Kasie Hunt, Garrett Haake, Dasha Burns, Kelly O`Donnell, Kathy  Park, Deepa Shivaram, Vin Gupta \par \uc2 8,062 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KASIE HUNT, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): If it`s Monday, the Biden administration surges resources to the southern border as it confronts a growing humanitarian emergency and tries to navigate an issue that Republicans are seizing on.\par \par \uc2 Plus, President Biden is about to speak at the White House and Vice President Harris hits Las Vegas as the White House kicks off a major week of events rolling out its massive $1.9 trillion COVID rescue bill.\par \par \uc2 And Governor Andrew Cuomo has lost the confidence of Democrats in Albany and Washington as the allegations, the denials, and the calls for his resignation continue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): Welcome to Monday. It`s MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Kasie Hunt in for Chuck Todd. As the Biden administration looks to tout the success of its crisis response to COVID, it`s facing the emergence of a very different but also urgent crisis.\par \par \uc2 Due to a surge in migrant crossings and a surge particularly in unaccompanied migrant children, the White House is right now trying to surge resources to the southern border as federal facilities are being overwhelmed. They`re deploying FEMA and they`re building temporary shelters, but they`re housing migrant children for longer than the law allows. And they`re also facing difficult questions about how much their border and immigration policies might be contributing to the problem.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the White House`s coordinator for the border acknowledged that the surge might be a response to their softening of Trump`s hardline, and in some cases inhumane immigration policies. But she and other White House officials have emphasized "the border is not open." But as a New York Times notes, migrants hopes have been drummed up by human smugglers who promise that President Biden`s administration will welcome them.\par \par \uc2 Right now, House Republicans are about to hold a press conference in El Paso as they look to seize on the crisis politically. Immigration, of course, an issue that unites their party and tends to fire up their base. Amid the turmoil, House Democrats are also pushing a pair of immigration bills this week as progressive Democrats try to shape the White House`s agenda in its image.\par \par \uc2 And the current administration, they`re facing an uncomfortable reality. What`s happening at the southern border represents one of the most difficult emergency situations to navigate for any new administration because there are no easy answers politically, morally, legally or legislatively.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Garrett Haake and Dasha Burns are both following this story from the Texas border. Garrett is in El Paso where Republicans are holding a press conference. Dasha is outside of migrant detention facility in Donna, Texas. And I`m also joined by NBC News White House Correspondent Kelly O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to see you all. Garrett, let me start with you because we`re waiting on this press conference from House Republicans. What have you seen so far as you`ve been down there at the border in El Paso? And what are you expecting to hear from Republican leaders today?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, on the ground in El Paso, the reality of migration is well known. And this is a recurring issue. It`s a seasonal issue. You see more and more people start to come every year as the spring and summer began. Although the numbers, as you pointed out, particularly of unaccompanied minors are already on a track where we`re ahead of where we should be for February and March suggesting that this problem could get worse and worse into the spring and summer months.\par \par \uc2 And so, people here are, I think, relieved that FEMA is now getting involved, kind of the emergency disaster specialists, the logistics specialists for the federal government, who can help alleviate some of this problem.\par \par \uc2 On the politics end of it, on the surface, this is easy to understand why Republicans are coming after this issue. This is something that they feel like the Biden administration has made meaningful changes to immigration policy. They feel as though they can point to that and unite their own voters around an issue on which they`re very comfortable.\par \par \uc2 And the problem, I think, for Republicans is prescriptive. Beyond improving border security, what else can they offer here? What do they have as an answer for the humanitarian problems presented if you don`t allow these unaccompanied children into the country? Are they just supposed to wait in the desert on the other side of the fence until the pandemic is over?\par \par \uc2 If they are coming, what do you do about it? And I think, you know, when you are in the minority, and you can sort of say it`s up to the other side to govern, that`s part of it. But I think that`s the difference here. That`s what I would like to press Kevin McCarthy on is if you think this is a crisis, if this really is a humanitarian problem, what are we as a United States of American government prepared to do to solve it? And that`s what there`s just frankly not a lot of good answers for yet.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: It`s an incredibly thorny and difficult problem that so many administrations have had to grapple with. Garrett, what`s your take on the Democrats` approach to this in Congress in particular? I mean, this was tricky terrain for Democrats to talk about and to try and legislate around. Obviously, that took a dramatic shift under the hardline policies of the Trump administration, but it`s still difficult for them as well.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes. I mean, look, the House is voting this week on to immigration measures that were essentially broken off from the broader Biden administration`s comprehensive plan. They deal with DREAMers and they deal with farmworkers. And neither one of them would deal with this issue at all.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s no obvious kind of easy legislative solution to the problem of these particularly Central American, younger people working their way up through Mexico and coming here, or this would have been solved in the Bush or Obama administrations when these problems were first starting to creep up.\par \par \uc2 So, legislatively, it is a massive problem, because you have to deal not just with the pull factors from the U.S., which is what House Republicans accused Democrats of doing right, essentially making the U.S. more inviting, the idea that our policies have changed here, so more people will want to come here specifically.\par \par \uc2 But you also have to deal with the push factors, these incredible crushing poverty, the damaging hurricanes, all of the things that have hit the Central American countries so hard to give these -- the people there such a sense of desperation that they have to go somewhere. And solving both of those problems in tandem has proven too big for Congress`s going back decades.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, Garrett, standby for me. We see that press conference behind you, so we`ll let you go make sure you don`t miss any of that and bring back anything. If you do get some news for us, we`d be happy to come back to you.\par \par \uc2 Dasha Burns, let me talk to you about what you`re seeing from -- at one of these facilities. Because of course, the difficult reality for these children, there are in theory rules about how long they are supposed to be held, that they`re not being -- we`re not currently able to follow because we don`t have the kind of resources or we also have concerns about letting them go into the country potentially into the hands of people who might hurt them instead of people who are going to take care of them.\par \par \uc2 So, as you have conversations and see what`s going on what`s unfolding there?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC REPORTER: Yes, Kasie, as you heard from Garrett there, this is a real test and challenge for the Biden administration. They say they want to create a more humane immigration policy, but the reality is here on the ground that we`re hearing from the sources here is that they`re still pretty far from that, especially in places like this facility behind me here.\par \par \uc2 And the reality right now is, Kasie, we don`t know a whole lot about what`s going on in there. We have asked for access. We have not been granted that yet. CBP put out some images of the facility before there were people in it while it was under construction. So, we don`t know what it actually looks like right now.\par \par \uc2 We do know that it was set up to be -- to house around 250 people, but I`ve talked to the lawyers who have talked to children inside of this facility and they say there are a whole lot more people inside this facility than that right now. And that`s really where we`re getting a lot of our information is the lawyers who have been talking to these children. They say that they are all very distressed, they`re upset.\par \par \uc2 The woman I spoke with met with children as young as one to 17 years old. She says, unanimously they were freaked out. They were hearing children cry. They weren`t allowed to leave their partition for most of the day. They were getting limited access to showers and to hygiene.\par \par \uc2 I also spoke to the mother of a four-year-old girl who was detained here. She came to the border with her aunt, was separated from her aunt there. Her aunt was not allowed to enter the country. She was detained here, then sent to foster care although her mother and father are actually in the United States here. And thankfully, she was able to connect with a lawyer who is able to advocate on her behalf.\par \par \uc2 But as I heard from that lawyer, that is a very unique case. Most people don`t have access to something like that. And the stories that I heard from the mother of hearing her daughter on the phone, they got one phone call, hearing her daughter crying, scared, confused, and then seeing her in person and seeing that change within her.\par \par \uc2 She was terrified to be left alone. She said she was lonely. She was scared. And look, Kasie, the reality is nobody thinks this is good. You talk to advocates, you talk to Democrats, you talk to Republicans, nobody wants to see this. These are not facilities meant for children. These are meant for single men and they are meant to be very temporary, 72 hours. But a lot of kids are being held significantly longer than that at this point.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 HHS\uc2 , they`re supposed to be taking these kids from the border patrol facilities, putting them in more appropriate facilities that do have access to education, to outdoor space, places where kids can actually safely be. But they`re overwhelmed right now. And they`re not able to keep up with these numbers.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why the announcement about FEMA and others coming to help out here is huge. But right now, it`s a tough situation, not a lot of great solutions in the short term here, Kasie.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Right. Well, and of course, the people charged with trying to find solutions at the White House, Kelly O`Donnell, there has been a lot of back and forth. And obviously, this is a problem that the Biden administration inherited from the Trump administration. I want to show everyone a little bit of what the White House Chief of Staff Ron Klain had to say about this, and then we`ll talk about it. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): The Biden administration is trying to fix the broken system that was left to them by the Trump Administration.\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: We`re working very, very hard on this problem. We inherited a real lack of capacity. It`s hard to ramp up that capacity. You can`t just take children and put them in a hotel room. They need to be in a place where they are safely protected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, Kelly, I mean, you covered all of the Trump administration and are now covering the Biden administration. What is it exactly that they say was broken? And obviously, we`ve seen all of the horrible images. We know about the zero-tolerance policy. But the capacity here, this was in some ways predictable that we would see a surge like this. What work is being done behind the scenes by the Biden administration to try and address this as quickly as possible?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, on top of all the important points that have been made in this conversation, I think, when you look from the White House perspective, what they see is a very complex, difficult problem that is really the essence of being in the administration. Problems come to your doorstep that are not something you can control, and this certainly is one of those.\par \par \uc2 You can talk about the policy issues and things that the welcoming nature of the Biden administration as compared to the Trump administration toward immigrants broadly, and is that an influence. What they`re doing, in particular, is trying to surge some federal resources from different departments with expertise, FEMA and beyond, \uc2 HHS\uc2 , and so forth, to be on the ground and look for available spaces that are more suitable for young people.\par \par \uc2 That is something that happens in a very, you know, behind-the-scenes bureaucratic way to look for resolving this issue. What they`re not wanting to do here at the White House is call it a crisis, because words in politics matter. And that bumps it up to another level. And they don`t want to say that their policies are an inducement in any way.\par \par \uc2 Some of the migration is always seasonal. Some of it is based on conditions on the ground in the country of origin. And some of it has to do with what`s happening in the United States, and what the perceptions about what may or may not be doable at the border may be.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a complicated problem. One that the Biden administration would knew it would have to deal with overtime. Immigration, a big piece of its political agenda. But this particular surge of young people unaccompanied in particular, and not having the facilities, that`s really the kind of problem that a White House and a new White House, even with a lot of people with past government service, this is the kind of problem that pops up.\par \par \uc2 And it is nothing but bad options and bad choices for them to try to resolve this quickly. You don`t get much credit for moving fast in these situations, because there`s so much real-world human heartache that`s associated with it. Kasie.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: There really is. And Kelly, I mean, you and I have both covered just how difficult these conversations about immigration can become on the political level in Congress when a groups of people tried to fix this problem. You know, we`ve tried over and over again, I don`t even know how many times now in the last 10 years or so to grapple with this in a more comprehensive way.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden administration has said, yes, this is the thing we want to do right out of the gate. How do you think what we`re seeing happening at the border is going to affect whether or not they`re able to get anything done?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, it`s certainly when you have headlines that are inflaming the situation, that`s a very different political playbook than when you are choosing the kinds of issues you want to focus on. If they want to choose things that deal with other pieces of the large immigration picture, whether it`s DREAMers who are already in the United States, or it`s dealing with certain types of visas, those are different issues than a hot, urgent human problem like this.\par \par \uc2 And so, it compels the administration to have to alter its path to look at these issues to find resources and to try to both deal with the problem in the basics of what the problem resolves and deal with it politically. The obvious move by House Republicans to put an event on at the border is a way for Kevin McCarthy and his team to try to put pressure on the Biden administration on this issue and more broadly.\par \par \uc2 While the country may be looking at things like the COVID Relief Plan, and some things where the administration has something to cheer about, this is a big problem and one that will take a lot of work, a lot of expertise, and patience, which is hard to come by.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Indeed. All right, Kelly O`Donnell, thank you. I really appreciate your reporting. Our thanks to Dasha and Garrett as well.\par \par \uc2 And we do have a bit of breaking news here before we go to break. Authorities have arrested two men and charged them with assaulting Capitol Police Officer Brian Sicknick. Sicknick later died as a result of the injuries he sustained in the January 6th attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 According to court documents, the two men are not however being charged with Officer Sicknick`s death at this point. Their initial court appearances are scheduled for this afternoon. The case is of course part of the sprawling federal investigation into the siege, which is expected to result in charges against at least 400 people.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next here, the chair of the Congressional Hispanic Caucus joins us on Biden`s growing problem at the border. Plus, what he`s doing to help get vulnerable Americans vaccinated. And later, the latest state of New York is Governor Cuomo continues to resist the growing calls for his resignation. And President Biden weighs in on the investigation.\par \par \uc2 And as we go to break here, let`s look at -- look at something a little bit positive, positive signs of life after lockdown.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, welcome back. Any minute now we`re expecting a delegation of House Republicans to speak at the border in El Paso, Texas. Also happening right now, White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki is holding her daily briefing and she`s faced a number of questions about the administration`s response to the growing humanitarian crisis at the border as Republicans are looking to pin the situation on the president perhaps, of course, with an eye toward 2022.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is California Democratic Congressman Raul Ruiz. He is chair of the Congressional Hispanic Caucus. He`s also a medical doctor who today is administering COVID-19 vaccines, which I think helps explain your backdrop, Congressman. Thank you for that and I want to get to it in just one second.\par \par \uc2 But let`s start with the situation at the border because while we`re obviously seeing a political display here, I want to keep the focus on the humanitarian challenges that are facing people who clearly are making an incredibly gut-wrenching decision to, in many cases, have their children try and cross the border alone because the situation, the place where they`re coming from is so -- the risks are so terrible to their own health.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m wondering for you, what are the action items that you think need to be taken right now. Is bringing FEMA in a good first step? What more do you need to be doing?\par \par \uc2 REP. RAUL RUIZ (D-CA): Kasie, it`s very important to understand that this is a humanitarian issue because children are fleeing persecution and violence. We hear stories about how if the children aren`t joining these gangs or other criminal activities, that they would rape their family, their sisters, or they would even be killed. So, parents are scared, they`re trying to get their children to safety.\par \par \uc2 And what we need is a humanitarian response that puts the welfare of the children first. And that`s what the Biden administration is doing by ensuring that first, FEMA is there. And they will also increase staff with caseworkers, social workers, child specialists with psychology, psychiatry, and they`re -- and they`re looking at expanding short term housing for children in order to expedite their ability to get to a loving, safe, and caring relative`s home in the United States while their case is being processed through the courts.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, Congressman, I want to show you something that the Department of Homeland Security Secretary said earlier this month about how the new administration`s tone toward this may or may not be affecting what we`re seeing. Let`s watch what Alejandro Mayorkas had to say, and then I`ll ask you about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY: We are not saying don`t come, we are saying don`t come now because we will be able to deliver safe and orderly process to them as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, Congressman, I mean, should the Biden administration be telling people not to come?\par \par \uc2 RUIZ: Look, I think whether the Biden administration says it or not, we know that this has nothing to do with who`s in office and more to do with environmental and the failed states in their nation and the violence there. This happens every so often, usually in spring, it happened during the president.\par \par \uc2 And look, what we just witnessed for the past four years is a policy of hate, fear, cruelty, family separation, intimidation, and a useless border wall that was ineffective, and it still didn`t work. So, I think that the focus here is to follow our own asylum and refugee laws, and to ensure that the children are safe, to treat them with dignity, unlike the last administration, that could contain them and detain them in inhumane subhuman standards, which led to several deaths under their responsibility in CBP.\par \par \uc2 So, the approach is the right one. But it is very clear that this is not the time to come to the United States because the system has been dismantled by the Trump administration. And therefore, it needs to be built in a way that can process the claims as quickly and efficiently according to our laws as we possibly can.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, Congressman, the House is set to consider a couple of immigration-related pieces of legislation, including one that could offer a path to citizenship for migrant farmworkers. This is something that there has been some Republican support for in past congresses. Do you think now is the right time to be moving legislation like that through the House?\par \par \uc2 RUIZ: Absolutely. It`s absolutely the right time because we`re in the middle of a pandemic. And mind you, Kasie, I`m the son of farmworkers that grew up in a trailer park as well. I know how hard they work, and I understand the risk they`re taking in getting infected and dying from COVID-19 in order to feed America.\par \par \uc2 And so this bill will stabilize the workforce, It`ll help our economy, and it`ll help us rebuild during this pandemic. And for DREAMers, we know that DREAMers are doctors and nurses. In fact, there may be some dreamers here administering vaccines to save people`s lives in this event.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the right time to pass the Dream and Promise Act and the Farm Workforce Modernization Act. And I`m proud that the Congressional Hispanic Caucus have been leading the way to defend these human rights and to create pathways to citizenship for our hardworking immigrants who want nothing more than to work hard, contribute to America, keep America safe, and to contribute to our economy. And so, this is a great accomplishment.\par \par \uc2 And it is a bipartisan effort. We`re going to pass it out of the House and we`re going to give it to the Senate to make sure that they also pass it this year.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, Congressman, you mentioned vaccinations, and I know you are a physician who`s there administering doses now. I mean, what have you seen in terms of how these vaccinations are getting into arms, the logistics of that? Have you learned any lessons? And in fact, I`m particularly curious if you`ve had any conversations with people who`ve been reluctant to take the vaccine where you`ve had to reassure them that in fact, it is safe and how we`re doing that especially in underserved communities.\par \par \uc2 RUIZ: Kasie, there is no doubt that we`re in a turning point under the leadership of President Biden and here in California under the leadership of Governor Newsome, where we`re seeing an incredible increase in the amount of vaccines that are hitting our communities. And we`re going to do that even more so as the -- as the weeks and months go on for many different reasons.\par \par \uc2 One, is that we have a president who understands what the word equity means, and a governor who understands what equity means. And so the vaccines are getting to the people who need them the most. Right here, for example, in Coachella, and throughout the entire county of \uc2 Riverside County\uc2 , 47 percent of the population are Hispanics. They represent 65 percent of the cases of infections, but yet have only received 19 percent of the vaccines.\par \par \uc2 These are gross disparities that put lives and communities in jeopardy. So, President Biden`s focus on giving vaccines to community health centers, to right -- to the retail pharmacy programs, and Governor Newsome focus on making sure 40 percent go into medically underserved areas like Coachella, my hometown, is very important. And this is the manifestation of the hope and transformation that we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 Now, what I`m hearing from individuals, when I`ve been out in the fields or into these vaccination clinics into the community is some questions about the vaccines, but there`s nothing extraordinary that would prevent them from getting that vaccines.\par \par \uc2 For example, once you explain to them that the vaccine does not cause infertility or that the vaccine doesn`t cause people to get sick with COVID, then they are willing to get the vaccine. What`s more important and it which is a bigger factor of why the Hispanics and African Americans and Native Americans haven`t been vaccinated are not because of hesitancy, Kasie.\par \par \uc2 It`s very important to understand that the disparities exist primarily because it`s systemic barriers. There are no doctors, there`s no nurses, there`s all vaccines in their communities. Once you bring the vaccines here, you see the rooms full.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, in two hours, we vaccinated 700 individuals who work in the fields because they`re desperate to get protected to help their family and their community. So, this is where public health on the ground needs to -- needs to get organized and take the vaccines into the community in programs like this.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, Congressman Raul Ruiz, thank you very much for being with us today. We really appreciate your time and insights.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead here, more key Democrats are abandoning New York Governor Andrew Cuomo, but the governor is as defiant as ever. Plus, the White House kicks off a nationwide tour to sell the COVID Relief Package with President Biden expected to speak in just a few minutes. Don`t go anywhere. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. New York Governor Andrew Cuomo refuses to resign over sexual harassment allegations despite calls to do so from nearly every Democrat in the state`s congressional delegation and a majority of Democrats in the state assembly. And now, according to the "Washington Post," Cuomo`s vaccine czar Larry Schwartz called county officials in the state to gauge their loyalty to the governor.\par \par \uc2 According to the "Post," one county executive was so alarmed by the call, the official plans to file an ethics complaint with the state`s attorney general`s office. The executive feared the county`s vaccine supply could suffer if Schwartz was not pleased with the executive`s response to his questions about support for the governor.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Kathy Park joins me now from outside the state capitol in Albany.\par \par \uc2 Kathy, what is the latest that we are hearing from Cuomo especially considering this new reporting out from the "Post" today?\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Kasie. So, the governor was touring a vaccine site just moments ago. But he did not address the ongoing scandal that he finds himself in. But going back to what you were talking about earlier with reporting from the "Washington Post" and Larry Schwartz, that seems to be a big headline from this weekend. So he was hired back in 2011 to 2015 as secretary to the governor and was brought on as an adviser this spring to help with the vaccine rollout.\par \par \uc2 So "The Washington Post" first reported that he made -- Larry Schwartz made phone calls to county executives to basically gauge exactly where their loyalties lie with the governor as he finds himself in this scandal. And one county executive thought it was questionable.\par \par \uc2 Why is this vaccine czar calling me? And decided to flag this to the state attorney general`s office because he felt that he could potentially be impacting the vaccine rollout in his particular community.\par \par \uc2 This is something that Larry Schwartz says he denies. He said he did make these phone calls but did nothing wrong. And also said that he was making these phone calls as a friend to Governor Cuomo. Beth Garvey, who is representing Cuomo, also doubling down and issuing this statement saying, "Larry answered our call to volunteer in March and has since then worked night and day to help New York through this pandemic."\par \par \uc2 She went on to say, "Any suggestion that he acted in any way unethically or in any way other than in the best interest of the New Yorkers that he selflessly served is patently false." And Beth Garvey has been in the headlines a lot recently defending Cuomo as these allegations continue to surface. Kasie?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right, Kathy Park, thank you very much for getting us up to speed there.\par \par \uc2 With me now is Ruth Marcus, "Washington Post" deputy editorial page editor and columnist.\par \par \uc2 Ruth, always good to see you and get your perspective on things like this.\par \par \uc2 RUTH MARCUS, WASHINGTON POST DEPUTY EDITOR PAGE EDITOR AND COLUMNIST: Hi, Kasie.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Let`s start with the "Post" reporting here that we were just talking through. I mean, this understandably and for anyone who`s covered New York politics, it`s not at all out of the realm of possibility that a call like that could be read as a threat or as intimidation and in this time of this COVID-19 pandemic, it`s just so incredibly loaded and difficult to deal with.\par \par \uc2 I mean, how do you see what this report is saying, that essentially Cuomo`s top vaccine official was calling around, trying to figure out if he could find support for the governor potentially with their vaccine stake on the line?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: It`s really stupid and a ham-handed thing to do. And as you say, not entirely unexpected or out of the ordinary here. He has something that the people he is calling want. He has a supply of vaccines. He doesn`t need to mention that in the same call, though sometimes it seems he did, to know that when you`re calling and say, hey, I want to know how you`re feeling about the governor and where are you, are you with us or against us, that`s just inherently coercive, and inherently coercive in the same way that the governor`s alleged behavior towards subordinates is in part coercive.\par \par \uc2 And I think there`s a really fundamental irony here, which is that Governor Cuomo is calling -- and I have some sympathy for this -- for some due process and let the investigation uncover what the investigation will uncover. You can`t simultaneously call for due process and then try to affect the process and interfere with the process. You can`t have it both ways.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: You can`t have it both ways indeed.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: Well, you can try but you can`t.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: You can try. Yes, 100 percent. Do you think that there`s kind of a Trumpian aspects to the way Cuomo is handling this, essentially saying, well, the people of New York elected me, we`ve seen some polling that shows New Yorkers are still relatively speaking supportive of Cuomo continuing to hold the job?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: Well, there`s an obvious Trumpian aspect, which is, even going back before the handling of these allegations, the bullying behavior. And then there`s this really complicated question, I think, about how the political system, and when we`re dealing with elected officials, how we should deal with allegations of sexual misconduct and sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 When you -- as with the president, as with President Trump, not in terms of the allegations of sexual misconduct against him but the allegations that led him to be indicted twice by the House, when you don`t have a majority of people calling for you to be removed, as is the case according to the recent polling with Governor Cuomo, but you have a majority of both your senators, your state`s congressional delegation, the chorus of people in your state who are elected officials, who is the boss of you then?\par \par \uc2 The voters elected you and there`s a reasonable argument that if a majority of them don`t seem to want you to leave, why should you precipitously resign? At the same time I do not see how this is going to end well for Governor Cuomo.\par \par \uc2 The more facts that are going to come out, I don`t think from everything we`ve learned are going to be in his favor. So he is kind of playing desperately for time in a way that is not going to work out as well for him, I don`t think, as it did for President Trump. At least, you know, President Trump obviously was not re-elected, so it did not work out well for him in the end.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: But he was able to avoid being convicted.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Right. Is Ralph Northam an example, the Virginia governor who declined to resign? I mean, the difference it seems to me is that he of course could not run for reelection whereas Cuomo has not even gone so far as to say that he`ll rule that out.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS: I would bet a significant amount of money on Governor Cuomo not running for re-election and I would bet some money on Governor Cuomo not serving out his term. Governor Northam did something that was totally inappropriate and quite a long time ago. And he had the extra complicating factor or better for him factor of allegations against his lieutenant governor that arose at the same time.\par \par \uc2 But he did provide the brazen-it-out roadmap that Governor Cuomo seems to be following to a tee and hope will work out as well for him. But I think the measure as well for him as it did for Governor Northam is the same of serve out your term but honestly his political career is over.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: What a rise and fall. Ruth Marcus, thank you very much for being with us today. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And we are expecting President Biden to speak shortly as top administration officials are hitting the road to try and sell the stimulus. But in one of the country`s hardest hit areas, workers are worried the latest relief money still won`t be enough.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. The first stimulus checks of Biden`s presidency hit bank accounts this weekend. And the administration is launching a national "Help is Here" tour to promote their nearly $2 trillion COVID relief package.\par \par \uc2 The president is expected to kick it off with remarks from the White House in the next few minutes. And Vice President Kamala Harris is in Las Vegas today where the $1400 stimulus and extended unemployment benefits are providing much needed aid to service industry workers laid off from jobs and struggling restaurants and casinos.\par \par \uc2 But after a whole year of the pandemic, many Las Vegas workers are in a long-term economic hole that this upcoming check just isn`t going to fill.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Deepa Shivaram is in Las Vegas where she`s been talking to workers about the latest round of COVID relief.\par \par \uc2 Deepa, it`s great to see you. Thank you so much for being here. The reality here for so many people is that this has been a long year of scraping and patching and just trying to make it to the next month. But there have been some protections in place, eviction moratoriums, bars to foreclosures of homes. And just because we are coming to the end of this, it doesn`t mean that people have money to pay those bills that are still out there.\par \par \uc2 DEEPA SHIVARAM, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Right, Kasie. That`s exactly right. And workers that we talked to in the city are grateful for that check that`s coming in from the stimulus package. But the word that they have been using, it`s kind of calling it a band-aid solution. Right? And the vice president coming in to Las Vegas today, meeting with some union workers, meeting them at the Culinary Academy, and some of those local folks tell me that they plan to bring those concerns to the vice president.\par \par \uc2 You know, talking about the sustainability of these solutions. These are short-term is what one worker told me. We met a woman named Mary Ann this weekend who used to be a buffet worker at the STRAT Casino and Hotel. And she hasn`t been to work in almost an entire year. And some of that stuff, Kasie, is affecting these union contracts. So it means that after one year, she might not have a job at all. Take a listen to what she told us this weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY ANN BAUTISTA, FORMER BUFFET WORKER AT THE STRAT HOTEL AND CASINO: There`s still a lot of people that they laid off for almost a year now that they haven`t called, not even one day.\par \par \uc2 So that means in March 17, for those people haven`t been called back to work even one day, we`re automatically terminated. And for me, that`s not fair. I love my job. I`m not a lazy person. I appreciate the help from the government, don`t get me wrong. Like what I said, little thing helps. You know, I really appreciate. But those are short-term.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHIVARAM: Kasie, those are short-term solutions like you heard Mary Ann say there. You know, she`s grateful for this money coming in, but she calls it money already spent for her. It`s going towards her car payments which are already overdue.\par \par \uc2 And she needs that car to drive to the food pantry in order to feed her family and her neighbors. So the long-term solutions here still very much question marks around all of it. In the meantime, it`s a lot of stress and anxiety for many workers here in Las Vegas. Kasie?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And of course, underscores why it`s so important that we all get the vaccine when it`s our turn so that workers like the woman you just spoke to can get back to work.\par \par \uc2 Deepa, thank you very much for your reporting today.\par \par \uc2 And coming up next here, what Europe`s coronavirus surge means here at home.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: Once you declare victory, you know, that metaphor that people say if you go in for a touchdown, don`t spike the ball on the five-yard line, wait until you get into the end zone. And we`re not in the end zone yet, and that`s one of the issues that when you plateau, there is always the risk of a surge. That`s exactly what the Europeans have experienced.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Welcome back. That was President Biden`s chief medical adviser, Dr. Anthony Fauci, with Chuck on MEET THE PRESS yesterday, warning that our fight against the coronavirus is not over yet. Europe is seeing another significant surge in virus cases, including a 10 percent increase in Italy since last week. Italy, which was one of the hardest hit countries during this first surge last year, is under another national lockdown starting today.\par \par \uc2 Over the course of the pandemic, the U.S. has closely mirrored Europe`s pattern of surges and declines, lagging by just a few weeks or so. But this time experts say the United States` vaccination program may be getting the upper hand and could break the cycle.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, NBC medical contributor, pulmonologist and pandemic`s consultant, Dr. Vin Gupta.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gupta, always good to see you again. Thank you so much for being here. Let me ask you this, I mean, I think we`re all watching what`s happening in Europe with some concern, but on the other hand, our vaccination rates are going up and we seem to have some positive numbers. So which destiny are we headed for, do you think?\par \par \uc2 DR. VIN GUPTA, NBC NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Good afternoon, Kasie, good to see you. You know, the most likely destiny is normalcy by midsummer. That`s assuming, though, Kasie, that the warmer weather, of course coronaviruses don`t like to spread.\par \par \uc2 They don`t like warm humid air. So we`re going to be benefited by the environment or going to be benefited hopefully by at least 70 percent of the country. Those who are eligible getting the vaccine, although now there`s open questions, we`re seeing some polls suggesting increased skepticism. We have to address that head on.\par \par \uc2 And then of course the variants. The variants right now are already concerning. If we give them an upper hand to continue to change, to continue to evolve, that will be problematic. And that`s why you`re seeing the policies of Governor Abbott, for example, be so troublesome right now. Because it`s just giving these viruses more of an ability to change, more an ability to potentially escape the protective benefit that these vaccines will induce.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a lot of open questions. I`ll lastly say, Kasie, what you`re seeing out of Europe when it comes to, for example, the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine, this news just broke that multiple countries in Western Europe are now pausing its deployment. I think that`s a mistake. It`s OK to take precautions, but the chances that, for example, a blood clot phenomenon is related to the administration of vaccine is so highly unlikely.\par \par \uc2 It`s so unlikely and it`s so rare. These cases are so sporadic that all we`re doing here is we`re providing more fodder for those who are skeptical. The anti-vaccers. And so these are all challenges. There is a lot of threat here that might put at risk this hope for normalcy by midsummer.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: You know, I`m glad you brought up the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine. It`s here on my list of things that I wanted to ask you about, and I was hoping you could just explain a little bit more about what European observers, doctors, experts were seeing that caused them to take this step. And if you could just expand a little bit on why you think that people still should not be worried that it`s unsafe.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: So, Kasie, essentially what`s happened here is that there have been a few episodes of blood clots in vital organs, I mean, in a handful of them amongst the tens of millions who received the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine across the world. I mean, this is one of the most accessible vaccines worldwide, if not here in the United States, at least in the rest of the world. It`s very effective, we think, we believe, based on initial studies, at preventing hospitalizations and deaths, in an ICU for example.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that these very rare incidences of blood clots are happening after the administration of a vaccine is no reason to think the two are related. But things happen all the time. Normal medical complications happen all the time for people that are otherwise predisposed to things like blood clots or a heart attack, who knows, but it doesn`t necessarily mean that the incidents of that rare complication is because of the vaccine. It has nothing to do with -- the two are not necessarily related.\par \par \uc2 So what you`re seeing here are European regulators say, let`s pause, let`s take a look at the data. Every single expert out there that`s looking at what`s happening for all your viewers strongly does not believe that the two are related. Vaccines do not cause this type of phenomenon from happening. And so that`s why there`s a lot of concerns here, that we are drawing a lot of attention to this, but that it`s very unlikely that any type of vaccine is going to cause a blood clot.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: I see. Yes. No, that makes a lot of sense. I mean, my question broadly, too, I mean, I think people look at these surges, they see news about lockdowns, and they`re nervous that obviously that maybe it`s going to happen here again. Do you think that the European -- the way that Europeans are vaccinating their populations differ significantly from here? I mean, are we doing a significantly better enough job to avoid what is happening to them right now?\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: I think we are. The vaccination program here is by far a world beater, it`s one of the best in the world. So, yes, I am optimistic that we can avoid the worst of it and another lockdown as you`re seeing, for example, in Italy. But again, there are threats as mentioned earlier that could put that at risk.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: All right. Dr. Vin Gupta, thank you very much, as always, for your straightforward expertise. We really have appreciated your voice all the way along with us.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to all of you at home as well for being with us this hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with much more MEET THE PRESS DAILY and you can always catch me tomorrow morning and every morning on "WAY TOO EARLY" 5:00 a.m. ET.\par \par \uc2 Don`t go anywhere. MSNBC coverage continues with my friend Katy Tur right after a quick break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210315eh3f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 President Biden Calls Off Second Airstrike in Syria; GOP Proposes New Voting Laws in Georgia; New Book Details President Biden`s Path Victory, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Stephanie Ruhle, Anne Thompson,  Blayne Alexander, Jonathan Allen \par \uc2 7,941 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: It`s Friday, Republicans try to slow down COVID relief in the Senate any way they can, as progressive Democrats tried to squeeze their colleagues on the issue of raising the minimum wage any way they can. Plus, first light or fourth surge. The White House warns we`re at an inflection point in the war against COVID. Just as a new jobs report has the economy showing some renewed signs of life.\par \par \uc2 And one of New York Governor Andrew Cuomo his accusers opens up about an incident with the governor that she says left her terrified. This new reports lay out evidence of a potential cover up inside the governor`s office, tied to nursing home deaths.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Friday, it is MEET THE PRESS DAILY and I`m Chuck Todd. Concerns over a fourth COVID surge in COVID cases remain very real. This morning, the White House`s COVID Response Team reiterated their warnings that infection rates are still unacceptably high. As some states move to lift restrictions, including mask mandates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROCHELLE WALENSKY, CDC DIRECTOR: The current numbers remain concerning cases and deaths are still too high, and have now plateaued for more than a week at levels we saw during the late summer surge following six weeks of steady declines. This is why I`m asking you to double down on prevention measures. I know the idea of relaxing mask wearing and getting back to everyday activities is appealing. But we`re not there yet.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, DIRECTOR NATIONAL, INSTITUTE FOR ALLERGIES AND INFECTIOUS DISEASE: That plateau is about 60 to 70,000 cases a day. When you have that much of viral activity in a plateau, it almost invariably means that you are at risk for another spike.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Every day this week, we had 1000 to 2000 deaths every single day this week, folks. And Dr. Fauci also warned this morning that the more this virus replicates, the more risk there is for the spread of more dangerous variants, which then hits at our vaccination distribution issues. So the concern over another search comes into U.S. economy added nearly 400,000 jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 It far exceeded expectations but the number of workers who are now permanently unemployed has grown. And the economy is still 9.5 million jobs in the hole because of the pandemic. Senate Democrats today are moving towards a vote on President Biden`s massive $1.9 trillion relief bill as they navigate a wild west style vote-a-rama process so they can end up passing it without having to need a single Republican vote.\par \par \uc2 But there are divisions inside the Democratic side of the caucus. This morning Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  lashed out at his party`s decision not to fight the Senate Parliamentarian`s ruling on the minimum wage, forcing a potentially awkward vote on the issue for moderates in his party.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): I think the parliamentarian was dead wrong. But more importantly, it is an absurd process that we allow an unelected staff or somebody who works for the Senate not elected by anybody to make a decision as to whether 30 million Americans get a pay raise or not. I don`t care how the parliamentarian rules. No parliamentarian should have that power.\par \par \uc2 If people here want to vote against raising the minimum wage, I have that right. You want to vote for it and I hope you do, you have that right. But we should not shuffle off that responsibility to an unelected staff. That`s wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, here`s the current state of play in our war against the pandemic. The pace of vaccinations that`s quickening. But it`s not enough according to some public health experts, the economy, it`s recovering, but it`s not enough according to some economists. And COVID relief is winding its way through the Senate. But it`s not enough according to some top Democrats like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Leigh Ann Caldwell is on Capitol Hill with the latest on the COVID bill. Our senior business correspondent NBC, Stephanie Ruhle is standing by to break down this jobs report. And also with this former acting CDC director, \uc2 Dr. Richard Besser\uc2 , as you can see, we`re trying to get all three angles of COVID covered today. Let`s start with what`s going on in the Senate. There have been some important tweaks Leigh Ann.\par \par \uc2 The biggest is having to do with the special unemployment benefit, which got a one-month extension, but it got brought back its monthly payment got brought down by $100 each month. Anything else likely to change?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HOUSE REPORTER: We`re still waiting, Chuck. The fact that there was this last-minute change just moments before they started this vote-a-rama means that Senate Democratic leaders are still working really hard to make sure that they have the support of all 50 Democrats, because remember, they can`t lose a single Democrat if they`re guaranteed to pass this COVID relief bill.\par \par \uc2 But it seems like there might be some problems with this change in unemployment insurance, because that`s supposed to be the next amendment that they`re supposed to be voting on. But it`s -- things are stalled in the Senate right now. There are some negotiations and discussions that are happening that we`re not quite clear on yet. But we do know that it pertains to this amendment, and that they haven`t moved to it yet.\par \par \uc2 So, there are still problems for Democratic leaders. And it was evident also, in this last vote on the minimum wage that \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  brought to the floor. Everyone knew that the Senator Sanders amendment was going to fail because it needed the support of 60 senators to overrule the parliamentarian. But the fact that eight Democrats voted against a $15.00 minimum wage over the next four years is very telling that not even regardless of what the parliamentarian said, the party isn`t there yet.\par \par \uc2 Now, I`m told by my sources, that most Democrats other than Manchin and Sinema do support $15.00. But they say that it is very complicated the details how you get there, the indexing what to do after it reaches $15.00, what to do about tip and wage workers. So, there`s a lot of things that the Democratic Party still has to work out on some of these core economic issues still.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do we have any sense of -- does this end up passing tomorrow? Is the vote-a-rama going to go longer than that? What kind of -- I guess what kind of stamina does the Senate GOP have in him?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes, we`ll see. It`s very unclear. Senator Johnson is still threatening to perhaps force the clerk to read every single amendment. He, you know, amendments, obviously, aren`t as long as the 630-page bill. There are 900 amendments that have been filed. So far -- excuse me, there`s about 500 amendments that have been filed so far, doesn`t mean they`re going to get a vote on every single one of them.\par \par \uc2 In the last vote-a-rama, there`s about 900 amendments filed, and they only voted on 40. But they end when they think that they are done when each side has proven their point, mostly the opposition side, the Republicans, and these -- they`re going to try to force Democrats to take very political and difficult votes that could end up in campaign ads, as you know very well in the next election cycle. So, their effort is not to change the bill really, it`s to put democrats in a box.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, no, and many of these -- many of these amendments, whether they`re really written by the pollsters or the political consultants, or not, they sometimes feel like they are because they`re basically being written for direct mail pieces or T.V. advertisements. Let me move over to Stephanie Ruhle. Stephanie, this was a really good jobs report. But as we know, one month does not make recovery.\par \par \uc2 We have -- I think we`re all waiting for -- when do we have two or three in a row because this is -- it has been sort of an up or down thing, this sort of beginning of this recovery. So, where are we? Is this a top heavy recovery or are we starting to see some healing across the board?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, NBC NEWS SENIOR BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: We are definitely seeing healing, Chuck. Things are getting better. As the COVID numbers are coming down, businesses are opening where you saw the biggest spikes in job creation were the industries that were hit hardest? Leisure, hospitality, retail. Those are all very big positives across the board. However, you set it at the top, we`ve still got over nine million people out of work who had jobs before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And the longer people are out of work, long-term unemployment. That`s people who are not working for six months or longer. That`s doubled. 42 percent of the people who are out of work or out of work now over six months and the longer you`re out, the harder it is to get back in. I want to share a conversation I had with a woman down in Florida. She`s 16 years old. She has now been out of work for almost a year and she`s basically in a state of panic. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Florida. When you think about businesses that are open, Florida is more open than most states. Are there are jobs out there to be had? Are you applying for work?\par \par \uc2 RANDY LEVINE, FLORIDA RESIDENT: I`ve been applying. I`ve sent out hundreds of applications. I`m on indeed, I`m on other Web sites, word of mouth, just going to -- looking on the computer at, you know, companies in the area. But I am looking for work. It`s never -- when I sit on the computer, I`m always looking, always open.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Chuck, here`s what`s really important. People aren`t getting back to work, but many of these jobs are not paying them enough to survive. And while you got so many people what absolutely no job opportunities, yes, there`s a job to be had. But chances are, it`s part time or they`ve cut the hours back, or they`ve cut the pay. And workers don`t have a lot of leverage. When you go from no job at all, many people feel like any job is something.\par \par \uc2 And to Leigh Ann`s point in the beginning, the fact that unemployment benefits are going to go down in dollar amounts, that`s going to change the game because many people actually get paid more on unemployment than the jobs that are out there for them. But we`re not out of the woods.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you look at the jobs report, and where we were lagging and job growth, can you point to things in this COVID relief bill that -- is it -- is the COVID relief bill responding properly two our under employed part of the economy?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes, without a doubt. You are seeing targeted support for childcare centers, which we know have been decimated. We know a lot are going to states and localities. In the last month alone, we saw thousands and thousands ready for this of teachers. Teachers who had to be laid off because school systems cannot afford to make their budgets. With so much money going to local budgets, you`re going to see that, you know, reinvested in communities.\par \par \uc2 And when you target that small business money, not just to any business with less than 500 people, because we all know all sorts of small businesses that didn`t need the money took it. Now with the more targeted efforts. Hopefully, it`s going to get to businesses that need it most. There`s also 25 billion less than the restaurant act wanted. But it`s something towards restaurants and we know they need help. So yes, the stimulus bill is huge. But it`s not going to be one and done. They need more.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, good luck getting more. I think that`s why Democrats are struggling while I think some of them think it`s too big, they`re afraid of going smaller, because I don`t know if they think there`ll be the appetite for more. We`ll shall see. Stephanie Ruhle --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I appreciate that. Let me turn over to the health aspect of COVID. And that`s where we have \uc2 Dr. Richard Besser\uc2 . We`re in an inflection point. Everybody has said it, fork in the road, whatever, pick the metaphor here. Did the decisions -- these decisions to drop the mask mandates which feels like more consequential than the decision to open up? I mean, if everybody were wearing a mask, I think we`d be in a different situation. Did we already choose the wrong path? Is it already too late?\par \par \uc2 DR. RICHARD BESSER\uc2 , FORMER ACTING FOR THE CDC: Well, Chuck, I think that there are some states that are definitely choosing the wrong path here. You know, I`ve been saying over the past number of days that I`ve had cautious optimism. But that`s tempered when you see states removing mask guidance. You know, one of the things we learned over the course of last year was the value of masks.\par \par \uc2 And we went from a no mask situation, to one where we were able to show that wearing masks provides protection not only to the individual, but to all those people around that person. And when you -- when you say in a state that people don`t have to wear masks anymore, it`s not about that person`s individual choice, it`s about putting at risk. People out and about in the community, people who have to go to work each day to put food on the table.\par \par \uc2 It is the wrong direction to go. Especially in light of the -- of the warning we just heard from Dr. Fauci and Dr. Walensky that we`re plateauing at a level that`s as high as we were during the peak of last summer, when we started putting in place more restrictions. It`s just a bad situation to be in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you, if you had to choose the following sort of flawed option, right, when it comes to health mitigation, would you prefer restrictions on businesses and no mask or open 100 percent but a mask mandate?\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Well, it`s a false choice. And I avoid -- I avoid going --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand.\par \par \uc2 BESSER: -- through that because --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know.\par \par \uc2 BESSER: It needs to be a combination there. You know, you definitely -- no matter --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I guess what you`re seeing --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re seeing where I`m going here, right? We just like take Mississippi and Texas. Look, I understand that it is an easy dealing with the small business owners going, this is killing us. This is killing us. You have tax revenue issues. It -- so, I guess the thing is, if you had to make the false choice, is the mask mandate the thing to keep?\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Yes, you definitely want to keep the mask mandate because that`s something that`s been shown to reduce transmission. If you`re going to be bringing people into more indoor spaces, you definitely want to have them wearing masks to protect the other customers but to protect your employees, it`s not a fair thing to do to working Americans to put them in close contact with people who aren`t wearing masks.\par \par \uc2 And the thing that I find really exciting right now is that as more and more people get vaccinated, it will become safer and safer to start to reopen aspects of the -- of the economy. But you have to keep a close eye on the case counts and the direction there. You have to keep a close eye on the -- on the variants that are around. And you have to listen to Dr. Fauci`s warning that when you`re having this number of cases each day, it allows those variants to get a foothold.\par \par \uc2 If we can drive that leveling off level way down to 20,000, it`s a major difference from where we are right now where there`s significant transmission going on, that allows these variants to spread.\par \par \uc2 You know, the other thing, Dr. Besser, I`m curious what your thoughts are on this. We`ve had -- our case count has come down pretty dramatically. It does seem as if our -- the death rate is not falling as dramatically. And yet, you would think that would fall quicker because we`re -- because we`ve had targeted vaccinations for the most vulnerable. And yet worse, we`re -- you`re still seeing 1500 people a day die.\par \par \uc2 And I`m just curious, is that still a lagging indicator in your opinion from the last surge or is this proof that maybe these variants are just creating tougher outcomes?\par \par \uc2 BESSER: I think that the answer isn`t fully in on that. We`re going to have to see, but I think that some of it is that the death rate is a lagging indicator. One of the things that gives me hope is that if you break this down, and you look at deaths and long-term care facilities in nursing homes, where there was that early targeting for vaccination, the death rate has just plummeted.\par \par \uc2 And it shows how impactful it can be to vaccinate. There are still a lot of people who are in the highest risk groups of -- for dying from this, those who are who were the oldest, were there -- there`s more targeted outreach to get people vaccinated, in particular, among people of color. And that gives me some hope, you know, because the early focus was all around numbers. Now, there`s a lot more focus about getting this to the communities that have been hit the hardest.\par \par \uc2 And I think we`re going to see that payoff in terms of decreasing hospitalization rates, and hopefully decreasing death rates.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Man, I cannot wait to reach that cliff. That`s one of those cliffs you can`t wait to free fall on as far as the graphics are concerned. Anyway, \uc2 Dr. Richard Besser\uc2 , thank you very much. Before that, Stephanie Ruhle and Leigh Ann Caldwell, great way to get started.\par \par \uc2 Some breaking news now in the upcoming murder trial of George Floyd. The Minnesota Court of Appeals ruled today that a third degree murder charge against former police officer Derrick Chauvin should be reinstated. Now this ruling does not by itself reinstate the charge. The state can now try again to get that third degree charge reinstated, but Chauvin`s lawyers can appeal. Chauvin also faces charges of second degree murder and second degree manslaughter. Jury selection is scheduled to begin on Monday, but that now may be delayed.\par \par \uc2 All right. Up next, some new pressure on embattled Governor Andrew Cuomo as New York state lawmakers are preparing to strip the Democrat of this pandemic related emergency powers. We`ll have more on that. Plus, the person who could be his possible successor who would make history. We`re also keeping an eye on the White House press briefing right now and we`ll bring in any major developments. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back right now. New York state lawmakers are debating stripping Governor Andrew Cuomo in this pandemic related emergency powers. The move would return decisions on lockdowns to local governments and it comes as Cuomo is facing mountainy scrutiny on two fronts. The first reported by the Wall Street Journal and the New York Times that Cuomo advisors alternative report last summer to conceal the number of nursing home residents who died of COVID-19 in hospitals.\par \par \uc2 Oh, by the way, he was trying to finish a book at the time. And administration official says the hospital data was left out over concerns about its accuracy and that the omission did not change the conclusion of the report. But at the same time, one of the three women who is alleging Cuomo behaved inappropriately is speaking out on camera. Charlotte Bennett, a former Cuomo aide in sexual assault survivor, told CBS News she had a disturbing conversation with the governor in his office.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLOTTE BENNETT, GOV. ANDREW CUOMO ACCUSER: Yes, he asked if I had trouble enjoying being with someone because of my drama.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL, CBS ANCHOR: This is -- seems highly inappropriate.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes. The governor asked me if I was sensitive to intimacy.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In his office.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes. During the work day.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You have been quoted as saying that he also asked you about if you`d ever been with an older man.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes. He asked me if age difference mattered. He also explained that he was fine with anyone over 22.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: When asked for comment Cuomo spokesperson referred NBC to his news conference on Wednesday in which he apologized for acting in a way that made others feel uncomfortable. Threw in some ifs there. He also insists he never touched anybody and appropriately despite claims from former adviser Lindsey Boylan, who says he kissed her without her consent last year.\par \par \uc2 Anne Thompson has been reporting on Governor Cuomo all week. She joins me now. And there`s no doubt that Charlotte Bennett interview is only I think, adds to the impact of that side of the story. But the newest developments have to do with the nursing home numbers and sort of the other scandal that is engulfing the Cuomo administration here. It looks like they`re sort of admitting that yes, they did this. They`re just trying to claim it wasn`t nefarious?\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That`s what -- that`s exactly what they`re doing, Chuck, because they were saying that they couldn`t verify the numbers that the hospitals were giving them about the nursing home patients who had been transferred there and died and that`s why they weren`t in the report. But what makes this suspicious is at the very same time, Governor Cuomo was contemplating, writing a book bragging about his leadership during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 He had earned a lot of praise across the country for those daily briefings that he gave, explaining what was going on. And then the back beef -- the background to all of this and what Republicans have been harping on since the end of March was a directive that the governor sent out telling nursing homes that they could not reject any patient who had left that -- left gone to the hospital to be treated for COVID, they had to take that patient back.\par \par \uc2 And he says the reason he did that at the end of March is that they were so worried that they wouldn`t have enough capacity in hospitals, that the patients who were not as serious could go back to the nursing homes. Republicans have said, and many family members have said, that`s what sent COVID racing through nursing homes and caused so many deaths. 15,000 in the state. And -- but, you know, the governor has said all along, it wasn`t that it was staff members, people coming into the nursing homes.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s that debate. But when you put that all together, it sure looks like they were trying to make the numbers look better than they actually were.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is the nursing home part of this, Anne? What kind of legal trouble could he be in there? And then also, do we have any update on how the Attorney General`s going to handle the sexual -- the sexual harassment allegations?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Well, the U.S. Attorney in Brooklyn is leading the federal investigation into the accounting of nursing home desks. And there`s a question from what I understand and I`m not aware of, Chuck. So, if -- there is a question of whether that accounting impacted Medicaid or any kind of government records, were there any laws broken there and that`s what the U.S. attorney is looking at.\par \par \uc2 What the independent investigator will look at who has yet to be appointed, the state attorney general, Tish James, she will do that. That investigator is going to look into the sexual harassment claims. And from my understanding that investigator will be looking at civil claims there to see if any of those laws were broken in the governor`s approach to these young women.\par \par \uc2 But I think watching Charlotte Bennett last night was really just devastating to the governor`s, you know, argument that he was just you know, palling around.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. No, I think -- I think that is not going to help reinforce his side of the story. Anne Thompson, thank you. And because of that, we think it`s time to take a look at the person who could replace the governor. While he says he will not resign, it isn`t stopping the pressure from Republicans and some Democrats for the three-term Democrat to do. One of two things step down now, or at least say you`re not running for reelection which puts the Lieutenant Governor Kathy Hochul in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 Hochul, who has years of political experience would be first in line to succeed Cuomo. She`s a former member of Congress, Erie County clerk, so she has sort of the non-New York City credential that many people -- many Democrats statewide need to win statewide elections in New York. Our senior political editor Mark Murray joins me now. And so, Mark, it`s basically who is Kathy Huchol? So, give us a rundown of sort of her political career. How did she end up lieutenant governor?\par \par \uc2 MARK MURRAY, NBC SENIOR POLITICAL EDITOR: Yes, Chuck. She ended up becoming Andrew Cuomo`s a lieutenant governor nominee in his second bid, seeking a second term. And of course, she`s actually someone who`s very familiar to those of us who cover congressional elections and Congress. She`s appointing a special election in 2011 in a crowded seat to replace then, Congressman Chris Lee, a Republican who got caught up in scandal.\par \par \uc2 She won that primarily campaigning against Paul Ryan`s Medicare plans to cut Medicare, won that race but ended up losing in 2012 after redistricting the really close contest against Republican Chris Collins. Collins, of course got caught up in scandal himself. But then after all of that, she ended up becoming Cuomo`s lieutenant governor nominee and find herself in a situation she`s at.\par \par \uc2 TODD: One thing about her when she was in the state legislature, she was pretty front facing and forward leaning on issues of sexual harassment against women. She hasn`t spoken out much yet. And she`s not considered a total Cuomo insider here. At what point do we, you know, we -- if she speaks out, it could speak volumes.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Absolutely. And of course, Chuck, there`s a little bit trickier situation. She was Andrew Cuomo`s running mate. And so, sometimes when we look back in Virginia, when Justin Fairfax, as lieutenant governor who was thinking about the thoughts about what he end up replacing Ralph Northam, you in Virginia run kind of a separate slate. You run for -- you get elected alone, she got elected with Andrew Cuomo.\par \par \uc2 And so sometimes kind of speaking out, takes on a little bit of a different dynamic than if you`re not getting that spot just yourself.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, this is a tricky -- a tricky moment here. And if Cuomo is not going to willingly pass the baton, it puts her in a really unique and top spot. But there`s no doubt she`s going to be increasingly in the spotlight and someone folks need to get to know.\par \par \uc2 Mark Murray, thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, new questions about the Biden administration`s efforts to get Iran back to the negotiating table over a nuclear deal, as tensions continue to rise with Tehran.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Last night "The Wall Street Journal" reported and we have since confirmed here at NBC News that President Joe Biden had initially claimed two separate air strikes in Syria last week against Iranian militia groups but called one off after learning there was potential for civilian casualties including women and children. Now the other air strike did go ahead, bombing a logistics station the Pentagon said was used by an Iranian-backed militia.\par \par \uc2 That new bit of intel about calling off a second strike comes after an Iraqi base hosting U.S. troops came under another attack, a rocket attack, on Wednesday of this week. Now, neither President Biden, the Pentagon, nor the State Department say they can confirm Iran`s involvement or responsibility for that attack. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We cannot attribute responsibility for the rocket attacks at this time. Obviously, it`s a rocket attack and we have seen rocket attacks come from Shia-backed militia groups in the past. So in that way, it certainly coincides with our past experience here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The other code word you heard him say there, Shia-backed. So that`s what they`re -- also a way that they hint at Iran`s involvement.\par \par \uc2 All this is happening as the Biden administration is signaling openness to bring Tehran back to the table to renegotiate what`s left of this nuclear deal.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is former ambassador to Syria during the Obama administration, Robert Ford.\par \par \uc2 So, Ambassador Ford, to say we`re getting mixed messages is probably an understatement here. But I guess I want to lay out a few facts that have happened. And if you can just sort of try to explain, you know, how we should interpret this. So the Biden administration essentially sends a signal, we`re ready to talk with Iran. After that signal is sent, we have the -- those militia attacks on our troops. Biden responds.\par \par \uc2 Iran then responds to the response, it appears. And yet at the same time, a French diplomat indicated to Reuters today that, hey, it`s looking pretty good for talks. So explain why somehow we`re closer to possibly sitting down with Iran after all of this proxy fighting that`s been going on the last two weeks.\par \par \uc2 AMB. ROBERT FORD, FORMER AMBASSADOR TO SYRIA: So the simple answer is, you can negotiate the nuclear issue with Iran, and I think the U.S. has a huge interest in stopping the Iranian nuclear program. At the same time that there`s back and forth with these pro-Iran militias on the ground in places like Syria and Iraq, and American forces. You can do both at the same time. It is diplomatically possible to walk and chew gum.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK, but it`s not easy. I`m just curious, what message is Iran trying to send? Are they trying to test Biden`s resolve, see how serious he is? And is there a point where the American side looks or frankly, the entire sort of allied side here looks like they`re, you know, willing to take anything as long as it gets Iran back to the table? Is that a sign of weakness?\par \par \uc2 FORD: So there are two separate issues here. There`s the Iranian nuclear program. That`s one issue. And it`s a distinct issue. And then there`s a second issue, which is Iran`s regional expansion and the use of proxy militias, such as the ones in Syria and Iraq. That`s a separate distinct issue.\par \par \uc2 I do not think that these pokes at the American forces from these proxy militias is a message from Iran about the nuclear file. The Iranians, Javad Zarif, their foreign minister, just a couple of hours ago on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , said they`re going to come forward through proper diplomatic channels with a proposal.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure it`s going to have to be hugely negotiated, but I don`t think they`re signaling that they won`t talk. But in the meantime, we should expect that there will be more pokes at us from these proxy militias on the ground, in places like Iraq.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You have made -- took pains to say these are separate and distinct issues. A lot of folks on this side are not going to see it that way and it`s certainly what makes it sometimes politically nearly impossible to get any sort of bipartisan agreement on how to deal with Iran. And I guess, I would assume the Iranians know this, though. So, again, even if you -- we want to see it separately, does everybody else see it separately?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, I would not assume that the Iranians have a sophisticated understanding of American political dynamics. Maybe they do, maybe they don`t. I`m not sure that they do. In my experience negotiating with foreign governments over the years, often, they didn`t understand even simple things. So -- but, I think on this side of the water, in the United States, there needs to be a discussion about what to do about the Iranian nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 Maximum pressure under the Trump administration did not stop the Iranian nuclear program. If anything, the Iranians now have more materials with which to make nuclear weapons. They have more sophisticated production facilities with which to make them. And so the maximum pressure didn`t work. I think what the Biden administration needs to do is to move forward on negotiating with the Iranians, while at the same time pushing back on these proxy militias.\par \par \uc2 And the good news is, on pushing back on the proxy militias, we have allies. The Israeli Air Force is hitting those same militias regularly and having success. The government of Iraq in Baghdad, it`s weak, but it, too, wants to clip the wings of those proxy militias. And so working with friends like that, we should be pushing back on the proxy militias, even as we sit down to negotiate on the nuclear issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: By the way, pushing back on the proxy militias will probably gain Republican support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Let me ask you on the nuclear issue, do you believe Iran wants -- do you believe, ultimately, Iran is willing not to stop pursuing a nuclear weapon, or do you think at some point they just want to continue to buy time and at some point say, we have the weapon? Now what are you going to do?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I have no doubt there are people in the Iranian government that have that position you just mentioned. It sounds sort of like North Korea, doesn`t it? So the question is, can the entire Iranian government come to an agreement on stopping the nuclear weapons program. Iran, like the United States, has different factions, different parties. And so they will have disagreements amongst themselves just as we do.\par \par \uc2 The question is, can you get a binding agreement? And we won`t know until you go to the negotiating table.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There you go. And we have to see what talks will look like.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador Ford, really appreciate getting your perspective and expertise on this. We have a long way to go and this stuff is --\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- never as easy as it looks. Anyway, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`ll head to Georgia, where after suffering major election losses, Republicans are trying to change the rules of the game there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. After losing the presidential contest and two Senate seats in one year, Georgia Republicans are looking to make major changes. None to how they campaigned and persuade voters, but into the state`s voting laws. Republican lawmakers are backing multiple bills that would eliminate automatic voter registration, require excuses in most cases to vote absentee and curb early voting access by placing restrictions on drop boxes and limiting early voting on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say these bills would work to build voter confidence that was eroded amid former President Trump`s unfounded attacks on the integrity of the state`s election system, but critics say this will just simply make voting harder, which may be a feature of the bill, especially for black voters and are nothing more than sour grapes from a party looking to keep their grasp on power.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now on the ground in Atlanta is my NBC News colleague, Blayne Alexander.\par \par \uc2 Blayne, look, we`re seeing this all over the country and various Republican legislatures. I guess, the question is here, you had a Republican secretary of state and a Republican governor swear by the integrity of these elections. Are they going to support these changes?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And that`s exactly what I asked Gabriel Sterling when I spoke with him yesterday. He`s somebody that his name, his face certainly would be very familiar to our audience, because you`ll remember that when Georgia`s elections came under repeated attack from former President Trump, he was one of the strongest defenders of the process. He talked about the fact that there was no evidence of widespread voter fraud. That`s something we also heard from the Republican Secretary of State, Brad Raffensperger.\par \par \uc2 But he came out and said, you know, we have to stop the rhetoric, we have to stop the lies about the election. So when I spoke with him yesterday, I asked him if there are no issues, if there are no widespread issues that would result in a different outcome when it comes to Georgia`s elections, why would it be necessary to make some changes?\par \par \uc2 And he did acknowledge that some of these pieces of legislation go too far. But he said that there would be some components of them that would, in his words, shore up confidence when it comes to elections, and shore up security, and he says that`s the point.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you mentioned, Chuck, there is a good deal of opposition to this. Not only from voting rights groups, Fair Fight, of course, the group that was founded by Stacey Abrams, but even some business leaders in the community are kind of dipping their toe into the pool of politics and kind of putting out statements reiterating the importance of voting rights. So one thing that`s important to mention here, Chuck, is that it`s very unlikely that the versions that we see right now are the ones that would ultimately pass and make their way to Governor Kemp`s desk.\par \par \uc2 There would be a number of changes that have to go to the other sides of the House and Senate, but certainly a lot of opposition right now, as it stands, a lot of people speaking out against it. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Blayne Alexander with this reporting. And every time I still just sort of am shocked that all these Republican-backed changes to voting reforms that Republicans pushed so hard in the `90s and more absentee and things like that. But so be it.\par \par \uc2 Blayne Alexander, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back with a new look at how Joe Biden made it to the White House.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As president, Joe Biden is trying to steer the nation out of a crisis. In a way it echoes some of the challenges he faced to become president. In their new book "Lucky" NBC`s Jonathan Allen and "The Hill`s" Amy Harness detailed Biden`s path to victory via a campaign that nearly imploded around him at times.\par \par \uc2 Biden`s ability to traverse the challenges of the campaign was doubted by many Democrats, including his own campaign staffers and perhaps, most importantly, former President Obama. They write, "Obama wanted to make sure that Joe`s long-term reputation wasn`t going to be endangered. He certainly wasn`t confident Joe was going to win. No one was. But win or lose, Obama looked at the coming primaries, the wild west of politics, a lawless and unpredictable melee that could leave Biden not just beaten but permanently scarred."\par \par \uc2 And Jonathan Allen and Amie Parnes join me now.\par \par \uc2 And, Jon, let me start with you. Your book`s title is "Lucky." So are we to assume that is the conclusion you draw -- you guys draw here that Joe Biden is simply lucky to be president?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN ALLEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Joe Biden got lucky, the Democratic Party got lucky. What we saw after the election with the republic tested in a way that it never has been before and still standing firm, the nation is lucky for that. It means all of those things, Chuck. But we do take you through in thorough detail and new reporting that folks haven`t seen before just how dark the dark days were for Biden during the primary and all the breaks that went his way.\par \par \uc2 But I should say this, you know, lucky is not a pejorative term in any way, shape or form. Joe Biden set a message for his campaign and he stuck to it, the battle for the soul of America. He was able to put his policies into that message. It was something that the public wanted. And so luck was the residue of design here. He just was prepared to take advantage of those breaks. And if you listen to Joe Biden, he`s somebody who talks about luck all the time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Amy, I want to build on this discipline issue with him. On page 411 here you guys write, "Ridiculed for so long about his lack of discipline as a candidate, Biden stuck to his theory of the case of rather than a path forward he proposed backtracking to the era of a pre-Trump era. And despite a resume as strong as any modern presidential contender and a lifetime of building political relationships, Democratic voters have vetted and rejected two dozen candidates before settling on him. But he stuck to his theory of the case that during a brutal primary slog and in the face of pressure from his allies even members of his own staff to change his strategy and tactics in the general election."\par \par \uc2 The point is he did something that he was notorious for never doing. He stuck to a message, he stuck to a campaign plan, and we`re actually seeing it bleed into his habits early on in the first hundred days here. Do we think it sticks?\par \par \uc2 AMIE PARNES, THE HILL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: I mean that`s TBD, Chuck. But I think, yes, he is very scripted right now. And I think that`s something that he learned over the course of the campaign. I think that he did have some lucky breaks. And we talk about this a lot in the book. There were few people to sign on with him very early on. As you mentioned, President Obama was reluctant to do so. He had a meeting early on with his aides, with Biden`s aides basically wanting to reassure how they were going to run this campaign, how disciplined they would be because he was afraid that it would tarnish not only his friend and partner, former partner`s reputation but his own and the Obama legacy.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that was something that they took very seriously. So I think -- you know, and then he caught lucky breaks along the way. I mean, one thing that we point to is how Elizabeth Warren in a very key moment when Michael Bloomberg was getting so much traction took out Michael Bloomberg in a campaign -- in a debate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PARNES: And we`ve talked about how she did that and how she prepared for that comment. So we take you really inside all of these lucky breaks throughout the book.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting, Jon, the fact that -- and you know, I`ve talked to somebody very close to who sort of admitted, hey, he was right, I was wrong. There is -- I`m curious now, does that give him more of a mandate that this is his Democratic Party? You know, one of the favorite critiques on the right is, oh, he`s not really in charge, it`s really Obama, or it`s really Kamala, or it`s really Ron Klain.\par \par \uc2 Yet what your book describes and what a lot of our reporting says is Joe Biden stuck to his guns. Everybody else tried to talk him out of it. He was right, they were wrong.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: And Chuck, we go to it in the book. I mean, a perfect example, just a quick anecdote, Biden is in this place during the racial unrest, protests for racial justice and some of the violence accompanying that, what Donald Trump was doing in response.\par \par \uc2 And there were people in his own campaign, high up in his own campaign who said you need to move to where the activists are on this. What you need to do is apologize for the crime bill. What you need to do is embrace the slogan "Defund the Police."\par \par \uc2 And Biden just wouldn`t do it, he just wouldn`t budge. He went out and did an op-ed in "USA Today" and said I am not for defund the police. If you ask congressional leaders, what they will tell you is one of the reasons House members lost so many seats this last time is because Republicans made such an effective use of that on the campaign trail and they were not able to push Biden into that corner because he was so firm on it.\par \par \uc2 Now that may not be the policy that every Democrat wants, but I`m sure that almost every Democrat if not every Democrat is pretty happy that Joe Biden is the president right now and not Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good afternoon, I`m Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210306eh3500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for March 2, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Pete Williams, Garrett Haake, Paul Butler, Andrew  Weissmann \par \uc2 9,216 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY (R-LA): The Chinese Communist Party is destroying it. If Congress passed the bill and said to the good people of Hong Kong who yearn for freedom, come to America. We`re going to follow our friends in Britain and say, come here. You want to get out from under the thumb of the Communist Party, come to America. We welcome you. Do you think the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and law enforcement has the ability to screen for spies?\par \par \uc2 One of the criticisms of the proposition I just stated isn`t well, we would be living in spies. You think based on your knowledge of security, that we could catch most of the spies?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, FBI: Well, I yield to no one in my faith and confidence in the great work of the men and women of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . But I will tell you that the Chinese counterintelligence threat is the greatest threat -- certainly, the greatest counterintelligence threat that we face as a country and the sheer number of what we would refer to as non-traditional collectors working on behalf of the Chinese Communist Party is something that is a massive resource challenge for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: OK, that was a probably an unfair question. I`m not asking to guarantee anything. And the few seconds I have left and begging the indulgence of our esteemed Chairman who`s doing a much better job than Durbin by the way. Oh, he`s back. The Horowitz report, can you tell me how many people you have referred for prosecution at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as a result of the Horowitz report?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: For prosecution or for discipline?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: For prosecution first. Just give me numbers because I don`t want to abuse my time.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Well, you know, the prosecution issue related to anything to do with the Horowitz report is in the hands of the inspector --\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: How many have you fired? I get it. How many have you fired?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: So, all the people -- all the -- most of the people involved in Horowitz report are former employees. Of the ones who are current, every single one of them, even if mentioned only in passing, has been referred to our office of professional responsibility, which is our disciplinary arm. Now, that piece, and this is important, that piece of it because we`re cooperating fully with Mr. Durham`s investigation, at his request, we had slow that process down to allow his criminal investigation to proceed.\par \par \uc2 So, at the moment, that process is still underway in order to make sure that we`re being appropriately sensitive to the criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: OK, so you`ve had the hold up as a result of the criminal mess? I`m sorry, I went over, Mr. Chairman. And you`re I`m sure glad you here. Booker was just screwing everything up.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): Senator Padilla, and I`m sure you`ll do a better job than the previous questioner.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ALEX PADILLA (D-CA): Thank you, Mr. Chairman. I will do my best. Director Wray, other members of the committee have raised their concerns over the increase in hate crimes in recent years against Latinos, African Americans, LGBTQIA community, and others. Over the last year, we`ve seen a significant increase in violence specifically against Asian Americans, including in my home state of California.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in this hearing, members raised the recent lethal attacks in San Francisco and New York as some examples. Just last week in Sacramento, California, a man returned to the premises of an Asian family-run butcher shop with a mutilated cat carcass for no apparent reason other than to stoke fear. The incident is currently under investigation as a hate crime.\par \par \uc2 It`s clear to me that this uptick in violence against Asian Americans is the direct result of racist rhetoric used by political leaders with intentional regard to the coronavirus pandemic, such as when former President Donald Trump has used offensive references to the Coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, on March 2020, an \uc2 FBI\uc2  assessment conducted by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Houston office and distributed to law enforcement across the country, and I`ll quote, "It will it predicted a future surge in hate crimes against Asian Americans due to the spread of Coronavirus."\par \par \uc2 And I want to quote from that assessment. "The \uc2 FBI\uc2  makes this assessment based on the assumption that a portion of the U.S. public will associate COVID-19 with China and Asian American populations."\par \par \uc2 So, I know Senator Hirono has already raised the topic but I wanted to ask a couple more specific follow-up questions. To what extent, Director Wray, do you believe the increase in violence against Asian Americans has been influenced by reckless rhetoric concerning the pandemic? Two, what steps does the \uc2 FBI\uc2  taken to address the increase in hate crimes against Asian Americans? And three, part of that, I hope, is an update on how the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is proactively working to overcome trust issues in immigrant communities and communities of color.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: So, Senator, let me try to take all three questions in turn. First, I want to be careful as FBI Director not to start to get in the business of kind of weighing in and characterizing rhetoric, because as you know, you know, we focus on the violence, not on the ideology or the motivation. So, I would largely, on that issue, just reaffirm the intelligence assessment that`s already been produced through the appropriate channels.\par \par \uc2 On the second two questions, in terms of trying to be proactive, a number of things that we`re doing. So, in addition to our investigations, which we -- which we work closely with state and local, in some cases, tribal, and other federal law enforcement agencies, and that we have some cases, we`ll be able to bring federal cases, working with our civil rights division counterparts, the prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 In other cases, even if it`s going to be a state or local charge, which sometimes may be the best charge available based on the facts, we are trying to provide forensic support, other kinds of expertise and experience to help support the state-local prosecution.\par \par \uc2 We`re also trying to do a lot more public outreach, which is both with the community itself, but also with state local law enforcement. And in some cases, field offices are bringing them together. So, it`s a group discussion, which I think has a lot of value.\par \par \uc2 We`re also providing trainings. We`re doing a lot of training. We`ve done hundreds of seminars, workshops, for both law enforcement and community groups, religious organizations, and so forth. And that includes hate crimes training, not just for the hundreds of special agents at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , but for thousands, thousands of police officers.\par \par \uc2 When it comes specifically to the last part of your question, the trust issues, you know, part of that is demonstrating through our work, that we`re going to do the right thing in the right way, and that we`re going to respond just as aggressively and professionally to crimes against them as victims, as they see what other kinds of crimes.\par \par \uc2 And we have done just since March of 2020, I think, we`ve done 60 over 60 liaison events, or trainings specifically geared towards the Asian American Pacific Islander community. And we`ve also tried to put out intelligence reports like the one you referenced that call out the issue.\par \par \uc2 PADILLA: Great. But I think it just yet another example of the value of increased improve diversity, not just throughout the ranks of the agency, but especially amongst agents and amongst leadership. Now, your examples of collaboration with local law enforcement is actually a great transition to my next question.\par \par \uc2 Some of the most striking revelations in the aftermath of the January 6th insurrection here in the Capitol were reports that some members of the Capitol Police were sympathetic to the insurrectionists, that they pose for photos, provided directions, and may have even expressed support for those attacking the very building they`re sworn to protect.\par \par \uc2 I understand that six Capitol Police officers have been suspended and at least 29 others are under investigation for their alleged role in the attack. We`ve also learned that among those participating in the interaction were numerous off-duty law enforcement officers from around the country rooting out white supremacists and right-wing extremists is a challenge that local law enforcement agencies and even the United States military is facing across the country directory.\par \par \uc2 Director Wray, how is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  assisting law enforcement agencies across the country to root out white supremacy or other forms of extremism? And do you believe there`s a concerted effort by right-wing extremists to infiltrate law enforcement agencies?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: So, I guess a few things I would say on this topic. Certainly, it is true that in some instances, as we`re continuing to investigate the January 6th attack, there have been some instances of current or particular former military or law enforcement who participated and we want to pursue those cases just as aggressively as we would anybody else.\par \par \uc2 We are also though which may go more to the heart of your question, when appropriate, referring individuals to their -- the department that employs them for possible administrative or disciplinary action under their rules as appropriate.\par \par \uc2 We work very closely with both our law enforcement partners and our military partners in their efforts to address any kind of violent extremism that may be in their midst. We view that as an effective kind of insider threat, if you will. And they do too.\par \par \uc2 And I want to be clear that in my experience, and I`m dealing with our law enforcement partners and our military partners every single day, the vast, vast, vast, vast, vast, vast majority of the men and women in uniform, both in law enforcement and the military, are brave, selfless, professional, high integrity individuals. But when there are bad apples in the midst, we work with our partners to try to get ahead of it.\par \par \uc2 PADILLA: I agree with that final statement, but the threat and the danger that those few bad apples presents are to be taken very seriously. I understand. So, I hope to work with you possibly to develop further best practices and protocols to be shared with agencies around the country.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PADILLA: Thank you, Mr. Chairman.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Thank you. Senator Tillis.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): Thank you, Mr. Chairman. Director Wray, thank you for being here, for your years of service, and for the great work that so many of the people in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  do every single day. Before I ask you a question, though, I think it`s very important. I was the last member to leave the Senate chamber and observed the Capitol Police doing an extraordinary job in shepherding every single member and every single staff to safety.\par \par \uc2 So, I hope as they`re reviewing some of the officers, we should go through a review, I hope that we`re tracking their entire pattern of movement that day. I think you`ll see many of them were doing -- putting themselves between us and violence, and we need to make sure that we treat them fairly.\par \par \uc2 But back on the rioters on January 6th, can you give me a rough idea just of the crimes that many of them are being charged with or being pursued through investigations?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Well, we`re using a variety of statutory weapons. There are certainly assault charges. There are a number of charges that are specific -- by that, I mean assault against, you know, federal law enforcement, including the Capitol Police -- the brave men and women of the Capitol Police that I think you rightly credited there. There`s also various charges related to destruction of federal property, things along those lines.\par \par \uc2 We are now starting to begin to see, as we`ve sort of taken care of the most immediate easiest to prove -- I hate to use the word like low-hanging fruit charges, but now we`re starting to get more of the more advanced charges, if you will.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we`ve had some conspiracy charges recently, some of the people that are more involved with different forms of planning or coordination or preparation. Some of those charges are starting to happen. And I would expect to see that continue.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: And incidentally, Mr. Chair, I want to associate myself with Senator Graham`s comments earlier. I think your threats are going up. And we`ve got to match that with additional resources. So, I look forward to the committee continuing that.\par \par \uc2 Would you see any difference between the charges, the investigations that you`re pursuing in the events on January 6th, and charges it should be pursued against federal buildings and federal law enforcement officers being harmed in Seattle or Portland? Are there active investigations for either of those two events? And would they be treated any differently?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: As I think said in response to an earlier question, we are equal opportunity. So by that, I mean, we don`t care what ideology motivates you, if you`re engaged in violence, that violates federal law, we`re coming for you. And that`s true for the events over the summer, and some of the domestic terrorism that occurred --\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Are there active investigations related to those events.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Thank you. You know, I felt like you mentioned in response to one of the members` questions about this just increasing, the volumes increasing. I introduced in the last Congress, I intend to reintroduce a bill called Protect and Serve, which increases penalties for rioters for assaults on federal officers and more significant consequences. Do you think that those would be helpful tools for law enforcement --\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Certainly --\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: -- and for prosecution?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Sorry, I didn`t mean to --\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: I think, while I`m not familiar with the specific bill, I want to enthusiastically support the idea of looking at everything we can do to protect the men women in law enforcement. The threats, the violence against law enforcement in this country is one of the most tragic and sometimes least talked about challenges we face.\par \par \uc2 This year alone, this year alone, an officer is shot and killed in the line of duty at a rate of more than one a week. And when you think about what it takes for someone, to be willing to sacrifice his or her life for a total stranger, and how unusual that is just to begin with, and then you add on top of that, somebody who`s willing to do that, get up and do that every single day, day after day after day, and they never know when that day might be the day that they don`t come home to their families.\par \par \uc2 And so then you put that in the context of the way in which some violent opportunists or domestic terrorists hijack some of these protests, whether it`s the ones over the summer, or the ones on the sixth. And now you got some of these same selfless individuals who are in many cases killed. But for everyone who`s killed, there`s someone who survived, thank goodness, but whose life and his family`s life is forever altered. And I don`t think we could ever and should never take for granted those people because they protect all of us.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: I agree. I`m curious, with all the discussion of defund the police and systemic racism and all law enforcement agencies, some of the dialogue that`s out there, have you seen any measurable decrease in the number of people who are trying to apply to come in to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ? I know I`m saying it and State Troopers are telling me their applications for academies are down by over 70 percent. We see people accelerating their retirements. Do we have any potential threat out there either within the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or for law enforcement in general of having fewer people willing to get into this profession?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: So, certainly, when it comes to state local law enforcement -- because I talked to many of the same chiefs and sheriffs you do, the recruiting challenge is a real concern. It comes up all the time. It`s something we need to be concerned about. And all of these trends that we`re talking about will have -- I think we run the risk of that just making that trend worse.\par \par \uc2 At the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , happily, because we can all use some good news from time to time, last year and the year before, we tripled the number of people, Americans across the country applying to be special agent. So, when I took the job, it was around 11,000 or so a year of people applying to be special agents. In 2019, it was about 36,000.\par \par \uc2 And then last year, even with the pandemic, it was even higher than that. And that`s the highest number of people applying to work at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agents to put their lives on the line in about a decade. So, we`d like to think our work is earning people who want to come work for us. And we`re grateful for that.\par \par \uc2 And hopefully, we can do our part to try to encourage more people, because we can only take so many of them to pursue law enforcement jobs in other agencies.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Well, that is good news. I just wish you all the best of luck in prosecuting every single person that you can that reached the Capitol and every single person on the grounds who assaulted or threatened a police officer. If there`s anything that we can do to help. And I will follow up with the department to get your perspective as to whether or not you think the Protect and Serve Act would be helpful. I think that it will be but I`d like your professional judgment. Thank you, Mr. Chair.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Thank you, Senator. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Thanks, Senator. Senator Ossoff.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JON OSSOFF (D-GA): Thank you, Mr. Chairman. And Director Wray, greetings from your home state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OSSOFF: Thank you for your service. There`s been a significant increase in shootings and violent crime nationwide over the last 18 months. There were at least 10 people shot in Atlanta, Georgia on Sunday. What does the \uc2 FBI\uc2  assess is driving this crime wave?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Well, certainly, I am following the same trends you are with concern not just -- not just in Atlanta, but in other cities around the country. I`m not sure there`s any single factor that`s driving it. I think it`s a variety of things. We are seeing, you know, some of it may be, you know, the pandemic itself in its own way has had an impact.\par \par \uc2 You know, people who are maybe not at jobs or not, you know, not in school or not otherwise available in that more -- there`s more potential for wrongdoing to occur. We talked about some of the challenges with local police departments. And some of the issues there, in terms of their recruiting and staffing, a lot of them are understaffed in addition to the recruiting challenge. So, that`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 So, the variety of drivers that we think contribute to it, but the violent crime problem over the last year in particular, 2020, is something that is a great concern and that we`re very wearily keeping our eye on it doesn`t get the same kind of headlines than some of the other threats we`ve talked about today. But as your question I think quite rightly implies, it`s a subject that`s near and dear to the hearts of all the people we know back home.\par \par \uc2 OSSOFF: Yes. With that many shootings in Atlanta on Sunday alone, this increase in violent crime is of grave concern to Georgians and people across the country. Will you work with this committee and my office to try to refine that assessment of the drivers of this violent crime wave?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: We`d be pleased to do that. I commend you for your interest in the violent crime problem back in our home state.\par \par \uc2 OSSOFF: I appreciate that, Director Wray. Next week will be the first anniversary of the shooting death of Breonna Taylor, a young woman who died when police officers in Louisville, Kentucky entered her home with a battering ram executing a no-knock search warrant connected to a narcotics investigation.\par \par \uc2 Miss Taylor was not the subject of that warrant. There`s obviously been deepening and grave concern about equal justice, due process, the extent of brutality, harassment, discrimination faced by Black Americans in the criminal justice system growing over the last year given incidents such as this one.\par \par \uc2 Without commenting on the specifics of the late Miss Taylor`s case, is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  prioritizing investigations of cases involving color of law violations under 18 USC 242? And what resources have you instructed your field offices to commit to those investigations?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: So, as you say, like I can`t discuss the Taylor case specifically. We have an ongoing federal investigation there. But we are definitely trying to push forward on color of law cases. We have -- we do that through our civil rights division within our Criminal Investigative division.\par \par \uc2 We are, in addition to the investigations we`re pursuing, and we have quite a number around the country, we`re also trying to contribute by doing different forms of training and outreach to state local police departments so they understand better, kind of where the --- where the lines are, and where we fit into it. So that`s, that`s a, you know, part of it as well.\par \par \uc2 We`re also trying to contribute to the situation by encouraging better reporting. And we`ve had a lot of conversations this morning in other contexts about statistics and reporting. And when it comes to use of force, we are trying to build out a use of force database that involves use of force by police departments, law enforcement agencies around the country.\par \par \uc2 It`s voluntary. We know we can`t mandate all these local police departments to supply the information. But we are doing a lot to encourage them to submit their data. And my pitch to them has been, we`re going to be talking about these issues, no matter what. And we should all want the conversations to be based on the actual facts and the actual data as opposed to what some random person thinks the facts or data are.\par \par \uc2 And so we`ve -- we`re out now reached one of the thresholds where we can start doing some of the reporting related to this effort. We need to get to a certain threshold statistically. I think it`s like 80 percent or something at police departments before the data is considered statistically reliable. But so, investigations, training and outreach, more complete statistical reporting and use of force.\par \par \uc2 OSSOFF: Thank you, Director Wray. And I know the committee is considering legislation to make that data more available to you from local agencies. And will you provide to the committee and to my office and accounting of the FBI`s investigations over the last two years colorable law violations title 18 USC 242?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: I`m happy to see if we can provide you some more information on that.\par \par \uc2 OSSOFF: OK, I look forward to that.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 OSSOFF: Director Wray, is the recently revealed \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  breach, a major cybersecurity breach, a failure, a counterintelligence failure by the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WRAY: I don`t know that I would describe it that way. Certainly, the \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  intrusion is something that reflects a trend that we`ve been calling out for some time, but it takes it to the next level. So, for years we`ve been warning of both China and Russia in efforts to inject malware in and to undermine our trust in software that organizations all rely on.\par \par \uc2 We`ve also called out the intrusions into managed service providers which allow our adversaries to reach a far greater number of networks through sort of single entry points. The \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  intrusion essentially takes us to the next level purposely infecting product that;` widely used to manage networks.\par \par \uc2 And so, the scope, the scale that somewhat indiscriminate nature of the intrusion is something we take very seriously with our partners.\par \par \uc2 OSSOFF: But Director Wray, my time being limited.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OSSOFF: If I might surely, for malware to be embedded on sensitive U.S. networks at that scale and without detection for that duration must constitute a counterintelligence failure?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Well, I think it`s a cybersecurity issue. I think of counterintelligence a little bit differently than just the kind of context you`re talking about. But as I said, in some ways, it`s analogous to what I said about some of the earlier threats. You know, we -- our goal is to try to battle thousands. And anytime we don`t battle a thousand, we`re obviously looking hard to see what we can do better to prevent that from happening again.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to cyber intrusions, in particular, though, we have long past the world where it`s a question of if an organization is going to be the victim of cyber intrusion or in the world of when. And the question is not whether somebody was subject to the cyber intrusion, but how fast is it get detected, how well does it get mitigated, etcetera.\par \par \uc2 The scope, the scale, the range of attack methods, the number of adversaries involved in sophisticated cyberattacks dwarfs what it was when I was in law enforcement and national security before. And a huge amount of the information, a huge amount of the information for America in particular is in the hands of the private sector.\par \par \uc2 And so, unlike some of the other kinds of threats we`ve been talking about here this morning, the partnership between the Intelligence Community, other federal agencies, and the private sector is at the heart of the issue.\par \par \uc2 OSSOFF: Thank you, Director Wray. Thank you, Mr. Chairman.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Thank you, Senator Ossoff. I`m glad you brought up \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  because I think it`s the first reference at this hearing. I`d like to do a follow-up before I recognize Senator Blackburn. So, what do we do about it? We know that we don`t have an extradition agreement with Russia. So, even finding and naming the hackers doesn`t lead to any punishment of them. What is our response as a deterrent to future cyber intrusion?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: So, Mr. Chairman, I think discussing the response in any detail is probably something that would be better done in a classified setting. That by itself might give you a little bit of a hint. But what we have found -- speaking more generally, what we have found over the last couple years in the cyber arena, in particular, is that we are at our most effective when we have joint sequence operations, that essentially, think of it as the -- having the hole bigger than the sum of the parts.\par \par \uc2 So, you mentioned a few of the things that can be done. Any one of those things by themselves isn`t going to get the job done. But if you start putting some of those things together in a way where each amplifies the effect of the other, we have actually seen some pretty good results with some of our adversaries.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s everything from not just the law enforcement piece, its foreign partner participation, its private sector hardening, its Treasury sanctions, it`s a whole host of things. But when you put them together sequenced, while I would never suggest to you and you would never believe me if I did suggest to you that that`s going to somehow just eliminate the problem, it does push the adversary back and slow their progress. But this is going to be a long, hard slog.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Thanks, Director. I believe that Senator Blackburn is on remote. Senator? Technical difficulties. I want to -- she`s waited patiently, so I`ll give her another minute.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): Yes, Mr. Chairman, I`m here.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: OK, Senator, you have the floor.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: Thank you, sir. I appreciate that. And, yes, I think I don`t have enough bandwidth -- something is wrong with the video transmission. We`ll just go to audio.\par \par \uc2 To the chairman`s following up, Director Wray, I would just like to add, I think it would be excellent -- and, Mr. Chairman, I would offer to you -- as we`re looking at what is happening with cryptocurrencies and with the growth in that marketplace.\par \par \uc2 And how this currency could be used when it comes to cybercrimes and to terrorism, I think a briefing from you all on what you are tracking and what you`re seeing would be very helpful to us.\par \par \uc2 So I would just -- I would commend that as a second place for us to go. And, Director Wray, I want to say thank you to you for being here.\par \par \uc2 And thank you to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the U.S. attorney`s offices in Tennessee for the work they did after January 6th, striking down and catching the rioters from Tennessee that had taken part in those activities.\par \par \uc2 And there are a lot of questions that still remain. One -- and I know Senator Kennedy mentioned this -- but the National Guard and the timeline that was there, I`d like for you to speak to that if you can, the day of timeline.\par \par \uc2 Because I understand that Mayor Bowser spoke with the secretary of the Army twice, at 1:34 and at 2:22, and with the chief`s son. He spoke to the guard, the D.C. guard commanding general at 1:49. And the guard began to mobilize at 3:00, and the troops did not arrive until 5:40.\par \par \uc2 Is that your understanding of the timeline?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Senator, I appreciate the question. And I`m glad you tied me back to my exchange with Senator Kennedy because I fear that I may have contributed to a little bit of a muddle here.\par \par \uc2 First, let me say that my understanding is that -- on the question of authority is that the D.C. mayor and the \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  can ask for the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 That the secretary of defense has the authority on federal land. The secretary of the Army has authority on, in effect, D.C. or, when it`s not D.C., state land.\par \par \uc2 I really don`t have the specifics on exactly who requested what and when. I understand why it`s a topic of keen interest. But I, as FBI director, are not intimately involved in that process, so I don`t want to add to any confusion that`s out there.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: OK. So, then, it would be appropriate that we direct that, first of all, to the guard command and to the secretary of the Army. Is that what I`m hearing you say?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: I think so, yes.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: OK. All right. That sounds great. Thank you for that clarification because I do think that we do need some clarification there.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to some of the riots that have taken place around the country and the crime that has seemed to spike this year.\par \par \uc2 Previous to this, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  participated in Operation Legend. Of course, Memphis, Tennessee was a part of that effort. So we thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you, is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  tracking extremist groups like Antifa or other radicalism that are connected to violence in cities across the country, the violence and the looting that has taken place?\par \par \uc2 And we know Operation Legend wound down and ended in January. So how is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  going to continue assisting local law enforcement in these cities where you have these riots that have taken place?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Well, Senator, I appreciate the question. I think you`ve touched on two very important but distinct topics.\par \par \uc2 So one is the violence on our streets in a lot of our major cities, including Memphis, that Operation Legend was designed to address.\par \par \uc2 And the other is the violence that`s occurring amidst protests, where otherwise peaceful protests are hijacked by people who engage in violent criminal behavior.\par \par \uc2 So on the first, on what I would call the more sort of traditional violent crime side, in effect, the Operation Legend side, if I can just use that as a shorthand, we do think that was a successful operation, but it was, by its very nature, finite in duration.\par \par \uc2 What we`re doing since then is trying to work with our Safe Streets Task Forces, which have representatives of state and local and other federal agencies, and to try to bring a strategic intelligence-driven approach to the violent crime problem.\par \par \uc2 What I have found -- and I have talked with state and local police chiefs in all 50 states -- is that each city has its own idiosyncrasies. There may be certain common trends.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, there`s usually some kind of, in effect, "tail wagging the dog" that is contributing disproportionately to the violence crime problem to that community.\par \par \uc2 If everybody can be working together in an intelligence-driven way, they can prioritize the impact and dismantle the enterprise as opposed to just kind of pushing the problem around.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: OK. Let`s me - not to cut you off -\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: -- but to jump in there because, you`re correct, there are different sets of issues around the different types of crime. I understand that. And I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 But I think part of the frustration is -- let`s take July 4th last year with the Hatfield federal courthouse in Portland, with the fire that was started.\par \par \uc2 How did the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and federal and local law enforcement agencies attempt to track down those that were responsible? Was this an extremist group or groups, or was it individuals, like a lone actor, which you mentioned earlier?\par \par \uc2 And, of course, in Nashville on Christmas day, we saw the actions of a lone actor. And separately, at some point, I would like to get an update from you on that.\par \par \uc2 But let`s talk about what you`re doing to track those groups that are there, or like in Seattle, with the Capitol Hill autonomous zone, why they`re just really flouting the rule of law and trying to abolish a police presence.\par \par \uc2 How are you tracking these anarchist groups who are planning attacks, who are occupying public spaces? And what type of work are you doing to help protect communities from this?\par \par \uc2 WRAY: So, sure. That`s the second topic of the two that we touched on.\par \par \uc2 We do have a number of domestic terrorism investigations -- we would call them anarchist violent extremism investigations - into individuals, some of the most dangerous individuals involved in conduct, in particular, over the summer.\par \par \uc2 We are looking at everything from taxes to funding to logistics. And we`re pursuing all available charges against them.\par \par \uc2 I think I may have mentioned in response to an earlier question that, last year, in 2020, we arrested more anarchist violent extremists than the prior three years combined.\par \par \uc2 But in addition, I would say that in some of the activity you described from over the summer, when it`s targeting federal buildings, there are certain charges that may be available there, as was true with the courthouse in Portland, and as is true, obviously, with the capitol on the 6th.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to non-federal facilities, sometimes the charges end up being state and local charges where we work closely with and support our state and local partners as they bring charges.\par \par \uc2 So we are continuing to move full speed ahead. We`ve increased significantly the number of investigations into the kind of activity you`re describing, and we`re going to keep at it.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Mr. Chairman.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Thank you, Senator Blackburn.\par \par \uc2 As we conclude the hearing, in the area of shameless self-promotion, I`m not going to send you a QFR, but I`d like to send you a copy of my Domestic Terrorism Prevention Act and ask for your reaction to it.\par \par \uc2 You may suggest some changes to make it more effective, and I would appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 It`s been a while since you`ve been before the committee, and we`ve certainly tried your patience today, but you`ve been excellent in your presentation.\par \par \uc2 I just want to thank you and the men and women of the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  for the sacrifices they make to keep America safe. This meeting of the Judiciary Committee will stand adjourned.\par \par \uc2 WRAY: Thank you, Mr. Chairman.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Hello there. Welcome to Tuesday, and a truncated MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd. You`ve obviously -- we`ve all been watching FBI director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  testify publicly before the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 . This is the first time he`s testified since the January 6th siege on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a lot to unpack from his testimony. Wray was grilled about the FBI`s intelligence failures surrounding the attack. He said the agency quickly communicated to law enforcement authorities in three different ways -- raw and unverified intelligence about a threat to Congress on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps more importantly Wray also warned that the threat of domestic violent extremism, the kind which fueled the siege, remains one of the biggest security threats to this country and he painted a portrait of an \uc2 FBI\uc2  that is struggling to wrap its arms around the extent of what he admitted was a metastasizing threat. The agency currently has about 2,000 investigations into domestic extremists under way.\par \par \uc2 Just think about it. That`s a big number. While Wray said the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is determined to bat a thousand at thwarting future attacks it was fairly clear from his testimony the agency still does not fully understand why it missed the warning signs on January 6th. But despite hundreds of criminal cases and countless investigations about what did happen, there seems to be lingering concerns that something like it could happen again.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, we`ve got Garrett Haake, he is on Capitol Hill for us. Pete Williams, our justice correspondent, also with us is Andrew Weissmann, a former FBI general counsel, a senior member of the Mueller probe, and he`s also an NBC News legal analyst, and Paul Butler, former federal prosecutor, Georgetown law professor and NBC News legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Pete Williams, I want to start with you. I have to say, I think perhaps -- well, I`ll need to ask you the biggest revelation that you took away from this. Is it the 2,000 cases? Is it him admitting how much white supremacist groups are sort of at the top of all of this racially motivated violence? What stood out to you, Pete?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, not the white supremacist part because that`s been very clear from the beginning as the \uc2 FBI\uc2  began to file these charges, and they`re now up to well over 200 cases that have been charged -- I`m sorry, approaching 300 cases that have been charged and many more under investigation.\par \par \uc2 The 2,000 number was a revelation. That`s double what it was a year ago. And that`s a huge increase. That`s an enormous commitment of manpower. So I think for the FBI director to say, look, we were -- you know, we were warning people, expect violence around the election, it could be around the election or something afterwards, or the inauguration, and then we had this specific -- one specific piece of intelligence from Norfolk about the possibility of attacking the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 He did say that it`s clear that people were engaged in planning but, Chuck, I have to say, having looked at all the court documents the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says in criminal complaints and in affidavits, and in indictments in the sort of narrative section that there were plans to attack the Capitol on January 6th. I have yet to see a specific piece of evidence in any of these charges who was actually talking about storming the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 There`s lots of talk about violence in the streets, make members of Congress, hear our roar. But I don`t think they`ve yet cracked the code on exactly whose idea this was, when it came -- when it was finally formulated, how much of this was a "come as you are" party, how much of it was people arriving in Washington with the intention of doing exactly what we saw on the 6th.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s still a big unresolved question.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Garrett Haake, I think if there was any agenda items you saw from the -- on the political side of the aisle, it was -- it seemed -- and I`m curious where you saw this. It seemed that Democrats wanted to make it clear of Antifa`s role or lack thereof in the Capitol siege and it seemed as if particularly at the end there you could hear from John Kennedy and Marsha Blackburn, they were basically looking to focus on any other issue other than January 6th.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I think that`s right. And keep in mind this is an oversight hearing. Although the January 6th element was the focus, members hadn`t had Wray in front of that panel for something like 20 months, so there was a lot of interest in other topics here but that said you saw it very early on from Chuck Grassley, the leading Republican on this panel, tried to introduce the idea of what about Antifa violence in places like Portland and Seattle.\par \par \uc2 And Wray wasn`t really willing to take the bait on that. He said, you know, we don`t look at this on a left-right ideological spectrum. We don`t really care about your politics or what you talk about. What we care about is the violence, and that`s the kind of thing that we`re investigating.\par \par \uc2 A lot of the other Republican senators took different tacts on this. You mentioned Blackburn, Tillis sort of brought this up again there at the end, but Republican senators also focused on some of their pet issues.\par \par \uc2 You had Josh Hawley and Mike Lee talking about, you know, was cell phone tracking data used, perhaps inappropriately to track down some of these suspects here as a way to talk about January 6th adjacent issues that are more politically comfortable to them than the actual issue of, you know, prosecuting these domestic terror cases but again Wray I think kept sort of steering things back in that general direction in part by talking about just what a huge commitment of resources this is for the bureau.\par \par \uc2 The thing I wrote down, 55 \uc2 FBI\uc2  field offices, all but one are involved in investigating different elements of the January 6th attack.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Andrew Weissmann, I want to jump off on that point to you which is 2,000 cases. Considering -- and frankly where \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  admits that the threat is metastasizing, and, you know, you can`t always discern what`s real and not on social media, which requires even more resources here, what kind of strain is this putting on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  based on your experience?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FORMER FBI GENERAL COUNSEL: I don`t think this is a strain on the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . They have a lot of agents across the country and internationally, this is a question of priorities. What I was struck by is that this was really a hearing with a lot of kid gloves. January 6th was such a huge failure and, you know, the FBI director certainly has to perry away criticism that`s unfair. And so there`s no question there`s a place for that.\par \par \uc2 But he also has to own up to the places where there is a failure. And I was struck by this -- the questioning was not sharp on that. And I don`t think he was particularly humble or contrite about looking at the bureau in terms of what it needed to do better in that situation. I thought that was a failure by the people asking the questions and the person giving the questions there.\par \par \uc2 For instance, when he said we`re an equal opportunity agency, I personally would have pounced on that. I would have said, really, then why is it on the same day, in the spring of last year, when there were Black Lives Matter protesters in New York City, people are arrested the same day, and you are not doing that in connection with January 6th? What is the reason for that? How equal opportunity is that?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you bring up another point, Paul, and Andrew, and Paul I want you to address this, and that is -- and it`s something you and I have talked about before, Paul Butler, which is, is the problem sort of a failure to believe their own raw intelligence? But I want to play one \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  clip on the issue of white supremacy terrorism. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: We the \uc2 FBI\uc2  over the course of 2020 put out a number of intelligence products, specifically warning about domestic violent extremism, including specifically warning about it in connection with the election, including specifically warning about that threat in relation to the election, continuing past election day itself and up through the inauguration. And including a product that I think we put out with DHS in December of 2020.\par \par \uc2 So we have tried to push out information. I`m reluctant to armchair quarterback anyone else in their jobs. I can tell you we at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are determined to do our part, our part to make sure that what happened on January 6th doesn`t happen again.\par \par \uc2 Racially motivated violent extremism specifically of the sort that advocates for the superiority of the white race is a persistent evolving threat, it`s the biggest chunk of our racially motivated violent extremism cases for sure, and racially motivated violent extremism is the biggest chunk of our domestic terrorism portfolio, if you will, overall.\par \par \uc2 I will also say that the same group of people we`re talking about have been responsible for the most lethal attacks over the last, say, decade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So Paul, it`s that last comment, it`s that last bite about -- and the way he spoke. And I`ll be frank with you, I feel like he spoke like a bureaucrat, trying to use too many words to describe the threat when basically he could have done it in short. This issue of essentially white nationalist groups becoming violent, or white supremacist groups becoming violent is a growing problem. He could have said it in about eight words.\par \par \uc2 And I can`t help but look at that, and it feels like the more he talks the more it sounds like it`s getting watered down. I don`t think that`s his intent. But it comes across that way.\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, NBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Director Wray is definitely on the defensive. The question is looking now less as a catastrophic intelligence failure and more like law enforcement didn`t act effectively on the intelligence that it had.\par \par \uc2 And so there`s a lot of finger-pointing between the Capitol Police, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the military about who knew what when. And so we heard director Taylor say -- Director Wray say that he`s been on this case for years, that he`s testified 12 times to Congress about dangers posed by homegrown terrorists that white supremacists are as great a threat to our national security as ISIS.\par \par \uc2 But did they act like they knew? And so when we look at the issue with this report that was received the day before the insurrection that warned of war coming and that these folks were going to try to take over the Capitol and spill blood. The director says he communicated that three different ways. But he didn`t call -- nobody called the D.C. police chief. And apparently none of the other officers acted on it in an effective way.\par \par \uc2 So, again, I think that`s either discounting the great threat posed by national security or incompetence. And the \uc2 FBI\uc2  remains the world`s best law enforcement agency. So you kind of want to rule out incompetence and make it that they just don`t understand the scope of the problem.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andrew, this wouldn`t be a good excuse, but it could be the following, which is the passive sort of tone and sort of, I`d say, persona that the FBI director had today.\par \par \uc2 How much of that is sort of after four years of getting brow-beaten by President Trump and constantly having him undermine anything you try to do, particularly on this topic, right, it seems as if he was afraid to even talk about the threat of white supremacy over the last three years, which is something we can`t help but notice now that Trump is gone.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Well, there`s an issue about why was there inaction. Is it race, is it, you know, politics, the fear of the White House? But I don`t want to make light of this. But, you know, if you`re not willing to do your job, then you shouldn`t be the FBI director.\par \par \uc2 If you`re sitting on information that there is going to be violence, you`re the frigin` head of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . You know, you have a lot of power in terms of commanding your own people but also of talking to DOJ and getting people on board.\par \par \uc2 And if that`s going to lead to your being fired by a lame duck president, so be it. You know, people, as we know, people died as a result of the lack of preparation and action that was really striking in comparison to what happened in Black Lives Matter protests. And so, you know, to say I had a lot of information and we were warning people, that`s actually damning. That`s not a good fact for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and for the other law enforcement agencies.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Pete Williams, how would the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- how would \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  respond to this? How are they going to push back on this notion that they`ve been too passive on this issue? Will they lay this at Trump`s feet? How would they respond to this criticism?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think what they`re saying here is -- you know, and John Cornyn made a point today that perhaps this is like the comment that was made after 9/11 that it was really a failure of imagination. Any of these law enforcement agencies knew that, you know, remember, what the intel report was the night before the riots that was briefed by the Capitol Police. People are coming, there are violent extremists among them. They`re armed. They`re mad.\par \par \uc2 They see this as their last hope, they`re desperate. But, it said, we don`t expect to see very much violence. So that was the feeling. They knew these people were coming, but they had -- nobody thought that they were going to try to get into the Capitol. And I think, to me, watching all of these hearings so far, that`s what I keep waiting to hear.\par \par \uc2 Where was that intelligence that this is what they were going to do? Because all the law enforcement people were ready for sort of MAGA 3 like the demonstrations, the two that we had in Washington leading up to this with more pockets of violence. But where was the intelligence, or really was there any intelligence that said this is what they plan to do? You have to assume that some of them did plan it. They came with climbing equipment and weapons.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But where was that plan formed? Is that something that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  knew.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Pete, are they trying --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Or was it knowable?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Do you get a sense they`re trying too hard to connect all the dots? Is it possible you basically had 20 different groups planning 20 different ways to -- you know, there was less -- they all agreed on the goal of storming the Capitol, but they didn`t coordinate with each other?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So I think a good answer to that --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are we trying too hard to find one large -- yes. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, a good answer to that came from a study that came out today from the center on extremism at \uc2 George Washington University\uc2 , which says there were sort of three groups. The largest group were sort of people kind of swept up in it, had no relationship to each other.\par \par \uc2 They all came on their own. The second group had some sort of family connections. They were kind of like-minded into QAnon, very much supportive of the president thinking the election was stolen. And then you have this hard-core group that were at the front of all the trouble.\par \par \uc2 They were the first people to break through the lines. They were the first people to break into the Capitol. They were the first people to assault police officers. The total number of them is about 33. So, you know, they are what this report calls the pointy end of the spear. But how much planning among them was there? We still don`t know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Pete Williams, Garrett Haake, Paul Butler, Andrew Weissmann, thank you for helping us wrap up this three hour plus hearing today.\par \par \uc2 That will be all for us here at MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continue with Katy Tur will continue right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210303eh3200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O`Donnell for February 26, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Paul Butler, Michael McFaul, Brian Williams \par \uc2 7,441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: I`ll be bracing for what`s to come with perhaps the world`s top most expert on what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is capable of. His former personal fixer and confidant, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 . That`s tomorrow morning on my show "Velshi" from 8:00 to 10:00 a.m. eastern. Time now for "The Last Word" with Lawrence O`Donnell. Lawrence, good evening my friend.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Ali. You have a busy weekend of coverage this weekend.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I do, but I`m looking forward to it as always.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I`ll be watching. Thank you, Ali.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Thanks friend.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. We are covering two breaking news stories at this hour, and at the end of the hour we will cover the most important ongoing political story of our time, the Republican Party`s nationwide attempt in state legislatures to restrict voting in this country. We will be joined by Arizona`s secretary of state and she will get tonight`s last word.\par \par \uc2 The breaking news in Congress tonight is the voting on the Biden COVID relief bill. The other breaking news of the night is that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has singled out a suspect in the killing of Capitol Police Officer Brian Sicknick in the attack on the Capitol on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has found someone on video of the attack who appears to be responsible for the death of Officer Sicknick. We will have more details on this big break in the FBI`s investigation of the killing of Officer Sicknick after we begin with coverage of the breaking news in the House of Representatives of tonight.\par \par \uc2 The House Rules Committee\uc2  is voting right now on the $1.9 trillion Biden COVID relief bill that includes an increase in the minimum wage to $15 an hour. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was disappointed in last night`s ruling by the Senate parliamentarian, Elizabeth MacDonough that an increase in the minimum wage could not be included in the Biden COVID relief bill as part of a budget reconciliation package because of the unique parliamentary budget rules of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , rules that do not apply in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 It wasn`t the first time in her career that Speaker Pelosi was disappointed by a ruling by the Senate parliamentarian, and she could not have been surprised this time. Democrats and Republicans have always believed, unanimously believed, that it was impossible to include a minimum wage increase in a budget reconciliation bill in the Senate, which is why they have never tried to do it before. Never.\par \par \uc2 And Nancy Pelosi`s entire career in Congress, the Democrats have never attempted to increase the minimum wage in a reconciliation bill. Senator \uc2 Ted Kennedy\uc2  was the chairman of the Senate Labor Committee in three different decades, and he never once attempted to raise the minimum wage in a reconciliation bill because he believed it was against the rules of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Ted Kennedy\uc2  always wanted to raise the minimum wage. So, with decades of precedent stacked against them, the Senate staff of Chairman Bernie Sanders` budget committee still made a very powerful case to the Senate parliamentarian to include the minimum wage in the budget reconciliation bill that was so powerful an argument, that it took the Senate parliamentarian several days to decide the issue.\par \par \uc2 Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  today correctly did not blame the Senate parliamentarian. She blamed the rules of the Senate. And by implication, the senators who support those rules.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): As a matter of practice, I don`t get involved in the rules of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . But as a matter of values, I can just say we will not rest until we pass the $15 minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 We have been engaged in the fight for $15 for a long time. This is legislation that affects overwhelm -- a majority of women. So over 60 percent of the people making minimum wage are women. Many are moms. And it is so essential for us to do this.\par \par \uc2 And whether it happens when we send this over there, we are sending it as a symbol of a difference it will make in the lives of the American people, but not just a symbol, but as a solid proposal. If it doesn`t prevail because of Senate rules, we will persist. But we will not stop until we very soon pass the $15 minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Here`s what Speaker Pelosi said about the importance of the Biden COVID relief bill even without the minimum wage increase.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: This is a spectacular piece of legislation. While the Senate has prevented us temporarily from passing one aspect of it, let us not be distracted from what is in here because it is a great bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The House Majority Whip James Clyburn said that House Democrats decided to keep the minimum wage in their version of the bill even though they know it will not pass the Senate because they want to emphasize to the American people just how urgent it is to raise the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): We are going to do this. I think that the Speaker is correct. That`s why we`re keeping it in our bill. If we were going to be political about it, I think we would all just take it out of the bill. We are doing this because we are responding to the American people. We are that body here in the Congress that`s closest to the American people. We think $15 an hour ought to be the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading off our discussion tonight is Jared Bernstein. He`s a member of the president`s council of economic advisers. Thank you very much for joining us tonight on this important night. I want to get your reaction, first of all. Let`s deal with the parliamentarian`s ruling because I think you and I both would have bet that this is the way it would come out just because of literally decades of precedent.\par \par \uc2 But Bill Dauster, the chief counsel of the Senate Budget Committee, made obviously a very, very impressive argument to keep the parliamentarian thinking about it for several days. And we know Elizabeth MacDonough was not -- she was not looking for the pressure she got from this, but this is what I think most of us who`ve worked in this arena have always assumed the rule would be interpreted this way.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, BIDEN ADMINISTRATION COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Correct. And I also heard that Bill Dauster did a tremendous job. And as you said, that must have been the case because it took her a while to make the call that she did even though it was expected. I think the most important thing here is what we just heard \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and Congressman Clyburn say.\par \par \uc2 The people`s House is doing the people`s business, especially the Democrats over there. And the people`s business is working on not just getting the American rescue plan over the legislative goal line, that`s so important because there`s so much more in here than the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 But making sure that the president and the Congress find a way forward on the minimum wage as well. Given the parliamentarian`s decision, you know, it looks like it`s going to come out of the bill, as she said. That doesn`t mean this fight is over.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is committed to making sure that essential workers who have kept this country going through this pandemic crisis, this joint health care and economic crisis, get paid a fair wage. And that fair wage in his view is $15 an hour, and he has never wavered from that point.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ron Klain was on with \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  the other night and he said before the parliamentarian ruled that Vice President Harris would not attempt to overrule the parliamentarian`s call on this one, especially because the Democrats do not, plainly do not have the votes on the floor of the senate to sustain what would be the vice president`s overruling of the parliamentarian because you would only have, at most, 48 votes to support that.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Right. But I think what the Democrats do have on the floor of the Senate -- and I want to be very clear, this is a historic night for the House, but that doesn`t mean we can relax because we have to now move very quickly through the Senate so we can get this relief into the businesses and the families out there in America.\par \par \uc2 Not to mention the production and the distribution of the vaccine, the safe opening of schools, $2,000 of checks to hard-hit families, unemployment assistance, extension of the unemployment program that by the way, will expire mid-March. So that`s critical to get that out. Families facing a nutritional shortfall, eviction.\par \par \uc2 All these are components of the plan which Democrats in the Senate very broadly support. And just to be clear, we`re getting some poll numbers now. It`s not -- it`s only in Washington that this becomes a very partisan story.\par \par \uc2 I saw a poll number today, I believe it was a morning console poll from this morning or yesterday, 76% of the American people support the American Rescue Plan and 60 percent of Republicans, okay. Those are stratospheric numbers. Now, ask yourself why that`s the case?\par \par \uc2 It`s just because it`s not a "D" or an "R" preference to safely reopen schools, to support families and businesses to get through this crisis, to distribute the vaccine, to get shots in people`s arms, to make the kind of we`re in this together effort that the Biden administration has been doing in terms of getting the vaccine out there with the states relative to the Trump administration, which is very much you`re on your own, good luck. So, all of that is in the American Rescue Plan, not just the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We have Chairman Sanders of the Budget Committee and now, you know, in fact, more importantly in this instance Chairman Wyden of the Senate Finance Committee where this provision would have to be written. They are both advocating a provision to be added to the Senate bill which would in effect require very big companies to raise their minimum wage to at least the $15.\par \par \uc2 They put in a tax penalty if they don`t. They also have a tax encouragement for smaller businesses to raise their minimum wage up to the $15 level. Do you in the administration have a view that provision yet? Are you for that at this stage?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: The key word there is yet. Not yet because we just got that today and it`s a complicated piece of work. So we`re going to have to look at that very carefully. As you suggested, this is a tax penalty for large firms who don`t pay the minimum wage like they avoid the penalty by paying the higher wage.\par \par \uc2 I think where the president is coming from as we speak right now is that he is going to work with Congress to find a way forward on a $15 minimum wage that goes to everybody regardless of what size firm you work for because his value in this case is that essential workers doing the nation`s business should not live on a poverty -- should not be paid a poverty-level wage.\par \par \uc2 As we know, as I`m sure our viewers know, the federal minimum wage is $7.25 an hour. Full-time full year work, that comes to $14,500 a year. Nobody can sustain a family on that amount. Double that, you get to $15, and you`re talking about $30,000 a year. Certainly closer to the kind of living wage that this president is fighting for.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Jared Bernstein, thank you very much for joining us on this important night. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. And joining us now, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  of New York. He is a member of the Committee on Education and Labor. Congressman Bowman, thank you very much for joining us tonight and welcome to your first reconciliation bill where you`re discovering the Senate parliamentary rules actually end up controlling the final outcome as much as you would like to do it in the House.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Pelosi`s been through this kind of situation many times before. Let`s talk about what is in the bill other than the minimum wage at this stage and what it is that you consider the most important elements of this bill if the minimum wage doesn`t survive.\par \par \uc2 We have a sound issue with the congressman. Go ahead, congressman, we can hear you now, I think.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2  (D-NY): The entire bill is important, obviously, but for me as an educator, I`m looking at the $130 billion that`s going to our K-12 schools. My wife is a teacher. I have two young children in schools. Our schools need that money to open safely.\par \par \uc2 We need it for testing and contact tracing. We need it for ventilation. We need it for vaccination and PPE. We need it for additional teachers. So, I`m really excited about the education component to the bill. But the minimum wage, we got to continue to fight for the minimum wage. I mean, we`re talking $7.25 an hour. Even $15 an hour is a struggle for so many people across this country prior to the pandemic and since the pandemic has hit --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ve lost the congressman`s connection. And I want to -- as we struggle to get Congressman Bowman`s connection back, I want to show you what \uc2 Paul Krugman\uc2  said last night with Rachel Maddow. And this was when Rachel discussed the issue to the point of considering how good is this legislation even if they lose the minimum wage out of the bill. Let`s listen to what Nobel prize-winning economist \uc2 Paul Krugman\uc2  said about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL KRUGMAN\uc2 , NOBEL LAUREATE ECONOMICS: This is a -- I`m kind of almost pinching myself. I can`t believe that we`re about to get a bill that is as good. It`s not perfect, especially -- I miss that minimum wage, but they we`re about to get a bill that`s as good as it appears we`re going to get.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There is no more distinguished liberal economist than \uc2 Paul Krugman\uc2 , and he is right that in his time in watching this kind of legislation we have never seen anything this big, this good, even without the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 And of course, the loss of the minimum wage is a disappointment to everyone except the senators, a couple of senators who are opposed to it -- that level of increase in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . And so going forward now, the Democrats face the challenge of how do you pass the minimum wage?\par \par \uc2 And the lesson is you put it in a must-pass bill that even Republicans have to vote for, and the way they did it last time, the way they raised the wage last time is they put it in a defense spending bill. They stuck it in the defense spending bill. \uc2 Ted Kennedy\uc2  managed to get it jammed in there at the last minute and that bill got 80 votes in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  because Republicans had to vote for the defense spending bill.\par \par \uc2 And oddly enough, most of the votes against that bill were from Democrats who were opposed to the defense spending that was in that bill. And so, most of the no votes, the last time the minimum wage increase went through the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  was, in the oddity of it, from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And so, this story is not over. There will be a defense spending bill. There will be bills and legislative vehicles moving through the Senate that you can attach anything to, including a minimum wage. That`s the next chapter of this story.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to take a break here. When we come back, former federal prosecutor Paul Butler will join us the breaking news that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has singled out a suspect in the investigation of the killing of Capitol Police Officer Brian Sicknick in the attack on the Capitol. That breaking news is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is a live shot of the House floor where they are debating when to end the debate on the COVID relief bill. The vote on ending that debate should be soon. Then there should be a vote on the bill itself after that.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s breaking news in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is that "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has singled out a suspect in its investigation of the killing of Capitol Police Officer Brian Sicknick during the January 6th attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Katie Benner and Adam Goldman in "The Times" report the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has pinpointed an assailant in its investigation into the death of Brian D. Sicknick, the Capitol Police officer who was injured while fending off the pro-Trump mob that stormed the Capitol last month and later died.\par \par \uc2 According to two law enforcement officials briefed on the inquiry, investigators have now pinpointed a person seen on video of the riot who attacked several officers with bear spray, including Officer Sicknick, according to the officials.\par \par \uc2 And video evidence shows that the assailant discussed attacking officers with the bear spray beforehand, one of the officials said. "The Times" reports that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  suspects that Officer Sicknick`s death was "related to an irritant like mace or bear spray that he had inhaled during the riot."\par \par \uc2 Also, today, the Justice Department indicted six members of the so-called Proud Boys on conspiracy charges. The six suspects had already been charged in the attack on the Capitol, but they are now charged with conspiracy to obstruct the certification of President Biden`s electoral victory and to attack law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports, "The department said that the group coordinated their travel to Washington and stayed together in an \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  rental near the city. They brought and wore matching uniforms and carried paramilitary gear and supplies including camouflage, combat uniforms, tactical vests with plates, helmets, eye protection and radio equipment into the Capitol, according to the indictment. Once inside, they traveled as a group through the building, the department said.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now by phone is Paul Butler, a former federal prosecutor and law professor at Georgetown University. He is an MSNBC contributor. And Paul, let me begin with the breaking news that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has narrowed it down to someone they see on video and "The Times" reporting seems to indicate they do not yet have the specific identity of the person, but they have images of the person.\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR (via telephone): This is very significant. The investigation is focused on a person who attacked several officers with bear spray, a chemical, lethal or non-lethal agent. There is evidence of both advanced planning and a criminal conspiracy because reportedly there is video of the person discussing attacking officers with bear spray before the actual attack.\par \par \uc2 So, murder charges would require evidence of intent to kill, and chemical irritants like bear spray are not usually considered deadly force. So, the mental state of purpose to kill would be difficult to prove, but certainly very serious charges like assault on a law enforcement officer are entirely warranted based on this reported evidence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Paul, talk about in investigation terms, what does it take to go from that image of a person that they apparently have to getting an identity and a name and an arrest.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: So, the stakes are high. We know that 138 police officers were injured as a result of the insurrection. Injuries like concussions, burns or a heart attack and two suicides. Lawrence, this is probably the worst day for U.S. law enforcement officers since September 11th. So, this is a major priority.\par \par \uc2 What do we know? Six members of this hate group, the Proud Boys have already been charged with conspiracy both to obstruct the certification of the election and to attack law enforcement. The leader of the Proud Boys, this guy, Enrique Tarrio, whose name in the indictment as person number one, told these white supremacists that they should turn out in record numbers, pan across D.C. in small teams.\par \par \uc2 So, there is coordination. They wore matching uniforms. They carried paramilitary gear like helmets and eye protection. And so, they came hell bent on destruction. They successfully incited the crowd and they acted in coordination with these tragic results.\par \par \uc2 And so, Enrique Tarrio, who`s a known snitch, he`s reportedly cooperated with law enforcement agents in other investigations to get himself out of trouble. That is certainly one person who the \uc2 FBI\uc2  will turn to, to try to get information on who actually carried out the attack, who are the people who sprayed Officer Sicknick with this chemical poison that tragically resulted in his death.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And what are the effects of upgrading the charges against these six so-called Proud Boys to conspiracy? Presumably, that would add to any sentence. What else might it mean?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: Yes, you`re absolutely right. It enhances the sentence. But it also creates more incentive for these persons to turn states evidence, to cooperate with law enforcement to try to figure out who the leaders are and who are the people who actually carried out this act.\par \par \uc2 And this is welcome transparency from the Capitol Police who aren`t (ph) known for being all that transparent and haven`t been during this investigation. So, we didn`t actually know before this indictment how this officer was actually killed.\par \par \uc2 And there`s still other important information that the public hasn`t had access to from the Capitol Police. For example, we know that a number of Capitol Police officers are on leave. They`re being investigated for their conduct. What did that investigation reveal?\par \par \uc2 And we also don`t know about contacts between the rioters and Republican Congress people, including whether rioters were offered tours of the Capitol prior to January 6th. So these new charges are a welcome advance in terms of the public having more understanding of the dire stakes of what happened on January 6th, but it`s really just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Paul Butler, thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: Always a pleasure, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And just to be clear on this breaking news from "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " tonight, there are no arrests on this part of the investigation yet, but the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has singled out a potential assailant as "The Times" is reporting, of Officer Brian Sicknick. They have zeroed in on someone. Apparently, they do not yet have the full identity of that person.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we will be joined by Texas Congresswoman Lizzie Fletcher who accompanied President Biden on his visit to Texas today as the state recovers from the deadly failure of its power grid while the state`s junior senator was in Orlando screaming his way through an anti-Democracy rally. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  demonstrated that we once again have a president who believes he is president of all 50 states, including the states that he did not win in the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was welcomed politely and professionally today in Texas by Republican Governor Greg Abbott and the state`s Republican senior senator, \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , both of whom were vigorous opponents of Joe Biden`s presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 The state`s junior senator once again decided there was no point to pretending that he cares about the aftermath of the man-made power failure in Texas that cost at least 40 lives. He was found on a stage in Florida today screaming at an anti-democracy rally.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s first stop was to the Harris County emergency operations center where state and local officials briefed him about the recovery efforts and the challenges they still face.\par \par \uc2 The president and first lady met with volunteers at the \uc2 Houston Food Bank\uc2 , the largest food bank in the country that feeds over one million Texans.\par \par \uc2 Their final stop was to one of the federal community vaccination centers that the Biden administration has created.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , President of the United States: I know millions of Texans have been impacted by the destruction of community water systems as a result of the power outages. The \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  has deployed mobile drinking water labs in Houston, San Antonio and Fort Worth to test water so we can get boil-water advisories lifted and get safe drinking water flowing through those faucets again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Congresswoman Lizzie Fletcher of Texas who represents the Houston area. She is a member of the House Energy and Commerce Committee. She met with President Biden today in Texas. And she has the honor of representing the congressional district where the junior senator from Texas resides.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Fletcher, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Tell us what you were able to tell \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  today about what Texas needs in the aftermath and their recovery from this power failure system.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZZIE FLETCHER (D-TX): Well, thanks so much, Lawrence, for having me on tonight and for your focus on what`s happening here in Texas still.\par \par \uc2 I was really glad to be able to visit with President Biden here on the ground in Houston today. He is the leader that we need at this moment. He showed his commitment and his compassion for the people who live here. And I was able to talk to him about how meaningful it is for us to see that leadership, to see him here, and also that he moved so quickly to approve the disaster declaration before the disaster, before the storm hit, and to approve the major disaster declaration immediately after.\par \par \uc2 And he got to see today on the ground the incredible work of the public servants at every level of government who are committed to working on and helping the people who lived through that crisis and working on the ways that we can make sure it never happens again.\par \par \uc2 So I was just so grateful that he came and saw for himself the incredible community that`s working here in Houston to help people get back on their feet after last week.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s listen to more of what the president said today knowing that he was surrounded by Texas governing officials who did not vote for him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We`re not here today as Democrats or Republicans. We`re here today as Americans. The American leaders with responsibility, all of us here in this parking lot, responsibility to all the people we serve.\par \par \uc2 When a crisis hits our states like the one that hit Texas, it`s not a Republican or Democrat who`s hurting, it`s our fellow Americans who are hurting. And it`s our job to help everyone in need. Look out for one another. Leave nobody behind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to make clear to our viewers that you`re voting by proxy tonight in the House of Representatives even though you are in Houston because we`re covering the action on the house floor live during this hour.\par \par \uc2 And I want to get your -- what you can tell us about what this COVID relief bill will mean in Texas where many apparently of those $1,400 checks that will arrive for Texans will immediately have to go pay their power bills.\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER: That`s right, Lawrence. This COVID relief package is absolutely vital. And that`s another thing that I was able to talk about a little bit with President Biden today because, of course, he came here not only to see the relief efforts, but also the vaccination center, the new FEMA mass vaccination center at NRG Stadium.\par \par \uc2 And it`s important for us to get more vaccinations, to get those shots in people`s arms, to continue testing, and to have a robust program to ensure the health of people across the country and to combat the virus, as well as having direct checks for people who are hurting.\par \par \uc2 And I know that people are looking for additional unemployment and those economic impact payments. They call my office every day and we`ve been helping people get help with our Texas Workforce Commissioner, unemployment agency. We know small businesses are taking advantage of these PPP loans, but that their challenges are great. And after last week`s storm, they`re magnified.\par \par \uc2 People across our community have pipes that froze and are now broken. We were out of power then we were out of water. For many people who have busted pipes, even when you can get a plumber, there aren`t parts here.\par \par \uc2 And so, people are struggling in their homes and their businesses and it`s so important for us to help them as well as that critical funding for state and local governments.\par \par \uc2 And last week was a reminder that when we are in a crisis, we look to our government to help us. And what we saw today in the emergency operations center, what President Biden saw -- and he said he thought it was the best in the country -- were public servants who were spending the night both last week during this freeze, but also in relation to COVID.\par \par \uc2 They`re spending days and nights making sure that our community is safe and healthy. And we need to make sure that we have that aid to state and local governments who have struggled so much because of the -- all of the difficulties that the COVID pandemic has brought.\par \par \uc2 And so, there`s this vital relief in this bill and I will be voting in favor of it as soon as we have the opportunity to vote on final passage.\par \par \uc2 Were you surprised that the state`s junior senator decided it was more important for him to be on a stage in Orlando today instead of in Texas with you, telling the president what the kind of help Texas needs?\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER: Well, I certainly think that the opportunity to show the extent of the devastation as well as the efforts here on the ground is an important opportunity to be able to have that time with the president.\par \par \uc2 And I was disappointed in what the senator said, what was relayed to me that the senator said at that convention, kind of making light of not being here.\par \par \uc2 This is a time where we need our leaders. And that`s why it was so important for our community to see President Biden here and that`s an opportunity that I was very glad to be a part of as were all of our leaders here in Houston.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Texas congresswoman Lizzie Fletcher, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the director of National Intelligence has finally issued a report holding the Crown Prince of Saudi Arabia responsible for the murder of Jamal Khashoggi. Former ambassador Michael McFaul will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is a live shot of the House floor where they are voting on the rule that will determine how the bill proceeds through the House. The vote on the rule will succeed, of course. Then they will proceed on the vote on the bill. All of this is going to take a few hours.\par \par \uc2 Today the office of the director of National Intelligence released an assessment by the \uc2 Central Intelligence Agency\uc2  concluding that Saudi Arabia`s Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  approved an operation in Istanbul, Turkey to capture or kill Saudi journalist Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 Today President Biden said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I spoke yesterday with the king, not the prince and made it clear to him that rules are changing. We`re going to hold them accountable for human rights abuses. And we`re going to make sure that they, in fact, if they want to deal with us, they have to deal with it in a way where the human rights abuses are dealt with.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today President Biden said this about the first air strike that he ordered yesterday which targeted buildings in Syria they said used by Iranian-backed forces that launched certain strikes against American contractors and possibly American personnel in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Mr. President, what message were you sending to Iran with your first military action?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You can`t get -- you can`t act with impunity. Be careful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today Pentagon press secretary John Kirby told Andrea Mitchell that this was all legally authorized.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: The president acted well within his constitutional authorities under Article 2 as commander in chief of the United States to protect American service members involved in operations. Clearly there`s a constitutional authority here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now, Michael McFaul. He`s the U.S. ambassador to Russia during the Obama administration. He is an MSNBC international affairs analyst.\par \par \uc2 And Ambassador McFaul, a lot of pushback -- significant pushback from Democrats about the legality of that strike by President Biden. Democratic Senator Tim Kaine saying the American people deserve an explanation of the legal authority. He doesn`t see legal authority for this.\par \par \uc2 We go through this discussion every time and nothing seems to get resolved on the legal authority.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCFAUL, MSNBC INTERNATIONAL AFFAIRS ANALYST: You`re right. Nothing seems to get resolved and for decades, by the way, Lawrence. this isn`t just something new. The Congress wants the authority, but oftentimes they don`t want to take the responsibility.\par \par \uc2 I remember this very bitterly back in 2013 when President \uc2 Obama\uc2  was seeking their authority to do airstrikes in Syria and then it didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 In my opinion this was the right decision to take. The Iranians need to know that we will protect our troops. They were in danger and I think it was the right strike at the right time and proportionate to the threat that was at hand.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, on the killing of Jamal Khashoggi, what does the president accomplish in these conversations with Saudi Arabia? What does Saudi Arabia have to fear now, if anything, from the Biden administration?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, first, I think it`s a real improvement over the Trump era. Let`s be clear about that. We never talked about democracy and human rights at all during the Trump era with respect to Saudi Arabia, so I applaud that. I applaud the publication of the ODNI report, and I applaud the new list that Secretary Blinken list -- about 75 or so officials that will be on a visa ban list from Saudi Arabia because of this heinous murder that happened.\par \par \uc2 But I hope it`s not the last step because let`s be clear MBS, Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  is not being punished here. They didn`t even say his name. He was not sanctioned, and I think it sends a very weak signal that this is the last step that we see in this drama.\par \par \uc2 They promised us recalibration. That`s the word they`re using. We are looking to recalibrate our bilateral relationship with Saudi Arabia. I think that`s good. I just hope it`s comprehensive and not just this one off that we had today.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It certainly is unlike any other public document that the United States has issued about Saudi Arabia. This is an indictment. It`s in effect a criminal indictment. It says the Crown Prince has had absolute control over the kingdom`s security.\par \par \uc2 It`s a very specific indictment of the Crown Prince. But there`s that problem of -- they have the ability to indict but what do they have the ability to do beyond leveling the charge?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, they could do more, you know. They chose to do less. And you`re right. They have indicted them of a crime. And I really want to underscore, a heinous, a horrible crime.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Khashoggi was not trying to overthrow the regime. He wasn`t storming palaces. He was a rather moderate critic. I knew him. We had conferences together with him when I`m here out at Stanford.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s clear as day what crime was committed. It`s clear as day who ordered it; I would say directed it. I would use a stronger verb.\par \par \uc2 And they could do more. They could put MBS -- they could put him on the visa ban list. That`s a step they could take. They could move the people that they put on this new visa ban list onto the Magnitsky list and then they could name them.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, that would go a lot farther in terms of punishing them. By just doing it in this private way, that`s a much smaller bar than putting him on the Magnitsky list.\par \par \uc2 And then they could go farther. They could recalibrate the entire relationship. My own view is that we think we need Saudi Arabia. Saudi Arabia needs us. And I think we need to change fundamentally the balance of power in this relationship.\par \par \uc2 And I`m willing to wait. I`m willing to hope that the Biden administration will fundamentally recalibrate everything.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Former ambassador Michael McFaul, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Arizona secretary of state \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  will join us on the single most important ongoing political story of our time: the Republican attempts to restrict voting in this country with Arizona Republicans taking it to a whole new level.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Our LAST WORD tonight is nothing less than the most important political story of our time. The national effort by the Republican Party to prevent people from voting because they believe that that`s the only way they can win elections.\par \par \uc2 Already this year more than 250 bills have been introduced in state legislatures around the country that would make it more difficult for people to vote. Most of those bills focus on limiting mail-in voting, increasing voter ID requirements, and the process of deleting names from the voter rolls.\par \par \uc2 Arizona Republicans are so angry about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  winning their state that they have introduced 19 bills in the legislature designed to help the next Republican presidential candidate win Arizona and defy democracy in the process if necessary.\par \par \uc2 One of those Arizona bills would establish a special session of the legislature the Monday after election day to review or investigate the results of the election and give legislators the power to pick the state`s presidential electors for the electoral college.\par \par \uc2 Voting rights advocates say the bill would give the Republican legislature the authority to decide the election results.\par \par \uc2 Republican state Senator David Gowen, the bill`s sponsor, says the legislation isn`t meant to override the vote of the people but provides a process for auditing the election.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Arizona secretary of state \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . I guess she`s not joining us now. We have managed to lose more than one connection with our guests tonight in this terribly challenging time for our control rooms.\par \par \uc2 The state legislature in Arizona is considering a bill that will provide for a period of auditing and investigating the vote by the legislature itself. Arizona already has a process for doing this.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is joining us now to tell us what your process is now, which you have used extensively in this election for reviewing votes, recounting votes, and what the legislature wants to change it to.\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 , ARIZONA SECRETARY OF STATE: Well, we have already built in a logic and accuracy test in every county before each election and then after each election. So they`re testing all the equipment prior -- both before and after the election to make sure it is working properly. And there`s also a hand count audit in every county.\par \par \uc2 So Maricopa County has gone above and beyond this year and done an additional post-election logic and accuracy test and then just finished two other deep-dive audits on all of the equipment, and the results came back completely clean.\par \par \uc2 But the Senate has subpoenaed all of the equipment and all of the ballots to do their own so-called independent audits. A judge today ruled that that subpoena is valid, and the county has to turn those things over.\par \par \uc2 But they`re trying to put those things into statute into the future so that they would have just unfettered access to all of the equipment and ballots, and that`s concerning on many levels.\par \par \uc2 Number one, we already do all of those things.\par \par \uc2 But number two, there`s concerns about chain of custody and things like that which, you know, most people should have a little bit of skepticism about in terms of turning those things over to a third party.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. I mean the more the ballots move around, the more susceptible they become to manipulation.\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Absolutely yes. We certainly don`t want that to happen.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And in the senate`s review, they`re going to now review all of the votes cast in the last presidential election because they`ve managed through a subpoena to get custody of those ballots. What do you expect to happen in that review?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Well, I expect them to find nothing wrong. I expect the results to match what the tabulator results came up with from the election. We know that there was no widespread fraud or irregularity that changed votes. And their audit is going to come up the same way that all the other audits have come up. And the people that are hoping that something wrong is going to be found are going to be very disappointed at the end of this additional audit.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You have been doing mail-in voting since 1992 -- 28 years of experience with it in Arizona, absolutely no complaints about it until now.\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Well, you know, it has been a little bit more of a partisan issue in the last couple of years, but we -- it was enacted in a bipartisan manner and the permanent early voting list was put in place in a bipartisan manner. And 75 percent of voters in Arizona are on the permanent early voter list.\par \par \uc2 So, it is highly popular, and I think Republicans mess with that at their peril because it is not just used by Democrats in our state.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Arizona secretary of state \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH BRIAN WILLIAMS" starts now.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WILLIAMS, MSNBC HOST: Well, good evening once again.\par \par \uc2 Day 38 of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020210227eh2q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Al Cardenas about CPAC; Making the Vaccine; Interview with Representative Mike McCaul (R-TX) about President Biden`s Visit to Texas; MEET THE PRESS DAILY for February 26, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Tom Costello \par \uc2 8,772 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Friday the chances for President Biden`s $1.9 trillion COVID relief bill just went up after a push to include a minimum wage hike, get shut down. What`s next for all those progressives who badly wanted to include it? And for all those conservatives who did not. Plus, President Biden`s strikes is Syria. Getting Iranian-backed militias for their targeting of Americans in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 It`s his first use of military force as president. It`s got some Democrats uneasy. And as conservatives gather at CPAC to showcase their continued loyalty to former President Trump. Even Mitch McConnell says they`ll support Donald Trump if he becomes the Republican nominee in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Friday, it is MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd. The House is readying its vote on President Biden`s COVID relief bill tonight, as its passage in the senate just got a lot more likely. Ultimately, the Senate parliamentarians ruling that the chamber cannot use this reconciliation process to raise the minimum wage is going to make it easier, not harder to get this bill to President Biden`s desk as the country continues to reel from this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But let`s be clear here, politically, Democrats appear to be dodging a bullet. It`s a big deal. Democrats have no margin for error in the Senate. And now moderates like Joe Manchin and Kyrsten Sinema are more likely to vote for the Senate`s overall COVID relief bill than not. Republicans are also on shaky footing and their opposition to the relief bill, which polls continue to show is very popular.\par \par \uc2 Even as Mitch McConnell acknowledged earlier this week, everyone loves getting a check. But the Republicans had tied much of their opposition to the minimum wage, citing that CBO analysis that said raising it to $15.00 an hour would reduce employment by 1.4 million jobs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN BARRASSO (R-WY): One of the concerns that we have is the impact that this is going to have on small businesses and people at home, as the Democrats tried to have a nationally mandated increase in the minimum wage,\par \par \uc2 SEN. CYNTHIA LUMMIS (R-WY): placing that one size fits all standard on every state in this country is ill-advised and irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM (R-SC): The minimum wage has got nothing to do with COVID. Zero to do with COVID. It`s got everything to do with their liberal wish list.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JONI ERNST (R-IA): Specifically, when it comes to the $15.00 an hour federal minimum wage issue. This would be so harmful for our Iowa small businesses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can see the problem they have now, Republicans can`t really make any of those arguments if they choose to continue to oppose the relief bill. There`s no minimum wage anymore. But while some Democrats are happy, some progressive Democrats are not happy about this development.\par \par \uc2 The chair of the Congressional Progressive Caucus Pramila Jayapal said, "We simply cannot go back to the Black, Brown Asian Americans and Pacific Islanders, indigenous poor and working class voters who delivered us the White House and the Senate Majority and tell them that an unelected parliamentarian advised us based on arcane rules that we could not raise the minimum wage, as we promised."\par \par \uc2 Later today, the House will be voting on the relief bill, and they are keeping the minimum wage provision in there. But it`s going to have to come out at some point. Look, no one said COVID relief would be easy or pretty. But now members of Congress across all ideologies need to confront the political reality that COVID relief without the minimum wage as a clear path to the President`s desk. And the fight to raise the minimum wage will now move to a separate lane, if you will.\par \par \uc2 So for the latest on where things stand, I got it. Leigh Ann Caldwell joining me from Capitol Hill. So Leigh Ann, the House is going to go forward and, you know, politically, the House Democrats can all say they voted to raise the minimum wage, even if it -- even if it means it gets taken out of has to come back. But I`m curious about that, Leigh Ann. Is this going to cost them any votes right now considering that they know where the Senate is headed with this bill?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HOUSE REPORTER: It doesn`t seem to check. Well, Speaker Pelosi`s decision to keep the minimum wage component in the bill. Did -- there`s a lot of political considerations there despite what the Senate has to do. There has been -- there are threats from progressive saying that they would not vote for the bill if the minimum wage was taken out, even if it has no chance of pat going and being included in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, what Pelosi did is she seemed to indicate that the threats from progressives were much greater than the concerns from moderates over having to vote for this legislation, but she seems to be Keeping most of her caucus together and it`s expected to pass. But what this minimum wage thing has done it`s pretty much a proxy war for the filibuster. This is increased the demand --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes. From progressives to call on the senate Democrats need to get rid of the filibuster. And so this is going to put a lot of pressure on Senate Democratic Leader Chuck Schumer, not only now, but definitely down the road.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. But I mean, progressives, are they aware that Joe Manchin and Kyrsten Sinema are against getting rid of the filibuster?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: That was what my next point was going to be, Chuck. There`s not necessarily 50 votes at this point to get rid of the filibuster, which is what is needed to change Senate rules. So, you know, there`s a lot of frustration from progressives, I was just talking to representative Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez and she said, how can one or two people in the Senate hold up this major progressive agenda talking about Joe Manchin and to a lesser extent but to Kyrsten Sinema of Arizona as well.\par \par \uc2 So, there is a lot of -- there`s going to be a lot of pressure on Manchin but he -- this -- that`s not something that seems to work for him. He seems to be more emboldened the more progressive pressure he gets.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, as he can probably look at the history of many a member of the House of Representatives who wondered why a single senator has as much power as they do. If that is the way this system has been designed for decades, if you will. I want to move to the Republican side of the aisle, Leigh Ann, because the best message they thought they had in opposing this relief bill was by saying, hey, it`s not really targeted at COVID relief, like the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Well, the like the minimum wage is now gone. I`ve noticed they`re struggling coming up with a new rationale for opposing this bill. But what are they going to do?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: So, when they started going really hard on opposing the minimum wage, I thought that they were pretty confident that the Parliamentarian was going to rule in the Democrats` favor but they were just gearing up for that. But that didn`t happen. So, it`s got -- it`s unclear what they`re going to do right now. There are some who are talking about that there can -- the government can`t afford another $2 trillion bill.\par \par \uc2 But this goes back into the same pattern that Republicans usually take of when there`s a Democratic president then they start to get concerned about the deficit and the debt. They -- there`s also a divide in the party, Chuck, on the minimum wage, while those Democrats were, you know, disputing and disagreeing with a $15.00 minimum wage. We now have two Republican pieces of legislation that have been introduced on increasing the federal minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 One by Senators, Romney and Cotton, and another just today by Senator Josh Hawley. So, there`s still that populist movement in some components of the Republican Party that is really challenging the more business-oriented Chamber of Commerce portion of the party, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Leigh Ann, you look at it from 60,000 seat, and you`re like, there`s plenty of will here to raise the minimum wage, go do it. If you have to do it separately, then go do it. I think sometimes we`re also worried about the small and petty we forget. There`s a way to come to an agreement here. Anyway, Leigh Ann Caldwell getting us started on Capitol Hill. Leigh Ann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And with me now is Democratic Senator from Maryland, Chris Van Hollen. And Senator Van Hollen, let me start your reaction to Parliamentarian. It is what it is, do you think it was the right call?\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS VAN HOLLEN (D-MD): Look, I had a different interpretation of the rules. But the Parliamentarian gets to make that call. But it does underscore, Chuck, in my view, the craziness of a system where whether or not we get a majority vote on a fundamental question like the need to raise the minimum wage comes down to these quirks in the Senate rules. So, I`m an advocate for scrapping these reconciliation procedures.\par \par \uc2 And that means fundamentally, also getting rid of or reforming the filibuster rule. So, this will bring more momentum to that question. In the meantime, we`re going to press ahead with this very important $1.9 trillion reconciliation bill, which is you just said, you know, Republicans are going to have to explain why they`re opposing it now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just to wrap up on sort of the first part of what we were talking about there with the Parliamentarian. Are you -- do you -- what do you think of those calls that say, hey, overrule the parliamentarian? You could do this with 50 votes plus the -- plus the Vice President if necessary. Is that a path to go down or do you think that`s a debate to have later?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN: Well, Chuck, as you know, you need the votes to do that. And as you were just discussing, we don`t have currently 50 votes in the Senate Democratic Caucus that to overrule the Parliamentarian on this question. So look, my view is we should look for every way possible to pass the $15.00 minimum wage in the law, it takes place over five years. But we do need the votes. I think we need to scrap the senate filibuster rules.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, we`re looking at another option, it`s not as good as the straight increase the minimum wage, but Senator Wyden, Senator Sanders, myself and others are looking at ways that we could create a tax penalty for big companies that don`t pay their employees $15.00 an hour. We had a hearing in the \uc2 Senate Budget Committee\uc2  just yesterday, there was a Marylander who had worked for 20 years for \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  who is not getting paid $15.00 an hour.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  just announced another $20 billion stock buyback. So that`s just wrong.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m just -- you`re saying you`re looking at it, so -- can you give me some sense of how it would work, give me the size of company, what would be the sort of the bar of the size of company that would be impacted?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN: Well, this is a plan B that we`re having to look at now because of the decision by Parliamentarian. But the idea would be -- I mean, let`s take an obvious example, which would be \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2 . There`s a big company, they`re not paying their employees a $15.00 minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 So, you would say if you haven`t hit that mark, within a certain period of time, maybe it`s the same time period in the underlying bill year 2025 then you would pay a tax penalty, because American taxpayers are having to pay more to support these employees with respect to healthcare and other needs.\par \par \uc2 I mean, after all, the current minimum wage, you work full time you`re under the federal poverty level. So other anti-poverty programs kick in to help support those employees. When it`s the Wal-Marts of the world who just gave or announcing $20 billion stock buyback, they need to -- need to do their part and pay a decent living wage.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you open to coming up with a formula that took in geographical differences in cost of living and then maybe put it on a color track? You know, the way Social Security is and things like that?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN: Look, I`m opening -- open to discussions for how we ultimately get to $15.00 an hour minimum wage. For areas where you have, you know, where it`s -- it seems to be tougher for a variety of reasons on the current timescale, you could consider a somewhat longer basin. But, again, the fact that the Parliamentarian made this decision, I think, does Chuck reflect the anti-Democratic small D, nature of the Senate rules.\par \par \uc2 And the Senate, of course, is already an anti-majority institution. So, this just compounds the problem. We need to get to where we can have an up or down vote. And then of course, we need to get the votes. And that`s where this question you just asked comes in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious than last night`s strike in Syria on those Iranian militias. A couple of your colleagues, Senator Tim Kaine, in particular, wants to hear what the legal justifications are. And there`s been a lot of senators that have been concerned about any of this stuff in Syria because there hasn`t been authorization. What say you?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN: So, my view is that the strikes that have been aimed against ISIS, specifically, are covered under previous, you know, authorizations, although I also believe that we need as a Congress to rein in some of those expansive authorities that Senator Kaine has proposed. Also, in those cases like this one where you`re talking about Iranian-backed militia where the information I`ve seen indicates that this was in response for Iranian militia attacks against U.S. interests in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Then there are other set of, you know, interests that come into play with respect to self-defense. So again, I -- it is absolutely right, I believe to raise these fundamental questions, and I`m going to be looking for information from --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask it a very -- let me ask it a very clear cut way. Do you think this fell -- falls underneath the AUMF?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN: The AUMF in Iraq does allow the United States to take actions and that is still on the books. This was an action taken in response to attacks by Iranian militia inside Iraq. So, again, this could be -- this is a blurry area which is why I agree with people Senator Kaine who believed that we need to really clarify the current state of the AUMF.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think you just put a very well, there yourself, Senator Van Hollen saying. It`s a blurry area. Suddenly we`re talking Iranian militias in Syria that we`re attacking in Iraq. It -- there`s a lot of -- certainly a lot of blurring lines. Senator Van Hollen, Democrat from Maryland. Really appreciate you coming on today and sharing your perspective. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN: Good to be with you. Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re going to have more on the serious strikes in just a minute. But first, we got some breaking news. And it`s breaking news we`ve all been expecting. The Biden administration has just released the public intelligence report that Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman did approve the capture or killing of journalist Jamal Khashoggi. It says, "We base this assessment on the Crown Prince`s control of decision making in the kingdom since 2017.\par \par \uc2 The direct involvement of a key advisor and members of Mohammed bin Salman`s protective detail in the operation and the Crown Prince`s support for using violent measures to silent dissidents abroad, Khashoggi. Joined now by NBC News chief foreign correspondent Richard Engel. Richard, the substance of this is not news to Americans, not news to us who`ve been reporting on this.\par \par \uc2 But the significance of making this charge public. How will this be playing in the Middle East?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, MSNBC NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Well, it depends on which country you`re in. If you`re a country that opposes Saudi Arabia, Turkey, for example, doesn`t like the Crown Prince, then they will be embracing this report. They will say this is evidence that the Crown Prince was running a rogue hit squad because this report also talks about the rapid intervention force.\par \par \uc2 This this crew that was answerable to MBS, the Crown Prince of Saudi Arabia, the de facto leader that was doing renditions and in this case, an assassination around the world. So, if you are opposed to Saudi Arabia and opposed to the Crown Prince, you`re going to jump on this. If you are close with the Saudi Crown Prince, you`re probably not going to give it a lot of attention. But it is -- it is bold.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. didn`t need to do this. It is a story that is relatively well- known. People know that there was this hit squad that they arrived in Istanbul, at least one of them was carrying a bone saw they lured Khashoggi into the consulate in Istanbul. His fiancee was waiting outside, he never emerged. And the Turks had the building bugged. So, there are recordings of Khashoggi suffering and dying and the sound of his body being dismembered. A body that was never found.\par \par \uc2 So that the facts were known. But having it come out now, in the early days of the Biden administration is a -- is a slap in the face to Saudi Arabia, certainly a slap in the face to the -- to the Crown Prince. And those who oppose the Crown Prince are going to certainly be embracing this. And I think others who are more in the Saudis camp will be pretending that this report never happened.\par \par \uc2 TODD: While we`re on the 37th day of Joe Biden as president and he`s had a launch an airstrike into Syria, targeting Iranian militias who were attacking Americans in Iraq. And the reason I did all those points, I`m sure you heard my conversation with Senator Van Hollen. It -- this is -- we`re a long way away from that original AUMF and what is legal and where we should be fighting these hot conflicts.\par \par \uc2 What should -- what should we expect from this? Is this a surgical one strike or do we think retaliation is coming and that it will lead to even more?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Well, it`s a very dangerous game. It is a slippery slope. And anytime you take military action in the Middle East, there is a potential for escalation. Almost this exact scenario happened last year, and the U.S. and Iran almost went to war. But it was handled very differently. If you remember last year, I was in Iraq at the time and I was very nervous and there was a -- there was talk that this was going to go to war.\par \par \uc2 And because there was a series of escalations. First, the Iranian or Iranian-backed militias fired on U.S. contractors. Then the U.S. responded with a deadly attack against Iranian militias in which dozens of them were killed. Then those militias started to gather and hold protests in front of the U.S. Embassy in Baghdad, then the U.S. responded to those protests and the attack on the embassy by killing the top Iranian general and things escalated\par \par \uc2 And escalated ultimate culminated with Iran firing ballistic missiles. And we were all on tenterhooks, we thought one member -- that was that moment, President Trump came out and addressed in an empty podium and address the world. And they will -- it was it was touch and go. Are we going to war with Iran or not? Because there were these series of escalations. This time, it seems like they were running back militias.\par \par \uc2 They carried out a strike on U.S. personnel, on U.S. contractors. And in this case, the Biden administration, President Biden is trying to describe that this was a proportionate response, that this was in a border area. It was in Syria that also reduces some of the pressure. They`re letting the military do the talking. And so, there was definitely an effort on the -- on the administration side, not to see this escalate.\par \par \uc2 But the Iranians get a vote. And just earlier today, there was this mysterious incident, the Israelis are looking into an explosion on Israeli- owned cargo ships in the Persian Gulf. It doesn`t seem to have caused much damage, doesn`t seem to have caused much harm to personnel. But a lot of people, the Israelis are looking to see if there`s an Iranian connection there. So, there is the potential for escalation. But there is a big difference.\par \par \uc2 You have an administration that is trying to go slow. That is not putting the President front and center. It is not about Biden, so it doesn`t seem like they want it to escalate. And frankly, Iran doesn`t have an interest to have it escalate. And it`s not on the same scale like it was last year, there have been no killing of a top Iranian general. So, I think this stage, it seems like this can be contained for now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: One would assume the Iranians at least want to see what negotiations might look like on a new nuclear deal as well, which would -- you would assume motivate them not to retaliate for now. Anyway, Richard Engel on the ground for us. As always, sir. Richard --\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joe Biden just arriving right now in Texas, by the way, and still ahead. We have the FDA could be approving another COVID vaccine, literally sometime today. Plus, we finally got a rare look inside a facility where the vaccines get made. Amid this urgent push to ramp up production. Hopefully we get a better idea of why it`s so hard to ramp up production. Plus, as Donald Trump gets ready for his first post presidential public appearance. The former chairman of that event says he`s staying away. He joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Here`s a live look at Texas where the President just touched down. He`s going to be bidding. You`ve made a little joke about our polarization when he says he didn`t want to ruin governor Greg Abbott`s reputation, but he said he was looking forward to being with the governor today. Donald Trump, speaking of the Republican side of things will be making his first public appearance since leaving office on stage at CPAC.\par \par \uc2 As is -- the question really is whether CPAC pack is anything of what it used to be. It`s technically the annual Conservative Political Action Conference. But is it really about the conservative movement or is this year going to be just all things Trump? The events underway right now in Orlando, there`s more than 800 miles from its usual home inside the beltway. Many of the speakers and panels are pushing the President`s lies about election fraud.\par \par \uc2 Five years ago, Trump skipped CPAC because his conservative credentials were in question. But now the conference in the conservative movement have been reshaped completely in his image to the point of the CPAC`s unrecognizable. Because conservatism is now synonymous with loyalty to Trump. The latest example, Senator Mitch McConnell who earlier this month called Trump`s role in the January 6th riot, a, "Disgraceful dereliction of duty," while he struck a very different tone last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): There`s a lot that happened between now and 24. I`ve got at least four members that I think are planning on running for president plus some focus on governors and others. There`s no incumbent it should be a wide open race and fun for you all to cover.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: If the President was the party`s nominee, would you support him?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: The nominee of the party? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joined now by Al Cardenas. He`s a former chair of the Florida Republican Party. And more importantly, as a former chair of the American Conservative Union which is the organization that puts on CPAC. And he has publicly been distancing himself from this conservative concept this year. So Al, let me just ask this. Is this even because I`ve had somebody else who was active in CPAC for a long time who said, this is nowhere near what it --what it once was. What say you?\par \par \uc2 AL CARDENAS, REPUBLICAN PARTY OF FLORIDA FORMER CHAIR: I agree with that. Listen, I was a director of CPAC for 14 years (INAUDIBLE) and then I ran out a post twice for chairman of ACU. So, I think I know the institution very well. I think, if you looked at the guest list of speakers, and if you looked at the agenda for this year`s meeting, it`s a park cry from what we used to be.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives have always been at their core a little irreverent. We`ve always been a little difficult. We have had some unusual speakers speak to us, but by and large, every single CPAC that I was involved with, it was all about policies, whether it was immigration, taxes, foreign policy, social issues and, you know, sprinkled around those serious conversations where he irreverent speakers who light up that crowd such as Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 But this has become a cult, just they`re rolling up a golden statue of Donald Trump this morning, kind of set it off. And I`m sad, not only sad, but I`m also concerned. I think that what we saw January 6th was the result of Donald Trump`s irresponsible comments. If he does that again on Sunday, I`m concerned about the safety of a political process and who we have become. And so I hope he measures his words, although I wouldn`t go to the bank of that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, you have -- you have somebody that has tried to straddle this fence. You`re not a fan of Donald Trump. You`ve made that clear. You endorse Ron DeSantis. You helped him in his reelection. You know, at what point do you think it`s a mistake for somebody like Governor DeSantis to be associating himself with CPAC and the Trump wing of this party?\par \par \uc2 CARDENA: Well, I think the governor is very popular in Florida. A recent polls you all have taken show that that`s the case. Believe he stills a whole sense of loyalty to Donald Trump in his heart and he`s proven that to be the case. But to his credit, he didn`t mention Donald Trump once in his remarks at CPAC.\par \par \uc2 And I thought they were more geared towards policy issues and that was good for him. But we`ll see what the rest of the speakers do. I`ll have a sense that there will be a pilgrimage to CPAC. And I think that the remaining speakers will heighten the level of cult support for Donald Trump quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Were you disappointed that Senate Republican leader Mitch McConnell basically acquiesce that, well, if Donald Trump is the nominee of the Republican Party he`ll support the nominee?\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: That was a tough question, though. I mean, he started out talking about a wide open race and then he was asked a specific question and that is, well, you`ve got a binary choice in essence. Will you support Joe Biden`s reelect or are you going to support Donald Trump`s? So I get his answer. That`s not how I would have answered but I don`t think his answer was that outside the scope of reality in terms of what he`s got to do.\par \par \uc2 I do think these four years will be a long four years. Sunday will be a reminder that Donald Trump wants to be here for the next four years and beyond.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why do you think it is so hard to convince Republicans that Donald Trump has not been good for the party? I mean, the facts speak for themselves. There`s no power that Republicans have, right, and yet I talk to Republicans who are like, yes, but you have no idea about the base. And you`re sitting there going, but 48 percent is still 48 percent. Like you`re coming up short here in your Georgias. You`re coming up short in your Arizonas. You`re coming up short here in your Michigans. Like that`s not going to do it and it`s pretty clear who -- as to why.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think it is so difficult to convince them he`s the reason why they don`t have the Senate, they don`t have the White House?\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Well, I think they know that. The challenge is that they`re looking at their math and saying, well, you know, it takes half of the Republican vote as Trump cult supporters and the other half are not. And so if I do away with one-half, I can`t win a general election. I have to somehow hold my nose and run accepting the reality of Donald Trump and supporting that.\par \par \uc2 The challenge with that is that look at the hundreds of thousands of Republicans who have quit the party and since January 6th, there`ll be a shrinking base for years to come thanks in large part to Donald Trump. And so while in the short term there is a fear that without Donald Trump they lose general elections and perhaps primaries. The real reason is that the party will be extinct in 10 years if we follow this cult crowd.\par \par \uc2 So it`s a tough choice to make. The people who are willing to do a better job are resigning from Congress and from the Senate left and right. And so we`re being left more and more with people who are willing to do what it takes to stay in power.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It is a very diplomatic way of putting it and I think you are right. They`ll just do what it takes to stay in power.\par \par \uc2 Al Cardenas, as always, sir, thanks very much. Good to talk with you. Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, inside the complex and urgent process of making these vaccines. We finally have an inside, up-close look at these realities. We`re actually going to go inside a vaccine facility with a multi-step process begins. We`ll take you there after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY, CDC DIRECTOR: Things are tenuous. Now is not the time to relax restrictions. We may be done with the virus but clearly the virus is not done with us. We cannot get comfortable or give into a false sense of security that the worst of the pandemic is behind us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Here are the coronavirus facts at this hour. You just heard, that was CDC director, Dr. Rochelle Walensky, warning this morning that we`re not in a downturn anymore. We`re starting to see a plateau in the daily coronavirus cases. That may indicate that more transmissible variants are starting to take hold in the United States. Dr. Walensky says the data suggest that the more transmissible U.K. variant of the virus now accounts for approximately 10 percent of cases in this country.\par \par \uc2 For weeks cases and hospitalizations have been on the decline but the White House Coronavirus Response Team cautioned that the numbers are still extremely high. But a plateau of these cases means that we got to be careful with relaxing too many restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the FDA is relaxing some guidelines in how \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccines can be transported and stored, saying the vials can be frozen in a regular freezer for up to two weeks. The new guidance will make it easier to widely distribute the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine which until now needed to be stored in an ultra-low temperature freezer at more than 75 degrees below zero. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is also testing a third dose of its vaccine, by the way, as a potential booster due to the variants.\par \par \uc2 And right now an independent advisory board is meeting to consider recommending Johnson & Johnson`s single-shot vaccine for Emergency Use Authorization. This is the last major hurdle for the company`s vaccine candidate before FDA authorization. If approved, \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  says it could have up to four million vaccines out by next week with plans for 20 million by the end of March.\par \par \uc2 So our own Tom Costello is in one of the places that will make all of this happen if this new vaccine is approved. He`s at a lab in Baltimore with a rare inside look at just how these vaccine ingredients are mixed and developed, and in this case, Tom, at a location where they`re making the vaccine for \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, Tom, we keep hearing this is very difficult to ramp up. I`ve been dying to figure out. Well, take us inside. Explain why it`s so difficult to make this go faster. So tell me us, Tom.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So let me just tell you we are at Emergent Bio Sciences. This is a lab in Baltimore. And behind me they have been working here for the better part of, well, I would say about five to six months already, really around the clock, in creating and building the raw ingredients. The key ingredients to the vaccine for \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now what they do with that is, once they`ve got that, they then put it into a frozen form and they send it out to centers where they fill vials and put it into the liquid form. Then those centers begin the distribution process, right? So \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  has right now about four million shares -- sorry, doses ready to go right away and 20 million, as you said, by the end of March. So they are ready to go. And this facility has been really running full speed for months now.\par \par \uc2 In addition to this vaccine, for \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 , this facility also is working on the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine. Now as you know, that`s only available right now in the U.K. and in the E.U. Just today Canada approved the \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  vaccine. It could be a few more months before the FDA makes that the fourth vaccine. But I talked to \uc2 Sean Kirk\uc2 , one of the executives here at this company, about the entire process and precisely what do they do behind this glass window?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEAN KIRK\uc2 , \uc2 EMERGENT BIOSOLUTIONS\uc2 : We purify out the viral vectors, which are the active part of the vaccine. We should freeze it down, we ship it out of here, and it`s filled into the vials the patients or our customers are receiving or seeing when they get a vaccine shot.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: So this is the most critical -- these are the most critical ingredients for the vaccine but it still has to go and be put into liquid form?\par \par \uc2 KIRK: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: So with the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine, now we have three. We expect it`s going to have Emergency Use Authorization in a few hours or through the weekend. We`ll have three vaccines then with Moderna and with \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 . Potentially a fourth eventually with \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 . We had some good news out the White House COVID relief team today or the COVID team, I should say. Specifically they announced that nearly 50 percent of everybody over the age of 65 has already received at least one dose. Let me say that again. 50 percent of those 65 and older have already received one dose.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: So that is more evidence that this is really starting to pick up some momentum here, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No. That is a great stat. So, Tom, do you feel like you have a better understanding as to -- so ask these guys, OK, get us 100 million doses in a month. What would be the bottleneck for that? What is the most difficult part of this process? Is it procuring the ingredients? Is it the vial portion? Is it just simply that there is no new speed you can create? That there`s just a plotting along that has to happen? Do you feel like you understand the difficulty of the ramp-up part of this?\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Yes. And let me not speak specifically to J&J here, but just generally the vaccine makers and all of the above, everything you just said, listen, they were starting at ground zero, flat, right? Just a few months ago they didn`t have any production going. So the fact that they`ve now ramped up to the extent that they are now, with J&J talking 20 million doses at the end of March, you`ve got \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna talking about 300 million doses of course by the summer, I mean, that`s a tremendous surge in capacity when they were really standing still just four, five, six months ago.\par \par \uc2 So that`s number one. Number two, you`re right. There`s a bottleneck on raw supplies whether it`s the ingredients for the vaccine, whether it`s been the right glass for the vials, in the case of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  because they have to withstand all those ultracold temperatures. So all of that has been part of the equation here.\par \par \uc2 You know, I had a conversation just yesterday with somebody. We were talking about what`s the problem. Why is it that this has taken so long? We talked about the fact that they`re starting from zero, but also, you know, if you go to a stadium and it`s full, and you go to the restroom and you know there`s a long line to get into the restroom, right, because you can`t accommodate everybody all at once.\par \par \uc2 We have a country of 320 million people. And right now two vaccine makers, two, that are trying to move as fast as they can. I think it`s just simply a bottleneck and a matter of capacity and trying to move as fast as they can.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And the good news is, at every stadium you`ve been to, eventually you`ll make it to your part of the line as well.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Tom Costello, in Baltimore for us. Tom, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 And be sure to check out our interactive state-by-state guide to figure out when and where you can receive your COVID vaccine. Visit planyourvaccine.com to learn more. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. President Joe Biden and the first lady are in Texas today following the winter storm that just simply wreaked havoc over there the past couple of weeks leaving millions without power, heat, and they had the clean water issue for days. That was an issue. And as we told you earlier, Air Force One landed in Houston earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 It`s President Biden`s first trip as commander-in-chief to a state after a natural disaster. In just a few minutes, we`re expecting the president and Dr. Biden to visit the \uc2 Houston Food Bank\uc2  to package food and water for the local community. Then President Biden is expected to tour an emergency operations center. Later this afternoon, the president will also deliver remarks at a mass vaccination site before flying back to Washington this evening.\par \par \uc2 Several members of the Texas delegation are joining President Biden this afternoon including Governor Greg Abbott and the senior senator there, John Cornyn.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of Texas, joining me now to talk about the situation there, along with a number of headlines particularly in the foreign policy arena, is Texas Republican Congressman Mike McCaul. He`s the ranking member on House Foreign Affairs Committee and he`s a former chairman of the House Homeland Security Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, it`s good to see you. It`s been a while. But first I want to start with --\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE MCCAUL (R-TX): You too, Chuck. Good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. With President Biden going down to Texas. Look, it`s now been sort of a week after the heart of this disaster down there. And there`s going to be a lot of after-action reports. But let me ask the sort of -- one large sort of question. Do you think it`s time for Texas to join the national grid when it comes to power?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: You know, I think there`s going to be a lot of after-action studies being done. ERCOT was built to be self-reliable and not be tied to the other grids and that provides a cyber security protection. However, in the case of the arctic blast, we`re not able to share with other grids. And I think that is in time of an emergency, the ability to share with other grids because, you know, I went five days without power on my own home, and no water. And obviously mistakes have been made.\par \par \uc2 And I also want to thank the president of the United States for coming down to my state and I saw him in the White House and talked about the FEMA assistance, and he has granted assistance pretty much to every county now within the state.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s interesting, you talk about -- it sounds like you`d like to come up with a sort of Texas version. You`ll join the grid but it`s not a full joining of the grid. You think there`s a possibility you could create some sort of quasi-independence but the national grid could backfill and then vice versa, you know, if there are problems in, say, the southwest or in other close areas Texas could backfill?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Yes, I think maybe the sort of a hybrid version where you have a grid that`s self-reliant, is sustainable without being vulnerable to cybersecurity attacks. That`s been the strength of ERCOT. Obviously we saw the weaknesses last week with the grid going down. And I think on an emergency basis that would have been very helpful if it tapped in to the other grids.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve got to ask you, though, on your constituencies, you know, the idea of this deregulation was supposed to lower power bills. The data doesn`t back that up. People don`t have lower power bills the way that I think those proponents thought they would have. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, you know, I think the thought process and I know Governor Perry talked about this, as we wouldn`t be subjected to so much federal oversight regulations and that would bring the costs down. And now we`re seeing the reports that you mentioned. I don`t know the truth yet. We`re looking at all the facts coming in right now. Certainly that was the concept of why it was built independently in the first place.\par \par \uc2 So, look, my state legislature is looking at this very intensely. We are looking at this at the federal level, you know, as well how to fix this and what to do with the ERCOT organization and the board members who made mistakes and where, when and why.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to move to some foreign affairs issues. The president just released the Khashoggi intelligence report. Congress actually ordered former President Trump`s administration to do that. First of all, do you think it`s good that this is public? And if you do, what do you hope the impact is and what do you expect it to be when it comes to U.S.-Saudi relations?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, it was a gruesome murder. I`m not surprised by the director of National Intelligence report that was just released. It was classified. Most things that happen in the intelligence, they don`t happen by accident. And the crown prince is in charge of all that. It happened in the Saudi consulate in Turkey in a very gruesome murder in and of itself. So I can`t say I`m surprised by it, but I do think there should be consequences to it.\par \par \uc2 You know, we have looked at the idea of sanctions against the Saudis on this. I think there should be a strong condemnation of this murder and what took place, possibly by the king himself. And also, you know, I think that we`ve got to be looking at, you know, policies that -- countries that go against journalists and dissidents to restore trust. I think there should be a consequence.\par \par \uc2 And Chuck, what is the downside to all of this is that we were following moving forward with the partnership with the Saudi, the royal family and the kingdom. They are becoming aligned with Israel against the biggest threat in the region and that is Iran. So the geopolitical issue gets very messy, very tricky now in light of this gruesome murder.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Don`t you worry that MBS took advantage of that, that he knows we need them or he thinks we need them, we need the ally, Israel needs the ally, so I can get away with a little bit of stuff like this?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, I think it`s very reckless and it was very amateurish. And the idea that we wouldn`t suspect him to be behind it was, you know, a bit laughable. So I do think perhaps, but, you know, we obviously with the Abraham Accords and the alliances with the state of Israel, Saudi was going to be kind of the real prize here to get them to join the Abraham Accords. So this has really been a real big step back.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`ve met the crown prince on many occasions in Washington. He seemed to be wanting to move his country forward in a more progressive direction. But this -- again, this gruesome murder of Jamal Khashoggi, you know, Larry Wright is a good friend of mine, they were dear friends. And it`s a major setback in our foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about the strike last night. You came out and you were supportive of the president`s decision. But, you know, I`ve got to ask you, we launched an air strike in Syria against Iranian-backed militias for actions that took place in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Now I was speaking with Senator Van Hollen earlier and he essentially said, well, you could loosely make the case that, yes, that falls under the AUMF but we are getting toward a slippery slope here, are we not?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Yes, in the Foreign Affairs Committee, I`m the Republican leader, we do the authorizations and we will get a report, a notice of a use of military force within 48 hours on this particular event. I know in the Soleimani case that occurred in Iraq but it was a self-defense, Article Two constitutional self-defense strike. I`m assuming that`s what the administration is going to rely on in this case since they did hit our U.S. soldiers in both Irbil in northern Iraq and also got very close to our embassy in the Green Zone in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Both the Shia proxies, the Shia militias are behind these attacks, and I think that would be the strongest argument they could make. Some of this going back to the original AUMF from 2001 against al Qaeda. You know, I think a lot of both Republicans and Democrats think that we -- maybe we want to take a look at updating some of these authorized uses of military force.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. I mean, let`s remember there are now people who weren`t born who are being asked to engage in force essentially through an authorization that, again, was created before they were born.\par \par \uc2 One last question, and I guess I`m going to call this the McConnell question, since he was asked this last night. If Donald Trump is the nominee of the party in 2024, do you think you can support him?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Look, we`re four years out and there`s so much that`s going to happen over the next four years. I`m going to support the nominee. That`s what we do. But I think we`re going to have a lot of good candidates out there. I think, look, we can take a lot of the victories that the prior administration had. I think his appeal to the working man, the working class, blue-collar is something to take away from the previous administration, from Trump, and I think that`s the part of the Trump administration we want to carry forward that Reagan was able to win over a lot of these states, you know, blue states.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I -- I don`t know, we`ll see. We`re a long ways out, Chuck. And I`d be remiss if I didn`t mention the incredible White House invitation and visit with the president of the United States on the CHIPS for America Act which is this is about decoupling our supply chain out of China.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: From the Chinese Communist Party and then manufacturing and bringing jobs to the United States and manufacturing here while at the same time protecting our national security. I was really elated at the substantive conversation we had for about an hour with about eight combination of House members and senators, bipartisan. He said it`s the best meeting he`s had since he`s been president in the White House and I think this is a policy that`s so pro-American and so against the Chinese Communist Party, it`s a real winner. And with the president`s now endorsement and support, I think we`re going to get it done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Congressman Mike McCaul, Republican from Texas, there, you aren`t the only Republican praising that meeting yesterday. Mike Braun was on here yesterday doing the same thing. Anyway, appreciate your time and perspective, and I`ve got to rush off here.\par \par \uc2 I`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. If it`s Sunday, it`s "MEET THE PRESS" on NBC. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur, I apologize that I`m late, right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210227eh2q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O`Donnell for February 25, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, John Heilemann, Ben Rhodes \par \uc2 7,835 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 So, Jen once again goes above and beyond the call of duty.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Seriously. First of all, she is, like, the crisis manager of the staff in every circumstance, but at this point she`s 15 months pregnant as far as I can tell. It`s going to happen, we`re there, and she`s running everything, calm as a cucumber while you blew through two commercial breaks.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Uh-huh, and you know exactly how much work I put in if I had to go through one half day of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Me too.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Guys wouldn`t be showing up for work, I can promise you that.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Nor me. Nor me, my friend.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Oh, you`re a good soldier.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Rachel. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We are covering two breaking news stories tonight, and we have the perfect expert guests who cover both of those subjects in our opening discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  authorized his first military strike today. American officials told "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " it, quote, was essentially a tiny demonstration strike, one bomb dropped on a small cluster of buildings on the Syria/Iraq border, used to transit militia fighters and weaponry in and out of the country.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon press secretary, John Kirby, said, quote, these strikes were authorized in response to recent attacks against American and coalition personnel in Iraq. The buildings in Syria were reportedly used by Iranian- backed groups launching attacks against American personnel in Iraq. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports, quote, the Pentagon offered up several larger groups of targets, but Mr. Biden approved the smallest option, American officials said.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby said: This proportionate military response was conducted together with diplomatic measures, including consultation with coalition partners. The operation sends an unambiguous message, President Biden will act to protect American coalition personnel. At the same time, we have acted in a deliberate manner that aims to de- escalate the overall situation in both eastern Syria and Iraq.\par \par \uc2 The other big breaking news of the night is Senate parliamentarian Elizabeth MacDonough privately announced her ruling to Senate staff that a minimum wage increase would violate the budget reconciliation rules of the Senate if it is included in the Biden COVID relief bill in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  immediately released this statement. President Biden is disappointed in this outcome as he proposed having the $15 minimum wage as part of the American Rescue Plan. He respects the parliamentarian`s decision and the Senate`s process. He will work with leaders in Congress to determine the best path forward because no one in this country should work full time and live in poverty. He urges Congress to move quickly to pass the American Rescue Plan, which includes $1,400 rescue checks for most Americans, funding to get this virus under control, aid to get our schools reopened and desperately needed help for the people who have been hardest hit by this crisis.\par \par \uc2 The House of Representatives is scheduled to vote on the bill tomorrow with the minimum wage included in the House bill. Now, it is common for the House of Representatives to remove provisions when they know in advance that they will be stripped from the Senate bill because of a ruling by the Senate parliamentarian, if that parliamentarian`s ruling comes in advance, as it did.\par \par \uc2 But in a written statement tonight, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said the ruling from the Senate parliamentarian is disappointing. House Democrats believe that the minimum wage hike is necessary. Therefore, this provision will remain in the American Rescue Plan on the floor tomorrow. The Senate parliamentarian`s argument for including the minimum wage in the Senate bill, the arguments about that was led by the staff of the Senate Budget Committee Chairman Bernie Sanders.\par \par \uc2 The argument was strong enough that it took the Senate parliamentarian days to make a decision on an issue that was never even previously remotely considered as something that would fit within the Senate budget reconciliation rules. Chairman Sanders issued a written statement tonight saying, I strongly disagree with tonight`s decision by the Senate parliamentarian. Yet because of the archaic and undemocratic rules of the Senate, we are unable to move forward to end starvation wages in this country and raise the income of 32 million struggling Americans. That fight continues.\par \par \uc2 In what was the most important news of this morning, which seems like a long time ago now, the acting U.S. Capitol police chief, Yogananda Pittman, told a House hearing that the reason the Capitol grounds are still surrounded by a fence topped with barbed wire and protected by the National Guard is because the people who attacked the Capitol for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on January 6th would like to blow up the Capitol for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  during President Biden`s yet unscheduled first address to Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YOGANANDA PITTMAN, ACTING CAPITOL POLICE CHIEF: We know that the insurrectionists that attacked the Capitol weren`t only interested in attacking members of Congress and officers. They wanted to send a symbolic message to the nation as of who was in charge of that legislative process. We know members of the militia groups that were present on January 6th have stated their desires that they want to blow up the Capitol and kill as many members as possible with a direct nexus to the State of the Union, which we know that date has not been identified. So, based on that information, we think that it`s prudent that captain police maintain its enhanced and robust security posture until we address those vulnerabilities going forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: After that hearing this morning, the chairman of that subcommittee, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , revealed in an interview that federal prosecutors are now investigating possible reconnaissance missions in the Capitol led by Republican members of Congress or their staffs for visitors in the Capitol, some of whom then attacked the Capitol on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON EXAMINER REPORTER: I just was wondering if you`ve gotten any update about concerns about any House members giving tours to rioters on January 5th, if that`s still seen as a concern and contributing factor to what happened on the 6th, and whether a threat level of other members of Congress is a concern.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN\uc2  (D-OH): Yeah. Thanks for asking that. That is in the hands of the U.S. attorney here in D.C. now. They are reviewing the footage. That whole case is with the U.S. attorney, so we don`t have any visibility on that at all. But we know that they are looking at it and it`s been turned over to them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading off our discussion tonight, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2  of Colorado and Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  of New Jersey. They are both members of the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, Congresswoman Sherrill, let me begin with you on what we just heard from \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , because you were among the first to say you thought you saw what looked to you to possibly be reconnaissance missions in the Capitol the day or days before January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Can you tell us if you have been contacted by federal prosecutors about that investigation?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKIE SHERRILL (D-NJ): Lawrence, I`ve spoken to federal investigators, I`ve told them everything I know, and now that is under investigation.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And what is -- what is your sense if you can tell us about what the elements of that investigation include? Does it include possible investigation of members of Congress?\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL: You know, I don`t want to go any further than that. Like I said, I turned everything over to them and I know now it`s an active investigation.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let me also -- you rely on your military experience and your position on the Armed Services Committee to get your reaction to the air strike that President Biden ordered today.\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL: You know, I mean, this is -- it was almost like a weight had been lifted off my shoulders as those of us who served know when a president makes decisions as commander-in-chief, it affects so many troops on the ground. And to have a commander-in-chief in office who obviously looked carefully at his options, did not escalate the situation, we did not hear about this on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he looked at the ways in which he could ensure that you can`t attack U.S. troops with impunity. You can`t simply attack U.S. troops without any response.\par \par \uc2 And yet the response that he decided upon was measured. He looked to de- escalate the situation, not bring us to the brink of war.\par \par \uc2 I -- you know, as a member of the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2  with Representative Crow, we`ll continue looking at this and seeing the further developments and what needs to take place in the aftermath of this. But from what I`ve seen so far, it seems like a very measured and thoughtful response.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Congressman Crow, to you and your combat experience and your position on the Armed Services Committee, your reaction to the president`s airstrike, the first one that he has ordered today.\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON CROW\uc2  (D-CO): Yeah, thanks, Lawrence, for having us on tonight.\par \par \uc2 So, this is my view of that. We just spent the last four years witnessing a stunning abdication of responsibility by members of Congress vis-a-vis their responsibility to be a check and balance on the executive. Our responsibility is not to rubber stamp what the president does, whether it`s Republican or Democratic president. We saw the GOP basically do whatever President Trump wanted to do over the last four years.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the bottom line for me is, do I have more confidence in President Biden in doing the right thing and actually trying to do a measured response and appropriate response to protect our country? Yes.\par \par \uc2 But in the same way that I didn`t criticize \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  simply because he was a Republican, I`m not going to look at this and say, you know, yes, this is the right thing to do by President Biden simply because he`s a Democrat. My responsibility is to look into the intelligence to conduct appropriate oversight.\par \par \uc2 That`s what we`re going to as members of the Armed Services Committee. We`re going to make sure this was done right and all indications are that it was. But that`s our responsibility.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Representative Sherrill, did you have any information at the Armed Services Committee before today that the president was considering a strike like this?\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL: I had not heard that he was considering this strike from the Armed Services Committee.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s turn to what we`ve learned about the minimum wage and the COVID relief bill. It is not going to be in the Senate bill. The parliamentarian is going to rule that it violates Senate rules. It would take 60 votes to overrule the parliamentarian through a vote of the Senate. So, those votes don`t exist.\par \par \uc2 And so, as of tonight, we know by -- there`s no question about it, it will not survive, will not be in the Senate bill.\par \par \uc2 What should the House do about that? Speaker Pelosi said tonight she intends tomorrow to bring it to a vote with the minimum wage provision in it.\par \par \uc2 Has there been any consideration to try to send to the Senate the bill in a form that they could actually pass, taking it directly from the House?\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL: Lawrence, I serve on the Education and Labor Committee, and I have to tell you, it is high time we passed minimum wage. We have a pathway to that in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 I was a lifeguard in the late `80s earning $7.25. I`m sure many people would be surprised to hear that is the federal minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 And as I was on the Education and Labor Committee, I heard people talking about what a great first job. In many cases, this is not people`s first job. Over half of the people that are working at minimum wage jobs are between the ages of 25 and 54. Six in 10 are women, and we know that over a quarter of the people working in minimum wage jobs have children.\par \par \uc2 So, this is supporting families and it`s supporting them at poverty level. We know there`s no district in the nation where you can afford a modest two-bedroom apartment on the current minimum wage salary working 40 hours a week. So, it`s really time that we move this forward.\par \par \uc2 We have a bill together. We`re intending to pass it on Friday. I`m not sure we know at this point what the Senate is going to do. There`s several pathways that they have before them.\par \par \uc2 I think that sometimes we think the Senate`s going to do something based on what we hear and then we send bills over and they do something different. So, I think we got to get this bill to them and get it passed as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I understand how mysterious the Senate appears to be from the House of Representatives, and I want to assure you having worked in the Senate, the House appears to be equally mysterious to people who work in the Senate. It`s as if they`re thousands of miles apart instead of feet away.\par \par \uc2 Let`s listen to what Ron Klain said with \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  right here at last night about this situation with the parliamentarian.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: We`re going to honor the rules of the Senate and work within that system to get this bill passed. This is a $1.9 trillion package that is vital to getting this country in a position to crush the virus, in a position to get people vaccinated, in a position to get to schools open, and to help all those people that you talked about at the start of the show.\par \par \uc2 We are going to get this package passed. That`s our highest priority. We want the minimum wage as part of it. That`s what the president proposed. But we`re going to work with the Senate to get this package passed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, Congressman Crow, Ron Klain saying there`s absolutely no chance of Vice President Harris in her role as president of the Senate overruling the parliamentarian. That`s not going to happen. The parliamentarian`s ruling is going to stand.\par \par \uc2 So, it will not -- minimum wage won`t be in the bill in the Senate. The last time the minimum wage was increased, it was inserted in what everyone calls a must-pass bill, it was basically inserted in a defense spending bill and it passed the Senate with 80 votes. That looks like the way it`ll have to be done this year at some point.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Well, the bottom line is we have to do the right thing and the right thing is to provide relief to the American people and raise the minimum wage. You know, people are struggling. They`re barely getting by.\par \par \uc2 I haven`t had a single person in the community that I represent talk to me about reconciliation or the Senate rules or the process. They just want relief. They want this done.\par \par \uc2 You know, that`s what people on Main Street in my community are talking about. I remember when I was a veteran returning from the war in Iraq and Afghanistan. And I remember, you know, all the -- we support our troop signs and the yellow ribbons that I came home to.\par \par \uc2 But I couldn`t find a job. I couldn`t get my veterans benefits. I couldn`t barely afford to pay for my rent. That`s the situation our frontline health care workers, our service industry workers, the people that have been barely getting by that have been making this country run are facing right now.\par \par \uc2 So, the bottom line is they need relief. We need to get them relief. And we need to figure out how to get it done.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Representative Sherrill, we have heard Republican members of Congress complaining about why the Capitol looks like an armed encampment with barbed wire fence and National Guard still around. We learned today in this hearing that the acting chief of \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  said, oh, that`s because the Trump supporters who attacked the Capitol have been planning to blow up the Capitol when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  delivers an address there.\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL: Well, I think that when you have the former president of the United States who incited an angry mob to attack Congress, to attack me and Jason quite frankly, as we were doing our constitutional duty of certifying the election, you have to put up some protections. When the party -- when I don`t think Republicans as a whole have come to terms with what happened on January 6th and have in many cases not taken appropriate steps to ensure that it doesn`t happen again, then certainly you are going to see other measures to protect the Capitol. We are not going to let the Capitol be attacked again.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congresswoman \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  and Congressman \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2 , thank you both for starting off our discussion tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SHERRILL: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, more on the breaking parliamentary news of the night, which is challenging political news for the Democrats. The Senate parliamentarian has finally ruled the minimum wage increase cannot be included when the Biden COVID relief bill in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  says she`s still going to include it in the House bill. President Biden says he respects the parliamentarian`s ruling. Senate Budget Committee Chairman Bernie Sanders says he disagrees with it. What happens next?\par \par \uc2 John Heilemann and Alex Wagner will join us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Part of what the president and I are offering as part of the relief is the American rescue plan to say it can`t only be on folks. It has to be all of us working together. So that`s what everybody needs to do as an individual, get the vaccine, wear a mask, social distancing, wash your hands. What we need to do is pass the American Rescue Plan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Passing that COVID relief bill with a minimum wage increase in it has o now become virtually impossible tonight with the Senate parliamentarian ruling that a minimum wage provision cannot be included in a budget reconciliation bill, according to the Senate`s parliamentary rules.\par \par \uc2 The same parliamentary rules do not apply in the House of Representatives. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  issued a statement tonight saying the Senate parliamentarian ruling was disappointing and, quote, this provision will remain in the American rescue plan on the floor tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is rewriting the Washington formula for bipartisan. "The Washington Post" reports in his first five weeks in office, President Biden is spending as much time if not more courting Republican governors as he is wooing the senators, he needs to pass legislation.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what President Biden said today in a meeting of the \uc2 National Governors Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In every one of your states and territories, red and blue, millions of Americans are hurting badly.\par \par \uc2 We just have to step up. The economic toll, we have to address with the same aggression and seriousness and purpose as we do the virus. That`s what the American rescue plan does. Instead of chasing COVID-19, it allows us to get ahead of it.\par \par \uc2 The plan was created to create the things you collectively asked me for in conversation. The only way to contain this pandemic and help the American people as quickly as possible is passing the plan as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of the American people from both parties support it according to the data. I`m grateful that many of you Democrats and Republicans expressed support as well.\par \par \uc2 We are not the type of nation that can or will stand by and watch our people suffer needlessly through no fault of their own.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now are John Heilemann, NBC and MSNBC national affairs analyst and host of the "Hell and High Water" podcast, from "The Recount". Also, with us, Alex Wagner, contributing writer at "The Atlantic," both are co-hosts and executive producers of Showtime`s "The Circus".\par \par \uc2 Alex Wagner, let me begin with you. Things just got complicated for both the House of Representatives and the Senate because the Senate parliamentarian has ruled that you cannot include the minimum wage increase in the Senate bill. That means in the end, it`s not going to be in the legislation.\par \par \uc2 But the House is going to vote tomorrow, according to \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , on a version of the bill that includes the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 What happens next?\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, THE ATLANTIC: You know, I think that no matter what happens, progressive Democrats aren`t giving up on this. Bernie Sanders said he may try to tweak the language so that large corporations will not get tax credits, tax write-offs if they don`t pay their workers more than $15 an hour. That`s his end run around this. I think that`s probably unlikely to be in final passage, who actually knows.\par \par \uc2 But I spent a day on the Hill with House Progressives and they`re saying, look, if isn`t in the final recovery act, if this isn`t in the final relief bill, we are going to pass a stand-alone raising of the federal minimum wage. And there are two goals in that, Lawrence. One is to actually raise the federal minimum wage, and the other is to abolish the filibuster, which is actually the shiny apple at the end of the road for progressives as much as the federal minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 House Progressives believe that if they have a stand-alone bill to raise the federal minimum wage, they will pass it, they will send it to the Senate, and then the question will be on the front doorstep of our political system. Do we abide by the rules of the filibuster, which would kill that federal minimum wage raise, or do we actually use that moment, this inflection point to abolish the filibuster, which is something that the Biden administration has been asked about, which is Democrats would like to do?\par \par \uc2 This fight is going to lead to an even bigger fight over one of the biggest procedural hurdles to passing major progressive legislation in the Biden era.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: John Heilemann, it sounds like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Chuck Schumer have to swerve away from that fight because we know how it will turn out. The filibuster would not be overturned. There would not be 50 votes for that.\par \par \uc2 And the way to do it is to make as fast a move as they can on a plan for including the minimum wage in something like a defense spending bill, a so- called must-pass bill. The Congress has must-pass bills, and that`s how they raised it last time. That`s how they`re going to raise it the next time. That`s the only way they`re ever going to raise it, is put it in something that Republicans have to vote for.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HEILEMANN, MSNBC NATIONAL AFFAIRS ANALYST: Yeah, and something that the parliamentarian will rule as -- we`ll get around this issue of trying to put it in reconciliation and, therefore, at the whim or discretion of the parliamentarian. And I think, you know, Alex is putting her hand on -- her finger on one of the fundamental -- not just there`s that ideal disjuncture between moderates and progressives in the Democratic caucus, also one that`s a disjuncture between the House and Senate, right?\par \par \uc2 No one in the world is more frustrated with the Senate, particularly with the parliamentarian at the moment than House progressives in the Democratic caucus. And the reality, as you point out, Lawrence, is that there are not enough votes to pass the minimum wage hike to $15 with Democratic reluctance in the Senate. There`s not enough votes in the Senate to overturn the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 So the question becomes how do they find another way to get the minimum wage hike which is overwhelmingly popular around the country, whether that number is $15 or below that, is there some other vehicle? And I think that is where Chuck Schumer and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  are going to go.\par \par \uc2 Because actually when it comes to it, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn`t have the stomach to try to overturn the filibuster. Maybe even if he had the votes, I`m not sure \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would be for that. So I think that`s what we`re going to get is the solution that you just laid out.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, I`m completely in in favor of getting rid of the filibuster rules, but I know the votes aren`t there to do it.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Alex, the enthusiasm for passing what they can pass is the challenge for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the speaker and Chuck Schumer to maintain. And Joe Biden`s statement tonight was I respect the ruling of the parliamentarian, and then he went on to stress everything else in this bill that is great, the $1,400 checks and the COVID vaccine help, all of the other things that are worth voting for, and that`s going to be his message for the next 24 hours and beyond.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, CONTRIBUTING WRITER, "THE ATLANTIC": Absolutely. And you know, I think that the wisdom you hear from Democrats on the Hill, both moderate and progressive, is we`re not going to let perfect be the enemy of the good here.\par \par \uc2 The package is $1.9 trillion in funding that touches so many aspects of the American economy. I don`t think there`s a single Democrat who doesn`t want to see it pass. Not just because ideologically it dovetails with their entire agenda but because they believe that they have the vast support of the American public, including Republicans.\par \par \uc2 I was talking to House leadership, and they marvel at the fact that Republicans won`t vote for this. They look at the very naked reality that Americans are going to get checks for $1,400. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  understood what a political gift that was to the party that passes it. That`s why he wanted his party to do it.\par \par \uc2 The fact that the Republicans won`t play ball in this, that they`re going to have no fingertips on stimulus checks that are going to go to American families along with earned income tax credits, child tax credits, the average family of four could get $10,000 in stimulus checks and in tax credits.\par \par \uc2 That is a major boon. That is not something American voters forget. And that`s why you`ve seen such remarkable unity from the Democratic caucus on both ends of the spectrum on this. They will pass this bill. They will pass this bill.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Nobel prize winning economist \uc2 Paul Krugman\uc2  was on with Rachel in the last hour. He also happens to be politically liberal. He`s in favor of everything in this bill, including the minimum wage hike.\par \par \uc2 Rachel asked him if with the minimum wage hike removed from the bill, how good is the bill. And here is what \uc2 Paul Krugman\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL KRUGMAN\uc2 , ECONOMIST: This is a -- I mean, I`m kind of almost pinching myself. I can`t believe that we`re about to get a bill that is as good -- it`s not perfect, especially -- I missed the minimum wage. But that we`re about to get a bill that is as good as it appears, we`re going to get.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: John Heilemann -- \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  might be inviting \uc2 Paul Krugman\uc2  to speak to the Democratic caucus on this if they have to eventually vote on final passage on a bill that doesn`t have the minimum wage increase.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HEILEMANN, MSNBC NATIONAL AFFAIRS ANALYST: Yes, I mean. You point at the right way, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 I mean Alex, you know, points out that right now there`s a lot of enthusiasm for the bill. There has been. It`s been kind of amazing how the Biden administration with a bill this big that normally would have a lot of political risks for a lot of Democrats, they managed to get to this place where they have this overwhelming public support.\par \par \uc2 They`ve had a unified caucus in the House, they have mostly unity in the Senate on the key elements aside from the minimum wage, on everything else, right? Things have been going so well.\par \par \uc2 And in the same way that \uc2 Paul Krugman\uc2  is pinching himself for how good the bill is, a lot of people in the Biden White House are pinching themselves over how flawlessly this has all unfolded for them on the political side until this parliamentarian ruling.\par \par \uc2 And I think for their goal, you know, in terms of the messaging and \uc2 Paul Krugman\uc2  helps them with this, it`s just everybody, keep your eye on the ball here. Don`t get in the weeds on this question (INAUDIBLE). Minimum wage is super important but again, to Alex`s phrase, don`t let the perfect be the enemy of the good and keep this level of enthusiasm, keep this momentum going.\par \par \uc2 You know, the notion in politics that good gets better and that`s (ph) worst, right. Keep building on, get these points on the board right now, get that momentum going forward, get this bill done, $1.9 trillion and lay down a template for the next thing you want to do -- a $3 trillion infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 Don`t let -- don`t trip over your own shoelaces on the way to that. And I think that they have a pretty good chance of getting that done. Democrats often can get a little distracted and off in the weeds. I don`t think that there`s a high risk of that right now.\par \par \uc2 I do think as Alex said, there`s a lot of focus and a lot of concentration on how important it is to get this bill passed. And on the merits, as Krugman lays out, it`s a strong bill.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The costars of Sunday night`s must-watch Show Time`s "The Circus" Alex Wagner and John Heilemann, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And John, thank you tonight for being in a hotel where your window tells us exactly where you are for once.\par \par \uc2 HEILEMANN: I brought it for you, Lawrence. I brought it tonight. there you go.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. And I know which hotel that is. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Ben Rhodes has been in the room when the president is deciding on a proportionate response to military aggression. President Biden decided to launch his first air strike this morning against Iranian-backed forces in Syria. Ben Rhodes will take us inside the room and tell us how that decision is made. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tonight, Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  said this about the first air strike ordered by President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  today which targeted buildings in Syria used by Iranian-backed forces.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what the Defense Secretary said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2 , U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY: We`re confident that that target was being used for the same Shia militia that conducted the strikes. We`re very deliberate about our approach, as you would expect us to be. We allowed and encouraged the Iraqis to investigate and develop intelligence, and that was very helpful to us in refining the target.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Ben Rhodes, former deputy national security adviser to President Obama. He`s an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Ben, you`ve been in the room when these kinds of decisions are considered and, I`m sure been in the room when they`ve been rejected and when they`ve been decided upon\par \par \uc2 What do you think it was like today for the president to make this choice? And the latest reporting on it is that it was a specific response to a specific action taken by those Iranian-backed forces.\par \par \uc2 BEN RHODES, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well look, it`s the first time that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has issued an order like this. And even though he was in the room for eight years in these kinds of decisions, that`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 And it`s inside of Syria, so that`s also a big deal. But I think what you can tell is that there was a rigorous process here. There was an event where an Iranian-backed militia apparently inside of Iraq killed a contractor who`s working with the U.S. military, injured some others.\par \par \uc2 The military clearly worked up a set of options for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to consider. \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  was the commander of U.S. Forces in Iraq. He was a commander of Central Command in the Obama administration launching the war against ISIS in Iraq and Syria so he knows this part of the world. He knows his considerations.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  based on the reports did not pick the most aggressive option here. He wanted to send a response and to try to end the cycle of escalation. The risk, of course, is always that when you take a shot like this, there could be a reprisal and you could face escalation.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. The reports are that President Biden chose the most minimal of the military responses. But my question about a meeting like this in deciding how to respond to this. Is there someone in that room advocating no military response, a response other than a military action?\par \par \uc2 RHODES: Well, Lawrence, what you would have is the military probably worked up three different options and the more robust option probably would have targeted other infrastructures associated with this militia perhaps in Syria.\par \par \uc2 And usually there`s a lot of momentum to do something, particularly when there`s been an attack on U.S. forces inside of Iraq like this.\par \par \uc2 I think the cautionary note would be this is against an Iranian-supported target. It`s inside of Syria. This could escalate things with Iran, including at a time when they`re pursuing diplomacy over Iran`s nuclear agreement.\par \par \uc2 And so you might have some voices of caution saying that this could again, prevent the success of diplomacy. This could escalate a military conflict. This raises legal questions in terms of taking a shot inside of Syria.\par \par \uc2 But my guess is that given that this was a specific response to a specific attack against U.S. forces in Iraq, there probably was pretty broad consensus for some kind of response. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took a proportional response, not the response that might have been bigger that it might have had a higher risk of escalation.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Is the State Department typically represented in this kind of discussion? Or is it in effect represented through the national security adviser?\par \par \uc2 RHODES: No, I would assume, Lawrence, that everybody is at the table there in the situation room. This is -- I worked with a lot of these people. They`re process-oriented, which we didn`t always see in the Trump years which would mean you would have a meeting in the situation room I guess virtually maybe these days, with the Secretary of State involved as well as the Secretary of Defense and the Commander of the Joint Chiefs and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the vice president.\par \par \uc2 This obviously being the first type of decision like this that she`s participated in. I would assume that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would want to hear from all of his advisers and then he makes the call.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And so you`ve got a Secretary of State who`s trying to put the Iran nuclear deal back together, which is to say get the United States participation back in line with it. And this has to be a very, very difficult situation for everyone who`s concerned with putting the Iran nuclear deal back together.\par \par \uc2 RHODES: They`re weighing all manner of different conditions in the region right now, including pursuing diplomacy with Iran, including what we expect to have tomorrow with Saudi Arabia, including again some of these ongoing attacks in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 But the thing Lawrence is all of these people, nearly all of them, were present in the Obama administration when there was a negotiation that led to a nuclear agreement, even as we did occasionally have these kinds of incidents inside of Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Again what`s different here is that this one is inside of Syria. So I think in the past this team has seen that you can do what is necessary to protect U.S. forces even if it means taking a shot at an Iranian proxy while at the same time trying to deal with the nuclear issue through diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ben Rhodes, it`s just invaluable to hear from someone with your experience in the room in these situations. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 RHODES: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is famously bad at sleeping. He has chronic insomnia, always has. And he will try to fall asleep tonight knowing that his tax returns are now finally in the hands of criminal prosecutors in New York City.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The reason \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  used to tweet in the middle of the night and before dawn is that he`s never been very good at sleeping. Donald Trump`s worst nightmare is upon him tonight with his widely known chronic insomnia now being fueled by the horror of criminal prosecutors studying his tax returns in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been trying to avoid this for his entire adult life. He spent the last year and a half telling judges, including Supreme Court justices, that he appointed that a president should never have to hand over his tax returns to prosecutors, even though prior presidents willingly handed over their tax returns to the public. No previous president has ever been the subject of a criminal investigation until \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his businesses are being investigated by the Manhattan district attorney who has hired a high-profile special counsel to study the Trump tax returns along with a forensic accounting firm. They will soon find out why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  paid only $750 in federal income taxes while he was president and zero in income taxes for the years before he was president.\par \par \uc2 Manhattan district attorney Cyrus Vance`s investigation has extended from hush money payments to Stormy Daniels to focusing on possible bank fraud and insurance fraud.\par \par \uc2 Investigators are focusing on the roles of \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , along with Allen Weisselberg, the Trump Organization`s chief financial officer.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest, Tim O`Brien, is one of the very few people who have ever seen a Trump tax return. And he writes, "A dam that Trump has spent decades fortifying around his finances and tax returns has been broken and Vance`s investigation appears to be broad enough to pose a serious criminal threat to the former president, his three eldest children and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Tim O`Brien who has seen the tax returns joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will try to sleep tonight knowing that Manhattan district attorney \uc2 Cyrus Vance\uc2  has in his possession several million pages of Trump tax returns and financial records.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is someone who has seen Trump tax returns. Tim O`Brien, senior columnist for Bloomberg Opinion. He is the author of "Trump Nation". And Tim, just for the audience`s benefit, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sued you for libel, which he lost, you said he wasn`t as rich as he pretended to be and he sued you for that. You got a chance in that litigation to see tax returns but those were many years ago and what Cy Vance has tonight are the last eight years of tax returns.\par \par \uc2 TIM O`BRIEN, SENIOR COLUMNIST, BLOOMBERG OPINION: Right what I saw Lawrence, was the early 2000s to the mid 2000s and he has 2011 to 2019. I think it`s unfortunate actually that he doesn`t have the earlier years because there is a lot of important information in the 2000s. But this is better than nothing.\par \par \uc2 You and I have been talking about this topic now I think for five years. So you and I will sleep better tonight. And you`re right. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won`t sleep well tonight because he`s in the crosshairs.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, Tim this is the reason I always thought he was never going to run for president because obviously his tax returns would get exposed. I didn`t think they would get exposed through subpoenas from criminal prosecutors looking -- trying to find Trump business crimes.\par \par \uc2 The reporting indicates that because it`s the Trump company, that includes his children, it includes Allen Weisselberg who you`ve educated us about who is basically the chief accountant of Trump world, and Allen Weisselberg is someone who apparently knows everything.\par \par \uc2 And if he is in legal trouble, can he offer what he knows about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as his way of surviving the legal trouble he might be in?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, of course he can. And I think it`s likely that he probably will. I think the thing to really focus in on here is that it`s not just the tax records that Cy Vance has now. He probably has reams and reams of the accountant`s work product.\par \par \uc2 This is a criminal case. They`re going to need to prove criminal intent on the part of Trump, his three eldest children, Allen Weisselberg and anyone else in the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  who has fallen under the parameters of this investigation.\par \par \uc2 And if there are emails and notes and other records of communication about they intended to do when they inflated the value of buildings so they could get loans again them and then turns around and deflated the value of the buildings so they can pay lower taxes on them. And there is communication around that that predates any of these tax entries, that is gold for a prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 And that is the kind of stuff they can educate their grand jury with. And it`s the kind of stuff that substantiates the case well before they file something against the president or the former president.\par \par \uc2 And I think Trump`s been well aware of that. That is why he`s been resisting this for so long with this garbage excuses of being under an audit or being victimized by all this. He is very afraid of what`s in these documents, I think.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And if it`s email, we know the email is not from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because he doesn`t do email. But it could very likely be an Allen Weisselberg email to these corporate accountants saying this is the way we are going to do it.\par \par \uc2 And what Allen Weisselberg could be proposing is not exactly legal. And as the prosecutors zoom in on him, he would then be faced with the question of do I tell them that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told me to do this? And it`s also possible \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  he didn`t tell them to do it. And that may be --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- what is discovered.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: There is reference -- there could be references in the email for what he as directed to do by Trump, even if Trump`s not the authors of the email -- author of the email.\par \par \uc2 You know, and I think -- I think a lot of this will turn on how aggressive Cy Vance chooses to be. I think it`s also worth remembering that he had the Trumps in front of them for a fraud case in the past and he let them go essentially with a slap on the wrist in that one.\par \par \uc2 There is no indication given the kind of fire power he has assembled now that he is going to back off. But one of the issues in this is he is going to be leaving office this fall. There`s a new -- there`s going to be a new Manhattan D.A. in September. There`s a primary, a Democratic primary in June.\par \par \uc2 Will he will try to move forward to get some of this nailed down prior to that? I don`t know how quickly he can move. But it really will depend on how steely and aggressive he wants to be.\par \par \uc2 It`s a state investigation. There are no federal parameters around this to protect Trump anymore and he is really in deep on this one, I think.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And in the middle of it is the Stormy Daniels case which was successfully prosecuted by federal prosecutors against \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , the identical case can be made against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the state level.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to be following this one. Tim O`Brien, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Of course.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH BRIAN WILLIAMS" starts now.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WILLIAMS, MSNBC HOST: Well, good evening once again.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020210226eh2p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Andrew Cuomo Denies Sexual Harassment Allegations; Black Women Activists Aim to Make Red States Competitive; Interview with Senator Mike Braun (R-IN) about Meeting with President Biden; MEET THE PRESS DAILY for February 25, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Mike Memoli, Andrea Mitchell, Leigh Ann Caldwel, Chris  Jansing \par \uc2 8,287 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST (voice over): It`s Thursday. The Biden administration is planning to declassify a damning intelligence report which will likely put the U.S. and Saudi relations on a pretty tough place. How will that play itself out? So far, President Biden hasn`t yet connected with the king.\par \par \uc2 Plus, will a minimum wage increase make it into the final COVID Relief Bill? The House is set to vote tomorrow on a bill that may not be allowable under Senate rules. Democrats are at odds over how hard to fight.\par \par \uc2 And new allegations, new denials, and new problems for New York Governor Andrew Cuomo as a former aide goes public with allegations of sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Welcome to Thursday. It is MEET THE PRESS DAILY and I`m Chuck Todd. U.S. officials tell NBC News that the Biden administration will release an intelligence report that will conclude what a lot of people already knew, that the Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, known colloquially as MBS approved the 2018 murder of journalist Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 Moments ago, White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki affirmed to reporters that they remain committed to that report`s public release. She also said the administration is considering a range of options to hold the Saudis accountable for this action. This report`s public released by President Biden will mark a clear break from President Trump`s handling of U.S.-Saudi relations. He downplayed Khashoggi killing because of the kingdom`s lucrative financial ties to the United States, not to mention his personal business ties with the Saudis.\par \par \uc2 NBC News was among the news organizations that confirm the intelligence assessment back in 2018 following the murder. This public release, which will name and shame the crown prince will be a notable development, especially considering Biden ran for president vowing a hard line and a bit more of a confrontational stance with the Saudis, to hold them accountable for the Khashoggi murder.\par \par \uc2 In fact, take a look at this exchange that then-candidate Biden had with my colleague Andrew Mitchell during the presidential primaries.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Mr. Vice President, the CIA has concluded that the leader of Saudi Arabia directed the murder of U.S.-based journalist Jamal Khashoggi. The State Department also says the Saudi government is responsible for executing nonviolent offenders and for torture. President Trump has not punished senior Saudi leaders, would you?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes. I said at the time, Khashoggi was, in fact, murdered and dismembered. And I believe in the order of the Crown Prince. And I would make it very clear, we were not going to, in fact, sell more weapons to them. We were going to, in fact, make them pay the price and make them in fact the pariah that they are.\par \par \uc2 And I would also, as pointed out, I would end and the subsidies that we have and the sale of material to the Saudis where they`re going in and murdering children, and they`re murdering innocent people. And so they have to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, will the release of this intelligence report be the opening move of this administration to make the Saudis an international pariah or not?\par \par \uc2 Mike Momoli is standing by outside the White House for us. Also with us is our Chief Foreign Affairs Correspondent and the Chief Washington Correspondent for us as well, Andrew Mitchell, and Robin Wright, a contributing writer for The New Yorker and the author of several books on the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Mike, let me start with you. Take us a little bit through the delay here. It`s almost as if the Saudis don`t want to have this phone call happen, which one might understand.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chuck, as the White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki just indicated in her briefing, this isn`t really a delay in the call. She`s sort of downplaying the scheduling aspects of this as a little bit of miscommunication about when -- and misreporting, frankly, about when this was supposed to take place. But I think it is clear here, that this Biden administration is taking a very different approach to Saudi Arabia than their predecessor.\par \par \uc2 Now, listen, Joe Biden, as a former chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee, as the former Vice President, perhaps knows as well as anyone the nuances, the challenges, the pros and cons of working with Saudi Arabia as a key partner in regional stability there. But I think what we`re seeing is in some ways, a reaction to the real red carpet that was rolled out by the Trump administration to Saudi Arabia, a real change in the relationship under their administration, very much encouraged by Jared Kushner at the time.\par \par \uc2 We saw something very unusual in the Trump administration, which was first I was in the Oval Office for this meeting that President Trump held in March within months of taking office not with the King of Saudi Arabia but with at the time the Deputy Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman. He later did something, again, very unusual which was made his first destination overseas as president to Saudi Arabia here.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of this is about Mohammed bin Salman directly. There`s great discomfort. There was great hope placed within the larger foreign policy community about him as a potential moderating influence in the region -- in the regime there. But what we`ve seen, obviously, with Khashoggi`s murder as the clearest example is very troubling signs here.\par \par \uc2 And so, what the Biden administration is prepared to do with the release of this report is something it`s required legally to do as Senator Chris Murphy, one of the leaders on the hill of this just told Andrea in the last hour. This is something that the Trump administration did not comply with.\par \par \uc2 And so, what they`re doing in terms of releasing this report is not necessarily an indication of the broader concern here. But you also have to remember, Chuck, there are many members of this administration and the national security team specifically who were also veterans of the Obama administration, who saw another one of the pitfalls of the relationship with Saudi Arabia, which was challenging what was the signature foreign policy initiative, which was the Iran Nuclear Deal as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mike Memoli getting us started there. Mike, thanks. And in fact, I want to sort of emphasize a point, Andrea, that Mike made about basically, you know, Donald Trump was not subtle about his reasoning why he was not going to publicly condemn Saudi Arabia. Here`s what he told me two years ago when I asked him about Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you`re going to look at Saudi Arabia, look at Iran, look at other countries, I won`t mention names, and take a look at what`s happening. And then you -- outside of the Middle East, and you take a look at what`s happening with countries, OK. And I only say they spend 400 to 450 billion over a period of time or money, all jobs buying --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s the price. As long as they keep buying, they`ll overlook some of this behavior.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: But I`m not like a fool that says we don`t want to do business with them. And by the way, if they don`t do business with us, you know what they do? They`ll buy do business with the Russians and with the Chinese. They will buy -- we make the best equipment in the world, but they will buy great equipment from Russia and from China. Chuck --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Take their money. Take their money, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andrea, he made it crystal clear he was all about a transactional relationship with the Saudis. And this wasn`t a case where he was being subtle. He was -- he was oddly comfortable publicly defending them.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: You know, overnight, I reread that MEET THE PRESS transcript, Chuck, and it was so clear that it was transactional. And dare we say also transactional in terms of personal business. Going to Riyadh as their first summit was such an extraordinary change from what is normally done. It`s either Canada or Mexico. You know, you don`t go to Riyadh.\par \par \uc2 And now, most recently, not only Jared Kushner, but we learned from the Washington Post that Secretary Mnuchin on January 6th that week was in the region, was in Saudi Arabia, came back after the riot. But now has just, according to The Washington Post, announced a private investment firm with the Saudis and other Persian Gulf states, making the revolving door even more rapid. It`s, you know, another whole issue.\par \par \uc2 Look, this -- the question now is whether Joe Biden is going to do what he said to me in that November 19 debate and try to make Saudi Arabia a pariah or do something which Chris Murphy and others would recommend, you know, punish them, sanctions against members of the hit squad, others that we know of, not MBS so that we can still have relations.\par \par \uc2 He is the defense minister as well as the Crown Prince. He has recently talked to him, you know, General Austin, Lloyd Austin, our defense secretary. But he`s not going to be in the Oval Office. He`s never going to get a phone call from the president. It`s going to be the president to King Salman, ailing as he is.\par \par \uc2 And it`s going to be -- you know, I believe it`ll be tomorrow now because of the appendectomy overnight by the Crown Prince. But they are going to try to moderate it, but I think that this temporary freeze on the weapons to Yemen is most likely to become permanent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Robin, when you hear -- when you hear that Joe Biden said we`re going to call them out for the pariah they are, you think we`re going to hear President Biden use the word pariah?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN WRIGHT, CONTRIBUTING WRITER, THE NEW YORKER: I would doubt it. And I suspect a phone call with King Salman will be very congenial at the beginning saying how important Saudi Arabia is to American interest long term that the United States is committed to defend the kingdom.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that this is a turning point in the relationship that dates back 75 years to the moment Franklin Roosevelt met Ibn Saud, the founder of modern Saudi Arabia, on a ship in the Suez Canal. And that began something that became -- the Saudi Arabia became a pillar of U.S. foreign policy, not just in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 This is a humiliating moment for the royal family. Remember, the young Crown Prince, aggressive and ambitious as he is, wants to be not just the leader of Saudi Arabia, but the new nasir in the Middle East. He thinks that he can provide the kind of regional leadership, define what the region does next.\par \par \uc2 And this is a way of signaling not just to him and to the king, but to the whole royal family that the United States is not rolling out the red carpet, is not -- is not going to engage with him as has happened in the past. This is a really tough flow.\par \par \uc2 I think the question, of course, is, does the U.S. do something symbolic like wrap them over the knuckles, release the report, or does it sanction, for example, seize properties of the Crown Prince and his family, something more than just a travel ban that says you`re not welcome to Washington again?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andrea, what do you think -- how do you think Israel, and Jordan, and the UAE is going to respond to this? I say this, because, you know, the Saudis, you know, they`ve been -- they`ve been -- look, they`ve forged relationships with a lot of our key allies, their key allies on security for the United States. I mean, how is our other allies in the region going to handle this versus their loyalty to the United States?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, you know, it`s also complicated with the rest of the Gulf states with the UAE and, you know, with the Emiratis at a key moment also as the U.S. is trying to find some running room to negotiate again with Iran, with European allies, and with Russia, and China, the other members of the original Iran Deal, but to try to move beyond that Iran, get back into compliance, and then move on to missiles and Iranian aggression in the region.\par \par \uc2 So, they don`t want to lean too much, you know, against the Saudis. At the same time, they`re trying to, without getting too much criticism politically here at home for leaning forward and trying to reengage Iran. It`s very complicated. They`re going to try to walk on, you know, this balancing line.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why you`re not going to hear words like pariah. And I don`t think you`re going to see the kind of tough sanctions. I don`t know this yet but they want to say something strong about the security and safety of dissidents around the world, not being attacked, obviously, and dismembered the way Jamal was.\par \par \uc2 You know, Jamal Khashoggi was -- it was just a hideous, grisly murder. Maybe not what they intended, who knows? But that certainly needs to be punished. But I don`t -- I don`t think you`re going to see the kind of tough sanctions that would endanger a relationship that is so, so critical.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Robin, what do you expect -- how do you expect MBS to behave going forward? Does he become defiant, angry, or does he try to fight? Does he try to play the long game?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: I think he`s going to play the long game. Remember, he`s only in his mid-30s. And he expects to be around as the leader of the kingdom for the next 30, 40, 50 years. Saudi kings have a history of longevity. President Trump was worried about Saudi Arabia turning to Russia and China. And the reality is that those military systems are not compatible with what the Saudis have bought from the United States.\par \par \uc2 They may buy some material, but like many in the Gulf, there`s a Western bias in terms of they think that the equipment is superior. And they really turn more to the west in terms of where they think their security is long term.\par \par \uc2 So, I think he`ll lie low for a while. Remember, we`re all in the middle of a pandemic. He has not been very visible. Saudi Arabia was supposed to -- and they did, they host the G-20. And that was to be the moment last November that he made his debut and some even predicted a transition of power at the time, that would have to then be recognized by the world.\par \par \uc2 And none of that happened because of the pandemic. So, there`s not much reason for him to be visible.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Robin, any chance the king names a new Crown Prince?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: No, I don`t think so. I think the real -- the reality is that he is aged. He`s in his mid-80s. He is ailing in multiple ways. And he basically created a whole new royal family out of the 6,000 princes born by the founder of the modern kingdom. And he didn`t go to the next brother or the eldest son among those brothers. He went instead to his fourth son.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think he`s committed there. I doubt we`ll see a change. But the question is, will his powers be in some way diminished? He`s consolidated all five channels to power in the -- in the kingdom. And I doubt he`s going to give those up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Andrea, you know, it`s interesting here with President Biden, here`s a guy who as foreign relations -- Chair of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, former Vice President, had so many good personal relationships with so many of those leaders over there, but there is some discomfort in sort of our professional alliances all over the Middle East. Is there a leader that you feel as if President Biden is going to be the one that he`s like that he can have the candid -- I don`t think it`s Netanyahu, is it the King of Jordan?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Probably yes, who has been a reliable ally, the closest ally. In fact, Netanyahu and Biden clash memorably when Biden was Vice President on his first trip to Israel. And Bibi chose that moment to expand settlements, and got a tongue lashing from Biden, and that was, you know, really a raw moment. So, I don`t think it`ll be Netanyahu.\par \par \uc2 And certainly, we`ve got, you know, 30 days or fewer before the next election, the fourth election for Netanyahu, who`s got his own share of problems.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Mike, Andrea, Robin, thank you for getting us started. I think the bigger observation we all know is we don`t need Saudi Arabia`s energy the way we used to, which means the relationship and the terms of this relationship is going to continue to change. Anyway, tremendous to get your guy`s knowledge on here to get us started. Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 Up next, will minimum wage measures end up in the final COVID Relief Bill? We are still awaiting that decision from the Senate Parliamentarian.\par \par \uc2 And later, how Stacey Abrams is serving as a model for black women working to change the political landscape all over the South. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): We will -- we will pass -- we will pass a minimum wage bill. We must pass a minimum wage bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It is another busy day on Capitol Hill. But it`s also a busy waiting game, if you will. We`re expecting a decision from the Senate Parliamentarian any moment now. And I think House Democrats would like to know this answer before they vote. Because we`re all trying to figure out if this COVID Relief Bill can keep the provision that would also boost the minimum wage and allow it to be passed through this arcane process called the Budget Reconciliation Process.\par \par \uc2 The House is likely to vote on their version of the bill tomorrow. And as you heard a moment ago, Speaker Nancy Pelosi reiterated during her weekly press conference that the House`s version of the bill will include increasing the minimum wage regardless of Senate rules.\par \par \uc2 Also on the Hill today, we learn more about the security situation or lack thereof on January 6th. The Acting Chief of the \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  told lawmakers there were about 10,000 demonstrators on Capitol grounds and that 800 of them managed to break into the Capitol, far beyond what intelligence had prepared capital law enforcement to take on.\par \par \uc2 And the Senate, by the way, just confirmed Jennifer Granholm as President Joe Biden Secretary of Energy, making her the 10th Cabinet Secretary confirmed for the administration so far. And OMB nominee Neera Tanden`s confirmation is looking increasingly unlikely, but the White House continues to stand by her. And Ron Klain pledged last night on MSNBC, there will be a job for Neera Tanden if it is not at OMB.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann Caldwell joins me now from Capitol Hill for all of the updates. Let`s start with the any moment now Senate parliamentarian news. Any hint as to why this has taken a little extra time. Have there been more campaigning, more lobbying on each side of the -- of this debate?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chuck, our Capitol Hill team is joking that perhaps they`re -- she`s just waiting for all the senators to get out of town in about an hour to make her decision so that she`s not flooded with questions and complaints after her decision. So, we don`t really know.\par \par \uc2 Speaker Pelosi said at her news conference earlier today that the decision will come any minute. I texted one of my sources and said, is that accurate? And they said wishful thinking. So, no one really knows the timing except for the Parliamentarian here. But everyone is on pins and needles because it is going to be a big moment on how Democrats move forward and, you know, we`ve talked about this before.\par \par \uc2 But if the Parliamentarian just says that it cannot be included in the COVID Relief Bill -- and I just want to remind viewers that she`s not taking into consideration politics at all. She`s only looking at Senate rules and procedure. That is what she is basing her decision on.\par \par \uc2 But if she says that it can`t be included, then that perhaps is an easier lift for Democrats to get this COVID bill done. But we`re checking our phones every minute.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leigh Ann, you just -- you just got it sort of the conundrum here. If you`re in the leadership of the Democratic Party and your job is to get this bill passed, and you`re in the Biden White House, and your job is to get this bill passed, your view in the minimum wage thing as a well, it would be nice, but if we have to take it out fine, let`s do that.\par \par \uc2 Do you think there -- do you think there are those -- I mean, is that the divide, it`s less about the policy itself and more about is this worth the fight over COVID relief right now?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes. Well, that`s what Schumer`s message has been all week to his members. He`s been reminding them, do not sink this entire $1.9 trillion bill because of one or maybe two provisions that you don`t like. And so to me, that was a message to people like Senator Manchin and others who are threatening to do just that.\par \par \uc2 So, Schumer is trying to keep his members on board regardless of what comes down with the minimum wage ruling. It just seems that this is the most contentious part of it. But minimum wage has also been a Democratic priority for years. Pelosi today said that it is long overdue. It`s been 15 years since it was last increased.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that Democrats are having to figure out how big of a priority and how important this is, or they might have to figure out anyway.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And it pulls to the roof. I mean, this is -- which is why there`s a part of me that wonders, if you`re Biden, you know, there`s a bipartisan deal to be cut here if you made it stand alone. But it might not be the 15, which would disappoint some progressives.\par \par \uc2 I want to move to another topic, and that is the after-action report on January 6th, this 9/11 style commission. I`m curious, is there a dispute between some Democrats at what they want? Nancy Pelosi`s first obviously, offer had a lopsided, you know, more deals than ours. McConnell complained about that.\par \par \uc2 But Jim Clyburn seems to have hinted that he would like to see something that`s more 50-50. Are all the Democratic leaders on the same page here?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: So, it`s a good question. And Senator Coons is not a member of leadership, but is one of the more moderate members also said earlier today on TV that he would like to see a 50-50 split. So, Pelosi is today trying to spin it in the sense by saying we didn`t say it seven Democrats and four Republicans. We`re saying that Democrats get to a point seven seats, Republicans get to a point for seats, hinting that perhaps Biden will appoint or Democrats will appoint independents or maybe even Republicans to the board.\par \par \uc2 But she`s saying that that is really the easy part. And she`s saying that that`s something that can be easily negotiable. Now, what she`s starting to focus on is the scope. And that`s what we heard from Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell on the Senate floor yesterday when he suggested that perhaps if violent extremism is part of this commission, then perhaps violent extremism, which what he is calling is what happened to the social justice rallies and protests and, you know, looting in some times in the summer should also be included.\par \par \uc2 And so you know -- but what -- so the scope is what Pelosi is now trying to focus on, not the makeup of the commission. But there is one thing too. When Pelosi equates what Senator McConnell said yesterday on the floor to Senator Ron Johnson, who is basically denying January 6th, she is worried - - it seems that she`s worried that Republicans are going to appoint January 6th deniers to the commission.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, I get what she`s trying to do there, almost make that a non-starter. Leigh Ann, I can`t wait for you to one day write in your memoir, we`re all on pins and needles, the Parliamentarian speaks, the Parliamentarian speak, so run to the camera. It`s not often that we`re waiting for that. But trust me, we all would like to know. So, if it happens this hour, the camera is yours when you need it. So, thank you, Leigh Ann.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. All right, we got a little bit more coming up here, one more important item to note, in fact, on Capitol Hill today.\par \par \uc2 Today earlier, I believe, the House is expected to pass a landmark LGBTQ rights bill this afternoon. If passed by the Senate, the Equality Act would provide sweeping protections for the community, prohibiting sexual orientation or gender identity-based discrimination in employment, housing and education among other provisions.\par \par \uc2 The House previously passed a version of this bill in 2019. But it died in the Republican-controlled Senate.\par \par \uc2 And one last note here on the latest development in the legal battle over Trump`s taxes. New York City prosecutors now have the former President`s tax returns and corresponding financial reports. They`re in the hands officially of investigators. NBC News confirms that Manhattan D.A. Cyrus Vance received the documents earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the Supreme Court refused to block a New York grand jury from getting that paperwork. But it is not clear if these returns will ever be made public.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, New York Governor Andrew Cuomo pushes back against harassment allegations from a former aide. The latest reporting out of Albany, plus what this latest crisis means for Cuomo and his party. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Moments ago White House press secretary Jen Psaki said that New York Governor Andrew Cuomo`s accuser deserves to be heard. This comes as a former adviser is coming forward with allegations of what she calls pervasive harassment by the governor, which included kissing her.\par \par \uc2 In an essay posted online yesterday, Lindsey Boylan wrote, "I tried to excuse his behavior, I told myself it`s only words, but that changed after a one-on-one briefing with the governor to update him on economic and infrastructure projects. We were in his New York City office on Third Avenue. As I got up to leave and walked toward an open door he stepped in front of me and kissed me on the lips. I was in shock but I kept walking."\par \par \uc2 Boylan, who`s now running for Manhattan Borough president, tweeted in December that the governor sexually harassed her but did not get into specifics at that time. Governor Cuomo`s press secretary released a statement denying Boylan`s claims of inappropriate behavior. This came on the same day that House Republicans sent a letter to New York congresswoman and House Oversight Committee chair Carolyn Maloney, urging her to subpoena Cuomo to testify about COVID deaths in nursing homes. And we learned earlier this month that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating that matter.\par \par \uc2 So I`m joined now by Brendan Lyons. He`s the managing editor for the "Albany Times Union," overseeing the Capitol bureau and its investigations.\par \par \uc2 Brendan, thank you for doing this. So let`s -- just tell me the atmosphere right now in Albany politically when it comes to Cuomo. Is he a man on an island? Is he -- how under siege is he right now politically?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN LYONS, ALBANY TIMES UNION CAPITOL BUREAU MANAGING EDITOR: Well, Chuck, I don`t think it gets any worse for a sitting governor at this time for someone who had been really on top of his game last year. He had a national audience for his daily briefings. Many people believe that New York had really set the path for many states in how to deal with COVID and had been doing what people thought were so many things were right about it.\par \par \uc2 The nursing homes and the states, like many other states, the policy to -- the directive to have nursing homes accept COVID-positive patients has become an issue that will not go away. Initially they dismissed this as a political witch hunt by the Trump administration and the Justice Department`s Civil Division.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LYONS: But recently that became an \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation along with the Eastern District of New York. So it`s very unclear that they are the same investigations. And in fact, the Eastern District investigation may be based on additional or other information. And then in the backdrop of all this, you`ve got now allegations of the governor`s and his people, intimidating lawmakers, bullying lawmakers, and as you just noted, Lindsey Boylan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LYONS: A former staffer with economic -- you know, she comes forward in December, it sort of died after her tweets about that. But she wrote this very detailed essay yesterday and it has now brought even Democrats saying this cannot be ignored. The question is, who`s going to investigate it?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and I guess -- is there a place that would go, is that something that the legislature would decide to do? Is there -- you know, obviously if this weren`t in Congress it might go to the Ethics Committee. You might have something like that. What would -- what would an investigation look like in Albany of this?\par \par \uc2 LYONS: Well, quite frankly it would look like a mess because the \uc2 Joint Commission on Public Ethics\uc2  in New York state is -- many people believe that it`s controlled by the governor`s appointees. It has not done --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are these the folks he fired? Did he fire this group unceremoniously about a year ago?\par \par \uc2 LYONS: No, that was the Moreland Commission that had been set up to investigate corruption. And that commission set the framework for what Preet Bharara did in bringing down some of New York`s top political figures. But the JCO is the \uc2 Joint Commission on Public Ethics\uc2 . It`s not the panel that anybody would have faith and are many lawmakers would not have any faith in to get to the bottom of this. It doesn`t also handle sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 The Governor`s Office of Employee Relations does some of the sexual harassment investigations for the state. It couldn`t go there because it would be -- you know, the political connections are too strong. The legislature also would not be a good place. So the next question is, would the state attorney general or possibly the local district attorney in Albany pursue an investigation of this?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. So politically this is a man who doesn`t have a lot of friends inside the Democratic Party, let alone the Republican Party right now. Where does he go from here? I mean, is he -- the Cuomo I know I think tries to keep going and will cling to this and will keep running and probably not quit, or not -- or keep trying to run for reelection. But what`s the rest of the party going to do?\par \par \uc2 LYONS: It`s a good question. Yesterday we saw a lot of key Democrats, including the state Senate majority leader issue statements condemning any form of sexual harassment.\par \par \uc2 So they`re not circling the wagons right now for the governor. Earlier today his former chief of staff, his former secretary, Steve Cohen, who is an attorney and also had been a member of the U.S. Attorney`s Office in the Southern District held an extraordinary conference call with reporters to essentially argue for the governor, to act as a character reference for him, and say that through the years he had never seen this sort of behavior.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LYONS: But this is troubling for the governor. You know, I think, you know, on one hand you`ve got to deal with an \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation. The administration has been subpoenaed. They`re fighting on that front. You have the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LYONS: The Republicans who are pushing for subpoenas of the governor and more of an investigation of the nursing homes and how that was handled. So he`s taking it on all fronts right now and notably this week he has not held a briefing in a few days, I`ll tell you that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Brendan, the three highest profile New York politicians after Cuomo, right, are the two senators and AOC. Kirsten Gillibrand was the first senator to call for Al Franken`s resignation in the wake of those allegations against him. I can`t imagine Schumer or AOC coming to Cuomo`s rescue here. I mean, he does appear to be on a New York Democratic island here.\par \par \uc2 LYONS: He is. I think AOC has not supported him on this issue of the sexual harassment. Kristin Gillibrand has made some comments on it. Senator Schumer recently when he appeared in Albany for an unrelated press conference at a restaurant, the reporters had gathered and hoped to ask him about some of these things including, notably the \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation.\par \par \uc2 And the senator, who normally does not run from the press, quite literally did. He escaped out a back door and into a waiting car and got out of there. So he is not --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I think you`ll see a little bit more of that these days, Brendan, for the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 Brendan Lyons, appreciate it, "Albany Times Union," you guys have a pretty big story and a long year to cover it. Thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 LYONS: You bet. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still ahead, an effort to change the face of politics in the south. We take you live to Birmingham, Alabama, as part of NBC`s special "STATE OF THE STRUGGLE" series.\par \par \uc2 Plus the White House works to make good on a promise of a new era of political unity. We`ll talk to a Republican senator who met with the president yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we continue to honor and celebrate Black History Month all this week MSNBC is taking an in-depth look at where the country stands in the struggle towards anti-racism. For many activists fighting against inequality means we`re going to get more candidates of color in positions of power at all levels of government. Following the Democratic success in Georgia this year led by activists like Stacey Abrams a new political strategy is emerging in parts of the south driven by black women.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Chris Jansing is in Birmingham, Alabama. She`s speaking with black women trying to change the political status quo.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, Chris, the upset victory for Doug Jones back then, he always credited mostly to black women organizing and supporting him. So fitting that you`re in Birmingham.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, and they know that they have a very high hill to climb, not just from Doug Jones, but a Mississippi seat for governor that they thought they might be able to win that they didn`t win in `19.\par \par \uc2 Look, they are using Stephanie Abrams as a blueprint, including putting together a coalition of voting rights advocates, community organizers and being very targeted about the voters they`re going after, not just black women but young blacks and also poor rural blacks who felt disenfranchised and they`re going to them where they live, Chuck. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING (voice-over): In a state largely run by Republican white men, black women are shaking things up. Women like Arekia, Kimberly and Nsombi.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY BIGGS, FROM PRISON TO PURPOSE: And we just keep running and we keep going and we keep pushing and we keep building. We keep encouraging, we keep inspiring.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Each runs an organization aimed at growing voting power to build a progressive Mississippi, an overwhelmingly red state.\par \par \uc2 AREKIA BENNETT, MISSISSIPPI VOTES: Nationally there`s a misconception about like what`s possible in Mississippi. We will find resources to make sure that your voice is heard on election day, not even just stopping there, but to make sure that people`s actual material conditions, their needs of their lives are met afterwards.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So after a storm knocked out power and water in Jackson, Rukia Lumumba is delivering food, building relationships first and then registering new voters.\par \par \uc2 RUKIA LUMUMBA, PEOPLE`S ADVOCACY INSTITUTE: And not only are they coming out but we`re starting to see change. We get closer and closer to winning statewide office every single year.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All across the south black women are leading.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): Why does it always seem to take black women to get this stuff done?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: I mean, you`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS, VOTING RIGHTS ACTIVIST: We`re going to show America who we are.\par \par \uc2 JANSING (voice-over): Many of them young and inspired by Stacey Abrams` successful decade-long fight to flip Georgia. Alabama`s Cara McClure founded Black Lives Matter in Birmingham.\par \par \uc2 CARA MCCLURE, BLACK LIVES MATTER BIRMINGHAM FAITH AND WORKS: Until Alabama is the Alabama that we want, we`re going to make communities, small communities across the state the communities we want.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There are recent successes, including the Confederate symbol removed from the Mississippi state flag. And while there is still a statue honoring Confederate women outside the Mississippi Capitol these women are undaunted.\par \par \uc2 NSOMBI LAMBRIGHT, ONE VOICE: We have hundreds of Stacey Abrams in Mississippi working to change Mississippi. That`s an example all over this world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So they`re determined but of course there are things that Stacey Abrams have that they don`t have which includes she`s a fund raising juggernaut. Also Georgia had a big influx, as you know, Chuck, of more progressive voters, something they`re not seeing here in Alabama and in Mississippi but they will also say to you we`re going to be strategic about the races that we`re looking at starting local.\par \par \uc2 And if people had listened, if Stacey Abrams had listened 10, 15 years ago to folks who were saying this cannot be done, imagine where Georgia would be and, in fact, where Joe Biden would be right now, they say just watch us. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Chris Jansing, I think the numbers are definitely there in Mississippi. Alabama might be a little bit of a tougher climb, but when you look at the numbers in Mississippi, organization could go a long way there.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Chris Jansing, thank you for that great report.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All week on the platforms of NBC News we`ve taken a deep dive into the "STATE OF THE STRUGGLE" following this summer`s reckoning over racial justice. So head on over to NBCNews.com to see more of our in-depth coverage.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a critical area where Republicans and Democrats agreed. It was one of the best meetings I think we`ve had so far and we`ve only been here about five weeks. It`s like the old days, people actually are on the same page.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back, that was President Joe Biden yesterday after a meeting with bipartisan group of lawmakers about supply chain issues. The meeting included a number of Republicans, several -- a couple even who questioned the election results, some who had planned to object to the electoral certification. And at least one member of Congress who did. John Joyce from Pennsylvania who did object to certification, and yet the White House invited them and they were all there.\par \par \uc2 Now one of those senators on the Republican side of the aisle who was there was Indiana`s Mike Braun.\par \par \uc2 Senator Braun, you heard the president there. Do you concur with his remarks that it was one of the best bipartisan meetings he`s had so far?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE BRAUN (R-IN): Well, he hasn`t been here a long, but the meeting, my first time over there with him. I thought it was excellent because we`re talking about something like semiconductors, where that`s increasingly being built outside of the U.S., and in a state like Indiana where the auto industry is a big deal, they are running out of them. So talking about how you get the supply to this in place and kind of move it around to make sure we don`t shut down critical industries like the auto industry, appliances, and many others.\par \par \uc2 So I thought it was fruitful, I thought it was everybody engaged, everybody had a chance to speak their own two cents.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: And I like that, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, this is an issue where there is some broad support, but it`s easier said than done. The rhetoric is all there. Hey, we`ve got to make this stuff here. What`s realistic, though, about OK, semiconductor plant, go?\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: That`s the disconnect, Chuck. The business I ran for 37 years, logistics and distribution, pointed out yesterday, when you`ve got a supply chain glitch, whether it`s at the manufacturing end or the distribution end, if you`ve got a demand more than 5 percent than what you`re currently used to, that causes kinks in the system. Ten percent shuts some of it down. With what we`ve had with COVID and so forth, a lot of that you can`t do overnight.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I think the realism that`s set in, some of this is going to have to be pre-emptive and we may not get there on time on semiconductors. But we`ve got pharmaceuticals, we`ve got all kinds of other things that we use like rare earth minerals. That`s mostly housed in China so yes, that`s a bipartisan issue. We ought to all roll up our sleeves and you normally need time to fix the problem, you really do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let me pivot to the minimum wage and let me present an argument an advocate for the minimum wage would say. OK, and taking the CBO analysis into effect, it may increase the deficit. Well, a lot of these businesses just got a massive tax cut over the last couple of years. That also increased the deficit. And $15 an hour is OK, it`s barely a living wage. We`re not talking about people getting amazingly rich here on minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 So what do you view is the downside to a $15 minimum wage? Like I said, an advocate would point out these deficit issues, hey, tax cuts went to these business owners.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: Yes, I can`t disagree with any of that, really. I`ll qualify it a bit in this sense. I just got out of the budget meeting and it was a robust conversation. Probably went two hours. And my point was big difference between main street, places like Indiana, in terms of our cost of living, and Wall Street with the businesses that, you know, are touting $15 and $16 an hour wages. Many places in my hometown which is main street pay that or above.\par \par \uc2 The business I`ve got always prided in raising wages and raised some eyebrows when I said that`s a responsibility of business owners if you want to keep good employees.\par \par \uc2 Chuck, it`s one-size-fits-all. If you have the minimum wage to where you differentiated it by cost of living, and whether you differentiate it maybe by the type of wage, training wage, full time, part time, then you`d be maybe hitting the mark to where you aren`t going to lose any jobs but if you do it one-size-fits-all, all the jobs will be lost on main street. Small business.\par \par \uc2 Costco\uc2  was interviewed today. Love what they`re doing with their benefits and their $16 an hour wage. If you kind of make that relative to their capability, it should maybe be a lot higher. And many small businesses, the employees are like family, they pay their employees before they take money out of their business. You throw a one-size-fits-all, you`re going to eliminate some businesses and jobs.\par \par \uc2 I like it to be more thought out, not rushed. It`s a big deal. I believe in raising wages because there`s inequality in this country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: Mostly between large corporations and people at the lower end of the food chain that are coming into the labor market.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me ask this then. Is there a better way to incentivize wage increases? Because let`s be frank, I don`t think the tax cuts have had the impact on wages that many people advocated that they would. Is there another way we should do this outside of this mandate?\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: Chuck, again, in my own company, kids and a good young executive team run it now, we`ve raised wages for truck drivers, 20 percent to 30 percent in the market, pre-COVID in Indiana, and the lowest unemployment county in the state, my home county, we bid up wages and the market is really going to be what dictates it, and then you`re going to see where there`s, I think more obligation to do what`s in the spirit of what \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  is talking about where the larger companies kind of brag about it, got a lot more capability.\par \par \uc2 Those tax cuts mostly went to big C corporations that were paying an effective tax rate lower than the new nominal rate at 21 percent. Main street, you know, we got a little bit of a tax break, that`s where you reinvest, you build those jobs, and two sides to that story, too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, Senator Braun, you sound like you`re not a big fan of that tax cut bill. I don`t -- I knew you didn`t vote on it. That happened after you`ve got elected in `18. It sounds like you weren`t a fan of that bill, I don`t have much time, but were you?\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: I wasn`t a fan for C-corps being the only ones in mind, went from 35 to 21. Proprietorships, partnerships, LLCs, sub-SS. That`s the way most small businesses roll. They got the break that they needed from 40 to 30 roughly and that part was good.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: That should have been the priority. Just a few business owners that were in the Senate had to make sure that happened.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Got you.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: Otherwise it would have been the big guys again that got all the benefits.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator Mike Braun, it was a short visit, but I appreciate you coming on. Republican from Indiana. Thank you for coming on and sharing your views with us, sir. Always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY." And NBC`s coverage will continue with Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210226eh2p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for February 25, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Frank Figliuzzi \par \uc2 7,486 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 And I guess your sound, your audio is working okay tonight. Yeah, you can really hear me.\par \par \uc2 Imagine -- Rachel, imagine yourself in a House hearing with the likes of Jim Jordan. That`s where \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2  found himself today and he just kind of couldn`t take it anymore, and he really let loose, and he`s going to join us later tonight, because it`s going to be part of his decompression to come on this show.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST, "TRMS": That moment was so incredible. And it was so clear that he was just like, his exasperation was just like exploded in a burst of ad-lib expression. I absolutely want to know what he was thinking. And now that he`s had time to put it all together instead of just exploding in that moment, I`m looking forward to hearing you talking to him.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And here`s the worst part of it for \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2  -- it`s not the last time he`s going to be in a hearing with Jim Jordan. This is going to be a recurring problem in his life.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: He`ll be good at it. He`ll be good at it forever. Well done.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: He will.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We have breaking news tonight about the minimum wage increase in the Biden COVID relief bill. It`s all up to the Senate parliamentarian. That is what White House Chief of Staff Ron Klain said earlier tonight on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 And that was not necessarily the way this was going to go, but once again tonight what that means is an important change in American law that will improve the lives of tens of millions of people is all up to someone whose name has never been on a ballot and who has a job that most people have never heard of, Senate parliamentarian. Elizabeth MacDonough did not ask for the power she has over your life. Elizabeth MacDonough does not want the power to decide whether you get a raise if you`re a minimum wageworker.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  gave that to her in effect in a complex set of rules that apply to budget reconciliation rules in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . The parliamentarian in the House of Representatives does not have the kind of super powers the Senate parliamentarian has because the House has it`s own less complicated set of rules for budget reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 Today, in a private meeting, in the Senate parliamentarian`s office, Elizabeth MacDonough listened to the most authoritative debate anyone will ever hear on whether a minimum wage increase can be included in a budget reconciliation bill. And, of course, we were not allowed to hear that debate because it was behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 The most authoritative voice in that debate was Bill Dauster, who is the new budget committee -- new Budget Committee Chairman Bernie Sanders hired as the chief counsel of the Budget Committee. It`s a job that Bill Dauster has held before.\par \par \uc2 He was chief counsel to the Budget Committee 30 years ago when he literally wrote the book on the budget process. He was also chief counsel to the Senate Finance Committee. Bill Dauster served as director of the White House Economic Council in the Clinton administration and he served as counsel to Senate Majority Leader Harry Reid.\par \par \uc2 Bill Dauster made the argument to the parliamentarian today that an increase in the minimum wage does fit within the budget reconciliation rules because it does have a significant effect on the federal budget. The nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  issued a report the increase in the minimum wage would have a $54 billion net effect over ten years on the federal budget. The Republican Senate staff argued that is a relatively small amount of money over a ten-year period and that the budget effect is all the result of indirect economic effects of raising the minimum wage to $15.\par \par \uc2 And therefore it would be a violation of the Senate`s so-called Byrd Rule to include a minimum wage increase in the Biden COVID relief bill, which will be passed as a budget reconciliation bill. Elizabeth MacDonough is expected to reveal her parliamentary ruling on this piece of legislation before the bill is brought to the Senate floor.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth MacDonough is a fully respected Senate parliamentarian. She was hired -- appointed to that position by Harry Reid. The Senate majority leader can fire her at any time. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  kept her in that position. Chuck Schumer is keeping her in that position. She has the full respect of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And we now know that it really is all up to Elizabeth MacDonough because of breaking news delivered on MSNBC tonight by \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  in her important interview with White House Chief of Staff Ron Klain.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, as you know we`re all waiting on bated breath for a ruling from the Senate parliamentarian at least when I walked over had not yet come down. Her ruling is going to have a big impact on the future of that provision. The president believes we should have a $15 minimum wage. If it is ruled in order, then obviously that would allow it to move in this package. If it`s ruled out of order, we`re going to have to find other ways to get it done.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID\uc2 , MSNBC HOST, "THE REIDOUT": If in theory the parliamentarian were to rule against the administration`s plan for $15 an hour, can`t the vice president override the parliamentarian? Hasn`t that happened in the past?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Not sure if it`s ever happened in the past. Certainly, that`s not something we would do. We`re going to honor the rules of the Senate and work within that system to get this bill passed.\par \par \uc2 This is a $1.9 trillion package that is vital to getting this country in a position to crush the virus, in a position to get people vaccinated, in a position to get the schools open and to help all those people that you talked about at the start of this show. We are going to get this package passed. That`s our highest priority.\par \par \uc2 We want the minimum wage as part of it. That`s what the president proposed, but we`re going to work with the Senate to get this package passed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  is right. The vice president as the presiding officer of the Senate could overrule the Senate parliamentarian, but Ron Klain said we`re going to honor the rules of the senate.\par \par \uc2 So Senate Democrats are going to abide by the parliamentarian`s ruling and not have Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in her role as president of the Senate overrule the parliamentarian, which the vice president as \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  said is entitled to do but almost never does.\par \par \uc2 Chris Hayes asked a crucial follow-up question to Chairman Bernie Sanders tonight. What happens if the minimum wage increase is not included in the Senate budget reconciliation bill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST, "ALL IN": I do wonder if it wasn`t in this package, like, do you think it could win a floor vote on it? If you just said up or down --\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You couldn`t, okay.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: No, no. No, you could not -- as of now, there`s not one Republican who will support a $15 an hour minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You can`t --\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Yeah, I`m sorry, I didn`t mean to --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What you`re saying just to be clear here if you were in a filibuster world and you needed ten Republican votes, you`re saying there`s not ten Republican votes to raise the wage?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Let me be very clear about this. The only way that we are going to raise the minimum wage is through reconciliation or ending the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There is one other way. The way the minimum wage was raised last time in 2007. In 2007, the minimum wage in the country was $5.15, and Republicans opposed an increase from $5.15 to $7.25 phased in over two years. Republicans said that would be an impossibly high minimum wage, and so Democrats put the minimum wage increase in a defense spending bill, and that bill passed the Senate with 80 votes.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  of California. He`s a member of the Congressional Progressive Caucus. Also with us, Adam Jentleson, former deputy chief of staff to Majority Leader Harry Reid. And he is the author of the important new book "Kill Switch: The Rise of the Modern Senate and the Crippling of American Democracy."\par \par \uc2 If you read one book about the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , make it Adam`s.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Khanna, you are once again in the position that members of the House of Representatives often find themselves. On the edge of your seat waiting for a ruling from the Senate parliamentarian to decide what is ultimately possible in legislating this budget reconciliation package.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): Lawrence, you started out perfectly. There is no provision in the Constitution that says that the Senate parliamentarian has any power. And Ron Klain is a brilliant constitutional lawyer. He knows that the vice president has this decision.\par \par \uc2 And in 1975, Vice President Rockefeller disregarded the advice of the Senate parliamentarian when they actually lower the threshold on the filibuster from 67 votes to 60 votes. And Robert Dove, a Senate parliamentarian who I know you respected, you served in the Senate, had said many times that people disregarded the parliamentarian`s ruling.\par \par \uc2 So we should just be honest. If there are political reasons that the White House doesn`t want to disregard the parliamentarian for whatever reason, then just come out and say that. But no, this is the decision the White House has. The vice president has every power to disregard that decision.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I just want to stipulate for this conversation that Elizabeth MacDonough is one of the great Senate parliamentarians.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Sure.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And she didn`t ask to be put in this position.\par \par \uc2 Adam Jentleson, how did it come to this in the Senate, that these are the kinds of hurdles that the Senate has but the House doesn`t?\par \par \uc2 ADAM JENTLESON, FORMER DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF TO SEN. HARRY REID: Well, reconciliation is a function of the Budget Act that was passed in the 1970s and updated in 1985, with the Byrd Rule, which is really the issue here. The Byrd Rule is what makes the reconciliation process restrictive and hard to use to pass things like the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 So it`s that series of first along the `70s and the update in the `80s that has brought us to where we are today. But really the main factor here is the filibuster. Because if the Senate had -- it was still the majority rule institution it was for the first 200 years of its existence. You wouldn`t be trying to force big policies like the minimum wage through reconciliation. You`d simply be bringing them to the floor and having a simple up or down vote and letting it pass or fail on a majority vote basis.\par \par \uc2 Reconciliation is an attempt to get around the filibuster and what it has become today. It`s evolution from sort of the talking filibuster of the Jimmy Stewart goes to Washington days to the 60-vote threshold that makes it nearly impossible to pass everything. So, that really is sort of the proximate cause and the need to use reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Congressman Khanna, there is a fascinating thing about Republicans and the minimum wage. They always support the minimum wage increase that you did last time over their opposition. They all now support $7.25 as the minimum wage. And they always condemn any number that you pick as the increase in the minimum wage as something that`s going to be hugely destructive and harmful. And yet they always find themselves some years down the road defending that number that they used to oppose.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: You`re absolutely right, Lawrence. So they had the same two tired arguments each time. They say, well, this is going to hurt rural communities, small town communities. And here`s what I never understand about their argument.\par \par \uc2 Are they saying they want \uc2 Amazon\uc2  or Target to pay $15 to people in my district, but people in rural America or small town communities that are working for the same big corporation should get less? That makes no sense to me. Why should you have companies for the same work pay people in communities the same amount what they`re actually adding in value?\par \par \uc2 The second thing they say is, well, this is going to hurt small business in these communities. Well, there`s an economic paper actually that says in communities that have the most economic concentration, which is often small communities, increasing the minimum wage actually increases jobs because people have monopoly power there and they`re underpaying workers and increasing the wage will add jobs.\par \par \uc2 So it`s not based on any economics, and that`s why ultimately they end up supporting it after the fact.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Adam, I want to not exactly defend the Byrd rule so much as explain why it seemed like a reasonable idea at the time when Robert Byrd brought it into Senate budget process. First of all, Republicans, parties were not obstructionist. They were not trying to block every single thing that the other party was doing.\par \par \uc2 And it did seem as though the budget process needed its own rules as it did, for example, the international trade legislation. It made a certain sense at the time, I was working for the Senate when Robert Byrd was on the floor himself personally enforcing it against both parties when they tried to put things in bills he didn`t think should be there.\par \par \uc2 But now in today`s Senate, it carries a completely different effect than what was intended for that completely different Senate than it was written for.\par \par \uc2 JENTLESON: Yeah, that`s right. It was written in a Senate where it didn`t take 60 votes to pass everything. Most legislation passed on a majority vote in the `70s and `80s when these rules were developed. It`s just really important to underscore that for your viewers, that we`ve come to accept the Senate as a place that it takes 60 votes to pass things, but that is a relatively recent development.\par \par \uc2 So, in the Senate where most things pass or fail on a majority vote, the Byrd Rule and the process for reconciliation made sense as a special process for budget and budget related issues. But in a Senate where it takes 60 votes to pass everything today, and it`s nearly impossible to get 60 votes even for something as broadly popular as President Biden`s COVID relief plan pulling in the 70 percent, 80 percent range, it doesn`t make sense to have to force reasonable policies like raising the minimum wage to $15 through this process called reconciliation that it was never designed for.\par \par \uc2 The easiest way to do this if this gets struck out of this package is to bring it to the floor, bring other popular policies to the floor. And if they`re blocked by Republicans which they probably will be, Senate Democrats in the White House should get serious about reforming the filibuster so we can get things done.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: My personal TV moment of the night was Bernie Sanders literally laughing out loud of the idea of Republican votes for an increase in the minimum wage, something that is supported by 57 percent of the public. But, of course, is just a laughable thing to Senate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , Adam Jentleson, thank you both for starting us off tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, what happens when the Trump-appointed Postmaster General \uc2 Louis DeJoy\uc2  comes to testify to the House of Representatives with lying Republicans on the committee, Democrats get gloriously angry at the lies flying around the room. And in the process you are left with absolutely no doubt about how Congressman \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2  feels about democracy deniers like Jim Jordan. The honorable \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tempers flared in a House hearing with the Trump-appointed Postmaster General \uc2 Louis DeJoy\uc2  today after Republican Congressman Jody Heist of Georgia defended the postal service`s quote, almost perfect delivery of ballots in the previous election. Those were his words.\par \par \uc2 Democrat Steven Lynch made his Boston constituents proud with this mild- mannered retort. And, of course, I mean mild-mannered by Boston standards.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. STEPHEN LYNCH\uc2  (D-MA): Let me quote the gentleman from Georgia -- the almost perfect delivery of ballots in the previous election. Given that fact would you say it was unconscionable that someone would -- would dispute and vote to undo the results of that almost perfect delivery of ballots in the previous election?\par \par \uc2 Let me just recount the facts. The gentleman from Georgia voted to undo the elections in two separate states. And so he has just spent about five minutes reminding us in his own words that the delivery of ballots was almost perfect by the \uc2 United States Postal Service\uc2  in that election that he voted to undo.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: After an insurrection inciter and democracy opponent, Jim Jordan, tried to blame a lack of confidence in the Postal Service on Democrats? Our next guest, Congressman \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. GERRY CONNOLLY\uc2  (D-VA): All the gaslighting that we just heard does not change facts. It was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the Republican nominee, who was planting the idea aided and abetted by disruptive changes proposed by a new postmaster general and a compliant board of governors that actually eroded the public confidence in the ability to vote by mail.\par \par \uc2 That wasn`t a Democratic narrative. That was a Republican narrative by the president of the United States and enablers. And oh, by the way, inconvenient fact, Mr. Heist would have you believe it was partisans on this committee and we quoted a number of Democrats -- by the way, admitted Democrats.\par \par \uc2 For the record I`m an admitted Democrat and damn proud of it. I didn`t vote to overturn an election, and I will not be lectured by people who did about partisanship.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2  of Virginia. He`s a member of the House Oversight Committee.\par \par \uc2 And, Congressman Connolly, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 You are now a Virginian --\par \par \uc2 CONNOLLY: My pleasure, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I must for the record claim you for Boston because you are from Boston. And you have a lot of fans in Boston who are very proud of you today.\par \par \uc2 I just have to ask you, I mean, Rachel and I were talking about at the beginning of the hour -- what is it like? What is it like to be sitting in that hearing room with the likes of Jim Jordan and have to deal with that stuff?\par \par \uc2 CONNOLLY: I don`t want to disparage a colleague, but I do think there is a toxicity in relationships that did not exist before. When you look at somebody who joined in the purpose of the mob on January 6th, and that purpose was to stop, to prevent the validation of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Kamala Harris`s election by the Electoral College, normally a ceremonial event.\par \par \uc2 After all of the violence, after five deaths, they -- we reconvened and 144 of them voted to overturn the elections in two states, duly valid, audited, judicially reviewed election results.\par \par \uc2 And that means you may come across with the crowd that was violent, that was -- that engaged in premeditated, you know, insurgency and that led to five deaths, which could have been us. So, I think you look at people differently after January 6th than you did before.\par \par \uc2 They`re not just politically adversaries. They`re people who align themselves consciously with a violent mob that meant to do harm to you, your staff, the vice president of the United States, among others.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I have to say when you put it that way, which is the way we see it, which is -- which is the reasonable frame on what you experienced and what you experience on a daily basis, it is astonishing. It is just astonishing how you and Congressman Lynch and others are able to maintain the civility in that room the way you do.\par \par \uc2 I just -- I just don`t know how you do it. It`s literally something we kind of don`t know how you can do it.\par \par \uc2 CONNOLLY: Well, I believe in the power of truth and facts. And I believe calling out their fabrications, their alternate reality that is delusional and deliberately designed to persuade people to believe what they say, not what they see and know -- which is called gaslighting -- I think -- you know, the power of words and the truth still remains. And I believe in it, and I think it`s my duty and Steve Lynch and others to propound that truth, to make sure the public hears it and sees it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Can you explain to our audience why Donald Trump`s selection of postmaster general still has that job and what will happen to change that?\par \par \uc2 CONNOLLY: So the postmaster general is technically hired -- not technically. He is hired and reports to the Postal Board of Governors. The Postal Board of Governors last summer was entirely appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And the White House clearly put pressure on them to consider a candidate who had not been on the original list of candidates for postmaster general. And that was a big Republican donor and Trump supporter named \uc2 Louis DeJoy\uc2 , and he had no qualifications for the job other than he had political connections through fund-raising.\par \par \uc2 And so they actually installed him as postmaster general. He immediately set out on a series of what he called organizational efficiencies, a euphemism for actual disrupting of the Postal Service. All of which was cheerily designed to disrupt the reliability of the mail service in order to effect voting by mail or the November 3rd election. And although they didn`t really succeed in that because of brave post workers and/or federal court rulings that called them out -- and I read one of the opinions in the record this morning at the hearing -- actually fingering them for political motivation, that they were doing this with malice and forethought. This wasn`t just organizational efficiency.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, they succeeded I think mentally in planting a lot of doubts in the public`s mind about whether my ballot would be secure, whether it would arrive in time on mail day.\par \par \uc2 So in a sense, they -- they won the war while losing the battles. And that was a tragedy for America because although record number of Americans voted, I believe there are probably tens of millions who have voted had they had confidence in the mail.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman \uc2 Gerry Connolly\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CONNOLLY: My pleasure, Lawrence. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, it is called the Biden relief package because it is legislation that originated in the White House but is now under the control of Congress. White House veteran Jennifer Palmieri will join us on how difficult it is to legislate from the White House while trying to get cabinet members confirmed and managing foreign policy and everything else that a president and vice president have to do. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What we need to do is pass the American Rescue Plan so we can get those $1,400 checks to folks so we can save our small businesses, so that we can pass the child tax credit, so that families can lift half of American children who are living in poverty out of poverty.\par \par \uc2 Let`s extend the unemployment benefits. Let`s do all of these things, partnering together so that we can get through this moment of crisis because I`m telling you I have faith. I believe in our ability to get through this and to be better on the other side if we all work together to lift folks up and to lift ourselves up when we have the opportunity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: While Congressional Democrats are trying to pass legislation to improve the lives of Americans suffering through the health and economic consequences of the pandemic, Republicans are trying to pass hundreds of laws in state legislatures that will restrict the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what White House chief of staff Ron Klain told \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  about that tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFFF: We know that there are people in our country who are trying to suppress the right to vote. We know that we need to fight that at the federal level, at the state and local level. And so obviously standing up for democracy is what the Biden campaign was about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now Jennifer Palmieri, who served as White House communications director for President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . She is co-host of Show Time`s "The Circus" and hosts the podcast "Just Something about Her" from The Recount.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer, I juxtaposed those two pieces of video, the vice president and the White House chief of staff to show the range of urgent concerns in the White House while this White House is trying to pass a giant piece of legislation through the Congress. And how do you do that from the White House when you know in the end it`s all up to members of the Senate, member of the House?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER PALMIERI, FORMER OBAMA COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: You know, I talked to Kate Bedingfield today. I talked to her for "The Circus" and, you know, she said there`s sort of a calm at the White House, I think it`s because there`s such a clarity of purpose between having to get their nominations through, having to pass the COVID relief bill, getting vaccinations going and then like still working on an infrastructure package for Congress later down the road. And it`s sort of focused them -- I think they`re focused and they`re calm.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, they didn`t get a honeymoon, right? Congress spent the first three weeks sort of focused on the insurrection and impeachment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And so I think the White House looked at that and said ok, we`re going to go outside of Washington, do a lot of local news interviews.\par \par \uc2 I know \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2  at the White House worked really hard to get business, mayors, Republican mayors, can governors behind the COVID relief package, right? Go to the audience that you can reach.\par \par \uc2 And it has been very successful in getting the public behind the package. And I know when they`re looking for bipartisanship they`re looking for it out in the country.\par \par \uc2 You know, the Republicans on Capitol Hill are some of the few Republicans in the country that actually oppose the COVID relief bill because so many Republicans out in America support it. Seven out of ten Americans, even a majority of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So I think they`ve done a really good job in selling the package as you need it in terms of its scope, how big it has to be. And, you know, it`d be great if Republicans voted for it. But I think if you`re sitting in the White House what you`re really worried about is I want to get the bill passed and are the Republicans able to make an effective critique of the bill that might hurt us electorally or hurt our ability to pass something else?\par \par \uc2 And thus far Republicans really have not launched an effective critique against the bill.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes, it really is astonishing what the Biden-Harris communication about this in the campaign and in the White House has done. 76 percent support.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer, I don`t know that I`ve ever seen 76 percent support for major complex legislation like this. It`s really astonishing. 60 percent of Republicans support this legislation.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: And, you know, you saw -- I think part of the Republicans problem is what is an ideologically -- what does an ideologically Republican argument against this bill look like? After the Trump years. After there was so much spending on tax cuts and they didn`t pay for things.\par \par \uc2 And Trump himself promised a big, you know -- Trump himself was for a $2,000 bonus for families that were suffering because of COVID, right. So what -- how you even can launch an ideological argument against this? And then when they do get something together and we saw today the House Republican Caucus did a press conference to show -- the layout arguments against the bill, and there were some like relatively compelling arguments that they offered. But we didn`t hear any of them because all of the coverage was of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and Kevin McCarthy fighting about Trump at the end, right?\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hasn`t said anything publicly in days, but still the House Republicans can`t get out from under his shadow to even make an argument against what Biden has put in the COVID relief bill.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ron Klain made more news tonight with \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  about the Neera Tanden confirmation. He said if Neera Tanden is not confirmed she will not become budget director because there`s no process by which they could make her acting budget director.\par \par \uc2 But he did say we`ll find a place for her to serve somewhere in the administration that doesn`t require Senate confirmation. What was your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: I just wanted to be OMB director. You know, we talk, people -- there`s all the focus on Neera`s tweets which, you know, which we`re all very familiar with Republican hypocrisy and being concerned about some of these mean tweets.\par \par \uc2 But more over let`s talk about a what great budget director Neera would be. If you`re a senior government official either on Capitol Hill or in the administration you want a good budget director. And she would be great at that.\par \par \uc2 If it doesn`t work out I`m glad that she`s going to serve in some way. But I think the Biden team is right to keep fighting for her.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Jennifer Palmieri, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the year of Defendant Trump could produce more than one Trump defendant as investigations continue into the Trump businesses and what might we discover about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in further investigation of the planning of the insurrection at the Capitol?\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Two weeks ago \uc2 Donald Trump, Jr\uc2 . was forced to raise his right hand and promise to do something that he was never taught to do -- tell the truth, the whole truth and nothing but the truth. The District of Columbia attorney general put \uc2 Donald Trump, Jr\uc2 . under oath for a deposition in a civil case about misuse of funds by the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  and the Trump presidential inaugural committee.\par \par \uc2 In a court filing the D.C. attorney general said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Jr.`s testimony at his February 11, 2021 deposition raised further questions that required the pursuit of more evidence in that case.\par \par \uc2 "The Daily Beast reports that investigators with the Manhattan district attorney`s office "have been expanding their criminal probe into Trump`s business empire asking questions and grilling witnesses as recently as in the past few days not only about Trump but particularly about his eldest son, Don, Jr., and Allan Weisselberg, one of the former president`s most trusted officers.\par \par \uc2 This latest round of interest in Trump Jr. And Weisselberg`s activities underscore the resources and the gravity that New York prosecutors are devoting to the investigation."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Frank Figliuzzi, a former assistant director for counterintelligence at the FBI. He is the author of "The \uc2 FBI\uc2  Way: Inside the Bureau`s Code of Excellence. He`s an MSNBC national security analyst.\par \par \uc2 Frank, this first case that I mentioned is a civil matter in Washington, D.C. involving \uc2 Donald Trump, Jr\uc2 . and the use of funds at the inauguration. Is there a way that that could slip across into being a criminal matter?\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Indeed, it could. I believe there is criminal exposure here, and I believe there`s been reporting to that effect, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is a reminder that the former president and his family members can no longer seek shelter behind the Oval Office. And what I think is there`s some beauty here in pursuit of these cases is they`re not directed -- connected or directly related to his powers or decision making as president.\par \par \uc2 That means he can`t attempt to make -- successfully attempt to make claims of presidential privilege. Nor can his family members claim, hey, I did this in my capacity as an advisor or employee of the president. They are exposed here and it bears further watching.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Frank, as you watch the investigations unfold in the Congress and the Senate the other day about what happened on January 6th, what are the questions that they should be asking?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Yes, I was wholly unimpressed with what I heard in the first hearing. And I realize it`s likely just a prelude of what`s to come.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the bottom line. Try as I might, Lawrence, I cannot make sense out of the security failure that occurred on January 6th. And here`s why. Even when I factor in the very real civil liberties concerns and legal constraints on what law enforcement can and can`t do in monitoring social media and collecting intelligence on U.S. persons, I get that, I factor that in.\par \par \uc2 Even when I factor in the possibility of political intervention in the planning and later response to the insurrection, and that needs to be investigated.\par \par \uc2 Even when I do that I still am faced with a conflict here. We have former chiefs of police and sergeants at arms saying that the intelligence available to them did not indicate a threat in their professional minds.\par \par \uc2 Yet we know that the intelligence was crystal clear that people were violently going to target the Capitol, and the target was the center of the -- the Capitol was the center of the action that day.\par \par \uc2 So here`s my conclusion. We seem to collectively have a problem in this country, writ large and in the law enforcement community, with seeing people who look like us as threats even when the threat and intelligence is staring us in the face.\par \par \uc2 And I want to be clear I`m not saying that these officers, former executives in law enforcement are lying. I`m saying that would be something we could deal with. We have laws against lying to Congress.\par \par \uc2 I`m saying it`s more disturbing than that. They`re telling us the truth, that when they look at the available intelligence, intelligence that I saw sitting at home of what was being planned and what was going to be executed, they say that wasn`t a threat to them.\par \par \uc2 And I`m saying collectively the hard question here is why, why not? And when you contrast that on the flip side and say look at the deployment and security presence for Black Lives Matter protests where there was little to no intelligence indicating a threat, you see that disparity.\par \par \uc2 And you`re left with the conclusion I write about in my article released today that we`ve got a problem seeing ourselves as a threat. And we find it much easier to find people who don`t look like us as a threat even when they`re not.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So what`s an example of the way the Congress should focus its questions going forward?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: So we`re going to hear next week, I believe it`s on Tuesday -- we`re going to hear from the federal side. We`re going to hear from active duty \uc2 FBI\uc2  and other federal agencies. So we`re likely to hear very valid lessons that we all need to listen to about what they can and can`t do on social media.\par \par \uc2 You know, this myth that big brother government is listening to everybody and monitoring everything is simply not true. And we don`t want that to be true. But we need to ask those folks why is it that you find these other groups a threat yet the intelligence was so unapparent to you that you just send it casually in an e-mail? We need that answer.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Frank Figliuzzi, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You can read Frank`s new article about the questions Congress should be asking about the attack on the Capitol on MSNBC daily.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a new single dose \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine is getting closer to approval, but we are still facing challenges about how to get the vaccine to people who to get the vaccine to people who need it.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Raul Ruiz, who is a physician who will be making vaccinations himself, will join us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: One year ago today \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tweeted, "The coronavirus is very much under control in the U.S.A." He lied. As of today at least 506,588 Americans have died from the coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 Documents released by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  show that the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine is safe and 86 percent effective against the most severe outcomes of the illness. An \uc2 FDA\uc2  panel will meet on Friday and they are expected to approve the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine for emergency use authorization.\par \par \uc2 Once approved the company said it would be able to provide 20 million shots by the end of next month with an additional 100 million doses over the summer. The Biden administration announced today that it will send more than 25 million cloth masks to community health centers and food banks around the country for low income Americans.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today a plane with 600,000 vaccine doses arrived in Ghana as Ghana became the first of 92 low to middle income countries to receive the vaccine through an initiative known as COVAX. The COVID-19 vaccine`s global access \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  is providing syringes for those vaccine doses.\par \par \uc2 Today \uc2 Henrietta Fore\uc2 , executive director of UNICEF said "Today marks the historic moment for which we have been planning and working so hard."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Congressman Raul Ruiz who represents California`s 36th district. He is a physician and the chair of Congressional Hispanic Caucus. Congressman, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 You plan to be administering vaccines yourself, don`t you?\par \par \uc2 REP. RAUL RUIZ (D-CA): I do, Lawrence. I`ve been administering tests in the hardest to reach communities, the farm workers and the homeless since the summer, really taking a community-based approach, working with free clinics, non-profits and other volunteer health care providers to really go out there and overcome the barriers that are hard to reach and highest risk community members face and getting tests and also vaccines.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What are you finding in terms of those barriers? How much of it is reluctance? How much of it is lack of access?\par \par \uc2 RUIZ: You know, that is a very important question, Lawrence, because a lot of people on the other side of the aisle want the blame the minority populations and say well, they simply have hesitancy, and that`s why we see the disparities.\par \par \uc2 But I can tell you first hand from practicing years in the emergency department and as a public health expert in emergencies that the number one driving factor here is systemic inequalities with access to the resources that these community members need.\par \par \uc2 And as a doctor, I`m very privileged to have a very intimate conversation with patients and my constituents while I`m doing the tests or out there in vaccination clinics and I hear their stories.\par \par \uc2 They don`t have Internet to make the online appointments at the vaccination centers. They have to work at the high-risk essential jobs so they don`t have hours to spend on the phone waiting for somebody to pick up and give them a chance.\par \par \uc2 And they`re very concerned if they get the test, if they test positive, that they won`t be able to go to work.\par \par \uc2 I have a 55-year-old woman who told me that she didn`t want the test because she couldn`t miss working because she would miss out on the $300 a week that she`s relying on to pay the food, to purchase food for her child.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, the Latino population of California is 39 percent and yet they have 55 percent of coronavirus cases; 46 percent of coronavirus deaths. How do you get the vaccine delivery fast enough in this situation?\par \par \uc2 RUIZ: Well, Lawrence, you know, first you need to prioritize equity, which means that the resources should go to those that are at highest risk. And Hispanics are at high risk not only because they usually work the high risk essential jobs, but also there`s disparities in the chronic health illness like diabetes, obesity and asthma, and other cardio-pulmonary illnesses that render them further at risk of dying.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore in underserved areas, they are very at risk of not getting the resources that they need to protect themselves. And many of them who speak primarily Spanish aren`t getting the information in a language that they get.\par \par \uc2 So, first you need to prioritize the risk factors that put Hispanics at high risk of getting sick.\par \par \uc2 Second, you have to allocate the resources, the vaccines, into those communities.\par \par \uc2 But then finally the thing that most people forget is that public health is practiced on the ground by people who administer the vaccine in the arms of other people.\par \par \uc2 And you can send vaccines to a county, but if they`re not sending them or directing them to the right places and working with the right people on the ground to administer them to the hardest hit communities, then you`re setting yourself up for failure.\par \par \uc2 So, what we need is we need local partnerships with non-profits, local leaders, community health workers or in the Hispanic community we call them "promotoras" (ph). Usually people from the community who are paraprofessionals helping the physicians and nurses to educate the community.\par \par \uc2 You need to do a massive public health educational campaign in both English and Spanish, in radio, in mailers. You can knock on doors. And then you have to do these clinics to really get the trust and build public confidence to get them done.\par \par \uc2 Look, we are so fortunate that we have a president (AUDIO GAP) promotes equity, has an equity task force, has $25 billion in the rescue plan for these communities and focusing on sending vaccines into the retail pharmacies and their QHCs (ph).\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman and Dr. Raul Ruiz, thank you very much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH BRIAN WILLIAMS" is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020210225eh2o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Senate Hearing Deepens Questions About Security Failure During Capitol Attack; Trump to Speak at CPAC This Weekend; Postmaster General \uc2 Louis DeJoy\uc2  Testifies in Front of House Panel; MEET THE PRESS DAILY for February 24, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Carol Lee, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Sam Brock, Kavita  Patel; Paul Butler, Geoff Bennett \par \uc2 8,439 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST (voice over): It`s Wednesday. Washington`s political faultlines converges. Democrats debate how hard they want to fight to raise the minimum wage as part of President Biden`s massive COVID rescue bill. That provision faces a key test in the Senate today ahead of a full House vote later this week.\par \par \uc2 Plus, FDA staff finds that Johnson and Johnson`s COVID vaccine is both safe and effective, clearing the way for emergency approval of the nation`s first single-dose vaccine. But amid the pandemic optimism sweeping the nation, public health experts remain anxious. We`ll break down why.\par \par \uc2 And Postmaster General \uc2 Louis DeJoy\uc2  is grilled on Capitol Hill as Democrats pushed President Biden to pave the way for his ouster. And he says, get used to it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Welcome to Wednesday. It is Meet the Press daily. I`m Chuck Todd. With Republicans uniting in opposition to President Biden`s $1.9 trillion COVID relief bill, they`ve also dealt themselves out of the negotiations. Democrats, meanwhile, have no room for error if they want to pass it. But it is Democrats debating with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The House was planning to vote on the bill on Friday, but major questions do remain in the Senate where some rival factions in the Democratic Party are battling over how hard they want to push for an increase in the minimum wage as part of that COVID relief bill. Party leaders say Chuck Schumer is literally begging Democrats to hang together on this one.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN\uc2  (D-IL): And he`s begging all of us despite any differences with any one section of the bill that we hang together. This is the signature provision of the Biden administration in terms of dealing with the pandemic and the economy and we need to stick together. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Senate`s parliamentarian who is the top referee, if you will on the Chamber`s rules, could decide today that it would violate the rules to raise the minimum wage in this legislation which being fast-tracked through a wonky process called budget reconciliation. Now, a Democratic aide tells NBC News that the parliamentarian met with Capitol staff today and did not get any indications of how she`d rule.\par \par \uc2 If Democrats are told they cannot include a minimum wage increase, then progressives like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and Elizabeth Warren are expected to pressure Democrats to try to vote to override that ruling. But moderate Democrats in the party, I think, people like \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , Kyrsten Sinema, they have signaled that they would not support such a move.\par \par \uc2 Now, for his part, President Biden has signaled he`d side with the moderates. He seem already conceded that an increase in the minimum wage is likely will not end up in this final relief bill. He expects it to be done separately. But folks, these issues become tied to a massive multi-trillion dollar cornerstone of Biden`s agenda. It`s going to reveal where the power centers of the Democratic Party really are in these first 100 days.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen both Bernie and Manchin flexing muscle here. We`ll see which side is the Senate. Is it with the progressives or is it with those party moderate voices? And how to party leaders like President Biden, Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer, navigate those divisions in an environment where they can`t afford to lose the support of a single Senate Democrat?\par \par \uc2 A lot is on the line here, not just politically, because the nation continues to reel from the economic devastation of a pandemic that has killed more than half a million Americans and created so much more income inequality. Voters want some relief and want it now.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Carol Lee is outside the White House for us. Leigh Ann Caldwell is on the beat on Capitol Hill. Also with us is Carl Hulse, the Chief Washington Correspondent for The New York Times, and the unofficial dean of the Congressional -- the Congressional Press Corps, some might argue.\par \par \uc2 But Carol Lee, let me start with you and this sort of how the White House is bracing for this parliamentarian rule because it does seem as if President Biden signaled quite a while ago, a couple of weeks ago, that the minimum wage was not a must include in this COVID relief plan, and yet, the factions in the Senate are on pins and needles to find out how the parliamentarian is going to rule.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s right, Chuck. I mean, the president basically signaled that this is not something that he would -- this would live to fight another day, that this is not pertinent to getting this Coronavirus relief package passed. And in some ways, you know, if the parliamentarian were to rule that this can`t be included, it takes some of the burden off of the president in terms of how to navigate this spat within his own party, even if there is that effort to try to override that it`s an easier call for him and for his staff to try to figure out how to knit this together.\par \par \uc2 I mean at this point, they just want to get this done. I think the reality is has set in. It`s set in much earlier than, you know, more than four weeks ago when the President came in for some of the people around him, that this was not going to be something that was going to wind up being bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 But now, you know, they`re just a couple weeks out from this deadline, they`ve set for themselves as March 14 deadline, when some of these benefits begin to expire. That`s when they want to get this done. And they just want it to be completed.\par \par \uc2 And so, the minimum wage, while something that the President still believes in and the White House is still sort of fighting for, is not something that they think is absolutely pertinent in this particular piece of legislation, and that they could do it at a different time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, Carol, I`m curious, is the White House quietly trying to find some Republicans who will support COVID relief? Right now the job of the congressional leadership is to just get their bill through. They`re not looking for extra votes. But my guess is the Biden White House wouldn`t mind the extra votes. How hard are they pushing as Susan Collins or Lisa Murkowski for instance?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Look, there is -- what they say is that the President himself that starts at the top that he sets the tone, and this is something that he very much wants, so they haven`t fully given up hope is one person put it to me. You know, when the boss wants something, you got to make an effort and try for that. But a lot of them just don`t think it`s actually going to happen. At the same time, they`re not giving up until it`s the end.\par \par \uc2 And their argument is -- and this conversation sort of shifted once they started saying that well, we have support outside of Washington. The writing was on the wall there. And so, they`re trying to use outside pressure to pressure inside of Washington. It doesn`t seem to be working. So, they`re not fully giving up hope, at least the President isn`t, but there`s much more of a realistic point of view coming from the White House in terms of whether this will be bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But it is fascinating. In some ways, the Republican unity has dealt themselves out of this conversation almost completely. Carol Lee at the White House getting us started. Carol, thanks. Let me go over to Leigh Ann Caldwell in Congress.\par \par \uc2 So, Leigh Ann, when are we going to hear from the Senate parliamentarian? There was some thought it could happen yesterday. We think it was today. What`s -- what is the -- we`re waiting for white smoke, what`s the -- what`s the metaphor we`re looking for here?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Essentially, we are waiting for white smoke. We do know that that meeting happened and is now over between Republican staffers, Democratic staffers, and the parliamentarian where they made their case.\par \par \uc2 They debated why minimum wage should be included in the COVID relief bill and why it fits in with the Senate rules, as far as the Democrats are concerned. The Republicans, obviously, they argued the opposite, that it should not be included. So, she did not make a ruling at the end of that meeting, which is one option that was possible.\par \par \uc2 So, we do expect perhaps that she could rule at some point later today. But you know, it`s all up to her. She could go back and ask for more information. She could drag this process out because it is complicated. And she might need more information. But it`s going to be a big moment for Democrats because it`s going to set their strategy not only on this COVID bill, but also on minimum wage specifically on if it`s included at this point or if it`s going to have to be pushed down the line, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to play for you something Mitch McConnell said today, and I want you to try to tell us what we`re supposed to glean from that. It`s about McConnell bragging about Republican unity. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I think what you need to focus on is how unified we are today in opposition to what the Biden administration is trying to do. What we have seen here at the beginning of this administration is pretty far left across the board. And the best evidence of it is currently before us, is the way they`ve chosen to take a totally partisan approach to COVID relief.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, Leigh Ann, McConnell was trying to project unity in the face of what is obvious fractures inside the Republican Party. But what struck me about the comment is sort of like, hey, we`re united against this bill, as if that is good news for the party.\par \par \uc2 This bill is quite popular with the public, including about half of Republicans, you know, give or take, depending on which poll you want to pick. But we`re looking at anywhere from 40 to 60 percent of Republicans, which is not an insignificant chunk. What is their strategy here, just do no harm to themselves?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: I think it`s also a distraction away from the former president and to get focused and start to focus on the Biden administration and opposition to the Biden administration in general. Because the more they can unite around policy and things that do unite the party, the less they can bicker over if the former president is the future of the party and the fight between McConnell and the former president.\par \par \uc2 But yes, it`s also with McConnell taking the stance on this popular COVID bill. It`s a direct -- it`s not the populist -- it is a populist bill that helps a lot of people that a lot of Trump supporters would likely support. This is the type of thing that a Donald Trump Republican Party would get behind.\par \par \uc2 But McConnell is trying really hard to keep the party focused and not look at the other bright, shiny objects that are really hampering the party at this moment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, it is fascinating to watch them almost ignore the polling going on there. All right, Leigh Ann Caldwell on Capitol Hill. Leigh Ann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Carl Hulse. So, Carl, I want to start with this, which is this parliamentarian`s decision, I`m curious, is it possible if she makes a decision that minimum wage can be included, does the minimum wage debate overshadow the entire COVID relief bill all of a sudden?\par \par \uc2 CARL HULSE, CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Yes, it might. You know, I always think it`s interesting, Chuck, in this period when the Senate Parliamentarian becomes this big focus of public interest, which is exactly what the Senate Parliamentarian doesn`t want to be. But I do think that that could happen.\par \par \uc2 But I also think that Carol made a good point. She could save the Democrats from themselves here and take it out and make this a separate issue because 50-50 Senate, the saying is every man or every woman is a king or a queen. You know, you have this incredible deuce to be able to derail things. So, you could get into a fight over this that would -- it would be a fight among Democrats, which McConnell is really relishing.\par \par \uc2 I mean, listening to Mitch McConnell there, we`ve heard this before. You remember in 2009, this is his strategy when he is in the minority is to totally unify, because if he gets any slippage -- there`s a famous quote from McConnell, if you -- if you lose one vote, then it`s bipartisan. So, I do think that it has a tendency to overshadow things if the Democrats get into a big fight over this.\par \par \uc2 You know, one thing about the minimum wage, Chuck, it can be a bipartisan issue. It was passed -- the last time it has increased was 2007, and it was the big numbers in both the House and the Senate. You already see some rumblings from Republicans offering alternatives, say, well, let`s go this far, let`s go this far. I think, you know, there`s a possibility if you get into strictly a minimum wage debate, you could find a way. You wouldn`t get the $15.00 but you`d get something.\par \par \uc2 But you know -- and as far as the Republicans on this bill, they`ll find a few things to show, you know, how bad it is. The easy vote is no for them on this. And right now, I don`t see any Republicans getting aboard here. But they -- it`s funny about the Republican, Chuck, they`re blaming Ron Klain.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Carl -- in fact -- yes, why are they blaming Ron Klain? Explain that.\par \par \uc2 HULSE: They think that -- they think Biden actually really wants to do a deal with them, and that his staff is against it. They think if they could just get in a room with Joe Biden, and work out a deal with him, that he`s open to it, but the staff doesn`t want to do it. I -- you know, I think Joe Biden wants to get the biggest number he can. And you know, if they can get it through on reconciliation, that`s going to be a victory for them. And there`s a ton of stuff in this bill that they would be able to claim credit for.\par \par \uc2 The thing the Republicans are saying, it`s basically been a Christmas tree -- I mean, the Democrats, I`m sorry, it`s basically a big Christmas tree that has too much stuff.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. You know, Carl, it`s interesting to me. It feels like this minimum wage debate, if the parliamentarian says it`s in, then it feels like it`s \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  driving the agenda. If it`s not, it feels like \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 . And I`m being a little bit absurd there, right? You know, you`d be trying to go with the extremes.\par \par \uc2 But in many ways, that is what we`re watching here. It`s sort of like, OK, who`s the sender? What would you say to the answer to that question? Who`s the sender?\par \par \uc2 HULSE: Well, I mean, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  -- I was in the Capitol yesterday and he was surrounded by about 15 or 20 reporters who wanted to hear his every utterance and how he`s going to vote on anything, including nomination. So, Manchin has a lot of power here.\par \par \uc2 You know, generally speaking, most of the Senate Democrats are obviously aligned with \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and Chuck Schumer. They want this. But \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  is in the catbird seat. But he`s not the only one. Kyrsten Sinema of Arizona, who is not at all as vocal or visible as \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , she has similar views. I don`t think we`ve really heard from her on this -- on this particular issue. But I mean, Manchin has the cloud under the Senate rules.\par \par \uc2 And you know, you mentioned earlier could he override the parliamentarian, which would be what we would call like a mini nuclear option of getting rid of the filibuster, but you`d have to have Manchin`s vote to do that and he has so far said no way. So, he`s got a lot of power.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It does seem as if -- it does seem as if the strategic answer if your Biden is -- I almost think that`s one of the reasons why he threw minimum wage overboard as quickly as he did. I think he agrees with you, Carl. There actually is a bipartisan way to do this. It may not be 15, but you might get 12 and be able to claim some big bipartisanship and anyway. Carl Hulse, as always, sir --\par \par \uc2 HULSE: Yes, the history is really --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HULSE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, no, no, no, no, I`m always ahead of time here.\par \par \uc2 HULSE: Now, I would say, the history of it is bipartisan. The history is bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. And that is where this wants to go. Anyway, Carl Hulse, before that, Carol and Leigh Ann, thank you all for getting us started.\par \par \uc2 By the way, there was a lot of hearings that`s been going on this week. Senate Intelligence Committee, in fact, today is hearing from President Biden`s nominee to lead the CIA. William Burns, he seems expected to have a fairly easy time and confirmation.\par \par \uc2 But the U.S. is facing multiple intelligence threats from adversaries around the world. Burns` opening testimony made clear that he believes one country, in particular, poses perhaps the biggest challenge for America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BURNS, NOMINEE FOR CIA DIRECTOR: As President Biden has underscored, outcompeting China will be key to our national security in the decades ahead. There are, however, a growing number of areas in which Xi`s China is a formidable authoritarian adversary, methodically strengthening its capabilities to steal intellectual property, repress its own people, bully its neighbors expand its global reach, and build influence in American society.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still ahead, the intel factor. New insight on the January 6th riots casts new blame on the feds and accusations of missed intelligence. Plus, balancing new rays of optimism and the COVID fight with continued uncertainty and some public anxiety. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Today, the COVID News is mostly moving in a positive direction as the country appears to be finally rounding that corner that the former president talked about endlessly last year.\par \par \uc2 This morning, the FDA released documents showing Johnson and Johnson`s vaccine candidate is safe and effective, particularly against severe illness. And it paves the way for authorization ahead of a key meeting on Friday. This is a single-shot vaccine and it could be a major boost to our efforts to fight this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The company says it can have three to four million vaccines sent out next week, as many as 20 million vaccines distributed by the end of March. So, not a huge number, but those 20 million doses alone would inoculate an additional 20 million more Americans. It would double the number of Americans who are currently fully vaccinated right now, actually.\par \par \uc2 So, Moderna and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  are also ramping up surprise -- supplies. They are promising to double and possibly triple their deliveries over the next few weeks. That would be something else. New Coronavirus cases are down more than 60 percent from a month ago and hospitalizations are down more than 50 percent. Yet, we did have over 2,000 people die yesterday from it.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to Sam Brock. He is in Miami Beach where local officials are trying to prevent a Spring Break surge. And Sam, I think this is going to be probably the challenge of 2021. It feels better, everything looks encouraging, and there`ll be this moment where we hit a tipping point and everybody says hey, time for Spring Break. So, how`s Miami Beach handling it?\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Plus, it`s Florida, Chuck. So, in addition to the fact that we`ve seen all these markers milestones of improvements, you get people coming down here. I know you have a unique perspective on Florida given your roots, but it`s not just one or two weeks. We`re talking about a six or seven-week-long Spring Break period.\par \par \uc2 What Miami Beach is trying to tell all those folks who are coming from New York, $50.00 flights from New York to Fort Lauderdale, college students in the region maybe want to come here to pack the beaches is this is not business as usual.\par \par \uc2 You will see over my shoulder at some point police on ATVs that are patrolling not just the streets, but the sands as well. There are police officers at every access point seeing what people are taking onto the beach.\par \par \uc2 Chuck, there`s no alcohol, no coolers, no tents, all sorts of things you would expect to find on the beach. We talked to Mayor Dan Gelber here about his messaging right now at this crucial point in time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAN GELBER, MAYOR OF MIAMI BEACH, FLORIDA: This is not a place to come to let loose. This is a place to govern yourself. This is not a place where anything goes. We have loads of cops out and they`ve been advised to arrest people for all violations of our ordinances.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BROCK: And Chuck, I can`t overstate the economic implications as well. The Greater Miami Convention and Tourism Bureau crunched the numbers for us. In 2019 $5 billion of economic activity in February, March, and April. Those are the three months of high season. It`s not just Spring Breakers but also families, vacationers, people coming down here.\par \par \uc2 But they are trying to weigh these competing interests of getting financial lifeblood injected back into businesses but also keeping folks down here safe. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Sam, I get it. And also, they need those local tax revenues. Every rental car you have or hotel, those little surcharges are how it funds government in Florida both state and local. So, they need those revenues too. Anyway, Sam Brock for us in Miami Beach. Sam, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 And I think joining me now, I hope, is \uc2 Dr. Kavita Patel\uc2 , who of course a practicing primary care physician, former White House Director of Health Policy in the Obama administration. She`s also an MSNBC Medical Contributor.\par \par \uc2 So, Dr. Patel, it`s fascinating to watch the last 72 hours of what I would call sort of opinion or analysis writing on all things COVID. It`s like we`re having this raging debate that says, hey, we`re not being optimistic enough. We`re being too pessimistic about these new optimistic numbers. So, what say you Dr. Patel? Are we rounding this corner, is it looking good, or should we be a little more cautious a la the public health officials these days?\par \par \uc2 KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Chuck, good to be with you. Well, you know, I wouldn`t be a good doctor if I didn`t say we should be a little cautious. That`s kind of how we`re trained. But I will tell you, to your point and to Sam`s reporting, we should be optimistic, reinforced by the way today, Chuck, with that incredible data from \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Buried in the dozens of pages is something that leaked out to me a 74 percent reduction in asymptomatic infections or reduction of risk of an asymptomatic infection after 28 days from the shot. And what that translates to, Chuck, is more normalcy.\par \par \uc2 That means that if you get this vaccine, we now have data, that after about 28 days from your shot, one shot, that you have a much lower risk of giving the Coronavirus infection to anybody else. It`s the -- it`s the data we`re waiting for across the board. So, we are rounding a corner. We just have to have a little discipline to truly get around the corner to home base.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, it feels as if then we`ll now we are about to get to the point where we can now implement a testing and tracing program. What would you like to see implemented now if we`re going to -- we`re finally going to get to a point where we may be able to do this? We never got it right the first time. How can we get it right now so that we can essentially not get caught again by a variant?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, look, we saw the Biden administration just announced they`re going to put another $200 million into genetic surveillance, and hopefully increase the amount of tests that we run the genetic sequences on to 20 -- 10 to 20,000 a week. That`s still an undercount. So, we need to do that, Chuck, and we need to look at the populations that aren`t going to be immunized, especially children.\par \par \uc2 So, we need tests that are available, combined with the tracing that you talked about, combined -- Chuck, I`ll one-off you. We need better guidance in schools on ventilation and what they need to do to keep these mitigation kind of procedures in place to decrease children getting infected. Because even though it`s not a large population, we just don`t know where this will go, especially if they`re not vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 It helps to have herd immunity, but we will likely still see some infections, and we just have to be real about that. So, testing that`s more accessible, that`s affordable, it can`t be this clunky you order it and it takes $120.00, and maybe your insurance will pay for it, maybe they won`t. And then the tracing, which gets back to what you covered earlier, a stimulus package for discretionary funds that puts money into public health infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 The theme of the year is going to be public health infrastructure, even once we have this behind us, because it`s not going to be the last we have of something like COVID.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you feel like the odds are of one of these variance posing a real spike? Or do you think as long as this vaccine schedule continues as it is, the good news out of \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2 , and we are -- we are -- that we can manage the variance?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, I do think we`re going to see an increase. I don`t want Americans to think that we will not have an increase. It just won`t be a spike like we`ve seen in the past surge, certainly not. Hopefully, Chuck, it`ll be much less more likely the cases we saw in the beginning when we weren`t even really truly diagnosing these cases.\par \par \uc2 But you know, if we can get down to under 10,000 cases a day, I`ll call that a victory even if we have an increase from a baseline that could be driven by the variance. What the variance worried me more about, Chuck, is just how do we tweak these vaccines and what schedule will we need to ask Americans to be on?\par \par \uc2 Look at what it`s taken to get hundreds of millions of Americans vaccinated. Think about trying to do that every year. We`ll just need a lot better infrastructure to roll this out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, we do try it every year, and we get a flu vaccine that maybe 40 to 60 percent of the people take, right?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And so, sadly the track record is there. That isn`t the greatest anyway. Dr. Patel, as always, I appreciate your expertise. Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And make sure to check out our interactive state by state guide to figure out when and where you can receive your COVID vaccine. Find out different states, different counties have different procedures. So, please visit planyourvaccine.com to learn more. And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The Senate`s questioning of Capitol and D.C. Metro security officials has filled in some blanks about how events unfolded on January 6th. But yesterday`s hearing only deepened questions about the largest security failures and certainly how the other parts of the federal government worked.\par \par \uc2 Though officials offered different and at times conflicting explanations about those failures, they were clear that they felt the intelligence community should shoulder much of the blame.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JACKY ROSEN (D-NV): Do you think this was an intelligence breakdown or a resource issue?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT CONTEE, ACTING CHIEF, D.C. METROPOLITAN POLICE: I think that the intelligence is not -- did not make it where it needed to be.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN SUND, FORMER CAPITOL POLICE CHIEF: I think we need to look at the whole entire intelligence community and the view they have on some of the domestic extremist and the effect that they have. I look at this as an intelligence problem that impacted this event. Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We do expect Pentagon and \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials to respond to these accusations when they testify in front of this committee next week. These hearings are only the beginning of Congress` investigations in the events of January 6th. Nancy Pelosi wants to form a 9/11 style commission which is already facing pushback from Republican leadership who want the commission to be 50/50 between the two parties.\par \par \uc2 And senators will be hearing about the new security measures enacted in the wake of the attack at a briefing later this afternoon. By the way, Senators Susan Collins and Bob Menendez called for a 9/11 style commission for January 6th in a different way. So -- and that one could end up being the one people jump onto.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Paul Butler. He`s of course a professor at Georgetown Law. He`s an MSNBC legal analyst and a former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 So, Paul, let`s start what did you learn yesterday and what questions it now -- that it now presents to you? Because it feels like yesterday we got testimony from the on-the-ground commanders. But we have not heard from their bosses. So what questions do you have left?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So the Capital Police chief said that the insurrection was planned and coordinated. It was never just a protest but an orchestrated violent attack on the Capitol of the United States. And he brought receipts. The evidence of advance planning is the equipment the rioters brought. They brought climbing gear. They used hand signals to communicate with each other. They planted two explosive devices near the Capitol. They coordinated their use of weapons like chemical sprays.\par \par \uc2 And, Chuck, this matters because it`s proof that it`s not just people getting caught up in the moment. The insurrection happened because of a criminal conspiracy by Trump supporters, white supremacists and domestic terrorists. So we need to know not just who carried out the attack which is what the criminal investigation is now focused on, we also need to know who planned this orchestrated attack and who financed it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Paul, I kept hearing the words intelligence failure yesterday. And I kept thinking that was a cop out. And I`ll put it this way. Was it really an intelligence failure? The intelligence was there or was it a failure to believe well, they won`t do that? What did you hear yesterday?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: That`s a great question. So the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had intelligence that extremists were coming to D.C. to commit violence and wage war. The Capitol police chief says he didn`t see that. Neither did the House or Senate sergeant-at-arms. The D.C. police chief says he got an e-mail at 7:00 p.m. that night. Well, that is an extraordinary breakdown in the chain of communications. You don`t send the D.C. police chief an e-mail at 7:00 saying that violent insurrectionists are on their way to attack the government.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: And just hoping he sees it in his pile of e-mail. So you`re right, Chuck, there`s a lot of finger-pointing and blaming other departments back and forth. But one thing that the police chief says is that the intelligence community is not taking seriously the threats posed by white supremacist organizations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has been making this point for a while. But these police chiefs believe that the clear and present danger posed by domestic terrorism, from white supremacists, is still discounted. And that may help explain the intelligence and communication failures that allowed this insurrection to happen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a great word to use. That it was discounted. The intelligence was discounted but those interpreting it. That is a better way of framing it that I was looking for. That it was discounted. As Mitt Romney pointed out, Ann Romney was worried about Mitt Romney traveling to Washington. She didn`t need that e-mail from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you this as a lawyer and as somebody who`s practiced plenty of civil litigation. How much stronger did wrongful death lawsuits get after yesterday`s testimony?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: Well, the fact that there was this incredible breakdown intelligence, you`re right, Chuck, they knew. They just didn`t act like they knew. So this makes the government a lot more complicit isn`t the right word but negligence is a word that trial lawyers will use in these wrongful death cases that are almost certain to come.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Paul Butler, Georgetown University, always a great expert to have on the air and of course a contributor to us here at MSNBC as well. Thanks for coming on and sharing your expertise, sir.\par \par \uc2 Up next, curious Georgia and the future of the GOP. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Do you believe President Trump should be speaking -- or former president Trump should be speaking at CPAC this weekend?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Yes, he should.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Congresswoman Cheney?\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): That`s up to CPAC. I`ve been clear on my views about President Trump and the extent to which, following January 6th, I don`t believe that he should be playing a role in the future of the party or the country.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: On that high note, thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. House Republican leaders clearly divided over Donald Trump`s scheduled address at CPAC this weekend. But Republicans` consternation about Trump may play out as they try to win back control of Congress. In Georgia, former senator David Perdue announced he will not run against Raphael Warnock next year. Perdue`s decision came after meeting with Trump at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 And according to the "New York Times," the former president was more focused on exacting revenge against Republicans he claims betrayed him than on helping Republicans win elections. Trying to navigate a feud between the former president and his state`s sitting governor for the next two years was deeply unappealing to Mr. Perdue.\par \par \uc2 Perdue`s decision may also have to do with the dramatic growth of Atlanta suburbs which helped turn the state blue. In a new NBC News analysis from the political unit of presidential election results in every county in the country since 2008, three suburban Atlanta counties saw the biggest increase in the share of Democratic votes between 2008 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 When you think about what happened in Georgia, you now see it had been building for over a decade. So for more on this, I`m joined by Michael Steel. He`s a former senior adviser to John Boehner and Jeb Bush, and Stephanie Schriock, president of Emily`s List.\par \par \uc2 Michael Steel, let me start with you and the moment we just witnessed there, Kevin McCarthy and Liz Cheney. I guess I give McCarthy credit for having -- trying to have a sense of humor there at the end. But that is exactly what Donald Trump`s going to be re-introducing to the midterm conversation everywhere and it`s hard not to look at David Perdue and not realize that despite what Rick Scott said the civil war in the GOP is nowhere near over.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEEL, FORMER SENIOR ADVISER TO JEB BUSH AND JOHN BOEHNER: No, I think we should start with kind of expectation setting in the sense that if you look at both Georgia Senate races, if there had been a one-on-one, head-to-head race and both races in November, the Republican candidate would have won.\par \par \uc2 What happened after that with the president sowing poisonous lies about our election system, convincing Republican voters not to go to the polls, I think was the key determinant that led to Democrats winning those two Senate races and control of a \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  as a result.\par \par \uc2 Now the other thing that you talked about here with these suburban counties in Georgia is that they`re growing fast. We have energized people with Trump`s candidacy who feel like they live in areas that are left behind. Become a party of grievance. We need to get back to a Reaganesque sense that this is party of optimism. This is party of new Americans. This is a party that is growing and expanding and looking for that sort of change and opportunity that has historically defined the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Stephanie, when I look at where Democrats make gains and where Democrats struggle, it really is. If it was a state that had a growing suburban -- growing diverse suburban set of communities, that was an opportunity for Democrats. Where those ingredients didn`t exist, they came up short here. So, you know, obviously, long term potential good news for the Democratic Party overall. But it isn`t a magic elixir yet, is it?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SCHRIOCK, PRESIDENT, EMILY`S LIST: Well, we were just seeing just massive growth in these suburban parts of our country and it`s not just the Atlanta suburbs but you`re talking about the twin cities in Minnesota. You`re talking about the suburbs of Kansas City. You`re talking about the Texas suburbs. All are diversifying and growing rapidly. College educated, black, brown, I`m talking African-Americans, Latinx and Asian Americans moving in these communities.\par \par \uc2 It`s where Emily`s List has seen the biggest -- we saw the biggest victories in 2018 to take back the House in these suburbs. And there`s no shortage of women who want to run for office out of these suburbs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCHRIOCK: You know, `18 in Georgia you had Lucy McBath leading the way to have Carolyn Bourdeaux and of course the Senate seats ultimately win. No one got 50 on election day so nobody was going to pull it off. And that`s why you had those two runoffs in the Senate at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious, Stephanie, I want you to respond to something that Michael did say which seems to indicate that, I guess one of the things that we saw was Trump was an accelerant here. If Trump`s in the picture in `22, does that become an asset for the Democratic Party?\par \par \uc2 SCHRIOCK: I think he`s going to be -- I think he`s in the picture. He will be in the picture. He`s not going anywhere. You saw it in the press conference that you showed at the beginning of this segment. There is a serious debate on whether or not Trump should be. The catch is he is. He`s speaking at CPAC. He`s going be around and he is definitely one who galvanizes a lot of energy, to be fair, on both sides. And for Democrats in `22 --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCHRIOCK: -- he won`t be on the ballot but that doesn`t mean we have to assume his voters aren`t going to go. That`s going to be the wild card here. We always think midterms bad for the party in power. But Trump is such a wild card in here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCHRIOCK: All of our elections since `16 have been very unusual. And I expect to see an unusual voting turnout that looks a little bit different than normal which should be and could be good for Democrats if we get our folks energized and mobilized.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. You know, Michael, I go back to this David Perdue decision. And it`s an easy -- look, it`s an easy -- if I try to put myself at issues, and I`m thinking, yes, I want no part of this Trump feud right now. Maybe I`ll think about Jon Ossoff and challenging him in `26, when I have an idea of what the heck has just happened inside this party.\par \par \uc2 I got to imagine that there`s going be a lot of good candidates that could win general elections, of Republicans who are going to sit there and make the David Perdue decision, you know what, I ain`t navigating these Trump waters this cycle.\par \par \uc2 STEEL: Maybe. But I think you`re also going to see a counter reaction against President Biden pursuing this partisan, party line, over-reaching COVID response bill. I think you`re going to see a reaction against parents in suburban area frustrated that their schools aren`t open after a year of this pandemic. I think you`re going to see a lot of excited, new Republican candidates stepping up to reach those challenges.\par \par \uc2 TODD: One of the first, believe it or not, we`re going to have a litmus test, perhaps on sort of how things are playing in swing districts and we already have the first ad of a special election in the Texas congressional seat that would be replaced the gentleman who passed away from COVID, Ron Wright. Here`s that first ad from Democrat Shawn Lassiter. I want to play an excerpt of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN LASSITER (D), TEXAS SPECIAL ELECTION CANDIDATE: Too many of us are still hurting. And while neighbors are taking on added responsibility for each other, state leaders won`t take on any responsibility at all. Families who have power are bringing in strangers during a pandemic but our senator leaves for a beach vacation. It should be instinct for our leaders to care.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, Stephanie, I know Democrats have been down the Texas, you know, this is Lucy and the football with them. But I saw that ad and you`re like, wow. How quickly I think you could see a unified Texas Democratic message up and down the ballot there, perhaps.\par \par \uc2 SCHRIOCK: Yes, I`ve had a lot of conversations with former candidates, soon-to-be future candidates down in Texas since this has all gone on. I`ve talked to friends in Texas. I`ve got a staffer in Texas who -- I don`t think we understand as Americans not living in Texas how bad this situation is. I mean, I know you`ve been covering it but it is unbearable. Food not on shelves, people are hungry. I mean, this is a very dangerous time for the folks in Texas. And there is absolutely lack in leadership.\par \par \uc2 So I think you`re exactly right, Chuck. We had a good strong play in those districts. Without a Trump surge in turnout that we did see in Texas at the very end, we would have picked up four or five House seats. And I`ll tell you, most of them had Emily`s List candidates in them. I think this is -- the question is ultimately, one, will those folks who voted for Trump like an extra juiced up level in `20, come out in `22.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCHRIOCK: I don`t know with him not on the ballot. And two, you know, Michael suggesting moderate candidates are going to be coming through or other kinds of Republican candidates. They`re not going to get through these primaries because the ones that will -- the white extremists that are going to be on those primaries and that`s a problem for the GOP right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, as we said, getting a little start already in campaign 2021 politics there.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Schriock and Michael Steel, thank you both for coming on. Much appreciated.\par \par \uc2 Today, the postmaster general is facing some tough questions on the Hill over months of mail delays nationwide. That story and his confrontational tone after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COOPER (D-TN): Mr. DeJoy, you`re a political appointee, a holdover. No one knows how much longer --\par \par \uc2 LOUIS DEJOY\uc2 , U.S. POSTMASTER GENERAL: That`s incorrect. I`m not a political appointee. I was selected by a bipartisan board of governors and I would really appreciate if you would get that straight.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Well, how much longer are you planning to stay?\par \par \uc2 DEJOY: A long time. Get used to me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was Postmaster General \uc2 Louis DeJoy\uc2  and Democratic Congressman Jim Cooper of Tennessee in the last hour as DeJoy testifies for the House Oversight and Reform Committee today about the future of the \uc2 United States Postal Service\uc2 . The bipartisan committee has been questioning DeJoy and other members of the \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  for over three hours now. And as we just watched, there`s been fire. DeJoy is facing a tough line of questioning from some Democrats who want him ousted immediately, while other moderate Democrats are hoping to work with him to introduce new legislation to save the agency.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is NBC News White House correspondent Geoff Bennett who has been sort of on this as a side beat for some time now.\par \par \uc2 Geoff, I mean, the fact is, we all have the anecdotes of the 30-day Christmas card that showed up.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The 40 -- I mean it is an absurd amount of these anecdotal reports, my house included.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This has got to be animating this hearing.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes. And look, imagine if you`re one of those Americans who are waiting on prescription drugs or a paycheck to come in the mail, and that is two or three weeks late or more. And so that has been a main focus of this hearing. The other focus of this hearing has been trying to find ways to put the Postal Service back on a strong financial footing. \uc2 Louis DeJoy\uc2  is not solely responsible for the current problems at the Postal Service.\par \par \uc2 Congress, Chuck, as you well know, has a huge role to play here, too. Back in 2006. They legislated this sort of bizarre mandate that the Postal Service had to pre-fund all of the health and retirement benefits for all of its employees, to include future employees. And that`s one of the reasons why the Postal Service has posted billion-dollar losses year after year. So Congress is coming up with a plan to deal with that and DeJoy is coming up with a plan to deal with that on his own.\par \par \uc2 A couple of sources familiar tell me that he has in mind a cost-cutting plan that would dramatically change the way first-class mail works and it would basically eliminate two-day mail and it would also increase posted rates, especially for package delivery.\par \par \uc2 And meantime, there are those Democrats who are urging the Biden administration to basically cut this off at the pass. What they want President Biden to do is overhaul the governing body of the Postal Service. That`s the only way that Democrats could eventually get rid of \uc2 Louis DeJoy\uc2  is by changing the board of governors.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: And I`m told that the administration right now is vetting nominees to fill the vacancies on the board so that the board would have a Democratic majority and then the board could, if they so choose, replace the postmaster general, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, this is all part of a larger philosophical argument about whether the \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  is a service or a business.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Or a business, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Anyway, Geoff Bennett, on the Postal Service beat for us. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210225eh2o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Denver Aims to Vaccinate All Teachers by End of March; Average Number of Daily New Cases in the U.S. Falls Below 100,000; Senators Richard Burr and Bill Cassidy Censured for Vote to Convict Trump; MTP DAILY for February 16, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Peter Alexander, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Shaquille  Brewster, Morgan Chesky, Andrea Mitchell, Priscilla Thompson, Kavita Patel,  George Will \par \uc2 8,574 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 February 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC HOST: Remember to follow the show online on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @mitchelreports. Chuck Todd is up next with "MTP DAILY" only right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to Tuesday. It is MEET THE PRESS DAILY and I`m Chuck Todd. The White House`s daily briefing has just wrapped. And with the former president`s impeachment behind us, Washington`s focus now turns towards the current president and the daunting challenges ahead of all of us beginning with the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The White House is looking to turn the page on Trump by renewing its push for speedy passage of his massive $1.9 trillion COVID relief package. Press Secretary Jen Psaki said moments ago that it is the president`s top priority right now.\par \par \uc2 She also said that the White House Coronavirus Response Coordinator Jeff Zients told states on a call today that the government is again able to increase the vaccine supply enough to get us over a two million a day hump. The U.S. death toll from the pandemic is creeping towards a staggering half a million people. Just let that sink in for a moment. It`s going to hit that number before the end of this month.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden hits the road for his first domestic trip as president, if you don`t count uh going home to Delaware or Camp David, and it`s for a town hall in Wisconsin. Obviously, the pandemic will be a major topic. Vice President Harris then speaks exclusively with my colleague Savannah Guthrie tomorrow morning on "THE TODAY SHOW."\par \par \uc2 President Biden will then hit Michigan later this week to meet with vaccine distributors at \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 . As you can see a real ramp up here. Then, it`s the virtual G7 meeting which will also focus on COVID to be sure.\par \par \uc2 Turning the page on January 6th is not going to be easy by any means. We still have a lot of unpacking to do of what happened that day, everybody that was behind it. The Republican Party is still largely Trump`s party. The base is systematically punishing and shunning members who supported impeachment with state party censures, pretty meaningless tools right now. Even as party leaders like Mitch McConnell look for ways to slowly separate from trump as he continues to defend his decision to acquit as he makes the case for essentially a full-time denouncement of Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Speaker Pelosi is taking steps to establish a 9/11 style commission to fully investigate what happened and what led up to the capital attack, more importantly, why were we so unprepared for the attack. It`s expected to examine all components of the insurrection. And that likely is going to take years, not months.\par \par \uc2 So, amid the ongoing upheaval of Trumpism, the fallout from January 6th, and the pandemic, the question is, will President Biden be able to turn the page on his terms. His window to act though is now opened.\par \par \uc2 Our Chief White House Correspondent Peter Alexander is standing by in the Briefing Room. Leigh Ann Caldwell is on Capitol Hill. We`ve got Shaq Brewster on the ground in Milwaukee ahead of the president`s visit there. But let`s start with Peter Alexander.\par \par \uc2 And Peter, frankly, I think -- I think a lot of people -- this is like our second first day of school, I guess, if you will for the Joe Biden White House here. Do they accept this notion that hey, today is sort of inauguration part two now that Trump truly is in the background?\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chuck, I think it is being framed as sort of a relaunch, right, of the -- of the Biden presidency. But if you ask Jen Psaki as she communicated us here at this Briefing Room podium moments ago, they say they weren`t distracted by impeachment over the course of the last week. But their allies and those lawmakers on the Hill clearly were with their eyes focused on the last president not the present one and the present crisis at hand.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what I think what this administration, this president is as you noted with the timeline over the course of this week really trying to put his stamp on, really trying to drive home the urgency of this, again, seizing the spotlight here with those domestic -- first domestic business trips, the stop in Wisconsin today, the stop in Michigan a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just on that topic. You know, broadly they were speaking about housing affordability and the issues associated with that, as well today announcing that they would extend moratoriums -- the moratorium on evictions until June 30th for so many Americans who have been economically hit by the pandemic at this point.\par \par \uc2 But they do recognize the urgency, Chuck, as you indicated, and this is why they`re going outside the beltway in effect to put some pressure on those in the beltway, saying that most Americans broadly agree Democrats and Republicans even if Republicans in Washington do not agree with this plan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter, we`re -- I didn`t hear a lot on the next week`s speech to Congress that Joe Biden is giving, the don`t call it a state of the union state of the union that every first-year president gives is sort of a kickoff to their agenda. Obviously, the big focus is going to be selling the COVID relief plan since you will be within a three-week window of getting that done. But what is the next priority after that?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It`s a good question, one we`ve tried to drill them down on specifically. I think they would say there`s several things beyond the COVID relief package with its nearly $2 trillion price tag then. As they describe it, they have the sort of jobs council, the sort of plans to help build back better. They say, that`s when that begins in earnest focusing on issues like infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 But as evidenced by NBC`s reporting today and what we heard from Jen Psaki moments ago, immigration is another issue that the president is going to try to get to work on as it relates to his immigration reform bill that would include a plan for an earned pathway to citizenship for the 11 million-plus undocumented immigrants in this country.\par \par \uc2 It would also include a sort of shifting of refugee resettlements allowing for a lot more refugees to resettle in this country. And in place of the Trump border wall, a heavy focus on technology at the border. But Chuck, this all takes a lot of work and takes a lot of capital and it`s unclear as it relates to immigration if they do that in one big package or if they just sort of initiate the process by announcing it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There -- yes, and it -- and it`s -- look, that`s politically just what -- it`s a very divisive topic no matter how you do it. Probably better to piecemeal it. That`s for sure. Peter Alexander getting a start at the White House. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me go over to Capitol Hill, Leigh Ann Caldwell. And Leigh Ann, let me start first with specifics and the COVID relief package. This has basically been in the House`s hands while the Senate was doing impeachment. We`re going to get a vote this week or next week in the House?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Next week, Chuck. So, what we`re going to see the rest of this week is the committees are going to finish their work. And then it goes to the full House to vote. And then it comes over to the Senate, of course. The big question is on minimum wage, what they`re going to do with that and what they`re going to be able to do with that if that`s going to make it through the process.\par \par \uc2 And also the question is, are any Republicans going to support this. Democrats are moving along very quickly. You know, we just heard that Senator Mitt Romney, he just tweeted that he`s introducing a piece of an amendment with Senator Tom Cotton saying that they would do a minimum wage increase if it ensures that businesses cannot hire undocumented immigrants.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s obviously not going to pass, but it`s notable that this is a policy area that Republicans are very happy to be talking about. And it seems almost like an attempt by like -- people like Senator Mitt Romney and even Tom Cotton, with presidential ambitions, want to turn the page on the last four years and especially the last month, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, there`s room on minimum wage. There`s room to do a deal. It`s popular. And there`s room if you can figure out a way not to make it flat across the board, to figure out a geographic formula that maybe even becomes part of cost of living increases. There`s got to be a smarter way to do this that sort of gets at some of this stuff.\par \par \uc2 Let me talk big picture here. The Biden White House wants to turn the page on Trump. Is Congress ready to do that? How much time are they going to focus on unpacking the Trump era?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: That`s an excellent question. So, I think that the answer is different for Republicans and Democrats. You know, Democrats are still litigating the Trump area era. There was a lawsuit filed today by senator - - Representative Bennie Thompson of the Homeland Security Committee suing the former president over the insurrection. And we could see more things like that.\par \par \uc2 As far as Republicans are concerned, they -- it`s going to be hard for them to move on. They have a grassroots issue. You`re seeing it in the polls, the grass -- we`re seeing it with all these state and local government censures. And we saw Representative Adam Kinzinger who`s been a big critic of the president, he said, that he has got -- he got nasty -- you know, angry letters from members of his own family saying, how could you speak out against the former president.\par \par \uc2 And that`s not the only example of that. Senator Inhofe of Oklahoma, I was told, before he came out, saying that he would not object to the Electoral College vote on January 6th. He had to talk to members of his family and his closest friends. He had to write them letters explaining his position because he was not going to object on that vote.\par \par \uc2 So, while they are trying to move on beyond the former president, they still have to deal with all of these people in their base, in their states who still have a strong, strong support of him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, this is the major damage that the infotainment channels that dominate conservative media have just totally done a number on their constituents. It makes it very difficult for them to govern. Leigh Ann Caldwell on Capitol Hill, Leigh Ann, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Let me go on the ground in Milwaukee where we find Shaquille Brewster. And Shaq, you spent a lot of time in Wisconsin in 2020. Essentially, it was almost an adopted home state for you from your Chicago bureau beat there. What are you hearing on the ground? How is Biden doing in this first month? Are expectations high, medium, tempered? What are you finding out?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, I`ll tell you, Chuck. Based on all the conversations I had ahead of President Biden`s trip later today, you really get the sense that folks here want him to focus on getting through this coronavirus pandemic. I have a sample of sound that I did in that we -- interviews that we did today and yesterday that I want to play for you.\par \par \uc2 But before we do that, I just want to point out who you`ll be hearing from. The first person is a Trump supporter. Someone who voted for the president who says, Biden is not even a legitimate president because in his words he believes Biden stole the election. You`re going to hear from a business owner, and then you`re going to hear from a person whose father was the first person who lost his life here in Milwaukee from the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 As you listen to the sound, listen to see if you can find the common thread in what they`re saying. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE VITENSE, TRUMP SUPPORTER: Get the kids back to school. That would be priority number one.\par \par \uc2 RUBEN HOPKINS, CHAIRMAN AND CEO, WISCONSIN BLACK CHAMBER OF COMMERCE: The small business assistance, getting these small businesses the resources they need.\par \par \uc2 TIM REILLY, MILWAUKEE RESIDENT: Let`s focus on COVID, number one. All the doses that we can possibly buy in the world should be purchased.\par \par \uc2 ELVAUGHN RILEY, SON OF THE FIRST MILWAUKEE RESIDENT TO DIE FROM COVID: I really appreciate our President Biden for showing that leadership, showing that empathy. My father is gone from COVID. And so, like it`s -- when I hear the name COVID and I hear on the news people dying, like, I`m reminded of every day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: So, you hear there, focus on the schools, focus on the vaccines, that covert relief for small businesses. Guess what, all of those different measures are in President Biden`s COVID rescue plan, the plan that he`s coming to Milwaukee to pitch directly to voters in this state.\par \par \uc2 I think that gives you a sense of there is an opening there for the president. He believes that he can talk directly to voters. Even voters who don`t agree with him, they may find something that they like in his plan. And that -- you can tell that`s what the White House is banking on as they come to the ground here in Milwaukee. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I`ll tell you, I`m curious, Shaq. Let`s say, Biden in the next week or two, when he does his speech before Congress, and folks like that hear that, you know, he`s got a covet agenda but he also wants to start doing tackling other things like infrastructure, like immigration. Are they going to have patience for that or are they going to say hey, focus on COVID first before you start worrying about other stuff?\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: I think so many times, Chuck, you know, people aren`t focused on the exact particular. So, they`ll see the rescue plan. And for them, that means that stimulus check that will come out, that $1,400. Other people will see, oh, that was the school. That was the plan that got schools open. So, I think that`s something that I heard throughout the conversations.\par \par \uc2 You know, one person said, oh, I read the COVID relief bill and I didn`t like this part of it. Well, we haven`t really had a specific COVID relief bill that`s out there. We have an outline that`s there, but the House hasn`t passed any specific legislation or even have that legislative text. So, you get the sense that they will get the headlines. They`ll get a sense of the top lines. And whether or not they agreeing with that, that is what will come down to them and filter down to them.\par \par \uc2 I think one other point, Chuck, that I noticed in my conversations, it`s the idea that they are giving President Biden some time here. They understand he`s just about three weeks in. They`re being a little bit patient but they want his focus to be on COVID.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, to me, that`s just a yellow flag to the White House. Be careful moving on to other issues before the public thinks you`ve got your arms around COVID. Anyway, Shaquille Brewster on the ground for us in what looks like an extraordinarily cold Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Shaq, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get a check now, speaking of weather, on all of this breaking weather news. Extreme winter weather slamming more than half the country. It`s killed at least 28 people across the southeast. In Texas, the state power grid is overloaded. It`s left at least four million people to face historically low temperatures without electricity and often without heat.\par \par \uc2 My colleague, Morgan Chesky, joins us now from Dallas. And Morgan, about the only -- the only upside here is I assume the cold weather motivates people to wear a mask because it`s one extra layer on your face. But how bad is it, Morgan?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: It`s bad, Chuck. Right now, those numbers coming from the state aren`t just four million people, that`s four million households across the state of Texas that are without power. And that`s upwards of about 10 million people in this state that are now going on two-plus days now in sub-freezing temperatures.\par \par \uc2 And that`s simply a situation that not only the state is not prepared to deal with, but a lot of these folks aren`t prepared to deal with. We`re in a neighborhood here in South Dallas. I just had a chance to speak with the gentleman here, Steve Rivera. He moved his wife from this house across the street to a neighbor`s home because they have a fireplace, Steve doesn`t. You can see that firewood piled up in the front yard there.\par \par \uc2 The reason he had to do that is because his wife is in a wheelchair and diabetic and she`s unable to stay warm in their house. They have had three hours of power in the last three days. And right now, that thermostat is reading in the high 30`s. And I can bet that almost every house along this street would read the same.\par \par \uc2 The big concern is they don`t know when the power is going to come back on. And if it does, in some of these rolling outages, they don`t know how long they have to deal with to boil that water, to put in that thermos to hopefully stay warm, or to start any sort of steps going forward to be prepared to handle what`s to come.\par \par \uc2 We have another winter storm coming through here later today dropping a couple more inches of snow. Meanwhile, the energy providers are saying, hang tight as long as you can. But for the many Texans I`ve spoken to, Chuck, they don`t know how much longer they can do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And this is Dallas. This is Houston. We`re not talking -- they can`t say it`s rural areas. This is uh not a good sign and not a good sign for this power grid which the state controls. Anyway, Morgan Chesky on the ground for us in Texas. Morgan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up ahead, the COVID struggle for cities and states across the country. Local leaders, they`re desperate for more help from the federal government. They`re trying to prevent layoffs. I`ll talk to two big-city mayors, one Democrat, one Republican, united in their message to Washington.\par \par \uc2 And don`t miss an NBC News exclusive tomorrow morning. Savannah Guthrie sits down with Vice President Kamala Harris for a one-on-one interview. It`s her first network television interview. That`s tomorrow on "THE TODAY SHOW" live. By the way, it`s a live interview on your local NBC stations. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ, MAYOR OF MIAMI, FLORIDA: I think the president is very interested in having a bipartisan solution, obviously understanding the needs of the residents of America and certainly the needs of the residents of the city of Miami.\par \par \uc2 MIKE DUGGAN, MAYOR OF DETROIT, MICHIGAN: We are -- we don`t kid ourselves about the atmosphere in Washington. We know it`s partisan. But we`re really hoping that for the next couple of months, on this national issue, that they can set partisanship aside. And the President made it clear he really wants bipartisan support for America`s rescue plan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was the Republican mayor of the city of Miami and the Democratic mayor of the city of Detroit speaking after a bipartisan meeting of governors and mayors at the White House late last week with most Republicans in Congress against the White House`s $1.9 trillion COVID relief plan.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has been looking outside the beltway to find Republican support. And among mayors and some governors, he appears to be getting it. So, joining me now is Detroit`s Democratic Mayor Mike Duggan and Miami`s Republican Mayor Francis Suarez. Gentlemen, thank you both for joining.\par \par \uc2 And let`s -- and Mayor Suarez, I`m going to start with you because you`ve got particularly one of your two senators who is very much against this relief bill because it sends aid. He thinks, Rick Scott, too much aid to states and localities. Explain what you need the money for and how much of a shortfall you have, Mayor Suarez.\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: Well, we need the money to get it to our residents, the most needy in our community, those who are suffering. We have (AUDIO GAP) we`ve used it for mortgage assistance. We`ve used it to help, you know, people be fed. And we use it to help small businesses. So, we`ve used it to get to the most needy in the community the help that they desperately are asking for.\par \par \uc2 In the first COVID relief bill, we did not get a lot because (AUDIO GAP) the 500 000 population that prevented most cities like Miami and Detroit from getting its fair share based on its population. And we`re hoping that the president the White House doesn`t make that mistake again. And we have every reason to believe that they won`t.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mayor Duggan, are you facing the possibility of layoffs of government employees? Have you already had to do that? What is your situation there? Because that`s one of the arguments that some make of why you guys need this money. It`s also a way to stabilize the economy and stabilize the unemployment situation.\par \par \uc2 DUGGAN: Yes, we just need to get through the next six to nine months until the vaccines are there and we can reopen. So, Detroit came out of bankruptcy and we had five straight balance budgets. Our credit rating kept going up. GM and Ford and Chrysler and Amazon brought thousands of jobs and we felt like we had it going the right direction. And then overnight, we just got hammered with businesses closing. We had a 360 million dollar loss of revenue.\par \par \uc2 Today, I`ve got a thousand employees on partial layoff. And everything underlying is still good. Fiat Chrysler is still building. The factory it`s going to open next month with 5,000 jobs. GM is still renovating their electric vehicle factory right now. We`re going to be fine if we can just get through to the end of the year. And that was the message I think President Biden heard from Republicans and Democrats across the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mayor Duggan, I want to -- I`m curious, are you -- do you believe that post-pandemic you`re going to be able to continue your revitalization project in Detroit? Meaning, you`re still going to want to have people coming into the city. You`ve had people moving away. This a different problem that Mayor Suarez has, that`s why I wanted to separate this.\par \par \uc2 Post-pandemic, are you worried you`re going to lose that momentum that sort of had people coming back to Detroit, you know, as you were revitalizing the city?\par \par \uc2 DUGGAN: My friend Mayor Suarez has to give me some tips on the way he`s getting the tech companies in. But if you look at what`s happening in Detroit, there is more construction going on today in the city than any time in the last 50 years. There is a $2 billion rebuild of a GM plant. There`s an $800 million renovation at train station for Ford`s vehicles of the future. There`s $400 million in an Amazon distribution center.\par \par \uc2 People believe in our future and they`re continuing to invest in it. That doesn`t help the laid-off mom who`s in a house and she`s afraid now that landlord-tenant courts have been reopened that she`s going to be evicted. And we`ve got a course at restaurants dramatically reduced, stores dramatically reduced.\par \par \uc2 And so, the long-term prospect looks terrific, but that doesn`t help people who are afraid they may be out of their homes in a month. And that`s where the president, I think, is stepping in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mayor Suarez, like I said, on that score, you have a -- you have a different situation. But you`re establishing this early relationship with President Biden and you said you`ve already had more back and forth with this administration than you did the previous one. I assume though that after this you`ve got to start lobbying this administration for climate money and for different -- and mitigation thing.\par \par \uc2 I mean, is this something that you expect to have to have a relationship with the Biden team this way because of the needs Miami is going to have over the next five years?\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: Of course. I think any public official has to have a relationship with the White House. I`m reminded of another good friend, Mayor Bill Peduto, who`s a mayor of Pittsburgh who once reminded me there are three political parties in America. There`s Republicans, there`s Democrats, and there`s mayors. And the reason why is because mayors are expected to get things done.\par \par \uc2 And so, we`re required to be bipartisan. We`re required to reach across the aisle. On climate in particular, which is the issue that you highlighted, the President when he was President-elect, in a call that I had with the conference of mayors that I`m going to become the president of in January of next year, already look to me and to other mayors for advice and guidance on how to prepare a climate infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s imperative that we continue to have a strong relationship with the White House, because as you said, mayors are on the front line and they`re the ones that can guide a federal policy most effectively to help our residents. And the president understands that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you worried, Mayor Suarez, that you`re basically a better advocate to the Biden White House than your two Republican senators? And is that the way it should be?\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: Listen, I`m not worried about it at all. I have a good relationship with both of them. We talked and we spoke in anticipation of the meeting on Friday. You know, from my perspective, it`s not so much a partisan thing, it`s a relationship thing. I think -- the president said something that I found very interesting. He said, you know, I let -- I let my staff handle sort of the policy angles and I handle the politics.\par \par \uc2 And I think what he meant by that was, you know, he`s been a long time operator in Washington D.C. He understands how to build relationships, how to work across the aisle. And I think that`s necessary in order to accomplish legislative goals. And that`s something that I`m willing to do. And I`m sure that my colleagues in Florida will be as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mayor Duggan, on the minimum wage, you`ve said you`re a supporter of raising it. Do you think it`s necessary in this bill or I notice you said you`re learning a lot more about reconciliation than perhaps you ever wanted to? Do you -- do you sort of agree with where President Biden seemed to think a couple of weeks ago which is, it`s probably should be some -- probably it will end up being something negotiated separately?\par \par \uc2 DUGGAN: Well, mayors have been forced to learn the intricacies of the Washington appropriation rules. And Chuck, I know as you understand, a policy initiative like minimum wage is going to take a 60 vote margin in the Senate, whereas reconciliation that`s pure appropriation can be done at 50 plus one.\par \par \uc2 And so, the fact that we sat in the oval office for nearly two hours with four Democratic governors and mayors and four Republican governors and mayors where President Biden pitched this and says, he`s deeply committed to trying to get a 60-vote bipartisan agreement. And whether the 15 minimum wage is a part of that or whether it has to wait for another day, like Mayor Suarez, I`ll defer to President Biden in his judgment on that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mayor Mike Duggan, Democrat, Detroit. Mayor Francis Suarez, Republican, Miami. Thank you both for coming on and sharing your perspective on this. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SUAREZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I appreciate you coming on together. We want to show Washington that this is not something that`s a terrible thing to do. Up ahead, giving teachers a fair shot. How one major city is working to make sure schools are safe to reopen by getting the COVID vaccine into the districts that may need them the most.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a COVID progress report. New cases and hospitalizations are down. That`s the good news. Can we keep it trending in the right direction?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Across the country more teachers and students could soon be heading back to school after the new CDC guidance was released for returning to in-person learning. Now the guidance released on Friday emphasized the importance of universal mask wearing, physical distancing and routine cleaning.\par \par \uc2 The return-to-school guidelines don`t require vaccinations but they do recommend teachers are vaccinated as soon as supply allows. President Biden says states should prioritize teachers and many of them already are, calling it a national imperative to reopen schools.\par \par \uc2 Priscilla Thompson is in Denver for us, where they are vaccinating teachers in districts with high rates of poverty.\par \par \uc2 You know, Priscilla, the guidelines that they put out in some ways are no different than what the guidelines have been for everything, which is, well, you could do this as long as you wear masks, physically distance and clean. I mean, that`s been sort of the rule everywhere.\par \par \uc2 How are local schools interpreting this new guidance?\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chuck, I mean that`s essentially what the deputy superintendent said to me whenever I spoke to her. 60 percent of students here in Denver Public Schools are already back for some form of in-person learning or hybrid in-person learning, and so she said essentially the CDC guidance is in line with what the district has already been doing and the precautions they`ve already been taking and looking to get students back in to the classroom.\par \par \uc2 But what is unique here is that while the CDC has said that school districts are not required to vaccinate all of their teachers, Denver Public Schools is trying to do just that. They`re trying to get all 18,000 of their teachers vaccinated by the end of next month. And the way they`re doing it is really interesting because they`re placing a focus on equity. District officials tell me that they`re looking to serve the students who are often least served in these situations.\par \par \uc2 And so they made their distribution list based on the percent of students that are on free or reduced price lunch. And those are the teachers that are first in line to get these vaccines.\par \par \uc2 And I had an opportunity to speak with the school psychologist here at Doull Elementary, one of the first schools that was able to get vaccinated. And she told me that she had a lot of anxiety returning to in-person learning without being fully vaccinated because she didn`t want to be a source of community spread, not only in this school, but also in the larger neighborhood because as we know low-income areas, communities of colors have been disproportionately impacted by this virus.\par \par \uc2 And I talked to her about the impact that this was going to have, being vaccinated would have, take a listen to what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLA GRAEBER, SCHOOL PSYCHOLOGIST. DOULL ELEMENTARY SCHOOL: Many of our parents and families and guardians don`t have the option to stay home and work from home. They`re in community facing jobs, they`re doing -- working in restaurants, retail, construction. And so they don`t have the choice to stay home. And they`re more at risk for COVID for that reason. And they also need a safe place for the kids to go so that they can go do their essential jobs.\par \par \uc2 So it`s so important that we`re able to vaccinate our teachers and staff, and keep our school open. It sends a message to the community that we care about you, we care about the neighborhoods that have been hardest hit by COVID. And that means a lot to me and I think it means a lot to the community where I work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: And Denver Public Schools started this rollout last week. They`ve gotten through three -- more than 3,000 teachers. They have 18,000 in total that they`ll need to do -- they`re hoping to do that by the end of next month but health officials here of course say that will depend on how many doses of the vaccine they have to give. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Priscilla Thompson on the ground for us in Denver, Colorado. Priscilla, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And a special programming note here, all week long NBC News and MSNBC are highlighting a special series called "KIDS UNDER PRESSURE." We`re looking at ground breaking new research on how students are coping with remote learning and all the realities of this past year, and what we all can do to try to help. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Right now for the first time in a while we have multiple COVID metrics that all seem to be going in the right direction. Over the last two weeks all 50 states in Washington, D.C. have seen new coronavirus cases decrease or remain at a standstill. The average number of new daily infections is now nearing 87,000. That`s the lowest number since early November.\par \par \uc2 Now all of these, mind you, is higher than what we dealt with in the spring of last year, which is why you`ve got to careful popping the champagne corks here. Hospitalizations are down by nearly half from that record high number a month ago.\par \par \uc2 But even as the head of the CDC says we need to be doubling down on safety measures, some states are already easing restrictions as we`ve seen previously and health officials warn that letting our guard down, along with a growing number of variant strains, could jeopardize any progress being made. And even in the midst of that progress, daily deaths are still hovering around 3,000. And nearly half a million Americans have died throughout this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now to help us understand what`s going on is \uc2 Dr. Kavita Patel\uc2 , practicing primary care physician, former White House director of health policy in the Obama administration. She`s of course a MSNBC and NBC News medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Patel, you know, I feel like we have been through three agonizing months just with this pandemic. Forget politics and all of the horrible stuff that happened on January 6th, and it`s like this is the best it has felt on the pandemic, I guess, since September or October which isn`t great, right? It`s better but it isn`t great. What`s your explanation of, are we just through a tough peak and now we`re back to the grind? Or do you think this trend can continue?\par \par \uc2 DR. KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Chuck, good to be with you, and I agree. I mean, it feels like we`re finally getting to that proverbial light at the end of the tunnel we`ve been talking about. A couple of things. Number one, this is the natural ebb and flow of a peak.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we do traditionally, as you showed on that graph, see from our two previous surges we go up and then over a span of weeks largely because we have stay-at-home orders or a local kind of, you know, in-person dining gets ruled out and bans on certain activities go into place, cases come down, and then of course this incredible surge where hospitals were overwhelmed, have helped people try to double down on those safety measures.\par \par \uc2 Cases come down and then what we would normally see, Chuck, is that several, you know, months later cases pick back up again. The dynamic that we`re seeing here is that vaccinations, albeit slowly, are starting to ramp up. They are not an explanation for the decrease in cases but we are also seeing these variants of both homegrown in the United States, as well as the ones that we believe merged from other countries, making coronavirus more transmissible, picking up.\par \par \uc2 I think what we have to do is kind of now think about the traditional metrics, we`ve talked about. Decreases in death, decreases in hospitalizations, new cases dropping. But we`re going to have to be really attentive to these hotspots driven by the new variants, and our surveillance is frankly very poor but we at least know that parts of the country are starting to see an uptick from these variants.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. You brought up the surveillance. It does seem as if no matter what we`ve done, the rapid testing and surveillance, and the ability to test for antibodies is still extraordinarily unreliable. When does that get better? When we`re through the worst of this?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, yes, so the testing has been something that I think even both obviously the Trump administration and it`s still plaguing issues in the Biden administration where they`re trying to kind of, you know, secure contracts already with these direct at home, easy tests that we can do kind of in homes and in settings like schools and nursing homes.\par \par \uc2 But, Chuck, I have to be honest, I mean, especially when we`re going through surges some of those tests are not as helpful because of those turnaround times, or frankly even with the Biden administration negotiating prices, they`re still expensive.\par \par \uc2 So when does it get better? It gets better when we start to see more and more manufacturers kind of flood the market in a way that`s reliable to decrease the prices, but hopefully we`ll have had more people vaccinated at that point in time where we`ll really need those tests to identify cases post-vaccination.\par \par \uc2 I`ve kind of given up now on trying to find reliable ways to screen today, and we`re doing less testing than we were before because a lot of these counties and states are overwhelmed with trying to get the vaccine out, Chuck, so they can`t put out mass testing efforts and mass vaccination efforts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: It underscores the need for the stimulus funding to provide some of that relief, but I`m hoping we can get testing better for what I think will be kind of the next phase of coronavirus, post-vaccination to understand if people get infected, how are they getting infected and with what kind of mutations and strains and variants.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, do you think -- how much -- do you think we can get to a point where even if the U.K. variant is the dominant strain, it`s the dominant strain at 25,000 cases a day?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, I do believe it`s possible. And probably the best sign is that cases are dropping dramatically, day by day, and remember, the variants live by being -- you know, they proliferate through active cases. So the more cases that come down, first because of these measures to your point we have to keep a little bit of the metal on the brakes to not reopen too quickly, too soon but if we can keep cases down, get vaccinations up so that we do get to the ever precious herd immunity, the variants themselves will not have a way to propagate.\par \par \uc2 So B.117, the one that emerged from the U.K. might be the dominant, as you point it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: We`re talking about tens of thousands of cases, and only hundreds of deaths. Not what we`re seeing today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, like I said over the weekend it feels like finally we`re taking more steps forward than backwards. Let`s see if we can keep this up.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Kavita Patel\uc2 , as always, thank you for coming on and sharing your expertise.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And we`ve set up a state-by-state interactive tool to help you plan your vaccine. I hope you already know it. You should know by memory what is your -- your 1B, your 1C, your 2C, all those things, when your time for the vaccine, when you get to line. So guess what, if you`re not sure, then go to planyourvaccine.com. We`ve got the tools there to help you out because you do got to plan it, and you got to help your parents, if you have to, your kids, all of it.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back with a look at the future of the GOP. George Will joins us. You won`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. North Carolina Senator Richard Burr is now the latest Republican to be censured by a state party after voting to convict former President Trump in the impeachment trial. According to local news reports in Maine and Pennsylvania, both Senators Susan Collins and Pat Toomey could soon join that list as well. And they`ve been getting harassed and criticized by local Republican committees.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Senator Mitch McConnell, despite voting to acquit, continues to blame the former president for the January 6th attack on the Capitol writing in the "Wall Street Journal" today, "There is no question former President Trump bears moral responsibility."\par \par \uc2 And McConnell is not hiding that he has his eyes on the prize -- retaking the majority. He told "Politico," "My goal is, in every way possible, to have nominees representing the Republican Party who can win in November. Some of them maybe people the former president likes, some of them may not be. The only thing I care about is electability."\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by George Will. He`s a longtime conservative columnist and an NBC News political analyst.\par \par \uc2 George, I want to start with two extraordinary quotes from Republicans about different Republicans who have wanted to hold President Trump accountable. The first one is from Bruce Rauner. He`s a former governor of Illinois. And this was the actual quote in the Adam Kinzinger profile when he talked about the issue of character. Let me read it here. He says, "Former Governor Bruce Rauner," he said this, he said "Kinzinger could find himself a casualty of the bitter schism dividing the Republican Party."\par \par \uc2 And he says basically you vote on character. The only winners in the war between Trump and Republicans will be Democrats. For some voters, character matters. For most, it doesn`t. Then -- but wait there`s more, sir. This is what Dave Ball, the chair of the Washington County Republican Party in Pennsylvania said about Pat Toomey. "We did not send him there to vote his conscience. We did not send him there to do the right thing, whatever he said he was doing. We sent him there to represent us and we feel very strongly that he did not represent us."\par \par \uc2 Both of those quotes were stunning to me. Most voters in Illinois don`t vote on character, and a Republican official admitting, no, no, no, don`t vote your conscience. Where is the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE WILL, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, the Republican Party is not, although it sees itself to be conservative, it`s not with Mr. Conservative Edmond Burke who, addressing those who elected him from the constituency of Bristol, famously said you did not -- you voted to -- send me to London to House of Parliament to exercise my judgment, not to simply be a rubber stamp for whatever you are feeling at the moment.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Toomey and Mr. Kinzinger have exercised a Burkean kind of representation, exercising their judgment, and now they`re being judged harshly by the activists who run their particular parties and counties and districts in the state.\par \par \uc2 The problem, Chuck, is there`s a perennial problem in American politics. If there`s a large and today growing disjunction between the activists who run the party and the actual voters, which is why it is increasingly likely that Republicans are in danger of nominating people in March and primaries who can`t win in November.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, George, I feel like the phenomenon can be translated into these activists. And you see it with certain senators. And if I sit here and name-check them, then they become stories. So I`m not going to name check the senators. But you know the three or four I`m talking about who I would call extraordinarily online. Basically, they live online. There`s a couple of senators in particular who were bright young stars of the Republican Party a decade ago.\par \par \uc2 And they have just allowed their online personas to become real people. And I feel like that is the rot on the activist wing of the GOP. And, frankly, something we`re going to see in probably the other side as well.\par \par \uc2 WILL: The past of the Republican Party included Abraham Lincoln, Theodore Roosevelt, Dwight Eisenhower, Ronald Reagan. Lindsey Graham, the, shall we say, malleable senator from South Carolina, says the future of the Republican Party, the future of the party of Lincoln, Roosevelt, Eisenhower, and Reagan, is Donald Trump`s daughter-in-law.\par \par \uc2 Now I think he`s wagering on a brand that is perishable, badly tarnished by now. And so the idea that Donald Trump`s standing today, which is very high, is going to remain that way, is it seems to me, problematic.\par \par \uc2 Gallup has a poll out this morning, Chuck, that`s asked Republicans, do they want Donald Trump to play a large role in the party going forward? 25 percent say no. 75 percent say yes. Now that`s who runs the party in the sense that they gather in small rooms, as there are small numbers of them, and they vote censures. But those people it seems to me are not apt to elect the next generation of Republican leaders.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about Mitch McConnell here. He takes real umbrage with the criticism that says he voted to acquit and it was a cop-out. And he obviously believes his belief that this wasn`t constitutional, that he came about that position, you know, not in some cynical way and not in some way to avoid holding Donald Trump accountable.\par \par \uc2 Do you take Mitch McConnell at his word, and why, if you do?\par \par \uc2 WILL: As he said, the question of whether it is constitutional to impeach someone who has already been removed from office by the voters in the calendar, that is a question that as he said is a close question and there are brilliant lawyers on both sides of it, I think he means what he said about that and I think he also means what he said, and it`s perfectly consistent, about how deplorable and God-awful Donald Trump has been, continues to be and does not have to be in the future.\par \par \uc2 That is why, if the remarks Senator McConnell made immediately after the vote was over in the Senate trial, announced the coming shoot out of the Republican Party. (INAUDIBLE), the former president is going to pick in open -- in primaries for open seats and no doubt in some primaries against incumbent senators he disapproves of, he`s going to pick candidates who are like him. Gifted with the same kind of sulfuric rhetoric and bristling with resentments.\par \par \uc2 Mitch McConnell is not going to allow the Republican Party to be hostage to this. There`s going to be a fight and we`re going to see who wins. Mitch McConnell did not want, by the way, to cast a vote to convict Trump and therefore make all these subsequent incoming fights to be McConnell against Trump.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILL: But the fight is there nonetheless.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I thought that was the most compelling part of your analysis, George, is when you said that it at least provided I think a very plausible and reasonable explanation from Mitch McConnell`s perspective of why he wanted to avoid that back and forth.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, George Will, a nuance is something I know people don`t love a lot, but every once in a while we`re going to have nuance conversations. Thank you for coming on and sharing your perspective with us. As always, sir, I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`S coverage continues with Katy Tur, right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210216eh2g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O`Donnell for February 5, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Dylan Byers \par \uc2 7,999 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: I have moved the light beer into the fringe for its one time a year appearance. I have purchased the avocados and the potato chips. I have cleared my calendar. I will see you on Monday, fatter, slightly worse for wear, but very happy for having done what everybody else is also going to do this weekend.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me tonight. I will see you again on Monday after the Bucks win the Super Bowl. Now it`s time for "The Last Word" where Ali Velshi is filling in for Lawrence tonight. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: I was already, Rachel, ready for a fun show but you super charged it. A few minutes ago, I was just listening to you and Dan Goldman have a conversation when you uttered the words, the My Pillow guys soliloquy, words I just never thought I would hear in my life. So, I`m deeply appreciative for you kicking it off for us, Rachel. Have yourself a great game and we`ll talk about the Bucks on Monday.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I will do, Ali. Thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right. Thanks, friend.\par \par \uc2 All right. Well, tonight it is a tale of two parties, one is governing and the other -- it`s not really clear what the other party is doing. Republicans are destroying themselves from inside, consumed with feuds over how loyal they can be to a former president who incited an insurrection and fighting over what actions to not take about the dangerous conspiracy- spewing congresswoman, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Democrats want to pass laws and help Americans who are hurting from the pandemic. Today, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made it clear he will pass his COVID relief package with or without the support of the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: A lot of folks are losing hope, and I believe the American people are looking right now to their government for help to do our job, to not let them down. So I`m going to act and I`m going to act fast.\par \par \uc2 I have told both Republicans and Democrats, that`s my preference to work together, but if I have to choose between getting help right now to Americans who are hurting so badly and getting drive -- bogged down in a lengthy negotiation or compromising on a bill that`s up to the crisis, that`s an easy choice. I`m going to help the American people who are hurting now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Economic relief should not be a partisan issue. It`s clear. People are hurting. But why help millions of out-of-work Americans when you can instead try to oust \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  from her Republican leadership position because she got a case of morals for a moment and voted to impeach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 There is so little talk of solutions from the GOP. There hasn`t been talk of it for four years. There`s so little talk of helping the American people. It`s just about them and their petty little internal dramas. They barely have ideas of their own and yet they refuse to endorse solutions that are broadly supported by the American people.\par \par \uc2 Sixty-eight percent of Americans support President Biden`s COVID relief package according to a brand new \uc2 Quinnipiac\uc2  Poll. Seventy-eight percent are in favor of $1,400 stimulus payments. Even 64 percent of Republicans support the stimulus payments. The White House knows that Republicans are on the losing side of this. They even released a video about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: My message to our Republican elected officials is to put our country first.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: And I think there should be a bipartisan effort to step up and try and assist these people, and it`s imperative to act quickly before things get worse.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The sooner the better because a lot of people are hurting and need the relief as soon as possible. So I`m, you know, rooting for them to get that done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Here is the far deeper problem at the center of this thing. We don`t actually know what Republicans stand for anymore. They don`t support popular legislation or offer solutions of their own. Now, to be fair to Republicans, it`s hard to juggle ideas and solutions when your focus is pulled toward a dangerous conspiracy-spewing freshman congresswoman from Georgia whom you will defend at all costs for some unknown reason.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  was stripped of her committee assignments this week for indicating support for execution of prominent Democrats with whom she now serves. It seems like a fairly reasonable action to take but Republicans balked.\par \par \uc2 They spent more time talking about how unfair it was to the conspiracy spewer than the ever have talked about helping Americans in need. What do you stand for if you defend a woman who incites support for political violence?\par \par \uc2 Our next guest, Democratic Congressman Jimmy Gomez says removing Marjorie Greene from committees isn`t enough. Her toxicity and her terrifying effect on the Republican Party require her removal from Congress, according to the congressman.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans would rather focus on feuds than see the obvious danger of keeping Marjorie Greene or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for that matter in office. The very, very small group who have condemned Marjory Greene or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are paying the price.\par \par \uc2 Republican Congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  is being censured by Illinois county Republicans for taking actions "contrary to the values of the GOP." Contrary to the values of the GOP. Senator \uc2 Ben Sasse\uc2  might be censured for the second time by Nebraska Republicans for not being deferential enough to Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN SASSE\uc2  (R-NE): You are welcome to censure me again, but let`s be clear about why this is happening. It`s because I still believe as you used to, that politics isn`t about the weird worship of one dude.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The weird worship of one dude. The Republican Party is not that familiar anymore. It doesn`t have a set of values or principles. It defends a man who incites an insurrection. It defends a woman who supports political violence and it barely utters a peep about the millions, the millions of jobless Americans struggling just to survive through no fault of their own.\par \par \uc2 Maybe it`s time for the party to rebrand. Maybe they can take on a new party name that`s more reflective of reality, something like the do-nothing party. Sure, it sounds like it`s been used before, but a group that is devoid of ideas probably can`t come up with anything better anyway.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Democratic Congressman Jimmy Gomez of California. He`s a member of the House Oversight Committee. Congressman, good to see you. Thank you for being with us. I`m curious, I`m sort of asking everybody who has anything to say about \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  these days what the camel -- the straw that broke the camel`s back was for you.\par \par \uc2 What`s the thing, the part of the litany of things that she has done that makes you feel that she shouldn`t be in Congress?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIMMY GOMEZ (D-CA): For me it was something that was building over time. It was the fact that I was trapped in the gallery during the insurrection where people were beating down the door because of a lie that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spread that the election was stolen and the only way they could get it back is by overturning the election of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and storming the capitol.\par \par \uc2 She was part of that -- inciting that insurrection. And she has never backed down from the lie, she has never backed down of using hate speech. And it was that and also just as more information came out regarding saying that Parkland was a false flag, that 9/11 was an inside job.\par \par \uc2 It just showed that her ideas are dangerous. And the fact that she is willing to use them to gain political power and to incite political violence. I said I had enough and I thought she should be removed.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You know, I`m curious because on that day, on January 6th where you were trapped in the gallery, so many of your other colleagues were trapped including Republicans, if you feared for your life on that day you would not have been unreasonable to do so.\par \par \uc2 There are all sorts of things that scare us in life, but they were actually coming for you and your fellow members of Congress, Republican and Democrat. Why do your Democratic members of Congress, your Republican colleagues, why do they not see this?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: You know, I don`t know. And I also think that some of them are scared. I had -- we`re reaching out, trying to get people on a resolution to expel her and some people are concerned about their safety, some of them are concerned about the safety of their staff.\par \par \uc2 And they remind me and I remind people that the insurrectionists weren`t just coming to get \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . They were chanting, hang Mike Pence, hang Mike Pence. So she doesn`t pose just a threat to the Democratic Party, but she poses a threat to the Republican Party and to civil discourse in the United States of America. I`m deeply concerned about that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: What is your -- what is your argument that you make to the people of Georgia who elected her, who say, look, it`s our choice. We can elect whoever we want? She didn`t run against anybody, but she won by 50 points in her district. Is the root problem \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 ? Is the root problem \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , or is the root problem the people who actually elected her to Congress?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: When she continuously spreads a lie, you know, conspiracy theories and uses that to win votes, we can`t as a house disqualify her. And that`s what I`m saying that should be done because she is dangerous in the House.\par \par \uc2 Remember, there`s no rule that you can`t have a gun in the House office building. You`re not supposed to bring guns to the House floor. She has said that she was going to do that. She has walked around the metal detectors.\par \par \uc2 So we are fearful for her to be in the House with other members, and I`m not the only one. I have 70 co-sponsors to my resolution to expel her. So it`s not just about her say, but also just the violence and the rhetoric she has been using to incite violence. So, it`s a deep concern.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Two of your colleagues have been fined $5,000 under this new regulation which says that you cannot bypass the metal detectors that members of Congress now have to pass through to get to the floor of the House. Representative \uc2 Louie Gohmert\uc2  of Texas and Representative Andrew Clyde of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 What happens? I guess they just keep getting fined and they get fined more if they do it more often, it comes out of their salary so there`s no particular recourse?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: They have 90 days to pay it and then it comes out of their salary, but it really depends on how rich they are. For me $5,000 would be a lot of money since I still owe a student loan debt. I still have bills, but for some of them it`s not going to stop them. I think that it has to be more painful than that. It should double every time they have a violation. That would really get their attention.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Talking about $5,000 being a lot of money, it is a lot of money. In fact, to a lot of Americans $1,400, which is what they are hoping they`re going to get in the next round of relief checks, is a lot of money. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has said he is not compromising on the $1,400.\par \par \uc2 There are some Republicans who seem to be acting in good faith and negotiating with the president and the White House, some Republicans in the Senate. But Biden is saying he`s not budging on the $1,400. He may budge on how much you have to earn or how little you have to earn in order to get that $1,400. What`s your take on this?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: I always wanted to make sure that we got $2,000 a month during the pandemic. I`m a co-sponsor of the Emergency Money for the People Act during the pandemic. We`re not going as far as I like, but I`ll support a bill that gets that $1,400 extra to people.\par \par \uc2 People are hurting. I`ve been in food lines. I have been handing out food every weekend since the pandemic has started, and people are struggling to make ends meet. We are on the verge of an eviction crisis. So, yes, we have to get more money to people`s pockets.\par \par \uc2 And in California, the people -- who make $50,000, $60,000, $70,000, $80,000 are still struggling. So we got to keep them in mind because they`re also Americans who are being impacted by this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I want to ask you about this $15 minimum wage. You remember four years ago not everybody was on the same page about the idea that $1,500, which is $31,000 a year if you are lucky enough to work full time, just for those people who think that folks are running away with it.\par \par \uc2 Biden told Norah O`Donnell that his guess is that the minimum wage increase that he`s proposing does not make it into the final passage of the American Rescue Plan. But here is the thing. Look at this \uc2 Quinnipiac\uc2  poll, 61 percent of Americans support raising the minimum wage to $15 an hour, and a lot of states have actually voted for this in referendums. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: I voted for raising the minimum wage when I was in the California State Assembly to $15. It was during the fight for $15. I think that the United States should take that step to increase the minimum wage. It`s the one thing that will get more people out of poverty that will make sure that people can support themselves, to get them off government programs. $15 I see as a minimum and in certain states we should even go higher.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congressman, good to see you. Thank you for joining me. Jimmy Gomez is a representative -- Democratic representative from California and he is a member of the House Oversight Committee.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, as the path clears for President Biden to pass this COVID relief bill, Democrats are getting down to the nitty-gritty details of what that legislation is going to look like. One of the committee chairs who met with the president today on that very issue, Congresswoman Rosa DeLauro joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: On this vote, the yeys are 50, the nays are 50. The Senate being equally divided, the vice president votes in the affirmative and the concurrent resolution as amended is adopted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: A little bit of history there. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  casting her first tie-breaking vote in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  this morning. Now the Democrats have passed their budget resolution, the Biden administration`s COVID relief bill is closer to becoming a reality with or without Republican support.\par \par \uc2 And that relief is desperately needed. Today was Jobs Day. The jobs report showed the economy`s recovery is stalling. Projections indicate that at the current pace of progress, the United States will not return to full employment for a decade. Here is President Biden today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is what this moment comes down to. Are we going to pass a big enough package to vaccinate people, to get people back to work, to alleviate the suffering in this country this year? That`s what I want to do. We do have the resources to get to full employment sooner. We do have the tools to reduce a lot of suffering in this country. We just have to choose to use them. So it`s time to act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The president is now getting into the nitty-gritty of the legislative process. After the Senate vote this morning, he hosted House committee leaders to discuss the details of the bill with the goal of passing it before the weekly unemployment supplement expires on March 14th.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, one of the House Democrats who met with President Biden today, Democratic Congresswoman Rosa DeLauro of Connecticut. She is the Chair of the very important \uc2 House Appropriations Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, good to see you again. I like that we`ve had a chance now two times to speak during the last week. I want to ask you about this relief plan. There are questions about whether or not Democrats and the president should compromise on the $1,400 that is being suggested to augment the $600 that was passed last time and make it a total of $2,000 because some Republicans say if you -- if you give less money we`ll get on board. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 REP. ROSA DELAURO (D-CT): Well, first of all, thank you for having me and we got to stop meeting like this, Ali. This is great to be with you. But the package is the package and the discussion, first of all, was very rich with the president and the vice president. So totally engaged with the committee chairs and the leadership of how we move forward with this package in an effort to bring that kind of relief that you are speaking about.\par \par \uc2 The president was very, very clear that he was going to stand with the $1,400 plus the $600. I find it really -- look, it would be wonderful to have bipartisan support in this effort, but my Republican colleagues did not blink an eye when we passed a tax cut to benefit the richest one-tenth of 1 percent of the people in this country.\par \par \uc2 You and I know, and the American people know that they are on their knees. They`re suffering. A health care and an economic crisis that is once in a century. We need to move. We need to move fast, and we need to provide the health relief that they need and the economic relief they need to get us back on track.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: A number of economists have come out and said that there`s just no danger of doing too much. We`re nowhere near possibly over-stimulating the economy, and the fantastic thing about a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and interest rates is if we ever got to that point where the economy was too hot and inflation was too high, they could slam the brakes on that with interest rates if they needed to.\par \par \uc2 And yet Republicans are worried that this is too much, too big, and, god forbid, it will increase the debt.\par \par \uc2 DELAUREO: Well, you know, these are -- it`s amazing for me to listen to this debate from my Republican colleagues and that, you know, again, the tax cut was not too big, which has added to the debt. And that didn`t seem to faze them or bother them at all.\par \par \uc2 What is important and is critical within this package, which says that we then can look to reducing unemployment, reducing hunger, reducing poverty. We are going to make an investment in our health infrastructure system. We are going to make an investment in childcare.\par \par \uc2 We cannot get through a recovery if parents do not have a place to put their children. We are going to try to stabilize education so our schools can open, and our kids can go back to school. We`re going to look at the housing industry.\par \par \uc2 For me in particular, the child tax credit, which is in this proposal, has the ability -- it`s like social security, Ali. Social security lifted 90 percent of our seniors out of poverty. The child tax credit here will lift more than half of black children out of poverty, 40 percent of Latino children, 61 percent of Native American children.\par \par \uc2 It really has an unbelievable transformative impact on what can happen to families today. That`s the direction that we need to go. We need not to throw money at a problem or put Band-Aids on a problem, but look at how we address these issues as in building an architecture for the future which is what we need to do because of what this pandemic has exposed in terms of the inequities in our society and where we need to go.\par \par \uc2 We need to move quickly. We are prepared to do that. As you know, the House passed the budget resolution today. It now moves to the committees to put the bills together and then get them to the Senate. And it is our hope that we are going to be moving, you know, by the end of this month.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congresswoman, last night there were a number of your fellow members who were making speeches about January 6th and what happened. This coming week, this impeachment trial is going to go to the Senate. I want to play just a little bit of what your congresswoman -- your colleague, Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez said last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP))\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): With little to no accountability for the bloodshed and trauma of the 6th, some are already demanding that we move on, or worse, attempting to minimize, discredit or belittle the accounts of survivors.\par \par \uc2 In doing so, they not only further harm those who were there that day and provide cover for those responsible, but they also send a tremendously damaging message to survivors of trauma all across this country, that the way to deal with trauma, violence, and targeting is to paper it over, minimize it and move on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congresswoman, I`m going to ask you the same question I asked your colleague -- your colleague, Jimmy Gomez, just a few minutes ago. You all were there, and they were targeting you and they were targeting your Republican colleagues as well. I believe you were in the gallery as well. You and I were talking about this last week.\par \par \uc2 DELAURO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I mean, this is -- I`m kind of puzzled because if that crowd got in and they used that ammunition that they had, enough collectively to shoot every one of you there, those bullets had their name on it, too. I`m not understanding how this became partisan.\par \par \uc2 DELAURO: I don`t understand it either. I was in the gallery, as I said to you last week, Ali. I was with Jimmy Gomez. Jimmy Gomez grabbed me by the arm to, you know, help me make my way around as well. We were watching out for one another.\par \par \uc2 It didn`t make any difference if it was a Republican or a Democrat, we all needed to be protecting one another. And there needs to be the accountability. And I don`t understand. We cannot turn this under and push this under the rug.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`m reminded of what the -- in South Africa when they had the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, where one had to understand what happened, why it happened, who was accountable, in order for us to move to healing and to any kind of unity.\par \par \uc2 We just can`t say that people who -- even at the end of that day when we went back to the floor of the House, they voted to overturn the election that was fair by all measures. And so, it is a denial of the -- what the violence of the day, of who incited that violence.\par \par \uc2 And still the continued denial of the election of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as president and vice president of the United States. Why? What are they afraid of?\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: An important point you bring up about South Africa. There was no reconciliation until there was truth. You could not come and ask --\par \par \uc2 DELAURO: True.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: -- that commission for forgiveness unless you were prepared to say what really happened. We are not at that point in America where we`ve got the truth. Congresswoman, good to see you again. Thank you for joining us. We are glad that you and your colleagues are safe. Congresswoman Rosa DeLauro of Connecticut. She is the chair of the House Appropriations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, \uc2 Fox\uc2  has fired one of its most vocal Trump supporters just one day after a multibillion-dollar lawsuit was brought against the company for its election lies. More on that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We`re finally starting to see the consequences of lying for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Fox\uc2  Business has cancelled "Lou Dobbs Tonight", the show hosted by one of Trump`s most loyal media reporters. In the weeks after Trump lost the election, Lou Dobbs repeatedly peddled baseless and ridiculous theories of electoral fraud.\par \par \uc2 Fox\uc2  cancelled the show just one day after \uc2 Fox\uc2  and Lou Dobbs became defendants in a defamation lawsuit seeking $2.7 billion in damages. The lawsuit by the election technology company, \uc2 Smartmatic\uc2 , also names as defendants two other Trump`s favorite \uc2 Fox\uc2  anchors \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2  and Jeanine Pirro, as well as Trump lawyers \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and Sidney Powell who Lou Dobbs had on as guests to lie about the election.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit argues that Giuliani and Powell quote, "created a story about \uc2 Smartmatic\uc2  and Fox News joined Giuliani and Powell in championing the disinformation campaign against them \uc2 Smartmatic\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit argues, quote, "The story, of course, did more than just make defendants money and jeopardize Smartmatic`s survival. The story undermined the people`s belief in democracy. The story turned neighbor against neighbor. The story led a mob to attack the U.S. Capitol."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is my friend Dylan Byers, he`s a senior media reporter for NBC News and MSNBC. Dylan, good to see you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Let`s just get clear. What happened tonight? Is Lou Dobbs still in a job. I assume a guy like him has a contract that they would have to deal with in order to get rid of him.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN BYERS, MEDIA REPORTER, NBC NEWS AND MSNBC: Yes, he does have a contract. And Ali, to the best of our knowledge based off of the sources we have spoken with, he will see that contract out through the end of its term. But it would be a pretty safe bet to say you won`t see Lou Dobbs on \uc2 Fox\uc2  Business or on Fox News again.\par \par \uc2 And while the network is saying, you know, the network`s PR department is saying, look, we had plans to do something like this well before the lawsuit, the timing makes that claim rather dubious.\par \par \uc2 And what you have here is a lawsuit that at $2.7 billion would effectively wipe out all of the profit that Fox News made for Rupert and \uc2 Lachlan Murdoch\uc2  in the last year.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, you ask this question about is there a penalty for lying and you watch the rightward drift of Fox News and the answer to that question seems to be that there is a penalty (INAUDIBLE) threat to the business. You do not get rid of your highest rated anchor on \uc2 Fox\uc2  Business unless you feel like there`s a worse cost to incur from keeping him around.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: There was a precursor to this, and that was that it was a very weird thing. \uc2 Fox\uc2  and \uc2 Fox\uc2  Business put together this thing where some -- an election expert was asked questions that basically debunked the nonsense that Jeanine Pirro and Lou Dobbs and \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2  and others were saying on \uc2 Fox\uc2  and they actually had to do it in some of their own shows.\par \par \uc2 So it was a precursor. I think it was the threat of a lawsuit that led them to do that but that obviously wasn`t enough.\par \par \uc2 BYERS: Yes, that`s right. And if you look at that, that obviously looks like they`re preparing to make the argument, well, we presented the other side of the story or well, fact-checked ourselves. And even if you look at the Fox News statement what they`re -- they stress the fact that they`re not just a news network but they`re also an opinion network. You can see them starting to protect themselves against this defamation suit.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let me ask you about some of the things that are quoted in the suit. There are cited in the lawsuit by \uc2 Smartmatic\uc2  three instances of things that Lou Dobbs said.\par \par \uc2 On November 12th he said, "This looks to me like it is the end of what has been a four-and-a-half year long effort to overthrow the president of the United States."\par \par \uc2 A few days later, November 16th Lou Dobbs said, "There is no election in our presidential history, our nation`s history in which there were so many anomalies, so many irregularities and so much clear evidence of fraud."\par \par \uc2 On December 10th Lou Dobbs said, "We`re going to examine in some detail the reasons for what is apparently a broadly coordinated effort to actually bring down this president by ending his second term before it could begin."\par \par \uc2 Here is my question for you, Dylan. What`s so different about what Lou Dobbs did than what \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2  did or Jeanine Pirro did or for that matter \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 ? Why is Dobbs gone and they`re still in the job?\par \par \uc2 BYERS: You know, that`s a really good question. And I think we`re going to have to wait and see what happens with those other two \uc2 Fox\uc2  anchors you are talking about.\par \par \uc2 I mean you`re talking about statements that are just absurd on their face. Between those three anchors and then the two lawyers for President Trump, you have them alleging things like that \uc2 Smartmatic\uc2  software was being used in key battleground states to sway the election.\par \par \uc2 There was one county in which \uc2 Smartmatic\uc2  software was used and it was my county of Los Angeles. It wasn`t used anywhere else. So the claims out there are -- it is not that they`re toeing the line of fact and fiction, they are way, way out in deep water.\par \par \uc2 And again, it is this idea of like what is the limit of what you can get away with saying on a powerful cable news network. And again, if you wade into defamation, you pretty much crossed that line.\par \par \uc2 And I think Fox News knows and the Murdochs know that the (INAUDIBLE) actually have a pretty sound case.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Dylan, good to see you, my friend. Thank you for your reporting. Dylan Byers is a senior media reporter for NBC News. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Democrats turned Pennsylvania blue in the presidential election. Can they do it again in the upcoming Senate race? Pennsylvania Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman thinks he can do it. The first major candidate for that open Senate seat joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The race for Pennsylvania`s open Senate seat will be one of the most competitive in the country in 2022 as Democrats look to expand their slim majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 One man thinks he`s got what it takes to deliver for the Democrats. Pennsylvania Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman filed his paperwork this week to compete for that Senate seat and there`s support for his candidacy.\par \par \uc2 When he announced last month that he was considering a run, he raised more than $1 million in just two weeks, and that`s thanks in part to the visible role that the lieutenant governor played in shooting down Republican election lies about his state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. JOHN FETTERMAN (D-PA): The only election scandal in Pennsylvania is how did President Trump end up with 100 percent of the dead mother vote in our state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Joining us now, Pennsylvania Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman. Lieutenant Governor, good to see you again. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Talk to me about the evolution of this decision. Is it a decision, first of all, is it exploratory or do you believe that you are going to run as the Democratic candidate for the Senate in Pennsylvania in 2022?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Well, I mean we`re getting close to a decision. We are not quite there yet, but I just -- I just want to be able to thank everybody. You know, we`ve raised over $1.4 million and it is with over 36,000 grassroots donors. Our average donor amount is around $30 to $31. So the support has been overwhelming. I just can`t express my gratitude.\par \par \uc2 So a decision is going to be coming really soon. We filed some necessary paperwork over the last couple of days to take it that much closer, but we`re getting close for sure.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let`s talk about the situation -- the political landscape in Pennsylvania. It is not dissimilar to the situation in Ohio or North Carolina where you`ve got Republican senators who are not going to run again. \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2  in North Carolina, Rob Portman in Ohio and Toomey in Pennsylvania -- none of whom are really the kind of people who are showing their colors as Trumpians these days.\par \par \uc2 So the absence of those people causes a problem for Republicans in these states including in Pennsylvania because regular Republicans have to figure out whether they want to go with the looney tunes group or they want to do something else.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Yes, well, I mean I -- I don`t know the other senators, but Pat has been absent a backbone for the last, you know, forever years he`s been in the Senate. It has been nice to see he`s been liberated by the simple fact that he doesn`t have to run again and he can tell the truth. I mean what a concept, telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 I mean this idea that saying there was actually no election fraud and that the president`s lies incited the riot on Capitol Hill on January 6th, I mean that`s not a profile in courage. That`s just, you know, that`s the truth.\par \par \uc2 And you know, we`re going to miss Pat one way or the other, but I don`t want to ever get to a point where just telling the truth is a profile in courage.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: There is -- you know, while the attention in this election has been on Georgia with the Senate elections, the stuff that the legislature is doing or has been doing or tried to do with respect to voting in Pennsylvania is some of the most outlandish in America, the things that they`ve come up with.\par \par \uc2 And now again, they`re trying -- for instance, there are three different proposals to eliminate the no-excuse voting for no particular reason, just that it didn`t go the way Republicans wanted it to go in the last election cycle.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Yes. I call it the Pennsylvania GOP democracy for me but not for thee. You know, vote-by-mail was 100 percent their law. They voted for it unanimously, they loved this law.\par \par \uc2 And then after the president said that vote by mail was bad, they realized they had a line to toe and now they`re trying to repeal it. They couldn`t pile on fast enough, even though it was their law.\par \par \uc2 They are operating in an utterly fact-free environment and they relish it. They don`t see a problem with it. And they`re also going to attempt to gerrymander our state supreme court effectively out of existence, too.\par \par \uc2 So this idea, hey, they voted for me, there wasn`t any fraud in my election, but that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  guy, you know, we rigged Pennsylvania for him. It is baseless, it is vile, it`s un-American and it was incitement and we saw that play out in the Capitol on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Talk to me about your appeal in this state. We`re both in the same state right now. You have got Philadelphia in the east, Pittsburgh in the west, urban centers, multicultural populations, hospitals and schools. And in the middle you`ve got working class, in many cases anti-establishment folks who are angry about stuff. How do you bridge that gap? Who are you to all Pennsylvanians?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Right now I`m their lieutenant governor. But what I also am is the same person that showed up in a very marginalized, struggling community 20 years ago to work on a lot of key issues that I haven`t had to evolve on, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 I think the party has evolved more in my direction, whether that`s a $15 an hour minimum wage, whether that is environmental and social justice for marginalized people in these communities.\par \par \uc2 You know, health care being a basic, fundamental human right that we all need and we all deserve. Whether that is effective community policing. Whether that is rights, equal protection under the law for LGBTQIA communities. I mean all of these -- you know, ending the war on drugs, legalizing marijuana in this country.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, these are just fundamental, common sense things that we have seen that the party has moved towards these ideals as we should. And that`s where I`ve always been for the last 20 years. And I`m always going to run on what I know to be true.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Did you ever get your money, by the way, for the bounty that the guy in Texas put up if anybody found voter fraud? Because I think last we talked you were up to three cases that you had at least found in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Thank you so much for bringing that up. Yes. Deadbeat Dan still never has paid up a single dime. But we did find one additional instance of voter fraud here in Pennsylvania. It was --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Tell me.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: It`s actually in my home county of Allegheny County. It was a gentleman who voted for his deceased wife for Trump. So again, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again captured 100 percent of the dead relative vote here in Pennsylvania, out of seven million ballots.\par \par \uc2 That amounts to four cases, all going for the president. But Deadbeat Dan, you know, we`re still waiting. But, you know, we would love --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Hasn`t paid up yet. Do let me know if you ever get that money. Pennsylvania Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman. Thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Virginia is about to become the first southern state to abolish the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 One of the country`s most tireless anti death penalty activists, Sister Helen Prejean joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Virginia really is for lovers. Virginia is about to become the 23rd state and the first southern state to abolish the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 Today the Virginia House of delegates voted to ban executions in the commonwealth. The state Senate passed the same measure earlier this week. Now it`s one of several progressive measures the historically conservative state has taken up since Virginia Democrats took control of the state legislature in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The good news is because Virginia leads the nation in executions, this might actually make a difference. The bad news is how many people Virginia has executed. You might want to be sitting down for this.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 1,400 executions since 1608, when the first recorded use of capital punishment took place in what is now the United States. Democratic Governor Ralph Northam who is committed to signing the legislation told "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", quote, "It`s important that we shut down the machinery of death here in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Sister Helen Prejean. She is known around the world for her tireless work against the death penalty. She`s the author of "River of Fire: On Becoming An Activist".\par \par \uc2 Sister, thank you for being with us today. Tell me, first of all, your feelings about this decision in Virginia?\par \par \uc2 SISTER HELEN PREJEAN: It is a deep, deep rejoice seeing the first past confederate state to shut down (INAUDIBLE). I was with a man, Joseph Odell in Virginia when he was executed. And he was -- he was innocent, I believe.\par \par \uc2 But that fourth circuit was upholding those death penalties and Virginia was killing people at a faster rate than any other state.\par \par \uc2 And I watched Virginia at work. And it`s amazing to me. There`s a convergence of forces that have happened, I think, that have led to this moment.\par \par \uc2 And a key thread in it is African-American people. They have this really dynamic interfaith center for public policy, led by LaKeisha Cook. And they had 300 clergy, African-American people of color clergy that began to have vigils and began to speak out.\par \par \uc2 So you have African-American leadership just as in Georgia in the runoffs there. You have George Floyd being killed and you have Governor Northam with his own thing that he had done black face. He felt bad about it.\par \par \uc2 The confederacy statues are coming down. Black Lives Matter happened and so you have this convergence of voices now to show the legacy of slavery that Virginia and all the Deep South states have had with the death penalty. And it finally converged to a point there was enough consciousness in the country.\par \par \uc2 And the people on the board of Virginians for Alternatives that have worked for 35 years for this day. We`re given all these factors that had happened and the people were saying ironically, Trump is the reason why Virginia is putting down the death penalty. They were so outraged by the 13 killings that Trump did simply because he could that people were calling their legislators it just made the moment and the way it hit the shore.\par \par \uc2 VAUSE: And I want to ask you about this, Sister, because you have been doing what you have been doing for as long as I have been covering it. And one of the most -- where are we in this process because you are talking about this convergence of factors putting pressure on legislatures and officials to roll back executions.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, you just mentioned the 13 executions that took place in the last days of the Trump administration, which is by far, a record. And it may make some people feel that we are -- we`ve gone completely in the wrong direction. So where are we now?\par \par \uc2 PREJEAN: Ok look, different jurisdictions. What -- and I just traced this out in an article in the nation, it`s going to come out starting tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 When Greg v Georgia, when the supreme court put death back, it was faulty, it was skewed from the beginning. They did two impossible things.\par \par \uc2 First, they set as a criteria, that was impossible to carry out or know what it meant -- the worst of the worst. Nobody knows what that means. And they coupled it (INAUDIBLE) discretion of prosecutors to go for death or not.\par \par \uc2 The truth is, nobody gets death in a trial on up, if a prosecutor does not decide to seek death. Trump had complete discretion with federal executions. Even though 17 years before, there had been no executions. He starts killing people like knocking cans off a fence, because he could. He had the discretionary power.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what people could see. Look at this, but at the state level. We have been shutting death down for ten years. Last year, 2020 was the lowest number of executions almost since we started the death penalty, only seven.\par \par \uc2 We have prosecutors and you can see this in Virginia, not going for the death penalty. Juries not voting for the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 34 states have not had an execution in ten years. So, even as we are shutting it down in the states, Trump stood out as such an anomaly of killing because he had the discretion in the federal jurisdiction.\par \par \uc2 And here is Biden coming in, and look at Lisa Montgomery (ph), look at the three people killed in the last five days of Trump`s presidency. If they could have lived five more days Biden would have not killed them. Look at the vague reason, capriciousness, in the way death comes down.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Can the president do that? Can Biden, who has said, you know, it`s on the campaign Web site, the Biden campaign Web site said, "eliminate the death penalty because we cannot ensure we get death penalty cases right every time. Biden will work to pass legislation to eliminate the death penalty at the federal level and incentivize states to follow the federal government`s example".\par \par \uc2 How big a lift is that?\par \par \uc2 PREJEAN: It`s going to be big. But the first thing he can do is just simply say moratorium. No executions while I`m president.\par \par \uc2 The other thing he can do is commute all the sentences on death row. Like Governor George Ryan did in Illinois after the 12th innocent person was brought into his office. And remember George Ryan had enacted the legislation in Illinois to bring back the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 So he could commute, he has the power. It`s called the last divine right of kings, its power over life or death. And he can commute the sentences.\par \par \uc2 But finally to eliminate federal death penalty will be an act of Congress. Ok.\par \par \uc2 Got to go, great being with you.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Sister, you keep time better than I do. Thank you for joining us. Sister Helen Prejean is an --\par \par \uc2 PREJEAN: No, I have got voice in my ear.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: -- anti death penalty activist.\par \par \uc2 I think they meant to talk to me. They are telling me I`ve got to wrap up the show so I will.\par \par \uc2 But thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s LAST WORD, according to my producers.\par \par \uc2 You can catch me again tomorrow morning on my show, "VELSHI". I will be talking to top Biden economic adviser Jared Bernstein about what is next for the COVID relief plan, who`s going to get the much needed checks and when.\par \par \uc2 Sunday the countdown to Donald Trump`s second impeachment trial begins. I`ll be joined by Congresswoman \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  and Congressman James Clyburn.\par \par \uc2 That`s Saturday and Sunday mornings, 8:00 to 10 a.m. right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR" with my friend Brian Williams starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020210206eh2500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Meet the Press Daily for February 5, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Kristen Welker \par \uc2 11,187 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 February 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS MEMBER: -- could and should have. And I think that`s one of them. But for now, what we need to do is get this package out there and meet the urgency of the moment.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Kirsten.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Thank you. Thanks for being here. I want to ask you about some of the criticisms by one of your former colleagues, \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , of course former top economic advisor to former President Barack Obama, former Treasury Secretary, he has acknowledged the bailout in 2009, by his own admission he says, didn`t go far enough.\par \par \uc2 But he says that this $1.9 trillion proposal is so big that it risks progressive priorities in the future, and could potentially undermine the economy next year. Is the Biden administration going too big?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: No, I firmly would disagree with that contention. By the way, I wouldn`t call the other thing a bailout, that was the Recovery Act. And I think that also was an effective measure. But I think that the idea now is that we have to hit back hard, we have to hit back strong, if we`re going to finally put this dual crisis of the pandemic and the economic -- the economic pain that it has engendered behind us.\par \par \uc2 With respect to Larry`s point, one thing is just wrong, which is that our team is dismissive of inflationary risks. We`ve constantly argued that the risks of doing too little are far greater than the risk of going big, providing families and businesses with the relief they need to finally put this virus behind us. Second, I want to quote a Fed Chair Jerome Powell, who strongly reiterated this view the other day, I think it was just a week or so ago, that inflationary risks are also asymmetric right now.\par \par \uc2 When asked about this precise trade-off that you`re asking me about he said, and I`m quoting, I`m much more worried about falling short of a complete recovery and losing people`s careers and lives that they`ve built because they don`t get back to work in time. I`m more concerned about the damage that will do, not just to their lives, but to the United States economy, to the productive capacity of the economy."\par \par \uc2 I`m more concerned about that than about the possibility which exists of higher inflation. So, this is risk management, this is balancing risks. And in our view, the risks of doing too little are far greater than the risks of doing too much.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And one more question, the Senate moved forward with a measure that did not include an increase in the minimum wage. Has President Biden come to a determination that that`s not going to be a part of the final package in order to get this passed, in order to get the Democratic support that he needs?\par \par \uc2 Bernstein: The President has consistently argued that a minimum wage, $15.00 an hour, is essential to make sure that people, many of whom -- millions of whom are essential workers, are not toiling at a minimum wage of $7.25 per hour, which is the federal minimum wage. This idea that somehow, and I`ve heard this in many questions, it`s this idea that the minimum wage is somehow orthogonal to this package makes no sense to me because it is a efficient and effective way to raise the pay of people who are in the bottom end of this workforce, essential workers in retail trade, in healthcare, in sanitation.\par \par \uc2 People who are keeping this economy going, but consistently under compensated for it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But will it be a part of the final package?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I`m not going to negotiate that from the podium, as they say. Wait, I have --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Very good.\par \par \uc2 Bernstein: I have one other point I want to make though, getting back to the argument with Larry. This is -- this is key from my perspective as an economist, who throughout my career has been motivated and concerned, I think the theme of my work has always been making sure that this economy, that our economy, the American economy provides ample opportunities for people from all walks of life, from all parts of the income scale, men and women, persons of color, communities of color, have the opportunities they need to realize their potential.\par \par \uc2 And right now, there is deep unused capacity in this economy, which is targeted by the American Rescue Plan. There are 10 million unemployed people. There are 2-1/2 fewer women in the labor force than last year. As I mentioned, black and Hispanic unemployment rates are nine percent, we`ve got a job market in stall. The risk is a deflationary risk, which motivates us to go home or -- to go big or to go home.\par \par \uc2 And the costs of inaction, of not addressing these risks, are too steep and too costly to these vulnerable -- to these vulnerable groups, relative to the likelihood of overheating. That`s the way I think about it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Just one more follow up from the other Kristen. Is the White House`s Economic Team, is there anyone on the team that is concerned that the $1.9 trillion is too big, is too much, or is everybody in agreement?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: The White House Economic Team is in complete consensus on the urgency of the need for this American Rescue Plan, and in complete solidarity on the calibration of this plan, that it`s -- that it`s of the magnitude to meet the challenges we face. Again, the -- with respect to Larry and his piece, it`s just flat out wrong that our team is, "Dismissive of inflationary risks." Any -- Janet Yellen is our Treasury Secretary, OK?\par \par \uc2 She knows a little something about inflationary risks, and has tracked that kind of, you know, economic issue forever. You know, I just quoted from you from Jerome Powell, whose job is to manage that risk against the risk of slack in the job market, against the risk of persistent unemployment, against the risk of people getting stuck in joblessness so they can`t get back out, and get back into the job market, against the risk of scarring in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Meaning, not doing enough about current damages so that they become permanent damages, and people can`t get back into the labor market. And businesses that should and would be viable on the other side of this crisis fail because we haven`t taken the steps to get them through to the other side of the crisis. So, the team has all of our oars in the water, pulling in exactly the same direction on that.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: (INAUDIBLE) you`re going to have to be the last question.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: One other question on Larry Summers` criticism, not to belabor this point, but he also raises some questions about your future agenda. He notes that you will have committed 15 percent of GDP with this bill, with basically no increase in public investments. Is this a concern going forward? Where will you find the money to build back better, as the President has promised?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: You know, I think the way President Biden talks about this is not just resonant, but also follows an economic logic that I think is very strong. Rescue, recovery. The rescue plan, the American Rescue Plan gets this economy and the families and the businesses in it to the other side of this crisis by finally controlling the virus, producing and distributing the vaccine, and giving people the relief they need to get to the other side.\par \par \uc2 But simply getting back to where we were is a bar that`s far too low for the Biden-Harris administration, and that`s where building back better and the Recovery Plan comes in. These are structural changes. By the way, many of which, as the President has said, permanent programs should be paid for. So, these are structural programs that -- not in a cyclical sense, dealing with kind of getting to the other side of the crisis, but deal with the structural challenges we face.\par \par \uc2 In climate, in education, in care, in poverty, in racial discrimination and I am -- in infrastructure, I am wholly confident in this president and this administration`s ability to go forth and make a strong case for rescue now, get folks, get the economy to the other side of the crisis, pursue the recovery, the Building Back Better agenda. As I say, I`m confident about that. One point, and then I`ll stop. Infrastructure, I would say I get asked about this a lot.\par \par \uc2 And the implication kind of the question is that, well, infrastructure is a Democrat thing, and you`ll have a hard time with that. Let me tell you a little anecdote. I was testifying sometime a year or two ago in the House. And when I finished my testimony, a couple of Republicans, and I won`t name them because this was a private moment, pulled me aside and said, hey, a Democrat, come here.\par \par \uc2 And they said, we want to do infrastructure, but we can`t do it because our boss, President Trump, doesn`t have a plan. The plan was really an asterisk. There are -- I guarantee you, there are politicians on both sides of the aisle who are champing at the bit to make investments in public goods in this country, to do an infrastructure bill that repairs, not just maintenance, but gets into clean energy, into broadband, into the kinds of investments that you`ve heard the President talk about. So, I am confident that we work on rescue now, we`ll get to recovery next.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thank you. Thank you, Jared, so much.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thank you. Well, people often ask me what my favorite part of this job is. It`s I get to call up Jared Bernstein or Jake Sullivan and talk to them about questions and bring them in here as often as we can, and that`s how it should work. I have a couple of items at the top just to go over and update you all on. First -- sorry. Let`s see. At 3:00 p.m. this afternoon, Vice President Harris and Secretary of the Treasury Janet Yellen will hold a virtual round table with participants from local Black Chambers of Commerce from across the country to discuss the importance of passing the American Rescue Plan.\par \par \uc2 Local Chamber representatives will share on-the-ground experiences during this crisis, ask the Vice President and Secretary questions, and discuss how small businesses in their community are faring right now and what they need. A brief note on the tragic deaths of two \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agents earlier this week. Acting Attorney General Monty Wilkinson will lead the delegation to both Special Agent Laura Schwartzenberger`s memorial service on Saturday and Special Agent Daniel Alfin`s memorial service on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 At the request of the President, Homeland Security Advisor Liz -- Dr. Liz Sherwood-Randall will accompany the Acting Attorney General to Sunrise, Florida this weekend to attend both services. Last item. Tomorrow, the White House -- sorry, and I have a week ahead. Tomorrow, the White House will launch a new effort for the President to regularly communicate directly with the American people. This was a question one of your colleagues asked earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 There is a time-honored tradition in the country of hearing from the President in this way, from FDR`s fireside chats to Ronald Reagan establishing the weekly presidential radio address. President Biden will continue that tradition, and we expect it to take on a variety of forms. The inaugural edition will be a conversation between the President and Michelle, who lives in Roseville, California, and lost her job at a startup clothing company because of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Look for that tomorrow on the White House digital channels. Finally, next week, the President will be focused on engaging with bipartisan groups on the American Rescue Plan and other key priorities, including current vaccine distribution and national security. On Monday, he will virtually tour a vaccination center. On Wednesday, he will visit the Pentagon to meet with the secretary of defense.\par \par \uc2 And on Thursday, President Biden will visit the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2 . So with that, Zeke.\par \par \uc2 ZEKE MILLER, WHITE HOUSE REPORTER, ASSOCIATED PRESS: Thanks, Jen. A couple of (INAUDIBLE) for you. Why is the President going to Delaware this weekend?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: He is from Delaware and has a home there and is going to spend the weekend with his wife and family there.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: But the guidance from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , as you know, in the White House briefing just a couple of hours ago was big X over airplanes, people should avoid travel. Is there an exception to that policy?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, the key, Zeke, is ensuring that people don`t take steps to make others vulnerable in the -- in our effort to get the pandemic under control. As you know, any president of the United States, Democrat or Republican, obviously takes Air Force One, a private plane, when they travel. Delaware is his home, and so he looks forward to spending the weekend there and some time with his family.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Changing gears a little bit, we know that the President`s been vaccinated. Has he been receiving regular tests for the coronavirus while he`s been here at the White House? We haven`t seen since the transition an update on his testing.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I`m happy to get back to you on that and provide you an update. He has, as you know, received his second vaccination, which was done in public.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: (INAUDIBLE) couple weeks ago, you were asked about the President`s policies towards federal executions. Does the President plan to put in place a blanket federal moratorium again?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: The President has spoken about his opposition to the death penalty in the past, but I don`t have anything to predict for you or preview for you in terms of additional steps.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: And finally, just one last one, sorry. I`m at the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It`s OK. It`s Friday, we`ve got to get it all out.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: It was a very different tone from the President. So, when did the President recognize that Republican -- that continuing to negotiate with Republicans wasn`t going to lead him anywhere, and they sort of had to embrace his proposal and get on board the train before it leaves the station. It seems the time for negotiation is over. It`s now the President`s trying to get this thing passed.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I wouldn`t say that`s an accurate characterization of his view or the view of any of us. Just to note, even as the package is moving through a reconciliation process, there is a great deal of time. The process enables for time for negotiations through committee work, which will happen next week. And also, the majority of reconciliation bills in the past have been bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 And so, we certainly are hopeful that there will be opportunities for amendments from Republicans, amendments from others across the board to be a part of this process moving forward. Go ahead, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Thanks, Jen. If I could actually just follow up with you and just read some of what President Biden said today. He said, if I have to choose between getting help right now to Americans who are hurting so badly and getting bogged down in a lengthy negotiation or compromising on a bill that`s not up to the crisis, that`s an easy choice. I`m going to help the American people who are hurting now.\par \par \uc2 So, has he resigned himself to the fact that he`s going to have to use reconciliation and move forward without 60 votes from Republicans?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, he wouldn`t use reconciliation, right. Congress would use that process. It`s a - it`s a -- it`s a parliamentary procedure. And just in a bill becomes a law, a moment here, if there is an opportunity to move forward with a bipartisan package at any moment, that can happen. But again, I believe it`s 18 of 24, and I can double-check this, of reconciliation bills in the past have been bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 And a bipartisan bill has 52 votes, 54 votes, 56 votes. But his point, on the last point, and then we`ll go to your next question, is that we are not going to sit here and wait for an ongoing negotiation where, frankly, we haven`t received an offer in return. Right? A response offer to what the President has proposed because the American people need the relief now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Understood.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But it seems as though he has resigned himself to the fact that there will not be 60 votes in the Senate for whatever passes.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I think the President listens to the American people, who are, frankly, not too worried about what parliamentary procedure gets them relief, gets shots in people`s arms, and reopens schools. And he is certainly hopeful that there is opportunity for this bill, whatever form it takes, to have bipartisan support. And there`s an opportunity to do that. History shows that`s precedent.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Let me, if I could ask you on foreign policy, there`s going to be a principals meeting on Iran today. President Biden so far has not accepted or has not moved forward with negotiations over a new Iran Nuclear Deal. When is the timeframe for that to happen? And does he think he`ll be able to get Democrats on board with this, Jen? They were quite critical in 2015.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first, you asked this first -- this question first, and smartly. So, on this meeting today and I know that the inter-agency process is a little foreign in this building and in the government because of the last four years. So, this is a principal`s committee meeting. We`re not going to confirm every one of these, but for the sake of educating everyone, not in this room, but people who are watching. It`s -- the focus is broadly on the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure Iran will be a part of the discussion as it`s an important issue and important priority for the President and for many of our partners and allies around the world. But this is not a decisional meeting. It`s not a meeting where policy will be concluded, and it`s not a meeting the President of the United States will be attending. So, this is a normal part of the inter-agency policy process, just as there are meetings about immigration, criminal justice, the economy every single day across government.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But on the topic of Iran, understood. Given that you say Iran will be raised in this meeting, is there a timeline for when President Biden would like to try to come back to the table and get a deal on the Iran Nuclear Deal?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, that`s really up to Iran. If Iran comes back into full compliance with the obligations under the JCPOA, the Iran Nuclear Deal just for people who don`t like acronyms, I personally hate them, but the United States would do the same, and then use that as a platform to build a longer and stronger agreement that also addresses other areas of concern. But that will be done in partnership with our P5+1 partners and also through consultation with Congress.\par \par \uc2 And I know I keep saying this, but we are still 2-1/2 weeks into the administration. So, this is part of how the inter-agency process should work, where senior members of the National Security team are meeting and engaging about a range of issues in the Middle East. And, you know, those - - but otherwise, it`s in Iran`s court to comply. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you, Jen. One more foreign policy question. During President Biden`s big foreign policy address yesterday, he didn`t really mention Afghanistan. Why not? And where is he on the full withdrawal of U.S. troops from that country?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I appreciate your question because he -- it was not meant to be a comprehensive foreign policy speech, and he will have a lot more to say about foreign policy and his approach to national security in the weeks ahead. But there were a lot of topics that weren`t discussed because it wasn`t designed to give the overarching Biden doctrine or give his comprehensive view on every issue globally.\par \par \uc2 In part because there are inter-agency processes that will be ongoing, consultations with our partners and allies are a key part of our policy development, as is consultations with members of Congress. So, there`s nothing I have in terms of an update as it relates to Afghanistan at this point in time, but he will have more to say on foreign policy in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. What is being done about -- what is being done, what could be done to provide COVID testing to migrants at the border? Because right now, the \uc2 U.S. Customs and Border Protection\uc2  is saying that they`re having to catch and release some migrants without giving them any kind of COVID test before they`re entering the community. So, what is being done, what could be done?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Are you suggesting they`re letting people in across the border without testing them? Or just tell me a little bit more about what your question is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That they`re being released because of the executive order that the President signed earlier this week, which --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Which executive? Which one?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Rescinding President Trump`s policy, which stopped catch and release. They`re saying that they`re having to provide -- they`re having to release some migrants into the community before they know for sure that they do not have COVID, and they`re worried that it could spread in the community. Is there anything being done at the federal level to make sure that this is not contributing to the spread of coronavirus in this country?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, certainly, the reason we`ve put in a number of protections in terms of travel and otherwise is to keep the American people safe. But I haven`t seen that report, I can`t validate the accuracy of it, but I`d certainly point you to the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  for more specifics about what`s happening at the border. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you. You continue to say that you`re hopeful that Republicans will still get on board, you can achieve bipartisanship. But we haven`t seen any movement on the Republican side. The fundamental differences remain the same. What gives you hope and optimism that Republicans are suddenly going to come around here?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I think, one, the vast majority of the American people support this package that would bring relief to American families, that would get shots in the arms of the American people, and would help reopen schools. Those are not Democratic ideals, they`re not Republican ideals, they are American ideals. So, we still keep the door open to seek ideas, ideas to make the package stronger, from any Republican or other Democrats who want to bring them forward.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And given the President`s remarks earlier and his change of tone, it does seem that he is now OK if this does happen just with Democratic support, despite those hopes and despite his calls for unity.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first of all, the President ran on unifying the country and putting forward ideas that would help address the crises we`re facing. He didn`t run on a promise to unite the Democratic and Republican Party into one party in Washington. This package has the vast majority of support from the American public. This is something that people want. They want to see it pass. They want these checks to get into communities. They want this funding to go to schools.\par \par \uc2 They want more money for vaccine distribution. He is certainly not -- I wouldn`t draw that conclusion. He is somebody who is keeping the door open. He will remain engaged with Republicans in the days ahead. As you know from covering The Hill, there`s still several steps in the process here to move it forward. We saw even some actions last night in Vote-a-Rama, which is my favorite term of the week, where there was a bipartisan support for ensuring the checks were targeted.\par \par \uc2 There was a bipartisan I know somebody asked a question about minimum wage earlier, there actually was bipartisan support on that, including from Senator Bernie Sanders, for making sure that it wasn`t implemented immediately. There is bipartisan support for helping small businesses. There`s disagreements certainly on the size, but there is a shared view that the American people need relief.\par \par \uc2 And we are -- it is our responsibility to keep the door open to any good ideas that come forward.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On the minimum wage, do you feel that this bill is your best shot at getting through a hike? Does it become more difficult going forward if you can`t get it done now?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: You know, I don`t want to get ahead of where we are in the process, but the President believes that increasing the minimum wage is something that would help American families, and it is essential to helping people who are struggling and something that workers certainly deserve. We will leave it to the Democrats and Republicans in Congress to see if this is possible through the parliamentary process of reconciliation. Go ahead, Karen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Jen, to follow up on that and the quote that Kristen read, but I also want to come back to the vaccine question. The President said, you know, it`s an easy choice between getting help to Americans who are hurting or getting bogged down in negotiations. What`s his definition of bogged down? What`s the timeline looking like? For him, he`s been involved in negotiations for many years. What does bogged down mean?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It means bogged down. It means --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I`m not going to set a timeline. I understand the desire and interest in that, but urgency means he would -- he is pleased to see that members of Congress, that leader Schumer and that speaker Pelosi are moving this forward rapidly, that there is a fire under the bellies -- in the bellies of people in Congress to get this package through. Move it through the process over the coming days and weeks. But that`s up to them on the timeline.\par \par \uc2 He just is going to continue to argue for urgency because the American people, until they know when they`re going to get checks, until they know when schools are going to get funding, it`s hard for them to plan. We know that there are timelines that are coming up. One, we`re at the brink of, you know, spending out the package from December. 600 billion of that has already been spent out.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of it is going to be spent out in the coming weeks. There is going to be a need for additional relief in all of these categories, so hence the urgency, but I don`t have an exact deadline or due date other than let`s keep moving.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE) vaccine.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`re hearing so much in our reporting about frustration across the country with people who are trying to navigate the system, they`re signing up on multiple Web sites through multiple means to hope to win a lottery and get a vaccine appointment. What is the federal government doing right now to address this challenge for so many Americans and why can`t there be a better system, so it`s easier to just sign up and wait for your turn?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We agree with you completely. That is completely confusing, has been around the country and states and localities. The American people who are just trying to do their job, take care of their kids, homeschool, balance, everything, everybody`s balancing right now just want to be able to go on a Web site and sign up for their vaccine. Now, one of the steps we`ve taken we announced earlier this week is of course, working with pharmacies to distribute about a million doses in order for Americans to be able to do that in certain communities.\par \par \uc2 That`s -- obviously, that number is going to be increased over time. That`s one way. There are large plans through FEMA to set up large vaccination sites. That is something that is starting to be underway this week, but our focus is very much on increasing communication, ensuring exactly as you said, that the American people know how, when they can get their vaccine and we fully agree.\par \par \uc2 There`s been a lack of communication, confusion, and we are trying to work out of that hole, but we`re only 2-1/2 weeks in here. So, we`re just -- it`s in process.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- healthcare.gov, but for vaccines. Jeffrey Zients obviously has a lot of experience with that. Could you do a federalized system?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: As does as Andy Slavitt. Some people back from the -- back from the healthcare.gov days. Look, I think there are a range of options under consideration. I have not heard them suggest that, but they are very open and discussing every day ways to make this more accessible, clearer to the American people and they just want to do it in a way that`s effective and efficient and reaches local communities where people are trying to get vaccinated. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. I know you just said that you refrain from giving a timeline on the COVID relief bill and its passage, but outside an hour ago, speaker Pelosi said, absolutely, when asked if the COVID stimulus would pass by March 15th. Does that -- do you also share that confidence that it would pass by mid-March when those unemployment benefits run out?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Never doubt speaker Pelosi, anything she says. That`s kind of a lesson I`ve learned in Washington. She is a powerful and fierce force up there. You know, we`re not going to set a timeline from here. It is a bill that would be passed by Congress of course. Speaker Pelosi is the speaker of the house, so certainly I would -- we would defer to her and the President looks forward to signing the bill when it comes to his desk.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you feel an urgency get it passed by that date then?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We feel an urgency to move it forward as quickly as possible. And I think what you`re referring to is kind of the unemployment cliff that will hit in March. But certainly, we would like to see action as quickly as possible as we`ve been saying, but I`m not going to set a new deadline from here. Obviously, we`re working closely with speaker Pelosi and Senator Schumer or leader Schumer, sorry, every day. Go ahead, in the way back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you. Thank you very much, Jen. Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Two Asia questions if I -- if I may.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Some engagement with China. Yesterday, President Biden said he will work with allies and partners. However, also on yesterday, French President Macron say "A situation to join all together against China. This is a scenario of the highest hospital conflict it will ever be. This one for me is counterproductive." So, what exactly does President Biden expect from the U.S. allies?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: From our U.S. allies? Well, we`re going to work in close consultation of course, and partnership with our U.S. allies on a range of issues. We talked about Iran a little bit earlier in the briefing. Of course, strategic competition with China is part of that, you know, but I can only really speak for what our policy is here from this White House and the United States. This administration sees the United States as engaged in strategic competition with China and technology is a central domain of that competition.\par \par \uc2 We should have no allusions about China`s objectives, which are to undercut America`s longstanding technological advantage, and to displace America as the global leader in cutting edge research and development and the technologies and industries of the future. The national security and economic consequences of allowing that to happen are simply unacceptable. That`s certainly what the President conveys in his conversations with our partners and allies.\par \par \uc2 But this is a major reason why the President`s committed to making major investments in science and technology research and development, as well as supply chain security. And we will leverage the full breadth of authorities available to us to protect U.S. national and economic security interests. That`s our position here. And obviously, he will communicate that to allies and partners as he`s having engagements with them. Did you have a second question?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Actually, Asian American woman actually have the highest jobless rates for the past six months. And a report indicates that`s because of racism during the pandemic against them. So other than sign (ph) a memo (ph), what President Biden can do to reduce the racism against Asian-Americans and help them to (inaudible) job or in this -- to deal with the current situation?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, certainly, one of the things that he can do is speak out against racism of any form, but also how it impacts a range of communities. Asian-Americans, of course, communities of color. And one of the factors we`ve seen in data about COVID, of course, is that the pandemic has had an undue impact on many communities, including -- I`m sorry -- many communities of color.\par \par \uc2 I don`t have the exact data on Asian-American communities specifically, though I`m happy to check on that. But you know, his focus is on getting the pandemic under control in order to help provide a bridge to economic relief and recovery, and that`s one of the ways he can help address that.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you, Jen. I have two rather quick questions, and then a little bit more meaty one, if that`s OK.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I like the setup so I can know what to prepare for. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: OK, so the first quick one, this -- I`ve heard (ph) this as a yes-no or maybe-perhaps.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I never like those questions, but go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Will President Biden use the power of the bully pulpit to help cajole teachers who are unwilling to go back to schools, to go back?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, one, I`m just going to reject the premise of the question. I will say, I have teachers in my family, as I`m sure many of you do. They are the first people to tell you that being -- teaching in the classroom and being able to engage with kids in the classroom, or middle schoolers, or high schoolers in the classroom makes their job more enjoyable, makes them more effective at what they do.\par \par \uc2 The president is absolutely committed to reopening schools. He wants them not just to reopen, but to stay open. And he wants to do that in a safe way. And we`re going to rely on CDC guidance which, again, is not officially out yet, to determine the best way to do it.\par \par \uc2 But there are several mitigating factors that we`ve seen in data to date that will help make it safe. Of course, vaccines are a part of that, but so is masking. So is social distancing. So is ensuring that schools have the ventilation and the facilities that they need in order to do it safely. That`s our focus.\par \par \uc2 So the president`s focus is on -- and that`s one of the reasons why he`s out advocating for the American Rescue Plan. Part of that is funding so that schools can do exactly that.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: It sounded like a yes with an asterisk. (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I -- I -- I -- you -- if you are the spokesperson for the White House you could certainly say that, but you are not. But you can ask me another question.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: My second quick question is last year, OMB and the Justice Department made it so that three cities -- New York, Seattle and Portland - - could be disfavored for federal grants. They were deemed, quote, "anarchist jurisdictions" for allegedly tolerating rising crime. There were violent protests. Has the Biden White House decided to reverse those policies disfavoring grants for those three cities?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: This is an OMB action from the Trump administration you`re asking about?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: OMB and the Justice Department, yes.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: OK. We are a new administration. We, of course, are reviewing a range of policies and charting our own path, but I don`t think I`m going to have any comments on policies from a year ago from the prior administration.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: The third -- the third more meaty question, if I could just follow up on it; quote from the president in December. He said, quote, "My son, my family will not be involved in any businesses -- any business, any enterprise that is in conflict with or appears to be in conflict with -- where there`s appropriate distance from the presidency and government." Just recently, there were reports that the president`s son still owns a 10 percent stake in a Chinese investment firm formed with state-owned entities. Do you have an update on his investment -- from that investment?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: He has been working to unwind his investment, but I would certainly point you -- he`s a private citizen. I would point you to him or his lawyers on the outside on any update.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thanks. I have two questions. Jared Bernstein was talking about the threshold of the stimulus checks and when they should phase out, and he said people who make over $300,000 -- or families, should not get those checks. We`ve seen Susan Collins and some other Republicans talk about having the checks phase out to $50,000. What is the White House`s position today on when those checks should start to phase out? And -- and who should not be eligible for them?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: There`s an ongoing discussion about it, and it is an active discussion. It -- the decision has -- a final conclusion has not been made, as Jared was saying. Those conversations are happening with Democrats and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And as I said, kind of, the other day, but it`s still the status today, the president is firm on the necessity that four -- people receive, who are eligible, $1,400 checks. He`s not movable on that becoming smaller. But there is a discussion, as Jared said, about the -- the phase-out and -- and what that looks like. Now, that doesn`t mean that somebody making -- that - - that it`s a -- it`s a dead cut-off; It means that it will be phased out to slightly less than that amount at whatever the cutoff is. But those are ongoing discussions, and a final decision hasn`t been made.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Secondly, there has been a lot of discussion today. You know, President Biden talked about the 2009 stimulus package and lessons that he and other people learned from it. What are some other points of economic crisis that the Obama administration dealt with that President Biden has drawn lessons from? Is it from auto bailout? You know, there was so much that -- that people were dealing with at that time. What other economic crises did he draw lessons from, and what are those? And how is he applying those to the situation today?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I haven`t had -- it`s a very interesting question. I have not had this in-depth discussion with him about the impacts of the Obama administration policies on his thinking.\par \par \uc2 I can say, broadly speaking, because there are people who -- of -- of course, a number of us who had served in the prior administration. Of course, there are lessons about ensuring we act swiftly when the American people need relief, looking for bipartisan support, which is something, certainly, the former administration also did.\par \par \uc2 There`s also lessons we`ve learned about how we sell the packages that we`re putting out there to the public, and that`s one of the reasons why we bring in some of our economic officials or policy experts so that they can help lay out for all of you, and hopefully, for the public, the thinking behind how packages are designed; and also, that we need to continue to think about how we break down these packages for the public to ensure we`re explaining why we`re doing what we`re doing. It`s not just a $1.9 trillion package, right? It is a package that has funding to reopen schools. It has a -- is a package that it has funding to help ensure cops and firefighters can stay in their jobs. It`s a package that will get vaccines in the arms of Americans, and it will -- it`s a package that will ensure that the one- in-seven Americans who don`t have enough -- are concerned about putting food on the table are able to do that.\par \par \uc2 So those are some of the lessons, but you know, I don`t have anything more about his specific -- what he`s specifically drawn from it.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: But it seems like part of that lesson is to not wait for Republicans forever. It -- it seems like you want to move quickly.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: The president wants to move quickly, as he talked about back on the campaign trail, too, because the American people need relief now, and then we don`t have the luxury of waiting months to deliver that relief to them. So that is about reacting and being -- responding to the needs of the American public at this moment in time and the crises we`re facing.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Jen, thanks. I`d like to follow up on the issue of school reopening. Dr. Walensky has said, at a previous briefing, there is increasing data to suggest that schools can safely reopen, and that they can safely reopen without teachers getting vaccinated. You then said the official CDC guidance is not out yet. But there is some urgency to this, because schools are making their decisions right now about how and when to reopen. So does the Biden administration have an assessment today about whether schools can reopen with or without teachers getting vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first, the -- the guidance will come officially from the CDC, as Dr. Walensky, who leads the CDC, would certainly convey to you. And what we are all conveying, and what I just did a few minutes ago, is convey that there`s a lot of data that shows that it is -- of course, we`re looking at vaccines. That`s an important part of keeping teachers and -- and the American public safe. But we also need to look at -- and other mitigation steps, I should say, including masking, social distancing, proper ventilation in schools. And the urgency should prompt Democrats and Republicans -- many Democrats are -- to come together to support the American Rescue Plan so we can get schools the funding they need. Schools are planning, but many of them don`t have the funding they need to take the steps necessary to reopen.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do you know when the CDC specifically is going to put out that guidance?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I would certainly point you to the CDC for more specifics on that.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead, Anita (ph).\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Do you have a sense at this point of when the Cabinet nominations will go through? Obviously, we`re right up against the impeachment trial. This is what you all didn`t want to happen, which is this -- this delay. We don`t know how long the trial will last. I`m specifically curious about the attorney general. I know you`re eager to get him in. So any sense of that? And I assume that the president has spoken to Senate leaders about that. Can -- is there anything that can be done?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, we are certainly hopeful that there -- more of our Cabinet nominees can move forward. We have seen a number of them move forward in the last couple of weeks, many with bipartisan support, with bipartisan votes. But you`re right that getting the attorney general through, Merrick Garland, is vital not only to the president, but should be vital to Democrats and Republicans in Congress in order to have a leader at the head of the department who can oversee an independent Justice Department, and you know, ongoing effort -- and reviewing the ongoing efforts or investigations that are happening there.\par \par \uc2 This is an issue -- broadly -- the confirmations in general -- he has raised in the past with members of Congress, and certainly there`s an understanding about the importance of having his people in place leading agencies but I don`t have anything specific for you to update on the Attorney -- on the timeline of an Attorney General being confirmed.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And just generally, there`s sort of no specific timeline on when these might be done, just as soon as possible, no timeline?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Certainly, Anita (ph), as soon as possible. You know, we are confident that the Senate can walk and chew gum at the same time. As -- as you well know, there was a delay in part because of the need to agree on a power sharing agreement. Obviously we`re past that and -- and certainly given many of the comments, including from many Senate Republicans about the qualifications of our attorney general -- the president`s attorney general nominee, and the value that I -- I think and hope we all share, to have an independent Justice Department, we`re certainly hopeful they can move forward as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: OK, and then really quick housekeeping -- excuse me, sorry...\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: ... about next week, you mentioned a couple of things that you`ll be doing next week. One, on the Pentagon visit, will -- are -- should we be expecting speeches, like we saw yesterday, at these -- I think Jake mentioned that he`ll be doing a round of visits. So should we be expecting a speech at that event next week?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: That`s a great question. I -- I don`t think we`re fully there, fully cooked yet in the process. Certainly part of his effort is to thank civil servants and members of the military, of course, for their -- the work they do every day protecting the American people, but in terms of what format the event will take, we`re not quite there yet in our planning process.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: The other thing about next week, you mentioned a couple of things but you didn`t mention meeting with members of Congress on the bill. I assume some of those meetings are going to happen next week. Can you tell us about any of those specific things? Will they come over here or...\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, they will be -- many of them will be on recess, so -- but he will be engaged, of course, continue to be engaged with members of Congress. Often those come together the day before, the night before, and we will of course keep you updated as -- as those engagements happen, our - - our plan for next week. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Yeah, so we know the president`s doing an interview before the -- the Super Bowl on Sunday. I mean, I`m guessing that`s going to be a -- an opportunity to reach a huge audience with your recovery. Is -- is that going to be what he`s going to use it for?\par \par \uc2 And I -- and I guess also, I`d like to know how will the president be watching the game and who he`ll be rooting for?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Who he`ll be rooting for? Oh, boy. I -- that`s -- that`s the -- my first way to get hate mail from one part of the country. There`s a division among our senior staff, I will admit, on this particular question but I won`t name names.\par \par \uc2 You know, the president will be watching the game in Delaware with his family, of course, his -- his wife Dr. Biden. In terms of the interview -- and -- and as you know, CBS is hosting the -- the Super Bowl this year and there`s a long tradition of -- of networks doing an interview with the president, so that will certainly be part of what you will see on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 He, of course, will -- you -- that -- the anchor who is doing the interview will ask whatever the anchor wants to ask, that`s how these things occur, but his objective is certainly to convey to the American people that he knows this time is difficult, he knows it requires a great deal of sacrifice, he is incredibly grateful to the health care workers, to the front-line workers, to people who are working every day to keep us safe and hopefully he will have the opportunity to reiterate the measures that we can take -- masking, social distancing, of course ensuring that people are getting the vaccine when they`re eligible to get the vaccine and update the public on his efforts to do exactly that.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: If I -- if I might just ask a second one for a colleague who can`t be here because of COVID restrictions, from Brian Karem (ph) of Playboy, I mean, we -- we saw a break with Saudi policy yesterday in the Middle East. So will the Biden administration openly condemn or implement sanctions against the Saudi government for the death of Washington Post writer Jamal Khashoggi?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So first, let me say and reiterate the murder of Jamal Khashoggi was a horrific crime. We are prepared to release an unclassified report with full transparency for Congress. This is the law and we will follow the law.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the Office of the Director of National Intelligence would have further details and I would refer you to them for additional specifics. We, of course, expect Saudi Arabia to improve its record on human rights, that includes releasing political prisoners such as women`s rights advocates for Saudi jails. We`re encouraged by the release yesterday of two dual national American-Saudi citizens. We hope to see further progress over the next coming months.\par \par \uc2 And as noted in a couple of areas we`ve talked about, there`s an ongoing review, of course, of our policies. You saw the president make an announcement yesterday about our engagement in Yemen, which of course is directly connected, but again, there`ll -- there`ll be ongoing discussions and reviews by our national security team and I don`t have any -- any policy decisions to read out for you particularly at this point in time. Go ahead in the back.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you, and I have two questions, as well.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: In keeping with the economic theme of the day, my first question -- given the most recent job number and the continued unemployment and -- and what you had to say today about -- particularly how minority communities have been affected, is this the right time to increase the number of refugees coming into the country and to also ease immigration and border restrictions?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, the president fundamentally believes that having a -- a -- a humane and moral immigration system in place strengthens our country, strengthens our economy and many, many business leaders across the country have said exactly the same thing.\par \par \uc2 The most powerful step that can be taken now is to pass the American Rescue Plan because that is a step that economists across the board have said will help expedite economic recovery, help expedite getting people back to work, and without it, we will be years behind by -- according to a lot of economic data, where we need to be.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And now, from the public health aspect of it, considering the coronavirus numbers are still where they are, the president has enacted more restrictions on travel restrictions, including South Africa. How does that play into it, with his immigration and refugee policy?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sorry, I don`t -- I`m not sure I`m understanding your question.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Well, I mean, it -- it -- considering the coronavirus numbers where they are and that we`re doing more restrictions on travel, does that not also then affect refugee policy and immigration, bringing more people into the country, sort of following up on Kristen`s...\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, the -- the refugee policy is increasing the cap. It doesn`t change what our travel restrictions, our travel policies are, and of course those are put in place to keep the American people safe. But those are not meant to do anything other than take necessary steps at this moment in time, based on the advice of health and medical experts, on where we need to restrict travel from.\par \par \uc2 Obviously when it`s safe to undo those restrictions, our health and medical team will advise us on exactly that.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And then my second question -- this is going to the Pentagon. This week, the Secretary of Defense announced a stand down for the military to discuss extremism and extremist -- extremist ideology. Why would the military need to stand down from enemies foreign and abroad to have these discussions?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I -- my bet is that Secretary Austin and my -- my friend John Kirby would disagree with your assessment there but the president has tasked a -- an overview and a review of domestic violent extremism in the country. That`s coming directly from him, happening in the White House.\par \par \uc2 I know that my friend John Kirby has a briefing later this afternoon and I certainly encourage people to ask him more about those plans.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Just further on that, there was -- Jake Sullivan mentioned the focus on domestic terrorism yesterday in this administration. I think a lot of people want to know: How does this administration define the term domestic terrorist?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: In what way?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Well, does that include Antifa specifically? What -- how do you set those parameters for domestic terrorists? Because essentially we see, you know, a lot of focus on the January 6th, maybe not as much focus on some of the extremism and violence in the -- the Northwest.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I`ve answered a version of this question a couple times before but I know everybody`s not in the briefing room every day. The reason we have the review, which is not a political review but is a review done by our national security team -- something tasked, again, to take review of domestic violent extremism. We`ll cover incidents across the board. When they have concluded that review, I`m sure they will have more to say on it.\par \par \uc2 OK. Go ahead, Trevor (ph), because I think I`ve been skipping you unintentionally. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: One -- just one quick foreign policy question, you know, just talking about Iran and China and the need to have allies that are willing to go into -- into those kind of thorny issues with you.\par \par \uc2 There`s been some reporting that the E.U. commissioner is going to have a phone call with President Biden and propose a six-month truce on trade tariffs. And I`m just wondering if he`s open to that and whether that would give you a united front going into some of these issues.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I know there`s a lot of interest in trade tariffs and that`s also under review now. I don`t have anything to preview for you. I can follow-up with our team on plans for a call with the E.U. commissioner. And if that`s being planned, we will, of course, provide you with a read out.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Yes, following up on the several questions about school reopenings, does the White House believe -- you know, schools are contemplating this right now, whether to go back and reopen their schools. Does the White House believe that they should hold off on reopening until the CDC guidelines are out and until the American Rescue Plan is passed?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I think some schools are looking for that guidance and also looking for funding. Obviously, different jurisdictions make decisions. But we are hopeful that when the CDC guidelines are out, that will provide some advice -- or from our medical -- more than that, specific guidance from our health and medical team, the expertise from Dr. Walensky and her team on exactly what mitigation steps can and should be taken to reopen schools safely.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: But for schools at the moment right now weighing whether they should reopen, you don`t have a recommendation one way or the other, just to wait for the guidelines? Or -- I`m just trying to clarify...\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We`ll -- we`ll have the -- I am not going to get ahead of what the guidelines are. I think there are different jurisdictions that make different decisions. A lot of school districts are certainly waiting for those guidelines but also waiting for additional funding so that they can reopen schools safely and make sure teachers are safe, students are safe, and families can feel confident in their kids being at school.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you. Following up on one of the questions from earlier and then I have a foreign policy question too. But is there any update on the state of a large-scale public, sort of, P.R. media campaign supporting the vaccination effort? Or is it difficult to do that without knowing whether or not the inventory is sufficient enough to actually tell people to go to CVS or Walgreens or wherever they may be supposed to go?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, certainly part of our commitment is to launch a massive public affairs campaign, which is something we`ve talked about a little bit in here. It takes a little bit of time to get all your ducks in a row to get that going.\par \par \uc2 But part of what we`re also trying to do is utilize our experts to be out there publicly, do these briefings three times a week; to -- as Karen (ph) was asking about earlier -- provide more accurate and clear information to governors so that they can also communicate with communities; empower local medical experts and doctors so that they can communicate more clearly with their communities, which are some of the most trusted sources.\par \par \uc2 We`ve had a lot of officials out on local television, doing local television interviews.\par \par \uc2 So even as we`re preparing for more of a widespread -- or broad-scale, I should say -- public campaign, we`ve also had a number of members of our team doing everything we can to communicate effectively and efficiently on this particular issue.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: And the foreign policy question, sometime overnight during the vote-a-rama in the Senate, there was a...\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: You just wanted to use that word, I know it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: ... there was a -- but there was a 97-to-3 vote in favor of supporting the location of the U.S. embassy in Jerusalem. Is there a position in terms of the Biden administration as to whether or not the Trump administration`s (ph) actually implementation of the Jerusalem Embassy Act should be maintained or whether it might move back to Tel Aviv?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It`s a great question. I have not talked to our national security team about it, I will venture to do that and circle back with you directly.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thank you, everyone. Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: We`ve been listening to today`s daily White House briefing with Jen Psaki there on what has been an extraordinarily busy morning of developments as President Biden and congressional Democrats step up their effort to fast track their $1.9 trillion COVID relief bill and actually there`s a flurry of activity today partially because they know next week with the impeachment trial it`s going to be a little harder to break through. So that`s why I think there`s this intensity today.\par \par \uc2 We just heard White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein speak about how this morning`s sluggish jobs report only reinforces their push for a massive relief bill. Repeating the White House`s mantra that the risk of doing too little or greater in the risk of doing too much. This comes after President Biden just delivered remarks on the economy where he said he would not get bogged down in negotiating a smaller package with Republicans. He was defiant that he`d rather go big and go fast on COVID relief even if that means going without bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 He spoke after meeting with House Democrats at the White House when Speaker Pelosi told reporters immediately afterwards that she guarantees they`ll get the relief bill done before March 14th. That`s when some COVID relief that they passed in December expires. This comes after the Senate pulled an all-nighter in order to create the structure to pass this COVID relief bill without Republican support.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to our chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker who`s with me now in the White House briefing room.\par \par \uc2 And Kristen, if there was any back and forth today, it really was between former Obama economic official and Clinton Treasury secretary \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  and Jared Bernstein, if you will, where \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  threw out this idea that, hey, is this too big of a one-time relief stimulus especially if you don`t get long-term bigger infrastructure like spending approved? The White House pretty sensitive to that \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  criticism.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE NEWS CORRESPONDENT: They were sensitive to that, Chuck. And you could tell that Jared Bernstein was ready for the questions that he got on that very topic. Of course I put that question squarely to him, is the Biden administration going too big with its $1.9 trillion COVID relief plan? And he said very firmly no and then went on to delve into a point by point --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: -- takedown of why he believes Larry summers is wrong in his criticism. And at the core of the argument here that you heard from Jared Bernstein and that you`re hearing from Biden administration officials, Chuck, is that the economic crisis, the COVID crisis is so deep that there`s a real failure -- just getting an announcement overhead, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Apologies, just pausing for that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I hear you. I got you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But the crux of the -- that`s right. The crux of the argument is essentially that if the Biden administration does not go big enough that these issues within the economy are going to go unaddressed and ignored.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And ultimately it is a crisis that will deepen. That is their argument.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And that`s the argument that you heard him make from the podium, Jen Psaki reiterated that argument as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But one point I think that is worth noting, Chuck, remember, they are very mindful of what happened during the Obama administration. That during that 2009 stimulus package.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That was criticized for not being big enough. And that was the product of long negotiations with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And so what you have here is President Biden going it in a very different way, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Very quickly, and I think it`s based on another question you asked. It is not clear to me where the president`s position is on reopening schools. Right? We know he wants it done, but this issue of is it safe and the teachers unions, it seems as if Jen Psaki keeps leaving it ambiguous. Purposeful?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I think so. And I think that they are waiting for that final guidance from the CDC about whether teachers need to be vaccinated in order to reopen schools safely, Chuck. But you`re right, President Biden continues to insist that that is what he wants to see happen in the coming months. And again there is an urgency to this because schools are making their plans as we speak, and so they need to know what guidelines are in place in order to move forward safely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now the CDC director, Dr. Walensky initially said it was her assessment and the science suggested that schools could start to reopen without teachers getting vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But she and the Biden administration have walked that back to some extent in the wake of her making those comments and she was very firm that they`re going to wait for CDC guidance before weighing in today, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Kristen Welker in the press room for us. Kristen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. If it`s Sunday it`s "MEET THE PRESS" on NBC. And check our digital feed. We have a tremendous interview about the Rooney rule in the NFL and black coaches in the NFL. Be sure to check that on our feed.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC coverage will continue with Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright